      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "BRCA1",
      "explanation": "Li-Fraumeni syndrome (p53 mutation) is associated with early-onset breast cancer, sarcoma and leukaemia\n\nP53 is the correct answer. The patient has Li-Fraumeni syndrome (LFS), which is caused by a p53 mutation. It is characterized by early-onset cancers including breast cancer, sarcoma and leukaemia. There are different classification criteria for Li-Fraumeni syndrome, which are used to select a population to undergo genetic testing to confirm a diagnosis. The classic LFS criteria are when a patient fulfils all three of the following:\n\nA sarcoma diagnosed before age 45\nA first-degree relative, meaning a parent, sibling or child, with any cancer before age 45\nA first-degree relative or second-degree relative, meaning a grandparent, aunt/uncle, niece/nephew, or grandchild, with any cancer before age 45 or a sarcoma at any age.\n\nPTEN (Phosphatase and tensin homolog) is incorrect. . Mutations in this gene are associated with Cowden syndrome, an inherited cancer syndrome that increases the risk of breast, thyroid and endometrial cancers.\n\nBRCA1 (Breast cancer type 1 susceptibility protein) is incorrect. An inherited mutation in this gene is associated most commonly with the development of breast and ovarian cancer. Less commonly associated cancers are peritoneal cancer, pancreatic cancer, prostate cancer, male breast cancer and Fanconi's anaemia.\n\nBRCA2 (Breast cancer type 2 susceptibility protein)  is incorrect. An inherited mutation in this gene is associated with the development of breast and ovarian cancer and less commonly peritoneal cancer, pancreatic cancer, prostate cancer, male breast cancer and Fanconi's anaemia. It is associated with similar cancers to BRCA1 (Breast cancer type 1 susceptibility protein) but in different proportions, at different age ranges and in some cases different histological subtypes.\n\nMSH2 (DNA mismatch repair protein) is incorrect. A mutation in this gene is associated with the development of hereditary non-polyposis colorectal carcinoma (Lynch syndrome)."
