      "explanation": "IgE is synthesised by plasma cells\n\nPlasma cells\n\nis the correct answer. This patient has presented with an infection with the parasitic helminth\n\nTaenia solium\n\n, and consequent eosinophilia. Plasma cells are mature B lymphocyte cells that are activated by infection and produce the IgE responsible for activating eosinophils and stimulating them to attack his helminth infection.\n\nBasophils\n\nis an incorrect option as these cells do not produce immunoglobulins. Basophils are a subset of granulocytes that degranulate in response to inflammatory stimuli.\n\nCytotoxic T-cells\n\nis an incorrect option as these cells do not produce immunoglobulin and do not play a significant role in the initial host immune response to parasitic infection. They have a more significant role in antiviral and anti-tumour immunity.\n\nEosinophils\n\nis an incorrect option as these cells do not produce the IgE involved in the host response to parasitic infection, but instead are activated by it.\n\nMast cells\n\nis an incorrect option as they also do not produce immunoglobulins. They perform an important part of the innate immune response to a range of antigenic stimuli."
