      "explanation": "Pellagra is caused by vitamin B3 (niacin) deficiency\n\nVitamin B3\n\nis the correct answer. The patient has evidence of dementia, diarrhoea and dermatitis. This is typical of Pellagra caused by niacin deficiency. It may result in death. These patients often have a characteristic rash around their necks called a Casal necklace. Alcoholism is a risk factor for niacin deficiency as it results in poor ability to use ingested niacin.\n\nVitamin B1\n\nis incorrect. Deficiency of thiamine (vitamin B1) is common in alcoholics and can cause Wernicke's encephalopathy. This is a cause of confusion but it is typically associated with ophthalmoplegia and ataxia, which are absent here. Additionally, it is not typically associated with dermatitis.\n\nVitamin B12\n\nis incorrect. Vitamin B12 deficiency is associated with a host of symptoms but it is not typically associated with dermatitis in this characteristic distribution or diarrhoea. In severe B12 deficiency, we may expect peripheral neuropathy with extensor plantar responses and abnormal reflexes (i.e. subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord), which are absent there.\n\nVitamin C\n\nis incorrect. This deficiency is more common in alcoholics. However, it is typically associated with bleeding from mucus membranes and easy bruising, which are not mentioned here.\n\nVitamin D\n\nis incorrect. Deficiency of this vitamin causes myalgia and proximal myopathy in severe cases. It is not associated with dementia, diarrhoea or dermatitis."
