      "correctText": "Renal cortical collecting duct",
      "explanation": "The majority of glucose reabsorption in the nephron occurs within the proximal convoluted tubule\n\nDapagliflozin is a reversible sodium-glucose co-transporter 2 (SGLT-2) inhibitor that acts on the\n\nrenal proximal convoluted tubule\n\nto reduce glucose reabsorption. The proximal convoluted tubule is the primary site of glucose reabsorption and so inhibition here leads to significant glucose loss to urine (glycosuria). Glycosuria has benefits for glycaemic control and weight loss but does increase the risk of urinary tract infections. Other SGLT-2 inhibitors include canagliflozin and empagliflozin (note the\n\n-flo-\n\nin the middle of the drug names can be used as memory aid: think of flow of urine).\n\nThe\n\nrenal distal convoluted tubule\n\nis important in the absorption of many ions include sodium, chloride, calcium and potassium. It has no significant role in glucose reabsorption.\n\nThe\n\nrenal cortical collecting duct\n\nregulates the reabsorption of water. It again has no significant role in glucose reabsorption.\n\nPancreatic beta cells\n\nare the main site of action for sulfonylureas, another class of drug in the management of diabetes.\n\nPancreatic acinar cells\n\nhave an important role in exocrine function but they are not the primary target of SGLT-2 inhibitors."
