      "correctText": "Western blotting",
      "explanation": "Molecular biology techniques\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nWestern blotting\n\n. Western blotting is a widely used analytical technique in molecular biology and medicine to detect specific proteins in a sample of tissue homogenate or extract. It uses gel electrophoresis to separate denatured proteins by the length of the polypeptide. The proteins are then transferred to a membrane, where they can be stained to show the pattern of protein expression. This makes it an excellent tool for detecting and quantifying viral proteins.\n\nPolymerase chain reaction (PCR)\n\nis incorrect as this technique is primarily used for amplification and detection of DNA and RNA sequences, not proteins. PCR can indirectly suggest the presence of a virus if the viral genome or transcripts are detected, but it does not directly quantify viral proteins.\n\nNorthern blotting\n\nalso would not be suitable for detecting viral proteins as it is a technique used in molecular biology research to study gene expression by the detection of RNA (or isolated mRNA) in a sample.\n\nSouthern blotting\n\nis another technique that wouldn't be appropriate for this purpose. Southern blotting is used for detection of a specific DNA sequence in DNA samples. While it's useful in some aspects of virology such as determining virus integration into host DNA, it doesn't detect or quantify viral proteins.\n\nFinally,\n\nEastern blotting\n\n, while less commonly known than its counterparts, is also not suitable here. Eastern blotting refers to either one of two different methods (depending on who you ask), but neither involves the direct detection or quantification of viral proteins: one method involves analysis post-translational modifications of peptides and proteins such as lipids and phosphates; the other refers to transfer of already blotted (Western, Northern or Southern) biomolecules onto PVDF or nitrocellulose membranes for further analysis using other probes."
