      "explanation": "Tall, long fingered, downward lens dislocation, learning difficulties, DVT - homocystinuria\n\nThe unifying diagnosis in this patient is homocystinuria - an autosomal recessive condition caused by defects in methionine metabolism. Features consistent with homocystinuria in this patient include malar flush, tall stature, arachnodactyly, myopia, learning difficulties and unprovoked DVT at a young age. The significance of his immigration status is that babies born in the UK would usually be screened for homocystinuria via the newborn heel prick test, whereas this may have been missed in the given scenario.\n\nCystinuria is an autosomal recessive condition caused by inadequate reabsorption of cysteine in the proximal convoluted tubule, leading to recurrent nephrocalcinosis.\n\nBehÃ§et's disease is a reasonable diagnosis to consider when dealing with a patient from Central Asia with an unprovoked DVT at a young age - however, nearly all patients with BehÃ§et's disease present with ulcerations, usually of the mouth. Other 'hallmark' symptoms of BehÃ§et's include uveitis or iritis; genital ulcers and skin lesions such as folliculitis, which are not seen here. BehÃ§et's does not provide an explanation for this patient's myopia, learning difficulties, or arachnodactyly.\n\nPhenylketonuria is a different inborn error of metabolism that can lead to learning difficulties and is usually picked up on the heel prick test. However, the other common defining feature of phenylketonuria is seizure activity, rather than any of the signs and symptoms described in this patient.\n\nTay-Sachs disease is a rare lysosomal storage disorder, which is associated with progressive neurological deterioration and is usually fatal before adulthood."
