      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Internal receptors",
      "explanation": "Adrenaline acts via G protein-coupled receptors\n\nThe drug which has been administered in this circumstance is adrenaline. Adrenaline binds to G protein-coupled receptors. G protein-coupled receptors, also known as seven-transmembrane domain receptors, are located in the cell membrane and bind to extracellular molecules and transmit signals intracellularly. When a ligand binds to the G-protein coupled receptor, it causes a conformational change in the receptor. This then causes an associated G protein to become activated by exchanging GDP for GTP. The two principal signalling pathways involved in G protein-coupled receptors intracellularly are cyclic AMP (cAMP) pathway and the phosphatidylinositol pathway.\n\nInternal receptors, or intracellular receptors, are in the cytoplasm of the cell and respond to hydrophobic ligand molecules that move across the plasma membrane. Examples include thyroid and steroid hormones.\n\nEnzyme-linked receptors, or a catalytic receptor, is a transmembrane receptor, whereby the binding of an extracellular ligand results in enzymatic activity within the intracellular side. Examples include receptor tyrosine kinase.\n\nIon channel-linked receptors open in response to specific ligand molecules binding to the extracellular component of the receptor protein. An example of this type of receptor is acetylcholine receptors.\n\nVoltage-sensitive ion channels are located in excitable tissues and are a potential target for drugs which block the channel or interfere with conductance in other ways. Example of ion channels which can be targeted include Na+ channels which are blocked by some local anaesthetics (e.g. lidocaine)."
