      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "One-way analysis of variance (ANOVA)",
      "explanation": "Wilcoxon signed-rank test - compares two sets of observations on a single sample, e.g. a 'before' and 'after' test on the same population following an intervention\n\nWilcoxon signed-rank test is the most appropriate test to use in this situation. As the data is paired but not normally distributed, non-parametric measures should be used in the analysis process (exceptions can sometimes be made if the sample size is extremely and the distribution only slightly skewed).\n\nWilcoxon signed-rank test is the non-parametric test used when data is in the form of two paired measurements (eg, 2 pulse measurements for the same patient in this case). It does not assume a normal distribution but does assume that the data is symmetrical. The test ranks the differences between the values and then calculates whether the values are likely to come from the same population (ie, whether there is a true difference between the before and after measurements).\n\nPaired sample t-test is incorrect; this is used for paired data as in this case, but one of the key assumptions for justifying the use of this test is that the data is normally distributed. This is not the case in this scenario.\n\nMann-Whitney U test is incorrect; whilst this is used for non-parametric data as in this scenario, it is not used for paired data.\n\nOne-way ANOVA is incorrect; this is used to compare the means of parametric data when there are more than 2 groups.\n\nIndependent sample t-test is incorrect; this is used for parametric, non-paired data."
