      "correctText": "Heart failure",
      "explanation": "Gaucher's disease is the most common lipid storage disorder and a cause of hepatosplenomegaly\n\nGaucher's disease is an autosomal recessive disease and it is the most common lipid storage disorder. It's a cause of hepatosplenomegaly. The family history in the history indicates Gaucher's disease as the most likely.\n\nHeart failure is unusual in children and does not usually cause massive splenomegaly.\n\nChronic myeloid leukaemia can cause massive splenomegaly but it is unusual in children.\n\nAcute myeloid leukaemia can cause splenomegaly but massive splenomegaly is rare. It is not usually inherited.\n\nKala azar, also known as visceral leishmaniasis, is a cause of massive splenomegaly but given the lack of travel history, it is unlikely."
