      "id": 45,
      "question": "A 31-year-old man is brought into the emergency department with a 2-day history of a severe headache and fever. He feels nauseated and has vomited 3 times in the last 6 hours. He reports being unwell during his adolescent years and more prone to bacterial infections.\n\nOn examination, he has a temperature of 39.2ºC. His heart rate is 110bpm and regular with a blood pressure of 103/60mmHg. He has a capillary refill time of 4 seconds with mottled skin peripherally. Chest sounds are clear and heart sounds are normal. His abdomen is soft and non-tender with no palpable organomegaly. Kernig's sign is positive.\n\nWhich of the following complement deficiencies increases the likelihood of this patient's presentation?",
