      "correctText": "Impaired vitamin B6-dependent neurotransmitter synthesis leading to neuronal dysfunction",
      "explanation": "Vitamin B1 (thiamine) deficiency may be caused by alcohol excess\n\nDecreased activity of thiamine-dependent enzymes impairing glucose metabolism\n\nis the correct answer. Thiamine, or vitamin B1, is an essential cofactor for key enzymes involved in energy metabolism, including pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and transketolase. A deficiency in thiamine leads to a reduction in the activity of these enzymes, which hinders glucose utilisation and results in an accumulation of lactic acid within the brain. Chronic alcohol consumption can further aggravate this deficiency by interfering with the absorption, transport, and storage of thiamine. The clinical presentation of confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia seen in this patient is characteristic of Wernicke’s encephalopathy—a condition that arises from an energy deficit in vulnerable areas of the brain, such as the mammillary bodies and thalamus.\n\nAccumulation of toxic branched-chain keto acids due to defective dehydrogenase activity\n\nis incorrect because it refers to maple syrup urine disease—a hereditary disorder marked by deficient activity of the branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase enzyme complex. This condition typically presents with neurological symptoms and metabolic acidosis during infancy rather than adulthood and has no link to alcohol intake. While both disorders involve enzyme deficiencies, the specific clinical features such as confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia observed in this adult patient are consistent with thiamine deficiency impacting glucose metabolism.\n\nDemyelination of central nervous system axons due to chronic alcohol use\n\nis incorrect since prolonged alcohol consumption may lead to demyelinating conditions like central pontine myelinolysis or peripheral neuropathy as a result of nutritional deficiencies. However, demyelination typically manifests with symptoms such as motor weakness, spasticity or dysarthria rather than the triad indicative of Wernicke’s encephalopathy. The acute presentation featuring confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia points towards a metabolic disturbance rather than structural damage—thus distinguishing thiamine deficiency from demyelinating pathologies.\n\nImpaired vitamin B6 (pyridoxine)-dependent neurotransmitter synthesis leading to neuronal dysfunction\n\nis incorrect because vitamin B6 is crucial for the production of neurotransmitters like serotonin, dopamine, and gamma-aminobutyric acid. Deficiency in vitamin B6 may cause mood disturbances, peripheral neuropathy and seizures but not typically ophthalmoplegia or ataxia—symptoms that are indicative of a thiamine deficit affecting neurons dependent on energy metabolism. Although excessive alcohol intake can result in the depletion of multiple vitamins, the neurological signs exhibited are more indicative of Wernicke’s encephalopathy.\n\nNeuronal oxidative stress secondary to alcohol-induced mitochondrial dysfunction\n\nis incorrect as although chronic alcohol use can impair mitochondrial function leading to oxidative stress that injures neurons, this tends to cause broad cognitive deficits and fatigue rather than focal neurological signs typical of Wernicke’s encephalopathy. Instead, the sudden onset of confusion along with ophthalmoplegia and ataxia experienced by this patient directly relates to disruptions in thiamine-dependent enzymatic processes—not oxidative stress-related neuronal injury."
