      "correctText": "Axillary",
      "explanation": "Shoulder abduction - deltoid muscle - axillary nerve (C5,C6)\n\nThe correct answer is the\n\nAxillary\n\nnerve. The axillary nerve originates from the posterior cord of the brachial plexus and provides motor innervation to the deltoid muscle, which is responsible for shoulder abduction. Damage or injury to this nerve can lead to an inability to abduct the shoulder.\n\nThe\n\nLateral cutaneous\n\nnerve, also known as the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm, is a branch of the musculocutaneous nerve and it supplies sensation to the lateral aspect of the forearm. It does not supply any muscles.\n\nThe\n\nSuprascapular\n\nnerve arises from the upper trunk of the brachial plexus and passes through the suprascapular notch under the transverse scapular ligament. It supplies two muscles - supraspinatus and infraspinatus. Supraspinatus helps in initial 15 degree abduction and infraspinatus does the external rotation of the shoulder joint. Neither of them is responsible for the main abduction movement which is performed by the deltoid muscle.\n\nThe\n\nMusculocutaneous\n\nnerve arises from the lateral cord of the brachial plexus and supplies three muscles in the anterior compartment of the arm - biceps brachii, coracobrachialis and part of brachialis. Biceps brachii does flexion at the elbow joint while coracobrachialis does flexion at the shoulder joint.\n\nLastly, the\n\nMedian\n\nnerve originates from both medial and lateral cords of the brachial plexus, supplying most of the flexor muscles in the forearm and several muscles in the hand but it doesn't have any contribution towards movements at the shoulder joint."
