      "explanation": "Leptin is secreted by adipose tissue\n\nLeptin\n\nis correct because it is a hormone secreted by adipose tissue that regulates appetite and energy balance by acting on the hypothalamus to suppress hunger and increase energy expenditure. In individuals with normal leptin function, increased fat stores lead to higher leptin levels, which signal satiety and reduce food intake. However, in common obesity, leptin resistance develops, meaning the brain no longer responds effectively to leptin’s satiety signals. This results in persistent hunger and difficulty losing weight despite elevated leptin levels. Unlike congenital leptin deficiency, which leads to early-onset severe obesity and can be treated with leptin replacement, leptin resistance cannot be treated with exogenous leptin alone because the issue lies in impaired hypothalamic signalling rather than hormone deficiency.\n\nAdiponectin\n\nis incorrect because while it is also secreted by adipose tissue, its primary role is to enhance insulin sensitivity and regulate lipid metabolism rather than control appetite. Unlike leptin, adiponectin levels are reduced in obesity and metabolic syndrome.\n\nGhrelin\n\nis incorrect because it is a hunger-stimulating hormone secreted by the stomach, not adipose tissue. Ghrelin increases appetite before meals and decreases after eating by acting on the hypothalamus. In obesity, ghrelin levels are often dysregulated but do not play a primary role in leptin resistance.\n\nNeuropeptide Y\n\nis incorrect because it is a hypothalamic neurotransmitter that strongly stimulates appetite but is not secreted by adipose tissue. Leptin inhibits neuropeptide Y to suppress hunger, meaning leptin resistance leads to unchecked neuropeptide Y activity, promoting food intake.\n\nPeptide YY\n\nis incorrect because it is secreted by intestinal L cells after food intake and helps reduce appetite by delaying gastric emptying. It is not directly affected by leptin resistance and does not originate from adipose tissue."
