      "id": 64,
      "question": "In the UK, we have numerous screening programmes for important health issues such as breast cancer. A new screening test for breast cancer is being evaluated compared to the conventional use of mammography. There are concerns this test appears to lengthen survival as the breast cancer is picked up at an earlier stage but the overall outcome remains unchanged.\n\nWhat type of bias is this?",
      "options": [
        "Length time bias",
        "Lead time bias",
        "Self selection bias",
        "Procedure Bias",
        "Recall Bias"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Procedure Bias",
      "explanation": "Lead-time bias - occurs when two tests for a disease are compared, the new test diagnoses the disease earlier, but there is no effect on the outcome of the disease\n\nLength time bias occurs where a disease may progress at different rates therefore slower growing or less aggressive disease has more opportunity to be picked up via screening than aggressive/fast-developing disease.\n\nSelf-selection or volunteer bias is due to the fact that people who tend to engage in screening programmes are not representative of the population (if they take part in screening, they will usually be of a higher socio-economic stance and practice other lifestyle choices that will improve their health as well as screening).\n\nProcedure bias is usually related to comparative studies. It is where patients are treated differently depending on their group allocation.\n\nRecall bias is where the accuracy of data is affected by the retrospective collection. For example, if you were looking at past asbestos exposure, a patient may not be able to recall the exact years they may have been exposed."
