      "explanation": "Dermatitis herpetiformis is associated with HLA-DR3\n\nDermatitis herpetiformis is associated with the\n\nHLA-DR3\n\nsubtype. It is a skin condition that is regarded as a cutaneous manifestation of coeliac disease. This patient has presented with a long history of fatigue and diarrhoea with a probable iron deficiency anaemia, which is consistent with a diagnosis of coeliac disease. Dermatitis herpetiformis commonly affects the forearms, legs and buttocks and is typically an itchy vesicular rash that resembles herpes virus infection. HLA is another name for major histocompatibility complex (MHC) proteins found in humans. Class 1 MHC proteins include HLA subtypes A, B, and C while class 2 MHC proteins include HLA subtypes DM, DO, DP, DQ, and DR.\n\nHLA-B27\n\nis associated with a range of diseases including ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis, and ulcerative colitis. It is not, however, typically associated with dermatitis herpetiformis.\n\nBehcet's disease is most commonly associated with\n\nHLA-B51\n\n.\n\nWhile coeliac disease is associated with\n\nHLA-DQ2 and DQ8\n\n, it is the HLA-DR3 subtype that is most commonly associated with dermatitis herpetiformis.\n\nType 1 diabetes mellitus is typically associated with\n\nHLA-DR4\n\n. It may also be associated with HLA-DR3."
