      "id": 73,
      "question": "You have been asked to prepare a presentation for the hospital journal club on a novel drug in the treatment of heart failure.\n\nYou find a relevant recent systematic review and meta-analysis which combines data from multiple randomised controlled trials investigating the effectiveness of the novel drug on five-year survival in patients with heart failure.\n\nA figure in the meta-analysis shows each study as a dot on a plot where the x-axis represents the odds ratio for each study and the y-axis is the standard error of the odds ratio for each study.\n\nWhat is the name of figure?",
      "options": [
        "Box plot",
        "Forest plot",
        "Funnel plot",
        "Kaplan-Meir curve",
        "Manhattan plot"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Manhattan plot",
      "explanation": "Funnel plots - show publication bias in meta-analyses\n\nThe plot described is a\n\nfunnel plot\n\nwhich is used to visually assess for publication bias in meta-analysis. Publication bias can result from differences in the chances of a study being published based on the findings of that study; typically, it stems from the relative under-representation in the literature of studies with non-significant results.\n\nA funnel plot is a scatter-plot where each study is plotted as a point. The x-axis is some measure of effect size (in this case odds ratio) and the y-axis is some measure of precision (in this case standard error of the odds ratio). The idea is that if there is no publication bias, larger studies with more precise results will cluster around the top of the plot, with smaller studies spreading out symmetrically below them (to create a sort of triangle shape/upside down funnel shape). The key is the symmetry of the plot: you expect as many smaller studies with results less extreme than the larger studies as smaller studies with results more extreme.\n\nA\n\nforest plot\n\nis another commonly used plot in meta-analysis and is a way to visualise results from multiple studies. Like funnel plots, forest plots also use effect size (e.g. odds ratio) on the x-axis. However, there is no formal y-axis. In place of a y-axis, there is a vertical line which represents the line of no effect. Each study is plotted as a box on a horizontal line above the x-axis; the position of the box along the x-axis is the estimated effect size for that study and the line spans the 95% confidence intervals for the estimate. A diamond is typically plotted at the bottom of the forest plot which shows the average effect size from all studies.\n\nA\n\nKaplan-Meir curve\n\nis a visual display of a survival function, which shows the probability of an event at a given time. It therefore has some measure of probability (usually survival) on the y-axis and a measure of time on the x-axis.\n\nA\n\nManhattan plot\n\nis used to display data from genome-wide association studies. It has the chromosomal position of a genetic locus on the x-axis, and the significance of the association of that locus with the disease on the y-axis.\n\nA\n\nbox plot\n\nis a commonly used plot to visually display the distribution and skew of data from a single study or experiment. It is not typically used in meta-analysis."
