      "explanation": "The main source of IL-1 is macrophages\n\nThe correct answer is macrophages.\n\nAnakinra is an interleukin-1 (IL-1) receptor antagonist. Interleukin-1 refers to a 'superfamily' of 11 cytokines that are responsible for acute inflammation and inducing fever. Blocking IL-1 with anakinra is beneficial in pro-inflammatory conditions such as rheumatoid arthritis. Il-1 is predominantly secreted by macrophages.\n\nB cells are also a source of IL-1, but to a much lesser degree than macrophages. B cells ultimately differentiate into plasma cells and primarily secrete antibodies.\n\nNatural killer cells also secrete IL-1, but to a lesser extent than macrophages.\n\nT helper cells secrete a variety of cytokines, including IL-4, which stimulates the proliferation of B cells, and IL-5, which stimulates the production of eosinophils.\n\nT killer cells primarily secrete perforin and granzymes, proteins that act to destroy pathogens, alongside antibodies released by plasma cells. They are not thought to be significant sources of IL-1."
