      "explanation": "Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) - site of action = collecting ducts\n\nThe diagnosis here is that of cranial diabetes insipidus, which can occur following head trauma. The urine osmolalities in this scenario confirm the diagnosis. The 'deficient substance' in question is therefore antidiuretic hormone (ADH), which is not adequately produced in cranial diabetes insipidus. ADH acts on the collecting ducts of the kidneys to promote water reabsorption.\n\nThe hypothalamus is incorrect - this is where ADH is synthesised (in the supraoptic nuclei), not where it ultimately acts.\n\nThe Loop of Henle is incorrect - whilst this is part of the nephron structure in the kidneys, this is not the primary site of action of ADH.\n\nThe posterior pituitary gland is incorrect - ADH is released from the posterior pituitary (after being synthesised by the hypothalamus); its action is in the collecting ducts.\n\nThe proximal convoluted tubule is another part of the structure of the nephron - it is not the site of action of ADH."
