      "question": "What is the main action of atrial natriuretic peptide?",
      "options": [
        "Promotes renin excretion",
        "Reduces excretion of sodium and water",
        "Vasodilation",
        "Promotes aldosterone excretion",
        "Vasoconstriction"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Vasodilation",
      "explanation": "Atrial natriuretic peptide - powerful vasodilator\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nVasodilation\n\n. Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) is a hormone secreted from the atrial cells of the heart in response to atrial stretching and an increase in circulating blood volume. ANP has been shown to promote vasodilation, which aids in reducing preload and ultimately decreasing blood pressure.\n\nThe option\n\nPromotes renin excretion\n\nis incorrect. ANP actually inhibits the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), leading to a decrease in renin release rather than promoting its excretion.\n\nReduces excretion of sodium and water\n\nis also incorrect. In fact, one of the primary actions of ANP is opposite to this; it increases renal sodium and water excretion, contributing to its diuretic effect which helps reduce blood volume and hence lower blood pressure.\n\nPromotes aldosterone excretion\n\nis not accurate either. While ANP does inhibit aldosterone secretion via its inhibition of RAAS, it doesn't directly promote aldosterone excretion.\n\nLastly,\n\nVasoconstriction\n\nis incorrect as this action would increase peripheral resistance and raise blood pressure, which contradicts the main function of ANP - lowering blood pressure through vasodilation and diuresis."
