      "correctText": "Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)",
      "explanation": "Human papillomavirus 16/18 is a risk factor for oropharyngeal cancer\n\nOf the options listed,\n\nHPV 16\n\nis the correct answer. This virus is strongly linked to oropharyngeal cancer, as is HPV 18. These two subtypes are also linked to the majority of other HPV-associated cancers, including cervical anal, penile and vulval cancers. In contrast to this, HPV 6 and 11 are associated with sexually-transmitted genital warts.\n\nEBV\n\nis incorrect. This virus is associated with nasopharyngeal, not oropharyngeal, cancer. It is classically also associated with Hodgkin's lymphoma and Burkitt's lymphoma.\n\nHBV\n\nis incorrect. This is associated with hepatocellular carcinoma, as is the hepatitis C virus. These are not linked with oropharyngeal cancer.\n\nHTLV-1\n\nis incorrect. This is associated with adult T-cell leukaemia, not oropharyngeal cancer.\n\nHHV-8\n\nis incorrect. This virus is associated with Kaposi's sarcoma, not oropharyngeal cancer, particularly in HIV-positive patients."
