      "id": 83,
      "question": "A 24-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a nosebleed that has been ongoing for 2 hours despite applying pressure. She reports having had three similar episodes in the past 6 months. She also mentions easy bruising and prolonged bleeding after dental extraction last month. She takes no regular medications and has no significant past medical history. On examination, she has multiple ecchymoses on her limbs of varying ages. Her vital signs are stable. Blood tests are performed.\n\n\nHaemoglobin\t118 g/L\t(115-160)\nPlatelets\t245 × 10^9/L\t(150-400)\nWhite cell count\t7.2 × 10^9/L\t(4.0-11.0)\nProthrombin time (PT)\t19.2 seconds\t(11.5-15.5)\nActivated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT)\t32 seconds\t(30-40)\nThrombin time (TT)\t14 seconds\t(14-16)\nFibrinogen\t2.8 g/L\t(2.0-4.0)\n\nWhat is the most likely bleeding disorder?",
      "options": [
        "Factor VII deficiency",
        "von Willebrand disease",
        "Factor VIII deficiency",
        "Factor IX deficiency",
        "Vitamin K deficiency"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "von Willebrand disease",
      "explanation": "Isolated prolongation of PT with normal aPTT→ ?Factor VII deficiency\n\nFactor VII deficiency\n\nis the most likely diagnosis in this case. The patient has a prolonged prothrombin time (PT) with normal activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) and thrombin time (TT). Factor VII is a crucial component of the extrinsic coagulation pathway, which is measured by the PT. When Factor VII is deficient, the PT is prolonged while the aPTT, which measures the intrinsic and common pathways, remains normal. This isolated prolongation of PT with normal aPTT is characteristic of Factor VII deficiency. Factor VII deficiency is a rare autosomal recessive bleeding disorder with an estimated prevalence of 1 in 500,000. The clinical presentation typically includes mucocutaneous bleeding such as epistaxis (nosebleeds), easy bruising, and menorrhagia in women, which aligns with this patient's presentation. The severity of bleeding can vary widely, from mild to severe, depending on the degree of factor deficiency.\n\nVon Willebrand disease\n\nis incorrect in this scenario. While it is the most common inherited bleeding disorder and can present with mucocutaneous bleeding similar to our patient, the laboratory findings would be different. Von Willebrand disease typically presents with a normal or slightly prolonged PT, potentially prolonged aPTT (especially in type 2N and 3), and reduced von Willebrand factor antigen and activity levels. The isolated prolongation of PT with normal aPTT seen in this patient is not consistent with von Willebrand disease.\n\nFactor VIII deficiency\n\n(Haemophilia A) is not the correct diagnosis here. Haemophilia A is an X-linked recessive disorder that primarily affects males, though female carriers can occasionally be symptomatic. More importantly, Factor VIII is part of the intrinsic pathway, so its deficiency would lead to a prolonged aPTT with a normal PT. Our patient has the opposite pattern with a prolonged PT and normal aPTT, ruling out Factor VIII deficiency.\n\nFactor IX deficiency\n\n(Haemophilia B) is also incorrect. Like Haemophilia A, it is an X-linked recessive disorder affecting the intrinsic pathway. A deficiency in Factor IX would result in a prolonged aPTT with a normal PT, which does not match our patient's coagulation profile showing a prolonged PT with normal aPTT.\n\nVitamin K deficiency\n\nis not the most likely diagnosis in this case. While vitamin K deficiency can cause a prolonged PT due to reduced synthesis of vitamin K-dependent factors (II, VII, IX, X), it typically affects multiple coagulation factors simultaneously. This usually results in prolongation of both PT and aPTT, especially in more severe deficiency states. Additionally, our patient has no risk factors mentioned for vitamin K deficiency such as malnutrition, malabsorption, antibiotic use, or liver disease. The isolated PT prolongation with completely normal aPTT is more suggestive of an isolated Factor VII deficiency rather than vitamin K deficiency."
