      "explanation": "Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) - site of action = collecting ducts\n\nThe 'deficient substance' being alluded to here is antidiuretic hormone (ADH); the patient has a diagnosis of cranial diabetes insipidus following head trauma, as evidenced by the urine osmolality results. ADH acts in the collecting ducts of the kidneys - it binds to vasopressin receptors and ultimately leads to upregulation of aquaporin-2 channels, responsible for water reabsorption.\n\nADH has some small action at the distal convoluted tubules. However, by the far the greatest action takes place at the collecting ducts, via the mechanism described above.\n\nADH does not act in the loop of Henle. Several medications exert their effect here, including the aptly-named loop diuretics.\n\nThe posterior pituitary gland is the site of release of ADH. After synthesis in the hypothalamus, ADH migrates to the posterior pituitary gland.\n\nProximal convoluted tubules are the site of action of several drugs, including diuretics, but not of ADH."
