      "id": 100,
      "question": "A 32-year-old woman is referred to the haematology clinic with persistent fatigue, night sweats, and weight loss for 3 months. Examination reveals cervical lymphadenopathy. A lymph node biopsy is performed, and the pathologist requests fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) to identify a specific chromosomal translocation.\n\nWhat is the most accurate description of this technique?",
      "options": [
        "It detects RNA transcripts using gel electrophoresis and radioactive probes",
        "It uses fluorescent DNA probes to bind to specific gene sequences for direct visualisation",
        "It identifies protein products of oncogenes using enzyme-linked antibodies",
        "It amplifies small segments of DNA through repeated cycles of denaturation and replication",
        "It measures gene expression levels through quantification of mRNA"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "It uses fluorescent DNA probes to bind to specific gene sequences for direct visualisation",
      "explanation": "Fluorescence in situ hybridization uses fluorescent DNA or RNA probe to bind to specific gene site of interest for direct visualisation of chromosomal anomalies\n\nIt uses fluorescent DNA probes to bind to specific gene sequences for direct visualisation\n\nis the correct description of fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH). In this patient's case, FISH would be particularly useful for identifying chromosomal translocations associated with lymphomas, such as t(14;18) in follicular lymphoma or t(11;14) in mantle cell lymphoma. FISH works by using fluorescently labelled DNA probes that are complementary to specific DNA sequences of interest. These probes hybridize (bind) to their target sequences in the patient's chromosomes, allowing direct visualisation of chromosomal abnormalities under a fluorescence microscope. This technique is valuable in haematological malignancies where specific genetic aberrations have diagnostic, prognostic, and therapeutic implications. FISH can detect translocations, deletions, amplifications, and other chromosomal rearrangements with high sensitivity.\n\nIt detects RNA transcripts using gel electrophoresis and radioactive probes\n\ndescribes Northern blotting, not FISH. Northern blotting is used to detect specific RNA molecules in a sample. The RNA is separated by size using gel electrophoresis, transferred to a membrane, and then hybridized with labelled probes. While both techniques involve hybridization, Northern blotting detects RNA rather than DNA and typically uses a blotting process rather than in situ detection.\n\nIt identifies protein products of oncogenes using enzyme-linked antibodies\n\ndescribes techniques such as immunohistochemistry or Western blotting, not FISH. These protein detection methods use antibodies rather than nucleic acid probes. In the context of lymphoma diagnosis, immunohistochemistry would be used to identify cell surface markers or abnormal protein expression, but would not directly detect chromosomal abnormalities.\n\nIt amplifies small segments of DNA through repeated cycles of denaturation and replication\n\ndescribes polymerase chain reaction (PCR), not FISH. PCR is an amplification technique that creates multiple copies of a specific DNA segment, whereas FISH is a hybridization technique that visualizes DNA sequences in their chromosomal context without amplification.\n\nIt measures gene expression levels through quantification of mRNA\n\ndescribes techniques such as real-time PCR or RNA sequencing, not FISH. These techniques quantify the amount of specific mRNA molecules to determine gene expression levels, whereas FISH localizes and visualizes specific DNA sequences within chromosomes."
