      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Development of fatty streaks",
      "explanation": "Smooth muscle proliferation and migration from the tunica media into the intima is the last step in the formation of an atheroma\n\nWe have an elderly patient coming with typical anginal pain. The most common cause of ischaemic heart disease is atherosclerosis, and this patient has risk factors for the same, namely smoking and hyperlipidaemia.\n\nThe pathological process involved in atherosclerosis begins with thickening of the tunica intima. Histologically, this is mainly composed of proteoglycan rich extracellular matrix and acellular lipid pools.\n\nFatty streaks are minimal lipid depositions on the luminal surface which are not necessarily a part of the atheroma. They can be seen in normal individuals also and begin as early as in the twenties.\n\nAs the pathological process evolves, we start seeing the early stages of fibroatheroma which is characterized primarily by infiltration of macrophages & T-lymphocytes, with the formation of a well-demarcated lipid-rich necrotic core.\n\nThe appearance of foam cells occur relatively early in the disease process and play a major role in the pathogenesis of atheroma formation.\n\nFurther progression leads to a thin cap fibroatheroma, wherein the necrotic core becomes bigger and fibrous cap thins out. Throughout the process, there is a progressive increase in the number of inflammatory cells. Finally, smooth muscle cells from the tunica media start to proliferative and migrate into the tunica intima."
