      "explanation": "Riboflavin deficiency causes angular cheilitis\n\nRiboflavin\n\nis correct. The patient's presentation of painful, cracked lesions at the corners of his mouth (angular cheilitis) and limited intake of dairy products and green leafy vegetables, which are rich in riboflavin, suggest a deficiency in this vitamin. Riboflavin (vitamin B2) is needed for many cellular processes and its deficiency can cause angular cheilitis. Other symptoms can include a sore throat, dry and scaly skin, and anaemia. However, these symptoms are not present in this patient.\n\nAscorbic acid\n\nis incorrect. Ascorbic acid deficiency primarily causes symptoms related to impaired collagen synthesis such as scurvy, which includes gingival changes, hyperkeratosis, and corkscrew hairs. The absence of these features in this case makes ascorbic acid deficiency a less likely diagnosis.\n\nCyanocobalamin\n\nis incorrect. Although angular cheilitis is associated with vitamin cyanocobalamin deficiency, the normal full blood count (FBC) and mean corpuscular volume (MCV) make this an unlikely cause. Cyanocobalamin deficiency presents with macrocytosis and in severe cases anaemia, thrombocytopenia, and leukopenia.\n\nFolate\n\nis incorrect. Although angular cheilitis is associated with folate deficiency, the normal FBC and MCV make this a less likely cause.\n\nZinc\n\nis incorrect. The clinical manifestations in severe cases of zinc deficiency include bullous-pustular dermatitis, alopecia, diarrhoea, weight loss, and hypogonadism in males. The absence of these clinical features makes zinc deficiency a less likely diagnosis."
