      "correctText": "CD15",
      "explanation": "CD15 is found on Reed-Sternberg cells\n\nCD15\n\nis the correct answer. Reed-Sternberg cells are characteristic of classical Hodgkin lymphoma and typically express both CD30 and CD15 on immunohistochemistry. The scenario describes a young adult with B symptoms (night sweats, weight loss) and painless lymphadenopathy, classic features of Hodgkin lymphoma. The identification of large binucleate cells (Reed-Sternberg cells) further supports this diagnosis. Understanding the immunophenotype of these cells—specifically co-expression of CD15 and CD30—is important for diagnosis according to UK haematology guidelines.\n\nCD20\n\nis a pan-B cell marker expressed in most mature B-cell neoplasms such as diffuse large B-cell lymphoma or follicular lymphoma, but it is usually absent or only weakly expressed in classical Reed-Sternberg cells. Although some non-classical variants may show CD20 positivity, the typical immunoprofile described here fits better with CD15.\n\nCD56\n\nis a neural cell adhesion molecule found predominantly on natural killer (NK) cells and some T-cell neoplasms, not in Reed-Sternberg cells or Hodgkin lymphoma.\n\nCD5\n\nis found on T-cells and also on mantle cell lymphomas but not typically seen in Reed-Sternberg cells or classical Hodgkin lymphoma.\n\nCD14\n\nserves as a marker for monocytes/macrophages but has no association with Reed-Sternberg cells or their diagnostic profile."
