      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Congenital adrenal hyperplasia",
      "explanation": "Autosomal recessive conditions are 'metabolic'  - exceptions: inherited ataxias\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nNoonan syndrome\n\n, which is a genetic condition inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. This means that only one copy of the altered gene is needed to cause the condition, and an affected parent has a 50% chance of passing the condition to their offspring. Noonan syndrome is caused by mutations in several genes, most commonly PTPN11, and is characterised by distinctive facial features, short stature, congenital heart defects (particularly pulmonary stenosis), and various other developmental issues.\n\nHomocystinuria\n\nis incorrect as it is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern. This metabolic disorder requires two copies of the defective gene (one from each parent) to manifest. It affects methionine metabolism due to cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency, leading to elevated homocysteine levels, lens dislocation, skeletal abnormalities, and thrombotic events.\n\nCystinuria\n\nis also inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern. This condition involves defective transport of dibasic amino acids in the proximal renal tubule and gastrointestinal tract, leading to recurrent kidney stones composed of cystine.\n\nCongenital adrenal hyperplasia\n\nis incorrect as it follows an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern. The most common form is 21-hydroxylase deficiency, which affects cortisol production and can lead to various presentations depending on the severity of enzyme deficiency.\n\nFanconi anaemia\n\nis also inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern. This rare genetic condition affects DNA repair mechanisms, leading to bone marrow failure, congenital abnormalities, and increased cancer risk, particularly acute myeloid leukaemia."
