      "explanation": "Tall, long fingered, downward lens dislocation, learning difficulties, DVT - homocystinuria\n\nThe correct diagnosis is\n\nhomocystinuria.\n\nThis is suggested by the patient's past medical history and examination findings on slit lamp examination which describe downward dislocation of the lens. Homocystinuria is an autosomal recessive condition caused by a deficiency of cystathionine beta-synthase. Classical features include a marfanoid body habitus, learning difficulties, seizures, downwards dislocation of the lens and increased risk of venous thromboembolism.\n\nAlkaptonuria\n\nis incorrect. Alkaptonuria is an autosomal recessive condition caused by a disorder in tyrosine and phenylalanine metabolism. It may cause pigmentation of the sclera and corneal deposits but is not associated with downward dislocation of the lens. Other classical features include urine which darkens on standing, renal stones and intervertebral disc calcification.\n\nCystinuria\n\nis incorrect.  It is an autosomal recessive disorder characterised by the formation of recurrent renal stones. It is not directly associated with any ophthalmic problems.\n\nMarfan's syndrome\n\nis incorrect. Marfan's syndrome is an autosomal dominant connective tissue disorder.  Whilst it is associated with lens dislocation, this is typically upwards. Other ophthalmic problems include blue sclera and myopia.\n\nPhenylketonuria\n\nis incorrect. Phenylketonuria is an autosomal recessive condition, caused by a disorder of phenylalanine metabolism. It is diagnosed via the Guthrie test and has onset in childhood. Features include developmental delay, seizures, learning difficulties and eczema. It does not typically present with ophthalmic problems."
