      "correctText": "180 / (180 + 20)",
      "explanation": "Specificity = TN / (TN + FP)\n\n200 / (200 + 20)\n\nis correct. This corresponds to a formula of true negatives / (true negatives + false positives). This is the equation that defines the specificity of a test. If a test is perfectly specific (i.e. 100%), there will be no false positives.\n\n180 / (180 + 20)\n\nis incorrect. This corresponds to a formula of true positives / true positives + false positives. This is the equation that governs the positive predictive value. This is the chance that the patient has the condition if the diagnostic test is positive\n\n180 / (180 + 50)\n\nis incorrect. This corresponds to a formula of true positives / true positives + false negatives. This is the sensitivity of the test i.e. the proportion of patients with the disease that are correctly identified as having the disease.\n\n180/(180+20) / 1 - (200/(200+20))\n\nis incorrect. This is the formula for the likelihood ratio for a positive result i.e. (sensitivity / 1 - specificity). This is how much the odds of the disease increase when a test is positive.\n\n200 / (200 + 50)\n\nis incorrect. This corresponds to true negatives / (true negatives + false negatives). This is the formula for the negative predictive value. This is the chance that the patient does not have the condition if the diagnostic test is negative."
