      "question": "An 18-year-old woman presents to the endocrine clinic after being referred by her GP due to primary amenorrhoea. Her history indicates that she was born at term without complications and had normal prenatal testing. On physical examination, she is noted to be short in stature with low-set ears and a webbed neck. No cardiac murmurs are detected on auscultation. Laboratory results are as follows:\n\n\nFree T3\t4.2\t(3.5-7.8 pmol/L)\nFree T4\t14.7\t(9.0-25.0 pmol/L)\nTSH\t2.1\t(0.4-4.0 mU/L)\n\nWhat is the most serious long-term complication associated with her underlying condition?",
