      "correctText": "Ventricular septal defect",
      "explanation": "An increased risk of aortic dilatation and dissection are the most serious long-term health problems for women with Turner's syndrome\n\nA female patient presenting with primary amenorrhoea, short stature, low-set ears, and a webbed neck suggests a diagnosis of Turner syndrome and would warrant referral for karyotyping to confirm.\n\nAortic dilatation\n\nis the most serious long-term complication of Turner syndrome, with an increased risk of progression to aortic dissection or aneurysm. Consequently, adults with this condition undergo regular surveillance to allow early detection and treatment. Additional cardiac complications commonly associated with Turner syndrome include bicuspid aortic valve and coarctation of the aorta.\n\nAtrial septal defects\n\n(ASD) and other septal defects are not typically seen in Turner syndrome. ASD is more frequently encountered in Down syndrome, with as many as half being affected by a septal defect as well as being at risk of other valvular and structural abnormalities. Turner syndrome is more often associated with aortic dilatation and subsequent dissection, other more frequent abnormalities include a bicuspid aortic valve and other great vessel abnormalities such as coarctation of the aorta.\n\nMitral valve prolapse\n\n, while not strongly connected to Turner syndrome, can present with a range of symptoms, from light-headedness and palpitations to syncope or even sudden death. It should be considered in patients displaying marfanoid habitus who present with syncope or have a family history of premature sudden cardiac death, as it may be inherited, particularly in Marfan syndrome. Aortic dilatation and risk of dissection are the most serious risks associated with Turner syndrome.\n\nPulmonary valve stenosis\n\nhas no significant association with Turner syndrome but should be considered in individuals exhibiting features such as short stature, ptosis, and hypertelorism suggestive of Noonan syndrome, along with symptoms like fatigue and breathlessness. Additionally, the absence of a murmur does not rule out valvular disease but its presence would increase suspicion; however, it seems unlikely in this case due to the lack of reported symptoms.\n\nA\n\nventricular septal defect\n\nis unlikely here as she is asymptomatic and this commonly presents in infancy with failure to thrive if the defect is small or symptoms of heart failure if larger. A small VSD can persist to adulthood, leading to the development of Eisenmenger syndrome due to the reversal of the left-right shunt. VSDs are also not typically associated with Turner syndrome but can be seen more frequently in Down syndrome and DiGeorge syndrome. The most serious complication of Turner syndrome is the increased risk of aortic dilatation and dissection."
