      "explanation": "Interferon-gamma is produced primarily by natural killer cells and T helper cells\n\nInterferon-alpha is produced by leucocytes along with dendritic cells and has strong antiviral action. Along with interferon-beta is activates natural killer cells to form an antiviral and anti-tumour response. It is also important in the inflammatory response mechanism causing pyrexia via prostaglandin release.\n\nInterferon-beta is produced primarily by fibroblasts. It has strong antiviral action. As with interferon-alpha it is important in the formation of antiviral and anti-tumour responses.\n\nInterferon-gamma is the correct answer. It is of key importance in macrophage activation which, in turn, lead to the formation of granulomas. It is released by natural kill cells and T helper cells (in particular Th1 cells). Interferon-gamma has also been shown to prevent tuberculosis inhibiting intracellular phagolysosomal maturation which, in turn, allows for destruction of infected cells. Interferon-gamma's strong involvement with tuberculosis is the reason for it's use in testing and diagnosis of it through quantiFERON.\n\nInterleukin-12 is important in tuberculosis infection but through activation of T helper cell differentiation and natural killer cell activation. It also aids in interferon-gamma release to further macrophage activation. Interleukin-12 itself does not lead to granuloma formation and therefore this answer is not correct.\n\nTumour necrosis factor alpha is important in causing signs of inflammation such as swelling, erythema and pyrexia. It is produced by activated macrophages along with many other cell types. It is not directly involved in granuloma formation."
