      "correctText": "Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease",
      "explanation": "Tall, long fingered, downward lens dislocation, learning difficulties, DVT - homocystinuria\n\nHomocystinuria is a rare autosomal recessive disease caused by a deficiency of cystathionine beta-synthase. This results in severe elevations in plasma and urine homocysteine concentrations. This patient presents with many of the classical features of homocystinuria including fine, fair hair, arachnodactyly, kyphosis, learning difficulty and seizures. He has also had downwards dislocation of his lens and recurrent deep vein thrombosis. Treatment is with vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) supplements.\n\nCharcot-Marie-Tooth disease, also known as hereditary motor and sensory neuropathy is an inherited condition which affects the peripheral nerves. Typical symptoms include muscle weakness, abnormal gait, high arched or flat feet and numbness. Features present in this vignette such as lens dislocation, cardiac abnormalities and recurrent thrombosis are not typical of this condition.\n\nEhlers-Danlos syndrome is a rare inherited connected tissue disorder. Typical features include joint hypermobility and stretchy and fragile skin. Features present in this vignette such as lens dislocation and recurrent thrombosis are not typical of this condition.\n\nFragile-X syndrome is an inherited neurodevelopmental condition caused by a mutation to the fragile X mental retardation 1 gene. Affected individuals typically have mild to moderate learning difficulties and typical physical features include a narrow face, large ears, flexible fingers, and large testicles.  Features present in this vignette such as lens dislocation and recurrent thrombosis are not typical of this condition.\n\nMarfan's syndrome and homocystinuria can be difficult to differentiate clinically. However, Marfan's syndrome is typically associated with upwards lens dislocation, unlike the downwards dislocation of the lens in this vignette. The heart is rarely affected in homocystinuria, whereas aortic regurgitation is common in Marfan's. The recurrent thrombosis noted in this case is also more typical of homocystinuria. Marfan's syndrome is inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion where homocystinuria is inherited in an autosomal recessive fashion."
