      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Apolipoprotein SP-B",
      "explanation": "Pulmonary surfactant - main constituent is\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nDipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine\n\n(DPPC), which comprises approximately 40-50% of pulmonary surfactant by weight. DPPC is a phospholipid that forms a surface-active monolayer at the air-liquid interface of the alveoli, reducing surface tension and preventing alveolar collapse during expiration. Its unique molecular structure, with two saturated palmitic acid chains, allows for optimal surface tension-lowering properties at body temperature.\n\nApolipoprotein SP-B\n\nis incorrect. While SP-B is an essential surfactant protein that facilitates the spreading and stability of the surfactant film, it only comprises about 2% of surfactant by weight. SP-B is crucial for surfactant function, but it is not the main constituent.\n\nPhosphatidylglycerol\n\nis incorrect. Although phosphatidylglycerol is an important phospholipid component of pulmonary surfactant, it only makes up approximately 7-10% of surfactant by weight. It plays a role in surfactant spreading and interaction with surfactant proteins.\n\nPulmonary elastase\n\nis incorrect. This is an enzyme produced by neutrophils and is not a component of pulmonary surfactant at all. Instead, it is involved in the breakdown of elastin and can contribute to emphysema when present in excess.\n\nApolipoprotein SP-A\n\nis incorrect. While SP-A is the most abundant surfactant protein and has important functions in host defence and surfactant homeostasis, it only comprises about 5-6% of surfactant by weight. It is not the main constituent of pulmonary surfactant."
