      "explanation": "DiGeorge syndrome - a T-cell disorder\n\nThe diagnosis here is that of DiGeorge syndrome, and T-cells are deficient. It has a wide variety of presentations, but its features can be remembered with the mnemonic 'CATCH22' - cardiac abnormalities, abnormal facies, thymic aplasia, cleft palate, hypocalcaemia/hypoparathyroidism and chromosome 22 deletion.\n\nA deficiency of both B-cells and T-cells would be seen with severe combined immunodeficiency. This would present differently, usually with a failure to thrive and recurrent infections, rather than the features found in the mnemonic for DiGeorge syndrome.\n\nA deficiency in B-cells, rather than T-cells, may lead to common variable immunodeficiency. presenting with recurrent infections and low immunoglobulin levels - not the features seen here.\n\nMonocytopaenia may happen for a number of reasons, including in acute infection, stress and some rare genetic conditions. It is not, however, associated with the distinct features found in DiGeorge syndrome.\n\nSimilarly, neutropaenia may be discovered for a number of reasons, associated with certain drugs, nutrient deficiencies and conditions, but not with the features seen in DiGeorge syndrome."
