      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Chromosome 9",
      "explanation": "HLA antigens are encoded for by genes on chromosome 6\n\nChromosome 6 is the correct answer. The patient has acute cellular rejection as evidenced by graft dysfunction and histopathological findings of interstitial inflammation and tubulitis. The rejection episode is likely caused by HLA mismatch. HLA antigens are encoded for by genes on chromosome 6.  The human leukocyte antigen (HLA) system is a group of proteins that are encoded by the major histocompatibility complex (MHC) gene complex. These cell-surface proteins are responsible for the regulation of the immune system. HLA A, B and C are class I antigens whilst DP, DQ, DR are class II antigens. MHC class 1 present peptides from inside to outside the cell while MHC class 2 present antigens from outside the cell to T lymphocytes, which in turn stimulate T helper cells and B cells. HLA matching is preferable for organ transplantation due to subsequent better graft function, fewer episodes of rejection and longer graft survival. However, finding a well-matched donor may not be possible for all patients and there is a balance struck between waiting for the perfect donor and the risks of a less well-matched organ. This patient has spent a considerable amount of time on the transplant waiting list and the eventual transplanted kidney may have had a higher frequency of mismatches.\n\nChromosome 5 is incorrect. Diseases associated with genes on this chromosome include homocystinuria and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.\n\nChromosome 7 is incorrect. Diseases associated with genes located on this chromosome include Romano-Ward syndrome and Maple syrup urine disease.\n\nChromosome 8 is incorrect. Diseases associated with genes located on this chromosome include Fahr's syndrome and Burkitt's lymphoma.\n\nChromosome 9 is incorrect. Diseases associated with genes located on this chromosome include Tangier's disease and Friedrich's ataxia."
