      "question": "You are working on a research project which is investigating a new diagnostic test for psoriatic arthritis. Having collected the data, you analyse it to work out screening test statistics such as the positive predictive value.\n\nA total of 100 patients are involved in the trial. 50 people test positive; of these, 40 are shown to have the disease whereas 10 patients do not have the disease.\n\nThe total number of patients that have a negative test is 50. Of those 50 patients, a total of 45 don't have psoriatic arthritis whereas 5 patients are found to have the disease present.\n\nWhat is the calculated positive predictive value?",
      "options": [
        "80%",
        "82%",
        "89%",
        "90%",
        "400%"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "80%",
      "explanation": "Positive predictive value - the chance that the patient has the condition if the diagnostic test is positive\n\nThe positive predictive value (PPV) is calculated by taking the number of true positives (those that test positive and have the disease) which is 40 and dividing this number by the total of the true positive and the false positives (the number who test positive but don't have the disease). This gives the calculation of 40/(40+10) which is 0.8. Therefore the PPV is 80%.\n\n89% is the sensitivity of the test.\n\n82% would be the calculated specificity of the test.\n\nThe negative predictive value would be 90%.\n\n400% is the value given if you divide 40/10 and convert it to a percentage. It is meaningless in this context."
