      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "None of his children will inherit the disease",
      "explanation": "For a man with mitochondrial disease, none of his children will inherit the condition\n\nThis is a case of patterns of inheritance of mitochondrial diseases.\n\nMyoclonic epilepsy with ragged red fibres (MERRF syndrome) is a mitochondrial disease which predominantly affects the nervous system and muscles. The predominant phenotypic features include myopathy and seizures (mainly generalised myoclonic seizures, but case reports have described many types of both focal and generalised seizures).\n\nAs detailed below, mitochondrial diseases are inherited from maternal mitochondrial DNA. This means that all children of an affected mother will inherit the disease. Given mitochondrial diseases can't be passed on via paternal sperm, Martin will not pass this onto his children and this should be the advice given.\n\nNow to look at the other options.\n\nAll his children being affected - this would be applicable if a female with a mitochondrial disease were to have children.  All of the mitochondrial DNA would then come from the mother.\n\nHis children would be carriers of the disease, but be asymptomatic - this scenario can occur in autosomal recessive conditions.\n\nHis daughter would inherit the disease - this could occur in several scenarios. Further reading on autosomal dominant and autosomal recessive inheritance would help further knowledge on this topic. Father to daughter inheritance would not fit the MERRF diagnosis.\n\nHis son would inherit the disease - in cases of males inheriting a disorder from their mother, one could think of X-linked inheritance. Usually, only males are affected and, although affected males may occur in consecutive generations, transmission is always through females. Females, on the other hand, would tend to be asymptomatic carriers as they have a second X chromosome which will often compensate for the problem in the other X chromosome."
