      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Fisher's exact test",
      "explanation": "Wilcoxon signed-rank test - compares two sets of observations on a single sample, e.g. a 'before' and 'after' test on the same population following an intervention\n\nThe Wilcoxon signed-rank test is used to assess a change in a particular measure in a single sample before and after an intervention. It can be used as an alternative to the student's t-test.\n\nFisher's exact test is a type of test used to assess whether there is a statistically significant difference between two frequencies. It is used in preference to the Chi-squared test when the sample size of the data is very small. The conclusion from the test leads to an acceptance or rejection of a null hypothesis.\n\nPearson correlation coefficient is used to assess whether there is a correlation between two sets of continuous data. It is expressed as a value between -1 and 1, where a result below zero indicates a negative correlation and above zero indicates a positive correlation.\n\nRegression analysis is a type of statistical modelling used for understanding whether there is a relationship between a dependant variable and one or more independent variables. The most common form of regression analysis is linear regression.\n\nThe weighted correlation coefficient is a variant of the Pearson where certain observations are adjusted for varying degrees of importance."
