      "correctText": "Coarctation of the aorta",
      "explanation": "Turner's syndrome - most common cardiac defect is bicuspid aortic valve (more common than coarctation of the aorta)\n\nBicuspid aortic valve\n\nis the correct answer. The patient's clinical features (primary amenorrhoea, short stature, webbed neck, and widely spaced nipples) are consistent with Turner syndrome, which is a chromosomal disorder characterised by complete or partial absence of one X chromosome (45,X). Bicuspid aortic valve is the most common cardiac abnormality in Turner syndrome, affecting approximately 15-30% of patients. This congenital anomaly involves the aortic valve having two leaflets instead of the normal three, which can cause a systolic murmur as heard in this patient. Regular cardiac monitoring is essential in Turner syndrome patients due to the increased risk of aortic dilatation and dissection associated with bicuspid aortic valve.\n\nCoarctation of the aorta\n\nis also associated with Turner syndrome but is less common than bicuspid aortic valve, occurring in approximately 5-10% of patients. Coarctation typically presents with upper limb hypertension, weak femoral pulses, and a systolic murmur best heard in the back. While this remains a possibility in this patient, bicuspid aortic valve is statistically more likely based on the information provided.\n\nAtrial septal defect\n\nis not particularly associated with Turner syndrome. It typically presents with a fixed splitting of the second heart sound and a pulmonary flow murmur. The clinical features described in this case are more consistent with the cardiac abnormalities commonly seen in Turner syndrome.\n\nMitral valve prolapse\n\nis not specifically associated with Turner syndrome. It typically presents with a mid-systolic click followed by a late systolic murmur. While mitral valve prolapse can occur in the general population, it is not a characteristic cardiac finding in Turner syndrome.\n\nVentricular septal defect\n\nis not particularly associated with Turner syndrome. It typically presents with a pansystolic murmur best heard at the left sternal edge. The clinical features described in this case point more towards the cardiac abnormalities commonly associated with Turner syndrome, particularly bicuspid aortic valve."
