      "explanation": "Type I hypersensitivity reaction - anaphylaxis\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nlatex allergy\n\n. This patient's presentation is highly suggestive of a type I hypersensitivity reaction to latex exposure from handling balloons. The combination of urticaria, angioedema, and bronchospasm (wheezing) represents a classic allergic response mediated by IgE antibodies. Latex allergy is particularly common in healthcare workers and individuals with frequent exposure to latex products, and latex balloons are a well-known trigger.\n\nC1-esterase deficiency\n\n(hereditary angioedema) typically presents with recurrent episodes of non-pruritic angioedema without urticaria. Unlike this case, it is not triggered by latex exposure and is not associated with wheezing. It is an inherited condition caused by deficiency or dysfunction of C1 inhibitor protein.\n\nAllergic contact dermatitis\n\nis a type IV delayed hypersensitivity reaction that typically develops 24-48 hours after exposure to an allergen. It presents with erythema, vesicles, and pruritus but does not cause immediate angioedema or respiratory symptoms. Common triggers include nickel, fragrances, and topical medications.\n\nPeanut allergy\n\n, while capable of causing similar symptoms through a type I hypersensitivity reaction, is not relevant in this scenario as there is no history of peanut exposure. The temporal relationship with balloon inflation strongly points towards latex as the allergen.\n\nIrritant contact dermatitis\n\nis a non-immunological reaction caused by direct damage to the skin from exposure to irritants. It does not cause systemic symptoms such as angioedema or wheezing, and symptoms are typically confined to the area of contact. It presents with burning, stinging, and erythema rather than urticaria."
