      "explanation": "Reticul\n\nPremature release of immature red cells with residual RNA\n\nis the correct answer. The mesh-like network seen represents residual ribosomal RNA present in reticulocytes. In a patient with anaemia, raised LDH and indirect hyperbilirubinaemia, there is likely haemolysis. An elevated reticulocyte count reflects a compensatory bone marrow response to this.\n\nAbnormal haemoglobin aggregation in mature red cells\n\nis incorrect. Haemoglobin aggregation (e.g., in sickle cell disease) causes changes in red cell shape and deformability but does not produce the mesh-like RNA network visible on supravital staining. Mature erythrocytes lack RNA and would not show this pattern.\n\nArtifact from staining procedure\n\nis incorrect. While staining artefacts can occur, the consistent presence of mesh-like reticular structures in the context of hemolysis and anaemia is a true reflection of reticulocytes containing residual RNA, not an artefact.\n\nFragmented red cells from mechanical hemolysis\n\nis incorrect. Schistocytes or fragmented red cells result from mechanical destruction and appear as irregular, jagged cells on a smear, but do not contain residual RNA. They lack the characteristic reticular staining seen in reticulocytes.\n\nMembrane defects causing spherocytes\n\nis incorrect. Spherocytes are spherical red cells due to membrane loss, as seen in hereditary spherocytosis or autoimmune hemolytic anaemia. They do not contain residual RNA or show the mesh-like network on supravital stain."
