      "id": 154,
      "question": "A 55-year-old male with a history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension is participating in a randomized controlled trial (RCT) comparing two antihypertensive drugs. After three months, he decides to withdraw from the study due to personal reasons. The trial's primary outcome is the reduction in systolic blood pressure at six months. When analysing the results, how should this patient be included?",
      "options": [
        "Include him in the analysis",
        "Exclude him from the analysis since he withdrew early",
        "Include him only if he completed at least half of the study duration",
        "Analyse his data separately as part of a per-protocol analysis",
        "Use his baseline data for endpoint estimation"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Use his baseline data for endpoint estimation",
      "explanation": "Intention to treat analysis - include patients who dropped out of trial\n\nInclude him in the analysis\n\n: In an intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis, all participants originally allocated to each treatment arm are included in the final analysis, regardless of whether they completed or adhered to the protocol. This approach maintains randomization benefits and provides an unbiased estimate of treatment effect by reflecting real-world scenarios where not all patients adhere to treatment.\n\nExclude him from the analysis since he withdrew early\n\n: Excluding participants who withdraw can introduce bias and compromise randomization integrity, potentially skewing results towards those more compliant with treatment or those who had less severe disease. ITT avoids these biases by including all initially randomized patients.\n\nInclude him only if he completed at least half of the study duration\n\n: This method would also introduce bias, as it selectively includes participants based on adherence or other factors that may correlate with outcomes, thus distorting true treatment effects.\n\nAnalyse his data separately as part of a per-protocol analysis\n\n: A per-protocol analysis includes only those participants who completed the study according to its protocol. While it can provide insights into efficacy under ideal conditions, it lacks generalizability and does not reflect typical clinical practice where non-adherence occurs.\n\nUse his baseline data for endpoint estimation\n\n: Using baseline data alone does not account for changes over time or interventions' potential effects; hence it fails to capture true outcomes influenced by treatments within trials."
