      "id": 157,
      "question": "A new diagnostic blood test for ovarian cancer is being trialled in a group of 1,100 women between 40-60 years of age referred with red flag symptoms.\n\nThe blood test identifies 100 positive cases and 1,000 negative cases. Patients were further investigated using CT scan and biopsy.\n\nOf 100 positive cases on the blood test, 95 patients were found to have ovarian cancer on either CT or biopsy, whilst 5 were cancer-free.\n\nOf the 1,000 negative cases on blood test, 2 patients were found to have cancer on either CT or biopsy whilst 998 were cancer-free.\n\nWhat is the negative predictive value of this diagnostic blood test?",
      "options": [
        "0.002",
        "0.95",
        "0.979",
        "0.995",
        "0.998"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "0.998",
      "explanation": "Negative predictive value = TN / (TN + FN)\n\nThe negative predictive value is the chance that the patient does not have the condition if the diagnostic test is negative. It is calculated by dividing the number of true negative cases by the total number of negative cases detected by the test (true and false negatives added together).  The formula simplified is: negative predictive value = TN / (TN + FN). In this case 998 true negative cases divided by total of 1000 negative cases give the answer,\n\n0.998\n\n.\n\nThe answer\n\n0.002\n\ndoes not reflect any statistical measure and would be calculated by dividing the number of false-negative cases by total negative cases. See the paragraph above for correct calculation for negative predictive value.\n\nThe\n\npositive predictive value\n\nis the chance that the patient has the condition if the diagnostic test is positive The formula simplified is: positive predictive value = TP / (TP + FP). In this case 95 true positive cases divided by total of 100 positive cases give the answer,\n\n0.95\n\n.\n\nThe\n\nsensitivity\n\nis the proportion of patients with the condition who have a positive test result. The formula simplified is: sensitivity = TP / (TP + FN ). In this case 95 true positive cases divided by (95 true positive cases + 2 false-negative cases) give the answer\n\n0.979\n\n.\n\nThe\n\nspecificity\n\nis the proportion of patients without the condition who have a negative test result. The formula simplified is specificity= TN / (TN + FP). In this case 998 true negative cases divided by (998 true negative cases + 5 false-positive cases) give the answer\n\n0.995\n\n."
