      "explanation": "Nonsense - a mutation that results in a stop codon\n\nNonsense mutation\n\nis the correct answer. Nonsense mutations are a type of substitution mutation in which a single base change creates a premature stop codon (UAA, UAG, or UGA). Proteins expressed are truncated and often nonfunctional, leading to disease states.\n\nBase deletion\n\nis incorrect. Although base deletions may lead to a frameshift and produce truncated proteins, the stem specifies that the pathological gene variant has a substitution mutation. Base deletions cannot be substitution mutations.\n\nIn-frame base insertion\n\nis incorrect. The mutation in question is a substitution, rather than an insertion/deletion. Base insertions may be considered 'in frame' if downstream triplet code is not disrupted (e.g. three bases are inserted together).\n\nFrameshift mutation\n\nis incorrect. Frameshift mutations, arising from base insertion or deletion, disrupt subsequent triplet code and lead to the translation of nonfunctioning proteins. Substitution mutations, as in this stem, do not cause frameshift.\n\nSilent mutation\n\nis incorrect. Silent mutations are single-base substitutions that do not lead to a change in the amino acid sequence due to the degenerate nature of the triplet code. These mutations are not associated with disease states."
