      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Noonan syndrome",
      "explanation": "Autosomal recessive conditions are 'metabolic' - exceptions: inherited ataxias\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nCongenital adrenal hyperplasia\n\n. This condition is inherited in an autosomal recessive fashion, meaning that both parents must carry a copy of the mutated gene and each child has a 25% chance of inheriting the condition. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia refers to a group of genetic conditions that affect the adrenal glands' ability to produce certain hormones.\n\nNow, let's discuss why the other options are incorrect:\n\nFamilial adenomatous polyposis\n\nis not inherited in an autosomal recessive manner but rather in an autosomal dominant pattern. This means only one copy of the altered gene in each cell is sufficient to cause the disorder. Familial adenomatous polyposis is characterized by the development of hundreds or thousands of polyps in the rectum and colon during adolescence or adulthood.\n\nSimilarly,\n\nNoonan syndrome\n\nfollows an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern. Individuals with this syndrome typically have distinctive facial features, short stature, heart defects present at birth, bleeding problems and skeletal malformations.\n\nMalignant hyperthermia\n\nalso follows an autosomal dominant pattern of inheritance. It's a severe reaction to certain drugs used during general anaesthesia for surgery. If one parent carries a gene associated with malignant hyperthermia, each child has a 50% chance of inheriting that gene.\n\nFinally,\n\nAntithrombin III deficiency\n\nis inherited as an autosomal dominant trait as well. Individuals with this deficiency have a higher risk of developing abnormal blood clots."
