      "correctText": "Cancer antigen 15-3 (CA 15-3)",
      "explanation": "A family history of breast and ovarian cancer suggests a mutation in the BRCA genes\n\nThe BRCA1 variant is associated with both breast cancer (~60% develop breast cancer by 80 years) and ovarian cancer (~50% will develop ovarian cancer by 80 years).\n\nCA 125\n\nis sometimes used as an annual screening test for ovarian cancer for patients who are known to carry the BRCA gene. Regular transvaginal ultrasound may also be used. Although routine genetic screening is not recommended in the UK it is possible to have these tests privately. NICE recommend that screening should only be offered to at-risk individuals (based on a validated risk score) in a specialist genetic clinic.\n\nAFP\n\nis a tumour antigen used to detect or monitor hepatocellular carcinoma and teratoma. Neither of these cancers are associated with the BRCA gene.\n\nCA 15-3\n\nis a marker used for breast cancer therapy monitoring. Patients known to have an increased risk of breast cancer may have more regular breast screening (mammography) or start screening earlier or have an MRI rather than mammography. CA 15-3 is not generally used to diagnose breast cancer, but rather for monitoring response to therapy.\n\nCA 19-9\n\nis a tumour marker used in patients with pancreatic cancer. There is an increased risk of pancreatic cancer in patients with the BRCA gene but the increase is low.\n\nCEA\n\nis associated primarily with colorectal cancer. There is no established association between colorectal cancer and the BRCA gene."
