      "id": 170,
      "question": "Which one of the following is the best definition of the p value?",
      "options": [
        "The probability of obtaining a similar result, assuming that the null hypothesis is false",
        "The probability that a replicating experiment would not yield the same conclusion",
        "The probability of obtaining a result at least as extreme, assuming that the null hypothesis is true",
        "The probability that the null hypothesis is true",
        "The probability of obtaining a result at least as extreme, assuming that the null hypothesis is false"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "The probability that a replicating experiment would not yield the same conclusion",
      "explanation": "P value - is the probability of obtaining a result by chance at least as extreme as the one that was actually observed, assuming that the null hypothesis is true\n\nThe correct answer is that the p value is\n\nthe probability of obtaining a result at least as extreme, assuming that the null hypothesis is true\n\n. This is the precise statistical definition of the p value. It represents the probability of observing data as extreme as, or more extreme than, what was actually observed, under the assumption that the null hypothesis is correct. This is fundamental to statistical hypothesis testing and helps researchers determine whether their results are statistically significant.\n\nThe probability of obtaining a similar result, assuming that the null hypothesis is false\n\nis incorrect because it fundamentally misunderstands the premise of p values. P values are calculated under the assumption that the null hypothesis is true, not false. Additionally, p values consider results 'at least as extreme' rather than 'similar' results.\n\nThe probability that a replicating experiment would not yield the same conclusion\n\nis incorrect. This is actually describing the concept of reproducibility or reliability, which is different from a p value. P values do not directly indicate whether results are reproducible.\n\nThe probability that the null hypothesis is true\n\nis a common misconception. The p value does not tell us the probability that the null hypothesis is true. Rather, it tells us how likely our observed results would be if the null hypothesis were true. This is a crucial distinction in statistical inference.\n\nThe probability of obtaining a result at least as extreme, assuming that the null hypothesis is false\n\nis incorrect because it states the wrong assumption. P values are always calculated under the assumption that the null hypothesis is true, not false. This is a fundamental aspect of null hypothesis significance testing."
