      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Amplify RNA",
      "explanation": "Molecular biology techniques\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nNorthern blotting\n\nis used to detect RNA. Northern blotting is a laboratory technique specifically designed to detect and analyze RNA molecules in a sample. The technique involves separating RNA fragments by size using gel electrophoresis, transferring them to a membrane, and then using labeled probes to detect specific RNA sequences. This makes it particularly useful for studying gene expression levels and identifying specific RNA transcripts.\n\nDetect and quantify proteins\n\nis incorrect as this is actually the function of Western blotting. Western blotting uses antibodies to detect specific proteins after they have been separated by gel electrophoresis and transferred to a membrane.\n\nAmplify DNA\n\nis incorrect as this is the function of Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR). PCR is a technique that creates multiple copies of a specific DNA sequence through repeated cycles of heating and cooling.\n\nDetect DNA\n\nis incorrect as this is the function of Southern blotting. Southern blotting is similar to Northern blotting but is specifically used to detect DNA fragments rather than RNA.\n\nAmplify RNA\n\nis incorrect as this would be achieved through techniques like reverse transcription PCR (RT-PCR) where RNA is first converted to complementary DNA (cDNA) before amplification. Northern blotting does not amplify RNA, it only detects existing RNA molecules."
