      "id": 179,
      "question": "A 32-year-old man presents with a focal-onset impaired awareness seizure. Prior to the seizure he experienced a headache and felt nauseated. He has had no fever, neck stiffness or photophobia and there is no history of head injury. He has a history of focal-onset seizures which are normally well-controlled on anti-epileptics.\n\nOn examination, there is spasticity and weakness of the right upper limb and bilateral clonus. He has expressive and receptive dysphasia and a right-sided visual field defect. His observations are all normal and he is apyrexial.\n\n\n\nHb\t160 g/L\tMale: (135-180)\nPlatelets\t300 * 109/L\t(150 - 400)\nWBC\t12.0 * 109/L\t(4.0 - 11.0)\nNa+\t140 mmol/L\t(135 - 145)\nK+\t4.1 mmol/L\t(3.5 - 5.0)\nUrea\t6.3 mmol/L\t(2.0 - 7.0)\nCreatinine\t100 µmol/L\t(55 - 120)\nCRP\t15 mg/L\t(< 5)\nLactate\t4.5mmol/L\t(< 2)\n\nA lumbar puncture is also performed.\n\n\n\tResult\tNormal range\nAppearance\tClear\t\nGlucose\t4.1 mmol/L\t(2.8 - 4.2)\nWhite cell count\tNormal\t\nProtein\tNormal\t\nCerebrospinal fluid lactate\t4.1 mmol/L\t(1.1 - 2.4)\nGram stain\tNo organisms seen\t\n\nAn urgent CT head demonstrates a hypodense area in the left hemisphere. MRI confirms an area of ischaemia involving the left temporal, occipital, and parietal lobes.\n\nDue to the unusual presentation, further investigations are planned to investigate for an inherited condition. His partner is pregnant with their daughter.\n\nWhat is the probability of his daughter being affected by the condition?",
