      "explanation": "For a man with mitochondrial disease, none of his children will inherit the condition\n\nThe correct answer is 0%, as he is unable to pass this condition on to his daughter.\n\nThis man has features suggestive of mitochondrial myopathy, encephalopathy, lactic acidosis, and stroke-like episodes (MELAS). This is supported by his history of seizures, clinical presentation, neuroimaging and elevated lactate.\n\nMELAS is a progressive disorder due to a mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) mutation. mtDNA mutations are maternally transmitted. MELAS is one of the most common mitochondrial disorders, which are inherited maternally.\n\nMELAS can manifest with different degrees of severity due to heteroplasmy - a mixture of normal and mutated mtDNA. The proportion of affected mtDNA influences the severity of symptoms. Patients usually have normal early development then develop features of MELAS before the age of 40.\n\nPatients may present with stroke-like neurological deficits, often associated with headaches and seizures. Diffusion-weighted MRI shows multi-focal, hyperintense cortical lesions. Patients have increased lactate levels in serum and cerebrospinal fluid. Muscle biopsy showing the presence of ragged red fibres is pathognomonic of a mitochondrial myopathy.\n\nAtypical stroke-like episodes with lesions that cross vascular territories, especially in young people, should raise the suspicion of MELAS.\n\nThe other answers would be correct for different patterns of inheritance.\n\n25% - e.g. two carriers of an autosomal recessive disorder.\n\n50% - e.g. autosomal dominant pattern in one parent.\n\n75% - e.g.  autosomal dominant pattern in both parents.\n\n100% - e.g. mitochondrial inheritance through the maternal line, although heteroplasmy has an effect on the observed haplotypes and subsequent phenotype."
