      "explanation": "Dermatitis herpetiformis is associated with HLA-DR3\n\nThis patient has presented with the cutaneous manifestation of coeliac disease dermatitis herpetiformis. Patients commonly report an intensely itchy, papulovesicular, blistering rash, similar to that seen in herpes infection (hence the name). Human leukocyte antigens (HLA) are genes that code for proteins that assist differentiate between self and non-self. Several conditions have been associated with specific HLA antigens including dermatitis herpetiformis which is associated with the\n\nHLA antigen DR3\n\n(HLA-DR3).\n\nAnti-cyclic citrullinated peptides\n\n(anti-CCP) are strongly associated with rheumatoid arthritis, being present in 70% of patients and linked to a poorer disease prognosis. This patient has no clear features of rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and anti-CCP is not associated with dermatitis herpetiformis or coeliac disease.\n\nHLA-B27\n\nis found in 90% of reactive arthritis patients as well as being associated with conditions including ankylosing spondylitis and acute anterior uveitis, however again it is not associated with coeliac or dermatitis herpetiformis.\n\nHLA-DR4\n\nis associated with type 1 diabetes mellitus and RA, and not with coeliac or dermatitis herpetiformis.\n\nRheumatoid factor\n\n(RF) is the autoantibody first found in RA. Although is associated with several connective tissue disorders other than RA (including systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), juvenile idiopathic arthritis and Sjogren syndrome) as well as non-connective tissue disorders (such as hepatitis, sarcoidosis, and tuberculosis) it is not linked to coeliac or dermatitis herpetiformis."
