      "explanation": "Wilcoxon signed-rank test - compares two sets of observations on a single sample, e.g. a 'before' and 'after' test on the same population following an intervention\n\nWilcoxon signed-rank test is the correct answer. The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is used to analyse non-parametric data to compare two paired groups. It can be used to assess for a change in a population group by analysing data from before and after a treatment has been administered to the group. It would be appropriate to use the Wilcoxon signed-rank test to assess the change in a group of patient's cravings from before and after the introduction of an NRT.\n\nChi-square test is an incorrect answer. The chi-square test is used for non-parametric data. It can compare proportions or percentages of unpaired groups. It would be an unsuitable test for this study as the groups are paired. The McNemar's test is an alternative to the chi-square test that can also analyse non-parametric data and it can be used to compare proportions or percentages of paired groups, however, it isn't an option in this question.\n\nMann-Whitney U-test is an incorrect answer. The Mann-Whitney U-test is used to compare ordinal non-parametric data from two independent groups. The two groups in this study are paired and the Mann-Whitney U-test is thus an inappropriate test. A study comparing a new treatment and a placebo in independent/unpaired groups would be appropriate for the Mann-Whitney U-test.\n\nThe Student's t-tests (paired and unpaired) are incorrect answers. The Student's t-tests require parametric data, which is not the case in this scenario. The results from this small study would certainly not follow a normal distribution."
