      "explanation": "Type II hypersensitivity reaction - ITP\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nType II reaction\n\n. Idiopathic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (ITP) is an example of a Type II hypersensitivity reaction, also known as cytotoxic hypersensitivity. In this type of reaction, the immune system produces IgG antibodies that bind to self-antigens on cell surfaces, in the case of ITP, these are platelet antigens. This leads to the destruction of the cells by phagocytosis or complement activation which results in thrombocytopenia and purpura.\n\nType I reaction\n\nrefers to immediate hypersensitivity reactions mediated by IgE antibodies. These reactions occur rapidly after exposure to an antigen and typically involve mast cells and basophils. Examples include allergic rhinitis, asthma and anaphylaxis. Thus, ITP does not fall under this category.\n\nType III reaction\n\ninvolves immune complex-mediated hypersensitivity. Here, soluble antigens combine with antibodies to form complexes that deposit in tissues leading to inflammation and damage. Conditions such as systemic lupus erythematosus and serum sickness are examples of type III reactions but not ITP.\n\nA\n\nType IV reaction\n\nis a delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction mediated by T cells rather than antibodies. This occurs several days after exposure to an antigen and includes conditions like contact dermatitis and tuberculin skin test reactivity.\n\nLastly,\n\nType V reaction\n\n, although not included in the original Gell-Coombs classification, often refers to stimulatory immune responses where autoantibodies bind receptors and mimic the action of the natural ligand leading to overstimulation. Graves' disease is a classic example where autoantibodies bind the thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor causing hyperthyroidism."
