      "explanation": "Fabry disease is caused by a deficiency of alpha-galactosidase A\n\nAlpha-galactosidase A\n\nis the correct answer. This young male patient's glove and stocking neuropathy, cataracts, angiokeratomas, and proteinuria are very suggestive of Fabry disease. The condition is x-linked recessive, thus early death from cardiovascular disease in a maternal grandfather would also support Fabry disease.\n\nFabry disease is a lysosomal storage disorder secondary to deficient or dysfunctional alpha-galactosidase A. The enzyme is responsible for the breakdown of glycosphingolipids.\n\nGlucocerebrosidase\n\nis incorrect. Glucocerebrosidase may be defective in patients with Gaucher's disease, a lysosomal storage disorder that presents in childhood. It is characterised by hepatosplenomegaly and bone pain.\n\nGlucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase\n\nis incorrect. Patients with glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase may develop haemolytic anaemia drugs/toxins/disease states that induce oxidative stress.\n\nHomogentisate oxidase\n\nis incorrect. This enzyme is defective in alkaptonuria, also known as black urine disease. The condition is characterised by arthritis, urine that turns brown/black when exposed to air, and soft tissue hyperpigmentation.\n\nPorphobilinogen deaminase\n\nis incorrect. This enzyme is defective in patients with acute intermittent porphyria. The condition is characterised by intermittent abdominal pain, peripheral neuropathies, psychiatric symptoms, and urine that turns dark red on standing."
