      "question": "A new drug is being developed for use in heart conduction disorders. As part of the experimental research, a laboratory scientist records the conduction velocities in different parts of a bovine heart using two electrodes. Following a series of velocity measurements, the scientist marks the part of the heart with two red hat pins where he has measured the highest velocity in the heart at around 4 m/s.\n\nWhat is the most likely location of the red hat pins?",
      "options": [
        "AV node",
        "Bachmann's bundle",
        "Bundle of His",
        "Myocardium",
        "Purkinje fibres"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Purkinje fibres",
      "explanation": "The fastest conduction velocities in the heart are in the Purkinje fibres\n\nPurkinje fibres\n\nis the correct answer. Purkinje fibres are of large diameter and achieve velocities of 2-4 m/s, the fastest conduction in the heart. This rapid conduction allows coordinated ventricular contraction.\n\nAV node\n\nhas the slowest conduction velocity in the heart (0.05 m/s). This conduction delay between the atria and the ventricles facilitates the completion of the ventricular filling from the atrial kick before the ventricular contraction begins.\n\nBachmann's bundle\n\nis a branch of the anterior inter-nodal tract that conducts electrical impulses from the SA node to the left atrium and thus facilitates coordinated atrial contraction.\n\nBundle of His\n\nis a group of fast-conducting muscle fibres that serves to transmit electrical impulses from the AV node to the Purkinje fibres at velocities up to 2 m/s. The right and left bundle branches conduct the impulses to the right and left ventricles, respectively.\n\nMyocardium\n\nconducts electrical impulses at a slower velocity (0.5 m/s). Premature ventricular complexes originating in the myocardium have widened QRS duration owing to the slow conduction of action potentials from myocyte to myocyte."
