      "id": 195,
      "question": "A case-control study is carried out to look at the effectiveness of Ibuprofen in improving confirmed gout pain compared to placebo. A marker of 50% or more improvement in a patients symptoms measured on a numbers scale is deemed significant. The table below shows the outcome of the study:\n\n\n\tTotal number of patients\tAchieved 50% improvement\nIbuprofen\t180\t90\nPlacebo\t200\t40\n\nWhat is the relative risk of ibuprofen achieving 50% improvement in symptoms for this study?",
      "options": [
        "0.2",
        "0.25",
        "0.5",
        "1",
        "2.5"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "0.2",
      "explanation": "Relative risk  = EER / CER\n\nThe\n\nrelative risk (RR)\n\nis the ratio of an event in the experiment group, known as the\n\nexperience event rate (EER)\n\n, to the event in the control group known as the\n\ncontrol event rate (CER)\n\n.\n\nRR = EER/CER\n\nFor this study, the EER is the number of patients who take ibuprofen and achieve significant symptom relief divided by the total number of patients who take ibuprofen.\n\nI.e. EER = 90 / 180 = 0.5\n\nThe CER on the other hand is the number of patients who take the placebo and achieve significant symptom relief divided by the total number of patients who take the placebo.\n\nI.e CER = 40/200 = 0.2\n\nSo the\n\nrelative risk (RR)\n\nis calculated via:\n\nRR = EER/CER\n\nRR = 0.5/0.2\n\nRR =\n\n2.5\n\nThe\n\nodds\n\nof something happening is a ratio of the number of patients who experience a specific outcome to the number of people who do not experience the outcome.   Therefore the odds of a patient achieving significant improvement when taking the placebo:\n\n40/200 - 40\n40 /160 = 0.25\n\nFinally, the\n\nodds\n\nof a patient achieving significant improvement when taking ibuprofen:\n\n90/180-90\n90/90 = 1"
