      "id": 196,
      "question": "A 36-year-old woman presents with progressive diplopia and limb fatigability over several months. Her symptoms are worse in the evening and improve with rest.\n\nNeurological examination reveals bilateral ptosis, weakness of extraocular muscles, and reduced strength in proximal limb muscles. Deep tendon reflexes are preserved, and there are no sensory deficits.\n\nShe joins a trial for a new drug that enhances skeletal muscle contraction by selectively targeting a receptor.\n\nWhat receptor is the most appropriate pharmacological target for this drug?",
      "options": [
        "L-type calcium channel receptor",
        "Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor",
        "Nicotinic acetylcholine receptor",
        "P/Q-type calcium channel receptor",
        "Voltage-gated potassium channel receptor"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "P/Q-type calcium channel receptor",
      "explanation": "Skeletal muscle contraction is dependent on acetylcholine which activates nicotinic acetylcholine receptors\n\nNicotinic acetylcholine receptor\n\nis the correct answer. The patient presents with diplopia, bilateral ptosis, and proximal limb fatigability that worsens in the evening — a classic constellation of symptoms seen in myasthenia gravis, a disorder caused by autoimmune disruption of neuromuscular transmission. The underlying pathology involves antibodies directed against nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the motor endplate of skeletal muscles, reducing the efficacy of acetylcholine binding and leading to progressive failure of muscle contraction during sustained activity. A drug that selectively targets and activates nicotinic acetylcholine receptors would directly compensate for this receptor loss or dysfunction, thereby enhancing synaptic transmission and improving the patient's symptoms, particularly ocular and limb muscle weakness. This approach would specifically address the fluctuating weakness by improving signal propagation at fatigued neuromuscular junctions.\n\nL-type calcium channel receptors\n\nare voltage-gated channels predominantly located in cardiac and smooth muscle, and are crucial in excitation-contraction coupling in those tissues. While they play a role in skeletal muscle, their activation is downstream of the initial nicotinic receptor-mediated depolarisation. In the clinical scenario of fatigable weakness and diplopia, the problem lies at the level of neuromuscular transmission, not calcium-mediated contraction. Targeting L-type channels would not address the underlying receptor defect and is thus not a rational therapeutic approach here.\n\nMuscarinic acetylcholine receptors\n\nare G-protein-coupled receptors found primarily in parasympathetic target organs, such as the heart, smooth muscle, and glands, not at the skeletal neuromuscular junction. Targeting these receptors would not enhance skeletal muscle contraction and may cause unwanted systemic parasympathetic effects (e.g., bradycardia, bronchoconstriction). In the scenario described, the muscle weakness arises from impaired signal transmission at the neuromuscular junction, where muscarinic receptors are not involved. Therefore, this receptor is not a suitable target for the intended therapeutic action.\n\nP/Q-type calcium channel receptor\n\nis located on presynaptic motor nerve terminals and is involved in the release of acetylcholine into the synaptic cleft. These channels are targeted in Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, a different condition causing muscle weakness, typically with autonomic symptoms and hyporeflexia. In the current scenario, reflexes are preserved, and the clinical pattern is more in keeping with postsynaptic receptor dysfunction rather than impaired neurotransmitter release. Therefore, this receptor is not the most appropriate target in this context.\n\nVoltage-gated potassium channels\n\nregulate membrane repolarisation and are involved in action potential termination in various excitable cells, including neurons. While antibodies against these channels may be implicated in some paraneoplastic or autoimmune encephalitides, they are not involved in the transmission of signals at the skeletal neuromuscular junction. In this case, the woman’s fatigable muscle weakness reflects a disorder of acetylcholine receptor function, not potassium channel dysfunction. Thus, targeting potassium channels would not improve skeletal muscle contraction in this scenario."
