      "explanation": "RNA splicing takes place within the nucleus\n\nThe\n\nnucleus\n\nis the primary site where RNA splicing occurs. RNA splicing is the action by which non-coding introns are removed from pre-mRNA and coding exons are joined together to form mRNA. These exons may also be joined together in a variety of different configurations in a process known as alternative splicing. Through this mechanism, a single gene can code for a variety of different protein.\n\nProteasomes\n\nare organelles responsible for the breakdown of large proteins in eukaryotic cells.\n\nRibosomes\n\ncarry out the translation of mRNA into peptide structures.\n\nThe\n\nrough endoplasmic reticulum\n\nis the site where proteins are folded into the appropriate shape.\n\nThe\n\nsmooth endoplasmic reticulum\n\nis responsible for steroid and lipid synthesis."
