      "id": 201,
      "question": "The commonest chromosomal defect in Down's syndrome is:",
      "options": [
        "Trinucleotide repeat disorder",
        "Autosomal dominant",
        "Translocation",
        "Mosaicism",
        "Nondisjunction"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Translocation",
      "explanation": "Nondisjunction is the commonest cause of Down's syndrome\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nNondisjunction\n\n. Nondisjunction is indeed the most common chromosomal defect in Down's syndrome, accounting for approximately 95% of all cases. Nondisjunction occurs during meiosis (typically maternal meiosis I) when homologous chromosomes fail to separate properly, resulting in an egg cell with 24 chromosomes instead of 23. When this egg is fertilised by a normal sperm with 23 chromosomes, the resulting zygote has 47 chromosomes, including three copies of chromosome 21 (trisomy 21). This is why Down's syndrome is also known as trisomy 21. The risk of nondisjunction increases with maternal age, which explains the higher incidence of Down's syndrome in children born to older mothers.\n\nTrinucleotide repeat disorder\n\nis incorrect. Trinucleotide repeat disorders are caused by an expansion of a sequence of three nucleotides within a gene. Examples include Huntington's disease, fragile X syndrome, and myotonic dystrophy. These disorders involve a dynamic mutation where the number of repeats can increase in successive generations, often leading to anticipation (earlier onset and increased severity in subsequent generations). Down's syndrome is not caused by a trinucleotide repeat expansion but by the presence of an extra chromosome 21.\n\nAutosomal dominant\n\nis incorrect. This refers to a pattern of inheritance rather than a chromosomal defect. In autosomal dominant conditions, a mutation in one copy of a gene is sufficient to cause the disorder. Examples include Huntington's disease and neurofibromatosis type 1. Down's syndrome is not inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern but results from a chromosomal abnormality (usually trisomy 21) that typically occurs sporadically rather than being inherited from a parent.\n\nTranslocation\n\nis incorrect as the most common cause, although it is responsible for approximately 4% of Down's syndrome cases. Translocation Down's syndrome occurs when part of chromosome 21 breaks off and attaches to another chromosome, typically chromosome 14. This is known as a Robertsonian translocation. Unlike nondisjunction, translocation Down's syndrome can be inherited from a parent who is a balanced carrier (has the correct amount of genetic material but arranged differently). This form of Down's syndrome is not related to maternal age.\n\nMosaicism\n\nis incorrect as the most common cause, accounting for only about 1% of Down's syndrome cases. Mosaic Down's syndrome occurs when nondisjunction happens after fertilisation, during early cell division of the embryo. This results in some cells having the normal 46 chromosomes and others having 47 chromosomes with an extra chromosome 21. Individuals with mosaic Down's syndrome often have fewer characteristics of the syndrome and may have less severe intellectual disability compared to those with complete trisomy 21, depending on the proportion of cells affected."
