      "explanation": "Sensitivity - proportion of patients with the condition who have a positive test result\n\nCTPA positive\tCTPA negative\nBlood test positive\t600\t600\nBlood test negative\t200\t2400\n\nThe correct answer is 0.75. Sensitivity is the proportion of patients with a condition who have a positive test result. In this scenario, 800 patients are identified as having PEs with the gold standard test. Of these, 600 have a positive blood test, and so 600/800 = 0.75.\n\n0.33 is not a meaningful figure for this scenario.\n\n0.5 refers to the positive predictive value - the chance that the patient has the condition if the new test is positive. In total, 1200 patients had positive blood tests, of which 600 actually had PE demonstrated on CTPA.\n\n0.8 refers to the specificity - the proportion of patients without the condition who have a negative test result. 3000 patients were found to not have PEs on the CTPA, of which 2400 had a negative blood result. 2400/3000 = 0.8.\n\n0.92 is the negative predictive value - the chance that the patient does not have the condition if the new test is negative. In total, 2600 had a negative blood test, of which 2400 did not have the condition. 2400/2600 is 0.92."
