      "id": 211,
      "question": "A research facility is trying to develop a new test to screen for breast cancer compared to current standards which include mammography. From preliminary data, the new screening test appears to be better at picking up early cancers. When comparing both these tests, however, there appears to be no obvious difference between survival rates.\n\nWhat is this an example of?",
      "options": [
        "Late-look bias",
        "Lead-time bias",
        "Publication bias",
        "Recall bias",
        "Work-up bias"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Late-look bias",
      "explanation": "Lead-time bias - occurs when two tests for a disease are compared, the new test diagnoses the disease earlier, but there is no effect on the outcome of the disease\n\nThis is an example of lead-time bias. When two tests are compared, one test may diagnose the disease earlier but doesn't alter the outcome.  This may result in survival times looking more promising for the new test.\n\nLate-look bias is a type of selection bias where gathering of information occurs at an inappropriate time. For example, studying a fatal disease many years after patients suffering from it have died.\n\nPublication bias occurs as a when negative or uninteresting results from valid studies are not published.\n\nRecall bias is particularly relevant for case-control studies. This is when there is a difference in the accuracy of the memories retrieved by participants.\n\nWork-up bias occurs in studies which compare new diagnostic tests with gold standard tests.  Some patients are given the 'gold standard' test, while others are not (mostly because many gold standard tests are expensive or invasive). This can alter values such as specificity and sensitivity."
