      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Myasthenia gravis",
      "explanation": "Kearns-Sayre syndrome\n\nKearns-Sayre syndrome\n\nis the most likely diagnosis in this case. This is a mitochondrial disorder characterised by the triad of onset before age 20, progressive external ophthalmoplegia, and pigmentary retinopathy. The patient presents with all three cardinal features: he is 17 years old, has ptosis and difficulty moving his eyes (external ophthalmoplegia), and has retinal pigmentary changes with night blindness (retinitis pigmentosa). Additionally, cardiac conduction abnormalities, such as the first-degree heart block seen in this patient, are common in Kearns-Sayre syndrome. The family history is also consistent with mitochondrial inheritance, as the condition was present in the maternal uncle. Mitochondrial disorders are maternally inherited because mitochondrial DNA is passed exclusively from mother to offspring. The combination of these clinical features and inheritance pattern strongly supports Kearns-Sayre syndrome as the diagnosis.\n\nChronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia (CPEO)\n\nis also a mitochondrial disorder characterised by slowly progressive weakness of the extraocular muscles and ptosis. However, CPEO typically does not include retinitis pigmentosa or cardiac conduction defects, which are present in this case. CPEO can be considered a milder form or a component of Kearns-Sayre syndrome, but the presence of retinitis pigmentosa and cardiac involvement makes Kearns-Sayre syndrome the more specific diagnosis.\n\nLeber's hereditary optic neuropathy\n\nis another mitochondrial disorder, but it primarily affects the optic nerve, causing acute or subacute central vision loss. It typically presents around age 30 and does not cause external ophthalmoplegia or retinitis pigmentosa. The pattern of visual loss in Leber's is characterised by central scotomas and loss of colour vision, rather than the night blindness described in this case.\n\nMyasthenia gravis\n\ncan present with ptosis and ophthalmoplegia, but these symptoms typically fluctuate throughout the day and worsen with fatigue, rather than being progressive. Myasthenia gravis would not explain the retinitis pigmentosa, cardiac conduction abnormalities, or the family history consistent with maternal inheritance. Additionally, myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune condition, not a mitochondrial disorder.\n\nOculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy\n\nis an autosomal dominant disorder that causes ptosis and dysphagia, but it typically presents after age 40, much later than in this case. It does not cause retinitis pigmentosa or cardiac conduction abnormalities. The inheritance pattern would also be different, with affected individuals in every generation, rather than the maternal inheritance pattern suggested in this case."
