      "explanation": "An increased risk of aortic dilatation and dissection are the most serious long-term health problems for women with Turner's syndrome\n\nAortic dilatation\n\nis correct because the patient has clinical features consistent with Turner’s syndrome, including short stature, primary amenorrhoea, and characteristic physical findings such as a high-arched palate, low hairline, and widely spaced nipples. Turner’s syndrome is associated with a range of cardiovascular anomalies, the most serious of which is progressive aortic dilatation, which can lead to dissection or rupture. This risk is increased even in the absence of hypertension or aortic valve abnormalities, and cardiovascular disease remains the leading cause of mortality in affected individuals. Lifelong cardiac monitoring is essential.\n\nAortic stenosis\n\nis incorrect because although it can occur in patients with Turner syndrome due to bicuspid aortic valve, it is not the most serious long-term risk. The more significant concern is progressive aortic root dilatation, which can lead to dissection or rupture—major causes of premature mortality in this population. Aortic stenosis typically presents later and progresses gradually, and while it may require monitoring, it is less immediately life-threatening than aortic dissection.\n\nCoronary artery disease\n\nis incorrect because although individuals with Turner’s syndrome may develop features of metabolic syndrome, including dyslipidaemia and hypertension, the risk of coronary events tends to occur later in life. With appropriate management, including aspirin and statins when indicated, the condition is generally modifiable and not the most immediate life-threatening concern.\n\nIschaemic stroke\n\nis incorrect because although Turner syndrome may be associated with vascular risk factors such as hypertension or congenital cardiovascular anomalies, ischaemic stroke is not considered a typical or leading complication. The most serious long-term risk is aortic dilatation, which can lead to dissection and sudden death. This risk often develops silently and requires active surveillance. While stroke is a serious condition, it is not as strongly linked to Turner syndrome nor as life-threatening in this context as aortic dissection.\n\nLeft ventricular aneurysm\n\nis incorrect because it is a rare complication that usually arises after myocardial infarction, which is not a recognised feature of Turner syndrome. There is no indication of ischaemic heart disease in this young woman, and her history and presentation do not support a prior cardiac event. While serious, this diagnosis would not be relevant in the absence of prior infarction or significant structural heart disease."
