      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Anti-double-stranded DNA (anti-dsDNA)",
      "explanation": "Goodpasture's syndrome is caused by autoantibodies against collagen type IV\n\nAnti-glomerular basement membrane (anti-GBM)\n\nis correct, as this patient’s haemoptysis, bilateral pulmonary infiltrates, haematuria, proteinuria, hypertension, and acute onset strongly suggest Goodpasture’s syndrome. The anti-GBM antibodies attack type IV collagen in the glomerular and alveolar basement membranes, leading to the simultaneous lung and kidney damage. The young male demographic (24 years old) and acute onset of pulmonary haemorrhage (bilateral infiltrates on chest X-ray) and glomerulonephritis (haematuria, proteinuria, elevated creatinine) strongly fits the picture of Goodpasture's syndrome.\n\nAnti-double-stranded DNA (anti-dsDNA)\n\nis incorrect, as anti-dsDNA is characteristic of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), which can cause pulmonary haemorrhage, though rarely. However, SLE typically presents with additional features such as malar rash, joint pain, or cytopenias, absent in this patient.\n\nAnti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP)\n\nis incorrect, as while anti-CCP is specific for rheumatoid arthritis, this patient lacks joint pain, synovitis, or erosive arthritis. Rheumatoid arthritis does not cause pulmonary-renal syndrome.\n\nAnti-myeloperoxidase p-ANCA (anti-MPO)\n\nis incorrect, as p-ANCA is most commonly associated with microscopic polyangiitis, which can also cause a pulmonary–renal syndrome. However, microscopic polyangiitis usually evolves over several weeks, with constitutional upset, arthralgia or a small-vessel vasculitic rash; none of these features is evident here. In contrast, anti-GBM disease presents far more abruptly and tends to be more severe in young men.\n\nCytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody (c-ANCA)\n\nis incorrect, as c-ANCA is associated with granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA), another cause of pulmonary-renal syndrome. Although GPA typically involves upper respiratory tract symptoms (sinusitis, nasal involvement) and granulomatous inflammation, these features may occasionally be absent. However, given the clinical scenario, age, and acute presentation without other GPA-related features, anti-GBM remains the more likely diagnosis."
