      "question": "A 14-year-old girl presents to the emergency department with fevers. Her past medical history includes juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA). She is on methotrexate. She attends school normally and lives with her parents.\n\nHer observations are as follows: heart rate 121 beats per minute, blood pressure 95/65 mmHg, respiratory rate 21/minute, oxygen saturation 97% on room air and temperature 39.8ºC.\n\nOn examination, she appears unwell. She is diaphoretic. There is clinical evidence of hepatomegaly and splenomegaly. Chest auscultation is unremarkable. Her Glasgow coma scale is 15/15.\n\nBlood tests:\n\n\nHb\t101 g/L\tMale: (135-180)\nFemale: (115 - 160)\nPlatelets\t89 * 109/L\t(150 - 400)\nWBC\t5.2 * 109/L\t(4.0 - 11.0)\nNa+\t136 mmol/L\t(135 - 145)\nK+\t4.9 mmol/L\t(3.5 - 5.0)\nUrea\t8.9 mmol/L\t(2.0 - 7.0)\nCreatinine\t141 µmol/L\t(55 - 120)\nCRP\t101 mg/L\t(< 5)\nFerritin\t6400 ng/ml\t(10 - 300)\nBilirubin\t24 µmol/L\t(3 - 17)\nALP\t89 u/L\t(30 - 100)\nALT\t101 u/L\t(3 - 40)\nÎ³GT\t144 u/L\t(8 - 60)\nAST\t122 U/L\t(1 - 45)\nAlbumin\t31 g/L\t(35 - 50)\n\nBased on the likely diagnosis, which cytokine is most likely to be activated from the list below?",
