      "correctText": "Interferon-gamma",
      "explanation": "Interferon-γ is responsible for activating macrophages\n\nInterferon-gamma is the correct answer. The patient has macrophage activation syndrome, which is an uncontrolled hyper-inflammatory state associated with many systemic autoimmune diseases but in particular JIA. ACR/EULAR classification criteria for macrophage activation syndrome (MAS) state that in a patient with JIA who presents with a fever, a diagnosis of MAS can be made if the ferritin level is > 684 ng/ml and any two of the following are present (platelets < 181 * 10\n\n9\n\n/L, AST > 48 U/L, triglycerides > 156 mg/dl, fibrinogen < 360 mg/dl). Refractory fever and hepatosplenomegaly are typical clinical features.  Interferon-gamma is responsible for the activation of macrophages and is heavily implicated in the pathogenesis of this condition.\n\nIL-3 is incorrect. This is not heavily implicated in the pathogenesis of MAS. It is associated with conditions such as mastocytosis and various leukaemias. It stimulates differentiation and proliferation of myeloid progenitor cells.\n\nIL-4 is incorrect. The main source of this cytokine is Th2 cells and it stimulates differentiation of B cells.\n\nIL-10 is incorrect. This is an 'anti-inflammatory cytokine' and is therefore not implicated in macrophage activation syndrome.\n\nIL-5 is incorrect. This stimulates the production of eosinophils."
