      "question": "A hospital team is evaluating the effect of a new antihypertensive drug. Twenty patients have their systolic blood pressure measured before and after 8 weeks of treatment. The data are not normally distributed. Which statistical test is most appropriate to compare the pre- and post-treatment blood pressures in this group?",
      "options": [
        "Student's paired t-test",
        "Wilcoxon signed-rank test",
        "Mann-Whitney U test",
        "Chi-squared test",
        "Spearman's rank correlation"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Chi-squared test",
      "explanation": "Wilcoxon signed-rank test - compares two sets of observations on a single sample, e.g. a 'before' and 'after' test on the same population following an intervention\n\nWilcoxon signed-rank test\n\nis correct because it is specifically designed for comparing two related samples or repeated measurements on a single sample, especially when the data are non-parametric (not normally distributed). In this scenario, each patient acts as their own control, with measurements taken before and after an intervention. The Wilcoxon signed-rank test evaluates whether there is a statistically significant difference between these paired observations, making it the best choice according to UK guidelines for non-parametric paired data.\n\nStudent's paired t-test\n\nwould be suitable if the differences between pre- and post-treatment measurements were normally distributed (parametric data). However, since the question specifies that the data are not normally distributed, using this parametric test would violate its assumptions and could lead to erroneous conclusions.\n\nMann-Whitney U test\n\ncompares differences between two independent (unpaired) groups when data are non-parametric. It cannot account for pairing within subjects; thus, it is inappropriate here where each subject has both pre- and post-intervention measurements.\n\nChi-squared test\n\nis used to compare proportions or frequencies between groups rather than continuous or ordinal variables like blood pressure readings. It does not analyse changes in continuous values within individuals over time.\n\nSpearman's rank correlation\n\nassesses the strength and direction of association between two ranked variables but does not compare means or medians of paired samples. It would not answer whether there was a significant change in blood pressure following intervention."
