      "correctText": "Axillary nerve",
      "explanation": "Shoulder abduction - deltoid muscle - axillary nerve (C5,C6)\n\nThe axillary nerve is the correct answer. This elderly man with osteoporosis has sustained a fall onto an outstretched hand resulting in a proximal humerus fracture. Fractures of this type are associated with axillary nerve injury, Injury to this nerve results in loss of the ability to abduct the shoulder due to denervation of the deltoid muscle.\n\nThe long thoracic nerve is incorrect. Injury to this nerve is typically caused by injuries to the ribs. It causes 'winging' of the scapula. The term 'winged scapula'  is used when the muscles of the scapula are weak or paralysed, resulting in limited ability to stabilise the scapula. As a result, the medial border of the scapula protrudes, like wings.  This is not an abnormality in this case. Additionally, the mechanism of injury and examination findings suggest a humeral fracture rather than a rib injury.\n\nThe musculocutaneous nerve is incorrect. Injury to this nerve causes an inability to flex the elbow. It is rarely an isolated injury and more commonly is caused by injury to the brachial plexus. Given the examination finding is an inability to abduct the shoulder rather than flex the elbow, this is less likely.\n\nThe radial nerve is incorrect. Injury to this nerve is often caused by a midshaft fracture of the humerus. It will result in a 'wrist drop', which is an inability to dorsiflex the wrist. This is therefore not the likely nerve that has been injured.\n\nThe ulnar nerve is incorrect. A possible mechanism of injury to this nerve is a fracture of the medial epicondyle. The resulting deformity will be a 'claw hand', which is when the fourth and fifth digits are flexed and the patient cannot straighten these two fingers. This is not the case here and this is, therefore, less likely."
