      "correctText": "Riboflavin",
      "explanation": "Riboflavin deficiency causes angular cheilitis\n\nThis patient is experiencing angular cheilitis - inflammation, fissuring, cracking and crusting at the angles of the mouth. It is caused by a variety of reasons, including infection, mechanical factors, drug-related side effects and nutritional deficiencies. In the case of the latter, deficiencies of iron, zinc and vitamins from the B group are the most common causes. Of those in the vitamin B group, deficiencies of\n\nriboflavin\n\n(B2), niacin (B3), pyridoxine (B6), folate (B9) and cyanocobalamin (B12) are typically linked to the condition.\n\nAscorbic acid\n\n(vitamin C) deficiency is not linked to angular cheilitis. It is classically associated with scurvy, which presents with bleeding gums and loosened teeth.\n\nColecalciferol\n\n(vitamin D3) deficiency is not associated with angular cheilitis. It can, however, affect gum health and overall tooth strength.\n\nPantothenic acid\n\n(vitamin B5) deficiency is not usually linked with angular cheilitis.\n\nThiamine\n\n(vitamin B1) deficiency is not normally linked with angular cheilitis. It can cause several medical conditions, including Wernicke's encephalopathy, wet beriberi and dry beriberi."
