      "id": 250,
      "question": "A randomised controlled trial compares two drugs used in the initial management of rheumatoid arthritis. After being assigned to the randomised groups a number of patients drop out due to adverse effects of the medication. How should the data be analysed?",
      "options": [
        "Recruit more patients",
        "For each patient who drops out, remove a patient from the other randomised group",
        "Include the patients who drop out in the final data set",
        "Remove patients who drop out from final data set",
        "Abandon the trial if more than 5% of patients drop out"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Recruit more patients",
      "explanation": "Intention to treat analysis - include patients who dropped out of trial\n\nThe correct answer, in this case, is to\n\ninclude the patients who drop out in the final data set\n\n. This approach is known as intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis, which is a standard method for analysing randomised controlled trials. The main principle of ITT analysis is that all participants are analysed according to their original treatment allocation, regardless of whether they completed the intervention or not. This helps maintain the benefits of randomisation and provides a more conservative estimate of the treatment effect.\n\nThe option to\n\nrecruit more patients\n\nmay seem like a reasonable solution, but it does not address the issue of how to handle missing data from those who have already dropped out. Additionally, adding more patients could introduce bias if the new patients are not randomly allocated.\n\nFor each patient who drops out, remove a patient from the other randomised group\n\nwould disrupt the balance achieved through randomisation and potentially introduce bias into the results. It also does not account for potential differences between those who drop out and those who remain in the study.\n\nRemoving patients who drop out from the final data set (\n\nremove patients who drop out from the final data set\n\n) may lead to an overestimation of treatment effects by only including those with favourable outcomes. This approach is called per-protocol analysis and can be biased because it does not account for non-adherence or loss to follow-up.\n\nFinally,\n\nabandoning the trial if more than 5% of patients drop out\n\nis an arbitrary threshold that may not be appropriate for all situations. Depending on the nature and severity of adverse effects, it might be important to continue investigating potential benefits or risks associated with these medications. Stopping a trial prematurely without sufficient evidence could lead to inconclusive results and wasted resources."
