      "id": 252,
      "question": "A 30-year-old man presents with several months of fatigue, unexplained weight loss, and recurring fever. During the examination, multiple enlarged lymph nodes were detected.\n\nAn urgent biopsy was conducted, revealing the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells, strongly indicating a potential diagnosis of Hodgkin's lymphoma (HL). To aid in the classification of the HL subtype and plan treatment immunohistochemistry testing is performed.\n\nWhat cell surface proteins are characteristic of these particular cells?",
      "options": [
        "CD14",
        "CD15",
        "CD21",
        "CD56",
        "CD8"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "CD15",
      "explanation": "CD15 is found on Reed-Sternberg cells\n\nReed-Sternberg cells, unique and enlarged lymphocytes, are a defining feature within the lymph nodes of Hodgkin's lymphoma (HL) patients. These cells are instrumental in HL diagnosis, actively participating in the immune response and the creation of an inflammatory environment that leads to swollen lymph nodes.\n\nCD15\n\n, a cell surface protein, is frequently present on Reed-Sternberg cells, assisting in the identification of HL. The use of immunohistochemistry enables the detection of CD15, serving as a vital diagnostic marker that sets HL apart from other lymphomas and lymph node disorders. Reed-Sternberg cells and the CD15 marker together play a pivotal role in confirming HL and guiding precise treatment decisions, offering a clear distinction from similar conditions.\n\nCD14\n\nis a cell surface receptor protein predominantly found on monocytes and macrophages and plays a vital role in recognising and responding to bacterial components, particularly lipopolysaccharides (LPS) found in the outer membranes of gram-negative bacteria. When CD14 on monocytes or macrophages encounters these bacterial components, it triggers an immune response, promoting the release of inflammatory mediators and facilitating the body's defence against potential infections.\n\nCD21\n\n, also known as complement receptor 2 (CR2), is a cell surface protein primarily found on B lymphocytes. CD21 plays a significant role in the context of Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection with the virus binding to the surface protein, facilitating its entry into these cells. Once inside, EBV can establish latent infections in B cells, leading to various EBV-associated diseases, including infectious mononucleosis and some lymphomas.\n\nCD56\n\n, also known as neural cell adhesion molecule (NCAM), is a cell surface protein found on various immune cells, nerve cells, and other tissues. It is particularly prevalent on natural killer (NK) cells, a type of lymphocyte with a critical role in the innate immune system. CD56 plays a crucial role in the function of NK cells by promoting cell adhesion and facilitating their interaction with target cells.\n\nCD8\n\n, also known as cluster of differentiation 8, is a cell surface protein primarily found on cytotoxic T cells. These CD8+ T cells are key players in the adaptive immune system, responsible for directly targeting and eliminating infected or abnormal cells. CD8 serves as a co-receptor for the T cell receptor (TCR), enhancing the T cell's ability to recognise antigens presented by major histocompatibility complex (MHC) molecules on target cells."
