      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Intracellular receptor",
      "explanation": "Atrial natriuretic factor - guanylate cyclase receptor\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nGuanylate cyclase receptor\n\n. Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) binds to natriuretic peptide receptors, specifically NPR-A, which is a membrane-bound receptor with intrinsic guanylate cyclase activity. When ANP binds to this receptor, it activates the guanylate cyclase domain, leading to the conversion of GTP to cyclic GMP (cGMP). This increase in intracellular cGMP acts as a second messenger that mediates the physiological effects of ANP, including natriuresis, diuresis, vasodilation, and inhibition of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. This receptor type is crucial for regulating blood pressure and volume homeostasis.\n\nLigand-gated ion channel\n\nis incorrect. Ligand-gated ion channels are transmembrane proteins that open to allow ion flux across the cell membrane when a specific ligand binds. Examples include nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, GABA receptors, and glutamate receptors. The ANP receptor does not function by directly opening an ion channel but rather by activating an enzymatic pathway that produces cGMP.\n\nIntracellular receptor\n\nis incorrect. Intracellular receptors are located within the cell cytoplasm or nucleus and typically bind lipophilic molecules that can cross the cell membrane, such as steroid hormones, thyroid hormones, and vitamin D. ANP is a peptide hormone that cannot freely cross the cell membrane and therefore binds to a membrane-bound receptor rather than an intracellular one.\n\nG protein-coupled receptor\n\nis incorrect. While G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) are indeed membrane receptors that transduce signals via G proteins, the ANP receptor does not use this mechanism. GPCRs typically activate adenylate cyclase or phospholipase C pathways, whereas the ANP receptor directly activates guanylate cyclase without requiring G protein intermediaries.\n\nTyrosine kinase receptor\n\nis incorrect. Tyrosine kinase receptors are transmembrane proteins that, upon ligand binding, dimerize and autophosphorylate tyrosine residues in their cytoplasmic domains. This creates binding sites for intracellular signalling molecules. Examples include insulin receptors and growth factor receptors. The ANP receptor does not possess tyrosine kinase activity but instead has guanylate cyclase activity."
