      "explanation": "Psoriatic arthritis is associated with HLA B27\n\nThe Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) system consists of antigen-presenting proteins encoded by a diverse set of genes. The various combinations of alleles lead to a broad spectrum of HLA types among individuals. Certain configurations within the HLA system can interact more robustly with the immune system, thereby mediating an inflammatory response. These responses may be autoimmune, thus establishing associations between certain HLA patterns and specific diseases. Considering the clinical presentation of arthritis in the distal interphalangeal joints (DIPJs), coupled with nail changes and lower back pain suggestive of potential sacroiliac involvement, this patient is most likely suffering from psoriatic arthritis. Psoriatic arthritis is associated with\n\nHLA B27\n\n, which can occur both with and without cutaneous manifestations typical of psoriasis.\n\nHLA B51\n\nhas been linked to BehÃ§et's disease, a form of vasculitis characterised by oral and genital ulcers, an increased risk for venous thromboembolism, and a rash. This condition has a strong correlation with smoking. This HLA marker is not as closely associated with psoriatic arthritis as HLA B27.\n\nCoeliac disease, which causes gastrointestinal symptoms due to gluten intolerance, is associated with\n\nHLA DQ2\n\n, rather than HLA B27 which is not as closely associated.\n\nHLA DR3\n\nis implicated in dermatitis herpetiformis - a pruritic papular rash frequently associated with coeliac disease - and Sjogren's syndrome, an autoimmune disorder targeting exocrine glands such as the lacrimal and salivary glands. It also plays a role in type 1 diabetes mellitus. Again, while HLA DR3 is associated with this autoimmune condition, it is not as closely related to psoriatic arthritis.\n\nHLA DR4\n\nshows a stronger association with autoimmune type 1 diabetes mellitus and rheumatoid arthritis. However, this form of arthritis seldom affects the DIPJs as observed in this patient, nor does it typically result in the accompanying nail changes."
