      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Methyldopa",
      "explanation": "Amiodarone in breastfeeding  must be avoided\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nAmiodarone\n\n. Amiodarone is a potent antiarrhythmic drug with a long half-life. It is highly lipid-soluble and therefore, extensively stored in body tissues, including breast milk. This can result in significant exposure to the breastfeeding infant, potentially causing neonatal hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism. Hence, it is contra-indicated during breastfeeding.\n\nAminophylline\n\nis used as a bronchodilator for managing asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Although it appears in breast milk, the amount ingested by the infant is small and usually without adverse effects on the infant.\n\nCarbamazepine\n\n, an anticonvulsant and mood stabiliser, does pass into breast milk but at levels that are generally considered safe for most infants. However, monitoring the nursing infant for potential side effects such as excessive sleepiness or skin reactions may be advisable.\n\nSodium valproate\n\n, another anticonvulsant and mood stabiliser, also passes into breast milk but again at levels that are generally considered safe for most infants. Monitoring of the nursing infant for symptoms such as poor feeding, irritability or abnormal liver function tests would be prudent.\n\nFinally,\n\nMethyldopa\n\n, an antihypertensive agent, has been shown to pass into breast milk in relatively small amounts. Methyldopa is often used during pregnancy and lactation and is generally considered safe for use while breastfeeding."
