      "id": 280,
      "question": "A new type of blood test is being studied that may be able to accurately detect the presence of a pulmonary embolism. One hundred and fifty patients who have pulmonary emboli confirmed via the gold standard, a CT pulmonary angiogram (CTPA), are recruited, along with one hundred and fifty patients who have had pulmonary emboli excluded via the same scan. They are all subjected to the new blood test and the results are as follows:\n\n\n\tDisease present on CTPA\tDisease absent on CTPA\nBlood test positive\t90\t30\nBlood test negative\t60\t120\n\nWhat is the positive predictive value?",
      "options": [
        "0.6",
        "0.67",
        "0.7",
        "0.75",
        "0.8"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "0.6",
      "explanation": "Positive predictive value = TP / (TP + FP)\n\nPositive predictive value (PPV) refers to the proportion of true positives out of the total of all positive results. In this scenario, the total number of positive blood tests is 90 + 30 = 120. The PPV is therefore 90 / 120 =\n\n0.75\n\n.\n\nThe figure of\n\n0.6\n\nrefers to the sensitivity of the test. Sensitivity is the proportion of patients with the condition who have a positive test result. There are 150 people in total with the disease identified on CTPA. Out of these, 90 have a positive blood result, and so 90 / 150 = 0.6.\n\nThe number\n\n0.67\n\n, in this scenario, is the negative predictive value (NPV) - the number of true negative results as a proportion of all the negative results. There are 60 + 120 = 180 negative blood results in total. Out of this, 120 are truly negative as per the disease being absent on CTPA. Therefore, 120 / 180 = 0.67.\n\nThe figure of\n\n0.7\n\nis not meaningful with respect to this scenario.\n\nThe figure of\n\n0.8\n\nrefers to the test's specificity - the proportion of patients without the condition who have a negative test result. In total, 150 patients are identified as having no disease on CTPA. Out of these, 120 have a negative blood result and so the specificity is 120 / 150 = 0.8."
