      "explanation": "Polymerase chain reactions are used to detect mutated oncogenes\n\nPolymerase chain reaction (PCR)\n\nis correct. The patient likely has a hereditary breast and ovarian cancer syndrome caused by the BRCA1 gene. Polymerase chain reactions are used to detect mutated oncogenes.\n\nBone marrow biopsy\n\nis incorrect. This is typically used to diagnose hematological disorders rather than identify the BRCA1 gene mutation. The axillary lymphadenopathy, in this case, is likely related to the breast lump, given the family history, rather than a primary haematological disorder.\n\nLymph node biopsy\n\nis incorrect. If the patient had regional lymphadenopathy, a biopsy may confirm metastases. However, it would not reveal the underlying cause of the cancers.\n\nFlow cytometry\n\nis incorrect. This is a technique used to detect and measure the physical and chemical characteristics of cells or particles. This is more commonly used to investigate haematological malignancies.\n\nPET scan\n\nis incorrect. This will show the extent of metastatic disease but will not clarify the underlying cause of the synchronous cancers, which is the BRCA1 gene. It is used also to monitor for the recurrence of malignancy post-treatment in some cases."
