      "correctText": "Proximal tubule",
      "explanation": "Phosphate is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule\n\nProximal tubule\n\nis correct. The patient has been recently been started on acetazolamide for IIH. Acetazolamide inhibits phosphate reabsorption at the proximal tubule. Profound hypophosphatemia can cause muscle weakness. Phosphate is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule.\n\nCollecting duct\n\nis incorrect. This final part of the nephron is involved in electrolyte and water homeostasis but is not the principal site of phosphate reabsorption.\n\nCollecting tubule\n\nis incorrect. The collecting tubule empties into the collecting duct. The major function of this part of the nephron is to reabsorb water in response to vasopressin and sodium in response to aldosterone.\n\nDistal convoluted tubule\n\nis incorrect. Distal convoluted tubule cells are largely impermeable to water and reabsorb sodium and chloride across the apical membrane via electroneutral pathways. it is not the major site of phosphate reabsorption.\n\nThick ascending loop of Henle\n\nis incorrect. The thick ascending loop of Henle is a major resorptive segment of the nephron and is responsible for the resorption of approximately one-quarter of the filtered load of sodium, chloride, and potassium ions."
