      "correctText": "10",
      "explanation": "Numbers needed to treat (NNT) is a measure that indicates how many patients would require an intervention to reduce the expected number of outcomes by one.\n\n50\n\nis the correct answer. NNT is the number of patients who must receive a treatment over a specified time to prevent one additional adverse outcome compared to a control group. It is calculated as the inverse of the absolute risk reduction (ARR). The absolute risk of a cardiovascular event without treatment is 10% (0.10). The treatment reduces this risk by 2%, so the absolute risk with treatment is 8% (0.08). Therefore, the ARR = 0.10 - 0.08 = 0.02. The NNT is the inverse of this (i.e. 1/0.02) and therefore is 50.\n\n5\n\nis incorrect. This would imply an absolute risk reduction of 20%, which is inconsistent with the data.\n\n10\n\nis incorrect. It corresponds to an absolute risk reduction of 10%, which is five times higher than the actual reported 2%.\n\n20\n\nis incorrect. This represents an absolute risk reduction of 5%, which is incorrect.\n\n100\n\nis incorrect. The absolute risk reduction would be 1% for this value, which overestimates the NNT."
