      "explanation": "Epidermis - 5 layers - bottom layer = stratum germinativum which gives rise to keratinocytes and contains melanocytes\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nStratum germinativum\n\n(also known as stratum basale), which is the deepest layer of the epidermis that lies immediately adjacent to the dermis. This single layer of columnar cells is responsible for the continuous regeneration of the epidermis through mitotic division. It contains keratinocytes and melanocytes, and is attached to the basement membrane via hemidesmosomes.\n\nStratum granulosum\n\nis incorrect as this layer lies above the stratum spinosum and contains keratinocytes filled with keratohyalin granules. It is the third layer from the basement membrane and plays a role in keratinisation.\n\nStratum lucidum\n\nis incorrect as this thin, clear layer is only present in thick skin (such as palms and soles) and lies between the stratum granulosum and stratum corneum. It consists of dead keratinocytes that have lost their nuclei.\n\nStratum corneum\n\nis incorrect as this is the outermost layer of the epidermis, consisting of multiple layers of flattened, dead keratinocytes that are continuously shed and replaced. It provides the main barrier function of the skin.\n\nStratum spinosum\n\nis incorrect as this layer lies above the stratum germinativum but below the stratum granulosum. It contains keratinocytes connected by desmosomes, giving the cells their characteristic 'spiny' appearance."
