      "correctText": "Cytomegalovirus",
      "explanation": "Congenital toxoplasmosis\n\nToxoplasma gondii\n\nis indeed the correct answer as chorioretinitis is one of the most characteristic manifestations of congenital toxoplasmosis. The classic triad of congenital toxoplasmosis includes chorioretinitis, intracranial calcifications, and hydrocephalus. The chorioretinitis typically presents as a necrotising retinitis that can lead to scarring, and whilst it may be present at birth, it can also develop later in life. The retinal lesions are often bilateral and can affect the macula, potentially causing significant visual impairment.\n\nCytomegalovirus\n\ninfection can affect the eyes in congenital infection, but it typically causes retinitis rather than chorioretinitis. CMV more characteristically presents with microcephaly, hearing loss, and hepatosplenomegaly in congenital infections. The retinal involvement tends to be more peripheral and has a different appearance from toxoplasma chorioretinitis.\n\nTreponema pallidum\n\n, the causative organism of congenital syphilis, can affect multiple organ systems but is not characteristically associated with chorioretinitis. The ocular manifestations of congenital syphilis typically include interstitial keratitis, which usually develops in later childhood. Other classic features include Hutchinson's teeth and saddle nose deformity.\n\nRubella\n\nvirus can cause congenital eye abnormalities, but these typically manifest as cataracts, microphthalmia, and pigmentary retinopathy rather than chorioretinitis. The classic triad of congenital rubella syndrome includes cataracts, cardiac defects (particularly patent ductus arteriosus), and sensorineural deafness.\n\nParvovirus B19\n\ninfection during pregnancy primarily affects the developing foetus by causing severe anaemia, which can lead to hydrops fetalis. It is not typically associated with chorioretinitis or other significant ocular manifestations. The main concern with parvovirus B19 infection during pregnancy is foetal loss, particularly if infection occurs during the first trimester."
