      "id": 298,
      "question": "Two siblings, a 12-year-old girl and a 15-year-old boy, present with clinical features of neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1).\n\nThe girl has multiple café-au-lait spots and two cutaneous neurofibromas but is otherwise healthy with no significant functional impairments. The boy, however, has a more severe presentation, including numerous large neurofibromas, axillary freckling, optic glioma, and scoliosis requiring bracing. Both siblings have been confirmed to carry the same pathogenic mutation in the NF1 gene.\n\nWhat accounts for the varying severities of the condition between the two siblings?",
      "options": [
        "Allelic heterogeneity",
        "Expressivity",
        "Germline mosaicism",
        "Penetrance",
        "Variable inheritance"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Allelic heterogeneity",
      "explanation": "In genetics, expressivity describes the extent to which a genotype shows its phenotypic expression in an individual\n\nExpressivity\n\nis the correct answer because it explains the varying severity of clinical features observed in the two siblings, despite both carrying the same pathogenic mutation in the NF1 gene. Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) is a condition where expressivity is a well-documented phenomenon, with family members often exhibiting a wide range of severity despite sharing the same genetic mutation. In this case, the 12-year-old girl has mild manifestations such as café-au-lait spots and a few cutaneous neurofibromas, whereas her 15-year-old brother has a more severe phenotype, including multiple large neurofibromas, optic glioma, and scoliosis. This variation illustrates the concept of expressivity, which refers to the degree or extent to which a genotype is phenotypically expressed. Factors such as modifier genes, environmental influences, or allelic variation contribute to this variability, resulting in the spectrum of clinical features seen in NF1.\n\nAllelic heterogeneity\n\nrefers to different mutations within the same gene causing similar clinical conditions. While allelic heterogeneity may contribute to phenotypic variability in some disorders, in this case, the siblings share the same NF1 mutation, so it does not account for the varying severities observed.\n\nGermline mosaicism\n\noccurs when a mutation is present in a subset of germ cells, potentially leading to offspring with genetic conditions despite unaffected parents. Here, both siblings inherited the same mutation from their parents, making germline mosaicism an unlikely explanation for their differing symptoms.\n\nPenetrance\n\ndescribes the proportion of individuals with a genotype who express the associated phenotype. Both siblings exhibit features of neurofibromatosis type 1, indicating full penetrance, but this does not explain the variability in severity, which is due to expressivity.\n\nVariable inheritance\n\nsuggests differences in the pattern of inheritance, such as autosomal dominant versus recessive. However, neurofibromatosis type 1 follows a clear autosomal dominant inheritance, so this term does not apply."
