      "question": "What are funnel plots primarily used for?",
      "options": [
        "Demonstrate the heterogeneity of a meta-analysis",
        "Demonstrate the existence of publication bias in meta-analyses",
        "Provide a graphical representation of the relative risk results in a case-control study",
        "Provide a graphical representation of the relative risk results in a cohort study",
        "Provide a graphical representation of the probability of a patient experiencing a particular adverse effect"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Provide a graphical representation of the relative risk results in a case-control study",
      "explanation": "Funnel plots - show publication bias in meta-analyses\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nDemonstrate the existence of publication bias in meta-analyses\n\n. Funnel plots are a useful tool in meta-analyses to visually assess the likelihood of publication bias. In an ideal scenario, where there is no publication bias, the plot should resemble an inverted funnel. This is because studies with high precision (large sample size) will be plotted near the average, and those with low precision (small sample size) will scatter more widely at both sides of the average - creating a 'funnel' effect. If there is asymmetry in the funnel plot, it suggests potential publication bias.\n\nDemonstrate the heterogeneity of a meta-analysis\n\nis incorrect as funnel plots are not primarily used to demonstrate heterogeneity within a meta-analysis. Although they can give some indication about heterogeneity if there's a significant scattering of studies which could suggest variability between study results, their main purpose is to identify potential publication bias. Heterogeneity in a meta-analysis is typically assessed using statistical tests such as Cochran's Q test or I^2 statistic.\n\nProvide a graphical representation of the relative risk results in a case-control study\n\nand\n\nProvide a graphical representation of the relative risk results in a cohort study\n\nare also incorrect options. While these types of studies often form part of meta-analyses, funnel plots themselves do not specifically represent relative risk results from individual case-control or cohort studies. The primary purpose of these plots is to assess for potential publication bias across all included studies.\n\nLastly,\n\nProvide a graphical representation of the probability of a patient experiencing a particular adverse effect\n\nis not correct either. While adverse effects can be one outcome measured in studies included in meta-analyses, again this isn't what funnel plots are primarily used for. They don't provide information on probabilities but rather aim to visualise potential biases across all included studies regardless of specific outcomes being measured."
