      "explanation": "CD8 - co-receptor for MHC class I\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nCD8\n\n. CD8 is a transmembrane glycoprotein that serves as a co-receptor for the T cell receptor (TCR). CD8 binds to MHC class I molecules, which present antigens from within the cell, such as those arising from intracellular pathogens or tumour cells. This interaction stabilises the TCR-antigen-MHC complex, enhancing the ability of the TCR to recognise and respond to its specific antigen.\n\nCD4\n\nis incorrect because it acts as a co-receptor for cells expressing antigens combined with MHC class II molecules, not class I. CD4 binds to MHC class II molecules, which present exogenous antigens typically derived from extracellular pathogens.\n\nCD2b\n\ndoes not exist and therefore cannot be a correct answer. It seems like this option may have been confused with CD2 or CD19b, neither of which act as co-receptors for cells expressing antigens combined with MHC class I molecules.\n\nCD1\n\nis also incorrect because it is not a co-receptor but rather an alternative form of MHC molecule. The CD1 family presents lipid-based antigens to T cells, unlike MHC class I and II molecules that present peptide antigens.\n\nFinally,\n\nCD2\n\n, like CD4 and CD8, is involved in T cell activation but it does not serve as a co-receptor for either MHC class I or II molecules. Instead, it mediates adhesion between T cells and other cell types (including other T cells), promoting cellular interactions necessary for immune responses."
