      "question": "A study is performed looking into the effectiveness of a novel auto-antibody test as a screening tool in bowel cancer. It identifies that out of a total of 1000 patients, 50 have a positive test, of which 30 go onto have confirmed bowel cancer via colonoscopy. Of the patients who tested negative, 5 of these were ultimately found to have bowel cancer.\n\nWhat is the positive predictive value of this test as a percentage?",
      "options": [
        "5%",
        "60%",
        "85.7%",
        "97.9%",
        "99.5%"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "97.9%",
      "explanation": "Positive predictive value = TP / (TP + FP)\n\nPositive predictive value is the probability that subjects with a positive screening test truly have the disease.\n\nPositive predictive value = TP/ (TP+FP).\n\nTrue positive (TP) = 30.\n\nFalse positive (FP) = 20.\n\nPositive predictive value as a percentage = TP/ (TP+FP) = 30/ (30+20) = 0.6 =\n\n60%.\n\n5%\n\nis the percentage of patients who had a positive test, not the positive predictive value of the test.\n\n85.7%\n\nis the sensitivity of the test. Sensitivity is the proportion of patients with the condition who have a positive test result.\n\nSensitivity = TP / (TP + FN) = 30 / (30+5) = 0.857/ 85.7%.\n\n97.9%\n\nis the specificity of the test. Specificity is the proportion of patients without the condition who have a negative test result.\n\nSpecificity = TN / (TN + FP) = 945 / (945 + 20) = 0.979/ 97.9%.\n\n99.5%\n\nis the negative predictive value. The negative predictive value is the chance that the patient does not have the condition if the diagnostic test is negative.\n\nNegative predictive value = TN / (TN + FN) = 945/ (945+5) = 0.995/ 99.5%."
