      "explanation": "Vitamin D increases serum calcium primarily through raising its absorption via the small intestine\n\nVitamin D promotes serum calcium through increasing its reabsorption by small intestine, increasing calcium reabsorption in the kidneys and promoting bone demineralisation. Whilst the latter 2 mechanisms are important, the major way in which calcium is increased by vitamin D through increasing its absorption by the gut. This occurs as vitamin D promotes transcellular calcium absorption via the apical calcium receptor and the TRPV6 as well as the intracellular movement of calcium using calbindin and the basolateral transfer of calcium out of cells via PMCA1b.\n\nVitamin D deficiency can initially cause hypocalcaemia. Patients may however, subsequently develop secondary hyperparathyroidism and therefore display normal serum calcium levels or develop hypercalcaemia.\n\nAfro-Caribbean and South Asian patients are more at risk of vitamin D deficiency compared to other patients and we should therefore suspect vitamin D deficiency more readily in these patients."
