      "id": 313,
      "question": "A 70-year-old woman with stage 3 renal failure is brought in with urinary incontinence and fever. The carers report she has had very little oral intake over the last few days, requiring a lot of encouragement to eat.\n\nOn assessment, the patient smells strongly of urine and has evidence of shock. A urine dip is positive for nitrates and leukocytes and a blood glucose recording is low. Urosepsis causing hypoglycaemia is suspected. As well as IV fluids and antibiotics glucose is provided orally and via IV as per hospital protocol.\n\nWhat part of the nephron is the majority of this substance normally reabsorbed?",
      "options": [
        "Ascending loop of Henle",
        "Collecting tubules",
        "Descending loop of Henle",
        "Distal convoluted tubules",
        "Proximal convoluted tubules"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Collecting tubules",
      "explanation": "The majority of glucose reabsorption in the nephron occurs within the proximal convoluted tubule\n\nDiabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) is a diabetic emergency and is often the first presentation of previously undiagnosed diabetic patients. The condition is due to a lack of insulin resulting in significantly increased levels of the monosaccharide glucose. Glucose is usually almost fully reabsorbed in the\n\nproximal convoluted tubules\n\n(PCT) by secondary active, sodium-coupled transporters (SGLT2). However, in diabetes the abnormally high levels can not be absorbed resulting in a significant amount remaining in the urine, drawing in a large volume of water. This produces the polyuria and dehydration seen in the condition.\n\nThe\n\nascending loop of Henle\n\nis made up of a thick and thin selection. Sodium, potassium and chloride ions are all reabsorbed in this segment of the nephrons via active transport, but glucose reabsorption is low.\n\nCollecting tubules\n\nare the final component of the kidney to influence substance and fluid reabsorption. This section of the nephrons is responsible for approximately 5% of sodium reabsorption and between 5% and 25% of water, depending on the level of antidiuretic hormone (ADH). Glucose reabsorption in the collection tubules is very low.\n\nThe\n\ndescending loop of Henle\n\nfunctions in connection with the ascending loop of Henle to control ion and water reabsorption. The descending aspect has low permeability to most ions resulting in a high level of water reabsorption. Again, glucose reabsorption is low.\n\nDistal convoluted tubules\n\n(DCT) plays a role in the regulation of potassium, sodium, calcium and pH. Several active and passive mechanisms are involved with the regulation of these ions which are affected by circulatory hormone levels (e.g. aldosterone). Almost all the glucose has already been reabsorbed by the time the filtrate reaches the DCT, reabsorption at this section is low."
