      "id": 326,
      "question": "A family is being investigated for an autosomal dominant disorder associated with a mutation in the BRCA1 gene. While the affected individual (proband) has breast cancer, her sister, who carries the same mutation, has not developed any malignancy.\n\nWhich genetic concept is this variation in clinical expression an example of?",
      "options": [
        "Anticipation",
        "Expressivity",
        "Incomplete penetrance",
        "Pleiotropy",
        "Variable mutation severity"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Variable mutation severity",
      "explanation": "In genetics, penetrance describes the proportion of a population of individuals who carry a disease-causing allele who express the related disease phenotype\n\nIncomplete penetrance\n\nis correct. Penetrance refers to the proportion of individuals with a particular genetic mutation who exhibit the associated phenotype. Incomplete penetrance occurs when some individuals carrying a disease-causing mutation do not express the associated phenotype. In this case, the sister carries the BRCA1 mutation but has not developed breast cancer, demonstrating incomplete penetrance. In BRCA1 and BRCA2 mutation carriers, the lifetime risk of breast cancer is high but not 100%, illustrating incomplete penetrance. Environmental factors, lifestyle, and modifying genetic variants contribute to whether the phenotype is expressed.\n\nAnticipation\n\nis incorrect. Anticipation refers to the phenomenon where a genetic disorder appears at an earlier age or with increased severity in successive generations, often seen in trinucleotide repeat disorders such as Huntington’s disease and myotonic dystrophy. This concept does not apply to the described scenario.\n\nExpressivity\n\nis incorrect. Expressivity refers to the variability in phenotype among individuals with the same genotype. While penetrance describes whether a phenotype is expressed, expressivity describes how it is expressed. This scenario highlights the absence of phenotype in one carrier, which is a penetrance issue, not a variability of expression.\n\nPleiotropy\n\nis incorrect. Pleiotropy occurs when a single gene mutation leads to multiple, seemingly unrelated phenotypic effects. For example, mutations in the Marfan syndrome gene can cause cardiovascular, skeletal, and ocular manifestations. This concept is irrelevant to penetrance.\n\nVariable mutation severity\n\nis incorrect. Mutation severity usually refers to differences in mutation types (e.g., missense vs. nonsense mutations) and their impact on protein function. This concept is irrelevant to the variation in phenotype expression among individuals with the same mutation."
