      "correctText": "X-linked dominant",
      "explanation": "Friedreich's ataxia is autosomal recessive\n\nAutosomal recessive\n\nis correct. The patient presents with a chronic history of dysarthria, and ataxia (broad-based gait). Moreover, he is noted to be areflexic. Together with echocardiographic findings of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, this suggests a diagnosis of Friedreich's ataxia (FA). The pattern of inheritance of this condition is autosomal recessive. It typically presents between the ages of 5-15 but may also have a later onset in some cases.\n\nAutosomal dominant\n\nis incorrect. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HOCM) is commonly inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. However, it is clear from the description that HOCM is not an isolated problem and there is a disorder with more diverse manifestations present.\n\nMitochondrial\n\nis incorrect. Most mitochondrial disorders are maternally inherited. An example would be neuropathy, ataxia, retinitis pigmentosa, and ptosis (NARP). While this patient has areflexia and ataxia. There is no suggestion of retinitis pigmentosa, and ptosis and so FA is the more likely diagnosis.\n\nX-linked dominant\n\nis incorrect. X-linked dominant inheritance means that the gene responsible for a genetic disorder is located on the X chromosome, and only one copy of the allele is required to cause the disorder. Examples of conditions inherited in this way are Rett syndrome and Alport syndrome.\n\nX-linked recessive\n\nis incorrect. This form of inheritance means that a mutation in a gene on the X chromosome causes the phenotype to definitely be expressed in males but only in females who are homozygous for the gene mutation. Examples of conditions with this inheritance pattern are red-green colour blindness and haemophilia A."
