      "correctText": "Immune-mediated vasculitis",
      "explanation": "Fabry disease is caused by a deficiency of alpha-galactosidase A\n\nDeficiency of alpha-galactosidase A\n\nis the correct answer because the patient’s presentation is classic for Fabry disease, an X-linked lysosomal storage disorder caused by a deficiency of alpha-galactosidase A. The resulting accumulation of globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) in vascular endothelial cells and other tissues explains the burning pain in the hands and feet (acroparesthesias), dark red angiokeratomas on the lower abdomen, and dark-coloured urine indicative of renal involvement (hematuria or proteinuria). The family history of early-onset renal failure further supports this diagnosis, as Fabry disease often leads to progressive kidney damage and end-stage renal disease (ESRD). Worsening pain with heat or exercise is a characteristic feature of Fabry disease due to impaired small-fibre nerve function.\n\nDefective collagen synthesis\n\nis incorrect because it refers to conditions such as Ehlers-Danlos syndrome or Alport syndrome. Alport syndrome can lead to renal failure but is primarily associated with progressive hematuria, sensorineural hearing loss, and ocular abnormalities, none of which are described in this patient. Additionally, the presence of angiokeratomas and burning pain is not characteristic of collagen synthesis disorders, ruling out this option.\n\nImmune-mediated vasculitis\n\n, such as Henoch-Schönlein purpura (HSP) or ANCA-associated vasculitis, can cause renal involvement and purpuric skin lesions. However, vasculitis typically presents with systemic symptoms such as fever, malaise, and palpable purpura, which are not seen here. The dark red skin lesions in this patient are consistent with angiokeratomas rather than vasculitic purpura. Furthermore, the family history and specific neuropathic pain (acroparesthesias) are not characteristic of immune-mediated vasculitis.\n\nPodocyte protein mutations\n\nare associated with hereditary nephrotic syndromes such as congenital nephrotic syndrome (e.g., NPHS1 mutation). While podocyte dysfunction can lead to renal failure, it does not explain this patient’s systemic symptoms such as neuropathic pain, angiokeratomas, or exacerbation of symptoms with heat or exercise. The absence of nephrotic-range proteinuria and oedema also makes podocyte protein mutation an unlikely mechanism in this case.\n\nHyperglycemia-induced vascular damage\n\nis incorrect because it refers to diabetic microvascular complications, including diabetic nephropathy and neuropathy. This patient’s young age, lack of diabetes history, and family history of renal failure without mention of hyperglycemia make this option less plausible. Additionally, the specific findings of angiokeratomas and exercise-induced pain are not features of diabetic complications."
