      "explanation": "Burkitt's lymphoma is commonly associated with c-MYC\n\nc-MYC\n\nis the correct answer because it is most commonly associated with Burkitt’s lymphoma, especially when the characteristic t(8;14) translocation is present. This translocation places c-MYC under the control of immunoglobulin heavy chain enhancers, leading to its overexpression and unchecked cellular proliferation. Recognising this association is crucial for understanding both diagnosis and targeted therapies in haematological malignancies.\n\nBCL-2\n\nis typically involved in follicular lymphoma rather than Burkitt's lymphoma. The classic translocation for BCL-2 is t(14;18), which leads to overexpression of BCL-2 protein, inhibiting apoptosis and contributing to tumour survival—features not seen in this scenario.\n\nn-MYC\n\namplification is characteristically found in neuroblastoma, particularly in children. It does not have a known association with Burkitt’s lymphoma or the t(8;14) translocation described here.\n\nABL\n\ngene rearrangements are most notably implicated in chronic myeloid leukaemia (CML), where a t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome creates a BCR-ABL fusion protein with tyrosine kinase activity. This mechanism differs significantly from that seen in Burkitt’s lymphoma.\n\nRET\n\nmutations are associated with multiple endocrine neoplasia types II and III (MEN II/III) as well as medullary thyroid carcinoma, but do not play a role in lymphomas or the specific genetic abnormality presented here."
