      "explanation": "Golgi adds mannose-6-phosphate to proteins for trafficking to lysosomes\n\nThe is responsible for modifying and packing molecules destined for secretion from cells. However, it is also responsible for the addition of\n\nmannose-6-phosphate\n\nto proteins destined for trafficking to lysosomes. Lysosomal storage disorders are uncommon disorders resulting in the dysfunction of enzymes within lysosomes. They are a form of an inborn error of metabolism. Research is being carried out looking into the trafficking of these faulty enzymes into lysosomes using the mannose-6-phosphate pathway.\n\nFructose-1,6-biphosphonate\n\nis produced by the phosphorylation of fructose-6-phosphate. Most glucose on fructose, on entering a cell, is converted to this molecule.\n\nAfter fructose enters cells, it is rapidly converted to\n\nFructose-1-phosphate\n\nbefore being stored in the liver and undergoing further conversions. Fructose-1-phosphate is found in abundance in hereditary fructose intolerance due to it's inability to be converted.\n\nFructose-6-phosphate\n\n, as with the other options, is involved in the glycolysis metabolic pathway. It is produced from glucose-6-phosphate and subsequently phosphorylated to produce fructose-1,6-biphosphonate. Fructose-6-phosphate can also be converted into mannose-6-phosphate via isomerisation.\n\nMannose-1-phosphate\n\nconverted from mannose-6-phosphate by phosphomannomutase."
