      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Heinz bodies",
      "explanation": "Reticul\n\nThe patient has had a significant upper gastrointestinal bleed as suggested by the clinical history, anaemia, and disproportionately raised urea.\n\nIn a bleeding patient with anaemia and a functioning bone marrow, then we would expect a compensatory reticulocytosis response.  This is in contrast to anaemia due to bone marrow failure where the reticulocyte count would be low or inappropriately normal.  Reticulocytes are larger immature red cells and hence can cause a spurious macrocytosis. They have a distinctive blood film appearance - appearing blue in colour. When present on a blood film in a significant number the term 'polychromasia' is used.\n\nBasophillic stippling\n\nrefers to the presence of numerous basophilic granules that are dispersed through the cytoplasm. They can be seen in heavy metal toxicity and myelodysplastic syndrome.\n\nHeinz bodies\n\nare associated with G6PD deficiency. They occur due to oxidative damage resulting in denatured haemoglobin. They are removed by the spleen resulting in the production of a 'bite cell'.\n\nHowell-Jolly bodies\n\n, target cells, and acanthocytes are features of hyposplenism.\n\nNucleated red blood cells\n\ncan be seen in many conditions such as myelofibrosis, thalassemia, and haematological malignancies."
