      "explanation": "Mann-Whitney U test - compares ordinal, interval, or ratio scales of unpaired data\n\nThe answer is the Mann-Whitney U test. The data are unpaired - each observation is independent of the other observations. This means we should use an unpaired test. Paired sample\n\nt\n\n-test and Wilcoxon signed rank test are used for paired data, e.g. a 'before' and 'after' test on the same population following an intervention.\n\nTo use Student's\n\nt\n\n-test we must be able to assume that the dependent variable in each group is normally distributed (but here the carrier group is not normally distributed). If the data are near-normal or a suitable transformation is applied to the data to make them normal, Student's\n\nt\n\n-test can sometimes be used. However, this is not stated in the question.\n\nTo help remember which non-parametric test is used:\n\nU\n\nrefers to a statistic calculated in the Mann Whitney\n\nU\n\ntest, but it can be used to remember than this test is used for\n\nU\n\nnpaired data. Whereas the Wilcoxon signed-rank test ranks which observation of a pair is greater, so it is used for paired data."
