      "correctText": "Collecting duct",
      "explanation": "The majority of glucose reabsorption in the nephron occurs within the proximal convoluted tubule\n\nProximal convoluted tubule\n\nis the correct answer. Sodium-glucose cotransporter (SGLT2) proteins are responsible for the resorption of approximately 90% of filtered glucose. Inhibition results in glycosuria and natriuresis as the protein is a cotransporter. SGLT2-inhibitors have been shown to significantly reduce the risk of hospitalisation in patients with heart failure. Interestingly, even though the glucose-lowering efficacy of SGLT2-inhibitors declines at lower eGFR rates, the cardiovascular benefits are preserved.\n\nAscending loop of Henle\n\nis not correct. Loop diuretics predominantly act at the apical membrane in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, inhibiting the sodium-potassium-chloride cotransporter.\n\nCollecting duct\n\nis not correct. Aldosterone receptor antagonists exert their diuretic effects within the collecting duct and distal convoluted tubule.\n\nDescending loop of Henle\n\nis not correct.  The descending limb is largely impermeable to ions and highly permeable to water. It contributes to generating the counter-current multiplication system and is responsible for most of the nephron's water-resorbing capacity.\n\nDistal convoluted tubule\n\nis not correct. Thiazide diuretics act on the sodium-chloride transporter in the distal convoluted tubule (DCT). There are SGLT1 proteins contained within the DCT, but these are unaffected by SGLT2 inhibitors."
