      "id": 368,
      "question": "As part of a research project you are trying to ascertain whether the use of dummies in infants is linked to sudden infant death syndrome. What is the most appropriate form of study design?",
      "options": [
        "Randomised controlled trial",
        "Cross-over trial",
        "Cross-sectional survey",
        "Case-control study",
        "Cohort study"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Randomised controlled trial",
      "explanation": "Case-control study - compares a group with a disease to a group without, looking at past exposure to a possible causal agent for the condition\n\nThe correct answer is Case-control study. This type of study design is most appropriate when investigating potential risk factors for rare outcomes, such as sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS). In a case-control study, researchers would identify infants who died from SIDS (cases) and compare them with matched controls (living infants) to retrospectively analyse dummy use. This design is particularly efficient for SIDS research as it requires relatively few subjects due to the condition's rarity, and it allows investigation of multiple potential risk factors simultaneously.\n\nRandomised controlled trial\n\nwould be unethical in this context as it would involve randomly allocating infants to either use or not use dummies, potentially exposing them to harm if there is indeed a link to SIDS. Furthermore, RCTs are typically used to test interventions rather than investigate risk factors.\n\nCross-over trial\n\nis inappropriate as it involves subjects receiving different interventions in sequence with a washout period between. This design would be impossible for SIDS research as the outcome is death, and crossover cannot occur.\n\nCross-sectional survey\n\nwould not be suitable as it only provides a snapshot of dummy use at one point in time and cannot establish temporal relationships between dummy use and SIDS. Additionally, as SIDS is rare, a cross-sectional survey would likely identify very few cases.\n\nCohort study\n\n, while potentially useful, would be less efficient than a case-control study for investigating SIDS. A cohort study would require following a very large number of infants over time to observe enough SIDS cases for meaningful analysis, making it more time-consuming and expensive than a case-control study."
