      "explanation": "Goodpasture's - HLA-DR2\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nGoodpasture's syndrome\n\n. This condition, also known as anti-glomerular basement membrane disease, has been found to be strongly associated with HLA-DR2. Goodpasture's syndrome is an autoimmune disorder characterised by the presence of circulating antibodies against type IV collagen in the glomerular and alveolar basement membranes. The reason for this association with HLA-DR2 is not completely understood but it is believed that certain HLA types may present self-antigens to T cells in a way that promotes autoimmunity.\n\nHaemochromatosis\n\n, on the other hand, is most strongly associated with HLA-A3. Haemochromatosis is an autosomal recessive disorder characterised by increased iron absorption leading to iron overload. While there are many types of haemochromatosis, type 1 (the most common form) is caused by mutations in the HFE gene which are often seen alongside the HLA-A3 antigen.\n\nType 1 diabetes mellitus\n\nhas a strong association with several different HLA antigens, particularly DR3 and DR4. These antigens are thought to contribute to the autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells that leads to type 1 diabetes. It should be noted though that while these associations exist, having these antigens does not guarantee a person will develop diabetes; other genetic and environmental factors play a role too.\n\nBehcet's disease\n\n, a rare multisystem inflammatory disorder, has been linked with HLA-B51 more than any other antigen. Behcet's disease causes inflammation of blood vessels throughout the body leading to symptoms such as mouth sores, eye inflammation and skin problems.\n\nLastly,\n\nCoeliac disease\n\nhas a very strong association with two specific HLA molecules: DQ2 and DQ8. Almost all individuals with coeliac disease possess one or both of these antigens. Coeliac disease is an immune reaction to dietary gluten leading to inflammation and damage in the lining of the small intestine."
