      "id": 373,
      "question": "Which one of the following immunoglobulins is involved in the activation of B-cells?",
      "options": [
        "IgD",
        "IgM",
        "IgE",
        "IgG",
        "IgA"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "IgM",
      "explanation": "IgD is involved in the activation of B-cells\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nIgD\n\n. Immunoglobulin D (IgD) is a monomeric antibody that is found primarily on the surface of B cells, where it functions as an antigen receptor. This role in B cell activation makes IgD crucial for the initiation of immune responses. When an antigen binds to the IgD molecule on a B cell, it triggers the cell to proliferate and differentiate into an antibody-secreting plasma cell.\n\nIgM\n\nis also found on the surface of B cells, but its primary role is not in activating these cells. Instead, IgM is released by plasma cells during the initial stages of an immune response to act as a first line of defence against pathogens. It can neutralise pathogens directly or activate complement cascades to enhance phagocytosis and inflammation.\n\nIgE\n\nplays a key role in allergic reactions and parasitic infections rather than B-cell activation. It binds to mast cells and basophils, causing them to release histamine when they encounter specific antigens. This results in symptoms such as itching, redness and swelling.\n\nIgG\n\nis the most abundant immunoglobulin in blood and extracellular fluid, providing long-term immunity against many pathogens. However, it does not play a significant role in activating B-cells. Instead, IgG antibodies are produced by differentiated plasma cells after exposure to an antigen.\n\nFinally,\n\nIgA\n\nis predominantly found in mucosal areas such as the gut, respiratory tract and urogenital tract where it helps prevent colonisation by pathogens. Like IgG, it does not have a major function in B-cell activation.\n\nTherefore, while all these immunoglobulins have important roles within the immune system, only IgD has a direct involvement with B-cell activation."
