      "id": 377,
      "question": "A trial is carried out to assess the risk of adverse skin reactions using a new topical glue for wound closure in children.\n\nA total of 2000 children were entered into the trial with 1000 randomly assigned to receive management with the new topical glue and 1000 using a standard topical glue.\n\nThe result showed that 10 of the children managed with the new glue sustained a skin reaction compared to 30 children managed using the standard topical glue.\n\nWhat best represents the absolute risk reduction for skin reactions using the new topical glue?",
      "options": [
        "0.01",
        "0.02",
        "0.03",
        "0.08",
        "0.8"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "0.02",
      "explanation": "Absolute risk reduction = (Control event rate) - (Experimental event rate)\n\nThe absolute risk reduction (ARR) is the calculated difference between the risk of an outcome in the exposed group and the unexposed group and is the inverse of the absolute risk reduction is the number needed to treat. The ARR is worked out by calculating the difference between the control event rate (CER) and the experimental event rate (EER) i.e. AAR = CER - EER\n\nCER = Number of patients who had a particular outcome with the control/ Total number who had the control. Whereas;\n\nEER = Number of patients who had a particular outcome with the intervention / Total number who had the intervention.\n\nIn this example, the control group were the children managed using the standard topical glue and therefore: CER = 30/1000 = 0.03.\n\nThe children managed using the new topical glue where the experimental group and therefore:  EER = 10/1000 = 0.01\n\nThis means to work out the ARR for the new topical glue we use the calculation:\n\nARR = CER - EER\n\nARR = 0.03 - 0.01\n\nARR therefore =\n\n0.02"
