      "explanation": "Specificity = TN / (TN + FP)\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nSpecificity\n\n. When the cut-off value for a positive test is increased, meaning a higher level of the protein is required to call the test positive, the specificity of the test will increase. This occurs because there will be fewer false positive results, as fewer patients without colorectal cancer will have protein levels above this higher threshold. Specificity is the proportion of true negatives that are correctly identified, so with fewer false positives, the specificity improves.\n\nThe p value\n\nis incorrect because the p value is a measure of statistical significance that indicates the probability of obtaining results as extreme as those observed, assuming the null hypothesis is true. Changing the cut-off value of a diagnostic test does not directly affect the p value of a study.\n\nLikelihood ratio for a negative test result\n\nis incorrect because when the cut-off value is increased, the negative likelihood ratio actually decreases. This occurs because there will be more false negatives with a higher cut-off, which reduces the test's ability to rule out disease when negative.\n\nSensitivity\n\nis incorrect because sensitivity decreases when the cut-off value is increased. Sensitivity is the proportion of true positives that are correctly identified. With a higher cut-off, more cases of colorectal cancer will be missed (false negatives will increase), thus reducing sensitivity.\n\nNegative predictive value\n\nis incorrect because this will decrease with a higher cut-off value. NPV is the probability that a person with a negative test result truly doesn't have the disease. With more false negatives occurring at a higher cut-off, the NPV will decrease as negative results become less reliable at ruling out disease."
