      "id": 384,
      "question": "What is the correct formula to calculate the negative predictive value of a screening test?\n\nTP = true positive; FP = false positive; TN = true negative; FN = false negative",
      "options": [
        "TN / (TN + FN)",
        "TP / (TP + FP)",
        "TN / (TN + FP)",
        "Sensitivity / (1 - specificity)",
        "TP / (TP + FN )"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "TN / (TN + FN)",
      "explanation": "Negative predictive value = TN / (TN + FN)\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nTN / (TN + FN)\n\n. The Negative Predictive Value (NPV) of a screening test is calculated by dividing the number of true negatives (TN) by the sum of true negatives and false negatives (FN). This formula gives us the probability that a person with a negative test result truly does not have the disease.\n\nNow, let's explore why the other options are incorrect.\n\nTP / (TP + FP)\n\nis actually the formula to calculate Positive Predictive Value (PPV). PPV tells us how likely it is that a person who tested positive for a disease actually has the disease. It's calculated by dividing true positives by the sum of true positives and false positives.\n\nTN / (TN + FP)\n\ndoes not correspond to any standard epidemiological measure. It would represent a proportion of correctly identified non-disease cases out of all cases predicted as non-disease, which is not an established or useful metric in medical statistics.\n\nSensitivity / (1 - specificity)\n\nis not related to predictive values at all. Sensitivity refers to how accurately the test identifies those with the disease, while specificity refers to how accurately the test identifies those without the disease. Their relationship does not provide information on predictive values.\n\nFinally,\n\nTP / (TP + FN )\n\nrepresents sensitivity, or true positive rate. This indicates what proportion of actual positives was identified correctly. It doesn't tell us anything about negative predictive value."
