      "correctText": "Hawthorne effect",
      "explanation": "Lead-time bias - occurs when two tests for a disease are compared, the new test diagnoses the disease earlier, but there is no effect on the outcome of the disease\n\nThe scenario above demonstrates an example of\n\nlead-time bias\n\n. This is where two tests for a disease are compared. The newer, 'better' test diagnoses the disease earlier and so patients appear to survive longer. However, there is no actual effect on the outcome of the disease. In this case, regardless of the test used, patients survive for a year after the emergence of symptoms.\n\nThe\n\nHawthorne effect\n\nrefers to a group changing its behaviour due to its knowledge of being studied. This is not being referenced in the scenario above.\n\nLate-look bias\n\nrefers to gathering information at an inappropriate time - for instance, studying a fatal disease many years later, when some of the patients may have died already. The scenario above is an example of lead-time bias, not late-look bias.\n\nPublication bias\n\nis the failure to publish results from valid studies, often when they show negative or uninteresting results. This is not described above.\n\nRecall bias\n\nis the difference in the accuracy of recollections of study participants, often between groups who have a disease and those who do not. For example, in a study of lung cancer, where patients are asked about any historical exposure to asbestos, those with lung cancer may have a better recollection of exposure than those without lung cancer."
