      "id": 391,
      "question": "A 6-year-old boy presents to the GP with recurrent chest infections and poor growth and weight gain.\n\nThe patient is referred by the GP to a paediatric clinic, who order further tests including a genetic test for mutations in the CFTR gene to screen for cystic fibrosis. This confirms a single nucleotide mutation in the CFTR gene resulting in a stop codon in place of arginine.\n\nWhat is the name of this type of mutation?",
      "options": [
        "Frameshift mutation",
        "Missense mutation",
        "Nonsense mutation",
        "Silent mutation",
        "Synonymous mutation"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Nonsense mutation",
      "explanation": "Nonsense - a mutation that results in a stop codon\n\nCystic fibrosis is a genetic condition caused mutations in the CF transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) gene, causing chiefly recurrent respiratory infections, pancreatic insufficiency and diabetes.\n\nIn around 10% of cases it may be caused by a nonsense mutation, whereby a single nucleotide base change means a stop codon is coded in place of an amino acid residue. This can have profound implications on the protein transcription and phenotype of that gene and occurs in approximately 5% of single nucleotide mutations.\n\nA frameshift mutation is an insertion or deletion of a number of nucleotide bases that cause a change in the 3-base reading frame of codons for transcription. For example, if the base C was deleted from the sequence TTCAGAA, the first two codons would change from 'TTC' and 'AGA'  to 'TTA' and 'GAA'. This changes the amino acids from phenylalanine and arginine to leucine and glutamic acid. The changes in amino acids transcribed can have very drastic effects on the subsequent proteins and functions thereof. Duchenne muscular dystrophy is an example of a condition that can be caused by this sort of mutation.\n\nA missense mutation refers to a single base change in a codon that changes the amino acid residue. These occur in 70% of single base mutations. This can have effects on structure and function of the transcribed protein, and cause pathology such as the inactivation of tumour suppressor genes. Conversely, amino acid changes can be neutral and not affect protein function.\n\nA silent mutation describes a single base mutation where the amino acid does not alter despite the altered codon - for example, both codons UGU and UGC code for cysteine. This property of the genetic code that multiple codons can code for the same amino acid is termed 'degeneracy'. This type of mutation is 'silent' when that the same protein is translated and there is no effect on the downstream processing or phenotype of the gene.\n\nA synonymous mutation is a single base change that does not alter the amino acid (as in silent mutations). However, a synonymous mutation does cause other changes in downstream processing (transcription, splicing, translation) or phenotype of the gene. Phenylketonuria and von Hippel-Lindau disease are examples of conditions that can be caused by this type of mutation."
