      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "X-linked dominant",
      "explanation": "Leber's Hereditary Optic Neuropathy: mitochondrial inheritance pattern\n\nMitochondrial\n\nis the correct answer. This patient most likely has Leber's Hereditary Optic Neuropathy (LHON), which is caused by mutations in mitochondrial DNA affecting the respiratory chain, and demonstrates a mitochondrial inheritance pattern. This presents in young adults (20-30 years old) with central scotoma, dyschromatopsia, and rapid visual loss, as seen in this patient. Fundoscopy showing hyperaemic optic discs with peripapillary telangiectasia without disc oedema supports this diagnosis. Besides the patient, only maternal relatives (mother, maternal uncles, and maternal grandmother) are affected, with no evidence of male-to-offspring transmission, consistent with mitochondrial DNA being inherited exclusively from the mother. Since mitochondria are transmitted via oocytes and sperm contributes no mitochondria to a zygote at fertilisation, a mother will pass LHON to all of her children. Affected males do not transmit the disease to offspring. While all children of affected females inherit the mitochondrial mutation, not all will develop the condition, as LHON shows incomplete penetrance, particularly in females. Environmental and genetic factors likely influence whether the disease manifests.\n\nAutosomal dominant\n\nis incorrect as LHON demonstrates mitochondrial inheritance and the family history shows no male-to-offspring transmission. In autosomal dominant disorders, both males and females can transmit the disease, which is not mentioned here. This option could be considered if both maternal and paternal sides showed transmission from affected males and females. An example of this is autosomal dominant optic atrophy (DOA), linked to OPA1 mutations, which typically presents with gradual bilateral vision loss in childhood, disc pallor rather than hyperaemia, and no maternal-only inheritance.\n\nAutosomal recessive\n\nis incorrect as LHON demonstrates mitochondrial inheritance, and all generations are affected in this scenario, making autosomal recessive inheritance unlikely. This could be considered in Wolfram syndrome, which is autosomal recessive. Alongside optic atrophy, it presents with associated with diabetes insipidus, diabetes mellitus, and deafness, which are not seen here.\n\nX-linked dominant\n\nis incorrect as LHON demonstrates mitochondrial inheritance and the family history only shows individuals affected on the mother's side, with no clear pattern of male-to-female transmission. In X-linked dominant disorders, affected males transmit the condition to all their daughters but none of their sons, and affected females can transmit to both sexes, resulting in a characteristic pedigree with sex-specific transmission patterns. An example X-linked dominant disorder that can cause retinal abnormalities is incontinentia pigmenti. However, this primarily affects females and is usually lethal in males, and is associated with skin abnormalities (e.g. blistering and hyperpigmentation in infancy), dental anomalies, alopecia, and central nervous system involvement (e.g. seizures and developmental delay). Fundoscopy would also show retinal ischaemia, neovascularisation, and optic nerve atrophy instead.\n\nX-linked recessive\n\nis incorrect as LHON demonstrates mitochondrial inheritance and both sexes are affected in this patient's family history. X-linked recessive disorders predominantly affect males, and transmission via unaffected carrier females would be expected. In this case, the mother is also affected. An example is choroideremia, which presents with night blindness and peripheral vision loss, not central vision loss with scotomas. Fundoscopy in X-linked recessive conditions like choroideremia shows widespread chorioretinal degeneration, unlike the hyperaemic discs and peripapillary telangiectasia seen here."
