      "explanation": "Interferon-γ is responsible for activating macrophages\n\nInterferon-Î³ is a pro-inflammatory Th1 cytokine that stimulates the activation of macrophages. In the context of an interferon-gamma release assay, quantification of interferon-gamma release by leukocytes in response to an immunological challenge with antigens from\n\nMycobacterium tuberculosis\n\ncan reveal active or latent TB infection. The utility of an IGRA in this patient is that, unlike the tuberculin skin test, it will not yield a positive result if he has in fact received the BCG vaccination, in the absence of natural infection by\n\nMycobacterium tuberculosis\n\n.\n\nBoth interleukin-8 and tumour necrosis factor-α are cytokines produced by macrophages, which promote neutrophil chemotaxis.\n\nEosinophil production is stimulated by the Th2 cytokine interleukin-5, rather than interferon-Î³. Other cytokines stimulating eosinophil production include granulocyte-macrophage colony stimulating factor (GM-CSF) and IL-3, via less specific promotion of granulocyte maturation.\n\nInterferon-Î³ does not directly induce fever. Pyrogenic cytokines include interleukin-1, which is produced by macrophages, and interleukin-6, which is produced by macrophages and Th2 cells.\n\nInterferon-Î³ is in fact a Th1 cytokine (produced by Th1 T cells), which stimulates the differentiation of Th0 cells into Th1 cells in a positive feedback loop."
