      "explanation": "CD15 is found on Reed-Sternberg cells\n\nThe correct answer is CD15. Reed-Sternberg cells are a pathognomonic finding of Hodgkin's lymphoma which express CD15 on their cell surface as well as CD30.\n\nCD4 is associated with T-helper cells, a subtype of lymphocytes that acts as a co-receptor for the T-cell receptor (TCR), assisting in communication with antigen-presenting cells. CD4, with TCR, binds to major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class II, which presents antigens. CD4 is not typically associated with Reed-Sternberg cells.\n\nCD8 is found on cytotoxic T-cells. It acts as a co-receptor for MHC class 1, alongside the TCR. CD8 is not typically associated with Reed-Sternberg cells either.\n\nCD14 is mostly found on macrophages and binds lipopolysaccharide. To a lesser extent, it is also expressed by neutrophils and dendritic cells, not Reed-Sternberg cells.\n\nCD45 is also known as protein tyrosine phosphatase and is present on all leukocytes. It is involved in signalling, regulating a number of cellular processes. It is not characteristic of Reed-Sternberg cells."
