      "explanation": "P value - is the probability of obtaining a result by chance at least as extreme as the one that was actually observed, assuming that the null hypothesis is true\n\nThe researcher is describing the P-value - that is, the probability of obtaining a result by chance at least as extreme as the observed result from the study (assuming that the null hypothesis is true).\n\nThe 'null hypothesis value' is incorrect - the null hypothesis is a statement that is negatively phrased to state that two treatments are equally effective.\n\nPower can be defined as the probability of correctly rejecting the null hypothesis when it is false - that is, the probability of detecting a statistically significant difference.\n\nA type 1 error is the rejection of the null hypothesis when it is actually true - that is, showing a difference between two groups where a difference does not actually exist (a false positive).\n\nA type 2 error is the acceptance of the null hypothesis when it is actually false - failing to spot a difference when there really is one (a false negative)."
