      "explanation": "Fabry disease: X-linked recessive\n\nX-linked recessive\n\nis the correct answer. The diagnosis is Fabry disease. Fabry disease is a rare X-linked recessive disorder of fat metabolism resulting from deficient activity of the lysosomal enzyme alpha-galactosidase. It is a multi-system disease, with full-body or extremity pain (acroparaesthesia) and gastrointestinal pain being common features. It can also result in renal impairment, with proteinuria an early feature. As in this case, it can also cause angiokeratomas and anhidrosis. Treatment strategies are two-pronged. First, to correct the underlying decreased activity of alpha-galactosidase A. Second, to reduce the burden of symptoms.\n\nAutosomal dominant\n\nis incorrect. Fabry disease is inherited in an X-linked recessive manner. You would not expect the condition to skip a generation in autosomal dominant inheritance.\n\nAutosomal recessive\n\nis incorrect. Both altered gene copies are needed to cause the disease in this pattern. There is no evidence of genetic disease on the paternal side.\n\nMitochondrial inheritance\n\nis incorrect. Fabry disease is an X-linked recessive genetic condition. In mitochondrial inheritance, both males and females are affected, and the disease is transmitted through the female to her offspring. If a male has a trait and his spouse does not, their offspring will not be affected.\n\nX-linked dominant\n\nis incorrect. X-linked dominant inheritance is less common than X-linked recessive inheritance (which is the pattern in Fabry disease). X-linked dominant inheritance affects females more than males and results in disease when there is a mutation in just one of the two copies of a gene."
