      "question": "A randomised controlled trial was conducted to assess the efficacy of a new anticoagulant in preventing strokes among high-risk patients. The study enrolled 2,000 participants who were evenly allocated to either a control group receiving standard care or a treatment group receiving the new anticoagulant. After the trial, the incidence of stroke was 15% in the control group and 10% in the treatment group.\n\nWhat is the Number Needed to Treat (NNT) to prevent one additional stroke?",
      "options": [
        "5",
        "10",
        "15",
        "20",
        "25"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "20",
      "explanation": "NNT = 1 / Absolute Risk Reduction\n\n20 is correct\n\nbecause NNT quantifies the number of patients needed to be treated with a specific intervention to achieve one additional positive outcome compared to a control group.\n\nCalculation process: ARR = Risk in the control group (R𝑐) − Risk in the treatment group (R𝑡).\n\nNNT = 1/ARR.\n\nThe Absolute Risk Reduction (ARR) is calculated as:\n\nARR = 𝑅𝑐−𝑅𝑡 = 15%−10% = 5% (0.05).\n\nUsing the formula NNT = 1/ARR:\n\nNNT = 1/0.05 = 20.\n\nEasier way : 100 / (15-10) = 20\n\nThis indicates that 20 patients need to be treated with the anticoagulant to prevent one stroke, aligning with the observed trial data.\n\n5 is incorrect\n\nbecause NNT of 5 implies an Absolute Risk Reduction (ARR) of 20% (0.20), which is much larger than the actual ARR of 5% (0.05) observed in this trial, making this value incorrect.\n\n10 is incorrect\n\nbecause NNT of 10 corresponds to an ARR of 10% (0.10), which is double the actual ARR calculated in this case, leading to an overestimation of treatment effectiveness.\n\n15 is incorrect\n\nbecause NNT of 15 would imply an ARR of approximately 6.7% (0.067), but the ARR in this trial is smaller (5%), making this answer an overestimation of the intervention's benefit.\n\n25 is incorrect\n\nbecause NNT of 25 corresponds to an ARR of 4% (0.04), which underestimates the actual ARR observed in the trial (5%), making this answer incorrect."
