      "question": "A scientist is studying the role of regulatory proteins in intracellular trafficking. He has isolated and identified various intracellular proteins tagged with mannose-6-phosphate.\n\nThese proteins are destined to which of the following organelles?",
      "options": [
        "Lysosome",
        "Smooth endoplasmic reticulum",
        "Rough endoplasmic reticulum",
        "Nucleus",
        "Mitochondria"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Rough endoplasmic reticulum",
      "explanation": "Golgi adds mannose-6-phosphate to proteins for trafficking to lysosomes\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nLysosome\n\n. Mannose-6-phosphate (M6P) is a specific molecular marker that targets proteins to lysosomes. This targeting system involves the addition of M6P residues to proteins in the Golgi apparatus, which are then recognised by mannose-6-phosphate receptors (M6PRs). These receptors facilitate the transport of the tagged proteins from the trans-Golgi network to lysosomes. This system is crucial for the proper functioning of lysosomes, as it ensures that hydrolytic enzymes and other essential lysosomal proteins reach their intended destination.\n\nSmooth endoplasmic reticulum\n\nis incorrect because proteins destined for the smooth endoplasmic reticulum do not require M6P tagging. The smooth ER primarily functions in lipid synthesis and calcium storage, and protein targeting to this organelle involves different mechanisms, typically through specific signal sequences or membrane-spanning domains.\n\nRough endoplasmic reticulum\n\nis incorrect because protein targeting to the RER occurs through an entirely different mechanism involving signal recognition particles (SRP) and signal sequences at the N-terminus of proteins. The RER is where protein synthesis and initial protein processing occurs, but M6P tagging happens later in the secretory pathway.\n\nNucleus\n\nis incorrect because nuclear proteins are targeted through nuclear localisation signals (NLS) that are recognised by importins, not through M6P tagging. The nuclear transport system uses a completely different set of targeting mechanisms involving the nuclear pore complex.\n\nMitochondria\n\nis incorrect because mitochondrial proteins use specific mitochondrial targeting sequences (MTS) that are recognised by the TOM/TIM complex for import. The M6P system is not involved in mitochondrial protein targeting, which requires its own unique protein import machinery due to the organelle's endosymbiotic origin."
