      "explanation": "Vitamin D increases serum calcium primarily through raising its absorption via the small intestine\n\nThis patient is displaying symptoms of osteomalacia. Osteomalacia is a metabolic bone disease characterised by incomplete mineralisation of the underlying mature organic bone matrix following growth plate closure in adults. Treatment would include oral vitamin D supplementation which increases serum calcium levels by\n\nincreasing its absorption via the small intestine\n\n. Calcium is absorbed by active transport (transcellular) and via passive diffusion (paracellularly) across the intestinal mucosa.\n\nIncrease calcium absorption via the large intestine\n\nis incorrect as there is limited absorption of calcium from this part of the bowel.\n\nIncrease calcium absorption via the proximal tubule\n\nis incorrect. This would be the mechanism by which increased parathyroid hormone (PTH) would increase calcium levels, by regulating the absorption of calcium in the kidney by blocking the reabsorption of phosphate in the proximal tubule while promoting calcium reabsorption in the ascending loop of Henle, distal tubule, and collecting tubule.\n\nIncrease calcium absorption via the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle\n\nis incorrect. This would be the mechanism by which parathyroid hormone (PTH) would increase calcium levels. PTH blocks the reabsorption of phosphate in the proximal tubule while promoting calcium reabsorption in the ascending loop of Henle, distal tubule, and collecting tubule.\n\nIncrease osteoclast proliferation and increase bone turnover\n\nis incorrect. RANKL (receptor activator of nuclear factor kappa beta ligand) leads to excessive proliferation and abnormal activation of osteoclasts causing increased calcium."
