      "question": "A gastroenterologist is writing a meta-analysis of randomised controlled trials into the comparative efficacy of octreotide and terlipressin at reducing morbidity associated with upper gastrointestinal haemorrhage.\n\nWhat might they wish to include to highlight any potential publication bias in their analysis?",
      "options": [
        "Blobbogram",
        "Box plot",
        "Forest plot",
        "Funnel plot",
        "Histogram"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Histogram",
      "explanation": "Funnel plots - show publication bias in meta-analyses\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nfunnel plot\n\n. Funnel plots are scatter plots which plot treatment effect size estimated from individual studies (x-axis) against the study size (y-axis). In an unbiased meta-analysis, results from small studies will scatter widely at the bottom of the graph, with the spread narrowing for larger studies. If there is bias, for example, because smaller studies showing no statistically significant effects go unpublished, this leads to an asymmetrical appearance of the funnel plot.\n\nBlobbogram\n\nis incorrect. Blobbograms are used to display the results of all the constituent studies included in a meta-analysis. Conventionally, each study is represented with a data point showing the odds ratio (e.g. of death in placebo vs control) with error bars representing a 95% confidence interval. They do not provide any information on publication bias in terms of which studies were included in a meta-analysis.\n\nBox plot\n\nis incorrect. Box plots are used for the visual representation of quantitative results. They are useful for showing the distribution of data points, such as the mean, range, and interquartile range  of a data set, as well as the presence of positive or negative skew.\n\nForest plot\n\nis incorrect, as this is an alternative term for a blobbogram.\n\nHistogram\n\nis incorrect. Histograms are a form of bar chart which graphically represent the number of a specific type of variable that occurs within a certain range (e.g. the number of myocardial infarctions on the y-axis against patient age by decade on the x-axis)."
