      "correctText": "8",
      "explanation": "NNT = 1 / Absolute Risk Reduction\n\nThe Number Needed to Treat (NNT) is calculated as 1 divided by the Absolute Risk Reduction (ARR). To find the ARR, we need to calculate the event rates in both groups.\n\n12.5\n\nis the correct answer. The Control Event Rate (CER) in the placebo group is 80/500 = 0.16 or 16%. The Experimental Event Rate (EER) in the treatment group is 40/500 = 0.08 or 8%. The Absolute Risk Reduction (ARR) is CER - EER = 0.16 - 0.08 = 0.08 or 8%. Therefore, NNT = 1/ARR = 1/0.08 = 12.5. This means that approximately 13 patients (rounded up to the nearest whole number) would need to be treated with the new antihypertensive medication to prevent one myocardial infarction over 5 years compared to placebo. Understanding NNT is crucial for clinical decision-making as it provides a practical measure of treatment effectiveness.\n\n8\n\nis incorrect. This value does not correspond to the correct calculation of NNT in this scenario. It may result from incorrectly calculating the ARR or misapplying the NNT formula.\n\n10\n\nis incorrect. This would be the NNT if the ARR was 0.1 or 10%, but the actual ARR in this scenario is 0.08 or 8%.\n\n20\n\nis incorrect. This would be the NNT if the ARR was 0.05 or 5%, but the actual ARR in this scenario is 0.08 or 8%.\n\n25\n\nis incorrect. This would be the NNT if the ARR was 0.04 or 4%, but the actual ARR in this scenario is 0.08 or 8%. This value might result from incorrectly calculating the event rates or applying the wrong formula."
