      "explanation": "X-linked conditions: Duchenne/Becker, haemophilia, G6PD\n\nAndrogen insensitivity syndrome\n\nis indeed inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern, making this the correct answer. The condition results from mutations in the androgen receptor gene (AR) located on the X chromosome. Affected individuals have a 46,XY karyotype but are phenotypically female due to complete or partial resistance to androgens. As males have only one X chromosome, they will manifest the condition if they inherit the mutated gene, while females require two copies to be affected (though they can be carriers with one copy).\n\nMyotonic dystrophy\n\nis incorrect as it follows an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern. It is caused by a trinucleotide repeat expansion in the DMPK gene on chromosome 19. The condition shows anticipation, meaning symptoms often become more severe and appear earlier in subsequent generations.\n\nvon Willebrand's disease\n\nis incorrect as it is predominantly inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern, though some variants can be autosomal recessive. It results from mutations in the VWF gene on chromosome 12, affecting the production or function of von Willebrand factor, essential for platelet adhesion and factor VIII stabilisation.\n\nEhlers-Danlos syndrome\n\nis incorrect as it typically shows autosomal dominant inheritance, though some variants can be autosomal recessive. The condition affects collagen synthesis and structure, with various subtypes involving different genes, most commonly those encoding type I, III, and V collagen.\n\nHuntington's disease\n\nis incorrect as it follows an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern. It is caused by a CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the huntingtin gene on chromosome 4. Like myotonic dystrophy, it demonstrates anticipation, with earlier onset in subsequent generations correlating with increasing repeat length."
