      "explanation": "DiGeorge syndrome - a T-cell disorder\n\nThis baby most likely has DiGeorge syndrome, which is also known as 22q11.2 deletion syndrome. This is associated with congenital heart defects, abnormal facies (including cleft lip and/or palate), congenital absence of the thymus gland and hypocalcemia. The mnemonic CATCH22 (cardiac abnormality, abnormal facies, thymic aplasia, cleft palate, hypocalcemia, chromosome 22) is commonly used to remember these clinical features.\n\nDiGeorge syndrome is a primary immunodeficiency characterised by T-cell deficiency as the thymus is critical in the development of the T-cell immune system. This manifests in severe and sometimes unusual viral and fungal infections in early infancy, such as bronchiolitis, diarrhoea, oral candidiasis and\n\nPneumocystis jiroveci\n\npneumonia.\n\nB cell deficiency is incorrect. An example of this disorder is a common variable immune deficiency (CVID), which is associated with a high risk of autoimmune disorders and malignancy, a well as severe bacterial infections.\n\nDefective complement system is incorrect. Conditions associated with this defect include mannose-binding lectin (MBL) deficiency, which is associated with recurrent bacterial infections.\n\nDefective neutrophils is an incorrect option. An example of this defect is a chronic granulomatous disease arising from certain X-linked recessive disorders.\n\nLeucocyte function defect is incorrect. An example of this defect is leucocyte adhesion deficiency, which leads to the inability of neutrophils to migrate to sites of infection and can result in delayed wound healing and deep-seated skin infections."
