      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Calcium ions",
      "explanation": "Troponin T binds to tropomyosin, forming a troponin-tropomyosin complex\n\nThis patient has presented with an ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI). Diagnosis of this cardiac condition is confirmed via ECG changes and raised serum cardiac biomarkers. Specific troponin levels can be used as biomarkers to confirm the presence of cardiac tissue damage, one of which is troponin T. This biomarker binds to\n\ntropomyosin\n\nforming the troponin-tropomyosin complex which regulates muscle contraction. As with the other troponin subunits (troponin I and troponin C), troponin T is released in cardiac cell damage (e.g. in acute coronary syndrome) although it is considered less sensitive and specific than troponin I in the diagnosis of myocardial infarction.\n\nActin\n\nholds the troponin-tropomyosin complex in place and is bound to by the cardiac biomarker troponin I, not troponin T. Troponin I is generally considered the most sensitive and specific biomarker when used for acute coronary syndrome (ACS) diagnosis and therefore its use is more widespread.\n\nCalcium ions\n\nare bound to by the third troponin subunit troponin C. This plays the main role in Ca\n\n2+\n\n-dependent regulation of muscle contraction. As one of the troponin subunits, it is released in cardiac cell damage and therefore can be used for ACS diagnosis however as troponin C is also found in skeletal muscle (unlike troponin I and T), it is less specific.\n\nMyoglobin\n\nis an oxygen-binding protein, similar to haemoglobin, found in cardiac and skeletal muscle cells. ACS can result in a raised myoglobin levels as it is released from damaged cells, but it has poor specificity for cardiac tissue injury and is not involved in the troponin-tropomyosin complex.\n\nTroponin I\n\nis one of the subunits of troponin which binds to actin. It is generally considered the most specific and sensitive of the three subunits for ACS diagnosis. Troponin I and troponin T do not bind with one another."
