      "correctText": "C1-INH",
      "explanation": "C5-9 deficiency predisposes to\n\nThis patient's presentation aligns with meningitis, and the isolation of a gram-negative bacterium with a polysaccharide capsule strongly indicates\n\nNeisseria meningitidis\n\nas the likely cause.\n\nC5-9\n\nare components responsible for encoding the membrane attack complex (MAC), which, with the help of the complement system, disrupts pathogen cell membranes. Consequently, individuals with deficiencies in C5-9 complement components are at an increased risk of infections, particularly those caused by\n\nNeisseria meningitidis\n\n.\n\nC1 inhibitor, abbreviated as\n\nC1-INH\n\n, plays a pivotal role in inhibiting the complement system, preventing its spontaneous activation. Deficiencies in C1-INH are closely linked to hereditary angioedema, believed to result from uncontrolled bradykinin release.\n\nDeficiencies in complement components\n\nC1q, C1rs, C2, and C4\n\ncan predispose patients to immune complex diseases like systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). These components play key roles in clearing immune complexes, and their deficiencies lead to the accumulation of these complexes, contributing to the autoimmune responses and inflammation characteristic of SLE.\n\nC5\n\nis another crucial component of the complement system, with roles in chemotaxis and the formation of the membrane attack complex (MAC), responsible for disrupting foreign cells. C5 deficiency is associated with Leiner disease, a disorder characterised by severe seborrheic dermatitis, chronic diarrhoea, and recurrent local and systemic infections starting in childhood.\n\nDeficiency in complement factor H-related protein 1\n\nCFHR1\n\ncan disrupt the regulation of the complement system. This disruption may lead to uncontrolled complement activation, contributing to conditions like atypical haemolytic uraemic syndrome (HUS), which is characterised by kidney damage and clotting disorders due to excessive complement activity."
