      "correctText": "Pulmonary artery vasoconstriction",
      "explanation": "Pulmonary arteries vasoconstrict in the presence of hypoxia\n\nIn contrast to the systemic circulation, pulmonary arteries constrict in response to hypoxia, diverting blood away from poorly oxygenated lung regions. This maximises blood flow to well-oxygenated regions of the lung. In patients that remain hypoxic despite optimal mechanical ventilation, inhaled nitric oxide can be used to induce pulmonary vasodilation to reverse this physiological response.\n\nIncreased tidal volume with decreased respiratory rate\n\nis incorrect. Although an increase in tidal volumes is a physiological response to hypoxia, this would typically be accompanied by an increase in respiratory rate.\n\nPulmonary artery vasodilation\n\nis incorrect; hypoxia induces vasoconstriction in the pulmonary vasculature as explained above.\n\nReduced tidal volume with increased respiratory rate is\n\nincorrect. Respiratory rate, although not a direct physiological response to hypoxia, will likely increase. However, tidal volumes will increase in response to hypoxia.\n\nSystemic vasculature, unlike the pulmonary vessels, vasodilates in response to hypoxia."
