      "id": 460,
      "question": "A 19-year-old man presents with acute, painless bilateral vision loss over 2 weeks. He reports a central scotoma but no eye pain. His mother and a maternal uncle experienced similar acute visual loss in early adulthood.\n\nFundoscopy reveals temporal pallor of the optic discs. Genetic testing is planned.\n\nWhich inheritance pattern is most likely responsible for this condition?",
      "options": [
        "Autosomal dominant",
        "Autosomal recessive",
        "Mitochondrial",
        "X-linked dominant",
        "X-linked recessive"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Mitochondrial",
      "explanation": "Leber's Hereditary Optic Neuropathy: mitochondrial inheritance pattern\n\nMitochondrial\n\nis correct, as Leber’s hereditary optic neuropathy (LHON) is caused by mutations in mitochondrial DNA, which is inherited exclusively maternally. This patient’s presentation with acute bilateral vision loss in a young male with a family history of similar symptoms is classic for LHON. Mitochondrial inheritance explains why the condition is transmitted through the mother (as sperm do not contribute mitochondria to the zygote) and why males are more frequently affected, despite the non-X-linked pattern.\n\nAutosomal dominant\n\nis incorrect, as autosomal dominant conditions (e.g., Huntington’s disease) affect both sexes equally and typically show male-to-male transmission, which is absent here.\n\nAutosomal recessive\n\nis incorrect, as autosomal recessive disorders (e.g., cystic fibrosis) require mutations in both alleles, usually with unaffected parents. The pattern of inheritance suggested by multiple affected relatives across generations makes this unlikely.\n\nX-linked dominant\n\nis incorrect, as X-linked dominant conditions (e.g., fragile X syndrome) rarely affect males more severely, as males inherit only one X chromosome. LHON’s male predominance is unrelated to X-linked mechanisms.\n\nX-linked recessive\n\nis incorrect, as X-linked recessive disorders (e.g., Duchenne muscular dystrophy) predominantly affect males, but carrier females are usually asymptomatic. Here, the presence of affected individuals across generations without a male-limited pattern makes X-linked recessive inheritance unlikely."
