      "explanation": "Endothelin causes pulmonary vasoconstriction (hence why antagonists are used in primary pulmonary hypertension)\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nendothelin\n\n. The disease described here is pulmonary hypertension, evidenced by progressive dyspnoea in a woman between the ages of 20 and 50, as well as the examination and imaging findings. Of the options listed above, endothelin is the only pulmonary vasoconstrictor.\n\nBradykinin\n\nis a peptide that causes indirect dilatation of arteries, not constriction - it promotes the release of nitric oxide and prostacyclin.\n\nIloprost\n\nis a synthetic form of prostacyclin. It is used in the treatment of pulmonary hypertension, acting as a potent vasodilator, not a vasoconstrictor.\n\nNitric oxide\n\nis a naturally-occurring vasodilator. It is therefore not the correct answer.\n\nProstacyclin\n\nis a naturally-occurring vasodilator. Synthetic forms, such as iloprost, are commonly used in the management of this condition and others requiring vasodilation, such as systemic sclerosis."
