      "correctText": "C5",
      "explanation": "C5-9 deficiency predisposes to\n\nThe symptoms being described here are those of infection with\n\nNeisseria meningitidis\n\n- meningitis, fever, petechial rash and signs of sepsis. This requires urgent treatment. Patients with C5-C9 deficiency are predisposed to infection with this organism.\n\nA deficiency in C1-inhibitor protein (C1-INH) causes hereditary angioedema, which would present with swelling, often of facial soft tissues and the lips. C1-INH deficiency is not linked to a predisposition to infection with\n\nNeisseria meningitidis\n\n.\n\nA deficiency of C1q, C1rs, C2 and C4 - the classical pathway components - typically predisposes patients to immune complex disease, not the infection seen here. Examples include systemic lupus erythematosus and Henoch-Schonlein purpura.\n\nA C3 deficiency may predispose patients to recurrent bacterial infections. Although\n\nNeisseria meningitidis\n\nis indeed a bacterium, it specifically is more typically associated with C5-9 deficiency.\n\nC5 deficiency alone (rather than C5-9 deficiency) is known to predispose to Leiner disease, symptoms of which include recurrent diarrhoea, wasting and seborrhoeic dermatitis."
