      "id": 474,
      "question": "Which one of the following best describes the characteristics of a negatively skewed distribution?",
      "options": [
        "Median < mode < mean",
        "Mean < median < mode",
        "Mode < mean < median",
        "Median < mean < mode",
        "Mean < mode < median"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Median < mean < mode",
      "explanation": "Skewed distributions\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nMean < median < mode\n\n. In a negatively skewed distribution, the tail on the left side of the distribution is longer or fatter than the right side. As a result, more values are concentrated on the right side of the axis, and this causes the mean to be less than the median and mode. The mean is influenced by outliers and skewed data. Since in a negatively skewed distribution, there are more low scores, these pull down the mean score. The median (middle value) and mode (most frequent value) remain less affected, hence they are greater than the mean.\n\nLet's now discuss why each of the other options is incorrect:\n\nMedian < mode < mean\n\n: This pattern describes a positively skewed distribution rather than a negative one. In a positively skewed distribution, there are more low scores with few high scores pulling up both the mean and mode.\n\nMode < mean < median\n\n: This option does not represent any typical skewness pattern in statistical distributions. Normally in both positive and negative skewness, mode tends to be at one end of the scale followed by either mean or median depending upon the direction of skewness.\n\nMedian < mean < mode\n\n: Similar to above, this pattern does not follow any recognised skewness arrangement as usually in both types of skewness patterns (positive or negative), the mode is at one extreme followed by either mean or median.\n\nMean < mode < median\n\n: This arrangement also doesn't fit into any standard skewness patterns. In both positive and negative skewness, it is common for the mode to be at one extreme followed by either the mean or median based on whether it's positively or negatively skewed."
