      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Rheumatoid arthritis",
      "explanation": "The HLA-DRB1 gene is the major genetic susceptibility locus for rheumatoid arthritis (DRB1*04:01 and DRB1*04:04 hence the association with DR4)\n\nThis scenario depicts very non-specific arthralgia with associated mild rises in inflammatory markers. These could be accounted for by all of the above five options. The important element of the question is that the patient is positive for HLA-DRB1. HLA-DRB1, and its association to HLA-DR4, is a genetic susceptibility locus in\n\nrheumatoid arthritis\n\n, making this the correct answer.\n\nAnkylosing spondylitis\n\nis the most common form of spondyloarthropathy and is associated with HAL-B27. It results in chronic inflammation leading to fusion of the spine. Patients commonly describe symptoms consistent with inflammatory back pain, such as early morning pain and stiffness. Approximately 1/3 of patients develop peripheral arthritis.\n\nBehcet's disease\n\nis a multisystem disorder. It is commonly associated with oral and genital ulceration, however, patients can develop arthritis, erythema nodosum, and uveitis, as well as varying cardiovascular, gastrointestinal, and neurological manifestations. It is associated with HLA-B51.\n\nReactive arthritis\n\nis a seronegative spondyloarthritis occurring after recent infection, most commonly urogenital or gastrointestinal. Reactive arthritis, as with ankylosing spondylitis, is related to HLA-B27. Reiter's syndrome is the combination of reactive arthritis, acute uveitis, and large joint oligoarthritis.\n\nSjogren's syndrome\n\nis related to HLA-DR3, and it is the result of immune-mediated lymphocytic infiltration of exocrine glands. Typically patients describe dry eyes and mouth along with fatigue and arthralgia."
