      "explanation": "Renal stones are most commonly composed of calcium oxalate\n\nThis patient has presented with renal stones causing the significant complication of hydronephrosis. Depending on their size, stones can normally be passed by patients however, if they are too large they can become stuck, resulting in significant backpressure and dilation of the renal nephrons; these stones require intervention to break up/remove them. Renal stones can be made up of several different materials but the commonest stones are composed of\n\ncalcium oxalate\n\naccount for approximately 85% of cases. Risk factors for these stones include any condition resulting in hypercalciuria and/or hyperoxaluria.\n\nCalcium citrate\n\nis used to prevent calcium oxalate renal stones as it can bind to oxalate in the gastrointestinal tract and increases renal urinary excretion of citrate, reducing the risk of calcium stone formation. Renal stones are not composed of calcium citrate.\n\nApproximately 10% of renal stones are composed of\n\ncalcium phosphate\n\n. They are more common in patients with renal tubular acidosis resulting in an increased supersaturation of urine with calcium and phosphate.\n\nStruvite stones are composed of\n\nmagnesium, ammonium and phosphate\n\nand are a result of urease producing bacteria. These account for somewhere between 2% and 20% of stones and again are much less common than those composed of calcium oxalate.\n\nRenal stones can be composed of the purine metabolism product\n\nuric acid\n\nbut this only account for approximately 10% of stones. Conditions that increase uric acid such as tissue breakdown (malignancy, tumour lysis syndrome etc) can result in an increased risk of these stones however they remain far less common than calcium oxalate stones."
