      "explanation": "P value - is the probability of obtaining a result by chance at least as extreme as the one that was actually observed, assuming that the null hypothesis is true\n\nThe correct answer is 'assuming that the null hypothesis is true, the probability of obtaining a result at least as extreme as the one observed is 0.01'.\n\n'Assuming that the null hypothesis is false, the probability of obtaining a result at least as extreme as the one observed is 0.01' is incorrect as the interpretation of a P value is based on the assumption that the null hypothesis is true.\n\n'The probability of a type I error is 0.01' is incorrect. A common misconception is that a P value is the probability of incorrectly rejecting the null hypothesis when it is true (a type I error). This is a description of alpha (α), which is set by researchers and is often 0.05.\n\n'The probability of a type II error is 0.01' is incorrect. This is known as β. This is related to the power of a statistical test, where power = 1-β.\n\n'There is no difference in mortality between the experimental groups' is incorrect. This is restating the null hypothesis, and this p value cannot be used to positively support this statement. A null hypothesis cannot technically be proved (only disproved), and regardless this p value lends support to rejection of the null hypothesis rather than its acceptance."
