      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Type IV reaction",
      "explanation": "Type I hypersensitivity reaction - anaphylaxis\n\nThe correct answer is a\n\nType I reaction\n\n. This is because the clinical presentation described in the question - facial swelling, erythematous itchy rash, expiratory wheeze, and hypotension - is consistent with an anaphylactic reaction. Anaphylaxis is a severe, life-threatening hypersensitivity reaction that occurs rapidly after exposure to an allergen. In this case, the allergen would be the HPV vaccine. Type I hypersensitivity reactions are mediated by immunoglobulin E (IgE) antibodies, which bind to mast cells and basophils causing their activation and subsequent release of inflammatory mediators such as histamine.\n\nA\n\nType II reaction\n\nis incorrect because these reactions are characterized by cytotoxic antibody-mediated responses involving IgG or IgM antibodies targeting antigens on cell surfaces or extracellular matrix components. This leads to cellular destruction through complement activation or antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC). Examples of Type II reactions include hemolytic transfusion reactions and autoimmune hemolytic anemia.\n\nA\n\nType III reaction\n\nis also incorrect as these reactions involve immune complex formation between antigens and IgG or IgM antibodies. These immune complexes deposit in tissues leading to inflammation and tissue damage due to complement activation and neutrophil recruitment. Examples of Type III reactions include serum sickness and certain forms of vasculitis, like systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).\n\nType IV reaction\n\nis not the correct answer because these are delayed-type hypersensitivity reactions mediated by T lymphocytes rather than antibodies. They involve antigen-presenting cells activating CD4+ helper T cells, which subsequently activate macrophages or CD8+ cytotoxic T cells that cause tissue damage. Examples of Type IV reactions include contact dermatitis (e.g., poison ivy rash) and tuberculin skin test reactivity."
