      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Achondroplasia",
      "explanation": "Autosomal recessive conditions are 'metabolic'  - exceptions: inherited ataxias\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nAlbinism\n\n. Albinism is not an autosomal dominant condition, but rather it is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner. This means that both parents must carry one copy of the mutated gene for a child to be affected. Albinism results from mutations in several different genes, most commonly the TYR gene, which leads to a reduction or absence of melanin production.\n\nNow let's discuss each of the incorrect options:\n\nRetinoblastoma\n\nis indeed an autosomal dominant condition. It arises due to mutations in the RB1 gene located on chromosome 13. The disease manifests as a malignant tumour of the retina usually affecting children under five years old. However, it should be noted that although Retinoblastoma follows an autosomal dominant pattern of inheritance, two hits (both alleles being affected) are necessary for tumour development according to Knudson's 'two-hit' hypothesis.\n\nNext,\n\nTuberous Sclerosis\n\n, also known as Tuberose Sclerosis, is another autosomal dominant disorder. It results from mutations in either TSC1 or TSC2 genes leading to the formation of hamartomas (benign growths) in various organs such as the brain, skin, kidneys and heart.\n\nAchondroplasia\n\ntoo follows an autosomal dominant pattern of inheritance. It results from mutations in the FGFR3 gene leading to abnormal cartilage formation and consequent dwarfism with characteristic features including short limbs and a relatively normal-sized trunk.\n\nFinally,\n\nMyotonic dystrophy\n\nis also an autosomal dominant condition caused by a CTG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the DMPK gene on chromosome 19. It manifests with muscle stiffness (myotonia), progressive muscle weakness and wasting. In addition to these muscular symptoms, individuals with myotonic dystrophy may also present with cataracts, cardiac conduction defects and endocrine disturbances."
