      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "It acts on target cells by stimulating guanylate cyclase",
      "explanation": "Endothelin causes pulmonary vasoconstriction (hence why antagonists are used in primary pulmonary hypertension)\n\nEndothelin antagonists are useful in primary pulmonary hypertension\n\nis the correct answer. Endothelin-1 is a potent vasoconstrictor peptide produced primarily by vascular endothelial cells. In primary pulmonary hypertension (PPH), also known as idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension, there is increased endothelin-1 expression and activity, contributing to the pathological vasoconstriction and vascular remodelling characteristic of this condition. Endothelin receptor antagonists (ERAs) such as bosentan, ambrisentan, and macitentan block the action of endothelin-1 on its receptors (primarily ETA and ETB), reducing pulmonary vascular resistance and improving exercise capacity and clinical outcomes in patients with PPH. These medications are recommended in UK and international guidelines for the management of pulmonary arterial hypertension and have been shown to delay clinical worsening and improve functional capacity.\n\nIt is a potent vasodilator\n\nis incorrect. Endothelin-1 is actually one of the most potent vasoconstrictors known, with approximately 10 times the vasoconstrictor potency of angiotensin II. It causes sustained vasoconstriction by binding to specific G-protein coupled receptors (ETA and ETB) on vascular smooth muscle cells, leading to increased intracellular calcium and subsequent smooth muscle contraction. This vasoconstrictor effect is particularly important in the pulmonary and renal circulations.\n\nIt is produced mainly by pulmonary tissue\n\nis incorrect. While endothelin-1 is produced in the pulmonary vasculature, it is primarily synthesised by vascular endothelial cells throughout the body, not specifically or predominantly by pulmonary tissue. Endothelial cells in various vascular beds, including systemic arteries, veins, and lymphatics, all produce endothelin-1. The peptide acts locally in a paracrine or autocrine fashion to regulate vascular tone.\n\nIt acts on target cells by stimulating guanylate cyclase\n\nis incorrect. Endothelin-1 acts by binding to specific G-protein coupled receptors (ETA and ETB), which activate phospholipase C, leading to the formation of inositol triphosphate (IP3) and diacylglycerol (DAG). This results in increased intracellular calcium and activation of protein kinase C, ultimately causing vasoconstriction. Stimulation of guanylate cyclase is actually the mechanism of action for nitric oxide, which produces vasodilation through the formation of cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP).\n\nRelease is stimulated by nitric oxide\n\nis incorrect. Nitric oxide actually inhibits the production and release of endothelin-1 from endothelial cells. These two mediators have opposing actions in vascular regulation: nitric oxide is a vasodilator while endothelin-1 is a vasoconstrictor. The release of endothelin-1 is stimulated by various factors including hypoxia, shear stress, thrombin, angiotensin II, inflammatory cytokines, and oxidised LDL cholesterol, but is inhibited by nitric oxide and prostacyclin."
