      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Ascending loop of Henle",
      "explanation": "Phosphate is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule\n\nA history of lead poisoning complicated by renal tubular acidosis should point towards acquired Fanconi syndrome. Fanconi syndrome is a disorder of inadequate reabsorption in the\n\nproximal convoluted tubule\n\ns of the kidneys. This commonly presents with hypophosphataemia as well as hyperchloraemia metabolic acidosis, hypokalaemia, and hypocalcaemia.\n\nThe\n\ncollecting duct\n\nsystem of the kidneys is the final portion of the nephron and is important for water and sodium reabsorption. They are the site of action for antidiuretic hormone.\n\nThe\n\nascending loop of Henle\n\nis commonly split into thin and thick sections. The thin ascending loop is impermeable to water but allows small passages of ions such as sodium through osmotic pressures. The thick ascending loop's job is to reabsorb ions including sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium, and chloride. A defect in the thick ascending loop is the cause of Bartter syndrome.\n\nThe main action of the\n\ndescending loop of Henle\n\nis to reabsorb water. Whilst doing this, it also absorbs small amounts of urea and electrolytes.\n\nThe\n\ndistal convoluted tubule\n\nplays an important part in sodium, potassium and calcium regulation. It is known as the area of action of thiazide diuretics and is the area of error in Gitelman syndrome."
