      "correctText": "HLA-DR4",
      "explanation": "Reactive arthritis - HLA-B27\n\nHLA-B27\n\nis correct. The patient presents with lower limb oligoarthritis after recent unprotected sex. The probable diagnosis is reactive arthritis secondary to a sexually transmitted infection. Reactive arthritis is more common in those patients that are HLA-B27 positive. It is typically a self-limiting condition but can develop into chronic arthritis in a smaller proportion of cases.\n\nHLA-B51\n\nis incorrect. This is expected HLA association for Behcet's disease. Patients with Behcet's tend to have recurring and severe oral or genital ulceration rather than just the 'occasional mouth ulcer', which is common in the general population. Other features of this condition are rashes, thrombosis, and ocular abnormalities.\n\nHLA-DR3\n\nis incorrect. Sjogren's syndrome is associated with HLA-DR3. This can cause arthritis but would typically cause small joint polyarthritis. Additionally, there may be Sicca symptoms i.e. dry eyes and dry mouth.\n\nHLA-DR4\n\nis incorrect. This is the expected HLA association with rheumatoid arthritis. This tends to present with more of a small joint polyarthritis rather than a large joint lower limb oligoarthritis.\n\nHLA-DQ2\n\nis incorrect. This is associated with coeliac disease. However, there is no mention of intolerance to gluten or abdominal symptoms. Coeliac disease can cause arthritis but in the context of this presentation, it seems unlikely."
