      "correctText": "NK cells",
      "explanation": "B cells mediate hyperacute organ rejection\n\nThe patient is describing hyperacute organ rejection. This occurs due to a type II hypersensitivity reaction and results in thrombosis and occlusion of the graft vessel. Pre-formed antibodies in the recipient's circulation (formed through previous organ transplantation, pregnancy or blood transfusions) recognise antigens on the endothelial lining of the donor organ; this triggers rapid complement system activation, thrombosis of vessels and organ necrosis. The transplanted organ must be removed immediately. It is most commonly due to ABO incompatibility and is very rare.\n\nB cells\n\nmediate hyperacute organ rejection.\n\nT cytotoxic\n\nand\n\nT helper cells\n\nmediate acute and chronic organ rejection, which occurs over weeks-months and months-years respectively.\n\nNK cells\n\nare implicated in priming the immune system following organ transplantation. Depending on the NK subsets activated, they either promote the activation/expansion of pro-inflammatory Th1 cells or regulatory Th2/T-regulatory cells thus tilting the balance of alloimmunity towards rejection or tolerance. They are not directly implicated in hyperacute organ rejection.\n\nMast cells\n\nare implicated in wound healing, defence against pathogens and allergic reactions. When activated, they degranulate, releasing heparin and histamine. This causes vasodilatation and inflammation. Mast cells are not implicated in hyperacute organ rejection."
