      "explanation": "Sepsis triggers the release of IL-1 causing vasodilation → hypotension\n\nIncrease production of IL-1\n\nis correct. The patient has a probable urinary tract infection and has developed hypotension in the context of sepsis. Hypotension in sepsis is predominantly caused by circulatory failure due to vasodilation. Sepsis triggers the release of interleukin-1 (IL-1) causing this vasodilation. Typically, patients will feel peripherally warm in the early stages due to this vasodilation.\n\nGlucocorticoid deficiency\n\nis incorrect. This is the mechanism by which hypotension develops in Addison's disease. There is no suggestion that this patient has underlying Addison's. Patients with severe sepsis secondary to meningococcal septicaemia (classically) can develop Addison's after adrenal hypoperfusion.\n\nImpaired contractility\n\nis incorrect. While there may be an element of impaired contractility in sepsis (i.e. septic cardiomyopathy), the principal cause of hypotension is vasodilation. This would be the major cause of hypotension in cardiogenic shock i.e. post-myocardial infarction.\n\nIncreased production of IL-6\n\nis incorrect. IL-6 is a major pro-inflammatory mediator in septic shock and is implicated in fever production in sepsis. However, it is IL-1 that principally causes vasodilation and this cytokine is responsible for hypotension in this circumstance.\n\nIncreased production of endothelin-1\n\nis incorrect. Endothelin-1 rises in the setting of sepsis as it is a pro-inflammatory cytokine. However, its effect on the vasculature is that of vasoconstriction rather than vasodilation and is therefore not the cause of hypotension."
