      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "1 in 2",
      "explanation": "X-linked recessive conditions - no male-to-male transmission\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nNo increased risk\n\n. Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency is an X-linked recessive disorder. This means that the disease occurs in males who have inherited the defective gene from their mothers. Females can also be affected if they inherit two copies of the defective gene, one from each parent. However, they are usually carriers and do not show symptoms unless both copies of the gene are defective. In this case, as the father has G6PD deficiency, he will pass on his Y chromosome to his son and not the X chromosome which carries the G6PD deficient gene. Therefore, there is no increased risk for his son to develop G6PD deficiency due to inheritance from him.\n\nDiscussing other options:\n\nThe option\n\n2 in 3\n\nis incorrect because it does not reflect any known inheritance pattern for G6PD deficiency or any other genetic disorder.\n\nThe option stating that the child\n\nWill definitely be affected\n\nis also incorrect. As mentioned earlier, since G6PD deficiency is an X-linked recessive disorder, a male child inherits his X chromosome from his mother and Y chromosome from his father. Therefore, if only the father has G6PD deficiency, there's no chance that he could pass this condition onto his son.\n\nThe option\n\n1 in 2\n\nwould be correct if we were discussing autosomal dominant or autosomal recessive disorders where each offspring has a 50% chance of inheriting a single-gene disorder from an affected parent but this isn't applicable here.\n\nLastly, the option\n\n1 in 4\n\n, while reflecting Mendelian genetics for autosomal recessive disorders where both parents are carriers of a disease-causing mutation, does not apply to X-linked disorders like G6PD deficiency."
