      "question": "A 45-year-old man presents to his GP with a new, intensely itchy rash that began over a week ago. He has a history of hypertension and commenced treatment with indapamide three months ago. His home-monitored blood pressure readings average around 155/90 mmHg, and he has had previous drug reactions to ACE inhibitors and calcium channel blockers.\n\nOn examination, there is a papular, violaceous rash on the bilateral flexor surfaces of his arms. The rash is polygonal in shape and features a white line-like pattern overlaying its surface.\n\nGiven the likely diagnosis, what other feature may also be seen?",
      "options": [
        "Altered sensation over lesions",
        "Hypopigmented, pink or brown lesions",
        "Itchy white spots on vulva",
        "Lesions run parallel to the line of Langer",
        "Oral involvement"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Itchy white spots on vulva",
      "explanation": "Lichen\n\nThe correct answer is\n\noral involvement\n\n. The most probable diagnosis in this instance is lichen planus, which is typically associated with an itchy rash forming polygonal papules, often found on the flexural aspects of the limbs. A hallmark of this condition includes Wickham's striae—fine white lines visible on the surface of the papules. In this patient, the recent initiation of a thiazide-like diuretic (indapamide) may have triggered the onset of this condition. Oral manifestations of lichen planus occur in approximately 50% of cases and present similarly with Wickham's striae, appearing as a white lace-like pattern on the buccal mucosa.\n\nAltered sensation over lesions\n\nis incorrect. Altered sensation often indicates skin carcinoma, where lesions are typically solitary and exhibit rapid growth in size and shape, along with irregularity. Lesions may also show variable pigmentation, and patients frequently report risk factors for skin cancer development such as extensive sun exposure and Fitzpatrick skin type I. However, the clinical description provided—a violaceous, polygonal rash—is more indicative of lichen planus than suggestive of skin cancer.\n\nHypopigmented, pink or brown lesions\n\nis incorrect. Such lesions are characteristic of fungal conditions like pityriasis versicolor, caused by Malassezia furfur infection leading to superficial fungal colonisation of the skin. Although pruritus can be a symptom and scaling patches can appear in tinea infections, these typically present with hypopigmentation rather than a violaceous colouration and do not match the pattern seen in this patient's clinical presentation; thus making pityriasis versicolor an unlikely diagnosis in this scenario.\n\nItchy white spots on vulva\n\nis incorrect. These are indicative of lichen sclerosus. While lichen sclerosus should be considered within the differential diagnosis owing to its inflammatory nature, it predominantly affects postmenopausal women and primarily involves the anogenital area. It presents as white patches that can lead to scarring. Despite both lichen sclerosus and lichen planus potentially being treated with topical corticosteroids, accurate differentiation between them is essential because lichen sclerosus has a higher risk for malignant transformation into vulval carcinoma.\n\nLesions run parallel to the line of Langer\n\nis incorrect. This feature characterises pityriasis rosea which usually starts with a single 'herald' patch before developing secondary erythematous oval lesions that display scaling margins aligned with skin cleavage lines—such distribution contradicts the presentation described in this case study."
