      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Hydroxychloroquine",
      "explanation": "Livedo reticularis can be caused by SLE\n\nThe rash described here is suggestive of livedo reticularis. This is a relatively common skin finding with the appearance of a mottled, reticulated, 'net-like' pattern. It typically affects the lower limbs. It is classically aggravated upon exposure to cold. Numerous conditions and medications may trigger the rash. Of the options listed, the most strongly associated culprit is\n\nsystemic lupus erythematosus\n\n(SLE).\n\nAzathioprine\n\nis incorrect. In this context, it may be used for the patient's Crohn's disease or for the SLE. It is not known to trigger livedo reticularis. Some case reports demonstrate success with using it to actually treat severe forms of the rash.\n\nCoeliac disease\n\nis incorrect. This is not typically associated with livedo reticularis. The classic skin rash associated with coeliac disease is dermatitis herpetiformis, which instead appears as intensely itchy papules and vesicles on an erythematous base, typically over the buttocks, elbows and knees.\n\nCrohn's disease\n\nis incorrect. This is not usually associated with livedo reticularis. Crohn's disease is classically associated with erythema nodosum, which appears as tender, firm, subcutaneous nodules, usually over the shins.\n\nHydroxychloroquine\n\nis incorrect. This is used in the management of SLE. It is not associated with livedo reticularis."
