      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Mutation in the MC1R gene",
      "explanation": "Melanoma: the invasion depth of the tumour is the single most important prognostic factor\n\nThe correct answer is the\n\nDepth of melanoma\n\n. In malignant melanoma, the most important prognostic marker is the Breslow thickness, which measures the depth of invasion of the melanoma into the skin. This measurement directly correlates with the risk of metastasis; deeper tumours are more likely to have spread to lymph nodes or distant organs. According to UK guidelines, any melanoma thicker than 1mm requires a sentinel lymph node biopsy to assess for nodal involvement.\n\nThe\n\nNumber of episodes of sunburn before the age of 18 years\n\nis incorrect because while it is true that sunburns in childhood increase the risk for developing malignant melanoma later in life, it does not carry prognostic information once a diagnosis has been made. The severity and prognosis of a diagnosed melanoma are not influenced by past sunburns.\n\nAge of patient\n\nis also incorrect as an answer choice. While age can influence survival rates in many types of cancer, it is not considered as significant a prognostic factor in malignant melanoma as Breslow thickness. However, older patients may have other comorbidities that could indirectly affect their prognosis.\n\nThe\n\nDiameter of melanoma\n\noption is misleading. While larger diameter may suggest advanced disease, it's not as accurate or reliable as Breslow thickness in predicting prognosis. A small but deep melanoma may be more dangerous than a large but shallow one.\n\nFinally, the presence or absence of a\n\nMutation in the MC1R gene\n\ncan impact an individual's risk for developing malignant melanoma but does not provide prognostic information once a diagnosis has been established. Hence, this answer choice would also be incorrect."
