      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Pyoderma gangrenosum",
      "explanation": "Grave's disease, orange peel shin lesions - pretibial myxoedema\n\nThe correct answer to this question is\n\nPretibial myxoedema\n\n. This condition is a dermatological manifestation of Graves' disease, an autoimmune thyroid disorder. It presents as non-pitting, symmetrical, erythematous and waxy skin lesions with an 'orange peel' texture located over the shins. The other options provided are incorrect for the following reasons:\n\nPyoderma gangrenosum\n\nis a rare, painful skin condition that causes large ulcers, typically on the legs. These ulcers often begin as small bumps or blisters but can rapidly develop into painful ulcers with a purplish border and may be accompanied by systemic symptoms such as fever and malaise.\n\nNecrobiosis lipoidica diabeticorum\n\n, although it does affect the shins and can occur in patients with diabetes mellitus (which could potentially overlap with those suffering from thyroid disorders), presents differently. It usually begins as shiny red-brown patches that slowly grow into larger plaques with a yellowish centre and atrophic, telangiectatic surface.\n\nErythema nodosum\n\nis characterised by tender red or purple nodules that most commonly appear on the shins. However, unlike pretibial myxoedema, these lesions are not waxy or orange-peel like in appearance.\n\nFinally,\n\nSyphilis\n\n, while it can present with various skin manifestations depending on its stage (primary syphilis presents with a painless chancre; secondary syphilis may present with a rash involving the palms and soles; tertiary syphilis may cause gummas), none of these presentations match the description given in the question."
