      "explanation": "A non-healing painless ulcer associated with a chronic scar is indicative of squamous cell carcinoma (SCC)\n\nThis patient's lesion is suspicious of a Marjolin ulcer. This is the development of a cutaneous\n\nsquamous cell carcinoma\n\n(SCC) at the site of an old scar. Typically, burn scars are involved, and the SCC develops some 20 to 30 years after the original injury. The mechanism of this is not completely understood. Marjolin ulcers typically present as non-healing sores and often bleed and discharge pus. This presentation warrants urgent referral for further investigation.\n\nAn\n\narterial ulcer\n\nwould instead have a deeper, 'punched-out' appearance. The patient would likely describe pain on elevation of the leg and on walking. The description here is instead more suggestive of SCC.\n\nBasal cell carcinoma\n\nis not as frequently associated with scar tissue as SCC. It would appear differently to the lesion described above - typically a well-defined nodule with pearly, rolled edges.\n\nMalignant melanoma\n\nis less commonly associated with scar tissue, although would also warrant urgent referral. It would appear differently, with dark pigmentation being a defining feature.\n\nPyoderma gangrenosum\n\ntypically starts as a blister or papule at a site of minor injury and rapidly becomes painful, dusky-purple and purulent. This does not fit with the lesion here, which is more suggestive of SCC."
