      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Scabies",
      "explanation": "Lichen\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nLichen planus\n\n. This condition is characterised by pruritic, polygonal, violaceous papules and plaques which often affect the flexor surfaces of the forearms. The description provided in the question fits this clinical picture perfectly. Lichen planus can also affect other parts of the body such as oral mucosa, genitals, scalp and nails.\n\nScabies\n\n, on the other hand, typically present with intense pruritus that is worse at night. The lesions are usually small vesicles or papules, especially between fingers and toes, around the waist and in genital areas. While scabies can cause a rash on various parts of the body, it does not typically present with polygonal and violaceous papules.\n\nThe third option,\n\nLichen sclerosus\n\n, predominantly affects post-menopausal women although it can occur in men and children too. It primarily involves anogenital region but extragenital lesions can occur. The typical presentation is with white shiny patches on the skin that may become thin and wrinkled. Itching is common but not always present.\n\nMorphea\n\n, also known as localized scleroderma, causes patches of hard, discoloured skin that may be oval-shaped or linear. These patches initially appear reddish or purplish before becoming whitish or yellowish in the centre due to sclerosis (hardening). Morphea does not typically present as itchy violaceous papules.\n\nFinally,\n\nPsoriasis\n\nis a chronic inflammatory skin disorder that commonly presents with well-defined erythematous plaques covered by silvery scales which are most often located on extensor surfaces like elbows and knees rather than flexural aspects. Psoriasis also has a variety of presentations such as guttate psoriasis or pustular psoriasis which do not match this case scenario.\n\nIn conclusion, based on clinical features described in the question stem; lichen planus is the most likely diagnosis considering its characteristic presentation with pruritic polygonal violaceous papules and plaques affecting flexor surfaces."
