      "id": 31,
      "question": "A 36-year-old dog walker presents with a skin rash.\n\nShe describes intermittent flushing, worse on the days when she is at work. She also complains of bloating and diarrhoea, interspersed with constipation, for the past 10 years. There is no history of rectal bleeding, weight loss or fever.\n\nShe would also like to discuss her low mood, which has been troubling her for the past year since the loss of her pet.\n\nOn examination, you notice three erythematous papules with surrounding telangiectasia on the upper aspect of the cheek. The abdomen is soft with no tenderness or palpable masses.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
      "options": [
        "Acne rosacea",
        "Allergic reaction",
        "Metastatic basal cell carcinoma",
        "Carcinoid syndrome",
        "Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia",
      "explanation": "Rosacea features:\n\nThis question highlights the importance of piecing information together and not merely reading keywords (such as 'flushing' and 'diarrhoea') and jumping to conclusions, a common way to get a question wrong in an exam.\n\nThe intermittent flushing, which is worse on the days when she is at work, is likely to be due to photosensitivity as she works as a dog walker (outdoors). This, together with papules with telangiectasia, points to a diagnosis of acne rosacea. The aetiology of acne rosacea is not known. However, flushing is often the first symptom, and it is typically exacerbated by sunlight.\n\nThe other symptoms are red herrings. Intermittent constipation and diarrhoea with bloating, especially with a normal examination and such a longstanding history, are likely due to irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) and unrelated to her skin rash. Low mood can exacerbate IBS symptoms and vice-versa. Patients will often present with several different symptoms, and they will often not know whether they are related or not. It is the clinician's job to determine whether they are relevant to the current presentation.\n\nCarcinoid syndrome can entail flushing and diarrhoea and therefore is the main differential here. In carcinoid syndrome, the aforementioned symptoms usually indicate that a neuroendocrine tumour has metastasised to the liver and is releasing serotonin into the circulation. However, it wouldn't cause papules, and there are no red flags for cancer in this patient's history - she denied fever, bleeding or weight loss. It is very unlikely that several years' history of diarrhoea, particularly mixed with constipation, would be due to carcinoid syndrome having gone undiagnosed. Lastly, acne rosacea is much more common, statistically speaking, than carcinoid syndrome.\n\nAn allergic reaction is unlikely to manifest with flushing and papules with telangiectasia. It would typically cause hay fever-like symptoms or an urticarial erythematous rash, if not anaphylaxis.\n\nHereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia may cause telangiectasias but would also cause gastrointestinal and/or nosebleeds. The patient is having gastrointestinal symptoms. However, without any signs of bleeding, this is unlikely to be the diagnosis.\n\nBasal cell carcinoma of the skin can cause telangiectasia, but it is improbable that three of them would arise together on the same patch of skin or cause intermittent flushing."
