      "explanation": "Pompholyx eczema is a subtype of eczema characterised by an intensely pruritic rash on the palms and soles\n\nThis patient is presenting with characteristic signs of pompholyx dermatitis (vesicular or dyshidrotic dermatitis). Her recent stay in a hot humid climate (Thailand) may have precipitated this flare-up. This type of dermatitis is particularly resistant to treatment. Topical treatments are typically ineffective because of the thick horny layer of skin on the hands and feet\n\n1\n\nHerpes virus infection can cause a vesicular rash but it is most typically around the mouth (HSV1) or in the genitals (HSV2). Eczema herpeticum can occur in patients with existing atopic dermatitis it is typically a widespread vesicular rash with associated constitutional symptoms.\n\nAllergic contact dermatitis is a type 4 hypersensitivity reaction resulting in a rash that appears 48 to 72 hours after exposure to an irritant. The history is not suggestive of this pathology.\n\nIrritant contact dermatitis represents an injury response to an irritant. Anyone can suffer from this, however, an irritant threshold may be different depending on the characteristics of your skin e.g. bleach can affect someone with sensitive skin in very small amounts but may need a significant amount of splash to affect another person. The history is not suggestive of this pathology.\n\nFixed drug eruption is an important differential but presents as well-demarcated erythematous oval lesions with or without a blister.\n\nReferences (1) Wollina, U. Am J clin dermatol (2010) 11: 305."
