      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Psoriasis",
      "explanation": "Otitis externa and blepharitis are common complications of seborrhoeic dermatitis\n\nThis is a history and examination suggesting anterior blepharitis. The most common underlying cause is\n\nseborrhoeic dermatitis\n\n. Seborrhoeic dermatitis normally causes eczematous lesions in the scalp, periorbital, auricular and nasolabial folds. It also can lead to otitis externa.\n\nAtopic dermatitis\n\n(otherwise called atopic eczema) is the most common inflammatory skin condition. It can affect the face more commonly in childhood but does not commonly lead to blepharitis. Important complications of atopic dermatitis include staphylococcal infection and eczema herpeticum.\n\nPsoriasis\n\ndoes not typically cause blepharitis or any eye pathology. Complications of psoriasis include arthropathy as well as increased incidence of metabolic syndrome, cardiovascular disease and venous thromboembolism.\n\nRheumatoid arthritis\n\ndoes not commonly cause blepharitis. However, ocular manifestations are common and include keratoconjunctivitis sicca, episcleritis, scleritis, corneal ulceration and keratitis. The examination findings in this question are not suggestive of any of these.\n\nRosacea\n\nis a condition causing flushing, telangiectasia, erythema and pustules of the face. It is a recognised cause of blepharitis but would be less common than seborrhoeic dermatitis in a 24-year-old male."
