      "id": 55,
      "question": "A 64-year-old woman presents with severe mucosal ulceration associated with the development of blistering lesions over her torso and arms. On examination the blisters are flaccid and easily ruptured when touched. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
      "options": [
        "Pemphigus vulgaris",
        "Pemphigoid",
        "Dermatitis herpetiformis",
        "Psoriasis",
        "Epidermolysis bullosa"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Psoriasis",
      "explanation": "Blisters/bullae\n\nThe most likely diagnosis, in this case, is\n\nPemphigus vulgaris\n\n. This condition is characterized by the presence of flaccid blisters and mucosal ulceration, which are easily ruptured when touched. Pemphigus vulgaris is an autoimmune disorder caused by autoantibodies against desmoglein 1 and 3, which are proteins responsible for maintaining the integrity of keratinocyte adhesion in the epidermis. The loss of adhesion leads to intraepidermal blister formation.\n\nPemphigoid\n\nis another autoimmune blistering disorder, but it differs from pemphigus vulgaris in that it involves the formation of tense blisters rather than flaccid ones. In pemphigoid, autoantibodies target components of the basement membrane zone, leading to subepidermal blister formation. Due to the deeper location of these blisters compared to those seen in pemphigus vulgaris, they are more resistant to rupture.\n\nDermatitis herpetiformis\n\nis a chronic autoimmune skin condition associated with gluten sensitivity and coeliac disease. It presents as intensely pruritic papules, vesicles, and excoriations typically distributed symmetrically on extensor surfaces such as elbows, knees, and buttocks. Although it can present with blisters, these tend to be small and clustered rather than large and flaccid like those seen in pemphigus vulgaris.\n\nPsoriasis\n\nis a common chronic inflammatory skin condition characterized by well-demarcated erythematous plaques with silvery scales. It does not typically present with blistering or mucosal involvement as seen in this patient's presentation.\n\nEpidermolysis bullosa\n\nrefers to a group of rare inherited disorders characterized by fragility of the skin and mucous membranes leading to blister formation. These blisters can be induced by minor trauma or friction, but the onset of symptoms is usually in infancy or early childhood, making it an unlikely diagnosis for a 64-year-old woman presenting with new-onset blistering lesions."
