      "explanation": "A non-healing painless ulcer associated with a chronic scar is indicative of squamous cell carcinoma (SCC)\n\nThe diagnosis is most likely squamous cell carcinoma (SCC). A skin biopsy would be required to confirm the diagnosis before initiating treatment.\n\nSurgical excision\n\nis correct. Surgical excision is the preferred treatment for localised disease.  Surgical excision with 4mm margins is preferred if the lesion is <20mm in diameter, and with 6mm margins if the lesion is >20mm in diameter.\n\nChemotherapy combined with immunotherapy\n\nis incorrect. Although initial surgical excision cures 95% of patients, a minority of cutaneous SCCs are considered unresectable, either locally advanced or with unresectable regional lymph nodes or distant metastases. These patients are offered systemic treatments with chemo-immunotherapy. Two checkpoint inhibitors, cemiplimab and pembrolizumab, have demonstrated a high response rate and survival with fewer toxicities than other available systemic agents. The CT scan in this case did not demonstrate any regional lymph node involvement or distant metastases and thus systemic treatment can be avoided if the lesion can be fully resected.\n\nCryotherapy\n\nis incorrect. Cryotherapy is used for some early squamous cell cancers, especially in people who can not have surgery. Superficial, in situ, or SCC less and equal to 1 cm has been successfully treated with cryosurgery with results comparable to traditional surgical excision. In this case, the lesion is > 1cm and therefore surgery is required.\n\nDiclofenac topical gel\n\nis incorrect. This can be used to treat actinic keratosis (the most common precursor lesion for squamous cell carcinoma). Actinic keratosis may be solitary but there are often multiple keratoses. The appearance varies. However, classically they present with flat to slightly raised, scaly, and crusty lesions which sometimes have a raised horn shape or bump. They usually occur on sun exposure sites (e.g. the scalp). In this case, the presence of ulceration favours a diagnosis of SCC.\n\nMohs micrographic surgery\n\nis incorrect. Mohs micrographic surgery may be used in high-risk patients and in cosmetically important sites (e.g. face). Mohs surgery is designed to minimise scarring at the outset, by creating the smallest post-surgical wound possible."
