      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Pemphigus vulgaris",
      "explanation": "Polymorphic eruption of pregnancy is not associated with blistering\n\nPolymorphic eruption of pregnancy\n\ndevelops in the third trimester and is characterised by the development of pruritic papules, often over abdominal striae, that evolve into larger erythematous plaques and may also spread to the buttocks and thighs. Treatment would involve emollients and topical steroids. Consider oral steroids if particularly widespread.\n\nAtopic eruption of pregnancy\n\ninvolves the development of a pruritic, eczematous rash, in the typical distribution seen in eczema. It is the most common skin problem in pregnancy, often presenting in the first or second trimester but may also present in the third. It often does not require specific treatment. The rash in this question does not have the eczematous distribution expected in the atopic eruption of pregnancy, and the evolution of pruritic papules to erythematous plaques is more characteristic of polymorphic eruption of pregnancy.\n\nBullous pemphigoid\n\nis an autoimmune condition characterised by a tense blistering rash, most commonly seen in the elderly population. It can often be complicated by secondary bacterial or viral infection. The age, normal observations and lack of blistering make this very unlikely to be the diagnosis.\n\nPemphigoid gestationis\n\nis characterised by pruritic, blistering lesions, initially periumbilical but later spreading to affect the rest of the torso and arms over weeks. It is most often seen in the second or third trimester, or early postpartum, and in subsequent pregnancies may present sooner. Treatment would involve steroids but may require immunosuppression if severe. It is unlikely in this case due to the lack of blistering and distribution of lesions.\n\nPemphigus vulgaris\n\nis characterised by the formation of sometimes painful, fragile blisters, particularly on mucous membranes and may be complicated by secondary infection. Patients tend to be aged between 30-60 and it may be idiopathic, drug-induced, less commonly paraneoplastic or secondary to trauma or infection. Treat initially with systemic steroids."
