      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Syphilis",
      "explanation": "Acute onset of tear-drop scaly papules on trunk and limbs → ?guttate psoriasis\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nguttate psoriasis\n\n. Guttate psoriasis is a type of psoriasis that presents as small, erythematous papules and plaques with fine scaling, often triggered by a preceding streptococcal infection such as tonsillitis. In this case, the patient's recent history of exudative tonsillitis, along with the description of the rash involving the torso and limbs with erythematous lesions and fine scale, strongly supports the diagnosis of guttate psoriasis.\n\nPityriasis rosea\n\nis an incorrect answer because although it can present with a widespread rash on the trunk and limbs, it typically begins with a single larger 'herald patch' followed by smaller oval-shaped lesions that have a collarette scale. Moreover, pityriasis rosea is not associated with preceding streptococcal infections.\n\nPityriasis versicolor\n\n(also known as tinea versicolor) is another incorrect option. This condition is caused by an overgrowth of\n\nMalassezia\n\nyeasts on the skin and presents as hypopigmented or hyperpigmented scaly patches typically found on the upper trunk. The distribution and appearance of this patient's rash do not fit with pityriasis versicolor.\n\nSyphilis\n\nis also an incorrect choice in this scenario. While secondary syphilis can cause a widespread rash that may involve the palms and soles, it usually presents as non-pruritic maculopapular lesions without scaling. Additionally, other symptoms such as fever, lymphadenopathy, or mucosal involvement would be expected in secondary syphilis.\n\nFinally,\n\ndiscoid eczema\n\n, also known as nummular dermatitis or discoid dermatitis, is not consistent with this patient's presentation. Discoid eczema presents as coin-shaped, pruritic, erythematous patches with scaling and crusting, typically on the limbs. The patient's history of preceding tonsillitis and the description of the rash make guttate psoriasis a more likely diagnosis."
