      "id": 63,
      "question": "Which one of the following factors would predispose a patient to forming keloid scars?",
      "options": [
        "Having white skin",
        "Incisions along relaxed skin tension lines",
        "Being aged 20-40 years",
        "Being female",
        "Having a wound on the lower back"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Having white skin",
      "explanation": "Keloid scars - more common in young, black, male adults\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nBeing aged 20-40 years\n\n. Keloid scars are a result of an overactive healing process where the body produces excess collagen, leading to raised and often itchy or painful scars. They are more common in individuals aged 10-30 years old, with a peak incidence between 20-40 years. This is because younger skin has a higher capacity for cellular proliferation and therefore tends to heal faster and produce more collagen.\n\nHaving white skin\n\nis not a predisposing factor for keloid formation. In fact, keloids are less common in people with lighter skin types. Darker-skinned individuals, especially those of African, Hispanic or Asian descent, have a higher risk of developing keloids due to genetic factors.\n\nIncisions along relaxed skin tension lines\n\nactually decrease the likelihood of keloid formation. These lines represent the natural orientation of collagen fibres in the dermis. Surgical incisions made along these lines result in less tension on wound edges during healing, reducing the risk of abnormal scar formation including keloids.\n\nBeing female\n\n, while may increase the likelihood of hypertrophic scarring (another type of raised scar), does not specifically predispose to keloid formation. Both sexes can develop keloids, though some studies suggest a slight female predominance which could be attributed to practices like ear piercing rather than biological sex itself.\n\nFinally,\n\nhaving a wound on the lower back\n\nis not particularly associated with an increased risk of keloid formation compared to other locations on the body. However, areas with high levels of tension such as sternum, shoulders and earlobes are known hotspots for keloids due to constant movement and stretching that can stimulate excessive collagen production during healing."
