      "explanation": "Bone marrow biopsy is the gold-standard investigation used to diagnose systemic mastocytosis\n\nBone marrow biopsy\n\nis the correct answer as it represents the gold-standard investigation for definitively diagnosing systemic mastocytosis. This patient presents with classic features including urticaria pigmentosa (brown macules that wheal when rubbed - Darier's sign), flushing, abdominal pain, monocytosis, and significantly elevated serum tryptase levels. While elevated tryptase (>20 ng/mL persistently) is a minor diagnostic criterion for systemic mastocytosis, definitive diagnosis requires bone marrow examination. The biopsy allows for histological assessment of mast cell infiltration, immunohistochemistry to identify aberrant mast cell markers (such as CD25 and CD2), and molecular testing for the KIT D816V mutation, which is present in over 90% of cases. These findings form the major diagnostic criteria according to WHO classification.\n\n24-hour urinary histamine\n\ncan be elevated in systemic mastocytosis and may support the diagnosis, but it is primarily used to monitor disease activity rather than establish the initial diagnosis. It is not specific for mastocytosis and can be elevated in other conditions causing mast cell degranulation.\n\nChromogranin A level\n\nis a neuroendocrine marker that would be more relevant in investigating carcinoid syndrome, which can present with flushing but would not typically cause the skin lesions or monocytosis seen in this case.\n\nCT chest, abdomen and pelvis\n\nmight identify organomegaly or lymphadenopathy associated with systemic mastocytosis, but imaging alone cannot provide the definitive diagnosis required for this condition.\n\nFlow cytometry of peripheral blood\n\nis not routinely used in diagnosing systemic mastocytosis as circulating mast cells are typically very low in number, even in systemic disease. The abnormal mast cells are predominantly found in tissue, particularly bone marrow."
