      "explanation": "Ketoconazole shampoo is used to treat pityriasis versicolor\n\nPityriasis versicolor is a common yeast infection of the skin, in which flaky discoloured patches appear on the chest and back.\n\nKetoconazole or selenium sulphide shampoo are antifungal shampoos which are often the first treatment recommended for pityriasis versicolor.\n\nTerbinafine cream along with clotrimazole cream and miconazole cream can also be used as topical antifungal agents to apply to the skin. These reduce the number of skin yeasts.\n\nHydrocortisone cream is not useful in pityriasis versicolor. Steroid creams can be helpful for some skin problems and can even temporarily reduce ringworm symptoms like itching and redness, but they don't kill the fungus. Steroid creams also can make conditions like ringworm worse because they weaken the skin's defences.\n\nVitamin D analogues such as calcipotriol and calcitriol help regulate the immune system in the skin and slow the overgrowth of skin that happens in psoriasis but are not useful in conditions like pityriasis versicolor.\n\nKetoconazole cream is indicated for the topical treatment of fungal conditions such as tinea corporis, tinea cruris and tinea pedis but not pityriasis versicolor."
