      "correctText": "Surgery",
      "explanation": "Immune related adverse events in a patient on checkpoint inhibitors (e.g. nivolumab)? Need to treat with corticosteroids\n\nInfliximab is incorrect. The diagnosis is an immune-related adverse event (in this case colitis) secondary to treatment with a checkpoint inhibitor nivolumab. The first-line treatment in moderately severe cases such as this one is oral prednisolone at a dose of approximately 1mg/kg. If the patient is refractory to steroids then an anti-TNF biologic such as infliximab may be used.\n\nMesalazine is incorrect. This is not used to induce remission in moderate-severe colitis but has a place in the maintenance of remission in mild-moderate disease.\n\nOral vancomycin is incorrect. The antibiotic trimethoprim is not typically associated with the development of\n\nClostridium difficile\n\ndiarrhoea. The context of this scenario points towards an immune-related adverse event.\n\nSurgery is incorrect. This woman's abdominal radiograph demonstrates a transverse colon diameter of < 6cm, indicating that she does not have toxic megacolon and therefore surgical intervention is not warranted. She should be managed medically in the first instance. Her lactate is only mildly elevated in the context of likely dehydration and acute kidney injury.\n\nOral prednisolone is correct. This woman presents with colitis-associated with nivolumab. She has moderately severe disease requiring hospital admission and treatment but does not require urgent surgical intervention or high dose intravenous steroids in the first instance. She should be treated with oral prednisolone and rehydrated before other options are considered."
