      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Oral flucloxacillin",
      "explanation": "Oral steroids are the first-line treatment for pyoderma gangrenosum\n\nOral prednisolone\n\nis the correct answer. The patient in this vignette has pyoderma gangrenosum. This typically presents initially with a small blood blister or red bump before progressing to a large deep violaceous ulcer, commonly affecting the lower limbs. The patient's background of ulcerative colitis is also a clue to the diagnosis, given inflammatory bowel disease can predispose patients to developing pyoderma gangrenosum.  Patients are often treated initially with oral prednisolone.\n\nOral cyclosporin\n\nis incorrect. This is an immunomodulating agent that can be given for treating pyoderma gangrenosum however, it is not first like management. This patient has not trialled any treatment for his diagnosis yet, and the question specifically asks for the first-line management of the likely diagnosis. Oral cyclosporin is given if patients poorly respond to oral prednisolone.\n\nOral flucloxacillin\n\nis incorrect. Oral flucloxacillin is the first line for bacterial skin infections such as cellulitis. There is no indication that this patient has a bacterial infection due to the absence of systemic features such as pyrexia, and lethargy, but also the absence of extensive erythema, oozing, and warmth over the affected region.  Pyoderma gangrenosum is non-infectious, and as this patient does not have any features to suggest co-existing bacterial skin infection oral flucloxacillin would not be indicated.\n\nOral pentoxifylline,\n\nis incorrect. Oral pentoxifylline is an oral vasodilator that is specifically given in venous ulceration to improve the healing rate. Typically venous ulceration is large, shallow, and irregular with a granulating/exudative base seen over the gaiter or medial malleolus region. The presentation described in this scenario is not characterised by venous ulceration as the ulcer is deep, and purple and the patient does not have a background of risk factors predisposed to venous disease such as smoking, obesity and varicose veins. The description of a small blood blister developing into a deep violaceous ulcer on a background of ulcerative colitis points more to a diagnosis of pyoderma gangrenosum, and the treatment for this is oral steroids as described above.\n\nTopical Dermovate\n\nis incorrect. Topical dermovate is a potent topical steroid rarely given for skin conditions.  Some indications for topical dermovate include chronic discoid lupus erythematosus, lichen simplex, hypertrophic lichen planus, and palmoplantar pustulosis. Although topical steroids can be given in mild, and local cases of pyoderma gangrenosum, this patient has an extension of his disease across the majority of his left leg, therefore oral prednisolone would be more appropriate to ensure sufficient, successful treatment of the condition and prevention of relapse."
