      "explanation": "Eczema herpeticum is a serious condition that requires IV antivirals\n\nAciclovir\n\nis correct. This is a classic description of eczema herpeticum which is a disseminated viral infection characterised by fever and clusters of itchy blisters or punched-out erosions. It is most often seen as a complication of atopic dermatitis/eczema. Most cases of eczema herpeticum are due to herpes simplex type 1 or 2. It is a dermatological emergency and requires prompt treatment with an anti-viral such as aciclovir or valacyclovir.\n\nAmphotericin B\n\nis incorrect. Amphotericin B is an antifungal medication used to treat mucormycosis, aspergillosis, blastomycosis, candidiasis, coccidioidomycosis, and cryptococcosis. The clinical examination does not support any of the aforementioned conditions.\n\nCidofovir\n\nis incorrect. Cidofovir is an antiviral that can be used in aciclovir-resistant herpes simplex infections. If the infection did not respond to aciclovir then sensitivity testing could be performed to assess for resistance.\n\nFlucloxacillin\n\nis incorrect.  Although the patient may well have a secondary bacterial infection (e.g. impetigo on their chest), the most important part of treatment is prompt initiation of an anti-viral. The addition of flucloxacillin to cover for a superadded\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\ninfection would be reasonable.\n\nVancomycin\n\nis incorrect. If the patient were to have a confirmed or suspected methicillin-resistant\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\n(MRSA), then it would be reasonable to initiate them on vancomycin in addition to aciclovir to cover for superadded bacterial infection."
