      "correctText": "Oral hydroxychloroquine",
      "explanation": "Rosacea: a combination of topical ivermectin +  oral doxycycline is first-line for patients with severe papules and/or pustules\n\nThe correct answer is\n\noral doxycycline + topical ivermectin\n\n. This patient's presentation is consistent with rosacea, specifically papulopustular (type 2) rosacea. According to NICE guidelines, the first-line treatment for moderate to severe papulopustular rosacea is oral tetracyclines (such as doxycycline) combined with topical therapy. Ivermectin 1% cream has shown superior efficacy compared to other topical agents and is now recommended as a first-line topical treatment. This combination therapy addresses both the inflammatory and antimicrobial aspects of the condition.\n\nTopical metronidazole\n\nalone would be insufficient for moderate to severe rosacea. While it is an appropriate topical agent for mild cases, this patient's extensive involvement would require systemic therapy. Metronidazole gel is more suitable as monotherapy for mild cases or as an adjunct to oral therapy.\n\nOral isotretinoin\n\nis reserved for severe cases of rosacea that have failed to respond to conventional therapy with oral antibiotics and topical treatments. It would be inappropriate as a first-line treatment due to its significant side effect profile and the need for careful monitoring.\n\nOral hydroxychloroquine\n\nis not indicated for rosacea. It is primarily used in autoimmune conditions such as systemic lupus erythematosus and rheumatoid arthritis. There is no evidence base for its use in rosacea, and it would not address the underlying pathophysiology of the condition.\n\nOral prednisolone\n\nwould be an inappropriate choice as systemic corticosteroids can actually worsen rosacea and lead to a steroid-induced rosacea-like condition. Corticosteroids can cause rebound flares and potentially aggravate the underlying condition. They should be avoided in rosacea unless there are specific indications such as severe inflammatory episodes requiring short-term use."
