      "explanation": "Blisters/bullae\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nbullous pemphigoid\n\n. Bullous pemphigoid is an autoimmune blistering disorder that commonly affects elderly patients. It presents with tense, pruritic blisters on the skin, often on the extremities, and typically spares mucosal surfaces like the mouth and vulva. The patient's age, presence of tense blisters, and lack of mucosal involvement make bullous pemphigoid the most likely diagnosis in this scenario.\n\nPemphigus\n\nis another autoimmune blistering disorder; however, it typically presents with flaccid blisters and erosions involving both the skin and mucosal surfaces. The absence of mucosal involvement and tense nature of the blisters make pemphigus less likely than bullous pemphigoid.\n\nA\n\ndrug reaction to aspirin\n\ncould potentially cause a cutaneous eruption; however, drug reactions usually present with a more diffuse rash rather than localized tense blisters. Additionally, the patient has been taking aspirin as part of her medication regimen without any previous issues mentioned.\n\nEpidermolysis bullosa\n\nis a group of inherited disorders characterized by skin fragility and blister formation following minor trauma. Given the patient's age and lack of history suggesting skin fragility or recurrent blistering throughout her life, this diagnosis is unlikely.\n\nScabies\n\nis a parasitic infestation caused by the mite\n\nSarcoptes scabiei\n\n. It typically presents with intense pruritus and a papular rash affecting primarily interdigital spaces, wrists, axillae, waistline and genital areas. The presentation of tense blisters on the legs without other typical features makes scabies an unlikely diagnosis for this patient.\n\nIn conclusion, based on the clinical presentation described in this scenario - an elderly patient with tense, pruritic blisters on the legs and no mucosal involvement - the most likely diagnosis is bullous pemphigoid."
