      "correctText": "Emollients alone",
      "explanation": "Lichen\n\nPotent topical corticosteroids\n\nare the mainstay of treatment for cutaneous lichen planus according to UK guidelines. They effectively reduce inflammation and pruritus associated with the condition, especially when lesions are localised as in this scenario. Topical steroids target the immune-mediated process underlying lichen planus and can help resolve both skin and mild oral involvement (although specific oral treatments may be needed if symptoms progress). Early initiation limits chronicity and scarring.\n\nEmollients alone\n\nmay provide symptomatic relief from dryness or minor irritation but do not address the inflammatory component of lichen planus. They are useful adjuncts but insufficient as monotherapy for active disease, particularly when pruritic papules and Wickham's striae are present.\n\nOral corticosteroids\n\nare reserved for severe or generalised cases or when topical therapy fails. This patient’s disease is limited to wrists, ankles, and mild oral involvement; systemic steroids would expose her to unnecessary side effects without clear benefit at this stage.\n\nOral antihistamines\n\nmight help relieve itching but do not treat the underlying pathology or induce remission of lesions in lichen planus. Antihistamines could be considered as adjunctive therapy for symptom control only.\n\nTopical tacrolimus\n\nis sometimes used off-label for refractory oral lichen planus or steroid-unresponsive cutaneous disease, but it is not recommended as first-line therapy for typical cutaneous presentations due to limited evidence and potential adverse effects such as local irritation."
