      "explanation": "Impetigo - topical fusidic acid if hydrogen peroxide not suitable\n\nThe correct answer is\n\ntopical fusidic acid\n\n. This patient presents with a typical case of impetigo, which is a superficial bacterial skin infection commonly caused by Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes. For small, localised lesions (less than 2% body surface area), topical antibiotics are the first-line treatment according to UK guidelines. Fusidic acid 2% cream or ointment applied three times daily for 5-7 days is recommended as first-line therapy.\n\nOral co-amoxiclav\n\nwould be excessive for a single small lesion. Oral antibiotics are reserved for more extensive disease, systemic symptoms, or when topical therapy has failed. Co-amoxiclav would be appropriate for more widespread infection or if there were signs of cellulitis.\n\nOral penicillin\n\nalone would not be appropriate as it does not provide adequate coverage against Staphylococcus aureus, which is a common causative organism in impetigo.\n\nOral flucloxacillin\n\nwould be excessive for a single small lesion but would be appropriate if oral therapy was required, as it provides good coverage against Staphylococcus aureus.\n\nOral flucloxacillin + penicillin\n\ncombination would be excessive and unnecessary for a single small lesion. This combination might be considered for more severe soft tissue infections where both staphylococcal and streptococcal coverage is essential.\n\nThe key principles in managing impetigo are to use the most targeted therapy possible while avoiding unnecessary use of systemic antibiotics. This helps prevent antibiotic resistance and minimizes side effects."
