      "explanation": "Dry skin is the most common side-effect of isotretinoin\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nDry skin\n\n. Isotretinoin, a retinoid, is commonly used for the treatment of severe acne. It works by reducing sebum production, which in turn reduces inflammation and comedone formation. The most common side effect of isotretinoin is dry skin, which occurs due to its effect on sebaceous glands leading to reduced oil production. This can affect all areas of the skin including the lips and eyes.\n\nLow mood\n\nis not the most likely side effect but it has been reported in some patients taking isotretinoin. However, it's worth mentioning that acne itself can be associated with low mood and depression due to its impact on self-image and quality of life. Therefore, it can be difficult to establish a direct causal relationship between isotretinoin use and mood changes.\n\nThrombocytopaenia\n\n, or low platelet count, is not typically associated with isotretinoin use. While isotretinoin can have an impact on blood cells, it more commonly causes a rise in erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) and haemoglobin concentration.\n\nRaised plasma triglycerides\n\ncan occur as a result of isotretinoin therapy but this is relatively uncommon compared to dry skin. Patients starting on isotretinoin should have baseline lipid levels checked before treatment initiation and monitored throughout treatment as per UK guidelines.\n\nFinally,\n\nReversible alopecia\n\n, although possible with isotretinoin use, is extremely rare compared to the other options listed above. In fact hair thinning or hair loss are more often reported with prolonged high-dose therapy or following discontinuation of the drug rather than during standard treatment courses for acne."
