      "explanation": "Zinc deficiency caused by total parenteral nutrition (TPN) can result in acrodermatitis herpetiformis\n\nVarious small bowel diseases that cause destruction or malfunction of the gut mucosa and malabsorption lead to zinc deficiency. This has similarly been noticed in patients on total parenteral nutrition (TPN) who do not receive zinc - resulting in acrodermatitis herpetiformis causing peri-orificial dermatitis and alopecia.\n\nSelenium deficiency can contribute to fatigue, hair loss, weight gain, joint and muscle pain.\n\nIron deficiency can contribute to fatigue, weakness, pale skin, chest pain, shortness of breath, headache, dizziness and cold peripheries.\n\nVitamin B12 deficiency can cause a smooth tongue, nerve problems like numbness or tingling, pale skin and weakness.\n\nFolate deficiency can cause a lack of energy, pale skin, palpitations, headaches and loss of appetite."
