      "id": 130,
      "question": "A 58-year-old man presents to the dermatology clinic with a 6-week history of a rapidly growing, dome-shaped, black nodule on his back. It bleeds occasionally and is sometimes itchy. On examination, there is a 1.5 cm raised, firm, black nodule with irregular borders. An excision biopsy is performed. Histopathology shows vertical growth phase melanoma with a Breslow thickness of 3.2 mm.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate next step in management?",
      "options": [
        "1 cm margin re-excision",
        "2 cm margin re-excision",
        "3 cm margin re-excision",
        "Sentinel lymph node biopsy followed by 1 cm margin re-excision",
        "Sentinel lymph node biopsy followed by 2 cm margin re-excision"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Sentinel lymph node biopsy followed by 1 cm margin re-excision",
      "explanation": "Nodular melanoma: Invade aggressively and metastasise early\n\nSentinel lymph node biopsy followed by 2 cm margin re-excision\n\nis the most appropriate next step in management for this patient. The clinical presentation is highly suggestive of nodular melanoma, which is characterised by its aggressive vertical growth pattern and early metastatic potential. The histopathology confirms a Breslow thickness of 3.2 mm, which classifies this as a high-risk melanoma. For melanomas with a Breslow thickness between 2-4 mm, UK guidelines recommend a re-excision margin of 2 cm. Additionally, sentinel lymph node biopsy (SLNB) is indicated for melanomas with a Breslow thickness >1 mm or those with adverse features, as it provides important prognostic information and guides further management decisions. The SLNB should be performed before the wider local excision to ensure accurate lymphatic mapping.\n\n1 cm margin re-excision\n\nis inadequate for this patient. A 1 cm margin is only appropriate for thin melanomas with a Breslow thickness of less than 1 mm. Given this patient's 3.2 mm thick nodular melanoma, a 1 cm margin would significantly increase the risk of local recurrence.\n\n2 cm margin re-excision\n\nalone, without sentinel lymph node biopsy, is insufficient management for this high-risk melanoma. While the 2 cm margin is appropriate for the Breslow thickness, omitting the SLNB would miss crucial staging information that impacts prognosis and potential adjuvant therapy decisions.\n\n3 cm margin re-excision\n\nis excessive for this patient. A 3 cm margin is typically reserved for very thick melanomas with a Breslow thickness greater than 4 mm. For a 3.2 mm thick melanoma, a 2 cm margin is sufficient according to UK guidelines and provides the optimal balance between reducing recurrence risk and minimising morbidity from extensive surgery.\n\nSentinel lymph node biopsy followed by 1 cm margin re-excision\n\nincludes the appropriate SLNB but with an inadequate excision margin. The 1 cm margin is insufficient for a 3.2 mm thick melanoma and would not comply with UK guidelines for management of intermediate-thickness melanomas."
