      "explanation": "Linear, well-demarcated skin lesions that appear suddenly, with 'la belle indifference'  → ?dermatitis artefacta\n\nThe description here points strongly towards dermatitis artefacta, also known as factitious dermatitis. This is a rare psycho-dermatological condition involving self-inflicted skin lesions. Patients demonstrate apathy/indifference toward the lesions, denying their own involvement, and are prompted to engage with healthcare professionals by relatives or friends. Regarding management, good rapport is crucial. Direct confrontation is often detrimental, and patients should be co-managed by psychiatrists and dermatologists.\n\nDermatitis herpetiformis is characterised by itchy blisters across the body. It is linked to coeliac disease, of which there is no mention in the stem.\n\nLichen planus appears as flat-topped, itchy raised violaceous papules, often in clusters. It is thought to be an autoimmune condition. This is not consistent with the scenario above.\n\nNeurotic excoriations are also psychocutaneous manifestations. These similarly result in skin lesions from picking and excoriating, but patients actively admit their own involvement in creating the lesions. This is unlike dermatitis artefacta, where patients deny involvement.\n\nTrichotillomania is the action of repeated hair-pulling, resulting in hair loss. Patients usually experience relief once they have performed this action. Although this is another psychocutaneous condition, the scenario here refers to dermatitis artefacta."
