      "correctText": "Autosomal recessive",
      "explanation": "Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia - autosomal dominant\n\nAutosomal dominant\n\nis correct. This patient has a definite diagnosis of hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia as evidenced by recurrent nosebleeds, telangiectases and an arteriovenous malformation (AVM). The inheritance pattern of this disorder is autosomal dominant. The recurrent nosebleeds affecting his mother suggests she also may have been affected.\n\nAutosomal recessive\n\nis incorrect. An example of a bleeding disorder (which can cause epistaxis) with this inheritance pattern is Bernard-Soulier syndrome. This is caused by a deficiency of the glycoprotein Ib-IX-V complex (GPIb-IX-V), the receptor for von Willebrand factor. Telangiectases and arteriovenous malformations (AVM) are not a feature of this condition so this is not the correct answer.\n\nMitochondrial\n\nis incorrect. Mitochondrial disorders are passed from mother to child as in this case. However, bleeding is not a common symptom of mitochondrial disorders, which typically have neurological manifestations such as strokes, dementia, myoclonus, and encephalopathy among others. This is therefore not correct.\n\nX-linked dominant\n\nis incorrect. Disorders characterized by an X-dominant pattern of inheritance are relatively uncommon. Examples include Fragile X and Rett syndromes. There is no X-linked dominant disorder that causes bleeding, telangiectasia, and hepatic AVMs and this is therefore incorrect.\n\nX-linked recessive\n\nis incorrect. This is the inheritance pattern of haemophilia A. This occurs almost exclusively in males due to its X-linked recessive inheritance pattern (as males only have one X chromosome). It can of course cause nosebleeds due to a genetic deficiency in clotting factor VIII. However, as with the other options, telangiectases and arteriovenous malformations (AVM) are not a feature of this condition so this is not the correct answer."
