      "explanation": "SCCs arising in a chronic scar are typically more aggressive and carry an increased risk of metastasis\n\nA history of a non-healing ulcer at the site of a burn injury should be investigated immediately for SCC. Squamous cell carcinomas arising in chronic wounds tend to be more aggressive so early diagnosis and biopsy are important to prevent metastatic disease. They can also be seen in other chronic wounded, scarred or inflamed skin e.g. skin overlying focus of osteomyelitis, scars, radiotherapy scars and venous ulcers.\n\nA literature review of 412 cases of malignancy at burn wound sites found that 293 (71%) SCCs 48 (12%) BCCs, 23 (6%) melanomas, 21 (5%) sarcomas and the remaining were other neoplasms\n\n1\n\nCutaneous T-cell lymphoma appears as scaly pruritic patches or plaques. It would not arise in a scar, wound or burn.\n\nReferences.\n\n(1) Kowal-Vern A, Criswell B. Burn scar neoplasms: A literature review and statistical analysis. Burns. 2005;31(4):403-413."
