      "explanation": "Rosacea: a combination of topical ivermectin +  oral doxycycline is first-line for patients with severe papules and/or pustules\n\nThe condition being described is rosacea. It is a chronic skin condition typically affecting women aged 30-60 years. Early features include flushing and telangiectasia of the nose, cheeks and forehead; for some people, this develops into persistent erythema with papules and pustules, as in this case. Common triggers which exacerbate symptoms include sunlight, spicy foods, alcohol, caffeine and stress.\n\nIn this case, the patient has erythema and severe pustules. The recommended first-line treatment for severe pustular disease in rosacea is\n\ntopical ivermectin and oral doxycycline\n\nso this is the correct answer.\n\nFor mild-moderate pustular disease,\n\ntopical ivermectin\n\nmonotherapy is recommended. However, given the severe pustules in this case, this is an incorrect answer.\n\nTopical metronidazole\n\ncan also be used as an alternative to topical ivermectin in mild-moderate disease. As above, this answer is incorrect in this question because the patient has severe pustules.\n\nTopical brimonidine\n\ncan be used for patients with predominant flushing but limited telangiectasia. This is not appropriate in this case since her erythema is persistent and she has severe pustules.\n\nTopical hydrocortisone\n\nis not used in the management of rosacea."
