      "correctText": "Squamous cell carcinoma",
      "explanation": "The most common malignancy in the lower lip is a squamous cell carcinoma\n\nThis patient has a persistent indurated ulcerating lower lip lesion with an outdoor occupation with significant sun exposure (tour guide) and smoking history. Given the risk factors, a diagnosis of squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is likely. SCCs are the most common malignancy of the lip and mostly occurring on the lower lip.\n\nBasal cell carcinomas can occur on the lip. However, up to 90% of malignant lesions are squamous cell carcinomas\n\n1\n\nAphthous ulcers commonly occur on the inner aspect of the lip and mouth.\n\nHSV 1 causes oral-facial herpes. This patient has recurrent cold sores,  recurrent lesions typically resolve within a shorter timeframe and can be triggered by stress, sun exposure or a URTI.\n\nActinic cheilitis is characterised by a diffuse or patchy area of dryness with a variable thickening. The skin is typically dry and fragile. Actinic cheilitis most commonly affects the lower lip vermilion.\n\nReferences.\n\n1. Loh T, Rubin A, Brian Jiang S. Management of Mucosal Basal Cell Carcinoma of the Lip. Dermatologic Surgery. 2016;42(12):1313-1319."
