      "explanation": "Flexural psoriasis - topical steroid\n\nPsoriasis is a scaly skin condition causing red scaly and thickened patches of skin. Flexural psoriasis often called inverse psoriasis describes psoriasis found in skin folds and genitals. It tends to be shiny and smooth.\n\nStronger topical steroids are needed for a few days, used thinly and accurately applied to the specific area affected by psoriasis. It is the most effective first-line treatment in this case.\n\nSystemic agents such as methotrexate or cyclosporin are rarely needed for limited flexural psoriasis.\n\nPhototherapy is generally deemed ineffective because the skin folds are hidden from light exposure, as would be the case here.\n\nTopical antifungal therapy is recommended if there evidence to suggest that there is complication by yeasts in the form of Malassezia, but this is not the case here."
