      "id": 165,
      "question": "A 62-year-old woman attends the gynaecology outpatient clinic complaining of vulval irritation and soreness for several months. She describes persistent itching, particularly at night, and occasional discomfort during urination. She has noticed white patches on her vulva but denies any abnormal discharge or bleeding. Her medical history includes hypertension, managed with a thiazide diuretic. On examination, there are thin, white plaques affecting the vulval skin with some loss of normal architecture; no ulceration or masses are seen. No oral or cutaneous lesions are present elsewhere.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate next step in her management?",
