      "correctText": "Excision biopsy",
      "explanation": "Lichen\n\nTopical clobetasol propionate\n\nis the correct answer because this patient’s presentation is classic for lichen sclerosus: postmenopausal woman with pruritic, white atrophic plaques on the vulva and loss of normal anatomy. UK guidelines recommend high-potency topical corticosteroids (such as clobetasol propionate) as first-line treatment to control symptoms, reduce inflammation and prevent progression. Early intervention reduces scarring and lowers risk of squamous cell carcinoma transformation.\n\nTopical oestrogen cream\n\nmay be considered if symptoms were due to atrophic vaginitis secondary to oestrogen deficiency (which presents with vaginal dryness and dyspareunia rather than white plaques). However, it does not address the inflammatory autoimmune process seen in lichen sclerosus.\n\nOral prednisolone\n\nmight be used for severe or refractory cases unresponsive to topical steroids or where disease is very extensive; however, initial management per guidelines should always start with topical potent corticosteroids unless there is widespread involvement beyond what is described here.\n\nAntifungal therapy\n\n, such as topical azoles, would be indicated if candidiasis was suspected (usually presenting with erythema and cottage cheese-like discharge), but this patient's features—chronicity, architectural change and lack of discharge—make fungal infection unlikely.\n\nExcision biopsy\n\nmay be warranted if there were suspicion of malignancy (e.g., ulceration, induration or failure to respond to adequate topical steroid therapy after several weeks). In straightforward cases like this one without atypia or suspicious features, empirical treatment can proceed without immediate biopsy."
