      "correctText": "Red scaly patches of skin with silvery white scale",
      "explanation": "Grave's disease, orange peel shin lesions - pretibial myxoedema\n\nThe patient exhibits features of hyperthyroidism and diffuse goitre. Coupled with ophthalmopathy, her condition is indicative of Graves' disease. The third component of the classic triad of Graves' disease is pretibial myxoedema. This condition is characterized by bilateral, symmetrical, non-pitting, scaly thickening of the skin\n\nwith an orange-peel appearance\n\n. Pretibial myxoedema most commonly occurs over the lower legs, particularly on the pretibial areas or dorsum of the foot.\n\nErythematous tender nodules that heal without scarring\n\non the shins and lower leg are suggestive of erythema nodosum, which is not associated with Graves' disease. Rather, it is associated with the development of various disorders, such as streptococcal infection (the most common infectious cause), tuberculosis, leprosy, drug exposures, sarcoidosis, inflammatory bowel disease, pregnancy, and malignancy.\n\nRed papule turning to a deep necrotic ulcer with violaceous border\n\nis consistent with pyoderma gangrenosum. It starts as painful red papules usually in lower limbs, that later break down centrally and progress to the red, deep, necrotic ulcer with undermined violaceous or purplish edge. It is associated with inflammatory bowel disease, inflammatory arthritis, blood dyscrasias, immunodeficiencies and human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection.\n\nRed scaly patches of skin with silvery white scales\n\nare indicative of psoriasis. Her features are not consistent with psoriasis.\n\nNecrobiosis lipoidica is characterised by\n\nshiny, painless areas of yellow to pink-brown or red-brown skin with telangiectasia\n\n. It usually occurs on the shin and is associated with diabetes mellitus, rather than Graves' disease."
