      "explanation": "Otitis externa and blepharitis are common complications of seborrhoeic dermatitis\n\nThis patient's history is suggestive of\n\nseborrhoeic dermatitis\n\n. The description of a red, itchy rash, along with greasy, flaky scales particularly in the nasolabial folds fits with the diagnosis. The past history of otitis externa also fits with seborrhoeic dermatitis, as the latter is known to lead to episodes of the former. Similarly, this patient's presenting symptoms of ocular burning and dryness, along with crusty, erythematous eyelids, fit with blepharitis. This is a common complication of seborrhoeic dermatitis and should be managed using warm compresses of the lids and dry eye drops as necessary.\n\nIn the absence of a description of an allergen,\n\nallergic conjunctivitis\n\nis less likely. The description of lid erythema and crusting points more towards blepharitis, associated with the underlying seborrhoeic dermatitis.\n\nEpiscleritis\n\nis incorrect - this would present with generally red eyes, rather than the eyelid signs seen here.\n\nInfective conjunctivitis\n\nis incorrect. The history would be more focused on significant crusting in the mornings and copious discharge, rather than eyelid erythema and the background of seborrhoeic dermatitis.\n\nKeratitis\n\nwould instead present with more significant redness of the eyes, typically eye pain, and may affect vision. In the absence of these features, the history and examination is more consistent with blepharitis and underlying seborrhoeic dermatitis."
