      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Autosomal recessive",
      "explanation": "Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia - autosomal dominant\n\nThis patient's presentation is suggestive of hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT), also known as Osler-Weber-Rendu syndrome. This is characterised by telangiectasia over the skin and mucous membranes, epistaxis and visceral lesions (such as the gastrointestinal telangiectasias and arteriovenous malformations seen here). The condition is inherited in an\n\nautosomal dominant\n\nfashion, although in this case, the patient does not know of his biological family history.\n\nAutosomal recessive\n\nis incorrect. HHT is inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion. Autosomal recessive conditions include cystic fibrosis and thalassaemia.\n\nMitochondrial\n\nis incorrect. Mitochondrial conditions are generally rare. As mitochondrial DNA is inherited solely from the mother, diseases are also passed from mother to child. Leber hereditary optic neuropathy is a rare condition associated with mitochondrial inheritance.\n\nX-linked dominant\n\nis incorrect. Examples of such conditions include Vitamin D-resistant rickets and most cases of Alport syndrome. HHT, however, is autosomal dominant.\n\nX-linked recessive\n\nis incorrect. Examples of these conditions include haemophilia A and Duchenne muscular dystrophy."
