      "correctText": "Malignant melanoma",
      "explanation": "A non-healing painless ulcer associated with a chronic scar is indicative of squamous cell carcinoma (SCC)\n\nThis man has two risk factors for developing an\n\nSCC\n\n; a previous thermal injury and smoking. A nodule which develops into a non-healing ulcer over weeks to months is typical for SCC. These lesions may be painless and may bleed.\n\nThis scenario could also represent a\n\nBCC\n\nwhich develops on sun-exposed skin and over a previous area of a thermal burn injury. They also tend to ulcerate. However, BCCs grow more slowly and tend to be described as a pearly nodule with a central depression or a rolled border. You would therefore not expect the lesion described to have developed to this size within 3 weeks if it was a BCC.\n\nActinic keratoses\n\nare pre-malignant lesions which appear on sun-exposed areas. They tend to appear warty or scaly but do not ulcerate unless they progress to SCC. There is an association with sun exposure but not with thermal burn scars.\n\nDiabetic ulcers\n\ntend to affect the feet initially and result from neurovascular compromise resulting from poorly controlled or long-standing diabetes. There are no features to suggest that this man may be diabetic, such as polyuria or polydipsia, and there is no suggestion that there is any sensory loss or vascular compromise.\n\nMalignant melanoma\n\nresults from the cancerous growth of melanocytes. The first sign is normally an unusual-looking freckle or mole that becomes itchy, irregular or bleeds. As they progress they tend to become thickened and raised but they rarely ulcerate. There is a strong association with sun exposure and sunburn but not with thermal burn scars."
