      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Stevens-Johnson syndrome",
      "explanation": "Pemphigus vulgaris often presents with mucosal blistering. The skin blisters are often painful but not usually itchy\n\nPemphigus vulgaris classically presents in middle-aged patients with flaccid blisters and erosions, which are Nikolsky's sign positive (see below). Most patients also present with mucous membrane involvement.\n\nBullous pemphigoid is a good differential diagnosis and shares many of the same characteristics. It is more common is the elderly, however, and the blisters tend to be tense. Acantholysis would not be present on biopsy.\n\nStevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) can also present with mucosal involvement. It is usually a reaction to viruses or medications, such as allopurinol. SJS usually begins with a prodrome of fatigue, sore throat and fever. Oral ulcers, often with desquamation, can later develop.\n\nContact dermatitis is an allergic reaction which can cause bullae to form. However, you would expect some occupational exposure to an allergen or irritant (for example, washing detergents, cosmetics or latex).\n\nHerpes simplex virus (HSV) can present with oral or genital ulcers. HSV-1 is highly contagious and is transmitted via oral contact to cause 'cold sores'. Painful blisters can occur around the mouth but you would not expect the widespread skin lesions seen in pemphigus vulgaris."
