      "correctText": "Shoe contact dermatitis",
      "explanation": "Lichen\n\nThe clinical presentation of this patient's rash strongly indicates\n\nlichen planus\n\n, characterised by its distinctive patchy, purple appearance with Wickham's striae evident on the surface. Lichen planus is known for being intensely pruritic, which accounts for the excoriations observed in this patient. The aetiology of lichen planus remains elusive; however, it is not associated with coeliac disease—this association is a red herring.\n\nDermatitis herpetiformis\n\n, while also pruritic and associated with coeliac disease, presents differently from the rash described here. It typically affects extensor surfaces such as the shins and manifests as vesicular or blistering lesions, unlike the presentation in this case.\n\nLichen sclerosus\n\ntends to present as pruritic white patches, most commonly affecting the vulva in elderly women, though it can also occur on male genitalia or around the anus. This condition does not produce violaceous lesions and would not typically manifest at the ankles.\n\nShoe contact dermatitis\n\ngenerally occurs in individuals who wear poorly fitting footwear over prolonged periods or those allergic to materials within their shoes, such as leather. The clinical features differ from those seen in this case; it usually affects both the volar and dorsal aspects of the foot with an erythematous and blistering rash that may be pruritic or painful.\n\nTinea pedis\n\n, commonly referred to as athlete's foot, is a fungal infection that predominantly affects interdigital spaces. Its presentation includes dry, scaly white patches and skin cracking—distinctly different from what has been observed in this patient."
