      "question": "A 48-year-old man presents to his general practitioner with a skin lesion that has been enlarging over the past 4 weeks. His past medical history includes hypertension, type 2 diabetes and a renal transplant six years ago. His medications include amlodipine, doxazosin, linagliptin, prednisolone, tacrolimus and mycophenolate mofetil. He is a non-smoker and drinks minimal alcohol.\n\nOn examination, there is a 2mm erythematous scaly lesion on his right cheek. There is evidence of ulceration. There is no regional lymphadenopathy.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
