      "explanation": "Renal transplant patients - skin cancer (particularly squamous cell) is the most common malignancy secondary to immunosuppression\n\nSquamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is the correct answer. The incidence of SCC type skin cancer in renal transplant patients is up to 100x the rate of the non-transplant population. SCC type skin cancers are characterized by scaly lesions with relatively rapid growth, occurring in sun-exposed areas. They have a tendency to ulcerate.\n\nBasal cell carcinoma (BCC) is incorrect. While the risk of non-melanoma skin cancers like basal cell carcinoma is increased in patients with solid organ transplants on immunosuppression, they are less common than squamous cell carcinoma skin cancers. Furthermore, the typical description of a BCC is of a slow-growing translucent lesion with rolled edges and overlying telangiectasia, which does not fit the description in question.\n\nCutaneous B cell lymphoma is incorrect. This malignancy can occur post-transplant but is less common than an SCC. Furthermore, this type of cancer is characterized by the development of nodules, papules or plaques and is often associated with lymphadenopathy and B symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss).\n\nMalignant melanoma is incorrect. The rate of this cancer is increased in those post-renal transplant but significantly less than the SCC type. Additionally, this is typically a macular pigmented lesion.\n\nMerkel cell carcinoma is incorrect. While immunosuppression is a risk factor for this rare, aggressive skin cancer. It typically presents as a nodule and occurs with considerably less frequency than the SCC type."
