      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Intravenous antibiotics",
      "explanation": "Oral steroids are the first-line treatment for pyoderma gangrenosum\n\nOral prednisolone\n\nis the correct answer because oral corticosteroids are considered first-line therapy for pyoderma gangrenosum, especially in cases at risk of rapid progression or significant pain. The scenario describes classic features: an association with inflammatory bowel disease, pathergy (lesion following minor trauma), rapid evolution, and characteristic appearance (painful ulcer with undermined, violaceous edge). UK guidelines recommend starting systemic steroids promptly to reduce inflammation and prevent further tissue damage.\n\nIntravenous antibiotics\n\nwould be indicated if there was evidence of secondary bacterial infection or sepsis; however, pyoderma gangrenosum is a sterile neutrophilic dermatosis and not caused by infection. There are no signs of systemic infection in this case (no fever or laboratory evidence provided), so antibiotics are not appropriate as first-line treatment.\n\nSurgical debridement\n\nshould be avoided initially in pyoderma gangrenosum due to the risk of pathergy—worsening or new lesions triggered by trauma or surgery. Surgery may exacerbate the condition before immunosuppression has brought it under control. Only when disease activity is controlled should any surgical intervention be considered.\n\nTopical tacrolimus\n\ncan be used for milder or localised cases but is insufficient as monotherapy for rapidly progressing or extensive ulcers such as described here. Systemic therapy is preferred in severe presentations to achieve faster disease control.\n\nCiclosporin\n\nis an effective immunosuppressive agent and may be considered for refractory cases or where steroids are contraindicated. However, according to current UK practice guidelines, oral corticosteroids remain the recommended first-line option due to their efficacy and rapid onset of action."
