      "explanation": "Dermatitis in acral, peri-orificial and perianal distribution → ?zinc deficiency\n\nZinc deficiency\n\nis correct. The patient has untreated Crohn's disease, which puts him at risk of zinc deficiency. Additionally, he is a thin homeless alcoholic and so his nutritional intake is likely to be poor. Dermatitis in an acral, peri-orificial, and perianal distribution suggests zinc deficiency.\n\nB12 deficiency\n\nis incorrect. This has myriad clinical features including sub-acute combined degeneration of the spinal cord. This patient has a normal neurological examination counting against this diagnosis. It can cause skin hyperpigmentation, vitiligo, angular stomatitis, and hair changes but does not typically cause this characteristic distribution of dermatitis.\n\nParkinson's disease\n\nis incorrect. This can cause seborrhoeic dermatitis but mainly affects the scalp. In addition, there are no other obvious neurological features to suggest the condition.\n\nPsoriasis\n\nis incorrect. This is typically a scaly rash affecting the scalp, elbows, knees, lumbosacral area, and the body folds.\n\nThiamine deficiency\n\nis incorrect. This can cause Wernicke's encephalopathy characterized by a triad of ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and confusion. Alcohol excess and malnutrition are risk factors."
