      "explanation": "Lichen sclerosus: itchy white spots typically seen on the vulva of elderly women\n\nThe excoriation simply points to the patch being itchy (and therefore the patient scratching it, causing excoriation). White patches are typical of lichen sclerosus, which often occurs near the genital area. Exam questions will often involve an elderly female and the lesion appearing on the vulva. However, it is important to note that it may happen in other areas, such as the thighs and anal region. In males, it usually affects the glans penis, in which case it is called balanitis xerotica obliterans.\n\nHerpes simplex would present with clusters of inflamed papules and vesicles and not with a single white patch. It would also be painful, rather than only itchy.\n\nVitiligo is a reasonable differential as there is a depigmented patch. Patchy loss of skin color, which usually first appears on the hands, face, and areas around body openings and the genitals is characteristic of vitiligo. Although it may appear at any time from birth to senescence, onset is most commonly observed in persons aged 10-30 years with an average age of onset of 20 years. It is not typically so itchy as to cause notable excoriation, unlike lichen. For these reasons it is not the most likely diagnosis.\n\nCandidiasis (thrush) would present with cottage cheese-like discharge and itching and does not typically present with a single white patch, though oral thrush may present with a white patch on the tongue that can be scraped off.\n\nPsoriasis would present with a well-demarcated erythematous patch covered with a silvery scale. It is unlikely to present for the first time in a patient this age and in this area."
