      "explanation": "Lentigo maligna melanoma: Suspicious freckle on face or scalp of chronically sun-exposed patients\n\nLentigo maligna melanoma is a type of invasive skin cancer that usually starts as a tan-brown macule or patch described here or Hutchinson's melanotic freckle. Lentigo maligna is an early form of melanoma whereby malignant cells are confined to the tissue of origin, the epidermis, hence it is known as in situ melanoma. It occurs in sun-damaged skin so is usually seen on the face or neck.\n\nAmelanotic melanoma is a type of skin cancer where cells do not produce melanin. They can be pink, red, purple or normal skin colour, hence hard to recognise. It has an asymmetrical shape and an irregular faintly pigmented border. Risk factors for developing amelanotic melanoma include ageing and sun-exposed skin.\n\nBasal cell carcinoma is incorrect as they typically present as waxy shiny papules with a central depression and are smooth in appearance. They can have telangiectasia over the surface. It is the most prevalent type of skin cancer but is not being described in this case.\n\nIntradermal naevus is also known as intradermal melanocytic naevus is basically a classic mole or birthmark. It typically appears as an elevated, dome-shaped bump on the skin surface. Therefore, this is unlikely to be the correct answer.\n\nCutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (cSCC) is a common type of non-melanoma skin cancer. It is derived from cells within the epidermis that make keratin. Clinically presenting as scaly or crusted lumps which are tender on palpation and may ulcerate. These features are not evident in this particular case."
