      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Porphobilinogen synthase",
      "explanation": "Porphyria cutanea tarda is caused by an inherited defect in uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase\n\nThe correct answer is a defect in\n\nuroporphyrinogen decarboxylase\n\n, leading to the presentation of porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT). Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase converts uroporphyrinogen III into coproporphyrinogen III, one of the many reactions in synthesizing heme.  The patient has a new blistering rash on sun-exposed areas of the body after a holiday to a country with a hot climate, implying photosensitivity. Given the familial history and raised urinary uroporphyrinogen the most likely diagnosis is PCT.\n\nPCT classically presents with a photosensitive, fragile and blistering rash on the face and dorsal aspects of the hands. Hypertrichosis and hyperpigmentation may also be present. Patients will have a raised uroporphyrinogen and pink fluorescence of the urine when viewed under Wood's lamp.\n\nAll of the options are involved in the biosynthesis of haem and are disrupted by other causes of pathologies:\n\nALA synthase\n\nconverts glycine and succinyl-CoA to delta-aminolevulinic acid. It is disrupted in X-linked sideroblastic anaemia.\n\nPorphobilinogen deaminase\n\nconverts porphobilinogen into hydroxymethylbilane and is defective in acute intermittent porphyria.\n\nPorphobilinogen synthase\n\nis responsible for the conversion of delta-aminolevulinic acid into porphobilinogen and is disrupted in lead poisoning. It is also known as ALA dehydratase.\n\nProtoporphyrin ferrochelatase\n\nis responsible for the conversion of protoporphyrin into haem and is also defective in lead poisoning. It is implicated more in the development of erythropoietic protoporphyria."
