      "explanation": "Porphyria cutanea tarda is caused by an inherited defect in uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase\n\nInherited defect in uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase\n\nis correct. The patient presents with a blistering rash on the face and dorsal hands provoked by sunlight and alcohol. In addition, there is hypertrichosis (excessive hair growth in non-androgen-dependent areas of the body). These findings suggest a diagnosis of porphyria cutanea tarda. This can be inherited or acquired due to hepatocyte damage (i.e. hepatitis C infection or alcohol excess). In the setting of negative testing for hepatitis C and the absence of alcohol excess, this is likely due to an inherited defect in uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase. Hepatitis C is a common cause of porphyria cutanea tarda and serology should always be checked in suspected cases.\n\nInhibition of Δ-aminolevulinic acid dehydratase (ALAD)\n\nis incorrect. This is another defect in the haem metabolism pathway and is the main mechanism by which lead poisoning causes iron deficiency. The patient is not anaemic and there is no suggestion of lead poisoning, which does not cause this type of blistering rash or hypertrichosis.\n\nMutations in porphobilinogen deaminase\n\nis incorrect. This is another defect in the haem metabolism pathway, resulting in acute intermittent porphyria. Recurrent attacks of abdominal pain and neurological dysfunction including peripheral neuropathy and seizures characterise this. These features are not present here and so it is PCT rather than AIP that is the likely disorder.\n\nMutations in the gene that encodes Δ-aminolevulinic acid (ALA) synthetase\n\nare incorrect. This is another defect in the haem metabolism pathway. This causes sideroblastic anaemia. This is a differential for microcytic anaemia. This patient is not anaemic and therefore this is not the diagnosis."
