      "explanation": "Keloid scars - more common in young, black, male adults\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nyoung black adults\n\n.\n\nThis patient has keloid scar formation, which is excessive scar formation usually occurring following skin injury, and which extends beyond the original wound margin. Keloid scars are more common in\n\nyoung black adults.\n\nIn a study, as many as 16% of black individuals reported keloid scar formation.\n\nThose with highly pigmented skin are 15 times more likely to develop keloid scars than those with less pigmented skin. Incidence is higher during puberty and pregnancy. While they can occur anywhere on the body, certain areas are more prone, such as the upper chest, shoulders, neck and ears.\n\nKeloid scar development is rare at the extremes of age. They rarely occur in the\n\nelderly\n\n, in\n\nyoung children\n\nand are less common in\n\ncaucasian\n\npatients."
