      "explanation": "Topical eflornithine is the treatment of choice for facial hirsutism\n\nEflornithine\n\nis the correct answer. The patient has thick dark hair on her face, which is consistent with facial hirsutism. It is likely that obesity is the cause in this case. Topical eflornithine is a treatment for facial hirsutism and when applied to the skin works by blocking the action of an enzyme in hair follicles that causes hair growth.\n\nCorticosteroids\n\nis incorrect. This condition would both worsen her obesity and may actually cause rather than treat hirsutism.\n\nDiazoxide\n\nis incorrect. This is used in the management of hypoglycaemia in those patients who use too much insulin. Rather than treating hirsutism, it causes hypertrichosis (which is androgen-independent hair growth).\n\nMetformin\n\nis incorrect. This may be effective in treating hirsutism in patients with polycystic ovary syndrome, which is not present in this case. In those patients with hirsutism limited to the face, topical eflornithine is a more appropriate choice.\n\nMinoxidil\n\nis incorrect. This is actually used to stimulate hair growth in men and women with hair loss."
