      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Still's disease",
      "explanation": "Livedo reticularis can be caused by SLE\n\nSystemic lupus erythematosus\n\nis correct. The description of a rash i.e. a net-like pattern of reddish-blue skin discolouration that is non-blanching is suggestive of livedo reticularis (LR). LR results from disturbed blood flow to the skin resulting in low blood flow and reduced oxygen tension at the skin surface. This results in higher amounts of deoxygenated haemoglobin resulting in subsequent discolouration. Postulated mechanisms in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) relate to microvascular thrombosis (in cases of an associated antiphospholipid syndrome), vasculitis and altered vascular tone. LR is associated with many other infective, inflammatory, haematological and malignant conditions.\n\nMeasles\n\nis incorrect. LR is not associated with measles. The pathognomonic rash in measles is 'Koplik spots', white spots on the buccal mucosa with a blue-white centre and surrounding erythema.\n\nRheumatic fever\n\nis incorrect. LR is not a feature of rheumatic fever. The cardinal rash in rheumatic fever is erythema marginatum described as a distinct type of annular erythema. The rash appears as red macules that spread outwards in a circular shape. As the lesions advance the edges elevate and become erythematous while the centre clears, giving the characteristic appearance.\n\nSarcoidosis\n\nis incorrect. There is a diverse range of cutaneous manifestations of sarcoidosis including erythema nodosum (violaceous tender nodules) and lupus pernio (violaceous indurated plaques on the central face) but LR is not a feature of sarcoidosis, unlike many other inflammatory conditions.\n\nStill's disease\n\nis incorrect. A transient rash can be associated with Still's disease. However, it tends to be erythematous and blotchy and occurs typically on the chest rather than the legs. It is not provoked by cold and does not have a 'net-like' pattern."
