      "explanation": "Eczema herpeticum is a serious condition that requires IV antivirals\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nintravenous aciclovir\n\n. This case describes eczema herpeticum (Kaposi's varicelliform eruption), which is a widespread herpes simplex virus infection occurring in patients with pre-existing skin conditions, most commonly atopic eczema. The presentation of clustered painful blisters, fever, and sudden worsening in a child with atopic eczema is characteristic. This is a medical emergency requiring prompt treatment with systemic antiviral therapy to prevent complications such as disseminated infection, keratoconjunctivitis, and potential mortality.\n\nAdvise paracetamol + emollients and reassure\n\nwould be inadequate management for eczema herpeticum. While these measures may provide symptomatic relief, they do not address the underlying viral infection which requires specific antiviral treatment.\n\nPotent topical steroid\n\nwould be contraindicated in this situation. Using topical steroids in the presence of active viral infection could worsen the condition by suppressing local immune responses and allowing the infection to spread.\n\nIntravenous flucloxacillin\n\nwould not be appropriate as first-line treatment. While secondary bacterial infection can occur, the primary problem is viral and requires antiviral therapy. Antibiotics may be added if there is evidence of bacterial superinfection.\n\nOral fluconazole\n\nwould not be appropriate as this is an antifungal medication. The presentation is not consistent with a fungal infection, and antifungal therapy would not address the underlying viral cause of eczema herpeticum."
