      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Gastroduodenoscopy",
      "explanation": "The most common malignancy associated with acanthosis nigricans\n\nThis patient has acanthosis nigricans, a dermatological condition characterised by darker and thickened lesions typically involving the axilla, groin and back of the neck. Given this patient's age and the rapid onset of his presentation, it is likely that this patient has malignant acanthosis nigricans. Symptoms of pruritis and the presence of more extensive lesions involving the palms, soles and mucosa is more typical of malignant acanthosis nigricans. The most common underlying malignancy associated with this condition is gastric cancer. Risk factors for gastric adenocarcinoma include smoking and male sex. Along with computed tomography (CT) imaging, a gastroduodenoscopy would be indicated to investigate for a gastric malignancy.\n\nAcanthosis nigricans is associated with multiple underlying conditions with one common condition being diabetes mellitus. Therefore, it is important to measure this patient's fasting blood glucose. However, diabetes is less likely to cause such severe symptoms compared to malignancy. Therefore, although it is a necessary investigation to complete, it will not confirm this patient's underlying diagnosis.\n\nA chest x-ray would be appropriate if an underlying lung malignancy was expected. Whilst it should be considered in a patient with appearances suggestive of malignancy and a background of COPD, the most common malignancy associated with acanthosis nigricans is gastric adenocarcinoma.\n\nA synacthen test is used to diagnose Addison's disease. Secondary to excess ACTH acting on melanocytes, patients with Addison's disease may have patches of hyperpigmented skin. However, the pattern of skin involvement is different to acanthosis nigricans and it would not explain this patient's pruritis or papilliform mucosal involvement, making it a less suitable choice.\n\nA skin biopsy can be taken in patients with acanthosis nigricans if there is no clear underlying cause. It is also a useful diagnostic test to rule out other dermatological conditions, particularly those also associated with malignancy such as dermatomyositis. However, this patient's presentation points toward an underlying malignancy for which the most appropriate investigation out the options given would be a gastroduodenoscopy."
