      "explanation": "Ketoconazole shampoo is used to treat pityriasis versicolor\n\nThe description of the rash is consistent with pityriasis versicolor (also known as tinea versicolor). It is a fungal infection of the skin (caused by Malassezia) that is localised to the outermost layer of the epidermis. It is characterised by scaly, discoloured patches that do not tan in the sun and may cause mild itching. Risk factors for this condition include hyperhidrosis, malnutrition, immunodeficiency and being in a warm, humid environment.\n\nKetoconazole shampoo\n\nis the first-line treatment for pityriasis versicolor as it targets the Malassezia yeast responsible for the condition. It is usually applied once daily for up to 5 days.\n\nHydrocortisone cream\n\nis a low-potency corticosteroid that is used to relieve symptoms in several skin conditions that are characterised by inflammation such as eczema and insect bites. Pityriasis versicolor does not tend to cause significant skin inflammation so steroid creams are not appropriate.\n\nOral prednisolone\n\nmay be used to treat severe inflammatory or autoimmune skin conditions (e.g. severe eczema). It is not used for fungal infections like pityriasis versicolor.\n\nOral terbinafine\n\nis an antifungal treatment used for dermatophyte infections such as onychomycosis or tinea corporis. It is not used in the treatment of pityriasis versicolor.\n\nTopical clindamycin\n\nis an antibiotic used for skin conditions that are associated with bacterial overgrowth (e.g. acne) and is not used to treat fungal skin infections."
