      "explanation": "Linear, well-demarcated skin lesions that appear suddenly, with 'la belle indifference'  → ?dermatitis artefacta\n\nDermatitis artefacta is the most likely diagnosis. It is a condition in which lesions are self-inflicted, usually owing to a psychological problem. It could be a form of deliberate self-harm or part of attention-seeking behaviour. Typically, it presents with skin lesions that are particularly geometrical (e.g. linear), appear overnight and in easily accessible areas (such as the limbs or face). These patients often deny causing the lesions themselves. The patient's grades have been falling, which could be a cause or a consequence of psychological difficulties, which may have led her to this particular type of self-harming behaviour.\n\nExcoriated acne simply refers to acne that has been 'picked' at or scratched, causing excoriation of the skin. 'Acne excoriee des jeunes filles' is a type of neurotic or psychogenic excoriation. It is a reasonable differential in that the excoriation is self-inflicted. However, there is no mention of any signs of underlying acne (papules, comedones or pustules) to begin with, and it would be an unusual location for acne. Furthermore, patients with excoriated acne tend to admit that they pick it themselves, as opposed to patients with dermatitis artefacta.\n\nDermatitis herpetiformis would present with itchy blisters and raised red skin lesions, typically on the buttocks, elbows, knees, lower back and scalp of patients with coeliac disease. None of these features is present in this patient.\n\nEczema causes erythematous patches of skin that are often dry, itchy, cracked and/or sore. While it is an extremely common condition, it does not match the patient's presentation - eczema does not tend to cause geometric well-defined shapes or to appear overnight.\n\nEczema herpeticum is a rare but serious manifestation of herpes infection, typically occurring in young children with eczema or a history of atopy. It often requires admission. Although this child has asthma, nothing else in the history points to this diagnosis, and this teenager is not unwell."
