      "explanation": "Niacin (B3) deficiency is characterised by dermatitis, diarrhoea and dementia, a condition known as pellagra\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nNiacin\n\n. The patient's symptoms of a red-brown, symmetrical, scaly rash on the skin, poor appetite, nausea and diarrhoea are characteristic of pellagra, which is caused by a deficiency in niacin (vitamin B3). Niacin plays a crucial role in cellular metabolism and energy production. Chronic alcoholism can lead to malnutrition and poor absorption of essential vitamins and minerals, including niacin.\n\nFolic acid\n\ndeficiency can cause megaloblastic anaemia with symptoms such as fatigue, pallor, shortness of breath and glossitis (inflammation of the tongue). Although alcoholics are at risk for folic acid deficiency due to malnutrition and poor absorption, the patient's symptoms do not fit this diagnosis.\n\nA deficiency in\n\nThiamine\n\n(vitamin B1) can result in Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome or beriberi. Symptoms include peripheral neuropathy, muscle weakness, confusion, memory loss and ophthalmoplegia. While chronic alcoholism is a common cause of thiamine deficiency, the patient's presentation does not match these conditions.\n\nVitamin B6\n\n(pyridoxine) deficiency may cause peripheral neuropathy, irritability and depression. However, these symptoms are not consistent with the patient's presentation.\n\nFinally,\n\nZinc\n\ndeficiency can lead to dermatitis (skin inflammation), hair loss and immune dysfunction but does not fully explain the specific rash pattern described in this case."
