      "question": "A 45-year-old male with known HIV attends the genitourinary medicine clinic for a review.\n\nA repeat CD4 count is taken:\n\n\nCD4 count\t197 cells/mm³ (500-1,400 cells/mm³)\n\nThe patient is started on co-trimoxazole, for prophylaxis against opportunistic infections, and continued on triple antiretroviral therapy of tenofovir, emtricitabine and raltegravir.\n\nEight days later the patient presents to the emergency department. He reports starting to feel generally unwell 3 days prior to his attendance with intermittent fevers and generalised myalgia. The patient also reports that he has developed a rash and sore eyes and lips in the last 24 hours.\n\nOn examination, the patient is found to have a tender area of erythema over the upper half of the torso with flaccid blistering of the skin. There is conjunctivitis in both eyes and tender blistering lesions are seen over the orobuccal mucosa.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
