      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Kaposi sarcoma",
      "explanation": "Mucosal involvement is prominent and severe in Stevens-Johnson syndrome\n\nPatients with HIV can present with multiple different dermatological conditions. Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) is 100 times more common in patients with HIV. In this scenario, the patient was started on co-trimoxazole, a sulphonamide antibiotic, which is known to precipitate SJS. Presentations of SJS typically start within 1 week but can present up to one month after starting antibiotics. Where anticonvulsants are the causative agent, the presentation can be delayed by up to two months.\n\nSJS presents initially with a prodromal illness of intermittent fevers, myalgia and a sore throat. After a few days, a painful, erythematous skin rash develops abruptly over the trunk and arms. Blisters then appear and merge to form sheets of detached skin. Mucosal involvement is prominent and severe with at least two mucosal surfaces becoming involved. In this case, the patient developed conjunctivitis and painful ulcerations on the lips.\n\nBullous pemphigoid presents with tense skin blistering and the mucosal surfaces are rarely involved. There is no increased association with HIV also.\n\nKaposi sarcoma is a condition more commonly seen in immunocompromised patients. It can present anywhere on the skin or mucosal surfaces, presenting initially as a painless erythematous lesion and can progress into painful ulcerations. The lesions are initially more discrete and present much more slowly than is reported here.\n\nPruritic papular eruption of HIV is a diagnosis of exclusion in patients with HIV presenting with a rash. Here there are other more likely differential diagnoses such as SJS. The condition can present at any time during HIV infection and presents with small erythematous papules which are intensely itchy. The mucosal surfaces are also spared.\n\nStaphylococcal scalded skin syndrome has been reported in the literature to occur in adult patients with HIV, however, this is extremely rare. It has a greater association in adults with chronic renal failure, but again this is still rare. This condition is typically seen in children under 5 years of age. The presentation is similar to SJS with a blistering, painful, erythematous skin rash extending over the body surfaces. Importantly, staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome does not involve the mucosal surfaces, whereas SJS does."
