      "correctText": "Topical ivermectin",
      "explanation": "Rosacea treatment:\n\nTopical ivermectin\n\nis correct. The patient presents with an erythematous rash along with papules/pustules affecting her nose, cheeks, and forehead. This is typical of rosacea. The first line of management in mild-moderate cases is topical ivermectin. The underlying aetiology of rosacea is complex and yet to be entirely elucidated. There is a probable multifactorial aetiology to the disease including immune and vascular dysfunction, neurogenic dysregulation, presence of cutaneous microorganisms, ultraviolet damage, and skin barrier dysfunction. Ivermectin is postulated to have an anti-inflammatory and anti-parasitic effect, targeting cutaneous organisms like Demodex mites.\n\nOral doxycycline\n\nis incorrect. This can be used in combination with topical ivermectin in severe cases of rosacea. It has anti-inflammatory and anti-angiogenic properties that make it useful in the treatment of rosacea.\n\nOral hydroxychloroquine\n\nis incorrect. This is an anti-malarial used in the management of systemic lupus erythematosus. It can be used for the skin manifestations of the disease. Malar rash affecting the cheeks and nose in lupus tends to spare the nasolabial folds, which are involved in this case.\n\nOral lymecycline\n\nis incorrect. This is an option for the treatment of acne. However, erythema in acne tends to be local to the papules/pustules rather than diffusely affecting a wider area like in rosacea. Therefore, rosacea is the more likely diagnosis in this case.\n\nTopical steroids\n\nare incorrect. This can be used for a wide variety of inflammatory skin disorders (although should be minimised on the face where possible). It is not the first-line treatment of rosacea, which is the most likely diagnosis here."
