      "explanation": "Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia - autosomal dominant\n\nThis case presents a patient with suspected hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT), also known as Osler-Weber-Rendu syndrome. HHT is an inherited condition characterized by an\n\nautosomal dominant pattern of inheritance and age-related penetrance\n\n, with the majority of individuals manifesting symptoms such as recurrent epistaxis (nosebleeds) during adolescence.\n\nIn cases where HHT is suspected, it is imperative to conduct a family history and consider referral for genetic testing. Most cases are attributable to mutations in the endoglin or ACVRLK1 genes, though mutations in other genes, including SMAD4, RASA1, and BMPR9, may also be causative.\n\nIt is essential to understand that HHT follows an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern; it does not conform to\n\nautosomal recessive, X-linked dominant, or X-linked recessive\n\npatterns.\n\nThe term\n\nanticipation\n\nrefers to the phenomenon where a condition will present itself at a younger age with each successive generation. This phenomenon is not observed in HHT."
