      "explanation": "Orlistat works by inhibiting gastric and pancreatic lipase to reduce the digestion of fat\n\nInhibits pancreatic lipase\n\nis correct. Orlistat inhibits pancreatic lipase, preventing the breakdown of dietary triglycerides into absorbable fatty acids and monoglycerides. Undigested fats reach the colon, where bacterial metabolism produces gas and draws water, causing steatorrhea, flatulence, and urgency. This mechanism directly explains her gastrointestinal symptoms and their timing after starting the medication.\n\nInhibits HMG-CoA reductase\n\nis incorrect because it reductase regulates hepatic cholesterol synthesis, and its inhibition (e.g., by statins) lowers LDL cholesterol. This mechanism does not affect fat digestion or absorption in the gut, so it cannot cause oily stools, flatulence, or urgent bowel movements. The patient’s symptoms are therefore inconsistent with this mechanism.\n\nInhibits acetyl-CoA carboxylase\n\nis incorrect because it is involved in fatty acid synthesis in the liver and adipose tissue, not intestinal fat digestion. Inhibiting it affects systemic lipid metabolism but does not lead to postprandial fat malabsorption. This mechanism cannot explain the patient’s oily stools or bowel urgency following orlistat initiation.\n\nInhibits fatty acid synthase\n\nis incorrect because it elongates fatty acid chains during lipogenesis. Its inhibition alters fat synthesis but does not impair dietary fat absorption, so it cannot account for steatorrhea or urgent bowel movements. The patient’s gastrointestinal symptoms are unrelated to fatty acid synthase inhibition.\n\nInhibits hormone-sensitive lipase\n\nis incorrect because it mobilises stored triglycerides from adipose tissue during fasting and does not affect the digestion or absorption of dietary fats in the intestine. Inhibiting this enzyme would not produce oily stools, flatulence, or postprandial urgency, so it does not match this patient’s presentation."
