      "explanation": "Raised total T3 and T4 but normal fT3 and fT4 suggest high concentrations of thyroid binding globulin, which can be seen during pregnancy\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nnormal pregnancy\n\n. This patient has a normal TSH, with normal fT3 and fT4 levels, suggesting normal thyroid function. The elevated levels of total T3 and T4 can be explained by the high concentrations of thyroid-binding globulin that are often seen during pregnancy. Although the patient has reported some fatigue and minor weight gain, these symptoms are non-specific in the context of pregnancy and do not necessarily point towards thyroid dysfunction.\n\nHyperthyroidism\n\nis incorrect. This would be associated with an abnormally suppressed TSH and raised levels of fT3 and fT4.\n\nHypothyroidism\n\nis incorrect. Although the patient's symptoms are seen in many patients with hypothyroidism, this would tend to be associated with a raised TSH and decreased fT3 and fT4.\n\nSubclinical hyperthyroidism\n\nis incorrect. This would tend to be characterised by an isolated suppressed TSH, with normal fT3 and fT4 and an absence of symptoms of thyroid dysfunction.\n\nSubclinical hypothyroidism\n\nis incorrect. This would normally present with an isolated elevated TSH, with normal fT3 and fT4."
