      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Deleted in colorectal cancer tumour suppressor gene",
      "explanation": "Familial adenomatous polyposis is due to a mutation in a tumour suppressor gene called adenomatous polyposis coli gene (APC)\n\nThis is a typical history of familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP), a rare autosomal dominant condition resulting from the inheritance of a mutated\n\nadenomatous polyposis coli tumour suppressor gene\n\n(APC). Children may be identified as carriers of this gene via genetic testing which is carried out due to strong family history of cancer. Once identified, children have regular sigmoidoscopies or colonoscopies from age 10-12 and once polyps are identified, a total colectomy is planned and normally happens in the patient's early 20s. Up to 100% of patients with FAP will develop colorectal cancer if polyps are not removed. FAP is also associated with stomach cancer and therefore patients also require regular upper GI endoscopy.\n\nAdenomatous polyposis coli oncogene\n\nis incorrect because APC is a tumour suppressor gene, meaning that it is the loss of function of the gene that results in an increased risk of cancer.\n\nHereditary non-polyposis colorectal carcinoma (HNPCC) is a slightly more common cause of colorectal cancer caused by autosomal dominant inheritance of mutated\n\nMutS Homolog 2 gene\n\n(MSH2) or MLH1 genes. Once diagnosed, HNPCC does not normally require screening with colonoscopy until at least aged 20. Regular upper endoscopy is required due to the risk of stomach cancer and regular pelvic ultrasound is also required in women due to the risk of ovarian cancer. The lifetime risk of colorectal cancer is 20-80% and therefore patients with HNPCC may eventually require colectomy, although at a later stage than FAP.\n\nSporadic colon cancer is more likely in patients with certain genetic mutations including allelic loss of the APC gene, deletion of the\n\nprotein P53 tumour suppressor gene\n\n(p53), deletion of\n\ndeleted in colorectal cancer tumour suppressor gene\n\n(DCC) and activation of K-ras. This scenario is not consistent with sporadic colon cancer due to the regular screening and early colectomy."
