      "explanation": "Angiodysplasia is associated with aortic stenosis\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nejection systolic murmur heard loudest on expiration\n\ndue to aortic stenosis. The patient presents with iron deficiency anaemia and has returned two positive faecal immunochemical tests (FIT), indicative of gastrointestinal haemorrhage. Angiodysplasia, when situated in the small intestine beyond the detection range of standard upper-GI endoscopy and colonoscopy, necessitates the use of capsule endoscopy for identification. It is important to note that angiodysplasia is associated with aortic stenosis, which is characterised by an ejection systolic murmur that can be heard radiating towards the carotid arteries and is loudest on expiration.\n\nAn\n\nearly diastolic murmur\n\ncommonly suggests aortic regurgitation. There is no established correlation between aortic regurgitation and angiodysplasia. While pulmonary regurgitation may also present with an early diastolic murmur, it is less frequently encountered than aortic regurgitation and similarly does not associate with angiodysplasia.\n\nAn\n\nejection systolic murmur heard louder on inspiration\n\nis characteristic of pulmonary stenosis or an atrial septal defect. Neither are associated with angiodysplasia.\n\nMid diastolic\n\nmurmurs are frequently associated with mitral stenosis, often due to rheumatic heart disease ' a condition that has seen a decline in prevalence within the UK. Mitral stenosis does not have a connection to angiodysplasia.\n\nPansystolic\n\nmurmurs are indicative of mitral or tricuspid regurgitation; neither condition has an association with angiodysplasia. Tricuspid regurgitation often occurs in association with infective endocarditis. Although mitral regurgitation can also result from endocarditis, it more commonly arises due to degenerative mitral valve disease (including mitral valve prolapse)."
