      "correctText": "Autosomal recessive",
      "explanation": "Peutz-Jeghers syndrome - autosomal dominant\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nAutosomal dominant\n\n. Peutz-Jeghers syndrome (PJS) is a rare hereditary condition that follows an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern. This means that an individual only needs to inherit one copy of the faulty gene from either parent to develop the condition. In this mode of inheritance, both males and females can be equally affected and have a 50% chance of passing on the gene to their offspring.\n\nAutosomal recessive\n\nis incorrect as this would require both parents to carry a copy of the faulty gene, with a 25% chance for each child to inherit the disease. Autosomal recessive conditions are often seen in consanguineous families or isolated populations, which does not fit with PJS.\n\nMitochondrial inheritance\n\n, also known as maternal inheritance, is incorrect because it refers to conditions caused by mutations in the mitochondrial DNA. These conditions are exclusively passed down from the mother to all her children but not by fathers. PJS is caused by mutations in nuclear genes (specifically STK11/LKB1), not mitochondrial genes.\n\nThe options\n\nX-linked dominant\n\nand\n\nX-linked recessive\n\nare also incorrect as they refer to genetic disorders caused by mutations on the X chromosome. Males with X-linked dominant disorders pass the condition onto all their daughters but none of their sons, while females can pass it on to both sons and daughters depending on whether they are heterozygous or homozygous for the trait. For X-linked recessive disorders, males are more frequently affected than females. Females carrying one copy of the mutation are carriers and can pass it on to their sons or daughters. However, PJS is not linked to any sex chromosomes but rather an autosomal chromosome."
