      "correctText": "Reduced secretion of gastrin",
      "explanation": "Secretin increases secretion of bicarbonate-rich fluid from pancreas and hepatic duct cells\n\nSecretin is a digestive hormone secreted by S cells in the upper small intestine in response to acidic chyme and fatty acids. Its main actions are to\n\nincrease the secretion of bicarbonate-rich fluid from the pancreas and hepatic duct cells\n\n, decrease gastric acid secretion and promote the growth of pancreatic acinar cells. It can be used as a stimulation test to investigate the cause of pancreatic malabsorption.\n\nOther gastric hormones such as cholecystokinin and vasoactive intestinal peptide also act to increase the secretion of pancreatic fluid.\n\nAs such, the answer of\n\nreduced secretion of bicarbonate-rich fluid from the pancreas and hepatic duct cells\n\nis incorrect since secretin has the opposite effect in healthy people.\n\nSecretin acts to reduce gastric acid secretion. Gastrin\n\nincreases gastric acid secretion\n\n. As such, this answer would not be expected of the test in this context so is incorrect.\n\nGastrin secretion is increased by stimuli such as distension of the stomach, vagal tone (mediated by gastrin-releasing peptide) and luminal peptides/amino acids. Secretin does not\n\nincrease gastrin secretion\n\nso this answer is incorrect.\n\nGastrin secretion is reduced\n\nby low antral pH and stimulation by somatostatin. Secretin does not directly affect gastrin secretion and hence this answer is incorrect."
