      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "D cells in pancreas and stomach",
      "explanation": "Somatostatin is produced by D cells in the pancreas & stomach\n\nThe medication that this man has been given most likely refers to octreotide, a synthetic somatostatin analogue that is licensed to treat symptoms associated with carcinoid tumours with features of carcinoid syndrome, particularly flushing and diarrhoea. It reduces the secretion of serotonin which is responsible for these symptoms. This peptide hormone is naturally produced by D (delta) cells in the pancreas and stomach and is thought to act to reduce acid secretion from gastric parietal cells.\n\nThe anterior pituitary gland is incorrect. As well as the delta cells in the gastrointestinal system, somatostatin is also released from the hypothalamus where it inhibits the release of growth hormone from the anterior pituitary. However, somatostatin is not synthesised in the pituitary gland itself.\n\nGastrin is secreted from the G cells in the antrum of the stomach and is not the correct answer here.\n\nThe gastric parietal cells are responsible for secreting hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor and is, therefore, an incorrect option.\n\nThe S cells in the small intestine is an incorrect option. These cells release secretin, which promotes the release of bicarbonate from the pancreas into the intestinal lumen."
