      "explanation": "Flushing, diarrhoea, bronchospasm, tricuspid stenosis, pellagra → carcinoid with liver mets - diagnosis: urinary 5-HIAA\n\nThis patient's presentation is suggestive of carcinoid syndrome. This is most commonly due to liver metastases, although can also occur due to neuroendocrine tumours of the gut and bronchi. The secretion of serotonin, kinins and amines leads to flushing - the earliest symptom - along with unexplained diarrhoea. This patient's heart murmur is suggestive of tricuspid stenosis, which may also occur and lead to signs of right heart failure. Diagnosis typically involves the 24-hour collection of\n\nurinary 5-HIAA\n\n, which is elevated. Treatment of the underlying malignancy will aid resolution of symptoms.\n\nSerum follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\n\nis incorrect. This may play a role in the diagnosis of menopause, which can also present with flushing. However, the other signs and symptoms here point toward carcinoid syndrome.\n\nSerum thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)\n\nis incorrect. Hyperthyroidism can present with diarrhoea and palpitations; however, the other features seen here point toward carcinoid syndrome as the underlying cause.\n\nUrinary catecholamines\n\nis incorrect. These are typically used in the diagnosis of phaeochromocytoma (although have now largely been superseded by urinary metanephrines). Whilst this may also present with palpitations, patients typically have hypertension and tachyarrhythmias rather than the signs and symptoms seen here.\n\nUrinary metanephrines\n\nis incorrect. These are used in the diagnosis of phaeochromocytoma."
