      "explanation": "Ursodeoxycholic acid is the first-line medication for primary biliary cholangitis\n\nUrsodeoxycholic acid\n\nis the correct answer. This patient has primary biliary cholangitis (PBC). Fatigue, jaundice, and right upper quadrant pain are all in keeping with this diagnosis. The raised IgM is highly suggestive of PBC. Additionally, PBC tends to affect middle-aged women such as this patient. Ursodeoxycholic acid is the initial treatment for PBC. In PBC, there is autoimmune-medicated progressive destruction of intrahepatic bile ducts, resulting in chronic cholestasis. Ursodeoxycholic acid increases the transportation of intracellular bile acids across the liver cell and into the canaliculus, therefore reducing liver damage in patients with PBC.\n\nAdvice on alcohol cessation\n\nis the incorrect answer. Alcohol consumption of two units per week is unlikely to cause liver damage and there was no evidence of liver cirrhosis on the abdominal ultrasound. Given this patient has PBC, alcohol cessation might nonetheless be considered given this patient is at high risk for liver damage. However, alcohol cessation will not treat PBC and therefore ursodeoxycholic acid is the more appropriate answer.\n\nColestyramine\n\nis the incorrect answer. Colestyramine is a bile-acid sequestrant that is useful to treat pruritus secondary to jaundice. This patient is not complaining of an itch and therefore colestyramine is not required at present - it would be reasonable to start should an itch develop. Colestyramine does not treat PBC.\n\nElective cholecystectomy\n\nis the incorrect answer. Gallstones could explain intermittent right upper quadrant pain and an obstructing gallstone could cause jaundice. However, there is no evidence of gallstones or biliary dilatation on the abdominal ultrasound, making this very unlikely. Gallstones would not explain the raised IgM.\n\nLiver transplant\n\nis the incorrect answer. A liver transplant would be considered if PBC progresses to cirrhosis, or if there is clinical deterioration with no response to ursodeoxycholic acid. The patient's abdominal ultrasound showed no evidence of cirrhosis and the blood tests show no evidence of decompensated liver disease (normal platelets and clotting) - therefore a liver transplant would not be considered at present."
