      "explanation": "Whipple's disease: jejunal biopsy shows deposition of macrophages containing Periodic acid-Schiff (PAS) granules\n\nMacrophages containing Periodic acid-Schiff (PAS) granules is correct. This patient presents with a multisystem disease with symptoms of abdominal pain, diarrhoea, steatorrhoea, migrating polyarthralgia, photosensitivity, skin hyperpigmentation, petechiae and peripheral oedema. This constellation of findings suggests Whipple's disease as the diagnosis. This is a multi-system disorder caused by infection with\n\nTropheryma whipplei\n\n, which is a gram-positive rod bacteria. It is diagnosed by demonstrating macrophages containing Periodic acid-Schiff (PAS) granules on jejunal biopsy. It is more common in males and those who are HLA-B27 positive. The bruising and oedema is due to malabsorption of protein, vitamin K and vitamin C. The hyperpigmentation is typically due to vitamin B3 malabsorption and subsequent deficiency.\n\nCaseating granuloma is incorrect. This would be a typical finding of tuberculosis. While abdominal TB can cause abdominal pain and fevers, it often causes ascites and intestinal obstruction in addition. It is not associated with skin hyperpigmentation or photosensitivity and is less likely to cause a migrating polyarthralgia.\n\nNon-caseating granuloma is incorrect. This could be reflective or sarcoidosis of crohn's disease. Neither condition is classically associated with hyperpigmentation or photosensitivity.\n\nVillous atrophy is incorrect. This a the characteristic finding of coeliac disease. This could cause abdominal pain and diarrhoea. It can also cause a rash (dermatitis herpetiformis), which may be aggravated by sunlight. However, it is not typically associated with fevers, skin hyperpigmentation or migratory polyarthralgia and therefore Whipple's disease is more likely.\n\nIncreased intraepithelial lymphocytes is incorrect. This a the characteristic finding of coeliac disease. This could cause abdominal pain and diarrhoea. It can also cause a rash (dermatitis herpetiformis), which may be aggravated by sunlight. However, it is not typically associated with fevers, skin hyperpigmentation or migratory polyarthralgia and therefore Whipple's disease is more likely."
