      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Peritoneal carcinomatosis",
      "explanation": "Ascites: a high SAAG gradient (> 11g/L) indicates portal hypertension\n\nPortal hypertension\n\nis the correct diagnosis. The patient presents with progressive abdominal distension, and clinical examination reveals ascites, as indicated by shifting dullness. To investigate this further, serum and ascitic fluid albumin levels are measured. The serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) is calculated by subtracting the ascitic albumin concentration from the serum albumin concentration; in this case, it is 31 - 11 = 20 g/L. A SAAG exceeding 11 g/L suggests portal hypertension as the underlying cause of the ascites. It is crucial to understand that portal hypertension may arise from a variety of pathologies, rendering it an unspecific indicator.\n\nHypoalbuminaemia\n\ndoes not explain this patient's presentation. Although slightly low serum albumin levels are noted, hypoalbuminaemia-induced ascites typically present with a SAAG below 11 g/L. Hypoalbuminaemia can occur due to multiple causes such as liver disease, renal disease, malnutrition, sepsis, and burns among others.\n\nPeritoneal TB\n\nis not a likely diagnosis in this case. Peritoneal tuberculosis generally results in a SAAG lower than 11 g/L and its diagnosis can be challenging, often necessitating peritoneal biopsy for confirmation rather than relying solely on fluid analysis. Additionally, symptoms such as fever, night sweats or weight loss would be expected in cases of peritoneal TB; these are not present in this patient.\n\nPeritoneal carcinomatosis\n\nis also inconsistent with the findings since malignant ascites typically yield a SAAG below 11 g/L. This indicates that such forms of ascites are not associated with increased hydrostatic pressure within the portal system.\n\nLastly,\n\nsevere malnutrition\n\ndoes not correspond with the clinical presentation as it also leads to a SAAG less than 11 g/L when associated with ascites."
