      "explanation": "CCK - I cells in upper small intestine\n\nI cells\n\nis the correct answer. The patient presents with colicky right upper quadrant pain after eating a fatty meal in the background of a raised body mass index. The likely diagnosis is biliary colic. CCK is released in response to a fatty meal and is the major hormonal stimulator of biliary contraction. It is the contraction of the gallbladder against a gallstone impacted in the bladder neck that causes the pain of biliary colic. I cells secrete CCK.\n\nBeta cells\n\nare incorrect. Beta cells in the pancreas secrete insulin. While insulin may be secreted in response to a fatty meal, it does not cause contraction of the gallbladder in contrast to CCK.\n\nD cells\n\nare incorrect. These cells secrete somatostatin. This decreases acid and pepsin secretion, decreases gastrin secretion, decreases pancreatic enzyme secretion, decreases insulin and glucagon secretion, inhibits trophic effects of gastrin and stimulates gastric mucous production.\n\nG cells\n\nare incorrect. The cells are located in the stomach and release gastrin. This is caused by distension of the stomach and exposure to luminal peptides and amino acids. While it can increase gastric motility it does not cause gallbladder contraction.\n\nS cells\n\nare incorrect. These cells secrete a bicarbonate-rich fluid from the pancreas and hepatic cells and are stimulated by fatty acids. However, it does not cause contraction of the gallbladder."
