      "explanation": "Diarrhoea, weight, arthralgia, lymphadenopathy, ophthalmoplegia ?Whipple's disease\n\nThis presentation, although rare, should raise suspicion of\n\nTropheryma whipplei\n\ninfection, also known as Whipple's disease. The infection is chronic and classically causes intestinal malabsorption. As the disease progresses, symptoms become more systemic with large joint arthralgia and lymphadenopathy often occurring. Less commonly, neurological manifestations such as ophthalmoplegia, confusion and facial muscle deficits can occur. Treatment is with a protracted course of antibiotics; penicillins and co-trimoxazole are often first-line.\n\nCampylobacter jejuni\n\nis a cause of bloody diarrhoea, however lymphadenopathy, arthralgia and ophthalmoplegia would be highly atypical. Although the infection is strongly associated with Guillain Barre syndrome, ophthalmoplegia would not be a typical manifestation of this.\n\nCryptosporidium\n\ncauses watery diarrhoea but is not associated with joint or neurological involvement. This parasitic infection typically is spread via contaminated water sources.\n\nDisseminated\n\nNeisseria gonorrhoea\n\nis not associated with malabsorption or diarrhoea. Arthralgia and arthritis can be expected, however, any neurological involvement is likely to manifest as a meningitis picture rather than ophthalmoplegia.\n\nTreponema pallidum\n\n, or syphilis, can cause weight loss and lymphadenopathy in the secondary stage. In its extreme form, neurosyphilis can cause cranial nerve palsy's, but this is often secondary to syphilis meningitis. Arthralgia is less associated with\n\nTreponema pallidum\n\n."
