      "correctText": "0.5 - 2%",
      "explanation": "Colorectal cancer screening - PPV of FOB = 5 - 15%\n\nThe correct answer is\n\n5 - 15%\n\n. According to the UK National Health Service, approximately 10% of individuals with a positive faecal occult blood test (FOBT) will be found to have colorectal cancer on subsequent colonoscopy. This percentage falls within the range of 5 - 15%, making it the most accurate answer.\n\nThe option of\n\n0.5 - 2%\n\nis incorrect as it significantly underestimates the prevalence of colorectal cancer in individuals with a positive FOBT. While not every individual with a positive test will have cancer, the likelihood is higher than this range suggests.\n\nSimilarly,\n\n20 - 30%\n\n,\n\n30 - 50%\n\n, and\n\n55 - 75%\n\nall overestimate the prevalence of colorectal cancer following a positive FOBT. While a positive test does indicate an increased risk for colorectal cancer, most individuals will not have cancer. These options may reflect the prevalence rates in high-risk populations or those with additional symptoms suggestive of malignancy, but they do not accurately represent the general population screened through the national programme.\n\nIt's important to note that while only around 10% of people with a positive FOBT will have colorectal cancer, many others may have other significant findings such as polyps or inflammatory bowel disease which also require further management and treatment. Therefore, even if the chance of having cancer is relatively low, it's still crucial for patients to undergo further investigation if they receive a positive result from their screening test."
