      "explanation": "Corticosteroids are used in the management of severe alcoholic hepatitis\n\nPrednisolone\n\nis correct. The most likely diagnosis is acute alcoholic hepatitis, given the history of chronic alcohol use with recent binge drinking, clinical features of jaundice and tender hepatomegaly, and laboratory findings including elevated bilirubin, prolonged prothrombin time (PT), raised gamma-GT, and raised ALT. The markedly prolonged PT and high bilirubin result in a Maddrey’s Discriminant Function score > 32 (around 61 in this case), indicating severe disease with poor prognosis. Prednisolone is indicated because corticosteroids reduce inflammation and improve short-term survival in patients with severe alcoholic hepatitis.\n\nLiver transplant\n\nis incorrect. Liver transplantation is contraindicated in active alcohol consumption without demonstrated abstinence. In exceptional cases, after a multidisciplinary meeting, early liver transplantation may be considered if there is a low risk of relapse post-transplant and if strict abstinence and psychosocial criteria are met. However, this would be considered if the patient had failed to respond to medical therapy (e.g. corticosteroids), which is not the case here.\n\nPentoxifylline\n\nis incorrect as the STOPAH trial demonstrated that it does not improve survival in acute alcoholic hepatitis when compared to prednisolone, which shows a short-term mortality benefit. It is no longer routinely recommended for the treatment of severe alcoholic hepatitis.\n\nRifaximin\n\nis incorrect, as this is used in the prevention of hepatic encephalopathy in patients with established cirrhosis by reducing gut ammonia-producing bacteria. In this scenario, this patient has no evidence of hepatic encephalopathy (e.g. asterixis, confusion). It plays no role in the acute inflammatory phase of alcoholic hepatitis.\n\nVitamin E\n\nis incorrect as this is not routinely used in alcoholic hepatitis. While it has demonstrated some anti-oxidant properties, it has mainly been studied in non-alcoholic fatty liver disease rather than alcoholic hepatitis."
