      "id": 73,
      "question": "The action of which one of the following brush border enzymes results in the formation of glucose and galactose?",
      "options": [
        "Dipeptidase",
        "A-dextrinase",
        "Maltase",
        "Lactase",
        "Sucrase"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "A-dextrinase",
      "explanation": "Brush border enzymes:\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nLactase\n\n. Lactase is a brush border enzyme located in the small intestines. It hydrolyses lactose, a disaccharide found in milk and dairy products, into glucose and galactose. This allows for the absorption of these simple sugars into the bloodstream. A deficiency in lactase can lead to lactose intolerance, where undigested lactose passes into the colon causing symptoms such as bloating, diarrhoea and abdominal cramps.\n\nDipeptidase\n\nis incorrect as this enzyme functions to break down dipeptides into individual amino acids. Dipeptides are molecules that contain two amino acids linked by a single peptide bond. Therefore, dipeptidases do not produce glucose or galactose.\n\nA-dextrinase\n\n(also known as isomaltase) is also an incorrect answer. This brush border enzyme acts on α-dextrins, which are intermediate products of starch digestion. It breaks α-1,6-glycosidic linkages producing glucose molecules but does not produce galactose.\n\nMaltase\n\nis another incorrect option. Maltase catalyses the hydrolysis of maltose (a disaccharide produced from starch) into two glucose units. While it produces glucose, it does not yield any galactose.\n\nFinally,\n\nSucrase\n\nis also not the correct answer. Sucrase breaks down sucrose (common table sugar) into glucose and fructose - not galactose. Like lactase, sucrase resides on the brush border membrane of the small intestine epithelial cells facilitating carbohydrate digestion."
