      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Delay gastric emptying",
      "explanation": "Gastrin increases H+ secretion by gastric parietal cells\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nincrease H+ secretion by gastric parietal cells\n\n. This patient has a gastrinoma, causing the clinical presentation of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome. Here, excess gastrin production causes an overly acidic environment in the duodenum, causing dyspepsia and malabsorptive symptoms (in this case, diarrhoea and weight loss) as the intestinal pH is no longer optimised for its digestive enzymes to function. A further clue to the diagnosis is the family history of multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1, with which around one-quarter of gastrinomas are associated. The normal role of gastrin is to increase H+ secretion by gastric parietal cells.\n\nDelay gastric emptying\n\nis incorrect. Gastrin secretion is stimulated by stomach distension and the presence of undigested peptides in the stomach. As such, it promotes digestion and increases gastric emptying.\n\nIncrease H+ secretion by gastric chief cells\n\nis incorrect, as the chief (or peptic) cells of the stomach secrete pepsinogen and gastric lipase to aid with the digestion of proteins and fats, rather than hydrochloric acid.\n\nStimulate pancreatic bicarbonate secretion\n\nis incorrect. This function is carried out by secretin, produced by duodenal S-cells, rather than gastrin.\n\nSuppress the release of pancreatic enzymes\n\nis incorrect. This is performed by somatostatin, which is produced in the delta cells of the stomach, duodenum and pancreas."
