      "correctText": "Dexamethasone",
      "explanation": "Surgery is the mainstay of treatment for hydatid cysts\n\nThis patient's presentation and imaging findings are suggestive of a hydatid cyst. These occur due to infection due to the tapeworm parasite\n\nEchinococcus granulosus\n\n, most commonly in the Mediterranean and Middle East. An outer fibrous capsule forms, containing 'daughter' cysts as seen here. Most commonly, hydatid cysts form in the liver. Definitive treatment for the condition involves\n\nsurgery\n\nto excise the cyst, ensuring not to rupture the cyst walls during the process.\n\nConcurrent treatment with antiparasitic medication, such as\n\nalbendazole\n\n, takes place preoperatively and postoperatively. However, this is not the definitive treatment option for the condition - surgery is necessary.\n\nDexamethasone\n\nmay be used in the management of allergic reactions in echinococcosis, especially following cyst rupture. It may also be administered as an adjunct to decrease perilesional oedema resulting from an intracranial hydatid cyst. The evidence supporting its use for oedema in hepatic hydatid cysts, however, is lacking. The definitive option here remains surgery.\n\nPraziquantel\n\nmay be used in conjunction with albendazole, as some studies show the combination is more effective than albendazole monotherapy. These drugs, however, are used alongside surgery, not instead of it.\n\nPAIR\n\nis incorrect. This procedure may be suitable for patients with a simpler hydatid cyst (a unilocular cyst without 'daughter' cysts). However, as this patient's cyst is multiseptated, surgery is more appropriate."
