      "explanation": "If\n\nThis patient has developed complications secondary to a\n\nClostridium difficile\n\ninfection most like a result of extensive broad-spectrum antibiotic use. The patient has evidence of severe infection with a raised white cell count and evidence of colitis on imaging. First-line therapy for\n\nC. difficile\n\nis oral vancomycin for 10 days however if no improvement is seen then second-line therapy, in the form of\n\noral fidaxomicin\n\nshould be trialled.\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\nis a monoclonal antibody shown to target\n\nC. difficile\n\ntoxin B, one of the main exotoxins produced by the bacteria causing intestinal damage and pseudomembranous colitis. Currently NICE do not support its use for the management of\n\nC. difficile\n\nas it is not cost-effective.\n\nIV vancomycin\n\ndoes not have a clear role in the management of\n\nC. difficile\n\ninfections with the oral form of the antibiotic used as first-line before escalating to other therapeutic agents.\n\nOral gentamicin\n\nhas very limited use for infection management in general with the IV formula used mainly for urinary infections and topical formula in eye and ear infections. Oral gentamicin has no role in the management of\n\nC. difficile\n\n.\n\nOral vancomycin and IV metronidazole\n\nis reserved for third-line or life-threatening cases of\n\nC. difficile\n\n. This treatment regime should therefore only be used if oral fidaxomicin fails or the patient has features such as hypotension, ileus or toxic megacolon."
