      "correctText": "Candidiasis",
      "explanation": "Whilst dysphagia of solids and liquids is a classic history for achalasia, certain features such as significant weight loss are not consistent and suggest cancer - 'pseudoachalasia'\n\nDespite dysphagia of solids and liquids starting simultaneously, this patient has lost a significant amount of weight in the past 6 months, and has several risk factors for oesophageal cancer including GORD, excessive smoking and alcohol use. This makes oesophageal cancer the more likely diagnosis.\n\nDilated tapering oesophagus is the finding on barium swallow for achalasia. Achalasia presents with dysphagia to both liquids and solids, regurgitation, cramps and weight loss. However, it is less likely in this situation due to the risk factors for oesophageal cancer coupled with excessive weight loss.\n\nCorkscrew oesophagus is a finding on barium swallow for oesophagitis. Oesophagitis more often presents with intermittent dysphagia and chest pains. It is less likely in this situation due to the risk factors for oesophageal cancer coupled with excessive weight loss.\n\nOesophageal stricture with apple core effect is the correct answer, this is a barium swallow result for malignant oesophageal stricture.\n\nWhile you can get candidiasis in malignancies, this is not likely to be the only finding. Oesophageal candidiasis more often occurs in immunocompromised patients and presents with dysphagia, dry mouth, nausea and chest pain.\n\nHypertensive lower oesophageal sphincter is a finding for achalasia. Achalasia presents with dysphagia to both liquids and solids, regurgitation, cramps and weight loss. However, it is less likely in this situation due to the risk factors for oesophageal cancer coupled with excessive weight loss."
