      "question": "A 73-year-old man has recently been admitted to hospital following a fall at home.\n\nHe has a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes and chronic kidney disease, for which he takes ramipril, metformin and atorvastatin.\n\nHis admission blood tests showed the following:\n\n\nHb\t102 g/L\t(135-180)\nPlatelets\t328 * 109/L\t(150 - 400)\nWBC\t10.5 * 109/L\t(4.0 - 11.0)\nMCV\t70 fl\t(80-100)\nFerritin\t12 ng/mL\t(20 - 230)\n\nHe denies any change in bowel habit or weight loss and has not noticed any obvious bleeding elsewhere. On examination, he is noted to have an ejection systolic murmur that is loudest in the aortic area. Abdominal examination is normal and digital rectal examination does not reveal any abnormalities.\n\nWhat is the most likely cause of his anaemia from the list below?",
