      "explanation": "Angiodysplasia is associated with aortic stenosis\n\nThis man has unexplained microcytic anaemia. There are a number of causes, of which angiodysplasia is the most likely option from the listed options. This is a common cause of iron deficiency anaemia and may present either with painless rectal bleeding or may be completely asymptomatic, as seen in this patient. It is thought to be associated with aortic stenosis, which is suggested by his ejection systolic murmur. Of course, it is essential to exclude more sinister pathologies such as colorectal cancer, hence further investigations would be required to confirm the cause of his anaemia.\n\nAnaemia of chronic disease is a possibility given his chronic kidney disease, but this would typically cause normocytic anaemia with a ferritin level that is either normal or raised, hence this is an incorrect option.\n\nCoeliac disease is incorrect. Although it is a common cause of iron deficiency anaemia in patients who may not necessarily display any gastrointestinal symptoms, it is less commonly diagnosed for the first time in this age group and angiodysplasia would be the more likely cause.\n\nHaemolytic anaemia would usually cause normocytic or macrocytic anaemia. Patients who have prosthetic heart valves can undergo haemolysis due to mechanical trauma to red blood cells, but there is no mention of this man having previous valve surgery, hence this answer is incorrect.\n\nLong-term metformin use is associated with B12 deficiency which can cause anaemia, but this usually results in macrocytosis, so would not be applicable to this patient."
