      "correctText": "Heartburn or indigestion",
      "explanation": "Clindamycin treatment is associated with a high risk of\n\nThe correct answer in this case is\n\ndiarrhoea\n\n. Clindamycin is an antibiotic belonging to the lincosamide class, and it is commonly used as a second-line treatment for cellulitis when patients are allergic to penicillins or when first-line treatments have failed. One of the most important side-effects to warn patients about when prescribing clindamycin is diarrhoea, which can range from mild to severe and may be associated with\n\nClostridium difficile\n\ninfection. This infection can lead to pseudomembranous colitis, a potentially life-threatening condition that requires prompt identification and management.\n\nRegarding the other options:\n\nHeartburn or indigestion\n\ncan occur with many medications, including antibiotics; however, it is not considered a major concern with clindamycin therapy. While it's important for patients to be aware of potential gastrointestinal side effects, diarrhoea remains the primary concern with this medication.\n\nJaundice\n\nis not a common side effect of clindamycin. Jaundice may indicate liver dysfunction or damage, but clindamycin-associated hepatotoxicity is rare. It would not be necessary to specifically warn the patient about jaundice as a side effect of clindamycin.\n\nSore throat, bruising or lethargy\n\nare not typical side effects associated with clindamycin use. These symptoms might suggest an underlying infection or another medical problem unrelated to the antibiotic treatment.\n\nAvoid any food or drink containing alcohol\n\nis not specific advice related to clindamycin use. Some antibiotics, such as metronidazole and tinidazole, have known interactions with alcohol that can cause unpleasant reactions like nausea, vomiting, flushing and headache (disulfiram-like reaction). However, there's no evidence suggesting that this reaction occurs with clindamycin."
