      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "I cells in the upper small intestine",
      "explanation": "CCK - I cells in upper small intestine\n\nCCK is a peptide hormone that increases the digestion of fats and proteins. It is synthesized and released from\n\nI cells in the upper small intestine\n\nin response to increased detection of partially digested proteins and fats. CCK results in several processes including the secretion of digestion enzymes from the pancreas, contraction of the gallbladder, relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi, decreased gastric emptying, and a trophic effect on pancreatic acinar cells all of which result in fat and protein breakdown as well as hunger suppression.\n\nB cells\n\nare part of the body's immune system, producing antibodies that serve as part of the B cell receptors. B cells are produced and released from the bone marrow before migrating to the spleen and lymphoid system. They can organise in the gastrointestinal lymphoid tissues and regional lymph nodes but play no role in satiety.\n\nSomatostatin is a peptide regulator hormone released from\n\nD cells in the pancreas and stomach\n\n. It is stimulated by the presence of fat, bile salt, and glucose within the intestines and results in a decrease in the rate of gastric emptying and gastrointestinal hormone release.\n\nGastrin is another hormone that increases acid release from parietal cells in the stomach and aids with gastric motility. It is released from\n\nG cells in the antrum of the stomach\n\non distension of the stomach, stimulus of the vagus nerves, and the presence of peptides/amino acids in the lumen.\n\nSecretin regulates enzyme secretion of the stomach, pancreas, and liver. It is released from\n\nS cells in the upper small intestine\n\non detection of acidic chyme and fatty acids and increases the pH of the duodenum."
