      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "D-xylose test",
      "explanation": "SeHCAT is the investigation of choice for bile acid malabsorption\n\nThe correct answer in this scenario is the\n\nSeHCAT test\n\n. Bile acid malabsorption (BAM) is a common cause of chronic diarrhoea, particularly in patients with Crohn's disease. The SeHCAT (Selenium-75-labelled homocholic acid taurine) test is considered the gold standard for diagnosing BAM. In this test, the patient ingests a capsule containing the radiolabelled bile acid analogue SeHCAT. After 7 days, whole-body gamma camera imaging is performed to assess the retention of SeHCAT. A low retention rate indicates bile acid malabsorption.\n\nThe\n\ncapsule endoscopy\n\nallows for direct visualisation of the entire small bowel and can be useful in identifying mucosal abnormalities such as ulcers or strictures in patients with Crohn's disease. However, it does not provide any specific information regarding bile acid malabsorption and would not be appropriate in this case.\n\nThe\n\n14C-glycocholic breath test\n\nis another method that has been used to investigate bile acid malabsorption, but it is less accurate and less commonly used compared to the SeHCAT test. The 14C-glycocholic breath test involves oral administration of radiolabelled glycocholic acid followed by measurement of expired 14CO2. An increase in 14CO2 levels suggests bacterial overgrowth and deconjugation of bile acids, which can lead to malabsorption.\n\nAn\n\nileal biopsy\n\ncould potentially show histological changes associated with Crohn's disease but would not specifically diagnose bile acid malabsorption. Moreover, obtaining an ileal biopsy may require invasive procedures like enteroscopy or surgery, which are not necessary for investigating BAM.\n\nFinally, the\n\nD-xylose test\n\nis used to evaluate the absorptive capacity of the small intestine for carbohydrates. It can help diagnose conditions like celiac disease or bacterial overgrowth, but it does not provide any information on bile acid malabsorption. Therefore, it would not be appropriate in this case."
