      "explanation": "In a mild-moderate flare of distal ulcerative colitis, the first-line treatment is topical (rectal) aminosalicylates\n\nRectal aminosalicylate\n\nis correct. This patient is suffering from a flare of her distal ulcerative colitis. Using the Truelove and Witts severity index for ulcerative colitis, this flare can be shown to be moderate. A rectal aminosalicylate is the first line in this instance.\n\nOral aminosalicylate\n\nis incorrect. This patient's disease affects only the distal colon. For a mild to moderate flare of distal colitis, rectal aminosalicylates are the first line. If there is no improvement in 4 weeks, an oral aminosalicylate may be added.\n\nIV corticosteroids\n\nis incorrect. IV corticosteroids are indicated in the management of a severe ulcerative colitis flare. Using the Truelove and Witts severity index for ulcerative colitis, this flare can be shown to be moderate. Rectal aminosalicylates are therefore first-line.\n\nOral corticosteroids\n\nis incorrect. Oral steroids are indicated in a mild to moderate flare of distal ulcerative colitis\n\nif\n\ntopical treatments (aminosalicylates and steroids) have failed.\n\nRectal corticosteroids\n\nis incorrect. Rectal steroids are indicated in the management of a mild to moderate flare of distal colitis\n\nif\n\nrectal aminosalicylates have been unsuccessful after 4 weeks."
