      "explanation": "The majority of gastrinomas are found in the first part of the duodenum\n\nThis patient has presented with features of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, where increased levels of gastrin are secreted by gastrinomas. These neuroendocrine tumours primarily form in the\n\nfirst part of the duodenum\n\ndue to it containing G cells, which naturally produce gastrin. Other areas where gastrinomas may develop include the pancreas and occasionally the gastrin-producing antrum section of the stomach.\n\nGastrinomas rarely develop in the\n\nileum\n\nas this region lacks the specialised G cells responsible for natural gastrin production. Gastrinomas typically arise where G cells are present, such as the duodenum or pancreas. The absence of these cells in the ileum makes it an uncommon site for gastrinoma development.\n\nGastrinomas rarely develop in the\n\njejunum\n\ndue to the unique distribution of gastrin-producing cells. G cells, responsible for producing gastrin, are primarily located in the antrum of the stomach and the duodenum. Consequently, gastrinomas tend to form in these regions or in the pancreas where G cells are also present. The absence of G cells in the jejunum makes it an uncommon site for gastrinoma development.\n\nGastrinomas rarely develop in the\n\npylorus section of the stomach\n\nbecause gastrin-producing G cells are predominantly found in the antrum and duodenum. The pylorus, responsible for regulating the passage of food from the stomach to the small intestine, lacks these specialised cells.\n\nGastrinomas rarely develop in the\n\nthird part of the duodenum\n\nas, being more distal in location, this area lacks the presence of gastrin-producing G cells where these neuroendocrine tumours primarily develop."
