      "explanation": "If\n\nIn a patient with\n\nC. difficile\n\nwho has not responded to first-line oral vancomycin, the next step is to give a course of\n\noral fidaxomicin\n\n, according to current guidelines. If this were to fail, or if the infection were more severe, oral vancomycin alongside intravenous metronidazole should be given.\n\nIntravenous fidaxomicin\n\nis incorrect. Whilst the choice of antimicrobial is correct, it should be given orally, not intravenously.\n\nIntravenous metronidazole\n\nis incorrect. As described above, this should be given alongside oral vancomycin in severe infection. It should not be given on its own.\n\nIntravenous vancomycin\n\nis incorrect. Whilst this is used for many other infections, oral vancomycin, rather than intravenous, is used for the management of\n\nC. difficile\n\ninfection.\n\nOral metronidazole\n\nis not routinely used. Intravenous, rather than oral, metronidazole should be given alongside oral vancomycin in severe infection."
