      "id": 113,
      "question": "There has been an outbreak of diarrhoea in an ortho-geriatric ward. 4 patients have been affected over a 3-week period.\n\nStool samples are sent. 3 patients are treated with oral vancomycin and 1 with oral fidaxomicin.\n\nWhat is the method of transmission for the causative organism?",
      "options": [
        "Aerosol transmission",
        "Direct inoculation into surgical wound",
        "Droplet transmission",
        "Ingestion of spores",
        "Ingestion of vegetative bacteria"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Ingestion of spores",
      "explanation": "Clostridioides difficile\n\nThe correct answer is\n\ningestion of spores\n\n. The cause of diarrhoea in this scenario is\n\nClostridiodes difficile\n\nas evidenced by the treatment given. Oral vancomycin is now the first-line treatment for non-life threatening\n\nClostridiodes difficile\n\ninfection. Oral fidaxomicin may be given for a first episode of infection in an immunosuppressed patient and may also be considered for recurrent infection in any patient.\n\nClostridiodes difficile\n\nis transmitted through ingestion of spores, which are bacteria which have entered a non-replicating stage and are resistant to temperature, drought, lack of nutrients and some chemicals. Spores are passed in the faeces of the first person, are then ingested from the hands of a second person, and transform back into replicating bacteria in the more favourable conditions in the gastrointestinal tract.\n\nAerosol transmission\n\nis incorrect. This is a type of airborne transmission involving small infectious particles of <5µm which can travel more than 1m from an infected person after being exhaled. Examples of diseases spread in this route are chickenpox and measles.\n\nDirect inoculation into surgical wound\n\nis incorrect. This route may cause infection of the surgical wound with other bacteria, but not diarrhoeal illness.\n\nDroplet transmission\n\nis incorrect. This is another type of airborne transmission but involves larger infectious particles of > 5µm which do not remain airborne for long after exhalation. Examples include whooping cough and influenza.\n\nIngestion of vegetative bacteria\n\nis incorrect. Vegetative bacteria are actively replicating bacteria.\n\nClostridiodes difficile\n\ncannot survive outside the host in its replicating state because it is an obligate anaerobe and is killed in the presence of oxygen. Only the spore form can survive outside the host."
