      "id": 122,
      "question": "A 39-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with a one-day history of rapid-onset abdominal pain and swelling.\n\nOn examination, she appears jaundiced with a distended abdomen. Shifting dullness is present, and a point-of-care ultrasound confirms significant ascites. An ascitic fluid sample is obtained for analysis.\n\nShe has a background of chronic kidney disease stage 3 and two previous deep vein thromboses.\n\nThe results of her investigations are as follows:\n\n\nBilirubin\t88 µmol/L\t(3 - 17)\nALP\t51 u/L\t(30 - 100)\nALT\t122 u/L\t(3 - 40)\nGT\t56 u/L\t(8 - 60)\nAlbumin\t32 g/L\t(35 - 50)\nAscites albumin\t12 g/L\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
      "options": [
        "Acute pancreatitis",
        "Hepatic portal vein thrombosis",
        "Hepatic vein thrombosis",
        "Hepatocellular carcinoma",
        "Nephrotic syndrome"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Hepatocellular carcinoma",
      "explanation": "Ascites: a high SAAG gradient (> 11g/L) indicates portal hypertension\n\nThe serum ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) is calculated by subtracting the albumin concentration in the ascitic fluid from that of the serum, which in this instance is 20 g/L. A SAAG exceeding 11 g/L typically signifies transudative ascites, commonly associated with portal hypertension. In contrast, a lower SAAG indicates exudative causes such as inflammatory conditions (examples include infection or pancreatitis) which lead to the leakage of albumin from the bloodstream into the peritoneal space, thereby reducing the gradient. Notably, nephrotic syndrome represents a significant exception; it results in a decreased SAAG due to loss of protein via urine, which diminishes serum albumin levels.\n\nHepatic vein thrombosis\n\n, also known as Budd-Chiari syndrome, presents with symptoms such as acute abdominal pain, ascites and jaundice. The obstruction of venous outflow from the liver leads to portal hypertension and consequently a high SAAG. Risk factors include pregnancy, hepatocellular carcinoma and inherited blood clotting disorders.\n\nAcute pancreatitis\n\nis an inflammatory condition that causes proteins to leak from the circulatory system into the peritoneal cavity, resulting in a low SAAG.\n\nHepatic portal vein thrombosis\n\ncan present similarly to hepatic vein thrombosis, however, the rate of accumulation of ascites tends to be slower. Furthermore, hepatic portal vein thrombosis tends to more commonly occur in patients with underlying liver disease, whereas hepatic vein thrombosis is associated with hypercoagulable states (e.g. previous history of DVTs).\n\nHepatocellular carcinoma\n\ncan induce ascites as malignancies increase vascular permeability resulting in protein leakage into the peritoneal space. This causes a low SAAG.\n\nNephrotic syndrome\n\nis characterised by significant urinary protein loss leading to reduced serum albumin levels and subsequently lowered plasma oncotic pressure. This impairs fluid reabsorption back into blood vessels and may cause ascites. Though it is not an inflammatory condition, the low serum albumin concentration in nephrotic syndrome gives rise to a low SAAG."
