      "question": "What is the most common cause of hepatocellular carcinoma in the United Kingdom?",
      "options": [
        "Haemochromatosis",
        "Hepatitis B",
        "Alcohol excess",
        "Aflatoxin",
        "Hepatitis C"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Hepatitis B",
      "explanation": "Hepatocellular carcinoma\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nHepatitis C\n\n. Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is the most common type of primary liver cancer, and in the United Kingdom, its most common cause is chronic infection with Hepatitis C virus. The virus causes chronic inflammation leading to cirrhosis, which predisposes to the development of HCC.\n\nThe first incorrect option is\n\nHaemochromatosis\n\n. Haemochromatosis is a condition characterised by excessive iron accumulation in the body. It can indeed lead to cirrhosis and subsequently HCC due to iron-induced oxidative damage; however, it's not the most common cause of HCC in the UK.\n\nNext, we have\n\nHepatitis B\n\n. While globally Hepatitis B is a major cause of HCC, in the UK Hepatitis C predominates due to higher prevalence. Nevertheless, both viruses can lead to chronic liver disease and eventually HCC.\n\nAlcohol excess\n\nis another significant cause of HCC. Chronic alcohol consumption leads to alcoholic liver disease (ALD), including fatty liver, hepatitis, fibrosis and cirrhosis. Cirrhosis secondary to ALD increases the risk for HCC; however, it still falls behind Hepatitis C as a causative factor in the UK.\n\nFinally,\n\nAflatoxin\n\n, a potent carcinogen produced by certain moulds often found on improperly stored grains and nuts, has been linked with an increased risk of HCC. However, this risk factor is more relevant in developing countries with poor food storage conditions and isn't a significant contributor to cases in the UK."
