      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Surgical excision",
      "explanation": "Surgery is the mainstay of treatment for hydatid cysts\n\nSurgical excision\n\nis the correct answer. The clinical presentation of this patient suggests a probable diagnosis of hydatid disease, which is caused by infection with the tapeworm parasite\n\nEchinococcus granulosus\n\n. This condition is prevalent in Mediterranean and Middle Eastern areas, aligning with the patient's travel history and occupation as a rancher, which involves regular contact with livestock. Hydatid cysts are formed following ingestion of\n\nEchinococcus granulosus\n\neggs (oncospheres). These cysts can grow slowly and may become symptomatic over time, manifesting as abdominal pain, jaundice or other complications due to their enlargement or rupture. Surgical excision is recognised as the definitive treatment for large (> 5 cm) or symptomatic hydatid cysts to prevent further complications. The surgical approach involves meticulous removal of the cyst without causing rupture and sterilisation of its contents. Pre-operative anti-helminthic therapy is commonly employed to reduce intracystic pressure and minimise spillage risk during surgery.\n\nTreatment with\n\nbroad-spectrum antibiotics\n\ndoes not effectively address parasitic infections like hydatid disease because they do not act on either the causative organism or its resulting cystic structures. While antibiotics may be useful in treating secondary bacterial infections that can occur, they are not considered primary therapy for this condition.\n\nLong-term administration of anti-helminthic medications like mebendazole\n\nalone falls short as a treatment strategy for large or complex cysts presenting with severe symptoms or complications. Considering this patient’s progressive symptoms including jaundice and a pruritic rash indicative of obstructive jaundice likely due to biliary compression, surgical intervention becomes essential for effective management and prevention of complications. Nevertheless, long-term antiparasitic therapy may be contemplated as adjunctive treatment alongside surgery in comprehensive management strategies for hydatid disease.\n\nPercutaneous drainage followed by mebendazole\n\nis not recommended for managing large hydatid cysts (> 5 cm) according to NICE guidelines due to potential risks associated with cyst rupture during percutaneous drainage procedures. Such rupture could precipitate anaphylaxis or disseminate larvae throughout the body (leading to secondary hydatidosis). Therefore, surgical management is favoured for larger cysts to ensure complete removal and reduce recurrence risk.\n\nRegular monitoring with ultrasound every 3 months\n\nis not an appropriate management strategy for this patient because it does not address the underlying cause of the cystic lesion. While monitoring might be suitable for asymptomatic cysts or stable conditions, in the case of suspected\n\nEchinococcal disease\n\n, the presence of a large cyst and associated symptoms (like abdominal pain and jaundice) indicates a need for intervention.\n\nEchinococcal\n\ncysts can cause complications such as infection, rupture, or biliary obstruction. Thus, simply monitoring without treatment can lead to worsening of the condition."
