      "question": "Which one of the following investigations is considered the gold standard for the diagnosis of gastro-oesophageal reflux disease?",
      "options": [
        "Endoscopy",
        "24hr oesophageal pH monitoring",
        "Oesophageal manometry",
        "Barium swallow",
        "CT thorax"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Oesophageal manometry",
      "explanation": "24hr oesophageal pH monitoring is gold standard investigation in GORD\n\nThe correct answer is\n\n24hr oesophageal pH monitoring\n\n. This investigation is considered the gold standard for diagnosing gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD) because it allows for the direct measurement of acid exposure in the oesophagus over a 24-hour period. It provides quantitative data on the frequency and duration of acid reflux episodes, including those that do not cause symptoms, thus offering a highly sensitive and specific test for GORD.\n\nEndoscopy\n\n, while commonly used in the evaluation of GORD symptoms, is not as definitive as 24hr pH monitoring. Although it can identify complications such as oesophagitis or Barrett's oesophagus, it has limited sensitivity and specificity for GORD itself since many patients with typical reflux symptoms may have normal endoscopic findings.\n\nOesophageal manometry\n\nmeasures pressure changes within the oesophagus to assess its motor function. While this can provide useful information about peristalsis and lower oesophageal sphincter pressure, it does not directly measure acid exposure and hence is less accurate than 24hr pH monitoring for diagnosing GORD.\n\nA\n\nBarium swallow\n\nis an imaging study that can demonstrate anatomical abnormalities such as a hiatal hernia or severe oesophagitis but lacks sensitivity in detecting mild or moderate reflux disease. Furthermore, it cannot quantify acid exposure or correlate symptoms with reflux episodes.\n\nLastly, a\n\nCT thorax\n\nis not typically used in the diagnosis of GORD. It may incidentally show signs suggestive of GORD such as a hiatal hernia but lacks specificity and sensitivity compared to other investigations. Importantly, its use involves significant radiation exposure which makes it unsuitable for routine diagnostic purposes in this context."
