      "explanation": "Dietary modification and topical steroids are used in the management of eosinophilic oesophagitis\n\nEosinophilic oesophagitis is becoming a more commonly diagnosed condition in young children and adults with a prevalence of approximately 0.4%. It is a condition that displays a male predominance and has a close relationship to other atopic pathologies like eczema and asthma. Its clinical presentation in adults typically consists of dysphagia and food impaction but children can have nausea, vomiting, dyspepsia and failure to thrive. Eosinophilic infiltration of the oesophageal epithelium is characteristic but is not pathognomonic.\n\nTreatment of this condition consists of 3 approaches:\n\n1) Dietary changes (either by an elemental diet or elimination diet by excluding allergenic food groups).\n\n2) Drugs - Proton pump inhibitors can be used 1st line. If this fails to resolve symptoms, corticosteroids like fluticasone or budesonide can be used.\n\n3) Dilation - This is used to manage significant dysphagia that is not responsive to medical therapy.\n\nGiven his trial of treatment has not resulted in symptom improvement, it would be prudent to consider a trial of dietary change + steroids.\n\nAs we have not optimized his medical treatment, endoscopic dilation does not need to be performed just now.\n\nTrial of treatment of fluconazole can be used if there is a suspicion of oesophageal candidiasis as the cause of his symptoms. The lack of odynophagia and white plaques in the oesophagus makes this diagnosis less likely.\n\nHeller myotomy is the treatment of choice for achalasia. While this will cause dysphagia, it does not adequately explain the histological findings, making the diagnosis less probable."
