      "explanation": "Ursodeoxycholic acid is the first-line medication for primary biliary cholangitis\n\nThe underlying diagnosis is primary biliary cholangitis (PBC). This is a chronic liver disease, which causes inflammation of the interlobular bile ducts. It is strongly associated with other autoimmune conditions and classically presents in middle-aged women with fatigue and pruritis. AMA M2 subtype antibodies are present in the majority of patients with the disease.\n\nUrsodeoxycholic acid\n\nis the correct answer. It is used first-line in the treatment of PBC to improve liver function, slow disease progression and delay the need for liver transplant.\n\nCholestyramine\n\nis incorrect. Cholestyramine may be used for symptomatic relief of pruritis in PBC but is not a first-line treatment option.\n\nFat soluble vitamin supplementation\n\nis incorrect. According to the Royal Society of Gastroenterology, there is a role for supplementation of specific vitamins including A, D, E and K, but only in patients with advanced icteric disease, and not in place of ursodeoxycholic acid.\n\nLiver transplant\n\nis incorrect. This question refers to a patient who is in the early stages of PBC. Whilst liver transplant is indicated in decompensated disease (e.g. bilirubin > 100), it would not be an appropriate first-line treatment option in this context.\n\nPrednisolone\n\nis incorrect. It may be considered in patients who do not adequately respond to ursodeoxycholic acid, or in those with advanced disease, but is not used as a first-line treatment option."
