      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Reduced 24hr urinary copper excretion",
      "explanation": "Wilson's disease - serum caeruloplasmin is\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nreduced serum caeruloplasmin\n\n. Wilson's disease is an autosomal recessive disorder characterised by defective hepatic copper transport, leading to copper accumulation in various tissues. The most consistent laboratory finding in Wilson's disease is reduced serum caeruloplasmin concentration. Caeruloplasmin is a copper-carrying protein, and its synthesis is impaired in Wilson's disease due to intracellular copper overload.\n\nReduced hepatic copper concentration\n\nis not suggestive of Wilson's disease. On the contrary, this condition is characterised by increased hepatic copper concentration as a result of impaired biliary excretion of copper.\n\nSimilarly,\n\nreduced 24hr urinary copper excretion\n\nwould not be expected in Wilson's disease. In fact, 24-hour urinary copper excretion is often increased in untreated Wilson's disease because the kidneys attempt to compensate for the defective biliary excretion by increasing renal clearance of copper.\n\nIncreased skin pigmentation\n\n, while it can occur in several systemic diseases, it isn't typically associated with Wilson's disease. It might suggest conditions such as Addison's disease or haemochromatosis where there is an increase in melanin production.\n\nLastly,\n\nincreased serum copper\n\nlevels are not characteristic of Wilson's disease either. Although there may be excess free (non-caeruloplasmin-bound) copper circulating in the late stages of the condition due to massive release from damaged hepatocytes, total serum copper levels are usually normal or even low because ceruloplasmin (which carries most of the body's copper) level is low."
