      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Coeliac disease",
      "explanation": "Whipple's disease: jejunal biopsy shows deposition of macrophages containing Periodic acid-Schiff (PAS) granules\n\nThe correct diagnosis, in this case, is\n\nWhipple's disease\n\n. The presence of macrophages containing Periodic Acid-Schiff (PAS) positive granules in the jejunal biopsy is a classic histological finding in Whipple's disease. This rare systemic infectious disorder is caused by the bacterium\n\nTropheryma whipplei\n\n. It primarily affects middle-aged men and can cause a variety of symptoms, including weight loss, diarrhoea, joint pain and abdominal discomfort.\n\nBacterial overgrowth\n\nis an incorrect answer. While bacterial overgrowth syndrome does involve an abnormal increase in the number of bacteria in the small intestine, it doesn't lead to the deposition of PAS-positive granules within macrophages. Its symptoms may include bloating, gas, abdominal pain and diarrhoea but these are due to malabsorption caused by competition for nutrients between the patient and the excess bacteria.\n\nSimilarly,\n\nCoeliac disease\n\n, another condition associated with malabsorption, would not present with PAS-positive macrophages. Coeliac disease is an autoimmune condition where ingestion of gluten leads to damage in the small intestine. Histologically, it presents with villous atrophy and crypt hyperplasia on jejunal biopsy rather than PAS-positive granules.\n\nTropical sprue\n\nis also incorrect as this condition refers to a syndrome characterised by acute or chronic diarrhoea and malabsorption that occurs in residents or visitors to tropical regions. Its exact cause remains unknown but it's thought to be related to bacterial or viral infection causing damage to the intestinal lining. Again, it does not feature PAS-positive granules within macrophages.\n\nFinally,\n\nSmall bowel lymphoma\n\nwould typically present with different histological findings such as infiltration of lymphoid cells into intestinal tissue rather than PAS-positive granules within macrophages. Lymphomas are cancers that start in lymphocytes - part of our immune system - which do not contain these types of granules. Symptoms can be nonspecific and may include abdominal pain or discomfort due to tumour growth."
