      "id": 162,
      "question": "A 67-year-old man with a history of oesophageal cancer is reviewed on the ward. He has been treated previously with an oesophagectomy and has recently had a course of chemotherapy.Unfortunately he has been feeling increasingly unwell recently and has not eaten anything for the 7 days due to dysphagia. An nasogastric tube is passed and it is decided to start enteral feeding. What is the most appropriate course of action to avoid refeeding syndrome?",
      "options": [
        "Provide 50% of normal energy and protein requirements for the first 2 days",
        "Start a saline infusion with 20 mmol of potassium for the first 8 hours",
        "Provide 75% of normal energy and protein requirements for the first 2 days",
        "Give furosemide 40mg IV at the mid-point of the first feed",
        "Give hydrocortisone 200mg IV at the mid-point of the first feed"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Give hydrocortisone 200mg IV at the mid-point of the first feed",
      "explanation": "Give 50% of normal energy intake in starved patients (> 5 days) to avoid refeeding syndrome\n\nThe correct answer is to\n\nprovide 50% of normal energy and protein requirements for the first 2 days\n\n. This approach helps to prevent refeeding syndrome, a potentially fatal complication that can occur when reintroducing nutrition to a severely malnourished patient. Refeeding syndrome is characterized by rapid shifts in electrolytes, particularly phosphate, potassium, and magnesium, which can lead to cardiac and neurological complications. By providing only 50% of the patient's normal energy and protein requirements initially, it allows their body to gradually adapt to the increased nutritional intake while minimizing the risk of refeeding syndrome.\n\nThe option\n\nstart a saline infusion with 20 mmol of potassium for the first 8 hours\n\nis not the most appropriate course of action as it does not address the need for a gradual increase in caloric intake. While supplementing potassium may be necessary in some cases of refeeding syndrome, this alone will not prevent its occurrence.\n\nProviding\n\n75% of normal energy and protein requirements for the first 2 days\n\nmay still place the patient at risk of developing refeeding syndrome. A more conservative approach with 50% caloric intake is recommended per UK guidelines to minimize this risk.\n\nAdministering\n\nfurosemide 40mg IV at the mid-point of the first feed\n\nis not appropriate in this situation. Furosemide is a diuretic that promotes fluid loss via increased urine output; however, it does not address refeeding syndrome's primary concern related to electrolyte imbalances caused by rapid reintroduction of nutrition.\n\nFinally, giving\n\nhydrocortisone 200mg IV at the mid-point of the first feed\n\nwould also be an inappropriate intervention. Hydrocortisone is a corticosteroid typically used to treat inflammatory conditions or adrenal insufficiency but has no direct role in preventing or managing refeeding syndrome."
