      "correctText": "Doppler ultrasound liver",
      "explanation": "Budd-Chiari syndrome - ultrasound with Doppler flow studies is very sensitive and should be the initial radiological investigation\n\nThis woman has a diagnosis of Budd-Chiari syndrome. Budd-Chiari syndrome or hepatic vein thrombosis is more commonly associated with a hypercoagulable state (e.g. pregnancy, combined oral contraceptive pill use, malignancy and polycythaemia). Liver function tests tend to show a markedly raised ALT. Jaundice may be present but not always in the acute presentation. The investigation of choice is a\n\ndoppler ultrasound of the liver\n\nto show the hepatic vein thrombus.\n\nAlpha-fetoprotein\n\nis a tumour marker used to aid the detection of malignancies such as hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC). HCC presents late with features of liver cirrhosis including jaundice, ascites and right upper quadrant pain. Typically in patients with cirrhosis, liver enzymes remain relatively normal with the most prominent derangements being a raised INR, reduced blood sugar and reduced albumin levels.\n\nAn\n\nascitic tap\n\ncan be a useful investigation to determine the aetiology of the underlying condition and to rule out infectious causes such as spontaneous bacterial peritonitis. However, the lack of a significant inflammatory response makes infection less likely. Furthermore, an ascitic tap is not the investigation of choice in Budd-Chiari syndrome.\n\nA\n\nCT abdomen\n\ncan show signs of Budd-Chiari syndrome such as enhancement of the caudate lobe and central liver around the inferior vena cava but is not used in the first instance.\n\nA\n\nliver biopsy\n\nis associated with multiple complications including infection and bleeding. It is less frequently used in the diagnosis of hepatic disorders and plays no role in the investigation of Budd-Chiari syndrome."
