      "correctText": "Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography",
      "explanation": "Imaging is required before making a diagnosis of PBC to exclude extrahepatic biliary obstruction\n\nThis is a patient with dry eyes and mouth, fatigue, pruritis, and obstructive hyperbilirubinaemia. With positive anti-mitochondrial antibodies, the likely diagnosis is primary biliary cholangitis (PBC). The dry eyes and mouth are common in Sjogren's syndrome, which is commonly associated with PBC.\n\nIt is vital to ensure that there is no extrahepatic cause of biliary obstruction prior to making this diagnosis, especially as the age commonly affected by PBC overlaps with those affected by cholangiocarcinoma and pancreatic malignancies. As such, an ultrasound scan would be a useful investigation.\n\nAnti-Ro/SS-A and anti-La/SS-B antibodies\n\n- are positive in Sjogren's syndrome. While these would be useful to confirm the diagnosis, it is more important to exclude extrahepatic biliary obstruction.\n\nEndoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography\n\n- can be used if there remains uncertainty as to the diagnosis, but is invasive and not useful at this stage.\n\nLiver biopsy\n\n- there is no need to conduct a liver biopsy in cases of PBC.\n\nCT thorax, abdomen, and pelvis\n\n- this would be a useful investigation if there was evidence of malignancy on other investigations, to stage the tumour or seek a primary in cases of metastatic infiltration. It would not be the next step in the management of this patient, however."
