      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "IV esomeprazole",
      "explanation": "Surgery is indicated in patients with ongoing acute bleeding despite repeated endoscopic therapy\n\nThe patient presents with an upper gastrointestinal (GI) bleed secondary to a gastric ulcer that has shown resistance to multiple endoscopic interventions. Endoscopic techniques include the injection of adrenaline directly into the ulcer to promote vasoconstriction and achieve haemostasis, as well as the mechanical application of clips to arrest the bleeding. When such endoscopic measures fail despite repeated attempts,\n\nreferral to general surgery\n\nis the next appropriate step in management.\n\nThis individual has been subjected to two separate endoscopies, including adrenaline being administered to the ulcer. Considering this context, additional\n\nendoscopic adrenaline injections\n\nare unlikely to provide further benefit. Instead, the patient should be referred to general surgery.\n\nIV terlipressin\n\nis given in cases of upper GI bleed caused by oesophageal varices rather than for peptic ulcers. Terlipressin acts by causing constriction of the splanchnic blood vessels, thereby reducing blood flow to the portal vein and decreasing pressure in the portal vein and the varices.\n\nFollowing the identification of a gastric ulcer as the source of bleeding, the patient should have received treatment with\n\nIV esomeprazole\n\n. Nonetheless, in cases where an upper GI bleed proves refractory to standard treatments, IV esomeprazole does not constitute definitive management; surgical intervention is required.\n\nA\n\ntransjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt\n\n(TIPS) procedure is typically reserved for managing intractable bleeding from oesophageal varices associated with alcoholic liver disease by diverting blood flow from the portal vein into the systemic circulation. This reduces portal hypertension and subsequently decreases pressure on the varices prone to bleeding. However, there is no indication that this patient suffers from portal hypertension; therefore, TIPS would not be a suitable therapeutic option in this scenario."
