      "id": 173,
      "question": "Which one of the following enzymes is mainly responsible for breaking starch down into sugars?",
      "options": [
        "Amylase",
        "Sucrase",
        "Alpha-glucosidase",
        "Maltase",
        "Lactase"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Amylase",
      "explanation": "Amylase: breaks starch down to sugars\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nAmylase\n\n. Amylase is an enzyme that is mainly responsible for breaking down starch, a complex carbohydrate, into simpler sugars. It is produced in the salivary glands and the pancreas, and it starts the digestion of starch in the mouth and continues it in the small intestine.\n\nSucrase\n\nis incorrect because this enzyme breaks down sucrose into glucose and fructose. Sucrose is a disaccharide sugar that we commonly know as table sugar or saccharose. While sucrase does break down a type of sugar, it does not act on starch.\n\nAlpha-glucosidase\n\nis also incorrect. This enzyme breaks down complex carbohydrates into glucose, but it acts primarily on disaccharides and oligosaccharides rather than on polysaccharides like starch.\n\nThe option\n\nMaltase\n\nis another incorrect choice. Maltase breaks maltose (a disaccharide) into two units of glucose. Although maltose is one of the products of starch digestion by amylase, maltase itself doesn't act directly on starch.\n\nFinally,\n\nLactase\n\nis also not correct. Lactase's function in our body is to break lactose (the sugar found in milk) into glucose and galactose for absorption. It has no action on starch breakdown."
