      "correctText": "Aspirin",
      "explanation": "Cessation of smoking may trigger an ulcerative colitis flare\n\nAbdominal pain, diarrhoea, and rectal bleeding in a patient with ulcerative colitis who recently stopped smoking suggest an exacerbation of ulcerative colitis. Smoking cessation is the likely cause of this. The mural thickening on a plain abdominal film is non-specific but, given the history of ulcerative colitis, a flare is a likely cause.\n\nDespite aspirin increasing the likelihood of rectal bleeding, it does not cause abdominal pain and diarrhoea.\n\nA high fibre diet could cause loose stools, and one could argue that patients with ulcerative colitis generally have rectal bleeding, but it does not explain the abdominal pain. This patient's presentation is suggestive of an exacerbation of ulcerative colitis.\n\nIschaemic bowel causes generalised abdominal pain, rectal bleeding, and diarrhoea in a patient with atrial fibrillation or peripheral vascular disease. Given that the ECG was sinus rhythm and VBG did not show elevated lactate, it is lower down on the differential.\n\nDespite bowel cancer being on the differential, it is less likely to explain this patient's presentation."
