      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Laparoscopy",
      "explanation": "Oesophageal/Gastric Cancer - Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) is better than CT or MRI in assessing mural invasion\n\nThe correct answer is endoscopic ultrasound (EUS). With regards to oesophageal adenocarcinoma, EUS is better than computed tomography (CT) and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scans at assessing mural invasion, and so is the investigation of choice at this stage. Several studies have demonstrated the accuracy of staging with EUS as being significantly higher than staging with CT or MRI.\n\nCT scanning is incorrect. Whilst also useful, and indeed performed for all patients with oesophageal or gastric cancer, it is not as good at assessing mural invasion as EUS. It is performed to assess the extent of metastases.\n\nLaparoscopy is incorrect. This may be considered in those with negative imaging before definitive therapy; to assess mural invasion, however, it is not useful.\n\nMRI scanning, similarly to CT, is useful to assess spread and metastases, but again, EUS is better at assessing mural invasion.\n\nPositron emission tomography scanning plays a role in pre-operative staging and is more sensitive than CT for detecting distant metastases. However, EUS is better for assessing mural invasion."
