      "correctText": "Glucose tolerance test",
      "explanation": "Faecal elastase is a useful test of exocrine function in chronic pancreatits\n\nGiven the symptoms seen here, the likely diagnosis is chronic pancreatitis. Faecal elastase can be measured both in the serum and stool and assesses for exocrine insufficiency seen in chronic pancreatitis. Faecal elastase is an exocrine product of the pancreas and is not broken down by gastrointestinal enzymes; therefore, its level can be detected and measured. These levels are decreased when the exocrine-producing tissue of the pancreas is damaged and therefore correlates with reduced function.\n\nFaecal\n\n13\n\nC analysis is not an established test for the measurement of endocrine function. Indirect\n\n13\n\nC breath tests have been developed, involving the ingestion of\n\n13\n\nC labelled lipids and measuring the exhaled\n\n13\n\nC to calculate lipid breakdown.\n\nGamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT) is predominantly used as a diagnostic marker for liver disease and has been shown to be increased in both acute and chronic alcohol use. A raised GGT may add weight to the possibility of alcohol abuse in this patient but does not assess exocrine function.\n\nGlucose tolerance test essentially measures the pancreas' ability to produce insulin so can be used to measure function; however, this is a measure of the pancreas' endocrine function, not exocrine.\n\nSerum amylase is useful in the diagnosis of acute pancreatitis and is normally raised in chronic pancreatitis; its level does not correlate with the severity of exocrine tissue damage or exocrine function."
