      "explanation": "Whilst dysphagia of solids and liquids is a classic history for achalasia, certain features such as significant weight loss are not consistent and suggest cancer - 'pseudoachalasia'\n\nThe correct answer is weight loss.\n\nAchalasia is a condition in which the lower oesophageal sphincter fails to relax, resulting in symptoms of dysphagia to both solids and liquids and heartburn.\n\nWeight loss is not usually associated with achalasia, and especially unintentional weight loss despite adequate dietary intake would suggest a more sinister diagnosis such as malignancy.\n\nNew heartburn is a concerning feature in a 55-year-old and paired with weight loss would warrant a two week wait referral. However, it is consistent with a diagnosis of achalasia.\n\nRegurgitation and dysphagia to both solids and liquids are also both symptoms of achalasia."
