      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Lactulose",
      "explanation": "NICE recommend avoiding lactulose in the management of IBS\n\nThe correct answer in this case is\n\nLactulose\n\n. According to the National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) guidelines, lactulose should be avoided in patients with irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) as it can exacerbate symptoms. Lactulose is a synthetic disaccharide that works by drawing water into the bowel, softening stools and increasing bowel movements. However, it can also cause bloating, flatulence, and abdominal discomfort, which are common symptoms of IBS.\n\nMebeverine\n\nis an antispasmodic medication that helps relieve muscle spasms in the gastrointestinal tract. It is commonly prescribed for IBS patients to alleviate abdominal pain and cramping associated with the condition. Therefore, mebeverine would not be contraindicated for use in IBS patients.\n\nIspaghula\n\n, also known as psyllium husk, is a bulk-forming laxative derived from the seeds of the Plantago ovata plant. It works by absorbing water in the intestines, forming a gel-like substance that promotes softer stool consistency and easier passage through the colon. Ispaghula may help improve both constipation and diarrhea symptoms in IBS patients.\n\nMethylcellulose\n\nis another bulk-forming laxative similar to ispaghula. It absorbs water in the intestines and increases stool bulk, making it easier for stools to pass through the colon. Methylcellulose may help manage constipation-related symptoms of IBS without causing significant side effects or worsening other IBS symptoms.\n\nFinally,\n\nSterculia\n\nis a natural gum extracted from the Sterculia species of trees. It acts as a bulk-forming laxative by absorbing water and increasing fecal mass while softening stools. Sterculia can be used to manage constipation in IBS patients and is generally well-tolerated.\n\nIn summary, the NICE guidelines recommend avoiding lactulose in IBS patients due to its potential to exacerbate symptoms. The other options listed, such as mebeverine, ispaghula, methylcellulose, and sterculia, may provide symptomatic relief for IBS patients without causing significant adverse effects."
