      "id": 213,
      "question": "A 23-year-old man is investigated for chronic diarrhoea associated with raised inflammatory markers. A bowel biopsy is taken. Which one of the following findings is most suggestive of ulcerative colitis?",
      "options": [
        "Multiple granulomas",
        "Goblet cell depletion",
        "Inflammation affecting the serosa",
        "Cobblestone appearance",
        "Skip lesions"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Cobblestone appearance",
      "explanation": "Ulcerative colitis - depletion of goblet cells\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nGoblet cell depletion\n\n. Ulcerative colitis (UC) is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease characterised by continuous mucosal inflammation starting from the rectum and extending proximally. One of the histological hallmarks of UC is goblet cell depletion, which refers to a decrease in the number of mucus-producing cells in the lining of the colon. This depletion contributes to a compromised intestinal barrier function, promoting ongoing inflammation.\n\nMultiple granulomas\n\nare more suggestive of Crohn's disease rather than ulcerative colitis. Granulomas, which are clusters of immune cells, are not typically seen in UC. Their presence helps distinguish Crohn's disease from other forms of inflammatory bowel disease.\n\nInflammation affecting the serosa\n\nalso points towards Crohn's disease as opposed to ulcerative colitis. In UC, inflammation is usually limited to the mucosal layer while in Crohn's disease, it can affect all layers of the bowel wall including the serosa.\n\nA\n\nCobblestone appearance\n\nagain suggests Crohn's disease rather than ulcerative colitis. This appearance results from deep ulcers that develop between areas of swollen intestinal lining, giving it a cobblestone-like look on imaging or endoscopy. This feature is not characteristic for UC where ulcers tend to be shallow and continuous.\n\nSkip lesions\n\n, which refer to areas of diseased intestine separated by healthy sections, are another characteristic finding in Crohn's disease but not in ulcerative colitis. In UC, inflammation typically starts at the rectum and extends continuously up the colon without skipping any parts."
