      "explanation": "PPIs are a cause of microscopic colitis, which can present with chronic diarrhoea, colonoscopy and biopsy should be considered when patients present in this way and are taking a PPI\n\nColonoscopy and biopsy\n\nis the correct answer. Microscopic colitis should be considered in patients with chronic diarrhoea, particularly those receiving PPI therapy. Suspected microscopic colitis should be evaluated with endoscopy and biopsies, as the macroscopic appearance of the colon is usually normal.\n\nFaecal elastase\n\nis incorrect. This is a test used in the diagnosis or exclusion of pancreatic exocrine insufficiency. Chronic pancreatitis is an unlikely diagnosis due to the lack of abdominal pain, nausea/vomiting, or weight loss.\n\nFaecal occult blood test\n\nis incorrect. Aside from diarrhoea, there are no other features to suggest gastrointestinal malignancy. There is no weight loss, and her diarrhoea is consistently non-bloody. A faecal occult blood test would add little to the diagnostic work-up of this patient.\n\nReduce dose of levothyroxine\n\nis incorrect. Whilst it would be reasonable to check her thyroid function, it would not be appropriate to reduce her medication dose without doing so. Furthermore, the use of omeprazole alongside chronic diarrhoea in a middle-aged woman should raise the suspicion of microscopic colitis.\n\nTrial of mebeverine\n\nis incorrect. Certainly, patients with diarrhoea-predominant irritable bowel syndrome may present similarly to this woman, but you would also expect abdominal pain. Abdominal pain in IBS is classically related to defecation and is associated with a change in the frequency or consistency of stool."
