      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Vasoactive intestinal peptide",
      "explanation": "Secretin increases secretion of bicarbonate-rich fluid from pancreas and hepatic duct cells\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nSecretin\n\n. Secretin is a hormone that plays a crucial role in the regulation of gastrointestinal functions. It is released by the S cells of the duodenum in response to low pH (acidic chyme entering from the stomach). One of its main effects is stimulating the pancreas to secrete bicarbonate-rich pancreatic juice into the duodenum. This bicarbonate helps to neutralise the acidic chyme, thereby protecting the intestinal mucosa and providing an optimal environment for pancreatic enzymes.\n\nSomatostatin\n\n, on the other hand, inhibits various endocrine and exocrine processes. It suppresses bicarbonate secretion by inhibiting secretin release, acting as a counter-regulatory hormone. Therefore, it does not promote bicarbonate secretion in the upper gastrointestinal tract.\n\nGastrin\n\nis primarily responsible for stimulating acid secretion by parietal cells in the stomach, not bicarbonate secretion. Gastrin also stimulates the growth of gastric mucosa and increases gastric motility but it has no significant effect on pancreatic bicarbonate production.\n\nCCK (Cholecystokinin)\n\nmainly stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzyme secretion into the small intestine following a meal. Its primary function is facilitating digestion within the small intestine by slowing gastric emptying and stimulating bile production. CCK does stimulate some bicarbonate secretion from the pancreas but this effect is less prominent than that of secretin.\n\nFinally,\n\nVasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP)\n\nacts as a neurotransmitter as well as a local hormone in many parts of the human body including the gastrointestinal tract where it inhibits gastric acid secretion and absorption from the intestine while promoting smooth muscle relaxation and blood flow. However, VIP's role in regulating bicarbonate secretion is limited compared to secretin."
