      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Stop venesection and monitor in 6 months",
      "explanation": "Ferritin and transferrin saturation are used to monitor treatment in haemochromatosis\n\nReduce to monthly venesection\n\nis the correct management approach for this patient. The goal of therapy in hereditary haemochromatosis is to reduce iron stores to normal levels, typically indicated by a ferritin level below 50 µg/L and transferrin saturation below 50%. In this case, the patient's ferritin has successfully decreased from 850 µg/L to 45 µg/L, which is within the target range. However, his transferrin saturation remains slightly elevated at 48%, though it has improved significantly from the initial 68%. This indicates that while substantial progress has been made, some ongoing iron removal is still needed. Transitioning from weekly to monthly venesection is appropriate at this stage to maintain iron levels within the target range without causing iron deficiency. This maintenance phase typically continues lifelong to prevent reaccumulation of iron.\n\nContinue weekly venesection\n\nwould be too aggressive at this point. The patient's ferritin is already at the lower end of the target range (45 µg/L), and continuing weekly venesection could lead to iron deficiency anaemia. Weekly venesection is typically used in the initial depletion phase of treatment, which this patient has already completed successfully as evidenced by his current ferritin level.\n\nCommence desferrioxamine\n\nis not appropriate. Desferrioxamine is an iron chelator used as second-line therapy in patients who cannot tolerate venesection or in cases of severe iron overload with cardiac involvement. This patient has responded well to venesection with no indication of intolerance or complications that would necessitate switching to chelation therapy.\n\nStop venesection and monitor in 6 months\n\nwould be premature. Although the patient's ferritin is now within target range, his transferrin saturation remains slightly elevated. Completely stopping therapy would likely result in reaccumulation of iron and progression of the disease. Maintenance therapy is essential in haemochromatosis management.\n\nPerform liver MRI\n\nis not indicated at this stage. Liver MRI is used to quantify hepatic iron stores and is typically performed during initial assessment or if there is concern about hepatic damage. This patient has responded well to treatment with normalising parameters and has normal liver function tests. There is no clinical indication for liver MRI at this point in his management."
