      "explanation": "Corticosteroids are used in the management of severe alcoholic hepatitis\n\nPrescribe prednisolone\n\nis correct. This patient’s acute jaundice, right upper quadrant pain, ascites, confusion (GCS 14), hepatomegaly, and AST > ALT pattern strongly suggest severe alcoholic hepatitis, further supported by his heavy alcohol use and prolonged PT (27 s), indicating coagulopathy. Prednisolone can reduce hepatic inflammation in severe alcoholic hepatitis. The decision to prescribe it can be guided by Maddrey’s discriminant function, with values > 32 or signs of liver failure indicating severe hepatitis (this patient's value is around 64.7). Given his altered mental status and coagulopathy, he is at risk of further deterioration. Early corticosteroid therapy improves short-term survival in these cases by modulating the inflammatory response.\n\nPrescribe pentoxifylline\n\nis incorrect. Pentoxifylline is not first-line for severe alcoholic hepatitis, as steroids offer a superior survival benefit. It was historically considered in patients who cannot receive corticosteroids (e.g., those with active infection or gastrointestinal bleeding); however, the STOPAH trial found that pentoxifylline does not improve survival. Hence, it is no longer recommended.\n\nPrescribe terlipressin and IV antibiotics, and refer for endoscopy\n\nis incorrect. Terlipressin is used for variceal bleeding, which this patient does not have, as there is no evidence suggesting haematemesis or melaena. In this context, IV antibiotics are indicated for preventing spontaneous bacterial peritonitis. Endoscopy is used for variceal bleeding or gastric lesions, which are not suspected in this case.\n\nPrescribe terlipressin and refer for endoscopy\n\nis incorrect. Terlipressin and endoscopy are used in the management of acute variceal haemorrhage. In this scenario, this patient has no haematemesis or melaena. Even if this patient had variceal haemorrhage, treatment would also involve the use of antibiotics to reduce the risk of infection, such as spontaneous bacterial peritonitis.\n\nRefer for liver transplantation\n\nis incorrect. Liver transplantation is contraindicated in active alcohol consumption without sustained abstinence. In very exceptional cases, after a multidisciplinary meeting, early liver transplantation may be considered if there is a low risk of relapse post-transplant and if strict abstinence and psychosocial criteria are met. However, this would be considered if the patient had failed to respond to medical therapy (e.g. corticosteroids), which is not the case here."
