      "explanation": "Diarrhoea + hypokalaemia → villous adenoma\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nvillous adenoma\n\n. A villous adenoma is a type of colonic polyp that characteristically presents with secretory diarrhoea containing mucus, leading to electrolyte disturbances - particularly hypokalaemia as seen in this case (K+ 3.1 mmol/L). The secretory nature of these tumors is due to their production of prostaglandin E2 which stimulates fluid and electrolyte secretion into the bowel lumen.\n\nDiverticular disease\n\ntypically presents with left-sided abdominal pain and altered bowel habit. While it can cause diarrhoea, it would not typically cause the mucoid secretory diarrhoea with electrolyte disturbances seen here. Bleeding, when it occurs, is usually acute rather than chronic.\n\nAngiodysplasia\n\nis characterized by vascular malformations in the gastrointestinal tract. It typically presents with intermittent gastrointestinal bleeding rather than mucoid diarrhoea. It is more common in elderly patients with aortic stenosis or chronic kidney disease.\n\nTubular adenoma\n\nis the most common type of colonic polyp but does not typically cause secretory diarrhoea or electrolyte disturbances. These polyps are usually asymptomatic and found incidentally during screening colonoscopy.\n\nColon cancer\n\ntypically presents with a change in bowel habit, weight loss, and often iron deficiency anaemia. While it can cause diarrhoea, it would not typically cause the secretory diarrhoea with electrolyte disturbances characteristic of villous adenomas. The stable weight in this case also makes cancer less likely."
