      "id": 243,
      "question": "A 79-year-old woman has been admitted to the gastroenterology ward with abdominal pain.\n\nShe has a past medical history of dyslipidaemia, hypertension, bronchiectasis and glaucoma.\n\nHer pain started two days ago but has been gradually becoming more severe. She has had a loss in appetite and diarrhoea. She is opening her bowels up to 5 times daily with some blood in the stool.\n\nOn examination, she has a mild tachycardia of 108 beats per minute, a blood pressure of 107/62 mmHg and pyrexia of 37.9 ºC.\n\nAbdominal x-ray showed evidence of mucosal thickening of the large bowel with 'thumbprinting'.\n\nDue to concerns for ischaemic colitis a CT abdomen with contrast is arranged which confirms the diagnosis.\n\nWhich part of the colon is most likely to have been affected?",
