      "correctText": "Rectum",
      "explanation": "The splenic flexure is the most commonly affected site in ischaemic colitis\n\nGiven this woman's signs and symptoms along with 'thumbprinting' on her abdominal x-ray it is likely that she has ischaemic colitis.\n\nIschaemic colitis most commonly affects 'watershed' areas. These are areas at the very edges of the territory supplied by particular vessels where there are fewer collateral vessels. At times of hypoperfusion, these sections of bowel are at an increased risk of ischaemia.\n\nThe two most common 'watershed' areas in the large bowel are at the splenic flexure and the rectosigmoid junction.\n\nThe splenic flexure is at the 'watershed' area of the superior mesenteric artery and inferior mesenteric artery, whilst the rectosigmoid junction is at the boundary between the inferior mesenteric artery and hypogastric artery.\n\nGiven the above options, splenic flexure is the correct answer."
