      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Abdominal ultrasound",
      "explanation": "Imaging is required before making a diagnosis of PBC to exclude extrahepatic biliary obstruction\n\nAbdominal ultrasound\n\nis correct. This 52-year-old woman presents with fatigue, pruritus, scleral icterus, hepatomegaly, and a cholestatic liver profile (raised ALP and mildly raised ALT), all of which point toward primary biliary cholangitis (PBC). Her positive anti-mitochondrial antibodies, raised IgM, and history of Sjögren’s syndrome further support this diagnosis. However, before confirming PBC, it is essential to exclude extrahepatic causes of cholestasis such as gallstones, strictures, or malignancy. Abdominal ultrasound is the first-line investigation to evaluate the biliary tree and liver parenchyma because it is non-invasive, accessible, and effective in identifying biliary dilatation or obstructive pathology. If no obstruction is seen and suspicion for PBC remains, more specific imaging such as MRCP may follow.\n\nCT abdomen with contrast\n\nis incorrect because it is not the first-line test for suspected PBC. Although it can detect large lesions or masses causing biliary obstruction, it involves unnecessary radiation and is less sensitive than ultrasound or MRCP for detecting small duct abnormalities. In this patient with no signs of malignancy (e.g. weight loss or palpable mass), and a likely autoimmune cause given the AMA positivity and Sjögren’s history, an ultrasound is more appropriate. CT may be considered if imaging later suggests malignancy or if symptoms suggest an alternative cause, such as pancreatic or biliary cancer.\n\nGallbladder biopsy\n\nis incorrect as it is not relevant to the diagnosis of PBC, which affects the intrahepatic bile ducts rather than the gallbladder. This invasive procedure is not part of routine workup and would not provide useful diagnostic information for PBC. It may be considered if imaging suggests gallbladder cancer or mass lesions causing biliary obstruction, which is not indicated in this case. Her symptoms and antibody profile instead point to an autoimmune cholestatic disease rather than gallbladder pathology.\n\nLiver elastography (FibroScan)\n\nis incorrect as it assesses the degree of liver fibrosis, not the cause of cholestasis. While useful in monitoring known liver diseases, it is not helpful in excluding extrahepatic obstruction, which must be ruled out before confirming PBC. Additionally, cholestasis can falsely elevate stiffness readings, making it unreliable during active disease. In this patient, who has not yet had biliary imaging, liver elastography is premature and not part of the initial diagnostic pathway.\n\nPercutaneous transhepatic cholangiography\n\nis incorrect as it is an invasive imaging test used for therapeutic or highly detailed diagnostic purposes when ultrasound and MRCP are inconclusive. It carries procedural risks such as bleeding or infection and is not indicated without prior imaging. In this patient with suspected PBC, non-invasive imaging such as ultrasound is preferred first. PTC may only be appropriate if there is confirmed or suspected obstruction not well visualised by other methods, or in cases requiring intervention, such as biliary drainage."
