      "explanation": "Urea breath test is the only test recommended for\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nUrea breath test\n\n. This is the recommended test to confirm eradication of\n\nH. pylori\n\nfollowing treatment according to NICE guidelines. The test should be performed at least 4 weeks after completion of eradication therapy to ensure accurate results. The urea breath test works by detecting the presence of\n\nH. pylori\n\nurease activity. The patient ingests 13C-labelled urea, which is broken down by\n\nH. pylori\n\nurease if present, producing labelled CO2 that can be detected in exhaled breath. It is non-invasive, highly sensitive and specific, and provides real-time results.\n\nCulture of gastric biopsy\n\nis incorrect because it is an invasive test requiring endoscopy. While it is highly specific and can provide antibiotic sensitivity information, it is not recommended for routine post-treatment confirmation due to its invasive nature and the availability of equally effective non-invasive alternatives.\n\nH. pylori serology\n\nis incorrect because antibodies can remain positive for months or even years after successful eradication. Therefore, serology cannot reliably distinguish between current and past infection, making it unsuitable for confirming eradication.\n\nHydrogen breath test\n\nis incorrect because this test is primarily used to diagnose small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO) or lactose intolerance. It measures hydrogen produced by bacterial fermentation of carbohydrates and is not specific for\n\nH. pylori\n\ninfection.\n\nStool culture\n\nis incorrect because while stool antigen testing can be used to diagnose\n\nH. pylori\n\n, routine stool culture is not appropriate for\n\nH. pylori\n\ndetection. The organism is difficult to culture from stool samples, and this method is not recommended for confirming eradication in clinical practice."
