      "explanation": "HBsAg negative, anti-HBs positive, IgG anti-HBc negative - previous immunisation\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nPrevious immunisation to hepatitis B\n\n. The presence of positive Anti-HBs (Hepatitis B Surface Antibody) and negative Anti-HBc (Hepatitis B Core Antibody) and HBs antigen (Hepatitis B Surface Antigen) indicates that the patient has been previously immunised against hepatitis B. This is because the presence of Anti-HBs shows that the immune system has mounted a response against the virus, but the absence of Anti-HBc and HBs antigen suggests that there is no active infection or exposure to the virus.\n\nChronic hepatitis B - highly infectious\n\nis incorrect because this would be indicated by a positive HBs antigen, which signifies an ongoing infection with hepatitis B. Additionally, in chronic infections, both Anti-HBs and Anti-HBc are usually positive.\n\nProbable hepatitis D infection\n\nis incorrect because hepatitis D infection occurs as a co-infection or superinfection with hepatitis B. Therefore, if there was a probable hepatitis D infection, we would expect to see evidence of active hepatitis B infection such as a positive HBs antigen.\n\nAcute hepatitis B infection\n\nis incorrect because during an acute infection, both HBs antigen and Anti-HBc would typically be positive. In this case, both are negative, indicating no current or past exposure to the virus.\n\nChronic hepatitis B - not infectious\n\nis incorrect because this status would be characterized by a positive Anti-HBs along with positive Anti-HBc and negative HBs antigen. This indicates that although there was prior exposure to the virus leading to immunity development (positive Anti-HBs), there is no ongoing active infection (negative HBs antigen). However, in this patient's case, both HBs antigen and Anti-HBc are negative, pointing towards previous immunisation rather than past infection."
