      "explanation": "Oesophageal/Gastric Cancer - Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) is better than CT or MRI in assessing mural invasion\n\nEndoscopic ultrasound is the correct option. This patient most likely has adenocarcinoma of the lower third of the oesophagus. The first-line investigation is upper gastrointestinal endoscopy; subsequently, an endoscopic ultrasound is the best imaging for mural invasion or tumour depth. This is because the local staging of cancer can be most accurately depicted by a local ultrasound rather than other imaging modalities.\n\nAn upper gastrointestinal endoscopy is an incorrect option as it does not measure tumour depth as accurately as the ultrasound wave modality.\n\nCT scan is an incorrect option as this is less accurate for mural invasion. Instead, it the best initial investigation for staging oesophageal cancer in the chest, abdomen and pelvis.\n\nPET CT scan is also incorrect as this option is reserved for those with oesophageal cancer in whom a peritoneal laparoscopy to search for peritoneal metastasis is negative. A PET CT scan is superior to a CT scan for this purpose due to the increase in uptake of the injected dye in areas of rapidly dividing metastatic cells.\n\nMRI scan is incorrect because it has been found to be inferior at accurately measuring the mural invasion/tumour depth of oesophageal cancers compared to endoscopic ultrasound."
