      "correctText": "Bay nursing, hand washing with soap, with single use mask/gloves/apron",
      "explanation": "Preventing\n\nThis 72-year-old woman has been treated with oral clarithromycin for four days and has subsequently developed profuse diarrhoea. This clinical presentation should raise immediate concern for a\n\nClostridioides difficile\n\ninfection, particularly given the severity indicated by the elevated white cell count and the increased frequency of bowel movements. Patients with suspected\n\nC. difficile\n\ninfection should be isolated in a\n\nside room and adhere to strict hand hygiene using soap and water while utilising single-use gloves and aprons\n\n.\n\nWhen considering\n\nbay nursing, hand washing with soap, with single-use mask/gloves/apron\n\n, it is important to note that patients with\n\nC. difficile\n\nshould not ideally be nursed in an open bay due to the high risk of transmitting the infection to others. Although hand hygiene with soap and water along with the use of gloves and an apron is recommended, masks are not generally necessary because of transmission of\n\nC. difficile\n\ntypically occurs via the fecal-oral route rather than through airborne spread.\n\nA negative pressure room is commonly reserved for airborne communicable diseases such as tuberculosis but is not warranted for a patient with\n\nC. difficile\n\ninfection; a standard side room will suffice. Therefore,\n\nnegative pressure side room, hand washing with soap, with single-use gloves/apron\n\nis considered less appropriate.\n\nThe recommendation of\n\nside room, hand washing with alcohol gel, with single-use gloves/apron\n\nincludes several correct measures; however, alcohol gel does not effectively eliminate\n\nC. difficile\n\nspores and should be substituted with soap and water for hand hygiene.\n\nLastly,\n\nside room, hand washing with alcohol gel, with single-use gloves/apron/mask\n\nalso incorporates the ineffective alcohol gel for this context. Additionally, as previously mentioned, face masks are unnecessary in managing patients infected by\n\nC. difficile\n\ndue to its mode of transmission."
