      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Ileocecal junction",
      "explanation": "The splenic flexure is the most likely area to be affected by ischaemic colitis\n\nIschemic colitis occurs when blood flow to part of the large intestine is temporarily reduced, either due to a blockage in the blood vessel or hypo-perfusion. Any part of the colon can be affected but it most commonly affects the left side.\n\nThe hepatic flexure is on the right side of the colon and has a good blood supply from a superior mesenteric artery (SMA) so this is less likely to be involved.\n\nThe ileocecal junction has a good blood supply from the ileocolic artery, a branch of the superior mesenteric artery.\n\nThe inferior mesenteric artery supplies the left colon from the splenic flexure to the rectum. The recto-sigmoid junction is a watershed area and vulnerable to ischaemic but it is less common than ischaemia at the splenic flexure.\n\nThe sigmoid colon has a good blood supply from the sigmoidal branches of the inferior mesenteric artery and is less likely to be affected.\n\nThe Superior mesenteric artery supplies the right colon from the cecum to the splenic flexure. The splenic flexure is therefore a watershed area and is the most likely area to be affected by ischaemic colitis."
