      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Alcoholic liver disease",
      "explanation": "Budd-Chiari syndrome is most likely due to a thrombophilia\n\nAntithrombin III deficiency\n\nis the correct answer because Budd-Chiari syndrome, or hepatic vein thrombosis, most commonly arises due to an underlying prothrombotic state such as a hereditary thrombophilia. Antithrombin III deficiency is one of the classic inherited causes, along with protein C and S deficiencies and factor V Leiden mutation. The scenario describes acute hepatic venous outflow obstruction—sudden-onset abdominal pain, ascites, tender hepatomegaly—in a young woman without risk factors for cirrhosis or chronic liver disease, making a procoagulant disorder most likely according to UK guidelines.\n\nAutoimmune hepatitis\n\ntypically presents with insidious onset of symptoms such as fatigue, jaundice, or mild transaminitis rather than acute vascular obstruction features. While it can cause chronic liver dysfunction and cirrhosis over time, it would not directly lead to sudden hepatic vein thrombosis or Budd-Chiari syndrome in the absence of other thrombotic risk factors.\n\nAlcoholic liver disease\n\ngenerally results from prolonged heavy alcohol consumption leading to steatosis, alcoholic hepatitis or cirrhosis. It rarely manifests acutely with severe pain and ascites unless advanced decompensated cirrhosis develops. There are no features in this scenario suggesting a history of alcohol misuse or stigmata of chronic liver disease.\n\nWilson’s disease\n\n, an inherited copper metabolism disorder, usually presents in younger patients with neuropsychiatric signs or chronic liver dysfunction rather than acute hepatic vein thrombosis. The laboratory findings here do not suggest haemolysis or low ceruloplasmin; nor are there neurological symptoms described.\n\nViral hepatitis B infection\n\nmay cause acute hepatitis but does not typically present with isolated hepatic vein thrombosis unless complicated by fulminant hepatic failure—which would be accompanied by coagulopathy and encephalopathy. There is also no mention of risk factors for viral transmission or prodromal illness."
