      "explanation": "Ursodeoxycholic acid is the first-line medication for primary biliary cholangitis\n\nPrimary biliary cholangitis (previously referred to as primary biliary cirrhosis) is a chronic liver disorder typically seen in middle-aged females and is usually associated with other autoimmune diseases such as Sjogren's syndrome. Anti-mitochondrial antibodies (AMA) M2 subtype are present in 98% of patients and are highly specific.\n\nUrsodeoxycholic acid is the first-line treatment that slows disease progression and improves symptoms.\n\nCholestyramine can be used to relieve pruritus but does not affect disease progression.\n\nLiver transplantation is an option in decompensated liver disease (e.g. if bilirubin > 100). R recurrence in graft can occur but is not usually a problem.\n\nFat-soluble vitamins can be supplemented but are not a first-line treatment.\n\nMethotrexate is not considered a treatment option for primary biliary cholangitis, in fact, it should be avoided in hepatic impairment due to dose-related toxicity."
