      "explanation": "Oesophageal/Gastric Cancer - Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) is better than CT or MRI in assessing mural invasion\n\nFrom the above options, endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) is best at assessing mural invasion of cancer for local staging. It is generally considered more accurate for this purpose than both computed tomography (CT) and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI).\n\nCT scanning, whilst often performed as the first step, and useful for identifying the overt metastatic disease, is not considered as accurate as EUS for local staging.\n\nThoracoscopy and laparoscopy would be used to detect occult peritoneal disease, but not local staging.\n\nMagnetic resonance imaging (MRI), similarly to CT, is useful as an initial scan - although not as cheap or as easily available as CT.\n\nPositron emission tomography may be used in those with negative laparoscopy, to assess peritoneal spread."
