      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Small bowel aspiration",
      "explanation": "Hydrogen breath testing is an appropriate first line test for diagnosis of small bowel overgrowth syndrome\n\nSmall bowel overgrowth syndrome would be suspected in this patient as:\n\nShe has a history of diabetes mellitus which is a risk factor.\nFaecal calprotectin, coeliac screen and inflammatory markers (CRP and platelets) are normal, ruling out inflammatory bowel disease and malabsorption such as coeliac.\nA trial of mebeverine has been of no benefit which can bring relief in irritable bowel syndrome.\n\nThe hydrogen breath test is the most appropriate first-line diagnostic investigation for small bowel overgrowth syndrome. In this condition, the large amounts of bacteria in the small bowel compete with the patients gut for nutrients. In the hydrogen beath teat, the patient is fasted and then given a glucose drink. Bacteria will metabolise the glucose and produce hydrogen compounds which are then measured in the breath. A significant rise in hydrogen compounds from baseline indicates small bowel overgrowth syndrome.\n\nGastric emptying studies would be of use in the diagnosis of gastroparesis.\n\nAbdominal x-ray has no role in the diagnosis of small bowel overgrowth syndrome. It can be of use in the diagnosis of conditions such as constipation, volvulus and toxic megacolon to name a few.\n\nSmall bowel aspiration is also diagnostic for small bowel overgrowth syndrome but is not appropriate first-line due to the more invasive nature of the procedure.\n\nThe urea breath test is used in the detection of\n\nHelicobacter pylori\n\ninfection."
