      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Refer to hepatologist for liver biopsy",
      "explanation": "In patients with non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, enhanced liver fibrosis (ELF) testing is recommended to aid diagnosis of liver fibrosis\n\nThis patient most likely has a non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD), given that she has features of metabolic syndrome (prediabetes and obesity), elevated liver function tests (LFTs) and fatty liver infiltration on ultrasonography. It is important to further assess the risk of advanced liver fibrosis in these patients, as LFTs and ultrasonographic findings do not necessarily reflect the stage of disease, and early identification of liver fibrosis correlates with long-term liver-related outcomes. NICE recommends that the enhanced liver fibrosis (ELF) test - an algorithm that determines a result based on serum hyaluronic acid, amino-terminal propeptide of type III procollagen (PIIINP) and tissue inhibitor of metalloproteinase 1 (TIMP-1) - is performed in these patients.\n\nArranging an MRI liver is an incorrect option. This would not provide limited further information on liver fibrosis in this patient. Instead, an alternative imaging method that is increasingly used to determine hepatic fibrosis is transient elastography (FibroScan) which gives a 'liver stiffness measurement' using pulsed-echo ultrasound as a marker of fibrosis.\n\nCommencing atorvastatin is incorrect. While this may eventually form part of the management plan, it would be more appropriate to further investigate for fibrosis at this point. Furthermore, her ALT being more than 3 times the upper limit of normal means that this should also be proceeded with caution, probably after seeking specialist advice.\n\nReferring to hepatology for a liver biopsy is incorrect at this stage. While a liver biopsy is the gold standard test for diagnosing and staging NAFLD, it should be reserved for those whose staging is an indeterminate or high risk following non-invasive investigations.\n\nSimply repeating the liver function tests in 3-6 months would not be a safe option, as this patient needs further evaluation. As discussed previously, blood tests and ultrasonography alone are insufficient to assess for liver fibrosis."
