      "correctText": "Dietary modification",
      "explanation": "Dietary modification and topical steroids are used in the management of eosinophilic oesophagitis\n\nDietary modification is the correct answer. This 38-year-old man presents with intermittent dysphagia to solids and food impaction in association with asthma and peripheral blood eosinophilia suggesting eosinophilic oesophagitis as a diagnosis. Management in the first instance is the removal of foods to which the patient is allergic, resulting in eosinophilic oesophageal inflammation. The lack of a response to a PPI is typical and is a way of differentiating between this diagnosis and gastro-oesophageal reflux. Treatment is with dietary modification +/- topical steroid in the first instance.\n\nNifedipine is incorrect. This is a treatment for achalasia. This condition is typically associated with dysphagia to solids and liquids at presentation and eosinophilia is not a predominant feature.\n\nOral steroids are incorrect. If the dietary modification fails, topical steroids are trialled before oral steroids.\n\nH2-receptor antagonist is incorrect. The patient has already failed a proton pump inhibitor as the pathology is not directly related to the reflux of acid into the oesophagus. Therefore, a H2-receptor antagonist is also unlikely to be successful.\n\nAmoxicillin and clarithromycin are incorrect. This is a reasonable empiric choice for the eradication of\n\nHelicobacter pylori\n\ninfection. However, that infection tends to present with dyspepsia rather than dysphagia."
