      "correctText": "Urinary 5-HIAA",
      "explanation": "Flushing, diarrhoea, bronchospasm, tricuspid stenosis, pellagra → carcinoid with liver mets - diagnosis: urinary 5-HIAA\n\nUrinary 5-HIAA\n\nis correct. The clinical features are highly suggestive of carcinoid syndrome, which is caused by a small bowel neuroendocrine tumour that has metastasised to the liver. The most common peptide produced by these tumours is serotonin. 5-HIAA is a breakdown product of serotonin and urinary 5-HIAA is used as the initial diagnostic test.\n\nCT chest, abdomen and pelvis\n\nis incorrect. Although this may well be indicated (for staging), in the first instance urinary 5-HIAA should be performed to confirm the diagnosis.\n\nUrinary chromogramin A\n\nis incorrect. Serum chromogramin A, rather than urine, is a sentive screening test for carcinoid syndrome.\n\nSerum serotonin levels\n\nis incorrect. Serum serotonin levels can be checked if the 5-HIAA test results are normal or near normal but the clinical suspicion of carcinoid syndrome remains high.\n\nUrinary catecholamines\n\nis incorrect. A phaeochromocytoma is a less likely diagnosis in this case, given the absence of hypertension and tachycardia."
