      "explanation": "Familial adenomatous polyposis - once diagnosed patients typically have a total proctocolectomy with ileal pouch anal anastomosis due to the extremely high risk of developing colorectal cancer\n\nTotal proctocolectomy with ileal pouch-anal anastomosis\n\nis the correct answer. In patients with confirmed familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP), prophylactic surgery is recommended due to the near certainty of progression to colorectal cancer, often by age 40 if left untreated. UK guidelines recommend total proctocolectomy with ileal pouch-anal anastomosis (IPAA) as the standard surgical approach, especially in young adults who have extensive polyposis involving both colon and rectum. This procedure removes all at-risk mucosa while preserving continence, making it optimal for long-term outcomes.\n\nAnnual flexible sigmoidoscopy surveillance\n\nis not sufficient once FAP is diagnosed and numerous polyps are present, as regular endoscopic monitoring cannot prevent malignant transformation given the sheer number of polyps and high risk of missed lesions.\n\nSegmental colectomy only\n\nwould leave behind significant at-risk mucosa in both the remaining colon and rectum. In FAP, disease affects the entire colorectum, so segmental resection does not adequately reduce cancer risk.\n\nChemoprevention with NSAIDs\n\n, such as sulindac or celecoxib, may reduce polyp burden but does not eliminate malignant potential or replace definitive surgical management according to current guidelines.\n\nNo further intervention until symptoms develop\n\nwould be inappropriate; delaying treatment exposes patients to almost inevitable development of invasive carcinoma before symptoms manifest—a late stage when prognosis worsens significantly."
