      "id": 306,
      "question": "A 67-year-old man is investigated for dyspepsia. A gastroscopy reveals a suspicious lesion which is biopsied. Which one of the following findings on biopsy would be most consistent with a diagnosis of gastric adenocarcinoma?",
      "options": [
        "Columnar metaplasia",
        "Histiocytic infiltration",
        "Paneth cell metaplasia",
        "Giant cell granulomas",
        "Signet ring cells"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Giant cell granulomas",
      "explanation": "Gastric adenocarcinoma - signet ring cells\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nSignet ring cells\n\n. Signet ring cells are a type of cell that can be seen in certain types of cancer, most commonly gastric adenocarcinoma. These cells are characterised by the presence of a large vacuole containing mucin, which pushes the nucleus to one side giving the appearance of a signet ring under microscopy. The presence of these cells on biopsy would be consistent with a diagnosis of gastric adenocarcinoma.\n\nColumnar metaplasia\n\n, also known as Barrett's oesophagus, is not typically associated with gastric adenocarcinoma. This condition is characterised by the replacement of squamous epithelium with columnar epithelium in the lower oesophagus and it's usually associated with gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). While this condition can increase the risk for oesophageal adenocarcinoma, it's not directly linked to gastric adenocarcinoma.\n\nHistiocytic infiltration\n\nrefers to an accumulation of histiocytes (a type of immune cell) within tissue. This can occur in response to inflammation or infection and is not specific for any particular type of malignancy including gastric adenocarcinoma.\n\nPaneth cell metaplasia\n\ninvolves the abnormal presence of Paneth cells, which are normally found in the small intestine and have a role in innate immunity, outside their usual location. This can occur in various conditions such as inflammatory bowel disease but is not characteristic for gastric adenocarcinoma.\n\nFinally,\n\nGiant cell granulomas\n\nare benign lesions that often occur in oral tissues but can be found elsewhere in the body. They consist mainly of multinucleated giant cells and are generally not associated with malignancy including gastric adenocarcinoma."
