      "correctText": "HLA-DQ8",
      "explanation": "Coeliac disease has a strong association with HLA-DQ2 (present in 95% of patients)\n\nHLA-DQ2\n\nis the genetic marker most strongly associated with coeliac disease, being present in approximately 95% of affected individuals in the UK. Its presence is not diagnostic as it also occurs in about 30% of the general population, but its absence makes coeliac disease highly unlikely. This strong association underpins its use in challenging diagnostic scenarios or when serology and histology are inconclusive.\n\nHLA-B27\n\nis primarily associated with spondyloarthropathies such as ankylosing spondylitis and reactive arthritis, not coeliac disease. Although HLA-B27 positivity can be seen in some gastrointestinal disorders (such as inflammatory bowel disease-associated arthropathy), it does not have a significant link to coeliac disease.\n\nHLA-DR4\n\nhas been linked to several autoimmune conditions including rheumatoid arthritis and type 1 diabetes mellitus but does not have a strong association with coeliac disease. While both conditions may coexist due to shared autoimmune predisposition, DR4 itself is not a key marker for coeliac disease.\n\nHLA-DR3\n\nshows some association with autoimmune diseases such as type 1 diabetes and autoimmune hepatitis, which are themselves more common in patients with coeliac disease; however, DR3 is not the principal HLA haplotype implicated in coeliac pathogenesis.\n\nHLA-DQ8\n\n, while present in around 80% of patients who do not carry DQ2, remains less prevalent than DQ2 overall. It accounts for most remaining cases of genetically susceptible individuals but does not reach the same level of association as DQ2."
