      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "List for urgent repeat endoscopy",
      "explanation": "Transjugular Intrahepatic Portosystemic Shunt  commonly causes an exacerbation of hepatic encephalopathy\n\nThis patient has presented with an upper gastrointestinal haemorrhage in the context of decompensated non-alcoholic fatty liver disease. He has undergone placement of a transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPSS) as an emergency measure to stem variceal bleeding. This involves the formation of an artificial conduit between the hepatic portal vein and the hepatic vein. In cirrhotic patients, advanced liver fibrosis obstructs the flow of blood from the hepatic portal vein through the liver, causing the build-up of high portal venous pressures. Bypassing the liver with a TIPSS reduces the blood flow to the bleeding varix. However, as a consequence, nitrogenous waste products in the portal circulation are unable to be processed into urea by the liver, leading to the accumulation of ammonia, and the onset of hyperammonemic encephalopathy. This is the cause for this patient's new disorientation, and is characterised by asterixis (a 3-5Hz asterixis hand 'flap'). Hepatic encephalopathy is best treated with the removal of ammonifying gut bacteria, by ensuring regular bowel motions. The presence of faecal impaction on the patient's original rectal examination ensures that rectal evacuation with an enema plus regular laxatives is the most appropriate strategy to reverse his encephalopathy.\n\nA non-contrast CT scan of the head would not reveal the diagnosis of hepatic encephalopathy (which is largely a clinical diagnosis). However, if his symptoms failed to resolve despite regular loose bowel motions, the diagnosis may need to be revisited, and a CT scan may become appropriate.\n\nAlthough infection is a common cause of decompensation of liver disease, and hence encephalopathy, by far the most likely culprit here is the placement of a TIPSS. With improving inflammatory markers, there is little evidence for an intercurrent infection, and so piperacillin-tazobactam is unlikely to be of benefit. It is reasonable to argue that this patient should have been given prophylactic fluoroquinolones during the initial management of his upper gastrointestinal haemorrhage, but broad spectrum antibiotics are not the most appropriate intervention at this stage.\n\nA repeat endoscopy would be an appropriate intervention if there was suspicion of re-bleeding. However, his repeat blood tests show an improved haemoglobin and decreasing urea, and there is no mention of further haematemesis or new melaena, making this unlikely.\n\nHepatic encephalopathy secondary to TIPSS placement may resolve spontaneously. However, given the original examination findings of faecal impaction, it is preferable to expedite this with minimally invasive interventions such as an enema and regular laxatives, rather than simply observe the patient."
