      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Oral methotrexate",
      "explanation": "If a patient with ulcerative colitis has had a severe relapse or >=2 exacerbations in the past year they should be given either oral azathioprine or oral mercaptopurine to maintain remission\n\nOral azathioprine\n\nis the most appropriate maintenance therapy for this patient. According to NICE guidelines, patients with ulcerative colitis who have had a severe relapse or two or more exacerbations in the past year should be offered either oral azathioprine or oral mercaptopurine to maintain remission. This patient has had three exacerbations in the past year requiring oral prednisolone, which indicates frequent disease activity despite being on mesalazine. Azathioprine is an immunomodulator that suppresses T-cell function and reduces inflammation in the bowel mucosa. It has been shown to be effective in maintaining remission and reducing steroid dependency in patients with frequently relapsing ulcerative colitis. The patient would need regular monitoring of full blood count and liver function tests while on this medication.\n\nOral budesonide\n\nis not recommended as a maintenance therapy for ulcerative colitis. Corticosteroids, including budesonide, are effective for inducing remission in active disease but are not appropriate for long-term maintenance due to significant side effects with prolonged use, including osteoporosis, diabetes, hypertension, and adrenal suppression. Furthermore, there is no evidence supporting the efficacy of budesonide in maintaining remission in ulcerative colitis.\n\nOral methotrexate\n\nis not recommended for the management of ulcerative colitis according to NICE guidelines. While methotrexate is used in Crohn's disease as a maintenance therapy, evidence for its efficacy in ulcerative colitis is limited and inconsistent. Therefore, it would not be an appropriate choice for this patient with frequently relapsing disease.\n\nRectal mesalazine\n\nis an effective maintenance therapy for patients with distal disease (proctitis or proctosigmoiditis), either alone or in combination with oral aminosalicylates. However, for a patient with multiple exacerbations in the past year despite being on oral mesalazine, adding rectal mesalazine would be insufficient. The history of three exacerbations requiring systemic steroids indicates the need for more potent immunosuppression with azathioprine or mercaptopurine.\n\nTopical corticosteroid\n\ntherapy, like systemic corticosteroids, is not appropriate for maintenance treatment in ulcerative colitis. Topical steroids are used for inducing remission in distal disease but are associated with local and systemic side effects if used long-term. They have not been shown to be effective in maintaining remission and would not address this patient's need for more effective prevention of frequent relapses."
