      "explanation": "Carcinoid syndrome can affect the right side of the heart. The valvular effects are tricuspid insufficiency and pulmonary stenosis\n\nCarcinoid syndrome is a neuroendocrine tumour. There are many locations that they can occur in such as in the GI tract, in the respiratory tract and many other places. They can secrete serotonin which leads to many of the symptoms this patient suffers. The syndrome is associated with right-sided valvular pathology. The most common pathology is tricuspid insufficiency and pulmonary stenosis. The best way to remember is the acronym 'TIPS'.\n\nHOCM is associated with inherited gene defects on the β-myosin heavy chain. This is not associated with carcinoid syndrome.\n\nAcquired VSD would be associated with an MI and not carcinoid syndrome.\n\nAortic dissection is associated with hypertension and connective tissue disorder.\n\nLoeffler endocarditis is endocarditis due to eosinophil proliferation in the heart. It is associated with helminthic infections and not carcinoid syndrome."
