      "explanation": "MRCP is the investigations of choice in primary sclerosing cholangitis\n\nThis patient's presentation is suggestive of primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC). This is a biliary disease of unknown aetiology that causes fibrosis and inflammation of both intra- and extra-hepatic bile ducts. It is strongly associated with ulcerative colitis (UC) - 80% of patients with PSC have UC (although only a minority of patients with UC will develop PSC). Around half of patients present symptomatically, with jaundice, pruritus, fatigue or right upper quadrant pain. Around half may present asymptomatically. ALP and bilirubin are typically deranged. The investigations of choice are\n\nMRCP\n\nand endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP), as these will demonstrate biliary strictures with a 'beaded' appearance, confirming the diagnosis.\n\nAbdominal ultrasound\n\nis incorrect. Although useful in assessing liver disease, it does not adequately evaluate the biliary tree to diagnose or exclude PSC.\n\nLiver biopsy\n\nis incorrect. Fibrous, obliterative cholangitis may be seen, often described as an 'onion skin' appearance, although findings are generally non-specific.\n\nMPO-ANCA\n\n(also known as p-ANCA) is incorrect. Whilst this is associated with both UC and PSC, it is not specific to either condition and thus is not particularly useful diagnostically.\n\nAMA\n\nis incorrect. This is classically associated with primary biliary cholangitis (PBC), rather than PSC. PBC typically affects middle-aged women and is not associated with inflammatory bowel disease."
