      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Superficial thrombophlebitis",
      "explanation": "Thrombophlebitis is a common side-effect of TPN, especially if given peripherally\n\nSuperficial thrombophlebitis\n\nis the most likely diagnosis in this scenario. The patient has developed pain, erythema, tenderness and induration along the course of the vein where the peripheral cannula is sited for TPN administration. These are classic signs of thrombophlebitis, which is inflammation of a vein associated with thrombus formation. Thrombophlebitis is a common complication of peripheral TPN administration due to the hyperosmolar nature of TPN solutions, which are irritating to peripheral veins. The high osmolality and sometimes acidic pH of TPN solutions can cause chemical irritation of the vein endothelium, leading to inflammation and subsequent thrombus formation. This is why TPN is ideally administered via central venous access for longer-term use. The patient's symptoms developed after several days of peripheral TPN, which is a typical timeframe for this complication to manifest.\n\nCellulitis\n\nis a bacterial infection of the skin and subcutaneous tissues, which can present with erythema, warmth, and tenderness. However, cellulitis typically presents as a more diffuse area of inflammation rather than following the linear course of a vein. While cellulitis can occur at cannula sites, the linear distribution of symptoms along the vein strongly suggests thrombophlebitis instead.\n\nCatheter-related bloodstream infection\n\n(CRBSI) is a serious complication of intravenous cannulation and TPN administration. However, patients with CRBSI typically present with systemic symptoms such as high fever, rigors, and signs of sepsis. This patient has only a minimal temperature elevation (37.2°C) and the inflammation is localised to the cannula site and along the vein, making CRBSI less likely than thrombophlebitis.\n\nExtravasation injury\n\noccurs when intravenous fluids or medications leak into the surrounding tissues rather than remaining in the vein. While extravasation of TPN can cause significant tissue damage and inflammation, it typically presents as a more localised area of swelling, pain and potentially tissue necrosis at the cannula site, rather than inflammation along the course of the vein as described in this case.\n\nAllergic reaction to TPN components\n\nis uncommon but can occur, particularly in response to lipid emulsions or certain additives. However, allergic reactions typically present with more generalised symptoms such as urticaria, pruritus, or in severe cases, anaphylaxis. The localised nature of this patient's symptoms along the vein makes an allergic reaction less likely than thrombophlebitis."
