      "explanation": "Ursodeoxycholic acid is the first-line medication for primary biliary cholangitis\n\nThe patient's cholestatic jaundice along with the raised IgM levels and positive anti-mitochondrial antibody suggests primary biliary cirrhosis (PBC) as the underlying diagnosis. It is common in middle-aged ladies and typically presents with general fatigue and itch due to jaundice. Treatment of this condition is normally with ursodeoxycholic acid. It is a secondary bile salt and studies have shown patients have decreased disease progression while on ursodeoxycholic acid. The precise mechanism of action is not well understood.\n\nObeticholic acid is a 2nd line treatment available for PBC. It is a semi-synthetic bile acid and also acts as a farsenoid X receptor agonist. It can reduce bile acid synthesis and may have a choleretic action. Obeticholic acid is used when patients cannot tolerate or displays disease progression while on ursodeoxycholic acid.\n\nCholestyramine is used as the 1st line treatment for symptomatic control of pruritis. It does not confer any benefit to slow disease progression.\n\nPrednisolone is not indicated in PBC and is used as part of the treatment in autoimmune hepatitis. Autoimmune hepatitis can also present in a similar indolent fashion but typically presents with raised transaminase levels with a positive anti-smooth muscle antibody. While a positive ANA typically points to autoimmune hepatitis, up to70% of patients with PBC can have a positive ANA.\n\nLiver transplantation is a treatment option but is typically only considered in patients with advanced liver disease."
