      "explanation": "Gastrin is produced by the G cells in the antrum of the stomach\n\nG cells\n\nis the correct answer. Gastrin is produced by the G cells in the antrum of the stomach. This patient's presentation is suggestive of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, a condition caused by a gastrin-secreting neuroendocrine tumour. This overproduction of gastrin leads to hypersecretion of acid and peptic ulcer disease, as seen here. The secretin stimulation test is often used as confirmation of the diagnosis - within 15 minutes of secretin infusion, a significant rise in fasting serum gastrin is noted.\n\nD cells\n\nof the pancreas and stomach produce somatostatin, not gastrin. Somatostatin decreases gastrin, acid and pepsin secretion.\n\nI cells\n\nof the duodenum produce cholecystokinin. This increases the secretion of enzyme-rich pancreatic fluid and decreases gastric emptying.\n\nK cells\n\nof the duodenum produce gastric inhibitory peptide (GIP), not gastrin. GIP is an inhibitor of gastric acid secretion and a stimulator of insulin secretion.\n\nS cells\n\nof the duodenum produce secretin. This increases the secretion of bicarbonate-rich pancreatic fluid and decreases gastric acid secretion."
