      "correctText": "Inflammatory bowel disease",
      "explanation": "Diarrhoea - biospy shows pigment laden macrophages = laxative abuse\n\nThe most likely diagnosis is\n\nlaxative abuse\n\n. Although the presentation of diarrhoea and weight loss is intentionally vague, colonoscopy findings of dark-brown discolouration in the proximal colon are suggestive of laxative abuse. This is confirmed by the biopsy results demonstrating pigment-laden macrophages in the lamina propria, termed melanosis coli. Specifically, senna and other anthraquinone compounds are associated with this appearance. She may be abusing laxatives for weight control, so further history is needed.\n\nCoeliac disease\n\nis incorrect. This also presents with diarrhoea and weight loss. Malabsorption typically leads to anaemia and nutritional deficiencies. Definitive diagnosis involves a biopsy of the small bowel. Features seen on microscopic examination include villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes, none of which are seen here.\n\nInflammatory bowel disease (IBD)\n\nis incorrect. Patients with IBD usually present with diarrhoea, sometimes bloody, and with or without abdominal pain and tenesmus. Blood tests often show anaemia and raised inflammatory markers. A colonoscopy would demonstrate ulcerations, erythema, pseudopolyps, or a cobblestone appearance with skip lesions. The findings here instead show melanosis coli and thus laxative abuse.\n\nMicroscopic colitis\n\nis incorrect. This involves chronic, watery diarrhoea. Colonoscopy is often normal, but biopsy reveals mononuclear infiltrates. The findings here do not support this.\n\nPeutz-Jeghers syndrome\n\nis incorrect. Although this also leads to melanotic discolouration of the bowel, polyps would also be expected, as well as pigmentation of the skin and mucous surfaces."
