      "explanation": "Secretin increases secretion of bicarbonate-rich fluid from pancreas and hepatic duct cells\n\nBased on the clinical presentation, and elevated serum gastrin levels, the patient likely has a a diagnosis of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (ZES).\n\nSecretin\n\nis correct. Secretin increases the secretion of bicarbonate-rich fluid from the pancreas and hepatic duct cells and decreases gastric acid secretion. The secretin stimulation test can be used to confirm ZES, with a positive result characterised by a marked increase in serum gastrin levels.\n\nCholecystokinin (CCK)\n\nis incorrect. CCK increases secretion of enzyme-rich fluid from the pancreas, contraction of the gallbladder and relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi, and decreases gastric emptying.\n\nGastrin\n\nis incorrect. Gastrin increases acid secretion via gastric parietal cells, pepsinogen, and intrinsic factor secretion, increases gastric motility, and stimulates parietal cell maturation. Although the raised gastrin levels are indicative of ZES, the secretin test is required to confirm the diagnosis.\n\nSomatostatin\n\nis incorrect. Somatostatin decreases acid and pepsin secretion, decreases gastrin secretion, decreases pancreatic enzyme secretion, and decreases insulin and glucagon secretion.\n\nVasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP)\n\nis incorrect. VIP stimulates secretion by the pancreas and intestines and inhibits acid secretion. Raised serum VIP levels are associated with VIPomas which present with diarrhoea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria."
