      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Irritable bowel syndrome",
      "explanation": "Diarrhoea, weight, arthralgia, lymphadenopathy, ophthalmoplegia ?Whipple's disease\n\nWhipple's disease is a rare bacterial infection that most often affects your joints and digestive system. It is a chronic, curable, systemic bacterial infection caused by\n\nTropheryma whipplei\n\n. The key features of this disease include chronic joint pain, chronic diarrhoea that can involve steatorrhea and significant weight loss, as seen in this case. They can experience crampy abdominal pains particularly worse after meals. Disturbances of ocular movement can be a common finding with the ophthalmoplegia almost always being of supranuclear gaze palsy type.\n\nAdult-onset Still's disease is a rare systemic auto-inflammatory disease characterised by the classic triad of fevers, joint pain, and a distinctive salmon-coloured bumpy rash. The absence of fevers and rash, in this case, makes the diagnosis unlikely.\n\nChronic pancreatitis presents with abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting, steatorrhea and weight loss. Joint pains and visual disturbances are rarely associated with this condition.\n\nCoeliac disease is a serious illness where the body's immune system attacks its own tissues when you eat gluten. Key features include malabsorption, including diarrhoea, steatorrhoea, weight loss, or failure to thrive. There is no suggestion of a dietary component to the history and such a late presentation would be rare.\n\nIrritable bowel syndrome is a diagnosis of exclusion consisting of features such as stomach cramps, bloating, diarrhoea and constipation that can be associated with stress."
