      "question": "A 65-year-old female presents with an 8-week history of non-bloody, watery diarrhoea. She is opening her bowels up to 9 times per day and has lost 4 kg in weight.\n\nShe has a past medical history of gastroesophageal reflux disease treated with lansoprazole. She is a long-term smoker and teetotal.\n\nHer vital signs are within normal ranges. Her body mass index is 19 kg/m². Clinical examination was unremarkable.\n\nInvestigations reveal the following:\n\n\nHb\t115 g/L\t(115 - 160)\nESR\t40 mm/hr\tWomen: < ((age + 10) / 2)\nPlatelets\t398 * 109/L\t(150 - 400)\nWBC\t8.2 * 109/L\t(4.0 - 11.0)\n\n\nClostridium difficile toxin\tNegative\nStool culture and microscopy\tNegative\nFaecal calprotectin\t110 mcg/g\t< 100\n\nWhich of the following investigations is most likely to reveal the correct diagnosis?",
