      "correctText": "Meigs' syndrome",
      "explanation": "Ascites: a high SAAG gradient (> 11g/L) indicates portal hypertension\n\nPortal hypertension\n\nis correct. This patient is presenting with ascites, and although clinical information is sparse, inferences as to the cause can be made from the ascitic fluid analysis. The serum ascites albumin gradient is the serum albumin level minus the ascitic albumin level. A high serum ascites albumin gradient suggests low levels of protein in the ascitic fluid - causes of a high gradient include portal hypertension and right heart failure.\n\nIntra-abdominal malignancy\n\nis incorrect. The ascitic fluid analysis in this question shows a high serum ascites albumin gradient. Intra-abdominal malignancy would produce a low gradient. There would likely be other suggestive features in the sample, such as raised white cell count/red cell count, and a bloody appearance to the sample. The high serum ascites albumin gradient in this sample suggests portal hypertension as the cause.\n\nMeigs' syndrome\n\nis incorrect. Meigs' syndrome is the triad of ovarian fibroma, ascites, and pleural effusions. The ascites caused by this can have a high serum ascites albumin gradient, but it would only be seen in patients with ovaries, so Meigs' syndrome is incorrect.\n\nNephrotic syndrome\n\nis incorrect. This patient's ascitic fluid sample shows a high serum ascites albumin gradient, suggestive of portal hypertension. Nephrotic syndrome would normally result in ascites with a low gradient.\n\nSpontaneous bacterial peritonitis\n\nis incorrect. Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis is a bacterial infection of ascitic fluid. Ascitic fluid analysis in a patient with spontaneous bacterial peritonitis would reveal a raised white cell count with predominantly neutrophils. The normal cell counts, lack of visualised organisms, and clear appearance of the fluid make spontaneous bacterial peritonitis unlikely in this case."
