      "id": 392,
      "question": "A 34-year-old man presents for follow-up at the inflammatory bowel clinic. He has a long-standing history of ileal Crohn's disease, having been diagnosed when he was in his early 20's.\n\nSix months ago he was hospitalised with a severe flare, that failed to respond to medical therapy. Following an ileal resection with primary anastomosis at that point, his symptoms and inflammatory markers settled and he was discharged.\n\nSince then, he has had no problems with pain but continues to have diarrhoea, and is now opening his bowels 5 times per day. Recent investigations, including serum CRP, faecal elastase, and faecal calprotectin, have been normal.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate medication to reduce this patient's diarrhoea?",
      "options": [
        "Cholestyramine",
        "Mesalazine",
        "Pancreatic enzyme supplements",
        "Ursodeoxycholic acid",
        "Vedolizumab"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Vedolizumab",
      "explanation": "Ongoing diarrhoea in Crohn's patient post-resection with normal CRP → cholestyramine\n\nThis is a man with ileal Crohn's disease who has diarrhoea post ileal resection. With a normal CRP and faecal calprotectin, this is likely secondary to bile acid malabsorption. In health, bile acids are secreted by the liver to aid in the emulsification of dietary lipids. Over 95% are subsequently reabsorbed in the terminal ileum. In patients with ileal Crohn's, especially those who have had a resection, this process is impaired. This results in bile salts progressing into the colon, where they stimulate increased water secretion and increased colonic mobility, giving rise to diarrhoea.\n\nCholestyramine is a bile acid sequestrant. It binds to bile acids within the GI tract and forms insoluble complexes which do not result in increased water secretion or colonic mobility. It is therefore the correct answer here.\n\nMesalazine is a 5-ASA class drug that is used to reduce inflammation in inflammatory bowel disease, including ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease. Given the normal CRP and faecal calprotectin, it is unlikely to be of benefit here.\n\nPancreatic enzyme supplementation is used in cases of pancreatic insufficiency. Reduced pancreatic lipase results in increased lipid concentrations in excreted faeces, resulting in steatorrhoea. Given the lack of history of a cause of pancreatic insufficiency (e.g. chronic pancreatitis) and the normal faecal elastase, this will not help this man.\n\nUrsodeoxycholic acid is a bile acid that was first isolated from the bile of bears, and hence was named after Ursus, a genus of bears. It is used primarily in primary biliary cholangitis, where it may offer a mortality benefit. It would not be useful in this man, and may in fact worsen his symptoms.\n\nVedolizumab is a monoclonal antibody used in refractory Crohn's disease. It binds to integrin α4β7, resulting in gut-specific anti-inflammatory activity. Given that there is no evidence of active Crohn's in this patient, this treatment would not likely be of any benefit."
