      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Constriction of the splanchnic vessels",
      "explanation": "Terlipressin - method of action = constriction of the splanchnic vessels\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nConstriction of the splanchnic vessels\n\n. Terlipressin is a synthetic analogue of vasopressin, and it works primarily by causing vasoconstriction of the splanchnic vessels. This reduces portal venous inflow, which in turn decreases portal pressure. It's used in the management of variceal bleeding, a serious complication of portal hypertension in patients with liver cirrhosis.\n\nThe option\n\nPortal system vasodilation\n\nis incorrect. Vasodilation would increase blood flow into the portal system, which would worsen variceal bleeding instead of alleviating it. Medications that cause vasodilation are typically avoided in this setting.\n\nAntifibrinolytic\n\nis also incorrect. Antifibrinolytics work by preventing the breakdown of fibrin clots, thus promoting clot formation and reducing bleeding. However, terlipressin does not have antifibrinolytic properties; its primary mechanism is vasoconstriction.\n\nThe option\n\nConstriction of hepatic venules\n\nis not correct either. While constriction of hepatic venules might theoretically reduce portal pressure, this is not how terlipressin works. Moreover, constricting hepatic venules could potentially exacerbate hepatic congestion and damage liver cells.\n\nFinally,\n\nEndothelin-1 antagonist\n\nis also incorrect as an answer choice. Endothelin-1 antagonists are used to treat conditions like pulmonary arterial hypertension by causing systemic and pulmonary vasodilation - they don't play a role in managing variceal bleeding or affect terlipressin's mechanism of action."
