      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Fusobacterium necrophorum",
      "explanation": "Patients with peritonsillar abscesses can develop Lemierre's syndrome (thrombophlebitis of the IJV)- this can present with neck pain, and can result in septic pulmonary embolism\n\nFusobacterium necrophorum\n\nis correct because the patient’s history of peritonsillar swelling, neck pain, and septic pulmonary emboli (cavitating lung nodules) strongly suggests Lemierre’s syndrome. It is the primary causative pathogen of Lemierre’s syndrome, a life-threatening septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein (IJV) that arises as a complication of peritonsillar infections, such as peritonsillar abscess or pharyngitis. The infection spreads from the oropharynx to the parapharyngeal space, leading to IJV thrombosis and subsequent septic emboli to the lungs, causing cavitating pneumonia.\n\nGroup A Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes)\n\nis incorrect because although it is a common cause of pharyngitis and peritonsillar abscesses, it does not typically cause Lemierre’s syndrome or IJV thrombophlebitis with septic emboli. It can, however, cause severe pharyngitis, scarlet fever, or invasive infections like necrotising fasciitis.\n\nHaemophilus influenzae\n\nis incorrect because while it can cause epiglottitis, otitis media, and pneumonia, it does not commonly lead to Lemierre’s syndrome or internal jugular vein thrombosis. It is more associated with respiratory tract infections rather than peritonsillar infections with septic emboli.\n\nKlebsiella pneumoniae\n\nis incorrect because although it can cause pneumonia and lung abscesses, it is not a common cause of peritonsillar infections or Lemierre’s syndrome. Klebsiella is more typically associated with alcoholism and diabetes-related pneumonia, particularly in East Asia.\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\nis incorrect because although it can cause septic thrombophlebitis and metastatic infections, it is not the most common pathogen implicated in Lemierre’s syndrome. It can be involved in septic emboli due to infective endocarditis, but does not typically arise from peritonsillar infections."
