      "correctText": "Once symptoms appear",
      "explanation": "Antiretroviral therapy should generally be started as soon as HIV is diagnosed\n\nThe BNF recommends that HIV positive patients should be offered antiretroviral treatment at diagnosis, irrespective of the CD4 count.\n\nIt is not recommended to wait for symptoms before starting antiretroviral treatment. Developing symptoms may indicate a need to alter antiretroviral therapy.\n\nA CD4 count < 200cells/µL defines HIV has progressed to acquired immune deficiency syndrome (AIDS).\n\nAn HIV-negative person has a CD4 count >500 cells/µL. It used to be that a CD4 count < 500 was recommended for starting treatment, but this is no longer the case.\n\nThe primary role of the viral load is to monitor response to antiretroviral therapy, the aim is for it to become undetectable."
