      "id": 9,
      "question": "A 38-year-old man with HIV presents with progressively worsening speech and behavioural problems over the past month. He has also developed difficulties with coordination, particularly affecting his gait. He denies any recent fever, headache, or seizures.\n\nOn exam, he appears alert but demonstrates mild cognitive impairment, slurred speech, and signs of ataxia, including unsteady gait and difficulty with fine motor tasks. Neurological examination also reveals a decrease in coordination and balance.\n\nGiven the most likely diagnosis, what would be the findings on an MRI head?",
      "options": [
        "Multifocal non-enhancing lesions",
        "Ring-enhancing lesions",
        "Solitary lesions",
        "Vasogenic oedema",
        "White matter hyperintensity on T2-weighted imaging"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Vasogenic oedema",
      "explanation": "A patient who is known to have HIV presents gradually worsening speech and behavioural problems associated with coordination difficulties. A MRI shows multifocal non-enhancing lesions - JC virus\n\nMultifocal non-enhancing lesions\n\nis the correct answer because these are typically seen on MRI scans in patients with progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), which is caused by JC virus. This infection primarily affects immunocompromised individuals, such as those with HIV. The lesions in PML are non-enhancing, which means there is no blood-brain barrier breakdown. The multifocal nature of the lesions is also characteristic of this condition, distinguishing it from other causes of brain lesions.\n\nRing-enhancing lesions\n\nis incorrect because these are typically associated with conditions like abscesses, metastases, or granulomatous infections, where there is blood-brain barrier disruption and oedema. In contrast, PML presents with non-enhancing lesions. This distinction is crucial because ring-enhancing lesions suggest an inflammatory or infectious process that involves blood-brain barrier compromise, which does not occur in PML.\n\nSolitary lesions\n\nis incorrect because PML commonly presents with multiple lesions, rather than a single lesion. The multifocal nature of the lesions reflects the widespread impact of the JC virus on the central nervous system. A solitary lesion is more suggestive of a focal neurological process, such as a tumour or abscess, rather than a viral infection like PML, which tends to affect several areas of the brain.\n\nVasogenic oedema\n\nis incorrect because, although PML lesions can occasionally show mild oedema around the lesions, significant vasogenic oedema with associated ring-enhancing lesions is not typical of PML. Vasogenic oedema is more characteristic of conditions with blood-brain barrier disruption, such as tumours or abscesses, which present with ring-enhancing lesions. The defining features of PML are its non-enhancing, multifocal white matter lesions, which may occasionally be surrounded by minimal oedema.\n\nWhite matter hyperintensity on T2-weighted imaging\n\nis incorrect because, while PML indeed shows white matter hyperintensities on T2-weighted MRI scans, these are part of the characteristic multifocal, non-enhancing lesions of the condition. The explanation should distinguish that white matter hyperintensities are not unique to PML and can be seen in various conditions, such as multiple sclerosis or chronic small vessel disease. The absence of enhancement and the multifocal nature of the lesions are the hallmark features that distinguish PML from other causes of white matter hyperintensity."
