      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Staphylococcus aureus",
      "explanation": "Eikenella\n\nEikenella corrodens\n\nis the most likely causative organism in this scenario. This is a classic presentation of a 'fight bite' injury, where a person's knuckle comes into contact with another person's teeth during a punch, resulting in a human bite wound. E. corrodens is a gram-negative facultative anaerobic bacillus that is part of the normal oral flora. It is particularly associated with infections following human bites, especially clenched-fist injuries where the knuckle strikes teeth. These infections typically present with cellulitis, abscess formation, and purulent discharge as seen in this patient. The delayed presentation (2 days after injury) is also characteristic, as patients often seek medical attention only when infection becomes established. E. corrodens infections are often polymicrobial, but this organism is considered a hallmark pathogen in human bite wounds.\n\nPasteurella multocida\n\nis primarily associated with animal bites, particularly from cats and dogs, rather than human bites. P. multocida infections typically present with rapid onset of symptoms (within 24 hours) and cause significant local inflammation. This would be an unlikely pathogen in a human bite scenario.\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\ncan be involved in human bite infections as part of a polymicrobial infection, and it is a common skin pathogen. However, it is not specifically characteristic of human bite wounds compared to Eikenella, which is more distinctively associated with oral flora transmitted through human bites.\n\nFusobacterium nucleatum\n\nis an anaerobic organism found in the oral cavity and can be involved in human bite infections. While it may contribute to the infection in this case as part of a polymicrobial process, it is less specifically associated with fight bite injuries than Eikenella corrodens.\n\nStreptococcus pyogenes\n\n(Group A Streptococcus) can cause skin and soft tissue infections and may be present in human bite wounds. However, it is not as characteristically associated with human bite infections as Eikenella corrodens. Streptococcal infections typically present with more rapidly spreading cellulitis rather than the localized infection described in this case."
