      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Bordetella pertussis",
      "explanation": "Cat scratch disease - caused by\n\nThe correct answer to this question is\n\nBartonella henselae\n\n. Cat Scratch Disease (CSD), also known as Cat Scratch Fever, is primarily caused by the Gram-negative bacterium\n\nBartonella henselae\n\n. This organism is typically transmitted to humans through scratches or bites from infected cats. The disease usually presents with regional lymphadenopathy, fever and other systemic symptoms.\n\nThe first incorrect option,\n\nBordetella pertussis\n\n, is a Gram-negative coccobacillus that causes whooping cough (pertussis). It's transmitted via respiratory droplets and doesn't have any connection with cat scratches or bites.\n\nMoraxella catarrhalis\n\nis another incorrect option. This Gram-negative diplococcus is one of the causes of otitis media in children and lower respiratory tract infections in adults with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Like Bordetella pertussis, it doesn't cause infection following a cat scratch or bite.\n\nThe fourth option,\n\nFrancisella tularensis\n\n, is a small Gram-negative coccobacillus responsible for causing tularemia. It can be transmitted through various routes including direct contact with infected animals (typically rabbits and hares rather than cats), insect bites, inhalation of contaminated dust or aerosols, and ingestion of contaminated water. While it can cause lymphadenopathy like CSD, the history of being scratched by a cat makes this less likely.\n\nFinally,\n\nYersinia enterocolitica\n\n, another Gram-negative bacillus, is an incorrect choice as well. It's primarily associated with foodborne illness presenting as gastroenteritis and mesenteric adenitis which mimics appendicitis. Transmission occurs mainly through the consumption of contaminated food or water rather than animal scratches or bites."
