      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Transitional cell bladder cancer",
      "explanation": "S\n\nSquamous cell bladder cancer\n\nis the correct answer as it is most strongly associated with infection by\n\nSchistosoma haematobium\n\n. This parasitic flatworm, endemic in parts of Africa and the Middle East, has a predilection for the venous plexuses of the urinary bladder. Chronic infection leads to persistent inflammation, which over time can cause squamous metaplasia of the transitional epithelium of the bladder. This metaplasia can progress to dysplasia and eventually squamous cell carcinoma. The mechanism involves chronic irritation from eggs deposited in the bladder wall, leading to granulomatous inflammation, fibrosis, and calcification (known as sandy patches). The International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC) has classified\n\nS. haematobium\n\nas a Group 1 carcinogen due to this strong association with squamous cell bladder cancer.\n\nTransitional cell bladder cancer\n\nis incorrect. While this is the most common type of bladder cancer in the general population (accounting for approximately 90% of bladder cancers in developed countries), it is not the type most strongly associated with\n\nS. haematobium\n\ninfection. Transitional cell carcinoma typically has different risk factors, including tobacco smoking, occupational exposure to aromatic amines, and chronic inflammation from other causes such as recurrent urinary tract infections or long-term catheterisation.\n\nLung cancer\n\nis incorrect. There is no established association between\n\nS. haematobium\n\ninfection and lung cancer. The primary site of pathology in\n\nS. haematobium\n\ninfection is the urinary tract, particularly the bladder. Lung cancer is predominantly associated with tobacco smoking, radon exposure, asbestos, and other environmental or occupational carcinogens, not with parasitic infections.\n\nHepatoma\n\n(hepatocellular carcinoma) is incorrect. While schistosomiasis can affect the liver, it is specifically\n\nSchistosoma mansoni\n\nand\n\nSchistosoma japonicum\n\nthat are associated with hepatic disease, not\n\nS. haematobium\n\n. These species can cause periportal fibrosis and portal hypertension, potentially contributing to hepatocellular carcinoma development. However, the strongest parasitic risk factor for hepatoma is chronic infection with\n\nClonorchis sinensis\n\nor\n\nOpisthorchis viverrini\n\n, not schistosomiasis.\n\nVulval carcinoma\n\nis incorrect. While female genital schistosomiasis caused by\n\nS. haematobium\n\ncan affect the vulva and other parts of the female genital tract, there is no strong established association between\n\nS. haematobium\n\ninfection and vulval carcinoma. Female genital schistosomiasis is associated with infertility, dyspareunia, and potentially increased susceptibility to HIV infection, but not specifically with vulval malignancy."
