      "explanation": "Treatment for invasive amoebiasis should be followed by a luminal amoebicide to eradicate the cystic stage which is resistant to metronidazole and tinidazole (which are used against the invasive stage)\n\nAmoebic colitis is a severe and occasionally life threatening condition which required a high index of suspicion and prompt management.  It is caused by Entamoeba histolytica and trophozoite stage cause significant damage to the bowel mucosa and can metastasise to other organs (most commonly the liver causing an amoebic liver abscess).\n\nThe trophozoite stage is responsive to metronidazole and tinidazole; however, once treated a dormant cystic stage remains and has the potential to cause further disease.  This stage is resistant to both metronidazole and tinidazole but can be eradicated with ten days of diloxanide furoate.  Paromomycin is considered a second line treatment for the cystic stage and is more commonly used in North America.\n\nIn many low/middle income countries treatment of the cystic stage is not recommended as re-infection is common, but in returning travellers with low chance of re-infection it is invariably given if available.\n\nAlthough metronidazole is the correct treatment dapsone is used for leprosy and occasionally for other mycobacterial diseases.\n\nQuinine is a treatment for malaria and although in severe cases meropenem might be considered to prevent secondary bacterial translation of bowel pathogens it would not treat the trophozoites.\n\nCeftriaxone again might be considered to prevent bowel pathogen translocation but is not effective again Entamoeba histolytica and the proton pump inhibitor would offer little benefit.\n\nClarithromycin is used to cover for atypical pneumonia amongst others and liposomal amphotericin B is used to treat a variety of fungal infections and hence neither would be appropriate choices here."
