      "question": "A 65-year-old woman who recently returned from Thailand was admitted after 5-days of a fever running at 38.5„ƒ which climbed to 40.2„ƒ on day-7 with confusion and delirium. Neurological examinations revealed impaired consciousness, saccadic eye tracking, hyperreflexia in all extremities, neck stiffness, and positive Kernig's sign. She also showed parkinsonian features, such as bradykinesia, a masked face, bilateral resting tremor in the hands, and rigidity. MRI brain revealed bilateral high-intensity lesions in the medial temporal lobes, thalamus, and substantia nigra.\n\nWhat is the overarching diagnosis?",
