      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease",
      "explanation": "Japanese encephalitis can present with Parkinsonism- this is a sign of basal ganglia involvement\n\nJapanese encephalitis\n\ncan present with Parkinsonism which is a sign of basal ganglia involvement as seen in this particular case. Japanese encephalitis is the most serious infectious disease of the central nervous system. Parkinsonism is one of the most common symptoms of these patients, being present in as many as 45% of Japanese encephalitis cases. Brain computed tomography (CT) and MRI studies often reveal lesions in the thalamus, basal ganglia, and midbrain.\n\nSporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease\n\n(CJD) is a subacute spongiform encephalopathy that presents with rapidly progressive dementia and is the most frequent among rare prion diseases. In such a patient like this with rapidly progressive dementia and focal neurological signs, CJD should be the first-line diagnosis given the triad dementia, myoclonus, and rigidity. An MRI of a CJD patient usually demonstrates hyperintense signal changes in the striatum or thalamus on T2-weighted images.\n\nDrug-induced parkinsonism\n\nis caused by medications that reduce dopamine levels in the brain and is a common cause of parkinsonism in older people. However, there is no suggestion of any such medications being taken here and would not really explain the encephalitis.\n\nMultiple system atrophy\n\n(MSA) is a devastating neurodegenerative disease characterised by the clinical triad of parkinsonism, cerebellar ataxia and autonomic failure with key features of orthostatic hypotension, urinary retention/incontinence, erectile dysfunction and anhidrosis. Given the absence of any autonomic features and ataxia, this diagnosis is incorrect.\n\nProgressive supranuclear palsy\n\ncrucially involves paresis of voluntary vertical eye movement alongside subcortical dementia with the presence of motor findings of pyramidal, extrapyramidal and cerebellar systems. Whilst this differentiates it from idiopathic Parkinson's disease it is the incorrect answer here."
