      "explanation": "Chickenpox exposure in pregnancy - first step is to check antibodies\n\nThe correct answer is 'arrange a blood test for varicella antibodies'.\n\nChickenpox infection during the first 20 weeks of pregnancy can cause foetal varicella syndrome. In the second of third trimester chickenpox can cause severe maternal disease. In late pregnancy infection (near delivery) there is a risk of neonatal varicella.\n\nIf a woman is exposed to chickenpox during pregnancy, a history should be taken of whether she has previously had chickenpox or varicella vaccination. If she has, no further action is needed.\n\nIf the woman is unsure about previous chickenpox or has never had chickenpox, a blood test for varicella zoster virus (VZV) antibodies should be performed.\n\nFor susceptible women (negative antibodies) exposed in the first 20 weeks of pregnancy, varicella zoster immunoglobulin (VZIG) is recommended.\n\nFor susceptible women (negative antibodies) exposed from 20 weeks of pregnancy, either VZIG or aciclovir from days 7 to 14 after exposure is recommended.\n\nGiving VZIG and aciclovir together is incorrect - this does not form part of the guidelines.\n\nOral aciclovir should be prescribed for pregnant women with symptomatic chickenpox if they present within 24 hours of the onset of the rash and if they are 20+ weeks' gestation. Use of aciclovir before 20 weeks can be considered but should be discussed with obstetrics."
