      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Serology",
      "explanation": "Mycoplasma? - serology is diagnostic\n\nThis patient's presentation is suggestive of infection with\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae\n\n, a cause of atypical pneumonia that often affects younger patients. It typically presents with a dry cough, mild fever, malaise and - classically - an erythema multiforme rash, described here by the presence of target lesions. Cold autoimmune haemolytic anaemia is also associated with this infection. Diagnosis is typically by\n\nMycoplasma\n\nserology\n\nand treatment involves doxycycline or a macrolide.\n\nBronchoalveolar lavage\n\nmay be used in the diagnosis of\n\nPneumocystis jirovecii\n\npneumonia. This typically causes clinical disease in immunocompromised patients, of which there is no history here.\n\nA\n\ncold agglutin test\n\nmay be positive, reflecting the cold autoimmune haemolytic anaemia (AIHA) caused by the infection. However, it is not diagnostic of infection with\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae\n\nitself, as cold AIHA has numerous causes.\n\nA\n\nfull blood count\n\nis important as part of the workup, as relatively low white blood cell counts may suggest an atypical pathogen. Haemoglobin may be low, indicating haemolytic anaemia. However, this is not specifically diagnostic of the infection.\n\nA\n\nurinary antigen test\n\nis typically used to diagnose\n\nLegionella\n\npneumonia, another atypical pneumonia. Whilst this also typically presents with a dry cough, other features such as shortness of breath and chest pain are often present. Classically, a history of exposure to contaminated water, such as unclean air conditioning units while travelling abroad, is present. Additionally, the rash in this scenario points more toward\n\nMycoplasma\n\ninfection."
