      "explanation": "Clostridium tetani\n\nThis patient, who is homeless, presents with a deep laceration on his arm that is likely to be the entry point for an infection with\n\nClostridium tetani\n\n. The toxin produced by this bacterium leads to progressive muscle spasms which evolve into widespread, painful hypertonia and may affect chest wall movement. This occurs due to the\n\ninhibition of GABA and glycine release\n\n.\n\nThe mechanism of action for the toxin produced by\n\nClostridium botulinum\n\nis the\n\ninhibition of acetylcholine release\n\n, resulting in flaccid paralysis as opposed to the spastic paralysis caused by the tetanus toxin.\n\nIt is important to note that\n\ninhibition of the GABA receptor\n\nis not associated with the mechanism of tetanus toxin. Instead, this describes how certain pharmacological agents diminish inhibitory neurotransmission. A well-recognised example is flumazenil, used as an antidote in benzodiazepine overdoses.\n\nAutoantibodies that lead to\n\ninhibition of the NMDA receptor\n\nare implicated in certain types of autoimmune encephalitis, which can be part of a paraneoplastic syndrome. However, this process plays no role in the pathophysiology of toxicity from\n\nClostridium tetani\n\n.\n\nLastly,\n\ninhibition of voltage-gated sodium channels\n\nis unrelated to the pathophysiology induced by tetanus toxin. This mechanism underlies the action of local anaesthetics such as lidocaine on nerve conduction."
