      "id": 51,
      "question": "A 22-year-old man is admitted with a complex fracture of his femur following an accident while working on a farm. The wound is contaminated with soil and there is significant devitalised tissue. He is planned for surgical debridement. As part of the childhood immunisation schedule he received five doses of tetanus vaccine at the standard times, with the last dose less than ten years ago. The surgical team ask for advice regarding tetanus prophylaxis.\n\nWhat advice should be given regarding tetanus prophylaxis?",
      "options": [
        "He should receive an immediate tetanus booster vaccine but does not require tetanus immunoglobulin",
        "He will require multiple doses of the tetanus booster vaccine",
        "No tetanus vaccine nor tetanus immunoglobulin is required",
        "Tetanus immunoglobulin should be administered",
        "Tetanus immunoglobulin is contraindicated in patients who have received a tetanus vaccination in the last 10 years"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "He will require multiple doses of the tetanus booster vaccine",
      "explanation": "If a patient has had 5 doses of tetanus vaccine, with the last dose < 10 years ago, they don't require a booster vaccine nor immunoglobulins, regardless of how severe the wound is\n\nThe correct answer is 'No tetanus vaccine nor tetanus immunoglobulin is required'.\n\nThis patient has a number of risk factors for tetanus - he has sustained a complex fracture which has been contaminated and has significant devitalised tissue.\n\nHowever, in 'Management of suspected cases and tetanus-prone wounds' Public Health England recommend that those aged 11+ who have received an adequate priming course of tetanus vaccine with the last dose within 10 years do not require further doses of tetanus vaccine nor any doses of human tetanus immunoglobulin, even if the wound is considered high risk tetanus prone.\n\nThis patient has received the normal course of childhood vaccination at the usual times, which includes five doses of tetanus vaccine. The 3-in-1 teenager booster, the last scheduled dose, is given at age 14.\n\n'He should receive a tetanus booster vaccine but does not require tetanus immunoglobulin' is therefore incorrect.\n\n'He will require multiple doses of the tetanus booster vaccine' is incorrect as he is fully immunised. Patients who are not fully immunised should offered an immediate reinforcing dose and later be offered further doses as required to complete the recommended schedule.\n\n'Tetanus immunoglobulin should be administered' is incorrect. Tetanus immunoglobulin is indicated in post-exposure prophylaxis in patients who are incompletely immunised with a tetanus-prone wound (e.g. open fractures, heavy contamination, delay to debridement).\n\n'Tetanus immunoglobulin is contraindicated in patients who have received a tetanus vaccination in the last 10 years' is incorrect, although it should be noted that tetanus immunoglobulin should be given at a site distant to the site of booster vaccination to prevent interference."
