      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Giardiasis",
      "explanation": "Amoebiasis should be considered in the presentation of dysentery after a long incubation period\n\nAmoebic dysentery\n\nis the correct answer because this patient presents with bloody diarrhoea after a long incubation period following travel to an endemic area, which strongly suggests infection by Entamoeba histolytica. The lack of fever or severe systemic upset, together with prolonged symptoms and absence of recent antibiotics, makes amoebiasis more likely than bacterial causes such as Shigella or Campylobacter, which tend to have shorter incubation periods and more acute presentations. The key learning point is that amoebiasis should be considered in returning travellers with chronic or subacute dysentery, especially when there is a significant gap between exposure and symptom onset.\n\nShigella infection\n\noften causes acute bloody diarrhoea but typically has a short incubation period (usually less than a week) and presents abruptly after exposure rather than several weeks later. It frequently causes high fever and systemic toxicity, which are not seen here.\n\nCampylobacter jejuni infection\n\nusually presents acutely within days of ingestion of contaminated food or water, often accompanied by fever and right lower quadrant pain mimicking appendicitis; it does not fit this patient’s subacute course or long incubation period.\n\nUlcerative colitis\n\ncan cause chronic bloody diarrhoea but would be less likely given the clear temporal relationship to foreign travel and lack of prior gastrointestinal history; laboratory results do not suggest significant inflammation typical for active inflammatory bowel disease.\n\nGiardiasis\n\ncommonly presents after travel but typically causes non-bloody, foul-smelling steatorrhoea rather than frank dysentery; it also tends to present earlier post-exposure."
