      "explanation": "Telbivudine is a synthetic thymidine nucleoside analogue\n\nReverse-transcriptase (RT) is an enzyme essential for viral replication. Nucleoside analogues, nucleotide analogues and non-nucleoside RT inhibitors are three classes of drugs which disrupt the activity of this enzyme.\n\nTelbivudine is an antiviral drug typically used in chronic hepatitis B infections. It is a thymidine nucleoside analogue that inhibits the replication of hepatitis B. Zidovudine and stavudine are two examples of drugs with the same mechanism.\n\nHydroxycarbamide is an example of a ribonucleotide reductase inhibitor which decreases the production of deoxyribonucleotides reducing DNA synthesis.\n\nProtease inhibitors such as ritonavir and darunavir are antivirals used against HIV and hepatitis.\n\nStatins are examples of HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors. They work by reducing LDL cholesterol. Adverse effects include myopathy and liver impairment.\n\nCalcineurin inhibitors are immunosuppressants which work through reducing t-cell differentiation. There are many adverse effects associated with these drug including gum hypertrophy, nephrotoxicity and hepatotoxicity."
