      "explanation": "Macrolides inhibit the 50S subunit of ribosomes\n\nInhibition of the 50S subunit of ribosomes\n\nis correct. This patient presents with fever, cough, shortness of breath, lung crackles, hyponatraemia and deranged liver function tests. In someone who has returned from holiday where they were exposed to a faulty air-conditioning system, this likely represents infection with\n\nlegionella pneumophila\n\n. A macrolide such as clarithromycin is an appropriate antibiotic choice for an atypical pathogen like\n\nlegionella pneumophila\n\n.\n\nActivation of a cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor and subsequent nuclear translocation\n\nis incorrect. This is the mechanism of action of prednisolone, which is a corticosteroid. Occasionally, steroids are used as an adjunctive treatment in respiratory illnesses e.g. infective exacerbation of COPD. However, they are not typically prescribed in isolation for a pneumonic illness,\n\nBinding of 30S subunit causing misreading of mRNA\n\nis incorrect. This is the mechanism of action of gentamicin. This would not be a typical choice for\n\nlegionella pneumophila\n\ninfection. Gentamicin can be used for a variety of severe gram-negative infections, typically affecting the gastrointestinal and urinary systems.\n\nInhibition of bacterial DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV\n\nis incorrect. This is the mechanism of action of ciprofloxacin. This is another choice of antibiotic that would be appropriate for a legionella infection but we are told that this patient is allergic so would not be a wise choice in this case.\n\nInhibition of bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding one of the penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs)\n\nis incorrect. This is the mechanism of action of amoxicillin. This is a reasonable choice of antibiotic in typical community-acquired pneumonia (as it has good activity against a broad range of gram-positive and gram-negative organisms). However, this patient presents with features suggestive of more atypical pneumonia (bilateral signs, hyponatraemia, deranged LFTs) and the environmental exposure suggests legionella. Amoxicillin is ineffective against this type of pneumonia and therefore is not the correct choice."
