      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "HIV viral load PCR",
      "explanation": "Combined HIV antibody/antigen tests are now the first-line test for HIV screening of asymptomatic individuals or patients with signs and symptoms of chronic infection\n\nRapid HIV antibody ELISA + p24 antigen test\n\nis the correct answer. Given this patient has never had a sexual health screening, it is important to test for HIV antibodies with an antibody ELISA to detect chronic HIV infection. However, this patient's flu-like illness could represent HIV seroconversion following HIV transmission from her partner 3-4 weeks ago. In this case, it is likely too soon for HIV antibodies to have developed. Antibody tests need to be performed three months after a possible transmission event to definitively exclude HIV. A p24 antigen test can become positive a few weeks after a possible transmission event and therefore is more useful at diagnosing acute HIV infection (AHI). Therefore, a combined antibody/antigen test is most appropriate in this scenario. Fourth-generation HIV tests are combined antibody/antigen tests and now represent the gold standard of care for all HIV testing.\n\nHIV viral load PCR\n\nis the incorrect answer. In adults, HIV viral load is only checked in patients with confirmed HIV infection, and it is not used as a screening tool. Viral load PCR is used as a screening tool in newborns and babies born to HIV-positive mothers as the transfer of the mother's antibodies may produce a false positive result with antibody testing.\n\nHIV viral load PCR + CD4 count\n\nis the incorrect answer. As above, HIV viral load and CD4 count are only checked in patients with confirmed HIV infection.\n\np24 antigen test\n\nis the incorrect answer. A p24 antigen test might aid the diagnosis of AHI as described above. However, given this patient has never had an HIV test and has had previous sexual partners, an HIV antibody test is also indicated. In any case, combined antigen/antibody testing is now the gold standard of care in all HIV testing.\n\nRapid HIV antibody ELISA\n\nis the incorrect answer. An antibody test is an appropriate test for chronic HIV. Given this patient is describing an acute seroconversion illness following a sexual encounter 3-4 weeks ago, an HIV antibody test will not test for AHI. Therefore, a combined test with a p24 antigen test would be the most appropriate answer. As above, combined antigen/antibody testing is now the gold standard of care in all HIV testing."
