      "id": 74,
      "question": "Which one of the following best describes the action of aciclovir?",
      "options": [
        "Inhibits uncoating of virus in the cell",
        "Inhibits DNA polymerase",
        "Interferes with the capping of viral mRNA",
        "Inhibits RNA polymerase",
        "Protease inhibitor"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Inhibits RNA polymerase",
      "explanation": "Aciclovir - guanosine analog, phosphorylated by thymidine kinase which in turn inhibits the viral DNA polymerase\n\nInhibits DNA polymerase\n\nis the correct answer as this accurately describes aciclovir's mechanism of action. Aciclovir is a nucleoside analogue that specifically targets viral DNA polymerase. It requires initial phosphorylation by viral thymidine kinase before being converted to aciclovir triphosphate, which then competitively inhibits viral DNA polymerase and acts as a chain terminator when incorporated into viral DNA. This selective action makes it particularly effective against herpes simplex and varicella zoster viruses while having minimal effect on human cells.\n\nInhibits uncoating of virus in the cell\n\nis incorrect. This mechanism is actually seen with drugs like amantadine, which prevents viral uncoating by blocking the M2 ion channel in influenza A viruses.\n\nInterferes with the capping of viral mRNA\n\nis incorrect. While some antiviral medications do affect viral mRNA processing, this is not the mechanism of action of aciclovir. This mechanism is more commonly associated with drugs targeting RNA viruses.\n\nInhibits RNA polymerase\n\nis incorrect. While some antivirals do work by inhibiting RNA polymerase (such as remdesivir for SARS-CoV-2), aciclovir specifically targets DNA polymerase in DNA viruses.\n\nProtease inhibitor\n\nis incorrect. Protease inhibitors represent a different class of antiviral drugs, primarily used in HIV treatment (such as ritonavir and darunavir). These drugs work by preventing viral protein processing rather than interfering with viral DNA synthesis."
