      "correctText": "Block HIV reverse transcriptase thereby preventing the HIV virion from replicating",
      "explanation": "Integrase inhibitors ('gravirs') - blocks the enzyme that inserts the viral genome into the DNA of the host cell\n\nPrevents the integration of the viral genome into the DNA of the host cell\n\nis correct. Elvitegravir, dolutegravir, and biktegravir are integrase inhibitors, which prevent the integration of the viral genome into the DNA of the host cell.\n\nBind to coreceptors necessary for HIV entrance into CD4+ cells\n\nis incorrect. Entry inhibitors bind to coreceptors necessary for HIV entrance into CD4+ cells. Drugs in this class include maraviroc and enfuvirtide.\n\nBlock HIV reverse transcriptase thereby preventing the HIV virion from replicating\n\nis incorrect. Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) bind to and block HIV reverse transcriptase thereby preventing the HIV virion from replicating. Drugs in this class include nevirapine and efavirenz.\n\nInhibit reverse transcription by being incorporated into the growing viral DNA strand and preventing further addition of nucleotides\n\nis incorrect. Nucleoside analogue reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) inhibit reverse transcription by being incorporated into the growing viral DNA strand and preventing further addition of nucleotides. Drugs in this class include zidovudine (AZT), abacavir, emtricitabine, lamivudine, and tenofovir.\n\nBinding to the catalytic site of the HIV protease, thereby preventing the cleavage of viral polyprotein precursors into mature, functional proteins\n\nis incorrect. The antiretroviral protease inhibitors act by binding to the catalytic site of the HIV protease, thereby preventing the cleavage of viral polyprotein precursors into mature, functional proteins that are necessary for viral replication. Drugs in this class include indinavir, nelfinavir, and ritonavir."
