      "id": 82,
      "question": "A 36-year-old woman is brought into the emergency department by ambulance. Her family report that she collapsed at home, having been feeling unwell for several weeks with fatigue and intermittent abdominal pain. They have also noticed some darkening of the skin of her hands.\n\nThe patient has a history of menorrhagia and takes tranexamic acid at the time of menstruation. She has no known allergies, is a non-smoker and consumes no alcohol. There is a family history of diabetes and Mycobacterium tuberculosis (TB). She grew up in Pakistan and moved to the UK in 2004. Her family report that she also seems to have lost weight, but cannot quantify how much.\n\nYou ask the patient's family to leave to allow examination. Once they have left, the patient tells you that she has recently been diagnosed with TB and has been commenced on treatment by her local TB clinic, however, she does not know what medications she is currently taking or what sort of TB she has.\n\nUrea and electrolyte results are demonstrated below:\n\nNa+\t125 mmol/L\t(135 - 145)\nK+\t6.0 mmol/L\t(3.5 - 5.0)\nBicarbonate\t13 mmol/L\t(22 - 29)\nUrea\t2.5 mmol/L\t(2.0 - 7.0)\nCreatinine\t60 µmol/L\t(55 - 120)\n\nWhat is the most likely cause of this patient's acute deterioration?",
