      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Oral antiretroviral therapy for 4 weeks",
      "explanation": "Post-exposure prophylaxis for HIV: oral antiretroviral therapy for 4 weeks\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nOral antiretroviral therapy for 4 weeks\n\n. According to UK guidelines, post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) should be started as soon as possible after a needlestick injury from an HIV-positive patient. This usually involves a combination of three oral antiretroviral drugs for four weeks. The aim is to prevent the establishment of HIV infection in the exposed healthcare worker.\n\nDiscussing the other options:\n\nHIV test of health care worker in 3 months to determine treatment\n\nis incorrect because it delays potential treatment. While it's true that antibodies to HIV often don't appear until several weeks after exposure and testing at three months can detect these antibodies, waiting this long could allow an infection to become established if PEP is not given immediately after exposure.\n\nImmediate p24 HIV test of health care worker to determine treatment\n\nis also not accurate. An immediate p24 antigen test would only detect an existing infection in the healthcare worker, not a new infection from the needlestick injury. Furthermore, this would not guide the need for immediate post-exposure prophylaxis which should be initiated regardless of this result.\n\nOral antiretroviral therapy for 3 months\n\nis incorrect because it extends beyond the recommended duration. UK guidelines suggest four weeks of PEP following a high-risk exposure such as a needlestick injury from an HIV-positive patient.\n\nLastly,\n\nIntravenous zidovudine\n\nis not recommended as first-line management for occupational exposures like needlestick injuries. Oral antiretroviral therapy has been shown to be effective and has largely replaced intravenous routes due its ease of administration and good patient compliance. Intravenous zidovudine may be used in certain situations but typically only when oral administration isn't possible or when higher drug levels are needed."
