      "correctText": "Serum cortisol",
      "explanation": "M. tuberculosis\n\nThe most appropriate answer is\n\nserum cortisol\n\n. The patient's history of tuberculosis, non-compliance with treatment, symptoms of fatigue and weight loss, and postural hypotension with hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia suggest the possibility of adrenal insufficiency. This can occur as a result of adrenal tuberculosis, which may have developed due to her discontinuation of anti-TB therapy. The most appropriate next step would be to check her serum cortisol levels to confirm or exclude this diagnosis. Other investigations listed may be relevant for different clinical scenarios but are less likely in this case based on the information provided.\n\nFaecal calprotectin\n\nlevels increase in cases of intestinal inflammation, which includes mainly inflammatory bowel disease (IBD). Patients with IBD present with fatigue and weight loss; however, they will also have a history of diarrhoea, which may be associated with blood. Since this patient has no history of diarrhoea, IBD is unlikely to be the diagnosis.\n\nLiver function tests\n\nwould not help in arriving at the cause of her symptoms. Typical clinical features of liver disease, such as jaundice and itching, are not present, and liver disease would not explain the postural hypotension and hyperkalaemia in this case.\n\nSince this patient presents with unintentional weight loss,\n\nthyroid function tests\n\ncould be considered, with hyperthyroidism as a differential. However, other typical symptoms of hyperthyroidism, such as heat intolerance, increased appetite, palpitations or tremors, are not mentioned in the scenario, making this less likely.\n\nPerforming an\n\nultrasound of the abdomen\n\ncan be done in patients presenting with abdominal pain. However, this patient has vague symptoms, and blood tests indicate electrolyte imbalances, which suggests adrenal insufficiency. CT scanning is preferred for adrenal imaging as ultrasonographic visualisation of the adrenals is difficult due to their posterior location within the abdomen. Adrenal imaging would typically be done after biochemical confirmation of adrenal insufficiency (with a serum cortisol test), not as a first-line investigation."
