      "explanation": "Atypical lymphocytes - ?glandular fever\n\nThe correct answer is\n\ninfectious mononucleosis\n\n. This patient presents with typical symptoms of infectious mononucleosis, including fever, fatigue, headache, and cervical lymphadenopathy. Additionally, the blood film shows atypical lymphocytes which can be seen in infectious mononucleosis. The disease is commonly caused by the Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) and often affects young adults.\n\nThe first incorrect option is\n\nacute lymphoblastic leukemia\n\n. While this condition may present with similar symptoms such as fever and fatigue, the blood count findings are not consistent with acute lymphoblastic leukemia. In this case, we would expect to see a higher white cell count with a predominance of blast cells.\n\nThe second incorrect option is\n\nHashimoto's thyroiditis\n\n. This autoimmune thyroid disorder typically presents with hypothyroidism symptoms such as fatigue, weight gain and cold intolerance. Although Hashimoto's thyroiditis can cause an enlarged thyroid gland that could potentially lead to cervical lymphadenopathy, the presence of pyrexia and atypical lymphocytes on blood film make this diagnosis less likely.\n\nThe third incorrect option is\n\nHIV seroconversion\n\n. HIV seroconversion can present with flu-like symptoms including fever and lymphadenopathy; however, the presence of atypical lymphocytes in the blood film is more suggestive of infectious mononucleosis. Furthermore, there is no mention of any high-risk behaviour or recent unprotected sexual encounters that might suggest an increased risk for HIV infection.\n\nLastly,\n\nsepticaemia secondary to streptococcal throat infection\n\nis also an incorrect option. Although streptococcal throat infections can cause fever and cervical lymphadenopathy, septicaemia would typically present with a much higher temperature and systemic signs of sepsis such as tachycardia or hypotension. Moreover, the presence of atypical lymphocytes on blood film is not typical for a streptococcal infection."
