      "correctText": "Prescribe intravenous ceftriaxone",
      "explanation": "Leptospirosis - give oral doxycycline or azithromycin for mild-moderate disease\n\nThis patient presents with features consistent with\n\nleptospirosis,\n\na zoonotic bacterial infection caused by Leptospira species. Transmission most commonly occurs through direct or indirect contact with the urine of infected animals—especially rats—or via contaminated water or soil, which is particularly relevant given his occupation in sewage treatment. Early symptoms include fever, headache, myalgia, and the characteristic finding of conjunctival suffusion. For mild to moderate cases of leptospirosis, oral doxycycline is considered the first-line treatment. It is effective in reducing the duration of symptoms and preventing complications. Such cases are generally managed on an outpatient basis unless there are signs suggesting severe disease.\n\nPrescribe intravenous benzylpenicillin\n\nis not appropriate in this scenario. Intravenous benzylpenicillin is reserved for severe leptospirosis (such as Weil’s disease), which may present with jaundice, renal impairment, haemorrhage, or haemodynamic instability. This patient does not exhibit any features of severe disease; therefore, intravenous antibiotics are not indicated at this time.\n\nPrescribe oral ciprofloxacin\n\nis also not recommended. Although some evidence exists for its efficacy in leptospirosis, ciprofloxacin is less effective than doxycycline or penicillin-based regimens and is not routinely used as first-line therapy according to most guidelines.\n\nPrescribe intravenous ceftriaxone\n\nwould not be appropriate here either. Ceftriaxone may be used in cases of severe leptospirosis or when oral therapy cannot be tolerated (for example, due to vomiting). However, given that this patient has a mild presentation and can tolerate oral medication, doxycycline remains the preferred option.\n\nPrescribe oral paracetamol\n\nis incorrect. While paracetamol can help alleviate symptoms such as fever and myalgia, antibiotic therapy is required even in mild cases to reduce the risk of progression and complications from leptospirosis. Prescribing paracetamol without antibiotics would risk undertreating a potentially serious infection."
