      "explanation": "Dexamethasone improves outcomes (by reducing neurological sequelae) in the treatment of bacterial meningitis\n\nThis patient has bacterial meningitis as indicated from the CSF analysis. NICE guidelines recommend treating bacterial meningitis with dexamethasone if lumbar puncture reveals either: purulent CSF, white cell count > 1000 cells/µL, raised white cell count plus protein count > 1g/L, or bacteria on gram stain. Dexamethasone should be given within 4 hours of starting antibiotics and should be avoided if the duration from starting antibiotics is beyond 12 hours.\n\nAciclovir is indicated to treat a viral meningitis. It is usually given at the same time of antibiotic treatment, prior to CSF analysis and blood culture results. However, the CSF analysis in this question shows evidence of a bacterial meningitis. A viral meningitis would typically be represented by an elevated protein level, normal glucose level and the presence of lymphocytes over polymorphonuclear leucocytes.\n\nAmoxicillin is indicated in treating meningitis in those who are more susceptible to infection with\n\nListeria monocytogenes\n\nincluding those over the age of 55 years, pregnant women and the immunocompromised.\n\nRifampicin and isoniazid are indicated for tuberculous meningitis. However, if tuberculous meningitis is suspected, treatment should include rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide and ethambutol for the first 2 months followed by rifampicin and isoniazid for a further 10 months. Tuberculous meningitis is a rare condition and is usually a complication of existing infection with\n\nMycobacterium tuberculosis\n\n.\n\nWhilst vitamin supplementation, including vitamin C and D is encouraged, it is not recommended in the treatment of bacterial meningitis."
