      "explanation": "Cryptosporidium can be diagnosed by modified Ziehl-Neelsen staining of stool to reveal red cysts\n\nCryptosporidium\n\nis a parasite that can cause gastrointestinal and respiratory illness. It is spread by farm animals and contaminated water. A modified Ziehl-Neelsen stain reveals red cysts in the stool of infected individuals, highlighting cryptosporidium oocytes.\n\nCapsule stains are useful to differentiate capsule material from the bacterial cell. These serve the purpose of identifying capsulated bacteria such as\n\nKlebsiella\n\n.\n\nCongo red stain is used to detect amyloid deposits or the cell walls of plants or fungi. It would not aid the identification of\n\nCryptosporidium\n\n.\n\nFite's Faraco is a stain used to identify mycobacterium leprae.\n\nGram stain is used to differentiate gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria, with the peptidoglycan wall of positive bacteria appearing purple, and negative bacteria appearing pink. Cryptosporidium would not show up on a gram stain."
