      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "IV benzylpenicillin",
      "explanation": "IV ceftriaxone should be used as first-line treatment of Lyme disease with disseminated or central nervous system involvement\n\nIV ceftriaxone\n\nis the correct first-line treatment for this patient with Lyme disease with central nervous system involvement. The patient presents with clinical features suggestive of meningitis (headache, neck stiffness, photophobia) and has evidence of CNS involvement with lymphocytic pleocytosis on lumbar puncture. She has a history of potential tick exposure during hiking, a characteristic erythema migrans rash, and positive Borrelia burgdorferi serology. According to UK guidelines, disseminated Lyme disease with neurological involvement (neuroborreliosis) requires intravenous antibiotic therapy, with ceftriaxone being the recommended first-line agent. The typical course is 14-21 days of IV ceftriaxone, which effectively penetrates the blood-brain barrier to treat the CNS infection. This patient's presentation represents a more severe form of Lyme disease requiring parenteral therapy rather than oral options.\n\nOral amoxicillin\n\nis an appropriate treatment for early, localised Lyme disease, particularly in patients who cannot take doxycycline, such as pregnant women or children under 8 years. However, it is not adequate for CNS involvement as seen in this case. Oral antibiotics do not achieve sufficient CNS penetration to treat neuroborreliosis effectively, making this option inappropriate for this patient with meningeal symptoms and confirmed CSF abnormalities.\n\nOral doxycycline\n\nis the first-line treatment for early, uncomplicated Lyme disease with erythema migrans but without evidence of dissemination or CNS involvement. While doxycycline does have reasonable CNS penetration compared to some other oral antibiotics, it is still considered insufficient for established neuroborreliosis. This patient's presentation with meningitis symptoms requires more aggressive parenteral therapy.\n\nIV benzylpenicillin\n\nhas activity against Borrelia burgdorferi but is not recommended as first-line therapy for neuroborreliosis in UK guidelines. Ceftriaxone has superior CNS penetration and a more convenient once-daily dosing schedule compared to benzylpenicillin, which requires multiple daily doses. While benzylpenicillin could be considered as an alternative in some cases, it is not the optimal first choice.\n\nIV aciclovir\n\nis an antiviral medication used primarily for herpes simplex virus (HSV) or varicella-zoster virus (VZV) infections. While viral meningitis can present similarly to Lyme meningitis, this patient has clear evidence pointing to Lyme disease (erythema migrans rash, positive Borrelia serology, and appropriate exposure history). Aciclovir has no activity against the bacterial spirochete Borrelia burgdorferi and would be ineffective in this case."
