      "explanation": "Pruritic rash on the buttocks or soles ?\n\nStrongyloides stercoralis\n\ninfection is correct.  This human parasitic disease caused by nematodes is endemic in Catalonia. The urticarial rash on buttock and soles is characteristic, as is blood eosinophilia. Treatment is with ivermectin.\n\nDermatitis herpetiformis is incorrect. This commonly occurs in association with coeliac disease and affects the buttocks. However, eosinophilia is not typical and the rash is vesicular rather than urticarial. Furthermore, this is less likely to occur in a patient with well-controlled coeliac disease. We are told that he follows a gluten-free diet strictly and there is no history given of disabling gastrointestinal symptoms.\n\nChurg Strauss syndrome is incorrect. This is an eosinophilic multi-system vasculitis that is closely associated with asthma and sino-nasal disease, neither of which are present in this case. Furthermore, an urticarial rash is not typical.\n\nPsoriasis is incorrect. The rash of psoriasis is typically described as red and scaly. Unless the psoriasis was very severe, eosinophilia would not be expected.\n\nHenoch-Schonlein purpura is incorrect. This is a differential diagnosis for a rash on the buttocks but it is purpuric rather than urticarial and is typically associated with either abdominal pain or arthralgia/arthritis."
