      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Hydrocortisone",
      "explanation": "Campylobacter\n\nClarithromycin is the correct answer. This patient likely has a\n\nCampylobacter\n\ninfection given his presentation of bloody diarrhoea, fevers, and consumption of unpasteurised milk.\n\nCampylobacter\n\ninfections are typically associated with the consumption of undercooked poultry or unpasteurised milk.\n\nCampylobacter\n\ninfection is often self-limiting but if severe then treatment with clarithromycin is indicated. Macrolide antibiotics (erythromycin, clarithromycin, or azithromycin) are the most effective agents for\n\nCampylobacter jejuni\n\n. This patient has a severe infection as he is experiencing fevers, passing more than 8 bowel motions a day, and has bloody diarrhoea.\n\nCiprofloxacin is an incorrect answer. Ciprofloxacin is an alternative antibiotic to clarithromycin that can be used in the treatment of severe\n\nCampylobacter\n\ninfections. However, clarithromycin is the preferred antibiotic as strains with decreased sensitivity to ciprofloxacin are isolated frequently.\n\nConservative management without antibiotic treatment or steroids is an incorrect answer. Steroids do not have a role in the management of this patient. However, he does require antimicrobial therapy as he has a severe infection.\n\nHydrocortisone is an incorrect answer. Hydrocortisone is not typically used in the treatment of\n\nCampylobacter\n\ninfections. However, it does have a role in the treatment of inflammatory bowel disease flares.\n\nMetronidazole is an incorrect answer. Macrolide antibiotics (erythromycin, clarithromycin, or azithromycin) are the most effective agents for\n\nCampylobacter jejuni\n\n. Metronidazole would therefore not be the preferred treatment option in this case."
