      "correctText": "Doxycycline",
      "explanation": "Visceral leishmaniasis: sodium stibogluconate is the recommended treatment of choice\n\nSodium stibugluconate\n\nis the correct answer. Night sweats, massive hepatosplenomegaly, pancytopenias, and cutaneous hyperpigmentation in a patient from subsaharan Africa (or India) are concerning for visceral leishmaniasis. Visceral leishmaniasis is caused by\n\nLeishmania donovani\n\n, a protozoan infection spread by sandflies. Diagnosis is confirmed by visualising amastigotes (nonmotile protozoan) on bone or splenic biopsies, though these may be seen on peripheral blood films in patients with high disease burden.\n\nSodium stibogluconate, an organic antimony derivative, is the treatment of choice for visceral leishmaniasis. Liposomal amphotericin B is an alternative drug treatment that is preferred in some parts of the world due to local resistance patterns.\n\nAciclovir\n\nis incorrect. Antiviral therapies are ineffective in visceral leishmaniasis, which is caused by the protozoan\n\nLeishmania donovani\n\n.\n\nBenznidazole\n\nand\n\nnifurtimox\n\nare incorrect. Benznidazole and nifurtimox are antiparasitic agents used to treat Chagas disease, also known as American trypanosomiasis. There is no role for benznidazole or nifurtimox in the management of visceral leishmaniasis.\n\nDoxycycline\n\nis incorrect. Conventional antibiotics are ineffective in treating visceral leishmaniasis caused by the protozoan\n\nLeishmania donovani\n\n."
