      "explanation": "Schistosomiasis is treated with praziquantel\n\nSchistosoma haematobium\n\nis one species of the parasitic trematode fluke species termed Schistosoma.\n\nSchistosoma haematobium\n\nis one of two species endemic in Africa which may induce schistosomiasis. This particular species is known to induce urinary symptoms similar to a urinary tract infection. The most common presenting features are haematuria, increased frequency, suprapubic discomfort, and burning pain on passing urine. If left untreated, the infection can cause fibrosis and will predispose infected patients to squamous cell carcinoma. It can also affect the genital organs leading to vaginal bleeding in women or haematospermia in men.\n\nThe diagnosis is made on history followed by microscopy showing viable eggs. Molecular techniques may also be employed as necessary.\n\nThe definitive treatment for infection is with praziquantel.\n\nDoxycycline is an anti-malarial as is quinine.\n\nArtemisinin has been used along with praziquantel for treatment of schistosomiasis but would not be a sole agent or first line.\n\nCiprofloxacin is used for the treatment of pyelonephritis."
