      "explanation": "Ivermectin is the drug of choice for treatment of strongyloidiasis\n\nThe correct answer is ivermectin.\n\nThe history and examination is describing a classic presentation of infection with\n\nStrongyloides stercoralis\n\n. The rash described is larva currens, which is pathognomonic for this infection. Ivermectin is used as first-line treatment.\n\nAlbendazole is incorrect - although another valid treatment option for\n\nStrongyloides\n\ninfection, it is second-line.\n\nPentamidine is incorrect - this is used for the management of American trypanosomiasis, which would present differently. Notably, the patient would have a painless chancre, intermittent fever and, later on, central nervous system involvement.\n\nBenznidazole is incorrect - this is used for a number of infections, including American trypanosomiasis (Chagas' disease), which would usually be asymptomatic initially, although a chagoma may be present. It would later lead to cardiac and gastrointestinal complications.\n\nBenzylpenicillin is incorrect - this is an antibiotic indicated for a variety of reasons, such as leptospirosis, but not for\n\nStrongyloides\n\ninfection."
