      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Bartonella bacilliformis",
      "explanation": "Mucocutaneous ulceration following travel? -\n\nLeishmania braziliensis\n\nis correct because it is the primary cause of mucocutaneous leishmaniasis, a progressive disease that begins with cutaneous leishmaniasis and later leads to destructive mucosal involvement of the nose, pharynx, and larynx. It is transmitted by sandfly bites and is endemic in Brazil and other parts of South America. The initial painless ulcer on the cheek is characteristic of cutaneous leishmaniasis, and months later, the parasite spreads via lymphatics to the nasal and oropharyngeal mucosa, leading to progressive ulceration, disfigurement, and nasal septal destruction. Diagnosis is confirmed by PCR, microscopy of biopsy samples, or culture, and treatment includes liposomal amphotericin B or miltefosine for severe cases.\n\nBartonella bacilliformis\n\nis incorrect because it causes Carrion’s disease (Oroya fever and verruga peruana), which presents with vasoproliferative skin lesions rather than mucosal ulceration. It is transmitted by sandflies in the Andes region (Peru, Ecuador, Colombia), but it does not cause progressive nasal destruction.\n\nHistoplasma capsulatum\n\nis incorrect because histoplasmosis can cause mucosal ulcers, but it typically presents as pulmonary or disseminated disease, especially in immunocompromised patients (e.g., HIV/AIDS). The lesions tend to be multiple painful oral or pharyngeal ulcers rather than progressive nasal destruction following a cutaneous ulcer.\n\nMycobacterium leprae\n\nis incorrect because leprosy primarily affects the skin and peripheral nerves, leading to sensory loss, hypopigmented patches, and thickened nerves rather than ulceration of the nasal septum. Lepromatous leprosy can cause nasal involvement, but it does not start as a skin ulcer before progressing to mucosal destruction.\n\nParacoccidioides brasiliensis\n\nis incorrect because it is a fungal infection endemic to Brazil and South America that can cause chronic mucocutaneous lesions and pulmonary involvement. However, it usually presents with multiple painful oral ulcers (‘mulberry-like’ stomatitis) rather than nasal septal destruction."
