      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Lichen planus",
      "explanation": "Measles is characterised by prodromal symptoms, Koplik spots. maculopapular rash starting behind the ears and conjunctivitis\n\nThis is a typical presentation of measles, a highly contagious airborne viral infection. Given the child's travel history and uncertain immunisation status, he is particularly susceptible.\n\nA prodromal phase (which develops 10-12 days after contracting the virus) usually lasts for 2-4 days before the characteristic maculopapular skin rash develops. This prodromal phase includes fever, malaise, cough, rhinorrhoea and conjunctivitis.\n\nKoplik's spots are pathognomonic for measles and may appear on the buccal mucosa at the end of the prodromal phase, around the same time as the rash, and disappear over the next couple of days. These consist of 2-3 mm red spots with white or blue-white centres.\n\nIn terms of the skin rash, it tends to appear on the face and behind the ears first, before spreading downwards and becoming confluent over the following 3-4 days before fading away usually within 1 week.\n\nLichen planus can present with oral mucosal lesions in the form of Wickham's striae (fine, white, lace-like lines on the surface flat-topped papules) but not other systemic features seen here.\n\nRubella can cause a similar rash which starts on the face and behind the ears, but a generally milder clinical picture with no Koplik's spots.\n\nScarlet fever typically presents as a maculopapular rash which appears on the abdomen which then spreads to the back and limbs. Sore throat is usually a prominent symptom and cough generally is not a feature. A 'strawberry tongue' may be visible.\n\nParvovirus B19 can cause a bright red rash on the cheeks ('slapped cheek syndrome') but a milder clinical picture with no Koplik's spots."
