      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Oral oseltamivir",
      "explanation": "Antivirals are of no benefit in the treatment of confirmed viral meningitis\n\nSupportive care\n\nis the correct answer for managing confirmed viral meningitis caused by enteroviruses. Viral meningitis is typically self-limiting, and specific antiviral therapy has not been shown to provide benefit in treating enteroviral infections, which are the most common cause of viral meningitis. Supportive care focuses on managing symptoms such as fever, pain, and hydration, ensuring the patient remains comfortable while the immune system resolves the infection. Antiviral agents like acyclovir are reserved for herpes simplex virus (HSV) or varicella-zoster virus (VZV) meningitis, making supportive care the standard approach for enteroviral cases.\n\nIV acyclovir\n\nis incorrect because it is effective against herpesviruses, such as HSV and VZV, but not against enteroviruses, which are the most common cause of viral meningitis in immunocompetent patients. Enteroviruses do not respond to antivirals like acyclovir, and its use would not improve patient outcomes in this case. The appropriate management for confirmed viral meningitis caused by enteroviruses is supportive care.\n\nIV ganciclovir\n\nis incorrect as it targets cytomegalovirus and related herpesviruses but has no efficacy against enteroviruses. This patient's confirmed enteroviral meningitis would not benefit from ganciclovir, making it an inappropriate treatment choice. Supportive care remains the standard management approach for enteroviral meningitis.\n\nIVIG\n\nis incorrect because it is not routinely indicated for the treatment of viral meningitis caused by enteroviruses. While IVIG may have a role in specific immunocompromised patients or certain viral infections, it is not effective for routine cases of enteroviral meningitis in immunocompetent individuals.\n\nOral oseltamivir\n\nis incorrect because it is specifically designed to treat influenza viruses and has no efficacy against enteroviruses, which are responsible for this patient’s viral meningitis. Enteroviral infections require no antiviral therapy, and supportive care remains the standard treatment approach."
