      "correctText": "Herpes simplex virus",
      "explanation": "Mumps meningitis is associated with a low CSF glucose\n\nThis man has\n\nmumps\n\nmeningitis. His CSF analysis shows a predominantly viral picture: clear appearance, normal opening pressure, raised white cells with lymphocyte predominance, and mildly elevated protein. The clue to mumps is the low CSF glucose. Low CSF glucose is classically associated with bacterial meningitis, with most cases of viral meningitis being associated with normal CSF glucose. The exception to this general rule (and therefore an exam favourite) is mumps (and sometimes herpes simplex virus) where CSF glucose is low. Meningitis is a common manifestation of mumps and can occur in approximately 10% of cases, although mumps meningitis is rare overall in populations with good vaccination coverage. A clue in this question outside of the CSF analysis is the fact that the patient is from a community with low vaccination rates.\n\nHuman enteroviruses including\n\ncoxsackievirus\n\nare the most common cause of viral meningitis. As described above, a similar CSF appearance would be expected to the one given but the CSF glucose would not be expected to be low.\n\nHerpes simplex virus\n\ncould account for the CSF findings given, including the low CSF glucose. However, the clues of the patient being from a community with low vaccine rates and of the neck swelling (parotiditis) make mumps meningitis more likely.\n\nIn bacterial causes of meningitis, including\n\nNeisseria meningitidis\n\nand\n\nStreptococcus pneumoniae\n\n, you would expect a low CSF glucose as in this case, but you would also expect cloudy CSF appearance, raised opening pressure, raised white cell cells with polymorph predominance, and a more significantly elevated protein."
