      "correctText": "Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome",
      "explanation": "Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome can occur in HIV positive patients when starting anti-retrovirals; this is an immune phenomenon that results in the clinical worsening of a pre-exisiting opportunistic infection\n\nImmune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) is the most likely diagnosis in this patient as he was recently started on antiretrovirals (didanosine) for HIV. IRIS is an exaggerated immune response to a pre-existing opportunistic infection in HIV patients who commence antiretroviral therapy. Fever and worsening respiratory symptoms are typical features of IRIS due to\n\nMycobacterium tuberculosis\n\n, a common underlying infection in IRIS. The patient may have been exposed to it while backpacking. The most common clinical manifestations of TB-IRIS are fever, lymphadenopathy, and worsening respiratory symptoms. New pulmonary infiltrates, mediastinal lymphadenopathy, and pleural effusions are also common.\n\nSide effects of antiretrovirals can be significant. However, none of them fit the patient's presentation better than IRIS does. Common side effects of didanosine specifically include gastrointestinal upset, headaches, rashes and changes in body fat distribution.\n\nHodgkin's lymphoma is a relatively common and important diagnosis in HIV patients. It is a good differential in this case. However, it would more commonly present in a subacute manner with B-symptoms, i.e. fever, weight loss and lymphadenopathy. The lymphadenopathy in lymphoma is usually painless instead of reactive lymphadenopathy (such as in this case), which is tender. The timing is more in keeping with IRIS because the patient has just been started on antiretrovirals.\n\nA pulmonary embolism would not cause lymphadenopathy and rarely causes radiographic changes (usually wedge-shaped infarcts).\n\nSeroconversion illness is unlikely to cause such a dramatic acute presentation. It also tends to occur much earlier than the patient being diagnosed with HIV and started on antiretrovirals, and therefore the timing does not fit."
