      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Toxoplasmosis",
      "explanation": "Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy is caused by the JC virus or BK virus\n\nJC virus\n\naffects significantly immunosuppressed patients. It causes demyelination and significant white matter changes and the clinical syndrome of PML which can include cognitive and behavioural abnormalities, sensory and motor deficits, ataxia, aphasia and cortical visual changes. Seizures are a common feature. The MRI findings of cerebral white matter change without gadolinium enhancement are typical of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML). CT brain would likely be performed first in this scenario but findings may be non specific and an MRI would be subsequently be performed. PML is caused by the JC virus.\n\nCryptococcus neoformans\n\nis a cause of cryptococcal meningitis. These patients may present with seizures due to increased intracranial pressure or meningeal irritation with other symptoms such as headache, fever, and altered mental status. MRI findings will be different to PML. MRI may show meningeal enhancement or a cryptococcal mass. There is commonly obstructive hydrocephalus due to the degree of inflammation. Extensive white matter changes would not be expected.\n\nCytomegalovirus\n\n(CMV) can cause CMV encephalitis in the context of profound immunosuppression due to HIV. It could present with seizures but normally presents with confusion and cranial nerve palsies. CMV typically affects both white and grey matter, but it often presents with ventriculoencephalitis and therefore MRI shows ventricular involvement or periventricular enhancement.\n\nEpstein–Barr virus\n\ncan lead to primary CNS lymphoma which could present as a seizure. However, an MRI would likely show a solid, heterogeneous mass lesion with ring enhancement.\n\nToxoplasmosis\n\ncan present with confusion, headache and seizures. MRI findings typically describe cystic, ring-enhancing lesions in the basal ganglia and corticomedullary junctions."
