      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Spiramycin",
      "explanation": "Immunocompetent patients with toxoplasmosis don't usually require treatment\n\nThis patient has presented with toxoplasmosis which is endemic to most parts of the world including Brazil. The vast majority of patients remain asymptomatic and are unaware they have contracted the parasitic infection or only suffer mild symptoms such as in this case with fever, malaise and sometimes lymphadenopathy. In immunocompetent patients\n\nno treatment\n\nis usually required and symptoms will resolve spontaneously. Treatment should be started in patients with immunocompromise (e.g. HIV) or if detected during pregnancy to prevent congenital toxoplasmosis.\n\nAtovaquone\n\nis used following acute treatment to manage the latent aspect of toxoplasmosis in immunocompromised patients. The\n\nT gondii\n\ncysts formed in the infection are immune to acute treatment drugs as these antibiotics do not reach the bradyzoites in sufficient concentrations, therefore, a quinone antimicrobial such as atovaquone must be used following active treatment. As this patient is not immunocompromised, treatment is not required.\n\nPyrimethamine/sulphadiazine\n\nis one of the first-line regimens used in the active/acute treatment of toxoplasmosis in immunocompromised patients. It must be followed up by latent treatment to ensure clearance of the parasitic infection.\n\nSpiramycin\n\nis the drug of choice if toxoplasmosis is diagnosed in the first or second trimester of pregnancy, as it has been shown not to have teratogen properties. Treatment of toxoplasmosis is important in pregnancy to prevent potential congenital toxoplasmosis complications.\n\nTrimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole\n\nis used as a prophylaxis agent to prevent toxoplasmosis in patients with known immunocompromise who are at risk of infection. However, it is not effective in the active treatment of the condition."
