      "explanation": "Chikungunya can present with debilitating joint pain\n\nChikungunya\n\ncharacteristically presents with severe bilateral polyarthralgia and fever. Macular rash may also be present. Symptoms can start 2-10 days after exposure and should be suspected in patients who have travelled to the Indian subcontinent. The degree of joint pain in this scenario makes Chikungunya the most likely diagnosis.\n\nLeptospirosis\n\nshould be considered in the returning traveller presenting with non-specific self-limiting flu-like illness (headache, cough, pruritic rash and myalgia), or as a more severe icteric syndrome. Myalgia and joint pains are common but not specifically severe joint pain as a presenting complaint.\n\nMalaria\n\nis also spread through mosquito bites and normally presents with flu-like features such as fevers, headache; or in severe cases with reduced consciousness, anaemia, renal failure and eventually multi-organ failure, but severe joint pain is not a typical feature.\n\nRickettsia\n\nis caused by a tick, flea or lice bite and presents as fever, headache and petechial or maculopapular rash. Severe bilateral joint pain is not a recognised feature.\n\nZika\n\nis also spread by mosquitoes. Patients will normally be asymptomatic or have a flu-like illness with conjunctivitis or maculopapular rash. Joint pain is a feature of Zika, but debilitating joint pain is not normally seen. This specific feature makes Chikungunya more likely based on the information given, but Zika would remain a differential."
