      "correctText": "Hypoadrenalism",
      "explanation": "M. tuberculosis\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nhypoadrenalism\n\n. The case described is suggestive of tuberculosis (TB), caused by\n\nMycobacterium tuberculosis\n\n. This infection is endemic in areas such as India and causes a subacute illness characterised by constitutional symptoms such as fever, weight loss and night sweats. Pulmonary involvement is most common and presents with cough which may include haemoptysis. Radiographic changes are most commonly apical and may include cavities. Tuberculous adrenalitis is a well-described complication, usually resulting from the haematogenous spread of bacilli from other sites. Untreated, it causes progressive destruction of the adrenal glands leading to hypoadrenalism.\n\nEncephalitis\n\nis incorrect. CNS involvement of TB is well-described but more commonly presents with meningitis. The history is often more indolent than with bacterial meningitis, usually presenting over weeks. Cranial nerve palsies, particularly the optic and abducens nerves, are common.\n\nErythema migrans\n\nis incorrect. TB can cause a variety of skin manifestations such as erythema nodosum. Erythema migrans is much more suggestive of Lyme disease.\n\nPancreatitis\n\nis incorrect. Pancreatic involvement of TB is very unusual, affecting < 1% of patients. It usually presents with features mimicking advanced pancreatic malignancy such as abdominal pain and jaundice.\n\nPeripheral neuropathy\n\nis incorrect. While this can be seen in patients being treated for TB, it far more commonly results from medication used to treat the illness such as isoniazid and linezolid rather than the disease itself. Pyridoxine is commonly co-administered to reduce the risk of this toxicity."
