      "explanation": "Chlamydia\n\nDoxycycline\n\nis correct. The patient has genital\n\nChlamydia trachomatis\n\ninfection. This is diagnosed by urine nucleic acid amplification. The first-line treatment is doxycycline.\n\nAzithromycin\n\nis incorrect. Azithromycin is no longer the first-line treatment for\n\nChlamydia trachomatis\n\ninfection. This is because of concerns regarding azithromycin resistance in the organism\n\nMycoplasma genitalium\n\n, which often co-infects those with chlamydia.\n\nBenzylpenicillin\n\nis incorrect. In a sexually transmitted infection context, this is used to treat\n\nTreponema pallidum\n\ninfection, or syphilis. Syphilis has a variable presentation, but primary infection is usually characterised by a painless syphilitic chancre. This is not seen in this question, and the nucleic acid amplification test has identified\n\nChlamydia trachomatis\n\n.\n\nCeftriaxone\n\nis incorrect. In a sexually transmitted infection context, a single stat dose of ceftriaxone is used in the treatment of\n\nNeisseria gonorrhoeae\n\ninfection. This patient has\n\nChlamydia trachomatis\n\ninfection, however, so this is incorrect.\n\nMetronidazole\n\nis incorrect. In a sexually transmitted infection context, metronidazole is the treatment for\n\nTrichomonas vaginalis\n\ninfection. This would present with similar symptoms in a male patient, however, the nucleic acid amplification test in this patient has identified\n\nChlamydia trachomatis\n\nas the causative agent."
