      "explanation": "Amantadine - inhibits uncoating (M2 protein) of virus in cell. Also releases dopamine from nerve endings\n\nAmantadine is no longer routinely used for treatment of influenza - but its mechanism of action still comes up in exams. The correct answer is the inhibition of viral uncoating in the cell, by targeting the M2 protein channel.\n\nInhibition of mRNA synthesis describes the action of interferon-alpha, which is used as an antiviral agent in some cases, such as the treatment of chronic hepatitis B and C.\n\nInhibition of neuraminidase is the mechanism of oseltamivir, used against influenza.\n\nInhibition of viral DNA polymerase describes the action of aciclovir and ganciclovir, which are used against a number of different viral infections, such as varicella-zoster virus and herpes simplex virus.\n\nInterfering with the capping of viral mRNA is the mechanism of ribavirin, which is used against chronic hepatitis C."
