      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Tuberculoid leprosy",
      "explanation": "Tuberculoid leprosy is suggested by a limited number of skin lesions, hypaesthesia and hair loss\n\nTuberculoid leprosy is the correct answer. Tuberculoid leprosy is suggested by a limited number of skin lesions, hypaesthesia, and hair loss. Mycobacterium leprae are scanty in tuberculoid leprosy lesions. It is a relatively benign form of leprosy and one of the least contagious.\n\nBullous pemphigoid is an incorrect answer. Bullous pemphigoid is an autoimmune disorder that usually occurs in those over 60 years of age. It is characterised by the formation of fluid-filled blisters between the epidermal and dermal skin layers.\n\nCutaneous leishmaniasis is an incorrect answer. Leishmaniasis is a parasitic disease that is found in parts of the tropics, subtropics, and southern Europe. Leishmania parasites use phlebotomine sand flies as disease vectors. Cutaneous leishmaniasis is characterised by an initial small red papule that enlarges in size and develops a central ulceration that can exude pus or dry with a crusted scab. Although cutaneous leishmaniasis can also be associated with solitary lesions and decreased sensation, the lack of ulceration described in this clinical scenario makes cutaneous leishmaniasis less likely than tuberculoid leprosy.\n\nLyme disease is an incorrect answer. Lyme disease is associated with an erythema migrans rash, which has an appearance like a 'bull's eye'.\n\nNecrotising fasciitis is an incorrect answer. Necrotising fasciitis typically occurs in patients who are immunocompromised and spreads rapidly. Patients are often systemically very unwell. Necrotising fasciitis is characterised by bullae formation, decreased skin sensation, presence of gas in tissues, and skin transitioning from a red to black colour due to thrombosis of blood vessels. It is frequently polymicrobial but methicillin-resistant\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\n(MRSA) is involved in up to a third of cases."
