      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Splenomegaly",
      "explanation": "One of the sequelae of diphtheria is cardiovascular disease; notably heart block\n\nHeart block\n\nis correct. The patient presents with a sore throat, bulky cervical lymph nodes, and a greyish-white membrane on the tonsils. In someone from the travelling community (where vaccination uptake is lower), this raises the suspicion of diphtheria. The diagnosis is confirmed with throat swab microscopy demonstrating a Chinese letter pattern which is typical of\n\nCorynebacterium diphtheria\n\n. Patients are typically treated with a combination of diphtheria anti-toxin and antibiotics. Heart block is a possible sequela of this condition.\n\nGlomerulonephritis\n\nis incorrect. This is a potential complication of streptococcus pharyngitis. However, the clinical history raises the possibility of another cause of pharyngitis and the throat swab microscopy confirms this is not a group A strep infection.\n\nSplenomegaly\n\nis incorrect. This is a possible complication of Epstein-Barr virus infection. This may also cause a sore throat and cervical lymph nodes. However, there is typically diagnosed with serology rather than throat swab microscopy.\n\nSubacute sclerosing panencephalitis\n\nis incorrect. This is a rare neurological complication of measles infection. This patient may not be vaccinated against measles but measles presents with a rash, which is absent here. It can occur years after an initial infection but we are told there is no history of major childhood illness.\n\nTrismus\n\nis incorrect. Trismus or 'lockjaw' is a complication of tetanus infection. This does not tend to present with a sore throat and lymphadenopathy. There is often a history of an exposure-prone wound, which is not mentioned here. This is therefore not likely."
