      "correctText": "Tetanus vaccine booster and tetanus immunoglobulin administration",
      "explanation": "If a patient has had 5 doses of tetanus vaccine, with the last dose < 10 years ago, they don't require a booster vaccine nor immunoglobulins, regardless of how severe the wound is\n\nNo tetanus vaccine booster, nor immunoglobulins, is the correct answer. The patient is up-to-date with childhood vaccinations, meaning that she will have had 5 doses of the tetanus vaccine. The last dose would have been at age 14, so she has had a dose within 10 years. Accordingly, regardless of wound severity, she already has adequate protection and does not require a booster or immunoglobulin.\n\nTetanus vaccine booster with antibiotics is incorrect. Although antibiotic prophylaxis can be considered for protection against other bacteria, the patient already has adequate tetanus protection and will not require vaccination.\n\nTetanus vaccine booster with tetanus immunoglobulin administration is incorrect. As explained above, the patient has adequate protection already. Although this is a high-risk wound, immunoglobulin would only be indicated if the patient had an incomplete/unknown vaccine history or it had been >10 years since the last vaccine.\n\nTetanus vaccine booster only is incorrect. The patient already has protection from her previous vaccinations.\n\nTetanus vaccine booster only if the patient becomes unwell is incorrect. This patient's risk of tetanus is incredibly low given that she is fully immunised. If she were to become unwell, other causative bacteria infecting the wound would be the likely cause, hence a tetanus vaccine would serve no purpose."
