      "correctText": "Dengue fever",
      "explanation": "Retro-orbital headache, fever, facial flushing, rash, thrombocytopenia in returning traveller → ?dengue\n\nOf the options listed above, the most likely diagnosis is dengue fever. The history of a retro-orbital headache, fever and facial flushing all point towards this diagnosis, as well as the blotchy rash. Thrombocytopaenia also provides weight to support the diagnosis of dengue fever. Clinically, all of these conditions can be hard to distinguish, often presenting non-specifically. Dengue is endemic throughout South America, with high rates in Brazil, as well as other tropical regions including South East Asia and parts of Africa.\n\nInfection with chikungunya virus classically presents with severe arthralgia/arthritis of distal joints, symmetrically. It may also present with hyperpigmentation and desquamation of skin, which are not mentioned here.\n\nTyphoid classically includes a stepwise progression of fever, constipation, rose spots on the abdomen (although rarely seen) and relative bradycardia, none of which are mentioned here. Splenomegaly may also be present; in this scenario, organomegaly is not found on examination.\n\nYellow fever may present with conjunctival injection, relative bradycardia (whereas heart rate is normal above) and jaundice. No jaundice is observed in this scenario.\n\nInfection with zika virus may present with conjunctivitis or conjunctival hyperaemia. Again, the history of retro-orbital headache, facial flushing and thrombocytopaenia all give weight to dengue fever. In practice, differentiation between these infections should be made with further investigation via PCR."
