      "correctText": "Mumps virus",
      "explanation": "The most common causes of viral meningitis in adults are enteroviruses\n\nThe most common causes of viral meningitis in adults are enteroviruses. Therefore, the correct answer from the above options is the echoviruses, which is are a family of enteroviruses.\n\nHerpes simplex viruses are also known to cause viral meningitis, but again not as commonly as enteroviruses. Herpes simplex virus 1 is more associated with encephalitis rather than meningitis.\n\nHerpes simplex virus 2 is associated more with meningitis than encephalitis, but not as commonly as enteroviruses.\n\nEpstein-Barr virus, known as the cause of infectious mononucleosis, can also rarely cause meningitis. This is not seen anywhere near as frequently as enteroviruses.\n\nMeasles was once a relatively common cause of meningitis in teenagers and young adults, prior to the measles-mumps-rubella (MMR) vaccination.\n\nSimilarly, mumps was once a much more common cause of meningitis in teens and young adults. At one time, prior to the vaccine, it was the most common cause of meningitis in the UK. There has been a recent upsurge in cases due to hesitancy around the MMR vaccine following the 'MMR-autism' myth."
