      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Streptococcus pneumoniae",
      "explanation": "Pneumonia caused by\n\nStreptococcus pneumoniae\n\nis the correct answer. This patient presents with classic features of pneumococcal pneumonia, which is the most common cause of community-acquired pneumonia (CAP), accounting for approximately 80% of cases. The clinical presentation strongly supports this diagnosis: acute onset of symptoms (2-day history), high fever (39.2°C), productive cough, and pleuritic chest pain. Importantly, the presence of herpes labialis (cold sores) is a characteristic feature associated with pneumococcal pneumonia. This association, while not fully understood, is thought to be related to the reactivation of latent herpes simplex virus during the acute inflammatory response to pneumococcal infection. The chest X-ray finding of lobar consolidation is also typical of pneumococcal pneumonia, which classically affects a single lobe (in this case, the right lower lobe).\n\nHaemophilus influenzae\n\nis an incorrect answer. While H. influenzae is a cause of CAP, it is less common than S. pneumoniae. H. influenzae pneumonia typically affects patients with underlying lung disease such as COPD or bronchiectasis. It does not have a specific association with herpes labialis. The presentation would be similar but would more likely show a patchy bronchopneumonic pattern on chest X-ray rather than the lobar consolidation described in this case.\n\nKlebsiella pneumoniae\n\nis an incorrect answer. K. pneumoniae pneumonia classically affects alcoholics, diabetics, and those with poor dental hygiene. It typically presents with 'currant jelly' sputum and has a predilection for the upper lobes. There is no association between K. pneumoniae pneumonia and herpes labialis. The patient in this scenario has no mentioned history of alcoholism or other risk factors for K. pneumoniae infection.\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae\n\nis an incorrect answer. M. pneumoniae causes atypical pneumonia with a more gradual onset and less severe symptoms than seen in this case. It typically affects younger patients and is often associated with extra-pulmonary manifestations such as bullous myringitis, erythema multiforme, or hemolytic anemia. The chest X-ray in Mycoplasma pneumonia often shows an interstitial pattern rather than lobar consolidation, and there is no association with herpes labialis.\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\nis an incorrect answer. S. aureus pneumonia is often seen as a complication following influenza infection or in intravenous drug users. It typically presents with a rapidly progressive, severe pneumonia that may be complicated by lung abscess, empyema, or pneumatoceles. There is no specific association between S. aureus pneumonia and herpes labialis. The patient in this scenario does not have risk factors for staphylococcal pneumonia."
