      "explanation": "ELISA is the first-line investigation for suspected Lyme disease in patients with no history of erythema migrans\n\nLyme disease is an infection caused by a group of spiral-shaped bacteria\n\nBorrelia burgdorferi\n\nwhich are transmitted to humans following a bite from an infected tick.\n\nSymptomatic infection is characterised by an expanding target-like rash (erythema migrans) which tends to occur 1-4 weeks after a tick bite. However, up to 1/3 of people with Lyme disease do not develop a rash. Other symptoms include as fever, malaise, headache and/or joint pains.\n\nA clinical diagnosis of Lyme disease can be made in patients with erythema migrans without any further investigations.\n\nFor people with a suspicious history but without erythema migrans (as in this patient), an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) test - which detects IgM and IgG antibodies to\n\nBorrelia burgdorferi\n\n- should be performed.\n\nIf ELISA is positive or equivocal, an immunoblot test should then be performed. If this is positive, then the diagnosis of Lyme disease can confidently be made.\n\nLyme disease is not cultured directly from blood, nor is it necessary to perform a lumbar puncture for cerebrospinal fluid analysis in a patient who does not have neurological complications.\n\nIt would not be appropriate to commence antibiotics for Lyme disease without diagnostic investigations in this patient who does not have an erythema migrans rash."
