      "correctText": "Schistosomiasis",
      "explanation": "Pruritic rash on the buttocks or soles ?\n\nStrongyloidiasis is most commonly caused by the nematode\n\nStrongyloides stercoralis\n\n. It is found worldwide and is endemic in many tropical and subtropical countries. It spreads via the faecal-oral route and commonly spreads by direct skin contact with contaminated soil. As the larvae enter the body, a papular rash can be seen on the feet, as seen above. Larva currens is also pathognomonic for strongyloidiasis as a linear, extremely pruritic rash that extends several centimetres over an hour as the larva moves within the dermis. Most patients are typically asymptomatic but can present with vague abdominal symptoms of abdominal pain and diarrhoea. Eosinophilia on a blood test may be the only sign of infection in some patients and stool cultures will show larvae within the stool. Treatment is typical with ivermectin or albendazole.\n\nAmoebiasis is a potential cause for diarrhoea in the returning traveller. It tends to cause non-bloody diarrhoea but does not tend to cause the significant eosinophilia seen here.\n\nSchistosomiasis is caused by the flatworm\n\nSchistosoma\n\n. It can cause diarrhoea and cause eosinophilia as well. The patient here also has freshwater exposure which increases the risk of this. Haematuria and fevers are typically seen in this condition and are not seen with this patient.\n\nLoiasis is a condition caused by the nematode\n\nLoa loa\n\nand the condition is initially asymptomatic. If symptoms occur, it can present with areas of angioedema. It does not usually cause diarrhoea that is seen in this case.\n\nOnchocerciasis is caused by the parasitic worm\n\nOnchocerca volvulus\n\nand is more commonly associated with significantly itchy skin and visual changes. It does not usually cause diarrhoea. Inflammation following the infection results in blindness via damage to the cornea and optic nerve."
