      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome",
      "explanation": "Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome can occur in HIV positive patients when starting anti-retrovirals; this is an immune phenomenon that results in the clinical worsening of a pre-exisiting opportunistic infection\n\nThis question is examining your knowledge of one of the important side effects of commencement of HAART - immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS). This condition features a paradoxical worsening of a pre-existing chronic (or latent) condition. In this case, the patient had previous tuberculosis (TB) infection, which is hinted at by his chest x-ray findings and previous work in an endemic area. The presentation with haemoptysis, fevers, and shortness of breath, as well as the new opacities on the repeat radiograph, suggest a worsening or reactivation of the latent TB.\n\nThe pathophysiology of IRIS is not fully understood but is thought to feature excessive immune activation as CD4 counts rise.\n\nThough HIV is considered to induce a hypercoagulable state, which can predispose to pulmonary embolisms and thus present with haemoptysis and SOB, you would expect pleuritic chest pain and would not expect a fever.\n\nGranulomatosis with polyangiitis is a systemic vasculitis that can also present with haemoptysis but would classically also cause kidney issues (particularly crescentic glomerulonephritis) and sinusitis.\n\nAn empyema is a pocket of pus cells within a cavity or potential space. It alone would not cause shortness of breath or haemoptysis. While a parapneumonic empyema (that is, an empyema associated with pneumonia) could cause fever and shortness of breath, they would not typically cause haemoptysis, and the recent commencement of HAART makes IRIS more likely.\n\nWhile this could be a newly acquired case of TB, it is highly unlikely given his evidence of previous TB infection on a radiograph, and the duration between his initial presentation and his subsequent development of symptoms."
