      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Non-compliance with antiretroviral therapy",
      "explanation": "The viral load determines the risk of HIV transmission following a needle stick injury\n\nIt is extremely rare to develop HIV from a needle stick injury. The CDC estimates the risk of transmission is less than 1%. However, high viral load will increase the risk of transmission if such an injury is sustained.\n\nShallow injuries confer a lower risk of HIV transmission, as does the use of small diameter needles.\n\nThere are some patients on antiretroviral treatment who still have a detectable viral load. Therefore the compliance of the patient with treatment is not the factor that will determine HIV transmission, but the viral load itself.\n\nWhilst an HIV patient may have another blood-borne virus (usually due to lifestyle behaviours that put them at risk such as intravenous drug abuse), it does not affect that rate of HIV transmission.\n\nLastly, a new diagnosis of HIV does not affect the risk of transmission itself. People get diagnosed at different times depending on how they present, therefore, they may have a low or high viral load."
