      "explanation": "Staphylococcus aureus\n\nStaphylococcus aureus infection\n\nis correct. The patient presents with fever and shortness of breath and has chest x-ray evidence of pneumonia. The superimposed areas of lucency suggest cavitation. There are a number of causes of cavitating pneumonia but pneumonia caused by\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\noften follows a recent viral illness and therefore this is the likely cause in this presentation.\n\nKlebsiella pneumoniae infection\n\nis incorrect. This can cause cavitating pneumonia but tends to occur in alcoholics. We are told she does not drink alcohol so this is less likely.\n\nMycobacterium tuberculosis infection\n\nis incorrect. This can cause cavitating pneumonia. However, TB tends to present in a more indolent fashion and a three-day history is probably unlikely. Additionally, there is no travel history, which counts against the diagnosis.\n\nStreptococcus pneumoniae infection\n\nis incorrect. This is probably the most common cause of cavitating pneumonia (although this complication is rare, it is the most common cause of pneumonia generally). However, the history of a recent viral illness makes\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\ninfection more likely.\n\nANCA vasculitis\n\nis incorrect. This is another cause of cavitating lung lesions. However, this tends to present more insidiously and a three-day history with a dramatic presentation would be unusual. Additionally, there is usually evidence of multisystem disease affecting the sinuses or kidneys which is not mentioned in this case."
