      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Salmonella",
      "explanation": "E. coli\n\nThis patient has presented with a history very much in keeping with traveller's diarrhoea. With over 24 hours of diarrhoea and the associated symptoms of cramping and nausea. The commonest cause of traveller's diarrhoea is\n\nEscherichia coli\n\n(\n\nE. coli\n\n) due to its widespread contamination of food and water sources in many travel destinations, the bacterium's adaptability to different environments, and its ease of transmission. Simple, conservative management is all that is required in the vast majority of cases.\n\nCampylobacter\n\nis a frequent cause of traveller's diarrhoea, typically contracted through the consumption of undercooked poultry or contaminated water sources. However, compared to E. coli, it is less commonly encountered due to its lower prevalence in contaminated food and water, fewer pathogenic strains, and reduced adaptability and ease of transmission.\n\nGiardiasis\n\ncan cause traveller's diarrhoea, however, it is more commonly associated with chronic diarrhoea lasting a few weeks. It typically requires the ingestion of cysts from contaminated water, which is less common in many travel destinations compared to the more varied and frequent exposure to\n\nE. coli\n\nthrough food and water consumption. This makes E. coli a more prevalent culprit in travellers' diarrhoea cases.\n\nSalmonella\n\nis another significant cause of traveller's diarrhoea but it typically results from consuming undercooked poultry or eggs therefore limiting its exposure in travel settings. It is therefore less common when compared to\n\nE. coli\n\n.\n\nShigella\n\nis less frequently implicated in travellers' diarrhoea due to its relatively limited prevalence in many travel destinations. It primarily spreads through poor hygiene and person-to-person contact rather than via food and water, also reducing the likelihood of exposure during travel when compared to other pathogens."
