      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Clostridium perfringens",
      "explanation": "Clostridium\n\nClostridium perfringens\n\nis the correct answer because it is a Gram-positive rod commonly associated with severe intra-abdominal infections, especially following bowel surgery or trauma. It can cause gas gangrene (clostridial myonecrosis) and sepsis in this setting due to its ability to produce potent exotoxins. The presence of Gram-positive rods in blood cultures in a post-surgical patient with abdominal signs strongly suggests Clostridium species as the causative agent.\n\nListeria monocytogenes\n\nis also a Gram-positive rod but primarily causes meningitis or sepsis in neonates, pregnant women, elderly or immunocompromised patients—usually without an association with recent bowel surgery or abdominal infection. It would be less likely to present acutely after abdominal surgery with these features.\n\nCorynebacterium diphtheriae\n\nis another Gram-positive rod but classically causes pharyngitis with pseudomembrane formation and systemic complications from toxin production. It does not typically present as a cause of acute postoperative intra-abdominal sepsis.\n\nBacillus anthracis\n\n, while a Gram-positive rod, causes anthrax which presents as cutaneous ulcers, pulmonary symptoms (woolsorter's disease), or gastrointestinal disease usually linked to specific exposures; it would be highly unusual outside of such contexts and not typical postoperatively.\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\nis a Gram-positive coccus (not rod) and therefore does not fit the microbiology findings described here. While it can certainly cause postoperative infections and sepsis, the question specifically describes Gram-positive rods on blood culture."
