      "id": 217,
      "question": "A 12-year-old boy from a rural area came into the hospital with a right axillary mass showing progressive growth for 3-weeks. He denied pain but had reduced right upper limb abduction. Initial treatment with cephalexin was not effective. He also complained of other enlarged lymph nodes in the neck and inguinal regions which resolved spontaneously and intermittent joint pain.\n\nHe appeared well with normal vital signs. Chest X-ray and abdominal ultrasound were normal. A subsequent lymph node biopsy with Warthin-Starry staining confirmed the diagnosis.\n\nWhat causative organism was most likely found upon staining?",
