      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Epstein-Barr virus",
      "explanation": "Cat scratch disease - caused by\n\nBartonella henselae\n\ncauses cat scratch disease. Patients typically develop low-grade fever, fatigue and malaise and regional ipsilateral lymphadenopathy. Diagnosis can be confirmed by microbiology through serology or culture, or histopathology through visualisation of bacilli in Warthin-Starry staining.\n\nBrucellae\n\nis a bacteria that can give you Brucellosis through the consumption of unpasteurised milk and cheese. Symptoms predominantly involve the gastrointestinal tract causing vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhoea and the skeletal system producing arthritis, spondylitis and osteomyelitis. Most of these features are absent here making the diagnosis incorrect.\n\nMycobacterium tuberculosis\n\ntypically stains with Ziehl-Neelsen stain showing acid-fast bacilli making the diagnosis incorrect here.\n\nToxoplasma gondii\n\ntypically causes infection in immunosuppressed individuals such as those with HIV/AIDS and can be rapidly diagnosed by making impression smears of lesions on glass slides. After drying for 10 to 30 minutes, the smears are fixed in methyl alcohol and stained with Giemsa stain. Therefore this is the incorrect diagnosis here.\n\nEpstein-Barr virus\n\ntypically causes swollen lymph nodes in the neck as well as an enlarged liver and spleen. Diagnosis is clinical and supported by antibody blood testing. Lymph node biopsy and subsequent staining are not routinely performed, making this diagnosis very unlikely."
