      "id": 218,
      "question": "A 32-year-old woman is 28 weeks pregnant with her first child. She works as a primary school teacher and reports that one of her pupils developed chickenpox yesterday. She is unsure if she has ever had chickenpox herself, but recalls her brother having it when she was a teenager. She is otherwise well with no significant medical history. Her BMI is 24 kg/m². She is concerned about the risk to her unborn baby.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate next step in management?",
      "options": [
        "Advise that no action is needed as she is likely immune",
        "Commence oral aciclovir immediately",
        "Perform urgent VZV IgG antibody testing",
        "Reassure and advise to return if she develops a rash",
        "Refer to fetal medicine for detailed ultrasound assessment"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Reassure and advise to return if she develops a rash",
      "explanation": "At-risk patients who are exposed to varicella should have an urgent blood test for VZV IgG antibodies\n\nPerform urgent VZV IgG antibody testing\n\nis the correct management approach in this scenario. This pregnant woman has had a significant exposure to varicella-zoster virus (VZV) through contact with a pupil who has developed chickenpox. While she mentions her brother had chickenpox when she was a teenager, she is unsure if she has ever had the infection herself, making her immune status uncertain. Chickenpox can cause severe complications in pregnancy, including pneumonitis in the mother and, depending on the timing of infection, congenital varicella syndrome or neonatal varicella in the baby. The appropriate first step is to urgently determine her immunity status through VZV IgG antibody testing, which can typically be performed within 24 hours. This will guide further management decisions. If she is found to be non-immune (VZV IgG <100 mIU/ml in immunocompetent individuals), post-exposure prophylaxis with oral antivirals would be indicated, starting from day 7 after exposure.\n\nAdvise that no action is needed as she is likely immune\n\nis incorrect. Although the patient mentions her brother had chickenpox when she was a teenager, suggesting possible exposure, she states she is unsure if she has ever had the infection herself. Without documented history of chickenpox or previous positive serology, her immune status cannot be assumed. Given the potential serious consequences of varicella infection during pregnancy, it would be inappropriate to assume immunity without confirmation.\n\nCommence oral aciclovir immediately\n\nis not appropriate at this stage. While oral antivirals are now the first-line post-exposure prophylaxis for non-immune pregnant women exposed to chickenpox, they should only be given after confirming the patient is non-immune through VZV IgG testing. Additionally, antivirals for post-exposure prophylaxis are typically started from day 7 to day 14 after exposure, not immediately. Starting treatment without confirming susceptibility would expose the patient to unnecessary medication.\n\nReassure and advise to return if she develops a rash\n\nis an inadequate approach. By the time a rash develops, the infection is established and the risk of complications is higher. The goal of post-exposure management in pregnancy is to prevent infection in susceptible women, not to treat established disease. Waiting for symptoms would miss the opportunity for effective prophylaxis.\n\nRefer to fetal medicine for detailed ultrasound assessment\n\nis premature at this stage. Ultrasound assessment would be indicated if the patient was confirmed to have contracted chickenpox during pregnancy, particularly during the first 20 weeks when there is risk of congenital varicella syndrome. At this point, the patient has only been exposed, and her immune status is unknown. The priority is to determine susceptibility and provide appropriate prophylaxis if needed."
