      "correctText": "Plasmodium falciparum",
      "explanation": "P. knowlesi\n\nOf the non-falciparum malaria species,\n\nPlasmodium knowlesi\n\nhas the shortest replication time. Its full replication cycle lasts just 24 hours, explaining the daily temperature spikes described in this scenario. No other malaria species could be attributed to a cyclical fever with a 24-hour interval.\n\nPlasmodium knowlesi\n\nis mostly found in Southeast Asia.\n\nPlasmodium falciparum\n\nreplication takes approximately 48 hours. This patient's daily cyclical fever is more in keeping with\n\nPlasmodium knowlesi\n\ninfection.\n\nPlasmodium malariae\n\nhas a replication cycle of approximately 72 hours. A cyclical fever of this duration, rather than 24-hourly, would therefore be expected.\n\nPlasmodium ovale\n\nhas a replication cycle of 48 hours, with a corresponding cyclical fever of this duration. This is double the length of the replication cycle of\n\nPlasmodium knowlesi\n\n.\n\nPlasmodium vivax\n\nalso has a replication cycle of 48 hours. As such, it is unlikely to be the cause of the 24-hour cyclical fever here."
