      "correctText": "Candida albicans",
      "explanation": "Bacterial vaginosis - overgrowth of predominately\n\nBacterial vaginosis (BV) is a common cause of change to vaginal discharge in all age groups with particular characteristic factors, including thin consistency, minimal external signs, and a foul, fishy aroma. It is more common in those who are sexually active (although it is not a sexually transmitted infection), have an intrauterine device in situ, and those who use vaginal cleansing agents due to the eradication of healthy vaginal commensal organisms.\n\nTo confirm the diagnosis swabs are taken, and pH measurements can be helpful in informing empirical treatment with metronidazole in the event of a pH >4.5. The swabs undergo microscopy for clue cells and culture for organisms.\n\nGardnerella vaginalis\n\nand\n\nUreaplasma urealyticum\n\nare both causes of BV, however,\n\nGardnerella\n\nis much more commonly implicated. It is worth noting that\n\nUreaplasma urealyticum\n\nis also an important cause to consider in non-gonococcal urethritis.\n\nCandida albicans\n\ncause vulvovaginal thrush and may be associated with a white discharge but there would often be associated itching and a thicker discharge rather than the watery, fishy discharge described.\n\nLactobacilli\n\nare normal vaginal commensals and as such would be at low levels during episodes of BV.\n\nTrichomonas vaginalis\n\nis responsible for trichomoniasis, a protozoal genital infection associated with frothy green or yellow discharge, it has symptoms of pain more commonly than BV but can be asymptomatic and as such is an important differential diagnosis.\n\nIn this case, there is the possibility of sexually transmitted infections as a cause but none are available as answers."
