      "correctText": "The number of amastigotes seen at biopsy",
      "explanation": "Cutaneous leishmaniasis acquired in South or Central America merits treatment due to the risk of mucocutaneous leishmaniasis whereas disease acquired in Africa or India can be managed more conservatively\n\nThis gentleman has acquired cutaneous leishmaniasis whilst living in Brazil.  The disease is contracted from the bite of an infected sand fly and normally presents with a painless papule which then ulcerates.  The duration of the lesion depends on the species of leishmania that has been acquired and the host immune response but is typically 6-12 months.\n\nMost lesions will self resolve, however where there is a risk of mucocutaneous leishmaniasis as a sequelae then systemic therapy is warranted.  This is only of concern with cutaneous leishmaniasis acquired in South America and of the Viannia subgenus (principally L. braziliensis and L. guyanensis).  None of the species of Leishmania found outside of the Americas can cause the mucocutaneous form.   Otherwise systemic therapy is considered if there are multiple (>4) lesions; if there are large lesions (>5cm); if the lesions involve the face, hands or genital; if the host is immunocompromised; or if local treatment has failed.\n\nThe location of this man's lesion is fairly innocuous; lesions on the face, hands and genitals are typically considered for systemic therapy.\n\nThe number of amastigotes seen is normally more reflective of the quality of the sample rather than the severity of disease.\n\nMost lesions are painless and this does not have a bearing on prognosis.\n\nThe lesion described is relatively small; lesions greater than 5cm would be considered for systemic therapy."
