      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Topical aciclovir",
      "explanation": "Genital wart treatment\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nTopical podophyllum\n\n. The patient's presentation of fleshy, protuberant vulval lesions that are slightly pigmented is suggestive of genital warts, which are most commonly caused by the human papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11. Topical podophyllum, a cytotoxic agent that inhibits cell division and DNA synthesis, is a suitable initial treatment for external genital warts according to UK guidelines.\n\nOral aciclovir\n\nis incorrect as it is an antiviral medication used in the management of herpes simplex virus (HSV), not HPV. While both viruses can cause genital lesions, the description of the lesions in this question aligns more with HPV than HSV.\n\nTopical salicylic acid\n\nis also an incorrect choice. While it can be used in treating warts, it's typically used for common or plantar warts on hands and feet rather than genital warts due to its potential to cause irritation or harm to the healthy skin surrounding the wart.\n\nThe option\n\nTopical aciclovir\n\n, similar to oral aciclovir, would be appropriate if the patient was presenting with herpes simplex virus (HSV) infections but not for HPV-induced genital warts.\n\nLastly,\n\nElectrocautery\n\ncould be considered as a treatment option for refractory or recurrent cases where topical treatments have failed. However, it would not be considered a first-line treatment due to its invasive nature and potential side effects such as pain and scarring."
