      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Schistosomiasis",
      "explanation": "Acute schistosomal infection can present with a syndrome known as Katayama Fever\n\nThis is Katayama fever, which is a systemic reaction that develops after acute schistosomal infection. This may occur weeks to months after initial infection. It can be very difficult to recognise but is often associated with a severely itchy and bumpy rash. Splenomegaly also points to schistosomiasis.\n\nAfrican trypanosomiasis (African sleeping sickness) is caused by protozoa of the species\n\nTrypanosoma brucei\n\n. It is initially characterised by headaches, joint pains, and itching, along with a chancre at the site of tsetse fly bite. If untreated can lead to neurological symptoms and altered sleeping pattern.\n\nCutaneous leishmaniasis is the most common form of leishmaniasis and causes skin lesions, mainly ulcers, on exposed parts of the body. It is a parasitic infection spread by the bite of sandflies.\n\nDengue fever presents with headache, severe myalgia and high fever. It has a characteristic rash that is measles-like in appearance.\n\nMalaria is unlikely in this case as he has taken prophylaxis. Malaria is spread by infected mosquitoes, and generally presents with non-specific symptoms such as headache, myalgia and fever."
