      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Benzylpenicillin",
      "explanation": "Latent syphilis (i.e asymptomatic syphilis) can occur at an early and a late stage and requires the same antibiotic treatment\n\nBenzylpenicillin\n\nis correct. This patient has syphilis. He has evidence of the primary infection (the painless penile ulcer), secondary infection (the genital/mouth ulcers and the condylomata lata), and then subsequent latent syphilis which recurred in early latency - within 2 years of the primary infection. The first-line treatment for latent syphilis is the same as other stages of syphilis - benzylpenicillin.\n\nAzithromycin\n\nis incorrect. In a sexually transmitted infection context, azithromycin is used in\n\nChlamydia trachomatis\n\nor\n\nNeisseria gonorrhoeae\n\ninfection. The history and examination findings in this question are not compatible with either of these, however. The painless penile chancre, followed by secondary infection symptoms, is highly suggestive of syphilis, for which the first-line treatment is benzylpenicillin.\n\nDoxycycline\n\nis incorrect. In a genitourinary medicine context, doxycycline is used first-line in patients with\n\nChlamydia trachomatis\n\ninfection. The painless penile chancre and secondary infection symptoms described in this question are not characteristic of\n\nChlamydia trachomatis\n\ninfection, however. Doxycycline can be used second-line in syphilis, but only if benzylpenicillin is contraindicated.\n\nCeftriaxone\n\nis incorrect. In a sexually transmitted infection context, a single stat dose of ceftriaxone is used in the treatment of\n\nNeisseria gonorrhoeae\n\ninfection. The history and examination findings in this case are highly suggestive of syphilis however, the first-line treatment for which is benzylpenicillin.\n\nPhenoxymethylpenicillin\n\nis incorrect. Phenoxymethylpenicillin has a similar antibacterial profile to benzylpenicillin but is not used in more severe infections as absorption and plasma concentration are unpredictable. Phenoxymethylpenicillin is commonly used in cases of streptococcal tonsillitis, respiratory tract infections in childhood, and in ongoing therapy after injections of benzylpenicillin have been given (but not in syphilis, where the first line treatment is a single dose of benzylpenicillin)."
