      "explanation": "Pregnant women with a UTI: nitrofurantoin is first-line unless the woman is close to term\n\nThe symptoms reported by the patient and the urine dipstick results are in keeping with a diagnosis of a lower UTI.\n\nNitrofurantoin is advocated as 1st line treatment for UTI in pregnancy.\n\nTrimethoprim is an appropriate antibiotic treatment for UTI, however, it is teratogenic and therefore contraindicated in pregnancy.\n\nCiprofloxacin is also another common treatment for UTI but the BNF suggests it should be avoided in pregnancy and only used if other alternative antibiotics cannot be used.\n\nAmoxicillin is a second-line agent for pregnancy-associated UTI and therefore is not the most appropriate option from the antibiotics listed.\n\nWhilst a urine sample should be sent for culture and sensitivities, it would be inappropriate to wait for the results (likely to take a few days) before prescribing antibiotics as the patient is symptomatic and the infection may progress/worsen in the interval."
