      "id": 251,
      "question": "An 18-year-old gap year student is brought to the emergency department by his mother who is concerned he looks 'yellowish'.\n\nHe returned from Bolivia 2 days ago and started feeling unwell on his flight. He tells you he feels he has the flu and vomited 'dark stuff' twice on the aircraft and twice since returning home.\n\nHe briefly felt unwell during the last week of his trip but had attributed this to a 'bad hangover' and had been feeling better. When questioned in private he states he smoked marijuana during his travels but this was his only drug use.\n\nHe has an episode of coffee-ground vomiting. He has rigors and is pyrexial as well as visibly jaundiced. He has tenderness in the right upper quadrant of his abdomen. Other systems examination is unremarkable.\n\nBlood tests are as follows:\n\nNa+\t134 mmol/L\t(135 - 145)\nK+\t3.6 mmol/L\t(3.5 - 5.0)\nBicarbonate\t25 mmol/L\t(22 - 29)\nUrea\t17 mmol/L\t(2.0 - 7.0)\nCreatinine\t220 µmol/L\t(55 - 120)\n\n\nBilirubin\t79 µmol/L\t(3 - 17)\nALP\t319 u/L\t(30 - 100)\nALT\t325 u/L\t(3 - 40)\nγGT\t140 u/L\t(8 - 60)\nAlbumin\t39 g/L\t(35 - 50)\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
