      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "IV ceftriaxone and amoxicillin",
      "explanation": "Antivirals are of no benefit in the treatment of confirmed viral meningitis\n\nSupportive management\n\nis correct. This patient is presenting with signs and symptoms suggestive of meningitis. Analysis of the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) shows a raised white cell count with a lymphocyte predominance and a CSF glucose > 66% of the serum glucose, suggesting viral meningitis. Viral meningitis can be managed supportively, with resolution expected within 1-2 weeks. Differentiating between viral and bacterial meningitis on clinical grounds alone is difficult, hence patients would usually be started on empirical IV antibiotics until the lumbar puncture has excluded bacterial meningitis, as in this case.\n\nStart IV aciclovir\n\nis incorrect. In the context of central nervous system (CNS) infections, aciclovir is used in suspected or confirmed viral encephalitis. Patients with viral meningitis should have normal cerebral function, differentiating this case from viral encephalitis. A patient with viral encephalitis would be expected to have altered behaviour, motor or speech changes, or a change in the Glasgow coma scale (GCS) (which are not featured in this patient's case).\n\nStart IV ceftriaxone\n\nis incorrect. In the context of CNS infections, this would be used in patients aged under 60 with bacterial meningitis. The patient, in this case, does not have bacterial meningitis, however. CSF analysis in a patient with bacterial meningitis would show a raised white cell count with a neutrophil predominance, as well as a serum glucose of less than 60% of the serum glucose. A cloudy or turbid specimen would also be suggestive.\n\nStart IV ceftriaxone and IV amoxicillin\n\nis incorrect. In the context of CNS infections, this combination would be used in bacterial meningitis in over 60s. The amoxicillin is given to cover for\n\nlisteria monocytogenes\n\n. The patient, in this case, has a\n\nviral\n\nmeningitis; however, this is demonstrated by the raised white cell count with a lymphocytic predominance.\n\nStart IV ganciclovir\n\nis incorrect. In the context of CNS infections, IV ganciclovir would be used in confirmed viral encephalitis (specifically cytomegalovirus infection). Encephalitis would be characterised by a change in the patient's cerebral function, seen as altered behaviour, motor or speech changes, or a change in GCS."
