      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Doxycycline",
      "explanation": "Ivermectin is the drug of choice for treatment of strongyloidiasis\n\nThis patient has presented with the human parasitic infection strongyloidiasis caused by the helminth\n\nStrongyloides stercoralis\n\n. Following the larvae penetrating the skin, commonly via patients walking barefoot, the parasite makes its way to the intestines where it lays eggs.  Unlike in other soil-transmitted helminth infections, these eggs hatch into larvae still within the intestines. Although most of these larvae will be excreted in stool, a portion of the larvae can mature and penetrate the intestinal wall or skin around the anus, immediately re-infecting the patient, a process known as auto-infection. These larvae can travel around the host body including reaching the lungs causing respiratory symptoms and eosinophils accumulation known as Loffler's syndrome. This auto-infection means without treatment a patient may remain infective with\n\nStrongyloides stercoralis\n\nfor life. Treatment of\n\nStrongyloides stercoralis\n\nis with the antiparasitic\n\nivermectin\n\nwhich interferes with the nervous and muscular functions of helminths. As ivermectin only kills the adult form of the worm and not the larvae, repeat dosing may be required to properly eradicate the infection.\n\nAlthough\n\nalbendazole\n\ncan be used as an alternative to ivermectin in the treatment of\n\nStrongyloides stercoralis\n\n, head-to-head trials have shown ivermectin to be more effective in curing people of the condition with a comparable side effect profile. As such ivermectin is the suggested first-line treatment\n\nDoxycycline\n\nis a broad-spectrum antibiotic within the tetracycline class primarily used for the management of bacterial infections but it can also be used to irradicate certain parasites. It is effective in the tropical bacteria diseases cholera and typhus and well as being used as a prophylactic for malaria. Doxycycline can also reduce the transmission of certain parasitic filarial nematode infections such as those responsible for onchocerciasis and elephantiasis. The antibiotic acts by killing the symbiotic bacteria\n\nWolbachia\n\nfound with these filariasis reproductive tracts making the nematodes sterile. Doxycycline has however been shown to be ineffective in the treatment of\n\nStrongyloides stercoralis\n\n.\n\nNifurtimox\n\nis mainly used in the treatment of the trypanosomiases infections of Chagas disease and sleeping sickness. The drug is known to interact with the parasite's DNA causing significant breakdown. Again there is no evidence to support its use in\n\nStrongyloides stercoralis\n\n.\n\nPraziquantel\n\ncan be used in the management of several parasitic infections however it is mainly used for the treatment of trematodes including schistosomiasis. Praziquantel is not commonly used in the treatment of strongyloidiasis."
