      "explanation": "Tetanus toxin (tetanospasmin) blocks the release of the inhibitory neurotransmitters GABA and glycine resulting in continuous motor neuron activity\n\nThis patient's presentation is highly suspicious of tetanus infection. This is caused by the bacterium\n\nClostridium tetani\n\nand is fatal if not treated. It is highly prevalent in soil and manure. Symptoms include trismus, muscle rigidity and spasms. The symptoms occur due to the bacterium's exotoxin, which\n\ninhibits the release of GABA\n\nand glycine neurotransmitters, leading to continuous muscle contraction.\n\nAn\n\nincrease in cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) release\n\nrefers to the action of the exotoxin secreted by\n\nBordetella pertussis\n\n. The symptoms here, however, are more suggestive of tetanus infection.\n\nInhibition of acetylcholine (ACh) release\n\nrefers to the action of botulinum toxin, released by\n\nClostridium botulinum\n\n. Rather than causing trismus and muscle spasms, this would lead to flaccid paralysis.\n\nInhibition of dopamine (D2) receptors\n\nrefers to neuroleptic malignant syndrome, which may present somewhat similarly to tetanus with muscular rigidity. However, other symptoms would include autonomic dysfunction and hyperthermia, and a history of antipsychotic use would be present.\n\nInhibition of eukaryotic elongation factor 2 (EEF2)\n\nrefers to the action of the exotoxin released by\n\nPseudomonas aeruginosa\n\n. However, the symptoms here fit with tetanus infection."
