      "explanation": "Doxycycline is typically used in the management of typhus\n\nThe correct answer is\n\ndoxycycline\n\n. This patient is presenting with fever, headaches, and a rash which started on his trunk and spread over his limbs with sparing of his palms and soles. There is also possible exposure to fleas given the history of vermin. This raises the suspicion of typhus. There is an increasing incidence of typhus across South and Southeast Asia, particularly scrub typhus. This intracellular pathogen must always be considered in the differential diagnosis of travellers presenting with fever within three weeks of their return. Antibiotics should be initiated as soon as possible, usually before serological confirmation of the diagnosis.\n\nBenzathine penicillin\n\nis incorrect. Penicillin would be an appropriate antibiotic to begin if syphilis was considered. This is unlikely given there was no history of a chancre and he was not sexually active whilst away. Primary, secondary and early latent syphilis recommended treatment is benzathine penicillin 2.4 mega units (intramuscular, single dose), with procaine penicillin as an alternative.\n\nCiprofloxacin\n\nis incorrect. Ciprofloxacin, along with azithromycin, are the first-line treatments for dysentery (presenting with acute watery diarrhoea after travel).\n\nCo-amoxiclav\n\nis incorrect. Co-amoxiclav is used for infections due to beta-lactamase-producing strains (where amoxicillin alone is not appropriate), including respiratory tract infections, bone and joint infections, genito-urinary and abdominal infections.\n\nErythromycin\n\nis incorrect. Erythromycin is active against gram-positive bacteria, gram-negative bacteria, and several other organisms. It is used in a vast array of conditions including community-acquired pneumonia, skin conditions and as an alternative antibiotic in penicillin-allergic patients."
