      "correctText": "Inhibition of cell wall formation",
      "explanation": "Rifampicin inhibits RNA synthesis\n\nRifampicin is an RNA synthesis inhibitor. It specifically inhibits bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase. In doing so, elongation is physically blocked and so, without adequate RNA, bacteria cannot translate proteins.\n\nExamples of antibiotics that inhibit DNA synthesis include metronidazole, sulphonamides and quinolones - such as ciprofloxacin.\n\nBeta-lactam antibiotics inhibit cell wall formation, including cephalosporins and penicillins. The latter group blocks cross-linking of peptidoglycan cell walls.\n\nTrimethoprim is an example of a folate synthesis inhibitor. This antibiotic inhibits dihydrofolate reductase. In doing so, dihydrofolic acid is not reduced to tetrahydrofolic acid, which is an essential precursor in the thymidine synthesis pathway.\n\nA number of antibiotics work by inhibiting protein synthesis. These include aminoglycosides (such as gentamicin), macrolides (such as erythromycin), tetracyclines and fusidic acid."
