      "correctText": "Intramuscular ceftriaxone",
      "explanation": "Trichomonas vaginalis\n\nThis patient's presentation is suspicious of infection with\n\nTrichomonas vaginalis\n\n. Her presentation with yellow-green malodorous discharge and vulval itching fits with this diagnosis. The description of an 'erythematous, punctate, and papilliform' - also known as 'strawberry' - cervix also supports the diagnosis. First-line treatment involves the use of\n\noral metronidazole\n\n.\n\nIntramuscular ceftriaxone\n\nis used to treat gonorrhoea infection. This would instead present with pelvic pain and mucopurulent exudate from the cervix, although is often entirely asymptomatic. The scenario here points more toward trichomoniasis.\n\nOral doxycycline\n\nmay be used for the treatment of chlamydial infection. As with gonorrhoea, many are asymptomatic, although the presence of abdominal pain, cervicitis and intermenstrual bleeding may help to differentiate from the yellow-green discharge and strawberry cervix seen with trichomoniasis.\n\nOral fluconazole\n\nis typically used to treat vulvovaginal candidiasis. Rather than yellow-green discharge, it is often described as thick, white and like 'cottage cheese'.\n\nTopical clotrimazole\n\nis an alternative treatment option for vulvovaginal candidiasis, prior to treatment with oral fluconazole."
