      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Inhibition of peptidoglycan synthesis",
      "explanation": "Macrolides inhibit the 50S subunit of ribosomes\n\nClarithromycin is one of several macrolide antibiotics. These act by binding to the\n\n50S subunit of ribosomes\n\n. Ultimately, this prevents the synthesis of proteins and stops bacteria from being able to multiply.\n\nThe\n\ninhibition of the 30S subunit of ribosomes\n\nis the mechanism of several classes of antibiotics, including tetracyclines such as doxycycline. It is also the mechanism of action of aminoglycosides, such as gentamicin.\n\nQuinolones act by\n\ninhibiting DNA topoisomerases\n\nand DNA gyrases. In doing so, they disrupt the ability of bacterial cells to replicate.\n\nTrimethoprim acts by\n\ninhibiting the enzyme dihydrofolate reductase\n\n. This prevents the synthesis of folic acid.\n\nInhibition of peptidoglycan synthesis\n\nrefers to the mechanism of action of beta-lactams. This class includes penicillin derivatives, cephalosporins and carbapenems."
