      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Reduce the risk of chloroquine-related retinopathy",
      "explanation": "Primaquine is used in non-falciparum malaria to destroy liver hypnozoites and prevent relapse\n\nThe correct answer is to\n\ndestroy liver hypnozoites and prevent relapse\n\n. Primaquine is an 8-aminoquinoline antimalarial drug used primarily for the radical cure of\n\nPlasmodium vivax\n\nand\n\nPlasmodium ovale\n\ninfections. These species can form dormant liver stages known as hypnozoites that can reactivate weeks to months after the initial infection, causing a relapse. Primaquine targets these hypnozoites, thereby preventing subsequent relapses.\n\nReduce the risk of chloroquine-related retinopathy\n\nis incorrect because primaquine does not have any protective effects on retinal toxicity associated with chloroquine use. Chloroquine-related retinopathy is a rare side effect of long-term chloroquine use, typically seen in patients receiving the drug for rheumatologic diseases rather than malaria.\n\nReduce the incidence of chloroquine resistance\n\nis also incorrect. While resistance to chloroquine has become increasingly common, particularly in\n\nPlasmodium falciparum\n\n, primaquine does not play a role in reducing or reversing this resistance. The primary function of primaquine remains its ability to target hypnozoites and prevent relapses.\n\nThe option to\n\ncover Plasmodium ovale in case of co-infection\n\nis partially correct but not the main benefit of primaquine in this scenario. As mentioned earlier, primaquine can be used for both\n\nP. vivax\n\nand\n\nP. ovale\n\n, so it would indeed provide coverage if there was a co-infection with both species. However, since the patient has been diagnosed specifically with\n\nP. vivax\n\n, the main benefit remains its ability to destroy liver hypnozoites and prevent relapse.\n\nLastly,\n\nprevent immature trophozoites forming gamatocytes\n\nis incorrect. While primaquine does have some activity against the sexual stages of the malaria parasite (gametocytes), its primary role in treating\n\nP. vivax\n\ninfections is to target the dormant liver stages (hypnozoites) and prevent relapses. Other antimalarial drugs such as artemisinin-based combination therapies are more effective at targeting gametocytes and reducing transmission."
