      "id": 278,
      "question": "A 30-year-old woman presents with a white, malodorous vaginal discharge. There is no associated itch or dyspareunia. A diagnosis of bacterial vaginosis is suspected.\n\nOvergrowth of which one of the following organisms is most likely to cause this presentation?",
      "options": [
        "Lactobacilli",
        "Trichomonas",
        "Candida",
        "Mycoplasma hominis",
        "Gardnerella"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Mycoplasma hominis",
      "explanation": "Bacterial vaginosis - overgrowth of predominately\n\nThe correct answer in this case is\n\nGardnerella\n\n. Bacterial vaginosis (BV) is a condition characterized by an imbalance of the normal vaginal flora, with a reduction in the number of\n\nLactobacilli\n\nand overgrowth of anaerobic bacteria, primarily\n\nGardnerella vaginalis\n\n. This overgrowth results in the production of a white, malodorous discharge which is typically non-irritating and not associated with itch or dyspareunia, as described in the clinical scenario.\n\nNow let's discuss why the other options are incorrect:\n\nLactobacilli\n\nare actually part of the normal vaginal flora and produce lactic acid, which helps to maintain an acidic environment that inhibits the growth of pathogenic organisms. A decrease in\n\nLactobacilli\n\nlevels can predispose to bacterial vaginosis, but they are not responsible for causing the symptoms.\n\nTrichomonas\n\n, specifically\n\nTrichomonas vaginalis\n\n, is a protozoan parasite that causes trichomoniasis. While it may present with a malodorous discharge, it typically also causes itching, burning sensation during urination, and dyspareunia - features that were not mentioned in this scenario.\n\nCandida\n\n, usually\n\nCandida albicans\n\n, is responsible for causing vulvovaginal candidiasis (VVC), also known as thrush. VVC usually presents with thick, white 'cottage cheese-like' discharge along with significant itching and irritation. The absence of these symptoms makes this option less likely.\n\nFinally,\n\nMycoplasma hominis\n\ncan be found as part of the normal vaginal flora, but it can also be associated with some cases of bacterial vaginosis. However,\n\nMycoplasma hominis\n\nis not considered the primary causative organism in BV and is less likely to cause the presentation described compared to\n\nGardnerella vaginalis\n\n."
