      "id": 279,
      "question": "A 28-year-old man presents to the emergency department with a 2-week history of intermittent fever, headache, and malaise. He returned from a 3-month volunteering trip to rural Brazil 10 days ago. On examination, he has a temperature of 38.5°C and a small, painless erythematous nodule on his left forearm which he noticed shortly after his return. Blood tests show mild lymphocytosis and raised inflammatory markers. A blood film reveals trypomastigotes. ECG and echocardiogram are normal.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate treatment?",
      "options": [
        "Benznidazole",
        "Melarsoprol",
        "Pentamidine",
        "Praziquantel",
        "Suramin"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Praziquantel",
      "explanation": "Benznidazole is used in the acute phase of Chagas' disease to manage the illness\n\nBenznidazole\n\nis the correct treatment for this patient who has acute Chagas' disease (American trypanosomiasis). The diagnosis is supported by several key features: recent travel to an endemic area (Brazil), the presence of a chagoma (the painless erythematous nodule on the forearm), systemic symptoms including fever and malaise, and most definitively, the presence of trypomastigotes on blood film. Chagas' disease is caused by Trypanosoma cruzi and is transmitted by triatomine bugs (also known as 'kissing bugs'). The acute phase typically occurs 1-2 weeks after infection and may last for up to 3 months. While many patients are asymptomatic during this phase, some present with non-specific symptoms as seen in this case. Treatment during the acute phase is crucial to prevent progression to chronic disease, which can lead to serious cardiac and gastrointestinal complications. Benznidazole is one of the two antiparasitic medications (along with nifurtimox) recommended for treatment of acute Chagas' disease, with higher cure rates when administered during this early phase.\n\nMelarsoprol\n\nis an arsenical compound used for the treatment of late-stage African trypanosomiasis (sleeping sickness) with central nervous system involvement. It is specifically indicated when there is evidence of CNS disease caused by Trypanosoma brucei gambiense or Trypanosoma brucei rhodesiense. This patient has American trypanosomiasis (Chagas' disease) caused by Trypanosoma cruzi, not African trypanosomiasis. Additionally, melarsoprol is highly toxic and associated with severe adverse effects including encephalopathy, which occurs in approximately 5-10% of patients. Therefore, it would not be appropriate for this patient.\n\nPentamidine\n\nis an antiprotozoal medication used primarily for early-stage African trypanosomiasis (without CNS involvement), Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, and leishmaniasis. It is not effective against Trypanosoma cruzi, the causative organism of Chagas' disease. Pentamidine would be appropriate for a patient with early-stage African trypanosomiasis, which typically presents with a trypanosomal chancre, intermittent fever, and lymphadenopathy (particularly posterior cervical lymphadenopathy, known as Winterbottom's sign). This patient's travel history and clinical presentation are not consistent with African trypanosomiasis.\n\nPraziquantel\n\nis an antihelminthic medication used primarily for the treatment of schistosomiasis and other trematode (fluke) and cestode (tapeworm) infections. It has no activity against protozoan parasites such as Trypanosoma cruzi. The clinical presentation described in this case is not consistent with helminthic infections, which typically present with more specific symptoms depending on the parasite and affected organ systems. Praziquantel would therefore be ineffective for treating this patient's condition.\n\nSuramin\n\nis another medication used for the treatment of early-stage African trypanosomiasis, specifically for Trypanosoma brucei rhodesiense infection. Like pentamidine, it does not cross the blood-brain barrier effectively and is therefore not used when there is CNS involvement. Suramin has no activity against Trypanosoma cruzi and would be ineffective for treating Chagas' disease. Additionally, suramin can cause significant adverse effects including nephrotoxicity, peripheral neuropathy, and hypersensitivity reactions. Given this patient's diagnosis of Chagas' disease, suramin would be an inappropriate choice."
