      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Confirmation of latent syphilis with PCR",
      "explanation": "Latent syphilis (i.e asymptomatic syphilis) can occur at an early and a late stage and requires the same antibiotic treatment\n\nThe correct approach is to\n\ncommence antibiotics for latent syphilis\n\n. Latent syphilis is diagnosed when an asymptomatic patient has serologic evidence of infection. According to the guidelines by the British Association for Sexual Health and HIV (BASHH), early latent syphilis is treated with benzathine penicillin G 2.4 million units IM as a single dose, and late latent syphilis is treated with benzathine penicillin G 2.4 million units IM weekly for three weeks (three doses total).\n\nThe\n\nuse of condoms\n\nprevents patients from acquiring syphilis. Since the patient in this scenario is already diagnosed with latent syphilis, this is not the right approach. Instead, the patient should be advised to avoid all sexual contact until he completes treatment for syphilis.\n\nConfirmation of latent syphilis with PCR\n\nis incorrect. This patient does not require further testing as he has tested positive for two treponemal tests, which is regarded as confirmatory of syphilis infection. BASHH guidelines recommend treponemal EIA as the screening test of choice for syphilis. A positive screening test requires confirmation with a different serological test.\n\nNo further treatment for latent syphilis\n\nis wrong, as patients with latent syphilis are infected and can develop active syphilis later on. All patients with latent syphilis need treatment to prevent disease progression.\n\nReassuring that progression is unlikely and to monitor for symptoms\n\nis wrong. Latent syphilis can progress to active syphilis if left untreated."
