      "explanation": "Japanese encephalitis can present with Parkinsonism- this is a sign of basal ganglia involvement\n\nBasal ganglia\n\nis the correct option. This patient is showing the classic tremor and rigidity of Parkinsonism. The unifying diagnosis, in this case, is one of Japanese encephalitis, which can present with Parkinsonism when the basal ganglia are involved. Clues pointing to this in the stem include fever, mosquito bites (Japanese encephalitis is transmitted via a mosquito vector), and recent travel to an area of high endemicity.\n\nCerebellum\n\nis incorrect as the patient does not show typical cerebellar symptoms such as dysdiadochokinesia, ataxia, nystagmus, intention tremor, slurred speech or hypotonia. There is also less of a link between Japanese encephalitis and cerebellar involvement.\n\nFrontal lobe\n\nis incorrect as inflammation in this area most commonly causes personality changes, which are not alluded to in the question stem.\n\nParietal lobe\n\nis incorrect as pathology in this region tends to result in sensory impairment and apraxia, and would not cause the classic Parkinsonism seen in the question.\n\nTemporal lobe\n\nis incorrect as inflammation in this lobe, most commonly found in herpes simplex encephalitis, would be more likely to cause confusion, memory loss and emotional lability rather than Parkinsonism."
