      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Discharge with worsening advice",
      "explanation": "ELISA is the first-line investigation for suspected Lyme disease in patients with no history of erythema migrans\n\nThis patient presents with a series of non-specific symptoms with a known history of an insect bite while hiking. This should raise suspicions for Lyme disease especially given the lack of symptoms to suggest other pathology.\n\nErythema migrans is the typical rash associated with Lyme disease and when this is seen, it is diagnostic of Lyme disease and doxycycline can be started. The patient in this case does not have a rash present and should not be started on doxycycline until confirmatory tests are performed.\n\nELISA antibody testing is the preferred 1st line test when a rash is not present but the diagnosis of Lyme disease is suspected. If the clinical suspicion is high, oral doxycycline can be started following the ELISA test while awaiting results. Should ELISA return negative and the index of suspicion remains high, a repeat ELISA test can be done 4-6 weeks after the initial ELISA test.\n\nImmunoblot test is used when the ELISA test returns positive or equivocal to confirm the diagnosis of Lyme disease. This also has a role in diagnosing suspected Lyme disease if the initial ELISA test returns negative and the patient has had symptoms >12 weeks.\n\nIt would not be appropriate to discharge this patient without further investigation given his symptoms had been ongoing for 4 weeks now."
