      "correctText": "Bacterial furunculosis",
      "explanation": "Anthrax presents with a black eschar that is typically painless; it is treated with ciprofloxacin\n\nAnthrax is caused by the gram-positive soil organism\n\nBacillus anthracis\n\n. Cutaneous anthrax represents over 90% of cases and it results from direct inoculation. Other forms include inhalation, injection and ingestion anthrax.\n\nAnthrax is typically acquired from infected animals. Hence being a farmer is a risk factor. It is a very rare disease, but is most common in Central and South America, sub-Saharan Africa, central and southwestern Asia, and southern and eastern Europe.\n\nIt typically presents with a pruritic vesicle that develops two to five days after the time of inoculation, and then rapidly develops into a black painless ulcer and eschar. There is normally marked oedema surrounding the lesion, which can lead to compartment syndrome or necrotising fasciitis. In about half of cases, there is regional lymphadenopathy. If left untreated cutaneous anthrax has a mortality rate of 20%.\n\nBacterial furunculosis is typically painful.\n\nErythema nodosum is painful and does not typically ulcerate.\n\nOrf does not typically present with oedema, it is more scab-like, and rarely affects the legs.\n\nPyoderma gangrenosum is typically painful, and there will often be clues in the stem relating to associated diseases."
