      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Ceftriaxone",
      "explanation": "Visceral leishmaniasis: sodium stibogluconate is the recommended treatment of choice\n\nSodium stibogluconate\n\nis the first line treatment for the treatment of visceral leishmaniasis, a severe protozoal infection most commonly seen in Brazil, east Africa and India. It causes fevers, weight loss, and massive hepatosplenomegaly. Generalised hyperpigmentation ('grey discolouration') of the skin is a late feature. Pancytopenia is seen due to hypersplenism. Renal failure is one of the complications which lead to death along with gastrointestinal bleeding, peripheral oedema and secondary bacterial infections. The incubation period is between weeks to months and illness can be gradual or abrupt onset. A bone marrow biopsy for suspected leishmaniasis would reveal amastigotes (the intracellular form of leishmania).\n\nAmphotericin B\n\nis an antifungal agent which may be used in the treatment of visceral leishmaniasis, but in the UK this would not be used as monotherapy and not as first-line treatment.\n\nArtemether with lumefantrine\n\nis the treatment of choice for uncomplicated\n\nP. falciparum\n\nmalaria. Renal failure may be seen in severe malaria, as can splenomegaly, but this would not explain the 'grey skin' which is seen in visceral leishmaniasis. The patient has anaemia and leukopenia. In malaria, anaemia may be seen due to haemolysis but white cells are typically normal or slightly elevated. Severe leukopenia is less common in malaria. Malaria is also less likely to present 4 weeks after returning from Brazil, as it will typically present more acutely.\n\nCeftriaxone\n\nis used in the treatment of multiple bacterial infections, but its use in this case would not cover the offending protozoal infection.\n\nDoxycycline\n\nis used in the treatment of rickettsial infection. Patients present after exposure via flea or tick bite with fever, headache and maculopapular rash. Rickettsia would not typically cause grey discolouration of the skin, pancytopenia, or acute renal failure."
