      "explanation": "Campylobacter\n\nClarithromycin\n\nis correct. Although\n\nCampylobacter jejuni\n\ngastroenteritis is often self-limiting, the presence of bloody diarrhoea suggests severe disease. Furthermore, the patient is immunocompromised. For these reasons, he should be treated with anti-microbial therapy. The first line treatment is with a macrolide such as clarithromycin.\n\nCiprofloxacin\n\nis incorrect. This antibiotic can be used for strains which are resistant to macrolides.\n\nLoperamide\n\nis incorrect. Antimotility agents, such as loperamide, can lead to prolonged illness or intestinal perforation in any invasive diarrhoea and thus should be avoided.\n\nMetronidazole\n\nis incorrect. Indications for metronidazole include giardiasis and\n\nC.diff infection\n\n.\n\nSteroids\n\nis incorrect. There is no role for steroids in the treatment of infective gastroenteritis."
