      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Non-competitive inhibition of HIV reverse transcriptase",
      "explanation": "Integrase inhibitors ('gravirs') - blocks the enzyme that inserts the viral genome into the DNA of the host cell\n\nBlocks enzymatic insertion of the viral genome into host DNA is correct. This is the 'gravir' class of medication, which are integrase inhibitors. The current BHIVA (British HIV Association) guidelines recommend bictegravir/tenofovir-alafenamide/emtricitabine - 'Biktarvy' - as first-line treatment for HIV in the absence of contraindications and drug-drug interactions. This regimen has a high barrier to resistance and is the optimal choice during the coronavirus pandemic where access to baseline testing for resistance may be less than normal. This patient has normal renal and hepatic function and is not on any other medications. Therefore, this combination is the typical initial choice.\n\nNon-competitive inhibition of HIV reverse transcriptase is incorrect. This is the mechanism of action of non-nucleoside analogue reverse transcriptase inhibitors. This class of medication includes nevirapine and efavirenz.  This would be another suitable choice in normal circumstances to add to a combination of two NRTIs. However, during the pandemic, the above combination is recommended.\n\nAntagonism of CCR5 is incorrect. This is the 'entry inhibitor' class of medication. This includes maraviroc and enfuvirtide. This is typically not part of a standard starting regimen for treatment-naive patients and is usually only used if patient-specific factors dictate its use.\n\nCompetitive inhibition of HIV reverse transcriptase is incorrect. This is the mechanism of action of the nucleoside analogue reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTI). This class of medication includes emtricitabine and tenofovir. Typically, starting HIV medication regimens use two NRTIs and another medication class in combination.\n\nBlocking proteases is incorrect. This is the protease inhibitor class of medication. Examples include ritonavir and indinavir.  This would usually be a possible choice for initial combination therapy in HIV. However, given the current pandemic situation, an integrase inhibitor is preferred as the 3rd drug."
