      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease",
      "explanation": "Japanese encephalitis can present with Parkinsonism- this is a sign of basal ganglia involvement\n\nJapanese encephalitis\n\nis correct. Japanese encephalitis is spread by Culex mosquitoes that breed in rice paddy fields, Japanese encephalitis is the most common cause of encephalitis in South East Asia, China and India. Most cases are asymptomatic. However, in more severe cases, symptoms include headache, fever, seizures and confusion. Parkinsonian symptoms may also be seen secondary to basal ganglia involvement, such as this patient's tremor and increased rigidity.\n\nHuntington's disease\n\nis incorrect. This is a neurodegenerative disease that targets the GABA-ergic cells in the corpus striatum of the basal ganglia. Symptoms of Huntington's disease typically occur between 30-50 years of age. Although, through anticipation, the following generations of patients can have an earlier onset of disease. The most classical symptom is chorea, an uncontrolled and uncoordinated movement of the body which differs from this patient's presentation of isolated jerking in his upper limb.\n\nParkinson's disease\n\nis incorrect. This is a neurodegenerative disease affecting the dopaminergic cells in the substantia nigra. Motor symptoms include bradykinesia, tremor, shuffling gait and rigidity. Although this patient has similar symptoms, the history of confusion and fever since travelling back from a southeast Asian country makes Japanese encephalitis more likely.\n\nRheumatic fever\n\nis incorrect. This is caused by infection with\n\nStreptococcus pyogenes\n\n. Symptoms include polyarthritis, carditis and valvulitis, erythema marginatum and chorea in late disease. It is not responsible for this patient's symptoms.\n\nCreutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD)\n\nis incorrect. This is a progressive neurodegenerative disease secondary to the presence of prion proteins. Symptoms of CJD include rapid onset of dementia, behavioural change and myoclonus. Most cases of CJD are sporadic, although some are familial."
