      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Oral metronidazole",
      "explanation": "Campylobacter\n\nCampylobacteriosis is usually associated with the consumption of contaminated food and drink, such as undercooked meat (especially poultry), unpasteurised milk, or untreated water, but the source of infection is often not found. Infection may be asymptomatic in 25-50% of people or cause diarrhoea (which may be bloody), nausea, vomiting, abdominal cramps, and fever.\n\nWhile it is frequently self-limiting with symptoms usually resolving within 1 week, it is recommended that those with severe symptoms (e.g. high fever, high-output or bloody diarrhoea) or who are immunocompromised (as is seen in this patient who has poorly-controlled diabetes) should be considered for treatment.\n\nFirst-line antimicrobial therapy is a macrolide such as clarithromycin, hence this is the correct option here. Azithromycin and erythromycin are suitable alternatives.\n\nMetronidazole and vancomycin are used in the treatment of\n\nClostridium difficile\n\ninfection.\n\nWhile ciprofloxacin can be considered as an alternative, the British National Formulary specifically states that 'strains with decreased sensitivity to ciprofloxacin isolated frequently', hence it is not first-line treatment.\n\nIt would be unsuitable to continue conservative management in this immunocompromised individual who continues to have frequent bloody diarrhoea after 6 days of symptoms."
