      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Doxycycline",
      "explanation": "Visceral leishmaniasis: sodium stibogluconate is the recommended treatment of choice\n\nSodium stibogluconate\n\nis the treatment of choice for visceral leishmaniasis, also known as kala-azar the most likely cause of this patient's presentation. This diagnosis is supported by the patient’s travel to an endemic region (South Sudan), prolonged fever, hepatosplenomegaly, pancytopaenia, and bone marrow aspirate showing amastigotes in macrophages. Amastigotes are the intracellular form of the\n\nLeishmania\n\nparasite, found within macrophages. Their identification in bone marrow aspirates is a hallmark diagnostic finding for visceral leishmaniasis. These features are characteristic of\n\nLeishmania donovani\n\ninfection. Sodium stibogluconate disrupts the parasite’s metabolic pathways, leading to its elimination. Prompt treatment is essential to prevent fatal complications such as severe anaemia, opportunistic infections, and multiorgan failure.\n\nThe patient’s travel history, prolonged systemic symptoms, and amastigotes in macrophages strongly indicate visceral leishmaniasis. While\n\namphotericin B\n\nis effective for the condition, it is typically used when resistance to antimonials like sodium stibogluconate is suspected or confirmed.\n\nDoxycycline\n\n, an antibiotic and antimalarial prophylactic, does not have any activity against leishmania species. Its mechanisms do not target the parasite’s life cycle, making it ineffective for this condition. Thus, its use would not address the patient’s symptoms or underlying disease.\n\nThe patient’s clinical presentation, including fever and splenomegaly, may resemble malaria; however, the presence of amastigotes in macrophages confirms visceral leishmaniasis.\n\nMefloquine\n\n, an antimalarial, has no activity against\n\nLeishmania donovani\n\n. Although travel to endemic regions raises the possibility of malaria, the diagnostic findings here clearly point to visceral leishmaniasis, for which mefloquine is not appropriate.\n\nThe combination of fever, hepatosplenomegaly, and pancytopaenia in a patient returning from an endemic area like South Sudan is suggestive of visceral leishmaniasis, not a protozoal infection treated by\n\nmetronidazole\n\n, such as\n\nEntamoeba histolytica\n\nor\n\nGiardia lamblia\n\n. Although metronidazole is useful for some parasitic infections, it does not target\n\nLeishmania\n\nspecies, making it ineffective in this case."
