      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Legionella pneumoniae",
      "explanation": "Pneumonia, peripheral blood smear showing red blood cell agglutination →\n\nThis patient has presented with clear features in keeping with pneumonia (respiratory symptoms and chest x-ray changes etc). They also have features indicating a potential atypical pneumonia with extra-pulmonary symptoms in the form of gastrointestinal (GI) upset, although these are non-specific findings. The key feature in this case is the peripheral blood smear showing red blood cell (RBC) agglutination making\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae\n\nthe most likely diagnosis. This agglutination is a result of the surface of the RBC becoming coated with antibodies and typically occurs in cold agglutinin disease, a form of autoimmune haemolytic anaemia which can be triggered by specific infections. Cold agglutinin disease is a well-recognised feature of\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae\n\n.\n\nEpstein-Barr virus\n\n(EBV) is the cause of the relatively rare infectious mononucleosis, also known as glandular fever. As the name suggests these patients typically present with enlarged lymph nodes in the neck as well as a sore throat and coryzal symptoms. EBV is not commonly associated with other respiratory or gastrointestinal symptoms and rarely causes consolidation on chest x-ray. Although EBV can be associated with cold agglutinin disease it is far less likely than\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae\n\nin this case given the patient's presenting features.\n\nCoxiella burnetii\n\nis the bacteria responsible for Q fever and is typically found in cattle, sheep and other domestic animals. Q fever can progress to atypical pneumonia and therefore the sign and symptoms of an infected patient may be similar to those described in this case. However,\n\nCoxiella burnetii\n\nis not associated with cold agglutinin disease and therefore RBC agglutination would not be expected.\n\nLegionella pneumoniae\n\nis a common cause of atypical pneumonia and similar in presentation to that of\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae\n\ninfections.\n\nLegionella pneumoniae\n\nis however not associated with cold agglutinin disease and therefore RBC agglutination on a peripheral smear would not be expected.\n\nStreptococcus pneumoniae\n\n, although one of the commonest causes of pneumonia, is not typically associated with extra-pulmonary features and does not cause cold agglutinin disease."
