      "explanation": "Pruritic rash on the buttocks or soles ?\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nStrongyloides stercoralis\n\n.\n\nStrongyloides stercoralis\n\nis a roundworm which causes strongyloidiasis. Larvae penetrate the skin and are carried via the blood to the lungs where they are coughed up and swallowed. Adult worms reproduce in the GI tract (causing symptoms of bloating, discomfort and diarrhoea) and larvae are passed in the stool. Auto-infection can occur through the GI tract or peri-anal skin. Larva currens is the characteristic skin eruption of\n\nStrongyloides stercoralis\n\n. It causes an urticarial band that typically starts in the peri-anal area. The rash rapidly migrates, more quickly than the rash of cutaneous larva migrans.\n\nAncylostoma braziliense\n\nis a parasite that affects cats and dogs. Humans are a dead-end host and do not develop GI symptoms but humans can develop cutaneous larva migrans which causes severely pruritic slowly migrating skin eruption.\n\nEggs of\n\nAscaris lumbricoides\n\nare ingested orally then travel to the alveoli, where they are coughed up, swallowed and travel to the small intestine.\n\nAscaris lumbricoides\n\nmay cause no symptoms, but as the worm burden increases there may be gastrointestinal symptoms e.g. diarrhoea, abdominal pain, malaise and weakness. Patients may even develop bowel obstruction and intestinal bleeding.\n\nEggs of\n\nEnterobius vermicularis\n\n(also known as threadworm) are ingested and the larvae then adult worms migrate to the ileum and colon. Enterobiasis can cause anal itching but wouldn't typically cause the migrating rash.\n\nTrichuris trichiura\n\neggs are ingested and develop in the colon. It is mostly asymptomatic but severe infections can cause abdominal pain, diarrhoea, weight loss and anaemia."
