      "id": 337,
      "question": "A 28-year-old man who is immunosuppressed secondary to HIV infection is admitted to hospital with dyspnoea and a dry cough. His chest x-ray shows bilateral interstitial pulmonary infiltrates and he is started on co-trimoxazole empirically. The following morning he complains of a sudden worsening of his dyspnoea associated with left-sided chest pain. Which complication is most likely to have developed?",
      "options": [
        "Empyema",
        "Pulmonary embolism",
        "Acute respiratory distress syndrome",
        "Pericarditis",
        "Pneumothorax"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Pericarditis",
      "explanation": "Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia - pneumothorax is a common complication\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nPneumothorax\n\n. In this case, the patient is immunosuppressed due to HIV infection and presents with dyspnoea and a dry cough. The chest x-ray shows bilateral interstitial pulmonary infiltrates, which are suggestive of an opportunistic infection such as\n\nPneumocystis jirovecii\n\npneumonia (PJP). Co-trimoxazole is an appropriate empirical treatment for PJP. The sudden worsening of dyspnoea associated with left-sided chest pain raises concern for a pneumothorax, a known complication of\n\nPneumocystis jirovecii\n\npneumonia.\n\nEmpyema\n\nis an incorrect answer because it refers to pus accumulation in the pleural space, which usually develops secondary to bacterial pneumonia or thoracic surgery. This patient's presentation and risk factors are more consistent with PJP rather than bacterial pneumonia, making empyema less likely.\n\nPulmonary embolism\n\nis also an incorrect answer. Although it can present with acute dyspnoea and chest pain, there are no specific findings in the clinical scenario that would suggest a higher likelihood of pulmonary embolism over other complications like pneumothorax. Additionally, the presence of bilateral interstitial infiltrates on chest x-ray makes pulmonary embolism less likely.\n\nAcute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)\n\nis another incorrect answer. ARDS typically presents with rapid onset hypoxemic respiratory failure and bilateral pulmonary infiltrates on chest imaging. However, ARDS would not be expected to cause unilateral chest pain as described in this case. Furthermore, the acute worsening of symptoms suggests a more localized process like pneumothorax.\n\nFinally,\n\nPericarditis\n\nis also an incorrect answer because it typically presents with sharp, pleuritic chest pain that improves when sitting up and worsens when lying down or taking deep breaths. Although pericarditis can be seen in patients with HIV, the presence of bilateral interstitial infiltrates on chest x-ray and the risk factors for PJP make pneumothorax a more likely complication in this patient."
