      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Penicillin allergy",
      "explanation": "URTI symptoms + amoxicillin → rash ?glandular fever\n\nInfectious mononucleosis\n\nis the correct diagnosis in this scenario. The presentation of severe sore throat, lethargy, and headache, followed by the development of a rash after amoxicillin administration, is highly characteristic of infectious mononucleosis (IM), caused by Epstein-Barr virus (EBV). Approximately 90% of patients with IM who receive aminopenicillins develop a maculopapular rash, which is not a true allergic reaction but rather a result of the interaction between the virus and the antibiotic. This is particularly common in adolescents and young adults, who are the typical demographic for IM.\n\nKawasaki disease\n\nis incorrect as it typically affects children under 5 years of age and presents with a constellation of symptoms including fever for at least 5 days, bilateral conjunctival injection, changes in the lips and oral cavity, cervical lymphadenopathy, polymorphous rash, and changes in the extremities. The age and presentation in this case do not fit with Kawasaki disease.\n\nPenicillin allergy\n\nis incorrect because while it can cause a maculopapular rash, it typically develops more rapidly after antibiotic exposure (usually within hours) and is often accompanied by other symptoms such as urticaria, angioedema, or respiratory symptoms. The timing and context of this rash, along with the preceding symptoms, are more consistent with IM.\n\nHIV seroconversion\n\nis incorrect because although it can present with similar symptoms (fever, sore throat, rash), the temporal relationship with amoxicillin administration and the typical age group make IM more likely. HIV seroconversion rash is not specifically associated with antibiotic administration.\n\nBeta-lactamase producing streptococcal sore throat\n\nis incorrect because while it would explain antibiotic failure, it would not account for the development of the rash after amoxicillin administration. Additionally, streptococcal infection typically improves within 24-48 hours even without appropriate antibiotics, and the constellation of symptoms (lethargy, headache) is more suggestive of a viral cause."
