      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Ceftriaxone",
      "explanation": "Fever and the presence of a eschar in a patient returning from South East Asia is strongly suggestive of scrub typhus (caused by\n\nThe likely diagnosis here is one of scrub typhus, which is caused by the obligate intracellular bacteria Orientia tsutsugamushi and is one of a group of related conditions collectively known as the 'tropical rickettsial infections'.\n\nScrub typhus is the most common tropical rickettsial infection in South-east Asia, where it is a common cause of febrile illness.  It is transmitted by the bite of a 'chigger' which live in dry grassy areas.  It presents as a vasculitis-like illness with regional or generalised lymphadenopathy and there is typically a black crusted lesion at the site of the bite called an 'eschar'.  Complications include hepatic and renal failure, myocarditis and (as seen in this case) meningoencephalitis.\n\nThe diagnosis of scrub typhus is almost invariably a clinical one and rests on plausible epidemiology and compatible symptoms.  Given that the clinical spectrum of disease is wide this can often be difficult and a very careful search for the eschar (often at the joins of clothing where the chigger mite is unable to climb further) is warranted as its discovery all but seals the diagnosis.\n\nDoxycycline is effective against all of the tropical rickettsial infections and may initially may need to be given down an nasogastric tube.\n\nArtesunate would be an appropriate treatment for severe malaria. Although malaria might give a similar picture to the one above it does not cause an eschar and his malaria rapid diagnostic test is negative.\n\nCeftriaxone is not effective again scrub typhus but rather would be a treatment for meningitis.  Although not impossible, is not the most likely diagnosis and the probability is significantly reduced by the lack of nuchal rigidity.\n\nAcetazolamide is used for altitude sickness which does not present this way.\n\nIVIG has a number of uses but has no effect against Orientia tsutsugamushi or other pathological rickettsia."
