      "explanation": "Oral ciprofloxacin or rifampicin is used as prophylaxis for contacts of patients with meningococcal meningitis\n\nThe presence of rash alongside the finding of gram-negative diplococci is highly suggestive of meningitis caused by\n\nneisseria meningitidis\n\n.\n\nProphylaxis against meningococcal disease should be considered for the following close contacts, regardless of meningococcal vaccination status. In particular, those with prolonged close contact such as the parents in this case.\n\nOral ciprofloxacin or rifampicin may be used for prophylaxis. The Health Protection Agency (HPA) guidelines now state that whilst either may be used ciprofloxacin is the drug of choice as it is widely available and only requires one dose. Therefore, a\n\nsingle dose of oral ciprofloxacin\n\nis the correct answer.\n\nRifampicin if chosen is twice a day for 2 days. Other disadvantages of rifampicin other than treatment length are that it has drug interactions, including oral contraceptives and is usually only available from hospital pharmacies.\n\n1 week course of oral ciprofloxacin\n\nis incorrect as only a single dose is required.\n\nSingle dose of intravenous ciprofloxacin\n\nand\n\n1-week course of intravenous ciprofloxacin\n\nare both incorrect as intravenous doses are not required for prophylaxis.\n\nObservation only\n\nis not appropriate given the parents have been in close contact with the patient over a prolonged period so are at high risk."
