      "correctText": "Varicella zoster virus",
      "explanation": "The most common causes of viral meningitis in adults are enteroviruses\n\nCoxsackievirus B\n\nis correct. The clinical features are suggestive of meningitis. The lumbar puncture results suggest a viral aetiology due to the pleomorphic lymphocytosis, normal glucose, and only moderately raised protein. Coxsackie or echovirus groups of enteroviruses are the most common cause of viral meningitis. They are the most common cause due to high prevalence, easy transmission, and a tropism for the meninges.\n\nHerpes simplex virus type 1\n\nis incorrect.  This virus is associated with viral encephalitis in adults. The clinical features, in this case, are more in keeping with viral meningitis rather than encephalitis which would typically present with seizures, confusion or reduced consciousness level.\n\nHerpes simplex virus type 2\n\nis incorrect. This virus strain can also be associated with encephalitis, however, It is herpes simplex virus type 1 which is more commonly implicated.\n\nNeisseria meningitidis\n\nis incorrect.  The CSF lymphocytosis with preserved glucose level suggests a viral meningitis rather than bacterial. Bacterial meningitis would tend to result in a neutrophilic pleocytosis with a CSF glucose < 50% of plasma glucose.\n\nVaricella zoster virus\n\nis incorrect. Although this remains within the differential diagnosis, the most common cause of viral meningitis in adults are the enteroviruses."
