      "id": 371,
      "question": "A 53-year-old man is seen in the respiratory clinic. He complains of frequent episodes of haemoptysis along with fever and shortness of breath. His past medical is significant for previously-treated tuberculosis and nil else.\n\nOn examination, there are scattered crackles on the right side of the chest. A chest X-ray shows a well-defined, rounded opacity in the right upper zone.\n\nSerum aspergillus IgG precipitins are positive. Sputum Ziehl-Neelsen staining is negative.\n\nGiven the likely diagnosis, what is the most appropriate treatment?",
      "options": [
        "2 months of rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide and ethambutol, followed by 4 months of rifampicin and isoniazid",
        "6 months of rifampicin and isoniazid",
        "Co-amoxiclav",
        "Oral steroids",
        "Surgical resection of the lesion"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "6 months of rifampicin and isoniazid",
      "explanation": "An aspergilloma may arise in a lung cavity that developed secondary to previous tuberculosis\n\nSurgical resection of the lesion\n\nis correct. The history of fever, shortness of breath and haemoptysis coupled with a well-defined rounded opacity on imaging is highly suggestive of an aspergilloma, formed in the cavity left by previous tuberculosis infection. The treatment for aspergilloma, especially in those with significant haemoptysis, is surgical resection.\n\n2 months of rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide and ethambutol, followed by 4 months of rifampicin and isoniazid\n\nis incorrect. This is the first-line treatment for active tuberculosis infection, but this clinical picture is suggestive of an aspergilloma. Tuberculosis is less likely in this patient as this would not explain the well defined rounded opacity. Additionally, the Ziehl-Neelsen staining is negative, and the serum\n\naspergillus\n\nIgG precipitins would not be positive in tuberculosis.\n\n6 months rifampicin and isoniazid\n\nis incorrect. This is the treatment for latent tuberculosis. This diagnosis is less likely as latent tuberculosis cannot explain the chest X-ray findings. The serum\n\naspergillus\n\nIgG precipitins would not be positive in tuberculosis.\n\nCo-amoxiclav\n\nis incorrect. The history here is suggestive of an aspergilloma. Co-amoxiclav would be a treatment for community-acquired pneumonia, amongst other things. Community-acquired pneumonia would not present as a rounded, well-defined opacity on imaging, but would show as a focus of consolidation.\n\nOral steroids\n\nis incorrect. This would be the treatment for allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA). ABPA would present in those with a history of asthma as wheeze, dyspnoea and productive cough. The chest X-ray would show infiltrates and evidence of bronchiectasis, but not a well-defined rounded opacity."
