      "correctText": "Bacillus anthracis",
      "explanation": "Enterococci - Gram-positive cocci\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nEnterococcus faecalis\n\n. This organism is a Gram-positive coccus, which is part of the normal gut flora. It can cause various infections, including urinary tract infections, endocarditis and bacteraemia. The term 'Gram-positive' refers to the ability of these bacteria to retain the crystal violet dye used in the Gram stain procedure, due to their thick peptidoglycan cell wall.\n\nMoraxella catarrhalis\n\nis an incorrect option as this bacterium is a Gram-negative diplococcus. It is one of the causes of otitis media (middle ear infection) and exacerbations of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD).\n\nHaemophilus influenzae\n\n, another incorrect choice, is a Gram-negative coccobacillus that can cause a variety of infections including pneumonia, meningitis and epiglottitis. Despite its name, it does not cause influenza; the 'influenza' part of its name comes from when it was originally thought to be the causative agent.\n\nNeisseria meningitidis\n\nalso does not fit with the correct answer as it's a Gram-negative diplococcus. This bacterium can lead to meningococcal meningitis and septicemia.\n\nFinally,\n\nBacillus anthracis\n\nis indeed a Gram-positive bacterium but it's not a coccus; instead, it's a rod-shaped bacillus. This organism causes anthrax which primarily affects livestock but can also infect humans."
