      "id": 378,
      "question": "Which of the following is true regarding the Salmonella species?",
      "options": [
        "Rose spots appear in all patients with typhoid",
        "They are normally present in the gut as commensals",
        "They are anaerobic organisms",
        "A relative bradycardia is often seen in typhoid fever",
        "Salmonella typhi can be categorised into type A, B and C"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "A relative bradycardia is often seen in typhoid fever",
      "explanation": "A relative bradycardia can point towards a diagnosis of typhoid fever\n\nThe correct answer is '\n\nA relative bradycardia is often seen in typhoid fever\n\n'. Relative bradycardia, also known as Faget's sign, is a clinical feature of typhoid fever caused by\n\nSalmonella typhi\n\n. It refers to the presence of a pulse rate slower than would be expected for a given elevated body temperature. This paradoxical presentation is not exclusive to typhoid fever but it is commonly associated with it.\n\nRose spots appear in all patients with typhoid\n\nis incorrect. Rose spots are indeed a characteristic skin rash associated with typhoid fever, presenting as faint salmon-coloured macules on the trunk and abdomen. However, they only appear in about 30% of cases and therefore cannot be considered a universal finding.\n\nThe statement that\n\nSalmonella species are normally present in the gut as commensals\n\nis also incorrect. While some bacteria live harmlessly in the human gut as part of the normal microbiota (commensals),\n\nSalmonella\n\nspecies are pathogenic organisms and are not part of the normal gut flora.\n\nSalmonella species are anaerobic organisms\n\n, this option too, is incorrect. The genus\n\nSalmonella\n\nconsists of facultative anaerobes, meaning they can grow without oxygen but will use it if available. They do not exclusively rely on anaerobic conditions for survival or growth.\n\nLastly, the assertion that\n\nSalmonella typhi can be categorised into type A, B and C\n\n, is false. There are no such classifications within this species. The confusion may arise from serotyping based on surface antigens which classifies Salmonella into several serogroups and serotypes but these are not referred to as types A, B or C."
