      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Discharge from hospital to avoid further exposure",
      "explanation": "Oral antivirals should be used for at risk individuals exposed to varicella, starting on day 7 from the date of exposure\n\nStart oral aciclovir on day 7 from exposure\n\n: This is the correct answer according to current UK guidelines as of September 2024, following withdrawal of VZIG from routine use for post-exposure prophylaxis in at-risk groups such as immunosuppressed individuals. Oral antivirals—typically aciclovir or valaciclovir—should be started between days 7 and 14 after significant exposure to varicella zoster virus in those without evidence of immunity, with treatment ideally commencing on day 7 after first contact and continuing for seven days. This approach aims to prevent or attenuate infection during the incubation period when viral replication begins but before onset of symptoms.\n\nAdminister varicella-zoster immunoglobulin immediately\n\n: This was previously recommended for high-risk exposures but is no longer advised in the UK since VZIG has been withdrawn as first-line post-exposure prophylaxis due to supply constraints and updated guidance favouring oral antivirals instead.\n\nDischarge from hospital to avoid further exposure\n\n: While minimising further risk is sensible, this does not address the fact that significant exposure has already occurred during the infectious period; simply removing the patient does not provide effective prophylaxis against developing disease.\n\nObserve for symptoms and treat only if rash develops\n\n: Waiting until clinical features appear misses the window where post-exposure prophylaxis can prevent or mitigate severe disease in an at-risk patient, especially one who is immunosuppressed and more likely to develop complications.\n\nStart oral aciclovir immediately\n\n: Starting too early may reduce efficacy because antiviral agents are most beneficial when initiated after viral replication begins but before clinical symptoms manifest—this typically corresponds with starting therapy on day seven post-exposure rather than immediately."
