      "id": 401,
      "question": "A 67-year-old man presents to the emergency department with a dry cough, fever and lethargy which started 2 days after returning from a holiday in Morocco. The patient is normally fit and well and takes no regular medications.\n\nA chest X-ray shows a right-sided mid-zone patchy consolidation.\n\nSputum is sent for culture and a urine antigen test is positive. He is well enough to be discharged with oral antibiotics. He has no known allergies.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate antibiotic choice?",
      "options": [
        "Cephalexin",
        "Clarithromycin",
        "Co-amoxiclav",
        "Co-trimoxazole",
        "Doxycycline"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Co-trimoxazole",
      "explanation": "Macrolides such as clarithromycin are used to treat\n\nClarithromycin\n\nis correct. Either a macrolide or a fluoroquinolone antibiotic should be used for confirmed\n\nLegionella\n\npneumonia. The British Thoracic Society (BTS) recommend either of these medications when treating\n\nLegionella\n\n. This patient has a typical history of\n\nLegionella\n\n: recent travel, dry cough and fever. The positive urine antigen test indicates the presence of\n\nLegionella pneumophila\n\nserogroup 1.\n\nCephalexin\n\nis not recommended by the BTS or NICE for oral treatment of community-acquired pneumonia in any context. It has little atypical coverage and macrolides are known to have greater efficacy against\n\nLegionella\n\nspecies. This presentation is very suggestive of\n\nLegionella\n\nwhich requires specific treatment with either a macrolide or fluoroquinolone.\n\nCo-amoxiclav\n\nshould only be used for the empirical treatment of high-severity community-acquired pneumonia as per the NICE guidelines, not in the targeted treatment of\n\nLegionella\n\n. This patient is suitable for outpatient treatment as this is not a high-severity pneumonia and therefore co-amoxiclav would not be an appropriate choice.\n\nCo-trimoxazole\n\nis not used in the treatment of\n\nLegionella\n\n. It is used in high doses for the treatment of pneumocystis pneumonia which is seen in immunosuppressed patients. A typical history would be a patient with poorly controlled HIV presenting with fever, dry cough and shortness of breath on exertion.\n\nDoxycycline\n\nis a tetracycline antibiotic which is generally not used in the treatment of\n\nLegionella\n\n. There is resistance to tetracyclines in certain strains of\n\nLegionella\n\nand there is stronger evidence that macrolides and fluoroquinolones have greater efficacy."
