      "id": 412,
      "question": "A 36-year-old man is seen in the emergency department after returning from a business trip to Nigeria where he had spent three weeks mainly in the cities but also travelling to factories in more rural locations. He was well during the trip and did not recall sustaining any bites. He did not take any malaria prophylaxis or apply DEET as a colleague had informed him that Nigeria no longer had malaria.\n\nUpon his return home he had felt generally unwell with malaise and myalgia and had presented after developing rigors. He was alert and oriented at admission and able to give a clear history. He had a past medical history of asthma which required a daily preventer and occasional use of a reliever, but no other medical history.\n\nHis observations show a temperature 38.9ºC, heart rate 112 beats per minute, blood pressure 122/82mmHg, respiratory rate 20/minute, oxygen saturation 99% on air.\n\nHis physical examination is unremarkable. His blood results are shown below.\n\n\nHb\t145 g/l\tNa+\t137 mmol/l\nPlatelets\t98* 109/l\tK+\t4.7 mmol/l\nWBC\t10.2 * 109/l\tUrea\t4.6 mmol/l\nNeuts\t3.2 * 109/l\tCreatinine\t90 µmol/l\nLymphs\t0.1 * 109/l\tCRP\t150 mg/l\nEosin\t0.0* 109/l\t\t\n\n\nMalaria thin blood film\tMultiple early and late stage trophozoites seen with morphology consistent with Plasmodium falciparum malaria infection. Schizonts of Plasmodium falciparum seen. No gametocytes seen. Parasitaemia 1.2%.\n\nWhat feature of this gentleman's presentation is the most concerning for his prognosis?",
