      "correctText": "Having asthma as a co-morbidity",
      "explanation": "Schizonts and late stages of trophozoites are typically sequested in Plasmodium falciparum infection and their presence on the peripheral smear indicates severe disease\n\nThe presence of schizonts on the blood film is very rare in falciparum infection and indicates imminent schizogony and consequently a significant rise in the parasitaemia. Most labs will call to alert the ward team if they see falciparum schizonts and it is normally taken as an indication for parenteral therapy.\n\nThrombocytopenia is almost invariably present with malaria and its absence should prompt questioning of the diagnosis.\n\nAlthough tachycardia and a raised temperature are indicative of infection, they are both often found in malaria infection of all grades of severity and neither are part of the World Health Organisation's criteria for severe malaria.\n\nAlthough any co-morbidities should be noted and taken seriously, well controlled asthma is not as concerning as the presence of schizonts on the blood film."
