      "id": 415,
      "question": "A 22-year-old male university student has recently returned from Sri Lanka where he had been working with an animal charity. Whilst there he had felt slightly unwell with vague abdominal cramping which had mostly settled with an irritable bowel syndrome medication a friend had bought over-the-counter back in the UK. He had returned to the UK a week later and resumed his studies but stopped attending lectures after his stomach pains worsened and he developed constipation. After a week back in the UK he went to see his general practitioner who found only a low grade fever of 37.8ºC and reassured him.\n\nThree days later he was taken to accident and emergency by a fellow student after he was found delirious in his room. On presentation he was unable to give a coherent history and was found to have a temperature of 38.9ºC, a heart rate of 48 beats per minute, a blood pressure of 98/48mmHg, a respiratory rate of 22/min, and oxygen saturations on air of 99%.\n\nHis capillary refill time was delayed at 3 seconds and his abdomen was guarded and elicited a painful response when palpated. He had no discernible meningism and no rashes could be found.\n\nYour FY1 has performed a CT scan which shows signs of small bowel perforation but no other pathology.\n\nWhich of the following is likely to provide a definitive diagnosis?",
