      "explanation": "Severe manifestations of enteric fever include bowel perforation and neurological complication. If these occur it is typically in the third week of illness\n\nThis patient has acquired typhoid whilst in Sri Lanka and has followed a fairly typical course.  Abdominal pains are common and constipation predominates over diarrhoea.  When they occur, severe manifestations such as typhoid delirium/encephalitis and abdominal perforation (which the patient in the vignette has experienced) typically occur in the third week of illness. Leukopenia is sometimes seen but the white cell count is normally within the normal range.  Rose spots are insensitive and their absence does not exclude the diagnosis. A relative bradycardia (Faget sign) is typical of typhoid fever.\n\nThe test of choice is a large volume blood culture (the large volumes are need to increase the yield as few bacteria are circulating).  The Widal test is commonly used globally but is not recommended for clinical use due to low sensitivity and specificity.   Bone marrow cultures have increased sensitivity compare to blood cultures are considered in exceptional circumstances.\n\nAn HIV test would be prudent but would not give the definitive diagnosis.\n\nCerebral spinal fluid also has the potential to yield a definitive diagnosis but with lower sensitivity than a large volume blood culture and is more technically difficult to obtain.\n\nA PET scan would be unlikely to help in the diagnosis."
