      "correctText": "A protozoa spread by sandfly bites",
      "explanation": "Leishmaniasis is caused by the intracellular protozoa\n\nA protozoa spread by sandfly bites\n\nis correct because the patient presents with slowly enlarging, painless ulcers with indurated edges and central ulceration after insect exposure in rural Brazil—features characteristic of cutaneous leishmaniasis. Histology revealing intracellular organisms within macrophages further supports this diagnosis. Leishmaniasis is caused by the intracellular protozoa\n\nLeishmania\n\n, which are transmitted to humans through the bites of infected female phlebotomine sandflies. The parasites replicate within host macrophages and can cause cutaneous, mucocutaneous, or visceral disease depending on the species and host response.\n\nA helminth acquired through freshwater exposure\n\nis incorrect because this describes schistosomiasis, which is contracted when cercariae penetrate the skin during contact with contaminated freshwater. It typically presents with systemic symptoms such as fever, rash, eosinophilia, or later urogenital or hepatic involvement, not painless, indurated skin ulcers. The clinical presentation and histology in this case are not consistent with a helminthic infection.\n\nA protozoa spread by tsetse fly bites\n\nis incorrect as this refers to African trypanosomiasis (sleeping sickness), caused by\n\nTrypanosoma brucei\n\n. It is endemic to sub-Saharan Africa and presents with systemic features such as fever, lymphadenopathy, and neurological signs in later stages. The patient has no neurological symptoms, and the exposure was in Brazil, making this diagnosis unlikely.\n\nA rickettsia spread by tick bites\n\nis incorrect because rickettsial infections, such as Rocky mountain spotted fever or other spotted fever group illnesses, typically present with systemic symptoms including fever, headache, and a widespread petechial or maculopapular rash. The patient is systemically well and afebrile, and there is no mention of recent tick exposure. The presence of painless, indurated ulcers following insect bites in Brazil is not consistent with the typical presentation of rickettsial disease.\n\nA virus spread by mosquito bites\n\nis incorrect because viral infections transmitted by mosquitoes (such as dengue, Zika, or chikungunya) usually present with systemic symptoms including fever, arthralgia, and rash. They do not cause slowly progressive, painless cutaneous ulcers or intracellular protozoa on biopsy. The clinical and histological features do not support a viral aetiology."
