      "explanation": "Supportive therapy is the mainstay of treatment in Cryptosporidium diarrhoea\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nSupportive therapy\n\n. Cryptosporidium is a protozoan parasite that causes diarrhoea, especially in immunocompromised patients such as those with HIV. The primary management of cryptosporidiosis is supportive care, including rehydration and electrolyte replacement. Antiretroviral therapy to improve the immune status of HIV-infected individuals can also help control the infection.\n\nDiscussing the incorrect options:\n\nMetronidazole\n\nis an antiprotozoal and antibacterial medication used for a variety of infections, but it is not effective against Cryptosporidium.\n\nSulfadiazine + pyrimethamine\n\nis typically used for the treatment of toxoplasmosis, another opportunistic infection seen in immunocompromised patients. However, this combination does not have activity against Cryptosporidium.\n\nRifampicin + ethambutol + clarithromycin\n\nare antibiotics used in the treatment of Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) infection, which can also occur in patients with advanced HIV disease. These drugs do not have efficacy against Cryptosporidium.\n\nFinally,\n\nCo-trimoxazole\n\n(trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) is commonly used as prophylaxis against Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia in HIV-infected individuals and to treat certain bacterial infections. Although co-trimoxazole has broad antimicrobial activity, it does not work against Cryptosporidium."
