      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Mycoplasma pneumoniae",
      "explanation": "Pneumonia in an alcoholic - Klebsiella\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nKlebsiella pneumoniae\n\n. This organism is a common cause of pneumonia in individuals with alcohol dependence. The classic radiographic presentation includes consolidation, often with abscess or cavitation formation, typically in the upper lobes. In addition to being a common pathogen in this patient population, the clinical and radiographic features described are characteristic of Klebsiella pneumonia.\n\nStreptococcus pneumoniae\n\nis an incorrect option. While it is the most common cause of community-acquired pneumonia overall, it does not typically produce cavitation on chest x-ray. Its presentation more commonly involves lobar consolidation without cavitation.\n\nLegionella pneumophilia\n\nis also incorrect. Legionnaires' disease can indeed present with nonspecific symptoms such as fever and malaise, but it tends to occur more commonly in patients who have been exposed to contaminated water sources. It does not characteristically cause cavitary lesions on imaging.\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\n, while capable of causing cavitary lung disease, is not the most likely causative organism in this case. Staphylococcal pneumonia more commonly occurs following influenza infection or in patients with severe immunosuppression, neither of which are mentioned in this scenario.\n\nLastly,\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae\n\nis another incorrect option. Mycoplasma pneumonia tends to affect younger individuals and presents as atypical (or 'walking') pneumonia with gradual onset of symptoms over several weeks. It usually causes diffuse interstitial infiltrates rather than lobar consolidation or cavitation on chest x-ray.\n\nTherefore, given the patient's history of alcohol dependence and his clinical and radiographic findings,\n\nKlebsiella pneumoniae\n\nis the most likely causative organism for his condition."
