      "explanation": "Chlamydia\n\nDoxycycline\n\nis now the first-line treatment for\n\nChlamydia\n\n. Previously, azithromycin was used, but due to increasing rates of coinfection with\n\nMycoplasma genitalium,\n\n, which has rising levels of macrolide resistance, doxycycline is now the first line recommended therapy.\n\nAmoxicillin\n\nis used to treat many infections, including chest and urinary infections. It may be used in the treatment of\n\nChlamydia\n\nin pregnancy, where doxycycline is contraindicated. However, azithromycin would still be preferred in pregnancy in these circumstances. In non-pregnant patients the drug of choice remains doxycycline.\n\nAzithromycin\n\nis one option that may be used if doxycycline is contraindicated, such as in pregnancy. However, doxycycline is preferred as a first-line treatment due to concerns about\n\nMycoplasma genitalium\n\n, which often coexists in patients with chlamydia and shows rising levels of macrolide resistance.\n\nCeftriaxone\n\nis used as the first-line treatment for gonorrhoeal infection, not chlamydia. It is given as a single intramuscular dose.\n\nCiprofloxacin\n\nis a fluoroquinolone antibiotic. It has no role in the treatment of chlamydia infection."
