      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Mumps",
      "explanation": "The most common causes of viral meningitis in adults are enteroviruses\n\nThis is a typical presentation of viral meningitis, the most common cause of which is the non-polio enteroviruses (e.g. Coxsackie virus, Echovirus). The presence of headache, with nausea and vomiting, along with examination findings of nuchal rigidity (inability to flex the neck / reduced range of motion of the neck), Kernig's sign (hip and knee flexed to 90º and then the extension of the knee cause pain), and Brudzinski's sign (forced flexion of knee causes reflex flexion of hips) all point towards meningism. The findings of the lumbar puncture are typical for 'aseptic meningitis' of which enteroviruses are the most common cause.\n\nLymphocytic choriomeningitis virus: this is a rodent-borne arenavirus and infection is usually linked to exposure to their urine and stool. This is unlikely given this patients occupational background.\n\nMumps: before the arrival of vaccinations, Mumps was a very common cause of aseptic/viral meningitis; however, this is no longer the case. Furthermore, this individual received his childhood vaccinations making this even more unlikely.\n\nNeisseria meningitidis\n\n: the lumbar puncture findings are certainly not typical of a bacterial infection. If it were\n\nNeisseria meningitidis\n\ncausing meningitis, you would expect a low CSF glucose, neutrophil predominance and high protein, and gram-negative diplococci on gram stain.\n\nHerpes simplex virus type 1: this usually causes viral encephalitis (HSV-2 generally causes more viral meningitis). Furthermore, HSV-related meningitis is more likely to occur with primary infections and this individual has no evidence of primary HSV infection (oral/genital ulcers); it is much less common in those with asymptomatic primary HSV infections. An HSV encephalitis typically presents with altered mental status and/or behavioural changes."
