      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Yeast cells",
      "explanation": "Bacterial vaginosis - overgrowth of predominately\n\nThe patient has presented with a typical history of bacterial vaginosis. There is a white homogenous discharge which is foul-smelling. Bacterial vaginosis occurs where there is an overgrowth of anaerobic bacteria, leading to a loss of lactic acid-producing lactobacilli. This leads to a rise in pH to above 4.5.  Whilst bacterial vaginosis is not a sexually transmitted infection. It is more commonly seen in people who are sexually active. The most commonly seen organism is\n\nGardnerella vaginalis\n\na peptidoglycan walled coccobacillus which can appear as both gram-positive or occasionally gram-negative due to its very thin wall.\n\nGram-negative diplococci would represent\n\nNeisseria gonorrhoea\n\nin a question about vaginal discharge. Whilst the patient is sexually active there are more pointers to suggest that this is bacterial vaginosis. Gonorrhoea infection would not change the pH in this way.\n\nIntracellular gram-negative bacteria are suggestive of\n\nChlamydia trachomatis\n\n. As above whilst the patient is stated to be sexually active. The history is more typical of bacterial vaginosis.\n\nLactobacilli make up the lactic acid-producing bacteria that normally lower the vaginal pH. These are the cells which would be seen less or absent under the microscope in bacterial vaginosis.\n\nYeast cells would be seen in a\n\nCandida albicans\n\ninfection. Typically candida infections manifest with vaginal itching and a thicker and less homogenous white discharge."
