      "explanation": "Aerosolized pentamidine is an alternative treatment for\n\nAerosolized pentamidine prophylaxis for\n\nPneumocystis jirovecii\n\npneumonia in patients with the acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) may often not be effective. Bullous changes and pulmonary cysts can form in lung apices from repetitive inflammation and cytotoxicity of HIV on the pulmonary macrophages. These changes persist in spite of prophylaxis against recurring pneumocystis infection with aerosolized pentamidine which increases the risk of spontaneous pneumothorax.\n\nGiven the acute presentation, it is less likely to be community-acquired pneumonia or empyema.\n\nEmpyema is a collection of pus in the pleural cavity which can result in fevers, chest pain, difficulty in breathing and productive coughing. This is most commonly caused by pneumonia but can be due to tuberculosis, bronchiectasis, chest surgery and trauma.\n\nFlail chest occurs due to trauma where a rib cage segment is broken and becomes completely detached from the chest wall resulting in chest pain and difficulty in breathing.\n\nWhilst these patients can get reactivation of latent TB, this would present with other symptoms such as fevers, chills, night sweats, cough and even haemoptysis in severe cases."
