      "explanation": "Live vaccines given by injection may be either given concomitantly or a minimum interval of 4 weeks apart to prevent risk of immunological interference\n\nGive both at the same visit or ≥4 weeks apart\n\n: This is correct. UK guidelines state that injectable live vaccines (such as MMR and varicella) can either be administered concomitantly (on the same day) or, if not given together, must be spaced by a minimum of 4 weeks. Giving them too close together but on different days may result in immunological interference, potentially reducing vaccine efficacy. This principle applies specifically to live parenteral vaccines.\n\nAdminister with a minimum interval of 1 week\n\n: A one-week interval is insufficient between two injectable live vaccines not administered simultaneously. Studies have shown that shorter intervals may lead to suboptimal immune responses due to interference between the attenuated viruses.\n\nGive MMR first, then varicella after 2 weeks\n\n: Two weeks is still too short an interval for separate administration of injectable live vaccines according to UK and international guidance. The recommended gap is at least four weeks if they are not given together.\n\nSeparate by at least 6 months\n\n: There is no requirement for such a prolonged interval between injectable live vaccines; this would unnecessarily delay full immunisation and leave the individual susceptible for longer than necessary.\n\nNo specific scheduling required for live vaccines\n\n: This statement is incorrect as there are clear recommendations regarding timing when administering multiple injectable live-attenuated vaccines. Failure to adhere could compromise vaccine effectiveness."
