      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Mycoplasma pneumoniae",
      "explanation": "Staphylococcus aureus\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\nis correct. Staphylococcus aureus is a frequent cause of secondary bacterial pneumonia following influenza A infection. The presence of cavitating lesions adds further weight to this diagnosis.\n\nChlamydia psittaci\n\nis incorrect. Psittacosis is an infection of birds caused by the bacterium\n\nC. psittaci\n\n. The disease has been described in many species of birds, particularly in parrots, parakeets, budgerigars and cockatiels. It typically causes flu-like symptoms (fever, headache, muscle aches) but can lead to severe pneumonia. The pneumonia is usually atypical with perihilar patchy reticular or reticulonodular opacities. The presence of lobar pneumonia and cavitation, in this case, makes psittacosis an unlikely diagnosis. Although she keeps a parrot as a pet, it is important to look at the overall picture.\n\nCoxiella burnetii\n\nis incorrect. Q fever is a disease caused by the bacteria\n\nCoxiella burnetii\n\n. This bacteria naturally infects some animals, such as goats, sheep, and cattle. The most common manifestation is flu-like symptoms however it can progress to an atypical pneumonia.  The presence of lobar pneumonia and cavitation, in this case, makes Q fever an unlikely diagnosis.\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae\n\nis incorrect. This pathogen presents with a flu-like illness, atypical pneumonia, and red cell agglutination on blood film analysis. The agglutination of red cells can result in haemolytic anaemia. The presence of lobar pneumonia and cavitation, in this case, makes\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae\n\nan unlikely diagnosis.\n\nVaricella zoster virus\n\nis incorrect. Varicella pneumonia is the most common and serious complication of chickenpox infection in adults. Secondary bacterial infections can also occur. The classic chest X-ray appearances are of multiple 5-10 mm ill-defined nodules that may be confluent and fleeting.  The chest x-ray appearances and absence of a rash, in this case, make Varicella pneumonia an unlikely diagnosis."
