      "id": 478,
      "question": "A 27-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with a blurred vision, slurred speech and weakness of both her arms. She reports that these symptoms started the previous day and has attended now as she is having difficulty swallowing. She denies any past medical history, or current medications.\n\nOn examination, the patient is unkempt. You note multiple track marks on both arms. Vital signs are within normal limits. Examination of the cranial nerves reveals a bilateral ptosis. The patient reports diplopia bilaterally and there is impaired pupil accommodation. The gag reflex is impaired, and the patient's speech is slurred. Examination of the upper limbs reveals hypotonia, hyporeflexia and power is 3/5 bilaterally. Sensation is intact. Examination of the lower limbs is unremarkable.\n\nYou suspect the patient may have an infection with clostridium botulinum, and send blood samples for clostridium botulinum toxin.\n\nWhat is the next most appropriate step in management?",
