      "correctText": "Administer botulism antitoxin",
      "explanation": "The treatment of botulism - supportive care and botulism antitoxin\n\nThe clinical syndrome produced by botulinum toxin is an afebrile, descending, flaccid paralysis. Presenting features typically include difficulty speaking or slurred speech, blurred or double vision, and/or dysphagia. Other features include ptosis and facial muscle weakness. Without treatment, paralysis may progress to the upper limbs, trunk, lower limbs and respiratory muscles.\n\nIntravenous drug users are particularly at risk of developing infection with clostridium botulinum, through injecting contaminated drugs into skin or muscle. Botulism spores are found naturally in the environment (such as in soil), so they can contaminate supplies of drugs such as heroin.\n\nIf botulism is suspected, swift administration of botulism antitoxin is essential in order to reduce the severity of symptoms, therefore this is the next most appropriate step in management of this patient.\n\nPublic Health England recommend that in cases where there is clinical suspicion of botulism, treatment with antitoxin should not be delayed for microbiological testing.\n\nSupportive care, including serial measurements of forced vital capacity, is also essential, however on its own this is not the correct answer.\n\nClostridium botulinum\n\nis sensitive to benzylpenicillin, and therefore this is also administered in patients with abscess formation at the injection site. However, this is not the most essential step in management.\n\nTetanus immunoglobulin is used in the management of tetanus, caused by\n\nclostridium tetani\n\n."
