      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Campylobacter",
      "explanation": "Bacillus cereus\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nBacillus cereus\n\n. This gram-positive, spore-forming bacterium is commonly associated with food poisoning from improperly stored and reheated rice dishes. The organism produces two types of toxins: an emetic toxin that causes vomiting (predominant symptom occurring 1-5 hours after ingestion) and a diarrhoeal toxin. The spores can survive cooking and, if rice is left at room temperature, they can germinate, multiply and produce toxins. Reheating does not destroy these pre-formed toxins.\n\nEscherichia coli\n\ntypically causes food poisoning through contaminated meat products or unwashed vegetables. The incubation period is usually longer (12-72 hours), and diarrhoea is the predominant symptom rather than vomiting. While it can cause severe illness, it would be less likely in this scenario involving reheated rice.\n\nCampylobacter\n\nis commonly associated with undercooked poultry, unpasteurised milk, and contaminated water. It typically has a longer incubation period (2-5 days) and presents with diarrhoea, often bloody, along with severe abdominal pain and fever. The presentation and food history in this case do not fit with Campylobacter infection.\n\nSalmonella\n\ninfection typically occurs through contaminated eggs, poultry, or dairy products. The incubation period is usually 12-72 hours, and symptoms include diarrhoea, fever, and abdominal cramps. The rapid onset of predominantly vomiting symptoms and the association with reheated rice makes Salmonella less likely.\n\nShigella\n\nis transmitted through the faecal-oral route and is commonly associated with poor hygiene rather than reheated food. It typically causes dysentery with bloody diarrhoea, fever, and abdominal pain, with an incubation period of 1-7 days. The clinical presentation and food history make this an unlikely causative organism in this case."
