      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Flucloxacillin",
      "explanation": "Human bites, like animal bites, should be treated with co-amoxiclav\n\nDoxycycline and metronidazole are an acceptable treatment of human bites if the patient is penicillin-allergic, however as this is not stated we assume that he has no allergies.\n\nCo-amoxiclav is the correct answer.\n\nAwaiting the swab results is not the correct answer. Human bites like animal bites are treated with co-amoxiclav. Antibiotic prophylaxis is recommended for: hand/foot/facial injuries, deep puncture wounds or wounds requiring surgical debridement, wounds involving joints/tendons/ligaments, suspected fractures, and in patients who are immunosuppressed, diabetic, cirrhotic, asplenic, or elderly. Antibiotic treatment is required for bites which look infected. In this case, the bite both appears infected (red, swollen), and is on the hand so would require antibiotics regardless of infection.\n\nFlucloxacillin is the treatment for cellulitis.\n\nClindamycin and ciprofloxacin are used in the treatment of severe human bites if penicillin-allergic."
