      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Tuberculoid leprosy",
      "explanation": "Tuberculoid leprosy is suggested by a limited number of skin lesions, hypaesthesia and hair loss\n\nThe presence of hypoesthesia with raised lesions along the arm raises the suspicion of leprosy as the underlying diagnosis. This is further evidenced by the presence of thickened cord-like structures that are tender, likely representing inflamed nerve fibres involved in the disease.\n\nTuberculoid leprosy is the preferred answer as this patient has limited skin lesions with the accompanying hypoesthesia. Accompanying thickened and tender nerves also contribute towards this diagnosis.\n\nLepromatous leprosy tends to be more systemic with widespread involvement of the skin forming nodules. This can also be associated with the leonine facies ( thickened forehead with loss of eyebrows and eyelashes). Associated systemic symptoms include hepatitis, orchitis, uveitis and corneal anaesthesia.\n\nPityriasis versicolor is a yeast infection driven by the genus\n\nMalassezia\n\nand can be associated with hypo-pigmented lesions. However, this does not result in reduced sensations or thickened nerve fibres.\n\nCutaneous leishmaniasis are caused by the\n\nLeishmania\n\nparasite and are transmitted by sandflies. Raised lesions can be found but this is normally accompanied by a degree of central ulceration. Hypoesthesia is not a feature in this.\n\nYaws is a tropical disease caused by\n\nTreponema palladium pertenue\n\nand usually presents as a single lesions on the skin with associated lymphadenopathy. This is erythematous and often accompanied by a yellow crust on the surface. There is usually no accompanying sensory changes."
