      "explanation": "Pregnant women with a UTI: nitrofurantoin is first-line unless the woman is close to term\n\nNitrofurantoin is recommended for the first-line treatment in pregnant women for urinary tract infections and is the most appropriate option. In pregnant women presenting with features of a urinary tract infection, treatment should be commenced immediately. An MSU should be sent to guide the antibiotic choice but this should not delay the initial treatment.\n\nTherefore, no treatment is not a safe option.\n\nGentamicin may be considered for pyelonephritis or other infections but is not first-line.\n\nAmoxicillin is a second-line choice, as is cefalexin.\n\nTrimethoprim should be avoided as it interferes with folate metabolism (especially in the first 12 weeks)."
