      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Streptococcus pneumoniae",
      "explanation": "Pneumonia caused by\n\nStreptococcus pneumoniae\n\nis correct.\n\nStreptococcus pneumoniae\n\nis a gram-positive cocci and a common cause of community-acquired pneumonia. It is frequently associated with herpes simplex reactivation resulting in cold sores.\n\nChlamydia psittaci\n\nis incorrect. Psittacosis is an infection of birds caused by the bacterium\n\nC. psittaci\n\n. The disease has been described in many species of birds, particularly in parrots, parakeets, budgerigars and cockatiels. It typically causes flu-like symptoms (fever, headache, muscle aches) but can lead to severe pneumonia. The pneumonia is usually atypical with perihilar patchy reticular or reticulonodular opacities. The presence of lobar pneumonia in this case makes psittacosis an unlikely diagnosis.\n\nCoxiella burnetii\n\nis incorrect. Q fever is a disease caused by the bacteria\n\nCoxiella burnetii\n\n. This bacteria naturally infects some animals, such as goats, sheep, and cattle. The most common manifestation is flu-like symptoms however it can progress to an atypical pneumonia. Although the patient, in this case, is a dairy farmer, the presence of lobar pneumonia makes Q fever an unlikely diagnosis.\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae\n\nis incorrect. This pathogen presents with a flu-like illness, atypical pneumonia, and red cell agglutination on blood film analysis. The agglutination of red cells can result in haemolytic anaemia. The presence of lobar pneumonia, in this case, makes\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae\n\nan unlikely diagnosis.\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\nis incorrect. Staphylococcus aureus is an uncommon cause of pneumonia however it can occur as a secondary bacterial pneumonia following influenza A infection. It can also result in lung cavitation. It is not classically associated with herpes simplex reactivation. Given the relative rarity of this pathogen and the presence of herpes simplex reactivation,\n\nStreptococcus pneumoniae\n\nis the most likely implicated pathogen."
