      "id": 507,
      "question": "A 29-year-old woman pregnant woman is referred to ambulatory care by her general practitioner. Over six days she had felt progressively more unwell and had developed a slight sore throat. Her general practitioner had noted a fever (38.1ºC) and been worried by how pronounced her cervical lymph nodes were. She had sent Epstein Barr virus (EBV) serology, which had been returned as negative and two throat swabs had demonstrated no bacterial growth.\n\nShe is normally fit-and-well and has never taken any regular medications. She has had no recent foreign travel and no new sexual partners. Although she had eaten at a barbecue two weeks prior to getting ill she had called all the other attendees none of whom had experienced symptoms. Aside from pronounced cervical lymphadenopathy and an erythematous pharynx she has no other examination findings. A careful search reveals no rash. She is in the third trimester of her first pregnancy and so far has experienced no complications.\n\n\nHb\t138 g/l\tNa+\t149 mmol/l\nPlatelets\t211 * 109/l\tK+\t4.9 mmol/l\nWBC\t10 * 109/l\tUrea\t4.2 mmol/l\nNeuts\t3.7 * 109/l\tCreatinine\t52 µmol/l\nLymphs\t1.1 * 109/l\tCRP\t95 mg/l\nEosin\t3.2 * 109/l\t\t\n\nWhat condition is it important to investigate for that would explain her clinical presentation and the results of her investigations?",
