      "explanation": "Pneumonia in an alcoholic - Klebsiella\n\nKlebsiella pneumoniae\n\nis correct. It is important to note the patient's alcohol history. The most likely pathogen in patients with alcoholism is\n\nKlebsiella pneumoniae\n\n. Recent research has shown that alterations in the intestinal immune response as a consequence of alcohol-induced dysbiosis contribute to increased host susceptibility to Klebsiella pneumonia.\n\nKlebsiella oxytoca\n\nis incorrect. This organism is implicated in urinary tract and soft tissue infections, however, is rarely a cause of bacterial pneumonia. Furthermore, this species is not associated with excess alcohol intake.\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae\n\nis incorrect. This pathogen presents with a flu-like illness, atypical pneumonia, and red cell agglutination on blood film analysis. The agglutination of red cells can result in haemolytic anaemia. The presence of lobar pneumonia, in this case, makes\n\nMycoplasma pneumoniae\n\nan unlikely diagnosis.\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\nis incorrect. Staphylococcus aureus is an unusual cause of pneumonia however it does frequently cause secondary bacterial pneumonia following influenza A infection.\n\nStreptococcus pneumoniae\n\nis incorrect.\n\nStreptococcus pneumoniae\n\nis a gram-positive cocci and a common cause of community-acquired pneumonia. The history of alcoholism favours the diagnosis of\n\nKlebsiella pneumoniae\n\npneumonia."
