      "explanation": "Aspergilloma on x-ray may show a fungal ball cavity with a crescent sign\n\nAspergilloma on x-ray may show a fungal ball cavity with a crescent sign and is the most likely diagnosis given the clinical and radiological findings in this case. Pulmonary aspergilloma is a mass or cavity that develops as a result of a fungal infection.\n\nNon-small cell carcinoma is unlikely given the relative absence of systemic symptoms in the form of weight loss and night sweats. The history further elaborates how he was not a previous heavy smoker.\n\nMiliary tuberculosis suggests uncontrolled tuberculosis infection which can manifest as uniform deposits <3 mm in size which is evenly distributed on a plain chest radiograph.\n\nAllergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA) is a result of an exaggerated immune response to\n\nAspergillus fumigatus\n\n, which frequently occurs within patients who have asthma or cystic fibrosis. High-attenuation mucus is a radiological finding that is associated with ABPA.\n\nLobar pneumonia shows up as an ill-defined area of heightened density affecting a particular segment or lobe of the lung."
