      "explanation": "An aspergilloma may arise in a lung cavity that developed secondary to previous tuberculosis\n\nMonod's sign is similar to the air-crescent sign, which is a classic radiographic feature of air around a ball of fungus (in this case, an\n\naspergilloma\n\n). Monod's sign is more specific for an aspergilloma whereas an air-crescent sign is an improving angioinvasive aspergilloma. The difference between the two signs is picked up by acquiring a chest X-ray in the prone position. Monod's sign implies the presence of a mobile mass, and so in the prone position would shift, whereas the air-crescent sign is fixed, and implies a reduction in the size of a fixed invasive aspergillosis mass.\n\nApproximately 20% of immunocompetent individuals who had pulmonary tuberculosis, left with a cavity measuring above 2cm, develop a form of aspergilloma. Symptoms typically are chronic in immunocompetent hosts rather than more rapid and aggressive forms seen in the immunocompromised.\n\nMelioidosis\n\nis a tropical infection caused by the bacteria\n\nBurkholderia pseudomallei\n\n, it is usually found in soil and affected by direct exposure (e.g. farmers, military personnel). It can cause a severe, acute pulmonary disease with high mortality. This patient has not left the UK in two decades, and the chronic nature of her symptoms makes this diagnosis unlikely.\n\nPulmonary abscess\n\nis a possible differential, but rather than an air-crescent sign would feature a meniscus line in a cavity resembling the gas-fluid interface. An air-crescent sign is indicative of a gas-solid interface.\n\nPulmonary fibrosis\n\nusually features reticular markings on X-rays, which were not seen here.\n\nPulmonary tuberculosis\n\nrecurrence is an important difference to consider, but overall is unlikely in this case, as this patient was fully treated two decades ago. Most often, recurrence occurs in the first 1-2 years post-treatment."
