      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Adenovirus",
      "explanation": "EBV: associated malignancies:\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nEpstein-Barr virus (EBV)\n\n. EBV, also known as human herpesvirus 4, has a well-established association with nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC), particularly in Southeast Asian populations. The virus infects epithelial cells and B lymphocytes, leading to latent infection. In nasopharyngeal tissue, EBV can trigger oncogenic transformation through various mechanisms, including the expression of latent viral proteins such as LMP1 and EBNA1, which interfere with cellular signalling pathways and promote carcinogenesis. The virus is detected in nearly all cases of undifferentiated nasopharyngeal carcinoma.\n\nAdenovirus\n\nis incorrect. Whilst adenoviruses can cause upper respiratory tract infections, conjunctivitis, and gastroenteritis, they have not been associated with nasopharyngeal carcinoma. They are non-enveloped DNA viruses that typically cause self-limiting infections in immunocompetent hosts.\n\nRhinovirus\n\nis incorrect. Rhinoviruses are the predominant cause of the common cold and are responsible for many upper respiratory tract infections. They are RNA viruses belonging to the Picornaviridae family and have not been linked to any human cancers.\n\nHerpes simplex virus\n\nis incorrect. While HSV-1 and HSV-2 are members of the herpesvirus family like EBV, they are not associated with nasopharyngeal carcinoma. HSV typically causes oral and genital lesions and can cause encephalitis in severe cases, but it has not been implicated in nasopharyngeal oncogenesis.\n\nPicornavirus\n\nis incorrect. Picornaviruses are a family of RNA viruses that include rhinoviruses and enteroviruses. While they can cause various human diseases including respiratory infections, meningitis, and myocarditis, they have not been associated with the development of nasopharyngeal carcinoma or any other human cancers."
