      "explanation": "NSAIDs are a cause of acute interstitial nephritis\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nacute interstitial nephritis\n\n, of which NSAIDs are among the known causes. The patient has presented with symptoms including fever, arthralgia, a maculopapular rash, mild renal impairment and eosinophilia shortly after commencing naproxen therapy. These clinical features are indicative of acute interstitial nephritis, for which NSAIDs are recognised as a causative agent. Other drugs implicated in the development of this condition include penicillins, rifampicin, allopurinol and furosemide.\n\nAcute tubular necrosis\n\ncan occur secondary to exposure to certain medications like aminoglycosides; however, NSAIDs are not typically associated with this condition. Clinically, acute tubular necrosis differs as it does not present with eosinophilia, rash or fever but is characterised by acute kidney injury (AKI) with the presence of muddy brown casts in the urine.\n\nWhile\n\ncholesterol emboli\n\ncan cause both eosinophilia and renal impairment, there is no indication from the patient's history that they are at risk for this condition. Cholesterol emboli typically manifest following procedures such as angiography or vascular surgery and do not produce a maculopapular rash but rather livedo reticularis.\n\nLupus nephritis\n\n, part of the spectrum of renal disease associated with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), presents either as nephrotic or nephritic syndrome'or occasionally both'but not with the constellation of symptoms observed in this case. While SLE can cause acute interstitial nephritis, it is less commonly implicated than NSAIDs. There is a lack of other common symptoms of SLE, such as malar or discoid rash, photosensitivity, serositis and anaemia, making SLE unlikely.\n\nLastly,\n\nrenal tubular acidosis\n\nresults in urine alkalinisation and systemic acidosis. Although NSAIDs have been identified as rare causes of both distal and proximal renal tubular acidosis (RTA), the clinical presentation described does not align with RTA diagnosis. Patients with RTA usually present with renal calculi and osteomalacia, and if occurring in childhood, it can also result in growth retardation. None of these features are present in this patient."
