      "correctText": "CT urogram",
      "explanation": "Micturating cystography is the investigation of choice for reflux nephropathy\n\nMicturating cystography\n\n(MCUG) is correct. This patient has features of vesicoureteric reflux (VUR) with reflux nephropathy. The history of recurrent urinary tract infections (UTIs) and episodic fever with loin pain, combined with hypertension, proteinuria, and impaired renal function (elevated urea and creatinine), suggests kidney damage secondary to repeated episodes of pyelonephritis caused by VUR. The absence of leukocytes and nitrites at presentation suggests that the patient currently has no infection. MCUG is the gold standard for diagnosing VUR as it directly visualises retrograde flow of urine from the bladder into the ureters and kidneys during voiding.\n\nCT urogram\n\nis incorrect as this is primarily used in imaging the kidneys, ureters, and bladder to evaluate urological malignancies, stones, or anatomical abnormalities. It is unsuitable for detecting VUR and does not demonstrate the dynamic retrograde flow of urine from the bladder to the ureters during voiding.\n\nDimercaptosuccinic acid scan\n\n(DMSA scan) is incorrect as this detects renal tissue scarring rather than identifying retrograde urine flow. In contrast, MCUG directly visualises reflux during voiding, which aids the diagnosis of VUR. A DMSA scan could instead be considered if the diagnosis of VUR is already established and the aim is to assess the extent and severity of renal scarring.\n\nRenal ultrasound\n\nis incorrect. While this is a non-invasive and widely available imaging tool, it is more useful for identifying gross anatomical abnormalities such as hydronephrosis or renal scarring, but it cannot provide functional information about urinary reflux. Ultrasonography could instead be considered as an initial test to screen for obstructive uropathy or structural abnormalities before proceeding to MCUG, however, the focus of this question is on the diagnostic test for VUR.\n\nUrodynamic testing\n\nis incorrect as this evaluates bladder storage and voiding dynamics rather than detecting retrograde urine flow. In contrast, MCUG directly visualises urine reflux while voiding, which aids the diagnosis of VUR. Urodynamic testing could be considered if there were concerns about bladder dysfunction, such as neurogenic bladder or urinary incontinence, which is not the case here."
