      "explanation": "ATN or prerenal uraemia? In prerenal uraemia think of the kidneys holding on to sodium to preserve volume\n\nPre-renal uraemia\n\nis the correct answer because this patient's history of fluid loss, clinical dehydration, and low urine sodium (<20 mmol/L) point towards hypovolaemia causing decreased renal perfusion without intrinsic kidney damage. In pre-renal states, the kidneys avidly reabsorb sodium to preserve volume, resulting in low urine sodium concentrations. The raised urea and creatinine further support acute kidney injury due to hypoperfusion rather than parenchymal disease.\n\nAcute tubular necrosis\n\ncommonly occurs after prolonged hypoperfusion or nephrotoxic insults but typically presents with higher urine sodium (>40 mmol/L) due to impaired tubular reabsorption. The scenario here suggests an early or reversible process; there is no mention of persistent hypotension or nephrotoxin exposure that would favour ATN.\n\nObstructive uropathy\n\nusually presents with a history of lower urinary tract symptoms, palpable bladder, or bilateral hydronephrosis on imaging—none of which are described here. Obstruction does not cause low urine sodium and would be less likely in a patient presenting primarily with gastrointestinal losses.\n\nRapidly progressive glomerulonephritis\n\noften presents with features such as haematuria, proteinuria, hypertension, and possibly systemic symptoms (e.g., rash, joint pain). There is no evidence provided for glomerular involvement or an autoimmune process in this case.\n\nAcute interstitial nephritis\n\ngenerally results from drug reactions or infections and is associated with pyuria, eosinophiluria, and sometimes a rash or fever—not simply dehydration and gastrointestinal fluid loss. The laboratory findings do not support this diagnosis."
