      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy",
      "explanation": "ADPKD is associated with mitral valve prolaspe\n\nMitral valve prolapse\n\nis the correct answer. This patient likely has autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease.  They are presenting with flank pain, haematuria, hypertension and palpable masses suggestive of large cystic kidneys on examination. Their family history is also indicative of the autosomal dominant nature of the condition. Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease is the most common inherited form of kidney disease. Classically extra renal manifestations are seen in patients with severe and progressive disease. One extra-renal manifestation of the condition is mitral valve prolapse. Although extra-renal manifestations are more common affecting the liver resulting in hepatic cysts and hepatomegaly.\n\nAortic regurgitation\n\nis incorrect. Aortic regurgitation is commonly seen in connective tissue disorders and severe hypertension. Although polycystic kidney disease can result in aortic root dilation, in rare cases predisposing to aortic regurgitation this is not as commonly seen as mitral valve prolapse.\n\nBicuspid aortic valve\n\nis incorrect. This is seen in Turner syndrome which is a chromosomal abnormality characterised by one X chromosome or the deletion of one x chromosome. This is not seen in polycystic kidney disease.\n\nDilated cardiomyopathy\n\nis incorrect. Various aetiologies exist in causing dilated cardiomyopathy although the most common cause is idiopathic. In saying that, autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease as seen in this patient is not a cause of dilated cardiomyopathy.\n\nHypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy\n\nis incorrect. This is also an autosomal dominant condition affecting the muscular tissue of the heart resulting in diastolic dysfunction. This is not typically seen in patients with polycystic kidney disease."
