      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis",
      "explanation": "Diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis, causes:\n\nDiffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis\n\nis correct. The most likely diagnosis is post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN), based on the history of a sore throat 3 weeks ago, followed by malaise, facial puffiness, dark-coloured urine, hypertension, haematuria with red cell casts, elevated urea and creatinine, and low C3 with normal C4. PSGN causes diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis through an acute inflammatory process involving nearly all glomeruli, with endothelial and mesangial proliferation and neutrophil infiltration. This results in the nephritic syndrome seen here: haematuria, hypertension, oedema, and impaired renal function.\n\nFocal segmental glomerulosclerosis\n\nis incorrect because it typically causes nephrotic syndrome with heavy proteinuria, hypoalbuminaemia, and oedema, not the nephritic features seen in this case. This patient has haematuria, red cell casts, hypertension, low C3, and a recent streptococcal infection, all features consistent with diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis. FSGS involves sclerosis in some glomeruli (focal) and in parts of affected glomeruli (segmental), and is more commonly linked to conditions such as HIV, heroin use, or idiopathic podocyte injury. It would be more likely if the patient had persistent nephrotic-range proteinuria without haematuria or complement abnormalities.\n\nMembranoproliferative glomerulonephritis\n\nis incorrect because it usually presents with a mixed nephritic–nephrotic syndrome, persistent low C3 and C4 levels, and occurs in association with chronic infections (e.g. hepatitis C), autoimmune diseases (e.g. systemic lupus erythematosus), or monoclonal gammopathies. This patient’s acute presentation, with a recent streptococcal infection, isolated low C3, and normal C4, is typical of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis rather than MPGN. MPGN would be considered if there were chronic proteinuria and haematuria with low levels of both complement components and signs of systemic disease.\n\nMembranous glomerulonephritis\n\nis incorrect because it presents with nephrotic syndrome, heavy proteinuria (> 3.5 g/day), hypoalbuminaemia, and oedema, without significant haematuria or hypertension. MGN results from subepithelial immune complex deposits, causing glomerular basement membrane thickening, not the diffuse inflammatory changes of PSGN. This patient’s haematuria, red cell casts, hypertension, low C3, and recent streptococcal infection are characteristic of a nephritic syndrome. MGN would be more likely with a gradual onset of peripheral oedema and frothy urine, without infection history, and often in association with autoimmune disease, malignancy, or NSAID use.\n\nMinimal change disease\n\nis incorrect because it causes a pure nephrotic syndrome, abrupt heavy proteinuria, hypoalbuminaemia, and generalised oedema, without haematuria, hypertension, or complement abnormalities. In contrast, this patient has haematuria, red cell casts, hypertension, and low C3 following a streptococcal infection, indicating an acute nephritic syndrome. MCD would be more likely in a child with sudden periorbital and peripheral oedema, frothy urine, normal blood pressure, and no haematuria or hypocomplementaemia, none of which are present here."
