      "id": 37,
      "question": "A 43-year-old man is referred to renal clinic after proteinuria was incidentally identified on urinalysis. He is systemically well with no fevers, night sweats or weight loss. He has no past medical history and is on no medications.\n\nOn examination, there is peripheral oedema to his knees. Chest auscultation is normal and his jugular venous pulse is not elevated. Abdominal examination is unremarkable.\n\nUrinalysis:\n\n\nNitrites\tnegative\nLeucocytes\tnegative\nProtein\t+++\nBlood\tnegative\nGlucose\tnegative\n\nBlood tests:\n\n\nHb\t141 g/L\tMale: (135-180)\nFemale: (115 - 160)\nPlatelets\t189 * 109/L\t(150 - 400)\nWBC\t4.4 * 109/L\t(4.0 - 11.0)\nNa+\t138 mmol/L\t(135 - 145)\nK+\t4.2 mmol/L\t(3.5 - 5.0)\nUrea\t5 mmol/L\t(2.0 - 7.0)\nCreatinine\t88 µmol/L\t(55 - 120)\nCRP\t4 mg/L\t(< 5)\nBilirubin\t12 µmol/L\t(3 - 17)\nALP\t88 u/L\t(30 - 100)\nALT\t32 u/L\t(3 - 40)\nÎ³GT\t44 u/L\t(8 - 60)\nAlbumin\t24 g/L\t(35 - 50)\n\nFurther tests:\n\n\nHepatitis B sAg\tnegative\nHepatitis C Ab\tnegative\nHIV\tnegative\nAnti-nuclear antibody\tnegative\nSerum protein electrophoresis\tnormal\n\nA chest x-ray is normal.\n\nA renal biopsy demonstrates a thickened basement membrane with subepithelial electron dense deposits on electron microscopy.\n\nGiven the clinical history, which antibody is most likely to be positive?",
