      "correctText": "Anti-glomerular basement membrane antibodies",
      "explanation": "Idiopathic membranous glomerulonephritis is related to anti-phospholipase A2 antibodies\n\nAnti-phospholipase A2 is the correct answer. This man has likely idiopathic membranous glomerulonephritis (GN) as evidenced by nephrotic syndrome (proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia and oedema), typical renal biopsy findings and negative testing for common secondary causes such has hepatitis and HIV. Idiopathic membranous GN is related to anti-phospholipase A2.\n\nDouble-stranded DNA is incorrect. While class V lupus nephritis can cause membranous GN, a negative anti-nuclear antibody make this diagnosis very unlikely.\n\nMPO-ANCA is incorrect. Patients with vasculitis tend to be systemically unwell, may have raised inflammatory markers and the renal picture often involves a nephritic picture (haematuria and proteinuria) and deranged renal function. Furthermore, there may be additional features of active disease involving other systems.\n\nPR3-ANCA is incorrect. Again, typically someone with an ANCA vasculitis will not present with a pure nephrotic syndrome and may have other extra-renal features e.g. pulmonary haemorrhage, sinusitis, arthritis, fever, night sweats and weight loss. Additionally, normal inflammatory markers decrease the likelihood.\n\nAnti-glomerular basement membrane antibodies is incorrect. This is classically associated with a pulmonary renal syndrome, which is not the clinical picture in this case."
