      "explanation": "G\n\nIgG deposits\n\nare correct. The diagnosis is anti-glomerular basement membrane (GBM) disease (previously known as Goodpasture's syndrome) is a rare type of small-vessel vasculitis associated with both pulmonary haemorrhage and rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis. It is caused by anti-glomerular basement membrane (anti-GBM) antibodies against type IV collagen. Goodpasture's syndrome is more common in men and has a bimodal age distribution. Risk factors for pulmonary haemorrhage include lower respiratory tract infection, smoking, and pulmonary oedema.\n\nIgA immune complexes\n\nare present in IgA nephropathy (also known as Berger's disease). It classically presents as macroscopic haematuria in young people following an upper respiratory tract infection.\n\nLongitudinal splitting of the lamina densa\n\nis seen on renal biopsy in Alports syndrome. This is usually inherited in an X-linked dominant pattern, affecting males more than females. It is due to a defect in the gene that codes for type IV collagen resulting in an abnormal glomerular-basement membrane (GBM).\n\nEpithelial crescents in Bowman's capsule\n\nare seen in granulomatosis with polyangiitis. This is an autoimmune condition associated with necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis, affecting both the upper and lower respiratory tract as well as the kidneys.\n\nSpike and dome appearance\n\nis seen in membranous glomerulonephritis. This is the commonest type of glomerulonephritis in adults and usually presents with nephrotic syndrome or proteinuria."
