      "correctText": "Class III: focal (and segmental) proliferative glomerulonephritis",
      "explanation": "Diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis is the most common and severe form of renal disease in SLE patients\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nClass IV: diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis\n\n. Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) is a multisystem autoimmune disease that can affect the kidneys, leading to lupus nephritis. According to the World Health Organisation (WHO) classification, Class IV or diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis is the most common and severe form of lupus nephritis. It is characterised by diffuse global involvement (>50%) of glomeruli with endocapillary hypercellularity and subendothelial immune complex deposits.\n\nClass II: mesangial glomerulonephritis\n\nis incorrect as it's less common and less severe than Class IV. It involves only the mesangial cells in the kidney which results in mild clinical manifestations.\n\nClass III: focal (and segmental) proliferative glomerulonephritis\n\nis also incorrect. Although it's more common than class II, it's still less frequent compared to class IV. This type of SLE renal disease involves fewer than half of the glomeruli and generally has a better prognosis than class IV.\n\nClass V: diffuse membranous glomerulonephritis\n\nis an incorrect option too. Despite being quite common, it's not as prevalent as Class IV. It's characterised by thickening of the capillary wall due to subepithelial immune complex deposits.\n\nFinally,\n\nClass VI: sclerosing glomerulonephritis\n\n, which represents an advanced or chronic stage of lupus nephritis, is also an incorrect choice for this question. This stage signifies irreversible damage and sclerosis in more than 90% of glomeruli but isn't typically seen at presentation; hence, it doesn't represent the most common type of SLE-associated renal disease."
