      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Pleural cysts",
      "explanation": "ADPKD is associated with hepatomegaly (due to hepatic cysts)\n\nThis patient has presented with multiple features consistent with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD), including flank discomfort, enlarged kidneys, and haematuria, along with a strong family history of a renal condition. The diagnosis is confirmed by the presence of over 10 cysts in both kidneys on ultrasound. ADPKD is associated with several common extra-renal complications, including cysts in other organs, such as the liver. Patients can develop multiple hepatic cysts, leading to liver enlargement,\n\nhepatomegaly\n\n. Unlike kidney cysts, which are primarily associated with mutations in the PKD1 or PKD2 genes, liver cysts in ADPKD have a different genetic basis and mechanism of formation. Liver cysts in ADPKD are typically associated with mutations in the PRKCSH or SEC63 genes.\n\nADPKD is known to be linked with various cardiac complications, including aortic root dilatation and thoracic aorta dissection. However, there have been few reported cases of the condition being associated with\n\nabdominal aortic aneurysm\n\n(AAA), and these incidents are likely attributable to concurrent hypertension, a common feature of ADPKD. In contrast, ADPKD is strongly correlated with the development of intracranial aneurysms, with patients having approximately a fivefold greater risk compared to the general population. However, this heightened risk does not extend to AAA. The underlying pathophysiology and the reasons for variations in aneurysm site risk remain unclear.\n\nSeveral genetic syndromic disorders, such as Hippel-Lindau (VHL) syndrome, multiple endocrine neoplasia (MEN), and neurofibromatosis, are known to be linked with\n\nphaeochromocytomas\n\n. However, there is no confirmed association between ADPKD and this uncommon adrenal tumour.\n\nThe formation of non-renal cysts is commonly associated with ADPKD with cysts development in the liver, spleen and ovaries all being linked to the disease.\n\nPleural cysts\n\nhowever remain very rare in patients with ADPKD.\n\nUveitis\n\nis an ocular manifestation of various systemic syndromes and diseases including inflammatory (e.g. inflammatory bowel disease), vasculitic (e.g. Kawasaki disease) and arthritic conditions (e.g. rheumatoid arthritis) however there is no established link between ADPKD and uveitis."
