      "explanation": "Alport's syndrome - X-linked dominant (in the majority)\n\nThe correct answer is X-linked dominant.\n\nAlport's syndrome is inherited in an X-linked dominant fashion in 85% of cases. The condition is caused by a defect in the gene which codes for type IV collagen resulting in an abnormal glomerular-basement membrane. The disease is more severe in males with females rarely developing renal failure. The patient in this vignette presents classically for this condition, with childhood progressive renal failure becoming dialysis dependent in mid-life.\n\nExamples of cases of Alport's syndrome inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion are rarely reported. However, this question asks for the mechanism of inheritance in the majority of cases.\n\n10-15% of cases of Alport's syndrome are inherited in an autosomal recessive fashion. However, this question asks for the mechanism of inheritance in the majority of cases.\n\nMitochondrial disorders are inherited down the maternal line, therefore, males with mitochondrial disease are not at risk of transmitting the disorder. Alport's syndrome is not a mitochondrial disorder. One example of a mitochondrial disorder is Leber hereditary optic neuropathy, which causes bilateral visual loss.\n\nX-linked recessive inheritance is a mode of inheritance in which there is a mutation on the X chromosome which causes the disease in males only as they only have one X chromosome. Females with one affected X chromosome are carriers. Typical examples of X-lined recessive disorders include red-green colour blindness and haemophilia A and B. Alport's syndrome is not inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern."
