      "explanation": "Bicalutamide is an androgen recept blocker\n\nBicalutamide is sometimes used in the management of prostate cancer. It is described as an\n\nandrogen receptor blocker\n\n. It is non-steroidal. A combination of approaches is often used with regards to treatment for prostate cancer, involving hormonal treatment, radiotherapy, chemotherapy and prostatectomy.\n\nAbiraterone is an example of an\n\nandrogen synthesis blocker\n\n, inhibiting enzymes needed for production. It is generally used as an option for hormone-relapsed metastatic prostate cancer, in patients who have no/mild symptoms after anti-androgen therapy has failed.\n\nGoserelin is an example of a\n\ngonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonist\n\n. This acts to ultimately downregulate sex hormones. It can cause an initial flare in testosterone levels and so is initially co-prescribed with an anti-androgen.\n\nMore recently,\n\ngonadotrophin-releasing hormone antagonists\n\nsuch as abarelix have also been used in the management of prostate cancer. Antagonising GnRH allows for quick suppression of testosterone without the initial flare seen when using agonists.\n\nCyproterone acetate is another drug used in the management of prostate cancer, exhibiting\n\nprogestogenic activity\n\n, as well as steroidal and antiandrogenic effects. It is used less commonly since the more widespread use of non-steroidal drugs such as bicalutamide."
