      "correctText": "Mitral stenosis",
      "explanation": "ADPKD is associated with mitral valve prolaspe\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nMitral valve prolapse\n\n. Adult polycystic kidney disease (APKD) is a genetic disorder characterised by the development of numerous cysts in the kidneys. It is associated with various extrarenal manifestations, including cardiovascular abnormalities. The most common cardiac abnormality associated with APKD is mitral valve prolapse, occurring in 26% of patients according to some studies. This condition occurs when the two valve flaps of the mitral valve do not close smoothly or evenly and bulge (prolapse) into the left atrium.\n\nDilated cardiomyopathy\n\nis not typically associated with APKD. Dilated cardiomyopathy refers to a disease of the heart muscle where it becomes stretched and thin leading to the inability to pump blood efficiently. While there are many causes for dilated cardiomyopathy including genetic predisposition, it's not commonly seen in patients with APKD.\n\nMitral stenosis\n\nand\n\nAortic stenosis\n\nare also not typically associated with APKD. Mitral stenosis refers to narrowing of the mitral valve which restricts blood flow from the left atrium to the left ventricle, while aortic stenosis refers to narrowing of the aortic valve which restricts blood flow from the left ventricle to the aorta. These conditions are more commonly related to rheumatic heart disease or age-related degenerative changes rather than APKD.\n\nLastly,\n\nRenal bruit secondary to renal artery stenosis\n\nis not commonly found in APKD either. A renal bruit would suggest turbulent flow due to narrowing of one or both renal arteries, often caused by fibromuscular dysplasia or atherosclerosis. Although hypertension is common in APKD due to activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system by cyst compression, this does not usually lead to renal artery stenosis or an audible bruit on examination."
