      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Glomerular sclerosis",
      "explanation": "Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, causes:\n\nGlomerular crescents\n\nis correct. This patient’s one-week history of worsening fatigue, haematuria, haemoptysis, blood-tinged nasal discharge, and chronic sinusitis indicates a systemic vasculitis affecting both kidneys, lungs, and upper respiratory tract, features characteristic of granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA). The urinalysis shows red cell casts and proteinuria, suggesting active glomerulonephritis, and the raised creatinine and urea confirm acute kidney injury. Palpable purpura indicates small-vessel involvement. GPA typically causes necrotising crescentic glomerulonephritis, in which glomerular crescents—proliferation of parietal epithelial cells in Bowman’s space due to severe capillary wall damage—are the hallmark finding. These crescents are associated with rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (RPGN), the pathological correlate of this presentation. The systemic involvement, acute deterioration, and biopsy pattern make glomerular crescents the most likely finding.\n\nGlomerular sclerosis\n\nis incorrect because it reflects chronic, irreversible scarring of glomeruli and is inconsistent with the patient’s acute, rapidly progressive illness. The patient has a one-week history of worsening symptoms, red cell casts, and an abrupt rise in creatinine, all indicating active glomerular inflammation rather than longstanding damage. Glomerular sclerosis involves extracellular matrix deposition and obliteration of capillary lumens, typically seen in chronic diseases like diabetic nephropathy, hypertensive nephrosclerosis, or advanced focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS). These conditions present with slow renal decline, persistent proteinuria (often nephrotic range), and relatively inactive urinary sediment. This option may be correct in a patient with a gradual onset of symptoms over months to years and no systemic vasculitic features.\n\nImmune complex deposits\n\nis incorrect because GPA is a pauci-immune vasculitis, meaning it shows minimal or no immune complex deposition on immunofluorescence. This patient’s presentation with pulmonary-renal syndrome, red cell casts, purpura, and chronic sinusitis aligns with GPA, which causes necrotising crescentic glomerulonephritis without immune complex formation. Instead, immune complex deposits are typical in diseases like lupus nephritis, post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, IgA nephropathy, and membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis. Those conditions often present more insidiously and have other distinguishing features, such as a malar rash or arthritis in lupus, or timing relative to infections in IgA nephropathy or PSGN. In such cases, biopsy would show granular deposits of immunoglobulins and complement. This option may be correct if the patient had features suggestive of SLE or a different immune complex disease.\n\nLinear IgG deposition\n\nis incorrect because it is characteristic of anti-glomerular basement membrane (anti-GBM) disease, not GPA. Although anti-GBM disease can present with haemoptysis, haematuria, and rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, it does not explain the chronic sinusitis or upper airway involvement seen in this patient. GPA is an ANCA-associated vasculitis that produces pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis, with minimal or no immunoglobulin deposition on immunofluorescence. Linear IgG staining, caused by anti-GBM antibodies targeting type IV collagen, is specific to Goodpasture’s syndrome. This option may be correct if the patient had pulmonary-renal syndrome without upper respiratory tract symptoms like sinusitis or epistaxis.\n\nLinear IgM deposition\n\nis incorrect because it is not a recognised feature of GPA or any specific glomerular pathology, and GPA is pauci-immune by definition. IgM deposition generally reflects chronic or nonspecific glomerular injury rather than acute inflammation. The patient's acute systemic illness, with red cell casts, haematuria, purpura, and pulmonary symptoms, is not consistent with the chronic disease patterns where linear IgM may be seen. Weak, segmental linear IgM staining can occur in hypertensive nephrosclerosis, diabetic nephropathy, or other chronic glomerular conditions without crescents or systemic vasculitic features. This option may be correct in a patient with longstanding renal impairment without signs of systemic vasculitis or rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis."
