      "question": "A 56-year-old man presented to the emergency department with a one week history of worsening abdominal pain, nausea and diarrhoea. He has a background of end-stage renal failure and is receiving peritoneal dialysis.\n\nHe is reviewed by the medical registrar on-call, he has a tender abdomen with guarding throughout on examination. There is a raised lactate on his venous blood gas.\n\nAn urgent erect plain chest radiograph is requested and there is no evidence of consolidation or pneumoperitoneum. Intravenous antibiotics are commenced and a sample of the peritoneal dialysis fluid is sent off to microbiology for cell count, gram stain, culture and sensitivity.\n\nWhich organism is the most likely cause of his peritonitis?",
