      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Tophi gout",
      "explanation": "Calciphylaxis lesion are intensely painful, purpuric patches with an area of black necrotic tissue that may form bullae, ulcerate, and leave a hard, firm eschar\n\nThe patient's lesions match the description of calciphylaxis, a rare complication of end-stage renal failure. These painful necrotic skin lesions are linked with hypercalcaemia and hyperphosphataemia, as seen in this patient, and normally affect the legs, buttocks and abdomen, leaving the classic black, eschar ulceration.\n\nArterial ulceration is normally seen as small punched-out lesions on the lateral aspect of the ankle or distal digits. The described lesions with black necrotic tissues are not commonly seen with arterial ulcers and the large area described in this case is extremely unlikely to be linked to this condition.\n\nCalcium pyrophosphate deposition disease, also known as pseudogout, is due to the deposition of calcium pyrophosphate crystals in and around joints. This disease presents similar to gout however it is not normally associated with skin lesions especially not the ones described in this patient.\n\nThe patient's lesions do not match pyoderma gangrenosum which is an inflammatory skin disease normally seen as open ulcers with undermined violaceous borders. Areas of necrotic black tissue are not classically seen with pyoderma gangrenosum and there is no association of the condition with renal failure. Pyoderma gangrenosum is normally associated with inflammatory bowel disease, liver disease or haematological conditions.\n\nTophi are deposits of urate crystals seen in patients with longstanding hyperuricemia and are pathognomonic for gout. They appear as small, rounded nodules normally on the digits or elbows and do not match the description of this patient's lesions."
