      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Hepatitis C virus",
      "explanation": "Mesangiocapillary glomerulonephritis (membranoproliferative)\n\nPartial lipodystrophy\n\nis correct because the clinical features and biopsy findings are characteristic of mesangiocapillary glomerulonephritis (MCGN), also known as membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis. The patient presents with nephrotic-range proteinuria (protein +++ on urinalysis), microscopic haematuria (blood ++), hypertension, and low serum C3 with normal C4, all typical features of complement-mediated glomerulonephritis. Renal biopsy demonstrating dense intramembranous electron-dense deposits confirms a diagnosis of type 2 MCGN, also called dense deposit disease. In contrast, type 1 MCGN is characterised by subendothelial and mesangial immune complex deposits. Type 2 MCGN has a well-known association with partial lipodystrophy, where complement dysregulation leads to selective loss of subcutaneous fat, typically affecting the face and upper body.\n\nANCA-associated vasculitis\n\nis incorrect because it causes a pauci-immune glomerulonephritis, typically characterised by minimal or absent immune complex or complement deposition on renal biopsy. In contrast, type 2 membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis (now termed dense deposit disease) shows dense intramembranous deposits and is strongly associated with dysregulation of the alternative complement pathway. ANCA-associated vasculitis presents with rapidly progressive renal failure, often with systemic features such as fever, weight loss, or pulmonary involvement—none of which are present in this case. It is not linked to dense deposit disease.\n\nHenoch–Schönlein purpura\n\nis incorrect because it causes an IgA-mediated small vessel vasculitis, typically presenting with purpura, abdominal pain, arthritis, and renal involvement. Renal biopsy usually shows mesangial IgA deposition, not dense intramembranous deposits. The clinical picture in this case, including the pattern of complement consumption and electron microscopy findings, is not consistent with Henoch–Schönlein purpura.\n\nHepatitis C virus\n\nis incorrect because while it is strongly associated with type 1 MCGN and mixed cryoglobulinaemia, it is not linked to type 2 MCGN. In hepatitis C-associated renal disease, immune complex deposition results in subendothelial deposits and hypercellularity on biopsy, differing from the dense deposit disease pattern seen in this patient.\n\nHodgkin lymphoma\n\nis incorrect because it is classically associated with minimal change disease, not MCGN. Hodgkin lymphoma may cause nephrotic syndrome through cytokine-mediated podocyte injury, leading to minimal change pathology, which presents differently both clinically and histologically from the findings in this case. Dense deposit disease and complement dysregulation are not features of Hodgkin-associated renal disease."
