      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Anti-centromere",
      "explanation": "Idiopathic membranous glomerulonephritis is related to anti-phospholipase A2 antibodies\n\nMembranous glomerulonephritis is a slowly progressive disease of the kidneys and the commonest type of glomerulonephritis in adults. Although there are some specific secondary causes for the condition including infections, malignancy and certain drug use, approximately 85% of causes have no cause identified and are classified as idiopathic. A major factor in a significant percentage of idiopathic membranous glomerulonephritis is the presence of immune complexes that are formed by circulating antibodies, the main of which is\n\nanti-phospholipase A2\n\n.\n\nAnti-cyclic citrullinated peptides (\n\nanti-CCP\n\n) antibodies are strongly associated with rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and are present in approximately 70% of RA patients. Anti-CCP antibodies have not been linked to membranous glomerulonephritis.\n\nAnti-topoisomerase (\n\nanti-Scl-70\n\n) antibodies are mainly seen in diffuse systemic scleroderma although they are also found in the more limited form, CREST syndrome. Anti-Scl-70 are normally found to be negative in membranous glomerulonephritis patients.\n\nAnti-centromere\n\nantibodies are associated with the limited cutaneous form of systemic sclerosis, also known as CREST syndrome, referring to the 5 main features of calcinosis, Raynaud's phenomenon, oesophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, and telangiectasia. Again, these antibodies are not associated with membranous glomerulonephritis.\n\nAnti-dsDNA\n\nantibodies are associated with systemic lupus and although they may also be raised in patients with membranous glomerulonephritis, a clear association between the antibodies and condition has not been established."
