      "explanation": "In a patient with hypercalciuria and renal stones, calcium excretion and stone formation can be decreased by the use of thiazide diuretics\n\nIndapamide\n\nis the correct answer. The patient presents with abdominal pain that radiates from the loin to the groin, suggestive of renal colic secondary to nephrolithiasis. Moreover, there is evidence of hypercalciuria. In such cases, thiazide diuretics like indapamide are beneficial in preventing further stone formation by reducing urinary calcium levels through enhanced reabsorption in the distal tubules.\n\nFurosemide\n\nis incorrect. This is likely to cause hypocalcaemia and therefore hypercalciuria and so would not be an appropriate choice to prevent this type of renal stone.\n\nAllopurinol\n\nis not the correct choice in this scenario. Allopurinol is indicated for the prevention of urate-based renal stones; however, given that serum urate levels are normal, uric acid stones are improbable here.\n\nPercutaneous nephrolithotomy\n\nis also incorrect. While this surgical intervention is used to remove symptomatic renal calculi, it does not confer any preventive benefit against future stone formation.\n\nTamsulosin\n\ndoes not represent the right answer either. As an alpha-blocker, tamsulosin aids in the acute management of renal colic by relaxing the ureters and facilitating stone passage; however, it does not prevent stone formation and is typically reserved for assisting in the passage of small stones less than 10mm."
