      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis",
      "explanation": "HIV-associated nephropathy (HIVAN) causes collapsing FSGS and usually presents as nephrotic syndrome\n\nUncontrolled HIV, along with reducing the body's immune system, is also associated with damage to multiple different organs. HIV-associated nephropathy is typically a result of collapsing\n\nfocal segmental glomerulosclerosis\n\n(FSGS) where scarring and damage to the glomeruli and renal podocytes develop. The cause of FSGS in HIV is unclear but may be a result of the virus directly infecting tubular epithelial cells and podocytes.\n\nMembranoproliferative glomerulonephritis\n\nresults from deposits within the glomerular mesangium and basement membrane thickening. This leads to activation of the complement system and damage to the glomeruli. This form of renal impairment is most commonly associated with hepatitis C infections as well as several autoimmune diseases (e.g. systemic lupus erythematosus). Patients typically present with a nephrotic, not a nephritis picture as seen in this case.\n\nMembranous glomerulonephritis\n\n(MGN) is a slowly progressive form of renal impairment with most cases found to be primary/idiopathic in nature. A small percentage of MGN cases are secondary to other causes including autoimmune conditions (e.g. systemic lupus erythematosus), infections (syphilis, malaria etc) and drugs (NSAIDs, penicillamine etc). Although MGN can be associated with HIV it is less common than FSGS.\n\nThe cause and pathophysiology associated with\n\nminimal change disease\n\nup until recently was unknown with the condition still considered idiopathic in nature. With the advancements in electron microscopy, a diffuse loss of podocyte foot processes, vacuolation and growth of microvilli on the visceral epithelial cells have been identified as causes of the condition, all resulting in the excess protein loss into the urine. HIV has not been associated with minimal change disease.\n\nRapidly progressive glomerulonephritis\n\n, as the name implies, is a condition resulting in accelerated loss of renal function. In most cases, glomerular crescent formation is seen on biopsy and the condition is secondary to another disease such as Goodpasture's syndrome or granulomatosis with polyangiitis. HIV is rarely associated with this form of renal disease."
