      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "CT angiography of renal vessels",
      "explanation": "Sharp rise in creatinine following ACE inhibitor initiation → ? underlying renal artery stenosis\n\nThis patient presents with hypertension and a significant rise in creatinine following the initiation of an ACE inhibitor, which is consistent with a diagnosis of renal artery stenosis. ACE inhibitors preferentially dilate the efferent glomerular arterioles by blocking the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, reducing GFR by reducing the angiotensin II effect. In the presence of renal artery stenosis, this haemodynamic effect is exaggerated as blood flow to the affected kidney is already significantly compromised. The best non-invasive imaging modality for investigating renal artery stenosis is\n\nMR angiography of the renal vessels\n\n. This technique is sensitive and specific and does not require iodine-based contrast agents.\n\nWhile\n\nCT angiography of the renal vessels\n\nmay be considered, it has several drawbacks. Firstly, it involves iodine-based contrast, which carries a risk of contrast-induced nephropathy (although more modern contrast media carry less risk of nephrotoxicity). Secondly, significant arterial calcification may lead to an underestimation of disease severity on CT angiography.\n\nA\n\ndimercaptosuccinic acid (DMSA) scan\n\nis inappropriate in this context. It is typically used to evaluate renal scarring. Although it provides information on renal perfusion and arterial supply, it involves the administration of a radionuclide and is unnecessary when other imaging modalities offer better anatomical detail and are more practical.\n\nA micturating cystourethrogram (MCUG)\n\nis used to diagnose vesicoureteric reflux and is most commonly employed to investigate recurrent urinary tract infections in children. It is therefore unsuitable in this clinical scenario of suspected renal artery stenosis.\n\nUS kidneys with Doppler imaging\n\ncan be a useful initial screening test to identify asymmetry in kidney size and assess vessel stenosis. However, it does not provide the detailed structural information available from MRI or CT imaging. Additionally, its utility may be limited by factors such as obesity or overlying bowel gas. Doppler ultrasound may also fail to detect stenosis in accessory renal arteries or identify fibromuscular dysplasia, which are better visualised using other imaging techniques."
