      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Anti-glomerular basement membrane",
      "explanation": "Idiopathic membranous glomerulonephritis is related to anti-phospholipase A2 antibodies\n\nThis patient presents with nephrotic syndrome (proteinuria >3.5g/24h, hypoalbuminaemia, oedema, and hypercholesterolaemia) and has renal biopsy findings characteristic of membranous glomerulonephritis (thickened basement membrane with subepithelial deposits creating a 'spike and dome' appearance). The most likely antibody to be detected in this case is anti-phospholipase A2 receptor (PLA2R) antibody.\n\nAnti-phospholipase A2 receptor\n\nantibodies are present in approximately 70-80% of patients with primary (idiopathic) membranous glomerulonephritis. These autoantibodies target the PLA2R antigen expressed on podocytes, leading to in situ immune complex formation, complement activation, and subsequent podocyte injury. The presence of these antibodies helps distinguish primary from secondary forms of membranous nephropathy. Anti-PLA2R antibody levels often correlate with disease activity and can be used to monitor response to treatment. The absence of other systemic symptoms or signs in this middle-aged man, along with the classic biopsy findings, strongly supports primary membranous nephropathy as the diagnosis.\n\nAnti-glomerular basement membrane\n\nantibodies are associated with anti-GBM disease (Goodpasture's syndrome), which typically presents with rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis and often pulmonary haemorrhage. The renal biopsy in anti-GBM disease shows a crescentic glomerulonephritis with linear IgG deposition along the glomerular basement membrane, not the subepithelial deposits seen in this case.\n\nAnti-neutrophil cytoplasmic\n\nantibodies (ANCA) are associated with small vessel vasculitides such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis, microscopic polyangiitis, and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis. These conditions typically present with rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, and renal biopsy shows pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis without immune complex deposition, unlike the findings in this case.\n\nAnti-double stranded DNA\n\nantibodies are characteristic of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). While lupus nephritis can cause membranous nephropathy (Class V lupus nephritis), it typically shows 'full house' immunofluorescence with deposits of IgG, IgA, IgM, C3, and C1q. This patient has no mentioned systemic features of SLE, making this antibody less likely.\n\nAnti-C1q\n\nantibodies can be found in patients with SLE, particularly those with lupus nephritis, and in patients with hypocomplementemic urticarial vasculitis. They are not specifically associated with idiopathic membranous glomerulonephritis. The absence of other clinical features of these conditions makes anti-C1q antibodies an unlikely finding in this case."
