      "correctText": "Furosemide",
      "explanation": "Minimal change disease - prednisolone\n\nThis patient’s clinical presentation is consistent with minimal change disease, with the onset of nephrotic syndrome following a viral upper respiratory tract infection. In minimal change nephropathy, a kidney biopsy shows normal appearances on light microscopy and electron microscopy would demonstrate foot process effacement when available. The correct course of action is to commence\n\nprednisolone\n\n. The Kidney Disease: Improving Global Outcomes (KDIGO) guideline recommends starting prednisolone at 1 mg/kg daily unless contraindicated.\n\nGiven that the patient is suffering from nephrotic syndrome with a significantly decreased serum albumin level, there is an elevated risk of venous thromboembolic events. Therefore,\n\napixaban\n\n, an anticoagulant, might be considered to mitigate this risk. However, treatment of the primary disease with prednisolone takes priority and is a better answer.\n\nWhile\n\nfurosemide\n\ncould help manage the patient’s peripheral oedema, it does not address the underlying pathophysiology of the disease and is therefore not the most appropriate option here.\n\nAlthough\n\nramipril\n\nis important for managing glomerular diseases due to its effectiveness in reducing hypertension and proteinuria, it is a less correct answer as treating the primary pathology takes precedence. ACE inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers play an important role in glomerular diseases as they control hypertension and proteinuria, slowing the progression of chronic kidney disease.\n\nLastly,\n\nrituximab\n\n, a CD20 inhibitor, is suggested by KDIGO as a potential second-line treatment for minimal change disease when glucocorticoids are contraindicated. In this case, since there are no contraindications to glucocorticoid use, prednisolone remains the more appropriate choice over rituximab."
