      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Basal cell carcinoma",
      "explanation": "Patients who have received an organ transplant are at risk of skin cancer (particularly squamous cell carcinoma) due to long-term use of immunosuppressants\n\nThis is likely to be a cutaneous\n\nsquamous cell carcinoma\n\n. Patients who are immunosuppressed following a renal transplant are at particular risk of skin cancer. In contrast to the general population, squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is more common than basal cell carcinoma (BCC). As described here, SCC typically presents as a quickly enlarging irregular crusted lesion that can ulcerate and produce pain or bleeding. It is commonly found in sun-exposed areas and can metastasise.\n\nBasal cell carcinoma\n\nis less common in renal transplant patients, does not metastasise, and is typically a slow-growing nodule that can bleed and ulcerate. In this case, the speed of onset makes this less likely as well as the epidemiology, with squamous cell carcinoma being more common in renal transplant recipients.\n\nKaposi's sarcoma\n\ntypically presents as purplish macular/nodules/papules and does not match the description here.\n\nMalignant melanoma\n\nusually starts as an enlarging mole that becomes irregularly pigmented and thickened over time. They may crust and bleed but do not tend to ulcerate. The lack of pigmentation and presence of ulceration, in this case, make this less likely.\n\nPyoderma gangrenosum\n\nusually presents as a rapidly enlarging ulcerated lesion that starts as a small papule, often at the site of trauma. It is typically intensely painful and can be associated with systemic illnesses such as inflammatory bowel disease. In the case described, neither rapid onset nor intense pain is described and there are no other features to suggest a new underlying illness."
