      "explanation": "Membranous nephropathy is frequently associated with malignancy\n\nThis patient's presentation of oedema, frothy urine and impaired renal function is suggestive of nephrotic syndrome. Given the patient's background of colorectal carcinoma, the most likely underlying cause is\n\nmembranous nephropathy\n\n, as this is the option most frequently associated with malignancy. Biopsy would show subepithelial immune complex deposits and management typically involves the use of ACE inhibitors and immunosuppression.\n\nAmyloidosis\n\nis another recognised cause of nephrotic syndrome. However, in the absence of any systemic features, and given the history of colorectal carcinoma, membranous nephropathy is most likely.\n\nFocal segmental glomerulosclerosis\n\nwould also present with a similar clinical picture and would require biopsy to definitively differentiate. However, given the history of colorectal carcinoma, membranous nephropathy is more likely.\n\nMembranoproliferative glomerulonephritis\n\nalso presents with nephrotic syndrome and should be distinguished via renal biopsy. It is not as strongly associated with a history of cancer as membranous nephropathy.\n\nMinimal change disease\n\nis more common in children and is classically preceded by an upper respiratory tract infection. It is not typically associated with malignancy, unlike membranous nephropathy."
