      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Membranous glomerulonephritis",
      "explanation": "Diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis, causes:\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nDiffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis\n\n. This type of glomerulonephritis is most commonly associated with post-streptococcal infections, especially in children. It typically presents 1-2 weeks after a streptococcal throat infection or 3-6 weeks after a skin infection. The pathogenesis involves the formation of immune complexes which deposit in the glomerulus, leading to inflammation and proliferation of cells within the glomerulus.\n\nFocal segmental glomerulosclerosis\n\nis incorrect because it is not typically associated with streptococcal infections. It's a cause of nephrotic syndrome in children and adults, often idiopathic but can be secondary to various conditions such as HIV infection, obesity or drug toxicity.\n\nMembranous glomerulonephritis\n\nis also an incorrect choice. This form of glomerulonephritis is more common in adults than in children and is usually associated with autoimmune diseases, malignancies or drugs rather than streptococcal infections.\n\nMesangiocapillary glomerulonephritis\n\n, also known as membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis, is not the best answer either. While it can occur following infections including streptococcus, it's more commonly seen with chronic immune complex diseases like systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) or chronic infections such as Hepatitis C.\n\nFinally,\n\nRapidly progressive glomerulonephritis\n\n(RPGN) refers to a group of diseases characterised by rapid loss of kidney function over days or weeks rather than a specific disease entity. RPGN can result from various underlying causes including anti-glomerular basement membrane disease (Goodpasture syndrome), vasculitis and post-infectious GN among others; however, its association with streptococcal infection isn't particularly characteristic compared to diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis."
