      "explanation": "Alcohol bingeing can lead to ADH suppression in the posterior pituitary gland subsequently leading to polyuria\n\nSuppression of antidiuretic hormone secretion\n\nis correct because acute alcohol ingestion suppresses release of ADH from the posterior pituitary, leading to impaired water reabsorption in the collecting ducts of the kidney, resulting in increased urine output (polyuria) and subsequent dehydration with hypernatraemia if fluid intake does not match losses. This explains both the patient’s recent binge drinking, rapid onset of symptoms, and laboratory evidence of mild hypernatraemia without significant hyperglycaemia or renal impairment.\n\nOsmotic diuresis due to hyperglycaemia\n\nwould present with significantly elevated blood glucose levels, usually well above the normal range, often accompanied by glycosuria and possibly ketonuria in severe cases such as diabetic ketoacidosis or HHS. In this scenario, glucose is within normal limits, making this explanation unlikely.\n\nNephrogenic diabetes insipidus\n\ntypically occurs in patients with chronic lithium use or metabolic disorders affecting renal response to ADH; it rarely presents acutely following an alcohol binge and usually has a longer history or relevant drug exposure.\n\nPrimary polydipsia\n\n, characterised by excessive water intake leading to polyuria and potential hyponatraemia rather than hypernatraemia, is less consistent with this case where there is evidence of dehydration and raised sodium concentration.\n\nHypercalcaemia-induced polyuria\n\ncan cause nephrogenic diabetes insipidus-like effects but would be expected to show elevated calcium levels on laboratory testing; no such abnormality is reported here."
