      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Prostate",
      "explanation": "Men with BRCA mutations have an increased risk of prostate cancer\n\nProstate\n\nis the correct answer. Prostate cancer in males is strongly associated with the BRCA genes. In the above case, the presentation is that of liver metastasis in a man who has a strong family history of BRCA genetic predisposition; this means that the prostate is a likely primary, which can metastasize to bone. Lymph nodes or liver most commonly. If another answer was breast cancer then that could be correct also.\n\nBrain\n\ncancer is incorrect because this cancer does not likely metastasize to the liver, most commonly the spread is the converse; liver to brain.\n\nColon\n\ncancer is an incorrect answer. Although colon cancer commonly metastasises to the liver, the BRCA genetic predisposition, in this case, is more indicative of a prostate or breast primary. Colon cancer has various genetic predispositions, including familial adenomatous polyposis.\n\nLung\n\ncancer is incorrect in this case as there are no lung symptoms or signs to point towards this diagnosis and lung cancers are not associated with BRCA mutations.\n\nRetinoblastoma\n\nis incorrect because this type of cancer primarily stays in the eye and very rarely metastasises to the liver. Moreover, retinoblastoma is associated with the retinoblastoma 1 gene mutation which occurs in utero and not the BRCA gene mutation which occurs later on in life."
