      "question": "A 35-year-old man with known HIV presents to the renal clinic with progressive ankle swelling and frothy urine over several weeks. He is not aware of any recent infections or medication changes. On examination, his blood pressure is 122/78 mmHg and there is bilateral pitting oedema up to the knees. Urinalysis shows 4+ proteinuria, and there are no active urinary sediments. Laboratory results are as follows:\n\n\nUrea\t12.5 mmol/L\t(2.0 - 7.0)\nCreatinine\t176 µmol/L\t(55 - 120)\nAlbumin\t23 g/L\t(35 - 50)\n\nRenal ultrasound demonstrates normal-sized kidneys with increased echogenicity.\n\nWhich renal pathology is most likely responsible for these findings?",
