      "explanation": "HIV-associated nephropathy (HIVAN) causes collapsing FSGS and usually presents as nephrotic syndrome\n\nCollapsing focal segmental glomerulosclerosis\n\nis the most likely diagnosis in this scenario, consistent with HIV-associated nephropathy (HIVAN). The patient has a background of HIV infection, significant proteinuria resulting in nephrotic syndrome (as evidenced by hypoalbuminaemia and oedema), raised urea and creatinine, and normal-sized kidneys—classic features of HIVAN. Collapsing FSGS is pathognomonic for HIVAN on histology, and the clinical picture fits UK guidelines regarding presentation and investigation of renal complications in people living with HIV.\n\nMembranous nephropathy\n\ncan cause nephrotic syndrome but typically occurs in older adults or secondary to malignancy, hepatitis B/C, or certain drugs—not as commonly in young patients with HIV without other risk factors. Renal biopsy would show subepithelial immune complex deposition rather than collapsing FSGS.\n\nMinimal change disease\n\nusually presents with nephrotic syndrome but is more common in children; when it does occur in adults, it often lacks significant renal impairment at presentation (urea/creatinine usually normal). It also does not have a strong association with HIV nor does it explain increased echogenicity on ultrasound.\n\nAmyloidosis\n\ncan present with nephrotic syndrome and enlarged kidneys but tends to occur in chronic inflammatory states or plasma cell dyscrasias rather than isolated HIV infection. Amyloid deposits would be seen on biopsy; however, the demographic and rapid progression here point away from amyloidosis.\n\nIgA nephropathy\n\n, while the most common glomerulonephritis worldwide, classically presents as episodic macroscopic haematuria following upper respiratory tract infections rather than heavy proteinuria/nephrotic syndrome. It also typically causes only mild to moderate proteinuria if present at all."
