      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Cranial diabetes insipidus",
      "explanation": "Fanconi syndrome is a reabsorptive defect in PCT where there is increased excretion of nearly all amino acids, glucose, bicarbonate and phosphate\n\nFanconi's syndrome\n\nis a generalised tubular defect causing impaired reabsorption of various electrolytes and proteins within the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT), resulting in the excretion of most amino acids, glucose, and phosphate. The patient, who has a background of Sjögren's syndrome is presenting with elevated urinary levels of all electrolytes, consistent with Fanconi's syndrome. Patients with Fanconi's syndrome often have metabolic acidosis due to type 2 renal tubular acidosis and hypokalemia, as observed here.\n\nCranial diabetes insipidus\n\nshould be considered as a differential for polyuria. However, the clinical presentation does not align with this condition. Significant glycosuria and high urinary potassium are inconsistent with cranial diabetes insipidus. This condition is caused by an impaired ability to release antidiuretic hormone (ADH) from the posterior pituitary gland, typically associated with a history of cranial disease (such as trauma, infection, tumour, or vasculitis). A fluid deprivation test combined with the administration of synthetic ADH is diagnostic; in such cases, urine concentration improves with synthetic ADH administration.\n\nNephrogenic diabetes insipidus\n\nis more common than cranial diabetes insipidus and must be considered. In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, there is resistance to ADH in the collecting ducts, leading to reduced aquaporin expression and decreased water reabsorption. The most common cause is lithium use, and it is diagnosed with a water deprivation test, where the administration of synthetic ADH fails to concentrate the urine. The elevated urinary electrolytes in this case are inconsistent with nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, and there is no history suggesting a potential cause for this condition.\n\nPsychogenic polydipsia\n\ncan also cause significant polyuria and is more common in psychiatric illnesses such as anxiety, but the history is not consistent with this. Psychogenic polydipsia would not cause increased urinary electrolytes as seen here. Patients with psychogenic polydipsia may produce up to 6 litres of dilute urine daily. A water deprivation test would demonstrate appropriate urine concentration in these patients.\n\nPoorly controlled\n\nT2DM\n\ncan cause polyuria and glycosuria. The patient in this scenario has a raised random glucose, but her HbA1C is well-controlled suggesting good management of her diabetes. This is therefore a less likely cause of her presentation."
