      "id": 170,
      "question": "Which of the following types of renal stones are said to have a semi-opaque appearance on x-ray?",
      "options": [
        "Calcium oxalate",
        "Cystine stones",
        "Urate stones",
        "Xanthine stones",
        "Triple phosphate stones"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Triple phosphate stones",
      "explanation": "Renal stones on x-ray\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nCystine stones\n\n, which have a semi-opaque (or faintly radiopaque) appearance on plain radiography. Cystine stones form in patients with cystinuria, an autosomal recessive disorder characterised by impaired renal tubular reabsorption of cystine and other dibasic amino acids. These stones have a characteristic appearance on X-ray with moderate radiodensity, appearing as homogeneous, ground-glass opacities that are typically described as semi-opaque. They account for approximately 1-2% of all urinary calculi and often form large, staghorn calculi that may recur despite treatment.\n\nCalcium oxalate\n\nstones are incorrect. These stones are the most common type of renal calculi, accounting for approximately 70-80% of all kidney stones. They are highly radiopaque (radio-dense) on X-ray due to their calcium content, appearing as distinct white opacities against the soft tissue background. They are not semi-opaque but rather fully opaque and easily visualised on plain radiography.\n\nUrate stones\n\nare incorrect. Uric acid stones are radiolucent on plain radiography, meaning they are not visible on X-ray. This is because they lack calcium and other elements that would make them radiopaque. They can only be detected indirectly on X-ray if they cause filling defects in contrast studies or are visualised using other imaging modalities such as CT scanning. Urate stones account for approximately 5-10% of all renal calculi and are associated with conditions causing hyperuricaemia such as gout.\n\nXanthine stones\n\nare incorrect. These are extremely rare stones that form in patients with xanthinuria or as a complication of allopurinol therapy in patients with severe hyperuricaemia. Like uric acid stones, xanthine stones are radiolucent and not visible on plain radiography. They would not appear semi-opaque on X-ray.\n\nTriple phosphate stones\n\nare incorrect. These stones, also known as struvite or magnesium ammonium phosphate stones, are associated with urinary tract infections caused by urease-producing bacteria such as\n\nProteus\n\n,\n\nKlebsiella\n\n, and\n\nPseudomonas\n\nspecies. They are fully radiopaque on X-ray due to their mineral content and often form large staghorn calculi that fill the renal pelvis and calyces. They appear distinctly white on X-ray rather than semi-opaque."
