      "explanation": "Alport's syndrome - X-linked dominant (in the majority)\n\nX-linked dominant\n\ninheritance is the most common mode for Alport's syndrome, accounting for approximately 85% of cases. This pattern explains why males are more severely affected (as in this scenario) and females are usually carriers with milder symptoms or asymptomatic. The clinical features—microscopic haematuria, progressive renal impairment, sensorineural deafness—and family history affecting male relatives on the maternal side further support this diagnosis. Electron microscopy findings of GBM splitting ('basket-weave' appearance) are characteristic.\n\nAutosomal recessive\n\nAlport’s syndrome does exist but accounts for only around 10-15% of cases and generally presents with similar features but without a clear X-linked pedigree pattern as described here (maternal uncle affected). It would be less likely given the male predominance and family history presented.\n\nAutosomal dominant\n\ninheritance is rare in Alport’s syndrome (<5%). If present, it often results in a milder phenotype and typically affects both sexes equally across generations; this does not fit the scenario provided where there is evidence of X-linked transmission.\n\nMitochondrial\n\ninheritance would show maternal transmission to all offspring regardless of sex and is not associated with Alport’s syndrome or its classical renal-auditory-ocular triad.\n\nY-linked\n\ninheritance would affect only males and be transmitted strictly from father to son, which is inconsistent with the maternal uncle being affected in this case. There are no known Y-linked forms of Alport’s syndrome."
