      "correctText": "Directly destroys Leydig cells",
      "explanation": "Bicalutamide is an androgen recept blocker\n\nInhibits androgen receptor binding\n\n: This describes the mechanism of non-steroidal anti-androgens such as bicalutamide, which are commonly used alongside GnRH agonists in advanced prostate cancer. Bicalutamide acts by competitively blocking androgen receptors, thus preventing testosterone and dihydrotestosterone from stimulating prostate cancer growth. This blockade is crucial during initiation of GnRH agonists to prevent tumour flare caused by transient increases in testosterone.\n\nSuppresses pituitary LH secretion\n\n: This describes the action of GnRH agonists and antagonists rather than anti-androgens like bicalutamide. GnRH agonists initially stimulate, then downregulate, pituitary receptors leading to decreased LH and FSH secretion over time, but do not directly block androgen receptors.\n\nBlocks conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone\n\n: This is the mechanism of 5-alpha reductase inhibitors (e.g., finasteride), which are not first-line agents in metastatic prostate cancer management or in preventing tumour flare from GnRH agonists.\n\nDirectly destroys Leydig cells\n\n: Bilateral orchidectomy results in rapid reduction of testosterone by removing Leydig cells; however, this is a surgical intervention and not relevant to the mechanism of bicalutamide or other oral anti-androgens.\n\nReduces adrenal androgen synthesis\n\n: Abiraterone inhibits CYP17 and reduces both testicular and adrenal androgen production. It is used later in hormone-relapsed disease but does not describe how bicalutamide functions."
