      "explanation": "Diabetes insipidus in patients taking lithium mechanism: lithium desensitizes the kidney's ability to respond to ADH in the collecting ducts\n\nThe water deprivation test results, along with the clinical symptoms of polyuria and polydipsia, indicate that this patient has diabetes insipidus. As the patient is on lithium and has no clinical features suggesting a cranial cause of diabetes insipidus, he most likely has nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. The exact molecular mechanism by which lithium causes dysfunction at the collecting duct is still to be fully determined, but it is currently accepted that lithium desensitises the kidney's ability to respond to antidiuretic hormone (ADH).\n\nThe anterior pituitary gland secretes 7 hormones: follicle-stimulating hormone, luteinizing hormone, adrenocorticotropic hormone, thyroid-stimulating hormone, prolactin, endorphins, and growth hormone. An anterior pituitary tumour often presents with bilateral hemianopia.\n\nThe posterior pituitary is the site of ADH release. Dysfunction at the posterior pituitary would cause cranial diabetes insipidus. Although cranial diabetes insipidus can be idiopathic, it classically presents after head trauma or pituitary surgery.\n\nThe distal convoluted tubule is the site off action for thiazide diuretics. Thiazides are used to treat nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. A mutation in the Na+-Clˆ’ co-transporter causes Gitelman syndrome.\n\nDysfunction of the proximal renal tubule can cause Fanconi syndrome; the proximal renal tubular cells are unable to absorb glucose, amino acids, uric acid, phosphate, and bicarbonate."
