      "correctText": "Haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelet count (HELLP) syndrome",
      "explanation": "With MAHA, once TTP and HUS are excluded, atypical HUS needs to be considered and treated\n\nAtypical haemolytic uraemic syndrome (atypical HUS) is a non-specific term encompassing several diseases which feature microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia (MAHA; non-immune haemolytic anaemia, i.e. Coombs negative) with schistocytes (due to fragmentation of red blood cells) and thrombocytopenia, with renal failure but without diarrhoeal symptoms.\n\nWhen assessing a patient with MAHA and thrombocytopenia, it is first essential to rule out systemic causes (e.g. rheumatological disorders, sepsis, severe hypertension, malignancy), the correction of which often improves the MAHA and thrombocytopenia. If there are no features of systemic diseases, TTP and Shiga-toxin producing\n\nEscherichia coli\n\nHUS needs to be excluded as the treatments for these conditions are very different: TTP requires life-saving plasma exchange, Shiga-toxin producing\n\nE. coli\n\nHUS is treated with supportive therapy, while atypical haemolytic syndrome -  if complement-mediated - responds to eculizumab.\n\nHaemolytic uraemic syndrome: the absence of relevant exposure history to\n\nE. coli\n\n, abdominal pain (usually severe) and diarrhoea (typically bloody) make this 'typical' form of HUS unlikely.\n\nThrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura: the absence of neurological and gastrointestinal symptoms (commonly involved organs in TTP), and no fever, all makes this diagnosis unlikely. Although renal impairment is one of the classical pentad of TTP, it is actually relatively uncommon in clinical practice.\n\nDisseminated intravascular coagulation: the normal clotting profile (particularly fibrinogen and D-dimer) makes this diagnosis unlikely.\n\nHELLP syndrome: this is 'haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets' which is a syndrome seen in pregnancy, which is almost impossible in this male patient."
