      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Shigella",
      "explanation": "Haemolytic uraemic syndrome - classically caused by E coli 0157:H7\n\nThe correct answer in this case is\n\nE. coli\n\n. The patient's presentation of bloody diarrhoea, reduced urine output, lethargy, and laboratory findings of anaemia (Hb 10.4 g/dl), thrombocytopenia (Plt 91 * 10\n\n9\n\n/l), and acute kidney injury (Creatinine 176 µmol/l) are suggestive of haemolytic uraemic syndrome (HUS). HUS is most commonly caused by Shiga toxin-producing\n\nE. coli\n\n(STEC) infection.\n\nCampylobacter\n\nis an incorrect option because although it can cause bloody diarrhoea, it is less likely to be associated with HUS compared to\n\nE. coli\n\n. Furthermore, the presence of thrombocytopenia and acute kidney injury in this patient makes\n\nE. coli\n\na more likely causative organism.\n\nGiardiasis\n\nis an incorrect option as it typically causes non-bloody diarrhoea and does not lead to HUS. Giardiasis is caused by the protozoan parasite\n\nGiardia lamblia\n\n, which primarily affects the small intestine and causes malabsorption.\n\nSalmonella\n\nis another incorrect option as it generally causes gastroenteritis with non-bloody diarrhoea. Although some strains can occasionally cause bloody diarrhoea, they are not typically associated with HUS or the other laboratory abnormalities seen in this patient.\n\nFinally,\n\nShigella\n\nis also an incorrect option because although it can cause bloody diarrhoea similar to STEC, it rarely leads to HUS. The presence of HUS in this patient makes\n\nE. coli\n\na more likely causative organism."
