      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Renal vein thrombosis",
      "explanation": "Heroin use is a risk factor for focal segmental glomerulosclerosis\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nFocal segmental glomerulosclerosis\n\n. This is a condition that affects the kidney's filtering system (glomeruli) causing scarring (sclerosis). It is one of the most common causes of primary glomerular diseases in adults and has been associated with heroin use. The clinical presentation often includes proteinuria, which matches this patient's urine dipstick result, and a gradual increase in serum creatinine levels, indicating a slow decline in renal function over time.\n\nAmyloidosis\n\nis incorrect because it usually presents with nephrotic syndrome, characterised by heavy proteinuria (>3.5g/day), hypoalbuminemia, and peripheral oedema. This patient only has mild proteinuria without any other features of nephrotic syndrome. Furthermore, amyloidosis is more commonly seen in patients with chronic inflammatory conditions or malignancies rather than being associated with heroin use.\n\nMesangiocapillary glomerulonephritis\n\n, also known as membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis, typically presents with both haematuria and proteinuria. Moreover, it's not specifically linked to heroin use. Therefore, given the absence of haematuria on urine dipstick and the history of heroin use in this case scenario, mesangiocapillary glomerulonephritis would be less likely than focal segmental glomerulosclerosis.\n\nChronic pyelonephritis\n\nresults from recurrent or persistent renal infections or vesicoureteral reflux disease. Patients typically present with symptoms related to urinary tract infection such as dysuria or flank pain and systemic symptoms like fever may be present too. In addition to these clinical features not being reported in this case scenario, chronic pyelonephritis generally leads to impaired concentration ability before progressing to renal failure. Hence it would not explain this patient's slowly rising creatinine levels.\n\nFinally,\n\nRenal vein thrombosis\n\nwould typically present acutely with loin pain and haematuria following a precipitating event such as trauma or dehydration rather than a chronically rising creatinine level as seen here. Furthermore, while intravenous drug users are at an increased risk for thrombotic events due to repeated vascular injury and potential for septic emboli, this usually results in acute kidney injury rather than chronic kidney disease."
