      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Anti-dsDNA",
      "explanation": "Idiopathic membranous glomerulonephritis is related to anti-phospholipase A2 antibodies\n\nThe raised urinary protein in the absence of blood with oedema and low serum albumin leads to the diagnosis of nephrotic syndrome. There are many causes of nephrotic syndrome including membranous glomerulonephritis, minimal change glomerulonephritis and focal sclerosing glomerulonephritis. Membranous glomerulonephritis is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in Europe and the findings on light microscopy are classical for this. Anti-phospholipase A2 is closely associated with idiopathic membranous glomerulonephritis and has a specificity of close to 100% with a sensitivity of 70-80%.\n\nAnti-dsDNA is associated with systemic lupus erythematosus and lupus nephritis. Lupus nephritis has the possibility of presenting with a nephrotic syndrome and 10% of cases present as membranous glomerulonephritis. However, the presence of a normal CRP along with normal complement levels makes this diagnosis less likely.\n\nAnti-glomerular basement membrane antibody is unlikely to be associated here as its syndrome will normally also present with pulmonary symptoms of shortness of breath and/or haemoptysis. The patient would also typically have a nephritic syndrome.\n\nAnti-proteinase 3 antibody (PR3) is associated with granulomatosis with polyangiitis. This usually presents with a nephritic syndrome. Also, this typically presents with a raised CRP and more systemic symptoms.\n\nGalactose deficient IgA1 has been shown to be associated with IgA nephropathy. This is normally associated with a nephritic syndrome and classically occurs several weeks after an upper respiratory tract infection."
