      "explanation": "Nephrotic syndrome is associated with a hypercoagulable state due to loss of antithrombin III via the kidneys\n\nThis patient has presented with peripheral oedema and nephrotic-range proteinuria (> 3 - 3.5g/24h). Although the cause of his nephrotic syndrome would require histological confirmation via a renal biopsy, the most likely scenario based on the vignette is membranous nephropathy secondary to chronic use of non-steroidal anti-inflammatory medications. Nephrotic syndrome is associated with hypercoagulability, due to loss of antithrombin III through an excessively permeable glomerular filtration barrier. This makes patients susceptible to venous thromboembolism. In this scenario, the combination of hypercoagulability, sudden-onset unilateral flank pain, acute deterioration in renal function and new haematuria point towards the unifying diagnosis of renal vein thrombosis.\n\nAn obstructing renal calculus is a plausible cause of flank pain, haematuria, and acute kidney injury. However, in this patient, the description of persistent pain, which came on very suddenly, is more in keeping with a sudden thrombotic event than the classic intermittent, colicky pain caused by renal calculi.\n\nTubulointerstitial nephritis (TIN) can also present with flank pain, haematuria and acute kidney injury. However, for exam purposes, TIN is classically associated with a combination of a fever, rash and/or blood eosinophilia, of which none are present here. It is often provoked by the introduction of a new medication, such as an antibiotic or anti-inflammatory. In this vignette, the only new medication that has been introduced is furosemide, which is a rare cause of TIN.\n\nRenal artery thrombosis is a possible cause of sudden onset flank pain and acute kidney injury. However, the incidence of renal vein thrombosis, and venous thromboembolism in general is far greater in the context of hypercoagulability in nephrotic syndrome than arterial thrombosis.\n\nPre-renal acute injury due to excessive diuresis can cause a deterioration in renal function, but would not explain sudden flank pain or haematuria in this patient."
