      "correctText": "Staphylococcus epidermidis",
      "explanation": "Coagulase-negative\n\nThe most common causative organisms of PD related peritonitis are gram-positive bacteria. Coagulase-negative staphylococcus is the most frequently identified organism followed by\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\n,\n\nStreptococcus\n\nand\n\nEnterococcus\n\n.\n\nGram-negative enteric causative organisms include\n\nE. coli\n\n,\n\nKlebsiella\n\nand\n\nEnterobacter\n\nwhich account for up to a quarter of infections. In addition to this, fungi and other gram-negative rods such as\n\nPseudomonas\n\nand\n\nAcinetobacter\n\nand account for a small percentage of infections.\n\nStaphylococcus epidermidis\n\nis part of the normal body flora and typically causes PD related peritonitis due to touch contamination. PD related peritonitis arises due to tube contamination, intraluminal catheter spread, haematogenous spread and rarely ascending infection through pelvic organs.\n\nPD related peritonitis arises due to tube contamination, intraluminal catheter spread, haematogenous spread and rarely ascending through pelvic organs."
