      "explanation": "Calcium resonium results in removal of potassium from the body, rather than shifting potassium between fluid compartments in the short-term\n\n'It increases potassium excretion by preventing enteral absorption' is the correct answer. Calcium resonium is a polystyrene cation exchange resin, acting to increase potassium excretion from the body through cation ion exchange. It exchanges potassium for the Ca++ in the resin. The onset of action is usually 2-12 hours when given orally, and longer if given rectally.\n\nCalcium resonium does not act on the Na+/K+-ATPase pump therefore this answer is incorrect.  An example of a drug acting on this pump is digoxin, which has an inhibitory effect.\n\n'It shifts potassium from the extracellular to the intracellular compartment' is incorrect. This is the mechanism of salbutamol nebulisers, which have already been given in the above scenario. They result in a short-term reduction in extracellular potassium.\n\nCalcium resonium does not stabilise the cardiac membrane, this is the action of calcium gluconate, which is also an important part of hyperkalemia management and has already been given in the above scenario.\n\n'It acts on the NKCC2 channel to increase potassium excretion' is incorrect. This is the mechanism of loop diuretics that can also be used to increase potassium excretion in hyperkalemia and have already been given in the above scenario."
