      "explanation": "Coagulase-negative\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nStaphylococcus epidermidis\n\n. This patient is presenting with signs and symptoms of peritonitis, a known complication of Continuous Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis (CAPD). In CAPD-related peritonitis, the most common causative organism is coagulase-negative staphylococci, which includes\n\nStaphylococcus epidermidis\n\n. These organisms are part of the normal skin flora and can be introduced into the peritoneal cavity during the process of connecting or disconnecting dialysis bags.\n\nStreptococcus pyogenes\n\nis an incorrect option. Although this organism can cause skin and soft tissue infections, it is not a common cause of CAPD-related peritonitis. Moreover, this organism typically presents with more severe clinical manifestations like necrotizing fasciitis or streptococcal toxic shock syndrome.\n\nEnterococcus\n\nis another incorrect option. While enterococci can cause peritonitis in patients undergoing CAPD, they are less common than coagulase-negative staphylococci or gram-negative organisms like\n\nEscherichia coli\n\n.\n\nStreptococcus agalactiae\n\n, also known as Group B Streptococcus (GBS), is an unlikely cause for this presentation. GBS primarily affects neonates and pregnant women and is not commonly associated with CAPD-related peritonitis.\n\nLastly,\n\nEscherichia coli\n\nis an incorrect option. Although gram-negative organisms like\n\nE. coli\n\ndo account for a significant proportion of CAPD-related peritonitis cases, they are still less common than coagulase-negative staphylococci such as\n\nStaphylococcus epidermidis\n\n."
