      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "MR angiography",
      "explanation": "Flash pulmonary oedema, U&Es worse on ACE inhibitor, asymmetrical kidneys → renal artery stenosis - do MR angiography\n\nThe onset of flash pulmonary oedema and an AKI in a patient who has recently started an ACE inhibitor makes renal artery stenosis (RAS) the probable diagnosis.\n\nMR angiography\n\nis now the investigation of choice for renal artery stenosis. This modality has a high sensitivity (>95%) and specificity (>90%) for the detection of stenoses of 50% or greater in diameter.  Due to its high sensitivity, and relative ease of access, it is now the preferred modality for the investigation of RAS.\n\nRenal angiography\n\nis an extremely sensitive diagnostic modality. However, catheter angiography is invasive, costly, and time-consuming, and it can lead to complications such as renal artery dissection or cholesterol embolisation. For these reasons, it is no longer the first-line modality.\n\nCT angiography\n\nand\n\nconventional renal angiography\n\nare suitable alternatives, however, MR angiography remains the gold standard.\n\nThe\n\ndexamethasone suppression test\n\nis used to diagnose Cushing's disease. Although Cushing's is a well-recognised cause of secondary hypertension, there are no other features in this case to support this diagnosis.\n\nThe\n\nrenin aldosterone ratio\n\ncan be used as a screening test for RAS however it is not the investigation of choice diagnostically. In renovascular disease, the kidney senses a low pressure, thereby releasing an increased amount of renin, which activates the RAAS cascade. Increased production of angiotensin causes vasoconstriction and the production of aldosterone results in increased sodium uptake and potassium excretion by the kidney. Sodium retention increases vascular volume and blood pressure. RAS results in secondary hyperaldosteronism characterised by raised renin and aldosterone levels."
