      "explanation": "ADPKD type 1 = chromosome 16 = 85% of cases\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nChromosome 16\n\n. Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) type 1 is associated with a mutation in the PKD1 gene located on chromosome 16. This gene encodes for a protein called polycystin-1, which plays an important role in maintaining the structure and function of the kidneys. Mutations in this gene lead to the formation of multiple cysts in both kidneys, causing them to enlarge and lose function over time.\n\nThe option\n\nChromosome 4\n\nis incorrect. Chromosome 4 does not contain any known genes related to ADPKD. Genetic disorders associated with mutations on this chromosome include Huntington's disease and Ellis-van Creveld syndrome, but not ADPKD.\n\nChromosome 8\n\nis also an incorrect answer. While there are numerous genetic disorders associated with mutations on this chromosome, including Burkitt lymphoma and Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A, it does not contain a gene that has been linked to ADPKD.\n\nThe option\n\nChromosome 12\n\nis another incorrect choice. The PKD2 gene, which causes autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease type 2 when mutated, is located on chromosome 4 rather than chromosome 12. Genetic disorders associated with mutations on chromosome 12 include hypochondrogenesis and achondrogenesis type II, but these conditions are unrelated to ADPKD.\n\nFinally,\n\nChromosome 20\n\nis also incorrect as it does not contain any genes known to be involved in ADPKD either. Alagille syndrome type II and adenosine deaminase deficiency are among the genetic disorders associated with mutations on this chromosome; however, they do not relate to ADPKD."
