      "question": "A 39-year-old man presents to his GP with a three-week history of increasing thirst and polyuria.\n\nPhysical examination is normal. He has a history of bipolar affective disorder for which he started a new medication approximately four weeks ago.\n\nThe GP conducts laboratory tests to confirm the suspected diagnosis.\n\nWater deprivation test results:\n\n\nSerum osmolarity\t330 mmol/L\t(285-295mOsm/kg)\nUrine osmolarity\t100 mmol/L\t(50-1200 mOsm/kg)\n\nPost-desmopressin test results:\n\n\nUrine osmolarity\t125 mmol/L\t(50 -1200 mOsm/kg)\n\nWhat is the underlying mechanism for the likely diagnosis?",
