      "correctText": "Mitral valve prolapse",
      "explanation": "ADPKD is associated with mitral valve prolaspe\n\nAutosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) is associated with numerous extra-renal manifestations, including the formation of hepatic and pancreatic cysts, intracranial berry aneurysms,\n\nmitral valve prolapse\n\n, colonic diverticula, and abdominal wall hernias. Patients who are newly diagnosed with ADPKD must receive comprehensive counselling about these potential comorbidities.\n\nBladder diverticula\n\nare protrusions of both the mucosa and muscle layer of the bladder. These can be congenital or acquired due to conditions such as bladder outlet obstruction or neurogenic bladder dysfunction. However, they do not have a recognised association with ADPKD.\n\nA\n\nChiari malformation\n\nrepresents a congenital defect characterised by the downward displacement of cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum. This neurological condition does not have a known relationship with ADPKD.\n\nSpontaneous coronary artery dissection (SCAD)\n\nis defined by an abrupt separation within the layers of the coronary artery wall, which may precipitate myocardial infarction. SCAD is typically linked to heritable connective tissue disorders like Ehlers-Danlos syndrome and Marfan syndrome but has no established link to ADPKD.\n\nSyringomyelia\n\ninvolves the formation of a cyst or cavity within the spinal cord, potentially leading to a range of neurological deficits. While often associated with structural abnormalities such as Chiari malformation, it can also arise secondary to spinal cord tumours or meningitis. Syringomyelia is not known to be related to ADPKD."
