      "question": "A 9-year-old boy presents with decreased urine output and swelling of the face, feet, and ankles. His past medical history is unremarkable except for a tonsillectomy performed two months ago due to recurrent tonsillitis.\n\nBlood tests reveal hypoalbuminaemia, and a urine dipstick test indicates massive proteinuria.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
      "options": [
        "Goodpasture's syndrome",
        "IgA nephropathy",
        "Minimal change disease",
        "Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis",
        "Systemic lupus erythematosus"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Goodpasture's syndrome",
      "explanation": "Nephrotic syndrome in children / young adults - minimal change disease\n\nMinimal change disease\n\nis the correct answer. The child presents with classical features of nephrotic syndrome, which include generalised oedema, hypoalbuminaemia, and proteinuria. Minimal change disease is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children and young adults. The mention of a tonsillectomy serves as a distractor; this patient exhibits classic symptoms of nephrotic syndrome.\n\nGoodpasture's syndrome\n\nis an autoimmune condition characterised by anti-glomerular basement membrane (anti-GBM) antibodies. This condition commonly leads to rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis and also affect the lungs, causing pulmonary haemorrhage. It does not fit the presentation of isolated nephrotic syndrome without pulmonary symptoms.\n\nIgA nephropathy\n\n, also known as berger's disease, often presents after a respiratory or gastrointestinal infection. It is characterised by haematuria and proteinuria. However, haematuria is more indicative of nephritic syndrome rather than the nephrotic syndrome symptoms described in this case, which includes significant proteinuria and oedema.\n\nPost-streptococcal glomerulonephritis\n\ntypically occurs following a streptococcal infection, such as from the throat or skin. It is characterised by nephritic syndrome, which includes haematuria, hypertension and oliguria. This condition does not match the presentation of isolated nephrotic syndrome symptoms like significant oedema and proteinuria without previous infection history.\n\nSystemic lupus erythematosus\n\n(SLE) can cause lupus nephritis, which involves various renal pathologies triggered by SLE. SLE often presents with additional symptoms such as a rash, arthritis, or serositis. The presence of only nephrotic syndrome symptoms without other systemic signs makes SLE a less likely diagnosis in this case."
