      "explanation": "Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, causes:\n\nThe diagnosis here is that of granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA) an ANCA-positive vasculitis. The history of recurrent upper respiratory tract symptoms with renal failure and cANCA blood test confirms this diagnosis. By far the most common form of renal failure in this condition is rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, with its severe and rapid loss of kidney function, haematuria and proteinuria. A renal biopsy demonstrates glomerular crescents.\n\nMesangial hypercellularity would be seen on biopsy in diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis. This is not typically linked with GPA. It is associated with post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis in children, and also with systemic lupus erythematosus.\n\nBiopsy findings of subendothelial and mesangial immune deposits - a 'tram track' appearance - is indicative of membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis. This is less likely to be seen in GPA, although would also present with haematuria and proteinuria. Type 1, the most common form, is typically associated with cryoglobulinaemia and hepatitis C.\n\nRenal biopsy demonstrating fusion of podocytes and effacement of foot processes suggests minimal change disease. This is classically seen in children and is usually idiopathic. It also presents with nephrotic syndrome.\n\nA thickened basement membrane is indicative of membranous glomerulonephritis. This is the most common form of glomerulonephritis, but not typically associated with GPA. It presents with nephrotic syndrome or proteinuria. It is often idiopathic or associated with drugs."
