      "explanation": "Alport's syndrome - X-linked dominant (in the majority)\n\nThe correct answer is X-linked dominant.\n\nThe presentation is suggestive of Alport's syndrome, which usually presents in childhood with progressive renal impairment, hearing loss and eye abnormalities. A renal biopsy demonstrates splitting of the lamina densa on electron microscopy. In most cases, Alport's syndrome is inherited in an X-linked dominant fashion.\n\nAutosomal dominant is incorrect. There are variants of Alport's syndrome which are inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion, but these occur more rarely than the X-linked dominant inheritance pattern.\n\nAutosomal recessive is also incorrect. 10-15% of Alport's syndrome cases are found to be inherited in this way - the most common is still X-linked dominant. A classic example of an autosomal recessive disease is cystic fibrosis.\n\nMitochondrial diseases are inherited via the maternal line since mitochondrial DNA is solely inherited from the mother (zygotic cytoplasm comes entirely from the ovum, not the sperm). These conditions are fairly rare, an example being Leber's hereditary optic neuropathy.\n\nX-linked recessive is incorrect. Common examples of these diseases include haemophilia and red-green colour blindness."
