      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Add morphine sulphate",
      "explanation": "Drugs for neuropathic pain are typically used as monotherapy, i.e. if not working then drugs should be switched, not added\n\nSwitch gabapentin to amitriptyline is the correct answer. The history and examination provided above are consistent with neuropathic pain secondary to a metastatic spinal deposit. The current NICE guidelines on the management of neuropathic pain advise amitriptyline, duloxetine, gabapentin or pregabalin as initial treatment. In the event of treatment failure, as described above, the NICE guidelines advise switching to one of the remaining 3 initial treatment medications rather than starting a new medication in addition to the ineffective medication. Amitriptyline is also a mild sedative and if taken at night may help with his sleep disturbance.\n\nAdd amitriptyline is an incorrect answer. Amitriptyline is the next appropriate agent to start and may help with this patient's sleep disturbance but it should be switched with gabapentin and utilised as a monotherapy in accordance with the current NICE guidelines on the management of neuropathic pain.\n\nAdd buprenorphine transdermal patch is an incorrect answer. A buprenorphine transdermal patch is not recommended in the NICE guidelines for the initial treatment of neuropathic pain. It is typically indicated for the treatment of pain where an opioid medication is necessary for maintaining adequate analgesia.\n\nAdd morphine sulphate is an incorrect answer. Neuropathic pain is typically opioid resistant. Morphine sulphate would be ineffective treatment in this case.\n\nSwitch gabapentin to carbamazepine is an incorrect answer. Carbamazepine is the initial treatment of trigeminal neuralgia recommended by the NICE guidelines. It is not recommended for the management of neuropathic pain excluding trigeminal neuralgia."
