      "id": 11,
      "question": "A 43-year-old woman attends the emergency department with acute confusion, left sided leg weakness and behavioural disturbance. She has a past medical history of multiple sclerosis for which she is taking natalizumab.\n\nShe is haemodynamically stable. On examination she is not orientated to time or place and is being aggressive, which her husband says is completely out of character. She has 3/5 weakness in her left leg. The rest of the examination is unremarkable.\n\nHer blood tests are:\n\n\nHb\t136 g/L\tMale: (135-180)\nFemale: (115 - 160)\nPlatelets\t336 * 109/L\t(150 - 400)\nWBC\t14.2 * 109/L\t(4.0 - 11.0)\nNa+\t140 mmol/L\t(135 - 145)\nK+\t4.6 mmol/L\t(3.5 - 5.0)\nUrea\t5.2 mmol/L\t(2.0 - 7.0)\nCreatinine\t106 µmol/L\t(55 - 120)\nCRP\t78 mg/L\t(< 5)\n\nWhich is the most likely diagnosis?",
