      "explanation": "Natalizumab can cause reactivation of the JC virus causing progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML)\n\nProgressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) is associated with natalizumab treatment in multiple sclerosis (MS).  It is caused by reactivation of the JC virus due to immunodeficiency secondary to natalizumab (it can also occur with a few other MS modifying drug treatments and HIV). The reactivation of the JC virus causes demyelination and the symptoms a patient has depends on the site and extent of the demyelination. The most common symptoms are confusion, behavioural change, ataxia, hemiparesis and visual deficits.\n\nBacterial meningitis can cause confusion and behavioural disturbance but is unlikely to cause left leg weakness. Additionally there is no mention of pyrexia and the inflammatory markers, while slightly up, are not significantly raised.\n\nThere is no preceding history of minor infection to suggest autoimmune encephalitis. The symptoms are not typical for autoimmune encephalitis which tends to develop subacutely (over days) rather than acutely.\n\nThere is no history of a headache to suggest intracranial haemorrhage and this is unlikely to cause unilateral weakness.\n\nThere are no risk factors for exposure to toxoplasmosis and there are no systemic symptoms to suggest this either."
