      "explanation": "Hemiballism results in involuntary, sudden, jerking movements which occur contralateral to the side of the lesion\n\nThis patient is displaying symptoms of hemiballismus - characterised by unilateral, repetitive, uncontrollable limb movements. These often decrease in intensity when the patient is relaxed or asleep.\n\nLeft basal ganglia\n\nis the correct answer. The lesion is contralateral to the side experiencing the movements. In hemiballismus, the lesion is most commonly found in the basal ganglia.\n\nLeft motor cortex\n\nis incorrect, a lesion here would cause a decrease in function of the right side of the body.\n\nPsychosomatic\n\nis incorrect. Although these movements reduce in sleep they are still there but at a lower intensity.\n\nRight basal ganglia\n\nis incorrect. In hemiballismus, the lesion is on the contralateral side. You would expect a right-sided basal ganglia lesion to cause left-sided movement disorder.\n\nRight motor cortex\n\nis incorrect. A lesion here would lead to decreased function of the left side of the body."
