      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Right temporal",
      "explanation": "Inferior homonymous quadrantanopias are caused by lesions of the superior optic radiations in the parietal lobe\n\nRight parietal is correct as damage to this region may cause the following symptoms which are present in this case: left-sided hemi-spatial neglect and left-sided homonymous inferior quadrantanopia. Other signs of parietal lobe damage include: loss of sensation like touch, alexia, agraphia, acalculia,, apraxias or astereognosis (inability to identify things placed in the hand).\n\nLeft parietal is incorrect, because damage to the left would cause right sided signs and symptoms not seen in this case.\n\nLeft temporal lobe is incorrect as damage to this region may cause the following: a change in behavior and emotions, forgetfulness, disruption in the sense of smell, taste, and hearing, language and speech disorders, including Wernicke's (receptive) dysphasia or superior homonymous quadrantanopia. Right temporal lobe is also incorrect as damage to this region would not cause the symptoms described in this case.\n\nLeft frontal lobe is incorrect as damage to the frontal lobe may result in: anosmia, Broca's (expressive) dysphasia, changes in personality and motor deficits on the right-side of the body."
