      "id": 36,
      "question": "A 75-year-old man attends the falls clinic with a 1-month history of increased falls. He reports falling five times over the past month. Previously, he was independently mobile, but now requires the assistance of a walking frame. His wife reports intermittent episodes of confusion followed by lucid periods.\n\nHe has a history of hypertension, alcoholic fatty liver disease, and gout.\n\nOn examination, he has normal power and sensation in the upper limbs. In the lower limbs, power is generally good, but he has a shuffling gait. Cranial nerve examination is unremarkable except for an inability to abduct his left eye on lateral gaze.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
      "options": [
        "Hepatic encephalopathy",
        "Lewy body dementia",
        "Normal-pressure hydrocephalus",
        "Subdural haematoma",
        "Wernicke's encephalopathy"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Normal-pressure hydrocephalus",
      "explanation": "Fluctuating confusion/consciousness? - subdural haematoma\n\nIn the elderly, especially those with a history of alcohol misuse, recurrent falls and fluctuating confusion should raise concern for a subdural haematoma. The left abducens (cranial nerve VI) palsy may represent a false localising sign due to raised intracranial pressure or midline shift from a space-occupying lesion. A CT head should be performed to assess for a subdural haematoma, which typically appears as a crescent-shaped collection of blood, most often due to rupture of bridging veins.\n\nHepatic encephalopathy\n\nis a differential in patients with alcohol-related liver disease. However, in the absence of decompensation features (e.g. ascites, jaundice) and the presence of a focal cranial nerve deficit, it is less likely.\n\nLewy body dementia\n\ncan present with fluctuating cognition, hallucinations, and Parkinsonism (e.g. shuffling gait, rigidity). However, it would not explain the acute onset or cranial nerve palsy.\n\nNormal-pressure hydrocephalus\n\npresents with the classic triad of gait disturbance (typically a magnetic, broad-based gait), cognitive impairment, and urinary incontinence. It tends to develop gradually, whereas this case is more acute and focal.\n\nWernicke’s encephalopathy\n\nis possible given the history of alcohol misuse. It typically presents with ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and confusion. However, the isolated abducens palsy without nystagmus or cerebellar signs, and the pattern of fluctuating confusion, point more towards a structural lesion such as a subdural haematoma."
