      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Carbamazepine",
      "explanation": "Focal seizures not responding to first-line drug - try lamotrigine or levetiracetam (i.e. the first-line drug not already tried) and if neither help then carbamazepine\n\nLamotrigine\n\nis the correct answer. The patient presents with simple focal seizures (i.e. seizures affecting one part of the body with preserved awareness). This is likely a consequence of perinatal hypoxic brain injury in this context. The first choice medication in this circumstance to prevent further seizures is either lamotrigine or levetiracetam. If one fails to adequately control seizures, the other is generally trialled.\n\nCarbamazepine\n\nis incorrect. This is a possible option to treat refractory focal seizures that have not responded to either lamotrigine or carbamazepine. It is not the first-line option.\n\nClobazam\n\nis incorrect. This is typically used as an add-on therapy in difficult-to-control epilepsy on standard treatment. It would not be used as first-line treatment or monotherapy.\n\nLorazepam\n\nis incorrect. This is used in acute active (typically generalized seizures) treatment. In this context, the patient is not seizing and requires treatment to prevent further seizures. Lorazepam is addictive, short-acting and can cause drowsiness so this would not be appropriate.\n\nSodium valproate\n\nis incorrect. This medication is effective in most forms of epilepsy. However, it is teratogenic and is a less suitable option for women of childbearing age."
