      "explanation": "Baclofen and gabapentin are first-line for spasticity in multiple sclerosis\n\nGabapentin is the correct answer. This patient has symptoms and signs of spasticity secondary multiple sclerosis as evidenced by increased tone and rigidity in her upper limbs. The first-line treatment for spasticity in MS is either gabapentin or baclofen.\n\nDantrolene is incorrect. This is a second-line option for the treatment of spasticity in MS. It is a muscle relaxant. It is also used in the treatment of malignant hyperthermia.\n\nDiazepam is incorrect. This benzodiazepine medication is a second-line option in the management of MS-associated spasticity if gabapentin or baclofen fail.\n\nTizanidine is incorrect. This is a short-acting muscle relaxant that can be used second line in spasticity associated with MS.\n\nRopinirole is incorrect. This medication is used to treat Parkinson's disease. There is increased tone in MS-associated spasticity but this is due to loss descending inhibition from the brain to the spinal cord, such that muscles become overactive and causes and ongoing level of contraction and resistance felt to passive stretch. This is a different mechanism to the increased tone associated with Parkinson's disease and therefore ropinirole is inappropriate."
