      "explanation": "Phaeochromocytoma, renal cell cancer → ?Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome\n\nThis patient has presented with a phaeochromocytoma secondary to the rare genetic disorder Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome (VHL). This syndrome is a result of a mutation in the VHL tumour suppressor gene situated on chromosome 3 and predisposes patients to visceral cysts and tumours. The commonest cancer associated with the condition is\n\nrenal cell\n\ncarcinoma and therefore this should be screened for in any patient confirmed with VHL syndrome.\n\nVHL syndrome is mostly associated with phaeochromocytoma, a tumour of the adrenal gland affecting the inner medulla region, not the outer\n\nadrenal cortex\n\n. As the medulla region is responsible for the release of catecholamines such as adrenaline, norepinephrine, and dopamine, phaeochromocytomas result in a massive increase in these hormones and the classic features of tachycardia, hypertension and sweating etc.\n\nVHL syndrome is associated with malignancies and cysts of multiple organs, specifically endocrine organs, however, defects of the\n\novaries\n\nare not commonly associated with the condition.\n\nPancreatic\n\ncyst is one of the most common features of VHL syndrome however malignancy of the organ is rare and far less common than renal cell cancers.\n\nAlthough predominately endocrine organs the thyroid and parathyroid are not commonly affected in VHL syndrome and therefore\n\nparafollicular cell\n\ncancer is rare."
