      "explanation": "Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy is classically associated with seizures in the morning/following sleep deprivation\n\nJuvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME), also known as Janz syndrome, typically presents in teenage years with a triad of:\n\nMyoclonic seizures (sudden, shock-like muscle jerks), present in all cases by definition, these may cause patients to drop things.\nGeneralised tonic-clonic seizures, present in 2/3 of the cases, may develop later.\nAbsence seizures (daytime 'staring spells'), present in 1/3 of the cases.\n\nAll of the different types of seizures in JME are more likely to occur in the morning, within hours of waking, and are worsened/triggered by sleep deprivation. JME is more common in girls. Up to 50% of cases may be photosensitive. EEG may show generalised spike and wave or polyspike and slow-wave discharges. Treatment is with sodium valproate.\n\nAtonic seizures are a type of generalised seizures characterised by loss of tone. While dropping things can be a feature in atonic seizures, they are more commonly described as 'drop attacks', where patients suddenly fall or limp, or their head may slump. Atonic seizures would not explain the staring spells.\n\nFocal seizures (partial seizures) can be divided into focal aware (simple partial), awareness unknown, or focal impaired awareness (complex partial). They may be motor (e.g. Jacksonian march), non-motor (e.g. jamais vu) or have other features such as aura. In this child, the presence of 'staring spells' (absence seizures) rules out partial seizures, as they are a generalised form of seizures.\n\nInfantile spasms (West's syndrome) are brief spasms in the first few months of life (up to 6 months) associated with progressive developmental delay and a poor prognosis. This child does not have developmental delay and her age makes this diagnosis extremely unlikely.\n\nLennox-Gastaut syndrome may be the progression into early childhood (onset around 1-5 years) of infantile spasms, typically presenting with falls, jerks and atypical absence seizures and in most cases associated with considerable developmental delay. Again, this child does not have developmental delay and her age makes this diagnosis less likely."
