      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Neurofibromatosis type 2 (NF2)",
      "explanation": "Loss of corneal reflex - think acoustic neuroma\n\nNeurofibromatosis type 2 (NF2)\n\nis correct. The clinical features are suggestive of bilateral acoustic neuromas. Weber's and Rinne's tests are consistent with bilateral sensorineural hearing loss. Bilateral vestibular schwannomas are frequently seen in NF2.\n\nLi-Fraumeni syndrome\n\nis incorrect. Li-Fraumeni syndrome (LFS) is an inherited predisposition to a wide range of certain cancers caused by pathogenic TP53 germline variants. Both children and adults are susceptible to developing multiple cancers, most notably soft-tissue and bone sarcomas, breast cancer, brain tumours, adrenocortical carcinoma and leukaemia. Acoustic neuromas are not classically associated with this condition.\n\nNeurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1)\n\nis incorrect. NF1 is a multisystem disorder characterized by multiple cafe au lait macules, intertriginous freckling, multiple cutaneous neurofibromas, and learning disability or behaviour problems. Acoustic neuromas are not classically associated with this condition.\n\nTuberous sclerosis\n\nis incorrect. This condition can manifest in multiple organ systems with the cutaneous and renal systems being the most commonly affected. Renal angiomyolipoma, cysts, and carcinoma can also occur. Acoustic neuromas are not classically associated with this condition.\n\nVon Hippel-Lindau syndrome\n\nis incorrect. Von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) syndrome is characterized by hemangioblastomas of the brain, spinal cord, and retina; renal cysts and clear cell renal cell carcinoma. Acoustic neuromas are not classically associated with this condition."
