      "explanation": "The medial longitudinal fasciculus is located in the paramedian area of the midbrain and pons\n\nLeft paramedian area of the midbrain and pons is correct as the left medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) is located in this region. The left sided lesion to the MLF is evidenced by the conjugate gaze palsy with the ipsilateral left eye unable to adduct and the contralateral right abduction nystagmus. The described clinical manifestation is more complex, with optic neuritis of the left eye evident from the pain, reduced visual acuity, RAPD and optic disc pallor on this side. Therefore demyelination of the left optic nerve would also be seen on MRI.\n\nLeft parietal lobe is incorrect as a lesion of the left parietal lobe optic radiation would typically cause an inferior homonymous quadrantanopia.\n\nOptic chiasm is incorrect as a lesion at the optic chiasm would result in a loss of both temporal (peripheral) visual fields.\n\nRight occipital cortex is incorrect as active disease or damage in the right occipital cortex would generally cause visual disturbances and a homonymous hemianopia (if in inferior bank of occipital cortex would cause superior homonymous quadrantanopia, whereas superior bank would cause inferior homonymous quadrantanopia) and the visual field examination in this case is normal.\n\nRight paramedian midbrain and pons is incorrect as a lesion in this region would affect the right side of the medial longitudinal fasciculus and cause the mirror opposite of the symptoms described above."
