      "correctText": "Aspirin 300mg once daily and manage conservatively",
      "explanation": "Large artery acute ischaemic stroke? Consider mechanical clot retrieval\n\nThe correct answer is to refer the patient to the interventional radiology team for thrombectomy. In the scenario presented the patient has had a 'wake-up stroke' and has been previously well in the last 24 hours. The NICE guidelines (NG 128) recommend that thrombectomy can be offered to patients with a confirmed ischaemic stroke of the anterior circulation confirmed on CT angiogram or MR angiogram if the patient was last known to be well in the last 24 hours. Importantly, this also includes 'wake-up' strokes.\n\nReferral to the neurosurgical team is incorrect as the most appropriate next management. Neurosurgical intervention may be required for a decompressive craniectomy, however, this is usually only necessary in cases of haemorrhagic strokes or within the first 48 hours of an ischaemic stroke where there is evidence of a compressive effect. In this case, more immediate management of the clot is required.\n\nThrombolysis with alteplase is indicated for patients in whom it is not contraindicated within the first 4.5 hours of symptom onset. It is not indicated in the case of 'wake-up strokes'. In this scenario, the last time the patient was known to be well is over 4.5 hours and hence this option would not be indicated. If there was no access to a local thrombectomy service then conservative management would have to be considered at this point.\n\nStarting apixaban at this stage would not be appropriate. The patient has atrial fibrillation and has a CHA²DS²-VASc score of 4 and so would be a candidate for long term anticoagulation, however, anticoagulation is not indicated in the acute phase.\n\nStarting aspirin 300mg as soon as possible is indicated in patients who have a confirmed stroke. However, the patient should be referred to the thrombectomy service in the first instance and it would be appropriate to withhold aspirin until a decision is made about this treatment. The patient should also be considered for thrombectomy before being simply managed conservatively."
