      "correctText": "The dorsal column and ventral spinothalamic tracts of the spinal cord",
      "explanation": "In subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord, the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts are affected\n\nSubacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord (SACD) occurs as a result of vitamin B12 deficiency. Vitamin B12  is absorbed in the terminal ileum, along with intrinsic factor.\n\nPatients at high risk of vitamin B12 deficiency include:\n\nVegans (due to decreased dietary intake)\nHistory of gastric or intestinal surgery\nPernicious anaemia\nMalabsorption (especially crohn's disease as it often affects the terminal ileum)\nMedications including proton-pump inhibitors and metformin can also reduce absorption of vitamin B12.\n\nSACD affects the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts of the spinal cord, resulting in loss of proprioception and vibration sense, followed by distal paraesthesia. It typically presents with a mixture of upper and lower motor neuron signs, classically with extensor plantars, brisk knee reflexes, and absent ankle jerks. Therapy with vitamin B12 results in partial to full recovery, depending on the duration and extent of neurodegeneration.\n\nIn a patient with both vitamin B12 and folic acid deficiency, the vitamin B12 deficiency must be treated first to avoid precipitating subacute combined degeneration of the cord."
