      "id": 82,
      "question": "A 37-year-old man is admitted to the neurology ward with a 10-day history of worsening headache and nausea. He has no significant medical history but recently underwent dental extraction for a molar abscess. On examination, he is alert but has left-sided facial weakness and mild papilloedema. CT brain shows a solitary ring-enhancing lesion in the right frontal lobe with surrounding oedema. What is the most appropriate initial intravenous antibiotic regimen?",
