      "explanation": "Brain abscess: IV 3rd-generation cephalosporin + metronidazole\n\nIV 3rd-generation cephalosporin + metronidazole\n\nis the correct answer as this combination provides broad-spectrum cover against both aerobic and anaerobic bacteria, including Streptococci and anaerobes commonly implicated in brain abscesses, especially those arising from oral or dental sources. UK guidelines recommend this regimen as first-line empirical therapy for brain abscess prior to culture results. The addition of metronidazole ensures adequate coverage of anaerobic organisms which are frequently encountered in abscesses related to dental sepsis.\n\nIV vancomycin + meropenem\n\ncould be considered if there was suspicion of resistant organisms (e.g., post-neurosurgical infection or immunocompromised patients), but it is not first-line for community-acquired brain abscess following dental infection. Meropenem does provide good CNS penetration, but vancomycin targets Gram-positive organisms like MRSA rather than typical causative pathogens seen here.\n\nIV flucloxacillin + gentamicin\n\nwould be used primarily for staphylococcal infections, such as endocarditis or bone/joint infections, and lacks sufficient anaerobic coverage needed for an abscess likely seeded from oral flora. Gentamicin also has poor CNS penetration and would not be effective monotherapy for a cerebral abscess.\n\nIV amoxicillin + aciclovir\n\nmight be used empirically where viral encephalitis (such as HSV) or Listeria is suspected, particularly in older adults or immunosuppressed individuals, but does not provide adequate anaerobic cover nor target typical pathogens in this scenario.\n\nIV piperacillin-tazobactam alone\n\n, while broad-spectrum and covering some anaerobes, may not achieve optimal concentrations within the CNS compared to a third-generation cephalosporin plus metronidazole. It is more often reserved for severe hospital-acquired infections."
