      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Myasthenia gravis",
      "explanation": "Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy: facial weakness (e.g. difficulty closing eyes, smiling, blowing), winged scapula and weakness of the shoulderm upper arm and hip girdle muscles\n\nFacioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy\n\n(FSHMD) is a form of muscular dystrophy mainly resulting in weakness of the facial muscles, the upper arms and the hip girdle. Symptoms normal develop from the age of 15 to 30 years and weakness can be asymmetrical. Other associated symptoms of FSHMD include high frequency-hearing loss and abnormality of the retinal arteries (although vision is rarely affected). Unlikely other forms of muscular pathologies, FSHMD is rarely associated with contracture, respiratory issues or weakness affecting eye and tongue movement. As an autosomal dominant genetic disease, children of affected parents (in this case the patient's father) have a 50% chance of inheriting the genetic abnormality and therefore the condition (the patient is affected but his sister is not). There is no cure for FSHMD and management is supportive.\n\nAmyotrophic lateral sclerosis\n\n(ALS), a form of motor neurone disease, is a neurodegenerative condition causing progressive loss of voluntary muscle control. As the disease progresses patients commonly develop global weakness with difficulty speaking and swallowing.  Approximately 50% of patients suffer at least mild cognitive issues. Most cases of ALS are sporadic with no clear cause or family history.\n\nBecker muscular dystrophy\n\nis another form of dystrophy associated with slowly progressive muscle weakness of the legs and pelvis. Weakness of the upper limbs is less severe than in other muscular dystrophies and facial weakness, hearing and ocular vessel changes are not associated with the condition. Becker's muscular dystrophy is an x-linked recessive genetic disorder which would present with a similar family tree as seen in this case (only the male member being affected) however an autosomal dominant condition such as FSHMD may present in the same manner with a 50% chance of offspring being affected regardless of sex.\n\nMyasthenia gravis (MG)\n\nis a neuromuscular disease due to auto-immune antibodies that inhibit or destroy nicotinic acetylcholine receptors (AChR) at the junction between the nerve and muscle. Patients normal experience fatigable weakness of eye movements and ptosis (drooping of the eyelids) instead of issues closing the eyes. Again MG is not associated with hearing and ocular vessel changes.\n\nMyotonic dystrophy\n\n(MD) is another form of dystrophy but, as the name suggests, is commonly associated with myotonia which is the delayed relaxation of muscles after contraction. The typical age of onset of MD is 40 years and non-muscular issues include cataracts, intellectual impairment and cardiac conduction abnormalities."
