      "explanation": "Visual field defects:\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nRight superior homonymous quadrantanopia\n\n. This is because the left temporal lobe, where the meningioma was located, contains Meyer's loop of the optic radiation. Damage to this area during surgery can result in a visual field defect known as 'pie in the sky', which is formally called the right superior homonymous quadrantanopia. This condition manifests as a loss of vision in the upper-right quadrant of both eyes.\n\nThe first option,\n\nRight inferior homonymous quadrantanopia\n\n, would be expected if there was a lesion in the parietal lobe, not the temporal lobe. The parietal lobe contains the superior optic radiation, and damage here affects the lower contralateral visual field.\n\nLeft inferior homonymous quadrantanopia\n\nand\n\nLeft superior homonymous quadrantanopia\n\nare incorrect because they suggest damage to the right hemisphere of the brain. In this scenario, we know that it is specifically the left temporal lobe that has been affected by surgery.\n\nFinally,\n\nRight homonymous hemianopia with macula sparing\n\nwould suggest a lesion at or around the primary visual cortex (occipital lobe). This type of visual field loss involves an entire half-field (right side) in both eyes rather than just one quadrant. Macular sparing often occurs due to dual blood supply from the middle cerebral artery and posterior cerebral artery to the macular region of the visual cortex. However, this does not correlate with our case as the patient had surgery on the left temporal lobe for meningioma removal."
