      "explanation": "Sensorineural hearing loss is the most common complication following meningitis\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nSensorineural hearing loss\n\n. This is because the patient's symptoms and management suggest a diagnosis of bacterial meningitis, which is an infection of the meninges in the brain. The most common long-term complication of bacterial meningitis, according to UK guidelines, is sensorineural hearing loss. It occurs due to damage to the cochlear nerve or inner ear structures during inflammation. Sensorineural hearing loss can be bilateral or unilateral, and may range from mild to profound.\n\nEncephalitis\n\nis not the most common long-term complication of bacterial meningitis. Encephalitis refers to inflammation of the brain parenchyma itself, often caused by viral infections such as Herpes simplex virus. While it can occur as a complication in severe cases of bacterial meningitis, it is not as frequent as sensorineural hearing loss.\n\nHydrocephalus\n\n, an abnormal accumulation of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) in the ventricles of the brain, can also occur following bacterial meningitis due to obstruction of CSF outflow pathways by inflammatory exudates. However, this tends to present acutely during illness rather than being a long-term complication.\n\nThe option\n\nSeizures\n\ncan be seen in acute stages of bacterial meningitis due to irritation of the cerebral cortex but are less likely as a long-term sequelae unless there has been significant brain damage or formation of scar tissue leading to epilepsy.\n\nFinally,\n\nCerebral abscess\n\n, while a serious potential complication, is relatively rare compared with sensorineural hearing loss following bacterial meningitis. Cerebral abscesses are usually secondary to direct spread from paranasal sinuses or middle ear infections or hematogenous spread from distant sites like endocarditis but are not typically associated with meningitis."
