      "explanation": "CT head showing temporal lobe changes - think herpes simplex encephalitis\n\nThis is a typical case of herpes simplex encephalitis with characteristic clinical signs and findings on CT imaging given the presence of tenuous low-density changes within regions of the anterior and medial temporal lobe as well as the island of Reil.\n\nCytomegalovirus (CMV) encephalitis is a CNS infection that almost always develops in the context of profound immunosuppression and there is no evidence to suggest that here given the absence of prior medical history, therefore is the incorrect answer.\n\nJapanese encephalitis is a disease spread which spreads via mosquito bites. Symptoms usually take 5-15 days to occur and involve fever, headache, vomiting, confusion, and difficulty moving. There is no travel history here and given her symptoms on presentation this is unlikely to be the case. In addition, imaging regardless of MRI or CT scanning shows classical bilateral thalamic involvement.\n\nLimbic encephalitis encompasses a group of autoimmune conditions which cause inflammation of predominantly the limbic system and other regions of the brain. The cardinal sign of limbic encephalitis is a severe impairment of short-term memory; other symptoms may include confusion, psychiatric symptoms, and seizures. Brain magnetic resonance imaging shows medial temporal lobe abnormalities typical of autoimmune limbic encephalitis in suspected cases, it should be noted that other diseases can have a similar appearance on imaging.\n\nVaricella-zoster virus (VZV) reactivation is one of the most common neurological infectious diseases and VZV the second most frequent virus causing encephalitis or meningitis. It develops following reactivation of the latently persistent virus in nerve ganglia after initial infection with chickenpox. This is unlikely to be the case here."
