      "explanation": "Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy is classically associated with seizures in the morning/following sleep deprivation\n\nThe description of preceding absence episodes followed by myoclonus is most typical of juvenile myoclonic epilepsy. As in this case, the condition most typically occurs in adolescent females following a period of sleep deprivation, suggested by exam stress and sleepovers in this vignette. There is usually a good response to sodium valproate.\n\nBenign rolandic epilepsy typically occurs in younger children than in this vignette. It also more commonly presents with paraesthesia than myoclonus and absence episodes.\n\nLennox-Gestaut syndrome presents in early childhood in children younger than described in this vignette. Although it also presents with absence episodes and jerks 90% of children have an underlying learning difficulty, which is not described in this vignette.\n\nAlthough this vignette describes absence episodes, typical absence seizures occur in younger children and are not associated with the myoclonus described in this vignette.\n\nWest's syndrome is also known as infantile spasms. This condition occurs in infancy, unlike in this vignette. Children display a typical spasm beginning with flexion of the head, trunk and limbs followed by extension of the arms, which can repeat many times in a row. This condition is also associated with progressive learning difficulties, which is not described in this vignette."
