      "correctText": "Anti-acetylcholine receptor (anti-AChR)",
      "explanation": "Miller Fisher syndrome - areflexia, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia\n\nThis patient's presentation is most suggestive of Miller-Fisher syndrome (MFS), a variant of Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS). This is an immune-mediated demyelination of the peripheral nervous system, classically triggered by an infection such as\n\nCampylobacter jejuni\n\n- the history of diarrhoea alludes to this. As seen here, MFS presents with descending paralysis, rather than the ascending paralysis seen in other forms of GBS, along with ophthalmoplegia and areflexia. It is most strongly associated with the\n\nanti-GQ1b\n\nantibody, which is present in 90% of cases.\n\nAnti-MuSK\n\nantibody is incorrect. This is associated with myasthenia gravis (MG), not MFS. MG would instead present with excessive fatiguability and diurnal variation, and reflexes would be preserved. This scenario is more in keeping with MFS.\n\nThe\n\nanti-GM1\n\nantibody is associated with GBS. However, it is only present in 25% of patients. With regard to MFS specifically, anti-GQ1b is the correct answer.\n\nThe\n\nanti-AChR\n\nantibody is associated with MG. This would instead present with diurnal variation, excessive fatiguability and preserved reflexes, whereas the scenario here is more fitting with MFS.\n\nThe\n\nanti-VGCC\n\nantibody is associated with Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS). This is typically a paraneoplastic disorder and is often a differential of MG, although LEMS typically shows improvement following exercise, rather than excessive fatiguability. The history of diarrhoea in this scenario, along with descending paralysis, favours a diagnosis of MFS."
