      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Cyclophosphamide",
      "explanation": "IVIG is generally used first-line for Guillain-Barre syndrome\n\nThis patient has developed Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS), as demonstrated by his progressive weakness, starting in his legs, and subsequent respiratory failure. Mechanical ventilation is required in around 15% of cases. While it classically presents following an infection, 33% of cases are not linked with recent infection.\n\nThe first-line treatment is IV immunoglobulins (IV Ig), which neutralise the antibodies that result in GBS. This is therefore the correct answer.\n\nAciclovir is an antiviral that is not used in GBS. Rarely, transverse myelitis secondary to HSV can cause paralysis and be treated with aciclovir, though this is less likely than GBS in this case.\n\nCyclophosphamide is used as chemotherapy for malignancies and for immune suppression. It has no role in the treatment of GBS.\n\nMethylprednisolone is useful in cases of spinal cord injury, according to a recent Cochrane review.  However, steroids are not a part of GBS management, and may in fact slow recovery.\n\nPlasmapheresis is the other treatment modality that has a proven survival benefit in GBS. It works by removing harmful antibodies from circulation. It is not considered first-line as it is generally less available, frequently slower to initiate, and is equally effective to IV Ig. There is no additional benefit to plasmapheresis combines with IV Ig."
