      "id": 118,
      "question": "A 24-year-old female presents to her GP due to increased frequency of migraine attacks. She is now having around four migraines per month. Which type of medication would it be most appropriate to prescribe to reduce the frequency of migraine attacks?",
      "options": [
        "Specific 5-HT2 agonist",
        "5-HT1 antagonist",
        "Tricyclic antidepressant",
        "Beta-blocker",
        "Specific 5-HT1 agonist"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Tricyclic antidepressant",
      "explanation": "Migraine\n\nThe correct answer is beta-blocker. According to NICE guidelines, beta-blockers (specifically propranolol) are recommended as first-line prophylactic treatment for migraine. Beta-blockers work by blocking beta-adrenergic receptors, reducing neuronal excitability and preventing vasodilation of cerebral vessels. They are particularly suitable for this patient as she is experiencing frequent migraines (four per month), which meets the threshold for considering prophylactic treatment.\n\nSpecific 5-HT2 agonist\n\nis incorrect. 5-HT2 receptor agonists are not used in migraine treatment. In fact, 5-HT2 receptor activation can cause vasoconstriction and might potentially worsen migraine symptoms.\n\n5-HT1 antagonist\n\nis incorrect. 5-HT1 antagonists would counteract the desired effect in migraine treatment. 5-HT1 receptor activation is actually beneficial in acute migraine treatment, as demonstrated by the effectiveness of triptans (5-HT1 agonists).\n\nTricyclic antidepressant\n\nis incorrect. While tricyclic antidepressants (such as amitriptyline) can be used for migraine prophylaxis, they are typically considered as second-line treatment after beta-blockers according to UK guidelines. They may be particularly useful if the patient has concurrent depression or tension-type headaches.\n\nSpecific 5-HT1 agonist\n\nis incorrect. While drugs in this class (triptans) are indeed used in migraine treatment, they are for acute treatment of migraine attacks rather than prophylaxis. They work by causing vasoconstriction and reducing inflammation through 5-HT1 receptor activation, but are not suitable for preventing migraine attacks."
