      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Regular chest physiotherapy",
      "explanation": "Motor neuron disease - treatment: NIV is better than riluzole\n\nThe correct answer to this question is\n\nNon-invasive ventilation\n\n. Non-invasive ventilation (NIV) has been shown to provide the greatest effect on survival in patients with motor neuron disease. The National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends offering NIV to people with motor neuron disease who have respiratory symptoms or signs, or if their forced vital capacity is less than 80% predicted. This intervention helps to alleviate symptoms of nocturnal hypoventilation, improve quality of life, and prolong survival.\n\nRegular chest physiotherapy\n\nis an incorrect option. Although it can be beneficial for maintaining lung function and managing secretions in some patients with motor neuron disease, there is no evidence that regular chest physiotherapy alone significantly extends survival in these patients.\n\nTotal parental nutrition\n\nalso does not significantly impact survival in motor neuron disease. While it may be required in later stages of the disease when swallowing becomes difficult, total parenteral nutrition only serves as a means of providing essential nutrients and does not alter the course of the disease itself.\n\nThe option\n\nRiluzole\n\nis incorrect because although riluzole is currently the only medication approved by the Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency (MHRA) for treating motor neuron disease, its effect on survival is modest. It has been shown to extend life by only a few months.\n\nLastly,\n\nAntioxidant supplementation\n\ndoes not have a significant effect on survival either. While there are theories suggesting antioxidants might help reduce oxidative stress associated with motor neuron disease, clinical trials have yet to demonstrate a clear benefit in terms of survival or slowing progression of the disease. Therefore, antioxidant supplementation cannot be considered as having the greatest effect on survival compared to other options listed here."
