      "id": 127,
      "question": "A 28-year-old woman undergoes a spontaneous vaginal delivery at 37 weeks gestation. Unfortunately, her newborn develops widespread scalp and skin bruising 12 hours post-delivery. Her past medical history includes gastro-oesophageal reflux disease, depression, pre-eclampsia, and epilepsy. Her drug history includes aspirin, folic acid, omeprazole, phenytoin, and sertraline. She has also been on low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) throughout the antenatal period due to a previous unprovoked deep vein thrombosis.\n\nWhich drug was most likely implicated?",
      "options": [
        "Aspirin",
        "Low molecular weight heparin (LMWH)",
        "Omeprazole",
        "Phenytoin",
        "Sertraline"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Phenytoin",
      "explanation": "Phenytoin induces vitamin K metabolism, which can cause a relative vitamin K deficiency, creating the potential for heamorrhagic disease of the newborn\n\nThe foetus most certainly has an early onset (<24 hours) haemorrhagic disease of the newborn. The early onset form of this disease is usually a consequence of maternal medications.\n\nPhenytoin\n\nis correct. Phenytoin induces vitamin K metabolism, which can cause a relative vitamin K deficiency, creating the potential for haemorrhagic disease of the newborn. Some guidelines recommend vitamin K supplementation of 10 to 20 mg orally per day starting at 36 weeks gestation and 1 mg given IM or IV to the newborn.\n\nAspirin\n\nis incorrect.  Low-dose aspirin is often used during pregnancy, most commonly to prevent or delay the onset of pre-eclampsia. Daily low-dose aspirin use in pregnancy is considered safe and is associated with a low likelihood of serious maternal, or fetal complications. The most recent Cochrane meta-analysis did not find an increased risk of neonatal intracranial haemorrhage or other neonatal hemorrhagic complications. Therefore, this is a less likely cause.\n\nLow molecular weight heparin (LMWH)\n\nis incorrect. Unfractionated heparin (UFH) and low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) do not cross the placenta and are safe for the foetus.\n\nOmeprazole\n\nis incorrect. Omeprazole has been shown to cross the human placenta, however, it is not associated with neonatal bleeding.\n\nSertraline\n\nis incorrect. Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) have been reported to inhibit serotonin uptake into platelets, resulting in decreased platelet function and increased bleeding risk. However, the clinical features, in this case, are more in keeping with haemorrhagic disease of the newborn."
