      "explanation": "Restless legs syndrome - ferritin is the single most important blood test\n\nRestless leg symptoms are exacerbated by decreased iron stores as shown by recent studies. Patients with a new diagnosis of restless leg syndrome or patients with a recent exacerbation of symptoms should check their serum ferritin levels.\n\nCreatine kinase levels are usually elevated when there is muscle inflammation or myositis. An example being rhabdomyolysis where the breakdown of skeletal muscle tissue occurs causing muscle pain, tenderness, weakness and swelling; is associated with significantly elevated levels of creatine kinase. There isn't described in this case.\n\nAlanine transaminase may be useful to check to rule out any underlying chronic liver disease or cirrhosis which could exacerbate the restless leg symptoms. This is less likely to be abnormal given the absence of chronic liver disease.\n\nPotassium contributes to muscle function. Restless leg syndrome and muscle spasms can commonly be symptoms of low potassium predominantly in women. Some doctors recommend a potassium supplement to prevent restless leg syndrome. This is less likely to be the case here.\n\nHaemoglobin A1c is important to check since uncontrolled high blood sugars in people with diabetes can cause nerve damage, and may lead to diabetic peripheral neuropathy. Damage to the nerves of the feet and lower leg from peripheral neuropathy is a contributor to restless leg syndrome."
