      "explanation": "If subarachnoid haemorrhage is suspected but a CT head done > 6 hours after symptom onset is normal, a lumbar puncture should be done to confirm or exclude the diagnosis\n\nThis history and examination are strongly suggestive of subarachnoid haemorrhage (SAH). The 2022 NICE guidelines state that a CT head without contrast can exclude SAH if performed within 6 hours of the onset of symptoms. In this case, a CT is performed at 8 hours and therefore a lumbar puncture to test for xanthochromia should be performed 12 hours after the onset of symptoms. Therefore,\n\nperform a lumbar puncture in 4 hours\n\nis correct.\n\nA\n\nCT angiography of the head\n\nis of little benefit to diagnose or exclude SAH. NICE guidelines only support the use of CT head without contrast. However, CT angiography of the head would be indicated if a SAH is confirmed either with the non-contrast scan or via detection of xanthochromia in the CSF. This is to identify the cause of the bleeding and to guide treatment.\n\nPerform a lumbar puncture immediately\n\nwould not be appropriate as 12 hours have not yet passed since the onset of symptoms. At this stage, spectrophotometry may be falsely negative for xanthochromia.\n\nPerform a lumbar puncture in 16 hours\n\nwould not be appropriate because it creates an unnecessary delay in the diagnosis. In some cases, it may be appropriate to wait for senior support to perform a lumbar puncture but delaying the lumbar puncture to 24 hours after the onset of headache would be unnecessary.\n\nWhilst headache and meningism are associated with bacterial meningitis, the absence of temperature and normal white cell count and CRP makes this less likely. The description of thunderclap headache is also far more suggestive of SAH than meningitis. Therefore, there is no indication to\n\nstart antibiotics for meningitis\n\n."
