      "explanation": "Percutaneous gastrostomy tube (PEG) is the preferred way to support nutrition in patents with motor neuron disease\n\nPercutaneous gastrostomy tube (PEG)\n\nis correct. The use of a PEG is the preferred way to support nutrition and has been associated with prolonged survival in patients with MND.\n\nNasogastric tube\n\nis incorrect. A nasogastric feeding tube may be used as an emergency measure however it is not recommended for long-term use.\n\nNasojejunal tube\n\nis incorrect. Nasojejunal tubes are generally recommended if the stomach needs to be bypassed (e.g. due to severe gastro-oesophageal reflux disease, very high risk of aspiration, gastric outflow obstruction or following gastric surgery). None of these indications are present in this case.\n\nRadiologically inserted gastrostomy (RIG)\n\nis incorrect. A PEG is the standard and most reliable way to perform a gastrostomy but it is not possible in all patients, in which case a RIG is a very safe alternative. A PEG is inserted via endoscopy and thus conscious sedation is required, and patients need to be able to lie comfortably flat for 30 minutes. In MND patients with significant respiratory weakness lying flat might be very difficult, and such patients might also be very sensitive to even small doses of sedatives. If a PEG is felt unsafe to do, then a RIG is an excellent alternative since it can be performed without sedation and the patient sat up. In this case, the patient does not have a significant demonstrable respiratory muscle weakness given the normal forced vital capacity (FVC) and thus a PEG is the preferred modality.\n\nTotal parenteral nutrition\n\nis incorrect. Total parenteral nutrition (TPN) should only be commenced in patients who do not have a functioning GI tract or who have disorders requiring complete bowel rest. Neither of these criteria applies in this case."
