      "correctText": "Motor neuron disease",
      "explanation": "Progressive supranuclear palsy: postural instability, impairment of vertical gaze, parkinsonism, frontal lobe dysfunction\n\nThe most likely diagnosis in this case is\n\nProgressive supranuclear palsy\n\n. This is because the patient presents with a combination of falls, shuffling gait, cognitive impairment, and difficulty following movement in the vertical plane. Progressive supranuclear palsy is a neurodegenerative disease characterized by parkinsonism (bradykinesia, rigidity, and postural instability), pseudobulbar palsy, and supranuclear ophthalmoplegia (typically vertical gaze palsy).\n\nParkinson's disease\n\nis another possible diagnosis given the patient's resting tremor, rigidity and bradykinesia; however, it does not typically present with early cognitive impairment or vertical gaze palsy. Parkinson's disease is characterized by motor symptoms including resting tremor ('pill-rolling' type), bradykinesia (slowness of movement), rigidity (cogwheel or lead pipe), and postural instability. Non-motor symptoms such as autonomic dysfunction and mild cognitive impairment can also be seen but usually occur later in the course of the disease.\n\nMotor neuron disease\n\nis unlikely to be the correct diagnosis as it primarily affects motor neurons causing muscle weakness and atrophy without cognitive impairment or ocular findings like vertical gaze palsy. Motor neuron diseases include amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) which affects both upper and lower motor neurons leading to progressive muscle weakness, spasticity, fasciculations, dysarthria, dysphagia, and ultimately respiratory failure.\n\nMultiple system atrophy\n\nis a rare neurodegenerative disorder that shares some features with Parkinson's disease such as parkinsonism; however, it also involves autonomic dysfunction (such as orthostatic hypotension) and cerebellar ataxia. While multiple system atrophy may cause gait disturbances similar to those seen in this patient, it does not typically present with cognitive impairment or vertical gaze palsy.\n\nDementia with Lewy bodies\n\nis characterized by fluctuating cognition, visual hallucinations, and parkinsonism. Although the patient exhibits some cognitive impairment and parkinsonism, he does not have visual hallucinations, and the presence of vertical gaze palsy makes dementia with Lewy bodies less likely than progressive supranuclear palsy."
