      "id": 143,
      "question": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with a 3-day history of fever, malaise, and a painful rash that began on her face and chest before spreading to her limbs. The rash consists of target-like lesions with central blistering. She has painful oral ulceration and conjunctival injection. She was started on lamotrigine 6 weeks ago following her first generalised tonic-clonic seizure. Examination reveals widespread epidermal detachment affecting approximately 8% of her body surface area.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate immediate management?",
      "options": [
        "Discontinue lamotrigine and start high-dose corticosteroids",
        "Discontinue lamotrigine and provide supportive care",
        "Continue lamotrigine and start topical corticosteroids",
        "Discontinue lamotrigine and start intravenous immunoglobulin",
        "Continue lamotrigine and start oral antihistamines"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Discontinue lamotrigine and provide supportive care",
      "explanation": "A rare but recognised adverse effect of lamotrigine therapy is Stevens-Johnson syndrome\n\nDiscontinue lamotrigine and provide supportive care\n\nis the correct answer. This patient presents with classic features of Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS), a severe cutaneous adverse reaction characterised by target-like lesions, mucosal involvement, and epidermal detachment affecting less than 10% of body surface area. Lamotrigine is a well-recognised cause of SJS, particularly within the first 8 weeks of treatment. The immediate priority is discontinuation of the offending drug and supportive care including fluid management, wound care, pain control, and monitoring for complications such as secondary infection. Early recognition and drug cessation is crucial as continued exposure can lead to progression to toxic epidermal necrolysis. Supportive care in a burns unit or intensive care setting may be required depending on severity.\n\nDiscontinue lamotrigine and start high-dose corticosteroids\n\nis incorrect. While drug discontinuation is essential, the use of systemic corticosteroids in SJS/TEN remains controversial. Current evidence suggests corticosteroids may increase the risk of complications including secondary infection and delayed healing, and they are generally not recommended in established SJS/TEN.\n\nContinue lamotrigine and start topical corticosteroids\n\nis incorrect. Continuing the causative medication would be dangerous and could lead to progression to toxic epidermal necrolysis, which carries significant mortality. Topical treatments are insufficient for this systemic condition.\n\nDiscontinue lamotrigine and start intravenous immunoglobulin\n\nis partially correct regarding drug cessation but IVIG use in SJS/TEN lacks strong evidence and is not considered first-line treatment. Some studies suggest potential benefit but this remains controversial.\n\nContinue lamotrigine and start oral antihistamines\n\nis incorrect. This approach fails to address the serious nature of SJS and continuing the causative drug could be life-threatening. Antihistamines are inadequate treatment for this severe condition."
