      "id": 154,
      "question": "A 45-year-old woman presents complaining of visual disturbance. Examination reveals a left congruous homonymous hemianopia. Where is the lesion most likely to be?",
      "options": [
        "Optic chiasm",
        "Left occipital cortex",
        "Right optic tract",
        "Right occipital cortex",
        "Left optic tract"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Left optic tract",
      "explanation": "Visual field defects:\n\nThe correct answer is the\n\nright occipital cortex\n\n. A homonymous hemianopia refers to a visual field defect that affects the same (homonymous) side of the visual field in both eyes. The term 'congruous' means that the pattern of vision loss is nearly identical in both eyes. This type of visual field loss is typically caused by a lesion located past the optic chiasm, at or beyond the optic tract. In this case, since it's a left congruous homonymous hemianopia, it indicates damage to the right side of the brain as each half of our brain processes information from the opposite (contralateral) side of our body and visual field. Specifically, it points towards a lesion in the right occipital cortex where primary visual processing occurs.\n\nOptic chiasm\n\nis incorrect because lesions here usually cause bitemporal hemianopia due to the crossing over of nasal retinal fibres from both eyes at this point.\n\nLeft occipital cortex\n\nwould result in a right congruous homonymous hemianopia, not left, due to contralateral processing.\n\nA lesion in\n\nright optic tract\n\ncould theoretically produce left homonymous hemianopia but it would be incongruous rather than congruous because different parts of the visual field are processed at different levels along this pathway.\n\nFinally,\n\nleft optic tract\n\n, like the left occipital cortex, would lead to a right-sided vision defect due to contralateral processing."
