      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Inferior frontal gyrus",
      "explanation": "Broca's dysphasia: speech non-fluent, comprehension normal, repetition impaired\n\nInferior frontal gyrus is the correct answer. This is the location of Broca's area. This patient has non-fluent speech, normal comprehension, and impaired repetition which is consistent with Broca's dysphasia. The animal naming test (ANT1) used in this scenario is a semantic fluency test that consists of naming as many animals as possible in 1 minute. A normal ANT1 score is typically considered to be >14. Comprehension remains relatively intact in Broca's dysphasia. In this scenario the patient is able to complete 3 stage commands, however, he may have difficulty if asked more complex comprehension questions, such as, 'If a seal was killed by a polar bear which one is still alive?'. It is also important to note that this patient may maintain automatic speech, such as counting to 10, as this function is thought to be located in an area distinct from Broca's area.\n\nAngular gyrus is an incorrect answer. Lesions in the angular gyrus are associated with Gerstmann syndrome. Gerstmann syndrome is characterised by dysgraphia or agraphia, dyscalculia or acalculia, finger agnosia, and left-right disorientation. Infrequently lesions in this area may produce dysphasia, however, they are unlikely to cause the characteristic signs of 'Broca's dysphasia' as seen in this scenario.\n\nArcuate fasciculus is an incorrect answer. Lesions in this area result in conduction aphasia. Conduction aphasia is characterised by fluent speech, relatively intact comprehension, and poor repetition. Lesions in this region would not account for the halting and effortful seen in this scenario.\n\nTransverse temporal gyri (Heschl's gyri) is an incorrect answer. This area is part of the primary auditory cortex. Interestingly, these gyri are the first cortical structures to process incoming auditory information. Lesions in the primary auditory cortex would certainly not account for this patient's non-fluent speech. A lesion here would also likely result in impaired comprehension which is not observed here.\n\nBrodmann area 22 in the superior temporal gyrus is an incorrect answer. Wernicke's area is traditionally thought to reside in Brodmann area 22, which is located in the superior temporal gyrus. This area 'forms' the speech before 'sending it' to Broca's area. Lesions result in sentences that make no sense, word substitution, and neologisms but speech remains fluent - 'word salad'. Additionally, comprehension is also impaired. Wernicke's dysphasia is a sharp contrast to the effortful speech seen in Broca's dysphasia. It is completely inconsistent with the scenario above."
