      "explanation": "In subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord, the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts are affected\n\nThe diagnosis here is that of subacute combined degeneration of the cord. The blood film points towards B12 deficiency, the cause of this condition. The dorsal column and lateral corticospinal tract are affected, and so this is the correct answer. These tracts are responsible for joint position and vibration sense, which are typically lost first in this condition, followed by distal paraesthesia and upper motor neuron signs in the legs. This correlates with the scenario mentioning the loss of proprioception and vibration sense on examination, as well as brisk knee reflexes - an upper motor neuron lesion finding.\n\nThe anterior and lateral corticospinal tracts are incorrect. Whilst the lateral corticospinal tract is involved, the anterior tract is not. The anterior corticospinal tract is usually small and controls the movements of axial muscles. It is not typically affected by this condition.\n\nThe spinothalamic tract and dorsal column are also incorrect. The dorsal column is affected but the spinothalamic tract is not. The spinothalamic tract is responsible for pain and temperature sensation, which are not affected in subacute combined degeneration of the cord.\n\nThe spinothalamic tract and lateral corticospinal tract is also, therefore, an incorrect combination."
