      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Optic nerve",
      "explanation": "The medial longitudinal fasciculus is located in the paramedian area of the midbrain and pons\n\nThe vignette describes an internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO) which results from a lesion in the medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) located in the paramedian area of the midbrain and pons. The medial longitudinal fasciculus connects the abducens nerve nucleus (CN VI) in the paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF) with the contralateral oculomotor nerve nucleus (CN III) and coordinates horizontal conjugate gaze. Central nervous system demyelinating disorders, such as multiple sclerosis, are a common cause of INO, and bilateral INO in particular is typically associated with multiple sclerosis.\n\nAll of the listed options will result in visual disturbances but are incorrect for the following reasons:\n\nA lesion of the occipital lobe causes a contralateral homonymous, macular-sparing quadrantanopia or hemianopia, depending on the extent of the lesion.\n\nA lateral medullary lesion (aka Wallenberg syndrome) typically results from a vascular insult from a PICA or vertebral artery stroke, and may cause an ipsilateral Horner's syndrome marked by ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis.\n\nOptic neuritis is a common manifestation of multiple sclerosis, but typically presents with blurred vision, colour desaturation, and eye pain. The symptoms would not be expected to improve on covering the unaffected eye, as is the case in binocular diplopia demonstrated in this patient.\n\nFinally, a lesion affecting the oculomotor nerve nucleus would also affect the ipsilateral eye's ability to abduct on horizontal conjugate gaze. However, the key distinguishing feature on physical examination is the test of convergence: asking the patient to focus on a nearby object in the midline which requires abduction of both eyes. The patient's ability to converge exonerates an oculomotor nerve or oculomotor nucleus lesion."
