      "explanation": "Parietal lobe lesions may cause astereognosis\n\nParietal lobe\n\nis correct. Astereognosis is defined as the inability to identify objects by touch, despite intact elementary tactile, proprioceptive, and thermal sensation. It occurs due to a lesion in the parietal lobe.\n\nInferior frontal gyrus\n\nis incorrect.  Lesions in this part of the brain result in Broca's dysphasia characterised by non-fluent speech but normal comprehension.\n\nLateral geniculate nucleus\n\nis incorrect. This is a component of the thalamus which is a very important component of the visual pathway. A lesion of this nucleus produces moderately to completely congruent visual field defects.\n\nMedial geniculate nucleus\n\nis incorrect. The medial geniculate nucleus is part of the auditory thalamus and represents the thalamic relay between the inferior colliculus and the auditory cortex. Although the medial geniculate nucleus is a well-known integration centre for the central auditory pathways, auditory symptoms caused by damage to these nuclei are rarely described. However, there are some case reports of auditory hallucinations.\n\nTemporal lobe\n\nis incorrect. The function of the temporal lobe centres around auditory stimuli, memory, and emotion."
