      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "C3 and C4",
      "explanation": "Klumpke's paralysis: T1 nerve root damage\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nC8 and T1\n\n. The patient's symptoms of tingling and numbness of the inner aspect of his right forearm and little finger, wasting of the hypothenar eminence and clawing of the fourth and fifth digits suggest lower brachial plexus injury. This is often referred to as Klumpke's paralysis, which typically results from damage to nerve roots C8 and T1. This clinical scenario hints at a Pancoast tumour, a type of lung cancer that can invade the lower part of the brachial plexus causing these neurological signs.\n\nSymptoms of\n\nC3 and C4 involvement\n\nare neck pain and pain over the trapezius muscle, which is not present in the given case, making involvement of these nerve roots unlikely.\n\nC4 and C5 nerve root involvement\n\nis not the correct option as damage to the C4 and/or C5 nerve roots typically presents with shoulder abduction weakness and decreased sensation over the lateral aspect of the upper limb.\n\nC5 and C6 nerve root involvement\n\nis also known as Erb's palsy. The most common cause is birth trauma, which occurs due to traction of the baby's neck at the time of delivery. The resulting deformity is called a 'waiter's tip hand', where the affected arm hangs limply with the shoulder in adduction, internal rotation of the upper arm and pronation of the forearm. The deformity in the given scenario has clawing of the fourth and fifth digits, which does not occur in Erb's palsy.\n\nC7 and C8 involvement\n\nis incorrect as C7 involvement would have sensory loss over the third digit and triceps weakness, which does not appear in this case. However, C8 nerve root involvement could be part of the clinical picture, given the patient's symptoms. C8 and T1 nerve roots will cause the clinical signs mentioned in the case, but C7 involvement is unlikely."
