      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Optic radiation",
      "explanation": "Internuclear ophthalmoplegia is due to a lesion in the medial longitudinal fasciculus\n\nThe most likely diagnosis for this patient is multiple sclerosis. Here she is presenting with internuclear ophthalmoplegia. Internuclear ophthalmoplegia occurs due to a lesion in the medial longitudinal fasciculus. The medial longitudinal fasciculus is a highly myelinated tract which communicates information from the vestibular nucleus to the oculomotor, trochlear and abducens nuclei to coordinate eye movements. She previously suffered an episode of what is very likely to be optic neuritis. This points further to a diagnosis of multiple sclerosis. Internuclear ophthalmoplegia is a disorder of conjugate lateral gaze in which the eye on the side of the lesion in the medial longitudinal fasciculus fails to adduct and the contralateral eye exhibits nystagmus. Multiple sclerosis is a disorder which affects the axonal myelin sheath and hence highly myelinated areas are commonly affected.\n\nA lesion of the optic chiasm is more likely to present with bitemporal hemianopia or tunnel vision. There would be no effect on eye movements and the visual disturbance is typically painless.\n\nA lesion of the optic radiation will present with homonymous hemianopia or quadrantanopia depending on which lobe the lesion is located. The control of eye movements is confined to the nuclei of the brainstem.\n\nPeriventricular lesions commonly present with numbness of the hands or feet and impaired motor function. Cranial nerves are not involved.\n\nLesions of the oculomotor nerve would cause a more significant ophthalmoplegia with the eye in the 'down and out' position. You would also expect to see ptosis and mydriasis in the affected eye. Lesions of the oculomotor nerve can be seen in multiple sclerosis but the presentation does not fit here."
