      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Refer to pain management clinic",
      "explanation": "Drugs for neuropathic pain are typically used as monotherapy, i.e. if not working then drugs should be switched, not added\n\nThis patient has presented with painful neuropathy secondary to poorly-controlled type 2 diabetes. In this scenario, NICE advocate starting any of pregabalin, duloxetine, gabapentin  or amitriptyline as first-line therapy. However, in the instance of treatment failure, as is the case here, a new medication for neuropathic pain should be started instead of, not in combination with the original agent. As such, stopping gabapentin and starting duloxetine is the best option here.\n\nContinuing gabapentin whilst starting duloxetine is in contravention of the principle of using drugs for neuropathic pain as monotherapy.\n\nCodeine is unlikely to be an effective additional analgesic for this patient's neuropathic pain.\n\nSubstituting gabapentin for amitriptyline would be a possible treatment strategy for some patients. However, for this patient, starting an antimuscarinic agent risks precipitating an exacerbation of symptoms from glaucoma.\n\nWere this patient's symptoms refractory to a second neuropathic analgesic, it may be appropriate to consider a pain management clinic referral. However, at this stage, NICE advise trialling an alternative neuropathic pain medication, and thus a pain clinic referral would be somewhat premature."
