      "explanation": "Migraine\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nSpecific 5-HT1 agonist\n\n. Sumatriptan is a member of the triptan class, which are specific serotonin (5-HT1) receptor agonists. They work by constricting blood vessels in the brain that are involved in causing migraine headaches. Sumatriptan does not have significant affinity for 5-HT2 or other 5-HT receptors and hence it doesn't act as an antagonist or partial agonist at these receptors.\n\nDiscussing the incorrect answers:\n\nBeta-blocker\n\nis incorrect because sumatriptan does not block beta-adrenergic receptors, which are the target for beta-blockers like propranolol. Beta-blockers are often used in the prophylaxis of migraines but they do not treat acute migraine attacks.\n\nAlpha-blocker and a partial 5-HT2 agonist\n\nis also incorrect. While alpha-blockers such as prazosin can be used in managing conditions like hypertension and benign prostatic hyperplasia, they do not have a role in treating migraines. Additionally, sumatriptan does not act as a partial 5-HT2 agonist.\n\nThe option\n\n5-HT2 antagonist\n\nis incorrect because, as mentioned earlier, sumatriptan specifically targets 5-HT1 receptors and has no significant action on 5-HT2 receptors.\n\nFinally,\n\nTricyclic antidepressant\n\nis incorrect because sumatriptan does not belong to this class of drugs. Tricyclic antidepressants like amitriptyline can be used to prevent migraines but they work through different mechanisms than sumatriptan, primarily by inhibiting the reuptake of serotonin and noradrenaline thereby increasing their concentration in the synaptic cleft."
