      "id": 202,
      "question": "A 72-year-old woman is referred to neurology with a 9-month history of progressive difficulty using her left hand. She reports occasional trembling and stiffness that worsens when anxious. She has noticed her handwriting has deteriorated and she struggles with buttons. She has a history of hypertension and depression. On examination, there is mild hypomimia, subtle rigidity in the left arm, and reduced arm swing on the left when walking. Her gait is otherwise normal.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
      "options": [
        "Corticobasal degeneration",
        "Drug-induced parkinsonism",
        "Essential tremor",
        "Idiopathic Parkinson's disease",
        "Multiple system atrophy"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Drug-induced parkinsonism",
      "explanation": "Asymmetrical symptoms suggests idiopathic Parkinson's\n\nIdiopathic Parkinson's disease\n\nis the most likely diagnosis in this case. The patient presents with several subtle but characteristic features that point to early Parkinson's disease. The key diagnostic clue is the asymmetrical nature of her symptoms, which predominantly affect her left side. This asymmetry is a hallmark feature of idiopathic Parkinson's disease, distinguishing it from other parkinsonian syndromes. Additional supporting features include the insidious onset over 9 months, hypomimia (reduced facial expression), rigidity, reduced arm swing on the affected side, and micrographia (deterioration in handwriting). The tremor that worsens with anxiety is consistent with the resting tremor of Parkinson's disease, which typically improves with voluntary movement and worsens with stress. Depression, which this patient has, is also common in Parkinson's disease, affecting approximately 40% of patients.\n\nCorticobasal degeneration\n\nis a rare neurodegenerative disorder that can present with asymmetrical rigidity and bradykinesia. However, it typically presents with more pronounced cortical features such as apraxia, alien limb phenomenon, cortical sensory loss, and myoclonus. The absence of these distinctive cortical features makes this diagnosis less likely in this patient. Additionally, corticobasal degeneration usually progresses more rapidly than what is described in this case.\n\nDrug-induced parkinsonism\n\nis an important differential diagnosis to consider, especially in elderly patients on multiple medications. However, drug-induced parkinsonism typically presents with bilateral and symmetrical symptoms of rapid onset, unlike the asymmetrical presentation in this case. The patient's medication history isn't detailed, but the gradual progression and asymmetry make drug-induced parkinsonism less likely.\n\nEssential tremor\n\nis characterized by a bilateral, largely symmetrical action or postural tremor affecting the hands and forearms. Unlike Parkinson's disease, essential tremor typically does not cause bradykinesia, rigidity, or reduced arm swing. The tremor in essential tremor is most prominent during voluntary movement rather than at rest. The patient's presentation with reduced arm swing, rigidity, and hypomimia is inconsistent with essential tremor.\n\nMultiple system atrophy\n\nis a neurodegenerative disorder that can present with parkinsonian features. However, it typically presents with prominent autonomic dysfunction (orthostatic hypotension, urinary incontinence, erectile dysfunction) and cerebellar signs. The absence of these features, along with the asymmetrical presentation, makes multiple system atrophy less likely in this case."
