      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Syringomyelia",
      "explanation": "Distal sensory loss, tingling + absent ankle jerks/extensor plantars + gait abnormalities/Romberg's positive → subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord\n\nSubacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord\n\nis the correct answer. This occurs due to vitamin B12 deficiency and impacts the lateral corticospinal tracts, dorsal column and spinocerebellar tracts. Patients may display symptoms and signs of peripheral neuropathy and loss of dorsal column modalities such as proprioception, vibration and fine touch often affecting their legs initially as seen in this patient. They also describe spastic weakness, in their legs. This vignette describes a positive Romberg test described as the patient losing balance when their eyes were closed. This is characteristically seen in patients with this diagnosis due to proprioception loss, and the dysfunction of the spinocerebellar tracts.\n\nBrown-Sequard syndrome\n\nis incorrect. This describes hemisection of the spinal cord and will impact both sensory tracts and the lateral corticospinal tracts. Patients will often describe a traumatic event and present with ipsilateral motor weakness due to loss of the corticospinal tracts, ipsilateral loss of dorsal column modalities and contralateral loss of spinothalamic modalities. Due to the patient in this vignette presenting with bilateral loss and the absence of any preceding trauma, this diagnosis is unlikely.\n\nFriedrich's ataxia\n\nis incorrect. Friedrich's ataxia is unlikely given that typically presents in childhood or adolescence onset due to its hereditary nature. Patients typically present similar to subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord however have more dominant cerebellar signs including intention tremor, nystagmus and dysdiadochokinesia. As the patient in this vignette does not have these signs and is a lot older on initial presentation this diagnosis is not likely.\n\nMultiple sclerosis\n\nis incorrect. This diagnosis is unlikely as it again presents much earlier and is more common in females. Furthermore, patients will typically present with upper motor neurone signs as pathology is based purely on the central nervous system. Patients with multiple sclerosis also typically describe general non-specific neurological signs such as fatigue, weight loss, depression and insomnia which is not described here.\n\nSyringomyelia\n\nis incorrect. This diagnosis occurs due to syrinx formation within the spinal cord in the central nervous system. Patients typically present with signs and symptoms affecting the neck, shoulders and arms. On initial presentation, patients often have preservation of the dorsal column modalities with only the spinothalamic sensory modalities affected unlike that seen in this patient. This occurs due to syrinx compression initially of the spinothalamic tracts due to their crossing within the anterior commissure of the spinal cord first before progression to other tracts within the spinal cord."
