      "question": "A 65-year-old female is returned to the surgical ward after an uneventful vaginal hysterectomy. She has no significant past medical history and is not taking any regular medications. She is provided with regular anti-emetics and pain relief as per routine surgical and anaesthetic postoperative instructions.\n\nThree hours after returning to the surgical ward, she complains of palpitations and worsening dyspnoea.\n\nOn examination, she appeared agitated with some accessory muscle use.\n\nAn updated set of observations are taken:\n\n\nHeart rate 156 beats per minute\nBlood pressure 102/65 mmHg\nOxygen saturations 98% on 3L/min nasal specs\nRespiratory rate 24/min\n\nA bedside ECG is performed which reveals polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.\n\nWhich of the following postoperative medications is the likely cause of her deterioration?",
      "options": [
        "Morphine",
        "Ondansetron",
        "Metoclopramide",
        "Diclofenac",
        "Cyclizine"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Cyclizine",
      "explanation": "5-HT3 antagonists such as ondansetron can predispose to prolonged QT interval and increased risk of polymorphic VT\n\nThis patient has developed polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (VT) postoperatively, most likely due to the administration of ondansetron. Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist, a medication commonly used for postoperative nausea and vomiting. It can be associated with prolongation of the QTc interval which can deteriorate into polymorphic VT or torsades de pointes. It is generally treated with a magnesium sulphate infusion.\n\nMorphine is not generally associated with polymorphic  VT, but is more associated with respiratory depression in high doses.\n\nMetoclopramide is a dopamine antagonist also used as an anti-emetic. It is not linked with polymorphic VT, but has been linked with Parkinson like symptoms and movement disorders, particularly in young females.\n\nDiclofenac is an example of a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory medication. Its use tends to be linked with renal dysfunction, but is not linked with VT.\n\nCyclizine is an antihistamine, also useful in treating nausea. Its use is linked with the development of some tachyarrhythmias, but is not linked to prolongation of the qt interval/ polymorphic VT."
