      "id": 239,
      "question": "A 41-year-old woman has acute paraesthesia and weakness in the right upper and lower limbs. Last year, she had similar symptoms and blurred vision, which resolved spontaneously. She has hypertension and alcohol use disorder.\n\nShe has spastic weakness in the right upper and lower limbs, a positive Babinski sign, and neck flexion causes electric shock-like sensations down her back. There are no visual signs or symptoms. An MRI shows demyelinating lesions in the periventricular and juxtacortical regions, all with the same intensities.\n\nWhich additional finding would best support the likely diagnosis?",
      "options": [
        "Atrophy and hyperintensities of the mammillary bodies",
        "Bilateral FLAIR hyperintensities involving the pulvinar thalamic nuclei",
        "Cortical atrophy mostly affecting the hippocampus and medial temporal lobes",
        "Delayed responses on visual evoked potentials",
        "Frontal and temporal lobe atrophy, with relative posterior area preservation"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Atrophy and hyperintensities of the mammillary bodies",
      "explanation": "Multiple sclerosis diagnosis that requires demyelinating lesions that are separated in space and time\n\nDelayed responses on visual evoked potentials\n\n(VEPs) is correct. This patient has features suggestive of multiple sclerosis (MS), including acute paraesthesia and weakness, a previous episode of blurred vision, suggesting optic neuritis, upper motor neurone lesion signs (spastic weakness, positive Babinski sign), and Lhermitte’s sign (electric shock on neck flexion). This MRI shows demyelination in typical MS sites (periventricular and juxtacortical areas), but since they are of the same intensity, this only provides evidence of dissemination in space and not time. Therefore, delayed VEPs, despite this patient having no current visual symptoms, can suggest prior optic nerve demyelination, which can demonstrate dissemination in time and aid the diagnosis of MS.\n\nAtrophy and hyperintensities of the mammillary bodies\n\nis incorrect as this finding is more suggestive of Wernicke’s encephalopathy (WE). While this patient has a history of alcohol use disorder, this patient lacks features that suggest WE, such as confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia.\n\nBilateral FLAIR hyperintensities involving the pulvinar thalamic nuclei\n\nis incorrect, as this finding is suggestive of variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob (CJD) disease (the 'pulvinar sign') over MS. Variant CJD could be considered if the patient had rapidly progressive dementia and myoclonus, which are not seen here.\n\nCortical atrophy mostly affecting the hippocampus and medial temporal lobes\n\nis incorrect, as this pattern is more suggestive of Alzheimer's disease (AD) rather than MS. AD tends to present with insidious memory loss and cognitive decline rather than the episodic focal neurological deficits seen in this patient.\n\nFrontal and temporal lobe atrophy, with relative posterior area preservation\n\nis incorrect as this is suggestive of frontotemporal dementia (FTD) rather than MS. FTD tends to present with progressive changes in behaviour and personality (e.g. disinhibition and impulsivity), or language rather than the episodic focal neurological deficits seen here."
