      "explanation": "Multiple sclerosis patient with bladder dysfunction - get an ultrasound first to assess bladder emptying\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nultrasound kidneys, ureters and urinary bladder (KUB)\n\n.\n\nThis patient has neurogenic bladder dysfunction secondary to MS. The next appropriate step in her management is thus to arrange an ultrasound KUB, to assess her bladder emptying - this will then determine what treatment options are available to her.\n\nBotulinum toxin type A injections\n\nis incorrect. NICE guidelines state botulinum injections may be considered for the treatment of urinary incontinence in neurological disease but only in patients (without a significant post-void residual volume) who have found anti-cholinergic medication ineffective.\n\nIntermittent self-catheterisation\n\nis incorrect. Intermittent self-catheterisation is only of benefit if the patient has a significant post-void residual volume and ultrasound KUB is first required to assess this. If the patient does not have a significant post-void residual volume, then intermittent self-catheterisation will not be a beneficial treatment option.\n\nMicturating cystogram\n\nis incorrect. A micturating cystogram is a fluoroscopic study of the lower urinary tract, in which contrast is introduced into the bladder using a catheter. It is used to evaluate the anatomy and function of the lower urinary tract system and tends to be the investigation of choice for suspected vesicoureteral reflux. Whilst it can also be used in the assessment of neurogenic bladder dysfunction, it would not be used as a first-line imaging modality and so is not the appropriate next step in this example.\n\nOxybutynin\n\nis incorrect. Oxybutynin is an anticholinergic medication, which may be of use in neurogenic bladder dysfunction if there is no significant post-void residual volume. If the patient has a significant post-void residual volume, however, the use of anticholinergic medications will likely precipitate urinary retention. As such, US KUB is required to assess bladder emptying before such drugs are trialled."
