      "explanation": "Patients with von Hippel-Lindau syndrome may develop retinal haemangiomas which present as a vitreous haemorrhage\n\nAll of the above answers can be grouped into an 'umbrella term' of phakomatoses.\n\nNeurofibromatosis type I (NF1) commonly causes the development of multiple neurofibromas of the skin and cafe au lait spots. NF1 commonly affects the eyes with the appearance of Lisch nodules and optic gliomas of the optic nerve. NF1 is not associated with phaeochromocytoma or vitreous haemorrhage.\n\nNeurofibromatosis type II (NF2) causes schwannomas and meningiomas with people commonly suffering from sensorineural hearing loss secondary to acoustic neuromas. NF2 generally affects the nervous system and doesn't have eye involvement.\n\nSturge-Weber syndrome is a rare condition associated with developmental delay, port-wine stains, glaucoma and seizures. There is an increased risk in the development of retinoblastoma.\n\nTuberous sclerosis commonly causes angiofibromas and moles of the skin along with developmental delay. It commonly affects multiple systems of the body, in particular the nervous system with epilepsy and seizures. Eye signs include astrocytic hamartomas.\n\nVon Hippel Lindau syndrome (VHL) is the correct answer. Patients develop multiple cysts and tumours affecting a large number of body systems. Subarachnoid haemorrhage is common secondary to cerebral haemangiomas and vitreous haemorrhage is a common presentation for retinal haemangiomas. Patients are also at increased risk of phaeochromocytoma, clear-cell renal cancer and cysts involving the pancreas, liver and epididymis."
