      "explanation": "Motor neuron disease - riluzole\n\nRiluzole is correct. The patient presents with insidious weakness and mixed upper (hyperreflexia, upgoing plantars) and lower (fasciculations, atrophy, hyporeflexia) motor neuron signs. The most likely cause is motor neurone disease (MND). Given the presentation is limb predominant, this is likely to represent the amyotrophic lateral sclerosis variant of this disease. Riluzole retards the onset of ventilator-dependence in some patients and potentially may increase survival by two to three months.\n\nIntravenous immunoglobulin is incorrect. This treatment may be indicated in conditions such as Guillain-Barre syndrome and inflammatory myopathies, which can present with weakness. However, these conditions are not associated with UMN signs and are therefore less likely.\n\nPrednisolone is incorrect. Corticosteroids steroids are used in a wide variety of neuro-inflammatory conditions including such diseases as inflammatory myositis and multiple sclerosis, which may present with weakness. However, the distribution of weakness and clinical findings, in this case, suggest MND. Steroids have no role in the management of this condition.\n\nPyridostigmine is incorrect. This is an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor used in the management of myasthenia gravis. The weakness in myasthenia is characterized by fatigability, which is not mentioned in this case. Additionally, upper motor neurone signs are not a feature of this condition.\n\nSurgery is incorrect. A cervical radiculomyelopathy (multilevel degenerative disease of the cervical spine) may present with a combination of LMN and UMN signs. Surgery may be indicated in this case. However, these patients typically have neck pain and sensory signs, which are absent in this case, making the diagnosis less likely."
