      "question": "Which one of the following features is most associated with frontal lobe lesions?",
      "options": [
        "Wernicke's aphasia",
        "Gerstmann's syndrome",
        "Perseveration",
        "Cortical blindness",
        "Superior homonymous quadrantanopia"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Perseveration",
      "explanation": "Frontal lobe lesions may cause perseveration\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nPerseveration\n\n. Perseveration refers to the repetitive and involuntary continuation of a response in the absence of the original stimulus. It is most commonly associated with lesions in the frontal lobe, specifically within the prefrontal cortex. This area is responsible for executive functions including decision making, behaviour regulation, and task flexibility. Damage to this area can disrupt these processes, leading to symptoms such as perseveration.\n\nWernicke's aphasia\n\nis incorrect as it typically results from damage to Wernicke's area located in the superior temporal gyrus in the dominant cerebral hemisphere, which is usually the left hemisphere. This condition is characterised by fluent but nonsensical speech with a lack of comprehension.\n\nGerstmann's syndrome\n\n, characterised by agraphia (inability to write), acalculia (difficulty with simple mathematical tasks), finger agnosia (inability to distinguish fingers on hand) and left-right disorientation, results from damage to angular gyrus of parietal lobe rather than frontal lobe.\n\nCortical blindness\n\narises from bilateral occipital lobe lesions which interrupt visual input to the primary visual cortex. Patients present with loss of vision but may have preserved pupillary light reflex due to intact brainstem pathways.\n\nLastly,\n\nSuperior homonymous quadrantanopia\n\n, also known as 'pie in the sky', results from an infarct in the territory supplied by inferior branch of MCA affecting contralateral inferior parietal lobule or temporal lobe. The lesion here will affect optic radiations carrying information from superior part of visual field resulting in loss of vision in superior quadrant contralaterally. Thus, this condition does not result from frontal lobe lesions."
