      "id": 265,
      "question": "Lateral medullary syndrome is caused by occlusion of which one of the following blood vessels?",
      "options": [
        "Anterior inferior cerebellar artery",
        "Posterior cerebral artery",
        "Lateral sinus thrombosis",
        "Middle cerebral artery",
        "Posterior inferior cerebellar artery"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Anterior inferior cerebellar artery",
      "explanation": "Lateral medullary syndrome - PICA lesion - cerebellar signs,  contralateral sensory loss & ipsilateral Horner's\n\nThe correct answer to this question is the\n\nPosterior inferior cerebellar artery\n\n. Lateral medullary syndrome, also known as Wallenberg's syndrome, results from occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA). This leads to infarction in the lateral part of the medulla oblongata, causing a constellation of symptoms including dysphagia, hoarseness, vertigo, nystagmus, loss of pain and temperature sensation on the ipsilateral face and contralateral body, among others.\n\nDiscussing each incorrect option:\n\nAnterior inferior cerebellar artery\n\n: Occlusion here typically results in lateral pontine syndrome rather than lateral medullary syndrome. Symptoms may include facial weakness and sensory loss due to damage to the facial nucleus and tract; hearing loss due to cochlear nuclei damage; ataxia resulting from middle and superior cerebellar peduncle damage; among others.\n\nPosterior cerebral artery\n\n: Occlusion of this artery often leads to occipital lobe infarction with consequent homonymous hemianopia. Other possible symptoms include visual agnosia or memory impairment but not symptoms typical for Wallenberg's syndrome.\n\nLateral sinus thrombosis\n\n: This condition does not cause lateral medullary syndrome. It is a form of venous sinus thrombosis which can lead to complications such as intracranial hypertension or cerebral venous infarction. Symptoms are usually related to increased intracranial pressure like headache, vomiting and papilledema.\n\nMiddle cerebral artery\n\n: Occlusion here causes one of the most common types of ischemic stroke - middle cerebral artery (MCA) stroke. This typically presents with contralateral hemiparesis and sensory loss (more severe in the face and arm than the leg), homonymous hemianopia and possibly aphasia if dominant hemisphere is affected or neglect if non-dominant hemisphere is affected. These are different from the symptoms seen in Wallenberg's syndrome."
