      "explanation": "Brain abscess: IV 3rd-generation cephalosporin + metronidazole\n\nThe underlying diagnosis explaining this presentation is a brain abscess. The patient's abscess has likely been caused by intracranial extension of his recent middle ear infection. Brain abscesses are a rare complication of otitis media, and this patient is at an enhanced risk of infection due to immunosuppression from etanercept. The correct option is IV ceftriaxone, a third-generation cephalosporin, plus metronidazole.\n\nIV piperacillin/tazobactam would be an appropriate empirical antibiotic regimen for sepsis of unknown origin, but the ring-enhancing lesion on contrast CT provides a credible focus of infection in the central nervous system, making targeted therapy more appropriate.\n\nThe first-line treatment for a suspected brain abscess is a 3rd-generation cephalosporin + metronidazole. Cefazolin is a 1st-generation cephalosporin, and does not penetrate the central nervous system as successfully as 3rd-generation cephalosporins.\n\nIV aciclovir would be an appropriate initial treatment for suspected viral encephalitis, which may also present with behavioural change and pyrexia. However, the presence of a ring-enhancing lesion on CT points away from this diagnosis.\n\nIV ceftriaxone would be an appropriate antimicrobial treatment for suspected bacterial meningitis in this age group. However, it lacks sufficient anaerobic cover to be used as monotherapy for a brain abscess."
