      "id": 276,
      "question": "A 49-year-old man is prescribed procyclidine for Parkinson's disease. What is the mechanism of action?",
      "options": [
        "Antimuscarinic",
        "Dopamine receptor agonist",
        "Decarboxylase inhibitor",
        "Dopamine receptor antagonist",
        "Monoamine Oxidase-B inhibitor"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Dopamine receptor antagonist",
      "explanation": "Procyclidine - antimuscarinic\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nAntimuscarinic\n\n. Procyclidine, a drug commonly used in the treatment of Parkinson's disease, works primarily through an antimuscarinic mechanism of action. It blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors in the central and peripheral nervous system. This results in a reduction of cholinergic activity which helps to balance the dopamine-acetylcholine ratio in the brain, thereby alleviating symptoms associated with Parkinson's disease such as tremors, rigidity and bradykinesia.\n\nThe option\n\nDopamine receptor agonist\n\nis incorrect. While it is true that some drugs used for Parkinson's disease work by directly stimulating dopamine receptors (e.g., pramipexole and ropinirole), procyclidine does not fall into this category. Its primary mechanism of action is antimuscarinic, rather than dopaminergic.\n\nSimilarly, the option\n\nDecarboxylase inhibitor\n\nis also incorrect. Decarboxylase inhibitors like carbidopa or benserazide are often given alongside levodopa to prevent its peripheral conversion to dopamine before reaching the brain. However, this is not how procyclidine works.\n\nThe option\n\nDopamine receptor antagonist\n\nwould be counterproductive in treating Parkinson's disease. These agents block dopamine receptors and are typically used to treat conditions like schizophrenia or bipolar disorder where there may be an overactivity of dopamine neurotransmission. Using a dopamine antagonist would likely worsen symptoms of Parkinson's disease.\n\nLastly, the option\n\nMonoamine Oxidase-B inhibitor\n\nis also incorrect. While MAO-B inhibitors such as selegiline and rasagiline do have a role in managing Parkinson's disease by slowing down the breakdown of dopamine in the brain, this does not describe the mechanism of action for procyclidine."
