      "id": 279,
      "question": "A 40-year-old woman presents with increasing muscle weakness. She reports the weakness mainly affects her shoulders, hips and upper arms. On questioning, she feels her symptoms are worse after a period of inactivity such as sitting and on repeat movement/exercise her weakness actually improves. She confirms she has associated symptoms of a dry mouth and sometimes has difficulty passing urine.\n\nOn examination, she has reduced power of the shoulders and upper arms with reduced reflexes throughout. The rest of her examination including peripheral nerve and cranial nerve exam is normal.\n\nWhat is directly affected in this patient's most likely condition?",
      "options": [
        "Chloride channels",
        "Myelin sheaths",
        "Nicotinic acetylcholine receptors",
        "Voltage-gated calcium channels",
        "Voltage-gated sodium channels"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Myelin sheaths",
      "explanation": "Lambert-Eaton syndrome - antibodies against the voltage-gated Ca2+ channels\n\nThis patient has presented with features in keeping with Lambert-Eaton syndrome, a myasthenic syndrome associated with muscle weakness, particularly affecting the limb-girdle region, which generally improves with repetitive contractions. Further features of the condition include hyporeflexia and autonomic disruption such as dry mouth, impotence and difficulty micturating. Lambert-Eaton syndrome is an autoimmune condition due to antibodies directly affecting the presynaptic\n\nvoltage-gated calcium channels\n\nin the peripheral nervous system.\n\nChloride channels\n\nare involved in several regulatory functions within the body including the transport of epithelial fluid in the lung, pancreas and other organs. The main condition associated with chloride channel disruption is the genetic disease cystic fibrosis causing symptoms of mucus thickening and pancreatic insufficiency from birth.\n\nThe\n\nmyelin sheaths\n\nis affected in several demyelination neurological conditions including multiple sclerosis, transverse myelitis and Guillain-Barre syndrome. In these conditions the myelin surrounding the nerve cell axons is affected, reducing the electrical impulses that are passed along the nervous system. The symptoms seen in these conditions are not improved with repeat use/contractions.\n\nBlockage of the\n\nnicotinic acetylcholine receptors\n\nis the pathophysiology of the neuromuscular disease myasthenia gravis (MG). MG is a similar autoimmune condition to Lambert-Eaton syndrome causing progressive weakness however MG is normally associated with ocular issues including ophthalmoplegia and ptosis. Also, in contrast to Lambert-Eaton syndrome, repeated muscle contraction and activity in MG results in muscle fatigue and a worsening weakness, not an improvement.\n\nVoltage-gated sodium channels\n\ndisruption is the pathophysiology in several conditions, in particular neurological syndromes resulting in increased seizure activity such as erythromelalgia and generalised epilepsy with febrile seizures plus (GEFS+) and electrolyte imbalance syndromes such as hyperkalemic and hypokalemic periodic paralysis. The sodium channels are not affected and play no role in Lambert-Eaton syndrome."
