      "question": "A 32-year-old man is brought in following a seizure. His family reports he has been severally unwell the past few days with a fever and vomiting. He was started on antibiotics by his GP last week for suspected bacterial sinusitis following a prolonged period of symptoms including thick nasal mucus and facial discomfort. However, his family confirms he did not complete the antibiotic course.\n\nA CT head with contrast is performed and demonstrates a ring-enhancing lesion with evidence of inflammation of the sinuses.\n\nWhat antibiotic regime should be commenced?",
      "options": [
        "IV ceftriaxone and ampicillin",
        "IV ceftriaxone and co-trimoxazole",
        "IV ceftriaxone and gentamicin",
        "IV ceftriaxone and metronidazole",
        "IV ceftriaxone and vancomycin"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "IV ceftriaxone and vancomycin",
      "explanation": "Brain abscess: IV 3rd-generation cephalosporin + metronidazole\n\nThis patient has presented with a brain abscess, secondary to spread from an initial sinus infection. Diagnosis is often confirmed via a contrast CT head showing a ring-enhancing lesion that results from the formation of a capsule surrounding the build-up of inflammation and concomitant dead brain tissue. A lumbar puncture is contraindicated due to the risk of potential cerebral herniation. If identified broad-spectrum antibiotics should be commenced immediately. A 3rd generation cephalosporin with additional cover for potential anaerobic and microaerophilic is the recommended regimen such as\n\nIV ceftriaxone and metronidazole\n\n. Dexamethasone should also be considered in patients with evidence of intracranial pressure.\n\nIV ceftriaxone and ampicillin\n\nis mainly used for the management of suspected meningitis in young children, those over 50 years or patients who are immunocompromised. The addition of ampicillin, to the standard 3rd generation cephalosporin, is recommended to cover for potential\n\nListeria monocytogenes\n\ninfection more common in these patient groups. This patient has presented with a brain abscess, not just meningitis, and is not a member of this high-risk patient group.\n\nIV ceftriaxone and co-trimoxazole\n\nare not commonly used in combination. Co-trimoxazole, which is made up of sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim, can cover a range of bacterial, fungal and protozoan infections however it is mainly used as a prophylactic drug to prevent infections such as pneumocystis pneumonia and toxoplasmosis in patients with HIV/AID or other immunosuppressive conditions.\n\nIV ceftriaxone and gentamicin\n\ncan be used in combination to treat certain infections such as infective endocarditis or neutropenic sepsis however they are not commonly used for cerebral infections e.g. brain abscess or meningitis.\n\nIV ceftriaxone and vancomycin\n\nshould only be considered for brain abscess treatment in countries where cephalosporin resistance is high (e.g. the US) or there is specific reason to suspect specific resistant-infections (e.g. in patient with known methicillin-resistant\n\nStaphylococcus aureus\n\nMRSA)."
