      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Interferon beta",
      "explanation": "Motor neuron disease - treatment: NIV is better than riluzole\n\nThis patient has a diagnosis of motor neuron disease, most likely of the amyotrophic lateral sclerosis subtype. Features consistent with this include his age (onset is most common between ages 55-75), a combination of lower (fasciculations) and upper (upgoing plantars) motor neuron signs, and the classic description of hypernasal speech. Management options for motor neuron disease are limited, with 50% of patients  dying within three years of diagnosis. Of the options above, non-invasive ventilation (NIV) confers the longest survival benefit (on average, 7 months), helping to mitigate type 2 respiratory failure in more advanced disease.\n\nInterferon alpha is used in the treatment of hepatitis C, and has no role in the management of motor neuron disease.\n\nInterferon beta is used in the management of multiple sclerosis relapses. The purely motor nature of the patient's symptoms, presence of lower motor neuron signs and older age of onset ensure that motor neuron disease is a more likely diagnosis than multiple sclerosis in this patient.\n\nRiluzole offers a mild survival benefit in the context of motor neuron disease via down-regulation of GABAergic transmission. However, this benefit is less marked than that offered by NIV.\n\nChest wall physiotherapy is an important tool in the management of secretions in motor neuron disease. However, its use is not associated with a significant survival benefit, as is the case with NIV."
