      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Continue amitriptyline the current dose and add in duloxetine",
      "explanation": "Drugs for neuropathic pain are typically used as monotherapy, i.e. if not working then drugs should be switched, not added\n\nThis woman is suffering neuropathic pain which has arisen from a disruption of the nervous signals at the L5 nerve root. Neuropathic pain can be very debilitating and cause patient significant discomfort. She has initially been trialled on amitriptyline and is now taking the maximum dose which can be prescribed safely in the community. The NICE guidelines advise that when a first-line medication is not effective then that medication should be stopped and another first-line medication should be tried. Neuropathic medications should be given as monotherapy outside of specialist pain clinics.\n\nStopping the amitriptyline and starting the new medication of duloxetine is the correct answer in this case for the reasons outlined above.\n\nWhen prescribing amitriptyline it should be trialled for a period of 6-8 weeks with at least 2 weeks at the maximum dose. Here the patient has been at the maximum dose of 75mg OD for 3 weeks and there has been no benefit. There is, therefore, no benefit in continuing at this dose for a further 2 weeks and a change should be made.\n\nMedications for neuropathic pain should be typically used as monotherapy and if a medication is not working then they should be switched and not added. The patient should, therefore, not have duloxetine added but rather be switched from amitriptyline to duloxetine.\n\nIncreasing the dose of amitriptyline beyond 75mg OD should not be attempted in community settings and an easier alternative is available.\n\nWhilst a referral to the pain services may be appropriate in the long term the patient can be managed with a change of medications in the first instance. Where this fails further management with the pain team may be appropriate."
