      "explanation": "Consider glycopyrronium bromide to manage drooling of saliva in people with Parkinson's disease\n\nGlycopyrronium bromide is the first line of medical management for drooling of saliva in people with Parkinson's disease. If this is not effective, not tolerated or contraindicated, consider referral to a specialist service for botulinum toxin A.\n\nScopolamine is also an antimuscarinic which can be used for drooling in Parkinson's disease but has a higher risk of adverse cognitive effects.\n\nIncreasing co-beneldopa will have no effect on non-motor symptoms of Parkinson's disease such as drooling.\n\nAtropine can be used but sublingual atropine ophthalmic solution is preferred to inhalers."
