      "correctText": "C7",
      "explanation": "Klumpke's paralysis: T1 nerve root damage\n\nC8/T1 nerve root damage is seen in Klumpke paralysis, which is a neuropathy of the lower brachial plexus that can occur from a difficult delivery. Typically, the eighth cervical and first thoracic nerves are injured either before or after they have joined to form the lower trunk. This injury can cause stretching (neuropraxia), tearing or scarring (neuroma) of the brachial plexus nerves.\n\n'Claw hand' is the main finding seen in this presentation where the forearm is supinated and the wrist and fingers are flexed. A reduction in sensation is seen along the medial aspect of the distal upper extremity along C8 and T1 dermatomes. Atrophy in the muscles of the forearm or hand may also be visible.\n\nC5-C6 root damage is seen in Erb's palsy which is an injury that affects the upper brachial plexus which usually results in findings along this myotome.\n\nC7 nerve damage can produce tingling, numbness and pain down the arm and into the middle finger. Weakness may be experienced in the back of the upper arm affecting the triceps muscle.\n\nC5 nerve damage can produce tingling, numbness and pain from the neck into the shoulder, down the arm and into the thumb. Weakness may be seen with the shoulder or upper arm.\n\nC8 isolated nerve damage would produce tingling, numbness, and pain down the arm, into the little finger. Handgrip strength may be reduced."
