      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Jerky nystagmus",
      "explanation": "Loss of corneal reflex - think acoustic neuroma\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nabsent corneal reflex\n\n. An acoustic neuroma, also known as vestibular schwannoma, is a benign tumour that arises from the vestibular part of the eighth cranial nerve (vestibulocochlear nerve). This nerve has two parts; the cochlear part which is involved in hearing and the vestibular part which is involved in balance. As the tumour grows, it can compress nearby structures including the fifth cranial nerve (trigeminal nerve), which mediates the corneal reflex. Therefore, an absent corneal reflex could be indicative of an acoustic neuroma.\n\nJerky nystagmus\n\ncan occur with vestibular schwannomas but it's not specific to this condition and can occur in many other conditions affecting the central nervous system such as multiple sclerosis or cerebrovascular disease. Additionally, jerky nystagmus would not typically present alone but with other symptoms of vestibular dysfunction like vertigo.\n\nLeft homonymous hemianopia\n\n, a loss of half of the field of view on the same side in both eyes, indicates damage to right optic tract or visual cortex rather than an issue with cranial nerves V or VIII. It's more commonly associated with stroke, brain injuries or tumours affecting these areas rather than an acoustic neuroma.\n\nThe option\n\ntongue deviated to the left\n\nsuggests damage to the hypoglossal nerve (cranial nerve XII), which controls tongue movements. If damaged, the tongue will deviate towards the side of lesion when protruded due to unopposed action of muscles on opposite side. It's not associated with acoustic neuromas.\n\nFinally,\n\nfasciculation of the tongue\n\nrefers to involuntary contractions or twitching in tongue muscle fibres. It's a common sign in motor neurone diseases and disorders involving lower motor neurons that supply tongue muscles. This symptom does not indicate an acoustic neuroma."
