      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Caudal pons at level of middle cerebellar peduncles",
      "explanation": "The medial longitudinal fasciculus is located in the paramedian area of the midbrain and pons\n\nThis patient has episodic neurological features, physical examination shows bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia, and MRI shows demyelination. The diagnosis is multiple sclerosis. Internuclear ophthalmoplegia is the inability to adduct the affected eye. When attempting to do so, it results in nystagmus and diplopia. The medial longitudinal fasciculus is affected, located in the paramedian area of midbrain & pons. It interconnects cranial nerves III, IV & VI and thereby facilitating conjugate eye movements.\n\nThe oculomotor nucleus is in ventral midbrain at the level of the superior colliculus. It innervates the inferior oblique, superior rectus, inferior rectus and medial rectus muscles for ocular movement. A lesion here will cause the eye to be pointed 'down and out', due to the unopposed action of lateral rectus and superior oblique. This patient can present with diplopia & inability to accommodate.\n\nThe nucleus of the trochlear nerve is in the ventral midbrain at the level of the inferior colliculus. Cranial nerve IV innervates the superior oblique, so any lesion here presents with diplopia, especially on downward gaze. The inability to intort the eye causes a characteristic head tilt towards the unaffected side.\n\nThe nucleus of the abducens nerve is in the paramedian area of dorsal pons. It is lateral to the medial longitudinal fasciculus. It innervates the lateral rectus so a lesion here would present with the inability of the affected eye to abduct. It can present with nystagmus and diplopia.\n\nThe facial nerve nucleus lies in the caudal pons at the level of the middle cerebellar peduncles. The efferent fibres leave dorsally and encircle the abducens nucleus. Then they exit at the pontomedullary junction to innervate the muscles of the face. Lesion here will cause palsies of the facial muscles."
