      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Medial longitudinal fasciculus",
      "explanation": "Internuclear ophthalmoplegia is due to a lesion in the medial longitudinal fasciculus\n\nMedial longitudinal fasciculus is the correct answer. This myelinated structure is located in the brainstem and is responsible for conjugate eye movements. This patient presents with tingling and double visual and examination findings suggestive of internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO). This is consistent with a diagnosis of multiple sclerosis. INO is a disorder of conjugate lateral gaze. When an attempt is made to look contralaterally from the affected side, the affected eye fails to adduct and the contralateral eye demonstrates nystagmus. The side of medial longitudinal fasciculus that is affected is that of the eye that fails to adduct.\n\nMamillary bodies is not the right answer. The ophthalmoplegia associated with Wernicke's encephalopathy can sometimes mimic internuclear ophthalmoplegia. However, there is no history of alcohol excess and the description is classic for internuclear ophthalmoplegia, typically associated with multiple sclerosis.\n\nNeuromuscular junction is incorrect. Ocular myasthenia can sometimes mimic an INO but this is not the likely diagnosis based on the history.\n\nOptic nerve is incorrect. A lesion of the optic nerve would cause monocular visual loss.\n\nOptic tract is incorrect. A lesion of the optic tract would cause homonymous hemianopia."
