      "id": 360,
      "question": "An 81-year-old man has been brought to the emergency department with worsening confusion. His partner explains that he fell downstairs 2 weeks ago, but that he felt well afterwards and refused to come into hospital for assessment. Since then, he has intermittently become drowsy and confused.\n\nUpon assessment, his GCS is 10 with no obvious lateralising neurology. A delirium screen is performed consisting of a panel of blood tests, a chest X-ray and a urine culture, all of which are unremarkable. A CT head scan is arranged.\n\nWhat radiological feature is most likely to be seen?",
      "options": [
        "Hyperdense biconcave collection",
        "Hyperdense lateral ventricle",
        "Hyperdensity within the brain parenchyma",
        "Isodense crescent-shaped collection",
        "Right-sided encephalomalacia"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Isodense crescent-shaped collection",
      "explanation": "Fluctuating consciousness = subdural haemorrhage\n\nA subdural haemorrhage is characterised by venous bleeding into the subdural space, situated between the dura mater and the arachnoid mater. This type of haemorrhage typically presents as a gradual bleed and is more commonly observed in older patients following trauma. As depicted in the clinical vignette, patients with a subdural haemorrhage may initially be asymptomatic after sustaining a head injury; however, as the bleeding progresses, symptoms such as fluctuating confusion and altered levels of consciousness may develop. On computed tomography (CT), a subacute subdural haemorrhage is visualised as an\n\nisodense crescent-shaped collection\n\n. It is important to note that the radiographic density of a subdural haemorrhage varies depending on its stage: it appears hyperdense during the acute phase, isodense when subacute, and hypodense during chronic stages.\n\nAn extradural haemorrhage originates from arterial bleeding and typically presents with more acute symptoms due to the rapid accumulation of blood. On imaging studies, it is seen as a\n\nbiconvex or lens-shaped hyperdense collection on CT scan.\n\n.\n\nBleeding into the lateral ventricles would appear on imaging as\n\na hyperdense lateral ventricle\n\n, indicative of an intraventricular haemorrhage.\n\nThe observation of\n\nhyperdensity within the brain parenchyma\n\non imaging suggests an intraparenchymal bleed.\n\nRight-sided encephalomalacia\n\ndenotes softening (manifested as hypodensity) of brain tissue secondary to liquefactive necrosis following an ischaemic stroke. This change corresponds to the area supplied by the occluded vessel."
