      "id": 367,
      "question": "An obese 24-year-old female presents with headaches and blurred vision. Examination reveals bilateral blurring of the optic discs but is otherwise unremarkable with no other neurological signs. Blood pressure is 130/74 and she is apyrexial. What is the most likely underlying diagnosis?",
      "options": [
        "Multiple sclerosis",
        "Meningococcal meningitis",
        "Brain abscess",
        "Normal pressure hydrocephalus",
        "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Normal pressure hydrocephalus",
      "explanation": "Obese, young female with headaches / blurred vision think idiopathic intracranial hypertension\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nIdiopathic intracranial hypertension\n\n(IIH). This condition, previously known as benign intracranial hypertension or pseudotumour cerebri, typically affects young, obese females. The presentation of headaches and bilateral papilloedema (blurred optic discs) without focal neurological signs, normal blood pressure, and absence of fever in an obese young woman is classical for IIH. The condition is characterised by raised intracranial pressure without any structural cause, and the diagnosis is one of exclusion.\n\nMultiple sclerosis\n\nwould be unlikely as the sole presenting feature would not typically be bilateral papilloedema. MS usually presents with various neurological symptoms including optic neuritis (which typically affects one eye), sensory disturbances, motor symptoms, or balance problems. The absence of other neurological signs makes this diagnosis less likely.\n\nMeningococcal meningitis\n\ncaused by\n\nNeisseria meningitidis\n\nis incorrect as the patient is apyrexial and lacks the classical features of meningitis such as neck stiffness, photophobia, and fever. Meningitis typically presents as an acute illness with rapid deterioration, not just headaches and papilloedema.\n\nBrain abscess\n\nis unlikely as the patient lacks fever or systemic symptoms of infection. Brain abscesses typically present with focal neurological signs, seizures, or altered mental status, depending on their location. They are often accompanied by systemic features of infection and risk factors such as immunosuppression or direct spread from nearby infections.\n\nNormal pressure hydrocephalus\n\nis incorrect as this condition typically affects elderly patients and presents with the classic triad of gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, and cognitive decline. The age of the patient and the absence of these characteristic symptoms make this diagnosis highly unlikely."
