      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Anterior corticospinal tract & lateral spinothalamic tract",
      "explanation": "In subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord, the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts are affected\n\nThis patient has a typical presentation of subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord (SACD) secondary to vitamin B12 deficiency. In SACD there is a patchy loss of the myeline primary affecting the ascending dorsal columns, involved in proprioception and vibrational sensation, and the descending lateral corticospinal tracts, affecting voluntary motor. As in this patient, SACD typically presents with progressive weakness, tingling and numbness as well as upper motor neuron signs of the lower limbs. Vision changes and cognitive decline may also be present.\n\nAlthough the dorsal column is affected in SACD, the ascending anterior spinothalamic tract, which carries crude touch and pressure information, is rarely involved. This patient has signs of muscle weakness due to lateral corticospinal tract involvement which is a hallmark of SACD.\n\nAgain, although the dorsal column is involved, the anterior spinocerebellar tract is not affected in SACD. The anterior spinocerebellar tract carries unconscious proprioceptive and cutaneous information from the lower aspect of the body which is not typically affected in SACD.\n\nIt is the lateral corticospinal tract and not the anterior tract involved in SACD leading to motor dysfunction. The lateral spinothalamic tract, which carried pain and temperature information, is not commonly involved in SACD and so these sensational modalities are unaffected.\n\nThe reticulospinal tract is primarily involved in locomotion and postural control whereas the vestibulospinal tract carries information from the vestibulocochlear nerve regarding changes in the head orientation. Both tracts are not commonly affected in SACD."
