      "explanation": "Spastic paraparesis can be caused by transverse myelitis\n\nThe symmetrical leg weakness with upper motor neurone signs describes the clinical picture of spastic paraparesis. In addition, there is a sensory level - pointing towards a spinal lesion.\n\nThe history of a blistering rash that sounds dermatomal should raise suspicions of varicella, which can cause transverse myelitis (focal inflammation of the spinal cord).\n\nVenous sinus thrombosis presents with headache, fever and cranial nerve palsies.\n\nGBS and vincristine neuropathy are pathologies of the peripheral nerves and therefore lower motor neurone  (LMN) signs would be expected.\n\nLeg weakness in the context of malignancy should always raise suspicions of cauda equina syndrome, however, it also causes LMN signs. Additionally, as the cauda equina starts at L1/2 its compression cannot cause a sensory level at T10 (umbilicus). Malignant\n\ncord compression\n\nwould be in the differential diagnosis."
