      "explanation": "Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy: facial weakness (e.g. difficulty closing eyes, smiling, blowing), winged scapula and weakness of the shoulderm upper arm and hip girdle muscles\n\nFacioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy is correct. The onset of this disorder is typically prior to the age of 20. It is characterized by facial weakness (difficulty closing eyes and smiling) and weakness of the shoulder and hip girdle muscles (i.e. the proximal muscles). A winged scapula may also be a feature.\n\nBell's palsy is incorrect. This would be of an acute onset rather than an insidious progression. Additionally, it is characterized by facial weakness and does not cause limb weakness.\n\nDermatomyositis is incorrect. There is no rash and therefore this is not dermatomyositis.\n\nDuchenne muscular dystrophy is incorrect. This is an X-linked recessive disorder and therefore doesn't affect females.\n\nMyasthenia gravis is incorrect. This disorder is characterized by 'fatigable weakness'. The examination findings, in this case, do not mention that there was no weakness initially and subsequent development of weakness on repeated examination."
