      "correctText": "Long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT)",
      "explanation": "Motor neuron disease - treatment: NIV is better than riluzole\n\nNIV is the correct option as this patient has a classical presentation of motor neuron disease (MND) with a mixture of upper motor neuron signs: hypertonia and most likely a spastic paralysis of the muscles of respiration (seen by the raised diaphragm) and lower motor neuron signs: muscle wasting, fasciculations and hyporeflexia. The most likely subtype of MND, in this case, is amyotrophic lateral sclerosis due to the early presentation with respiratory failure and profound diaphragmatic weakness. NIV has shown a survival benefit of 7 months on average.\n\nRiluzole is an incorrect option, as it prolongs life in MND by an average of 3 months.\n\nLTOT is incorrect as COPD is not the cause of the breathlessness, in this case, demonstrated by the minimal work of breathing (tachypnoea in COPD worsening), bilateral diaphragmatic palsy (hyper-expanded 'barrel' lungs in COPD) and the other neurological signs point towards MND as the most likely cause of the type 2 respiratory failure in this case.\n\nPulmonary rehabilitation is incorrect even though it has the best evidence base for prolonging the life expectancy of COPD patients. This is not the case when muscle function is being lost at the rate of this patient due to MND.\n\nPyridostigmine is incorrect because this is a drug used to reduce symptoms in myasthenia gravis. MND is a much more likely diagnosis, even though myasthenia gravis can present with respiratory weakness and type 2 respiratory failure, it typically presents with muscle fatigability, worse towards the end of the day and usually marked ptosis."
