      "id": 384,
      "question": "A 35-year-old man presents with recurrent headaches, dizziness, and visual disturbances. Examination reveals a palpable left-sided abdominal mass, and fundoscopy shows multiple bilateral retinal haemangioblastomas.\n\nAbdominal imaging identifies a solid mass in the left kidney, while brain imaging reveals a cerebellar haemangioblastoma.\n\nHe reports a family history of similar symptoms associated with a genetic disorder.\n\nWhat is the most commonly associated carcinoma with this patient's likely condition?",
      "options": [
        "Adenocarcinoma of the pancreas",
        "Clear-cell renal cell carcinoma",
        "Medullary thyroid carcinoma",
        "Papillary renal cell carcinoma",
        "Small-cell lung carcinoma"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Papillary renal cell carcinoma",
      "explanation": "Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome is associated with the development of clear-cell renal cell carcinoma\n\nThe patient’s presentation is strongly suggestive of Von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) syndrome. The presence of retinal haemangioblastomas, a cerebellar haemangioblastoma, a solid renal mass on imaging, and a family history of similar symptoms are hallmark features of this autosomal dominant condition. VHL gene mutations lead to the accumulation of hypoxia-inducible factors, which disrupt normal cellular response to low oxygen levels, promoting angiogenesis and the development of vascular tumours such as retinal and cerebellar haemangioblastomas, as well as clear-cell renal cell carcinoma.\n\nClear-cell renal cell carcinoma\n\nis the most common solid tumour associated with VHL syndrome and requires proactive screening and management due to its high prevalence in these patients.\n\nWhile VHL syndrome can involve pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours,\n\nadenocarcinoma of the pancreas\n\nis not a common manifestation. The vascular tumours and renal mass in this patient’s presentation are classic for VHL syndrome and clear-cell renal cell carcinoma, excluding pancreatic adenocarcinoma as a likely diagnosis. The genetic basis and tumour profile of VHL syndrome reinforce the association with clear-cell renal cell carcinoma.\n\nMedullary thyroid carcinoma\n\nis primarily associated with multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 (MEN2), which includes MEN2A (medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytomas, and parathyroid hyperplasia) and MEN2B (medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytomas, mucosal neuromas). These syndromes are caused by mutations in the RET proto-oncogene. In contrast, VHL syndrome is characterised by retinal and cerebellar haemangioblastomas, clear-cell renal cell carcinoma, pheochromocytomas, and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours, but not medullary thyroid carcinoma. Therefore, medullary thyroid carcinoma is incorrect.\n\nThe patient’s symptoms and imaging findings align more with VHL syndrome than with other hereditary cancer syndromes.\n\nPapillary renal cell carcinoma\n\n, typically linked to MET gene mutations, is not a feature of VHL. The hallmark vascular tumours and solid renal masses seen in VHL syndrome are most commonly clear-cell renal cell carcinomas, making papillary renal cell carcinoma an unlikely diagnosis in this context.\n\nSmall-cell lung carcinoma\n\nis incorrect because it primarily arises from neuroendocrine cells in the lungs and is strongly associated with smoking. While it may present with paraneoplastic syndromes or distant metastases, it is unrelated to the genetic disorder suggested in this patient, likely VHL disease. VHL is characterised by retinal and cerebellar haemangioblastomas, renal cell carcinoma, and other tumours linked to mutations in the VHL gene. Small cell lung carcinoma is not a feature among the tumours associated with VHL disease, making it an unlikely consideration in this context."
