      "correctText": "Continue amitriptyline and start tramadol",
      "explanation": "Drugs for neuropathic pain are typically used as monotherapy, i.e. if not working then drugs should be switched, not added\n\nThis question tests your knowledge of chronic pain management whilst the context may also bring attention to poorly controlled acute pain.\n\nIn general, the management of chronic pain revolves around the use of neuropathic pain agents to aid the downregulation of pain responses, these include agents such as duloxetine, gabapentinoids, and amitriptyline (a tricyclic antidepressant).\n\nIn general when a minimal benefit is received from the use of a neuropathic pain agent they should be swapped rather than added. This means the answers above that include starting duloxetine or gabapentin in addition to amitriptyline are incorrect.\n\nMorphine sulphate is used in the management of acute and cancer pain, it commonly leads to side effects, dependence, and worsening of chronic pain and as such isn't ideal in this question. Tramadol, rather than morphine or codeine, is recommended for the management of breakthrough pain in neuropathic pain sufferers however doesn't change a patient's chronic pain management."
