      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Anti-Hu antibody",
      "explanation": "Lambert-Eaton syndrome - antibodies against the voltage-gated Ca2+ channels\n\nVoltage-gated Ca2+ channel antibody\n\nis the correct answer. The patient presents with proximal lower limb weakness with a lower motor neuron pattern (weakness, areflexia) that improves with repeat testing. An EMG demonstrates an increment in muscle action potentials on repeated testing. These findings raised the suspicion of Lambert-Eaton syndrome. The pulmonary lesion likely represents a small-cell lung cancer, which is associated with this condition. Antibodies against voltage-gated Ca2+ channels are associated with Lambert-Eaton syndrome.\n\nAnti-Hu antibody\n\nis incorrect. This is an antibody associated with paraneoplastic syndromes in small-cell lung cancer. However, the clinical features are typically subacute sensory neuropathy and encephalomyelitis rather than lower limb weakness.\n\nAnti-Jo1 antibody\n\nis incorrect. This antibody is associated with myositis. In myositis, the reflexes will be preserved and the CK will typically be elevated, making this diagnosis unlikely.\n\nAnti-acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibody\n\nis incorrect. This antibody is associated with myasthenia gravis, which is the main differential diagnosis for Lambert-Eaton syndrome. However, in this disorder, we would see a weakness that worsens with exertion (i.e. fatigable) and EMG testing would demonstrate decrements of muscle action potentials on repeated testing. Ocular involvement is also absent in this case and is more common in myasthenia gravis than Lambert-Eaton syndrome.\n\nAnti-muscle-specific tyrosine kinase antibody\n\nis incorrect. This antibody is more likely to be associated with generalized myasthenia gravis and this is unlikely for reasons outlined above."
