      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Lamotrigine",
      "explanation": "A rare but recognised adverse effect of lamotrigine therapy is Stevens-Johnson syndrome\n\nThe correct answer is lamotrigine.\n\nThis woman has presented with Stevens-Johnson syndrome. While the BNF lists severe cutaneous adverse reactions (SCAR) under most anticonvulsants/anti-epileptic drugs (AEDs), patients who experience AED-induced Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) will still require ongoing treatment for their epilepsy with an option that minimises the risk of SJS. The greatest risk is with aromatic AEDs such as phenytoin, phenobarbital and carbamazepine, but lamotrigine is also a well-recognised cause.\n\nThere may be an issue with over-reporting / over-diagnosis of SCAR and SJS in patients taking AEDs due to an awareness of the possibility for reactions.\n\nNewer studies suggest that sodium valproate, gabapentin, pregabalin, levetiracetam, topiramate, clonazepam and ethosuximide are lower risk for SJS.\n\nThe other listed drugs can cause a range of other side effects.\n\nGabapentin can cause lethargy, nausea and vomiting, mood changes, blurred vision and dry mouth, erectile dysfunction, weight gain, headaches and memory impairment. It has been associated with a risk of severe respiratory depression even without concomitant opioids.\n\nLevetiracetam commonly causes drowsiness, headaches, nausea and vomiting. Levetiracetam has been associated with renal impairment and behavioural change including increased risk of suicidal thoughts.\n\nSodium valproate commonly causes nausea and vomiting, diarrhoea, tremors, headache, weight gain, thinning hair and irregular menses. It is also teratogenic - women of childbearing potential should use highly effective contraception during treatment.\n\nTopiramate commonly causes nausea, lethargy, diarrhoea, mood change and weight loss. It is importantly associated with risk of glaucoma, renal stones, metabolic acidosis and reduced sweating. It should be avoided in acute porphyrias."
