      "explanation": "Broca's dysphasia: speech non-fluent, comprehension normal, repetition impaired\n\nInferior frontal gyrus\n\nis correct.  The patient has Broca's dysphasia characterised by non-fluent speech but normal comprehension. The patient is able to follow complex commands indicative of normal comprehension. Broca's dysphasia occurs due to a lesion of the inferior frontal gyrus.\n\nLateral geniculate nucleus\n\nis incorrect. This is a component of the thalamus which is a very important component of the visual pathway. A lesion of this nucleus produces moderately to completely congruent visual field defects.\n\nMedial geniculate nucleus\n\nis incorrect. The medial geniculate nucleus is part of the auditory thalamus and represents the thalamic relay between the inferior colliculus and the auditory cortex. Although the medial geniculate nucleus is a well-known integration centre for the central auditory pathways, auditory symptoms caused by damage to these nuclei are rarely described. However, there are some case reports of auditory hallucinations.\n\nStriate cortex\n\nis incorrect. This is the part of the brain which deals with the processing of signals from the visual pathway. Lesions of the striate cortex can result in blindness.\n\nSuperior temporal gyrus\n\nis incorrect. Wernicke's aphasia occurs due to a lesion of the superior temporal gyrus.  In Wernicke's aphasia, the ability to grasp the meaning of spoken words and sentences is impaired. Patients with Wernicke's aphasia speak normal words fluently, often including meaningless phonemes, but do not know their meaning or relationships. The result is a jumble of words or 'word salad'. Patients are typically unaware that their speech is incomprehensible to others."
