      "correctText": "T1-weighted (T1W) sequence",
      "explanation": "Use MRI FLAIR sequence in diagnosis of multiple sclerosis vs. MRI STIR in flares of thyroid eye disease\n\nDynamic contrast-enhanced (DCE) MRI\n\nsequence is commonly used in oncology management and involves the administration of a contrast agent during imaging to assess blood flow and tissue permeability. Unlike static MRI sequences, DCE provides a dynamic view of contrast distribution especially valuable in oncology, as it helps evaluate tumour vascularity and guide treatment planning. DCE-MRI's ability to depict changes in tissue perfusion and capillary permeability enhances its role in understanding and describing the features of lesions, monitoring treatment response, and contributing to precise diagnoses in various medical contexts.\n\nMRI short tau inversion recovery (STIR)\n\nsequence selectively nullifies signals from fat, enhancing the visibility of pathological tissues. Unlike traditional sequences, STIR employs a short inversion time, suppressing the fat signal and emphasising water-based tissues. This makes it particularly useful for imaging musculoskeletal structures, joints, and soft tissues such as in thyroid eye disease (TED). In TED, STIR helps visualise soft tissue changes, inflammation, and oedema around the eye, aiding with assessing disease severity and guiding management.\n\nT1-weighted (T1W) MRI\n\nsequence highlights differences in the relaxation times (the time it takes for the nuclear magnetic spins within tissues to return to their equilibrium state) of tissues, producing images with distinct anatomical contrast. While T1W sequences are valuable for anatomical imaging, they have limited sensitivity to MS-related changes.\n\nT2-weighted (T2W) MRI\n\nsequence accentuates differences in tissue water content and is particularly adept at highlighting pathology. Its primary uses include detecting oedema, inflammation, and lesions, making it invaluable in neurological and musculoskeletal imaging. However, compared to FLAIR sequences, T2W is less effective in suppressing the signal from cerebrospinal fluid, often leading to reduced lesion conspicuity in certain scenarios, especially in neurological conditions like MS where FLAIR sequences are preferred for enhanced lesion visualisation."
