      "explanation": "Ondansetron is a 5-HT 3 serotonin antagonist\n\nOndansetron is a 5-HT3 serotonin antagonist, which blocks 5-HT3 receptors in the gastrointestinal tract and the central nervous system, and specifically at the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ). It is particularly effective for chemotherapy induced nausea and vomiting and is often used first line in this setting. However, constipation is a very common side-effect and so laxatives should usually be co-prescribed.\n\n5-HT1 agonists are used in the acute management of migraine. Examples include sumatriptan and rizatriptan.\n\n5-HT2 antagonists are used for migraine prophylaxis, however their use is limited by side effects:\n\nPizotifen: weight gain and drowsiness\nMethysergide: retroperitoneal fibrosis\n\nDopamine antagonists are also used in the management of nausea and vomiting. These include antipsychotic medications such as haloperidol and levomepromazine, as well metoclopramide and domperidone. Metoclopramide  acts directly on the gastro-intestinal tract and is useful to treat nausea and vomiting associated with gastroduodenal, hepatic, and biliary disease. However, it should be used with caution in young women as it may induce an acute dystonic reaction such as oculogyric crises in this group.\n\nH1 (histamine) antagonist is incorrect. Cyclizine is an example of a H1-antagonist, which is effective in treating nausea and vomiting resulting from many underlying conditions."
