      "id": 413,
      "question": "Each of the following features are seen in myotonic dystrophy, except:",
      "options": [
        "Mild mental impairment",
        "Round face",
        "Frontal balding",
        "Myotonia",
        "Cataracts"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Mild mental impairment",
      "explanation": "D\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nRound face\n\n. Myotonic dystrophy (DM) is an autosomal dominant multisystem disorder characterised by myotonia, progressive muscle weakness, and involvement of multiple organ systems. Patients with myotonic dystrophy typically present with a distinctive facial appearance, but this is characterised by a long, thin face with temporal wasting and ptosis, not a round face. The facial appearance is often described as 'hatchet-faced' due to the wasting of the temporalis and masseter muscles, along with weakness of facial muscles.\n\nMild mental impairment\n\nis indeed a feature of myotonic dystrophy, particularly in congenital and childhood-onset forms. Cognitive impairment in myotonic dystrophy can range from mild learning difficulties to more significant intellectual disability. Executive dysfunction, visuospatial deficits, and attention problems are commonly observed cognitive features.\n\nFrontal balding\n\nis a characteristic feature of myotonic dystrophy, particularly in males. This premature frontal balding (frontal alopecia) is one of the recognisable physical manifestations of the condition and contributes to the distinctive facial appearance of affected individuals.\n\nMyotonia\n\nis the hallmark feature of myotonic dystrophy, from which the condition derives its name. Myotonia refers to delayed muscle relaxation after contraction, which clinically manifests as muscle stiffness. Patients often describe difficulty releasing their grip after a handshake or may demonstrate the phenomenon when asked to make a fist and then rapidly open their hand. This feature can be objectively demonstrated by percussion myotonia, where tapping a muscle (commonly the thenar eminence) results in a delayed relaxation.\n\nCataracts\n\nare a common ocular manifestation of myotonic dystrophy. These are typically posterior subcapsular iridescent cataracts, which may be detected on slit-lamp examination before they become visually significant. Cataracts can sometimes be the presenting feature of myotonic dystrophy, particularly in milder forms of the condition, and may develop at a relatively young age compared to age-related cataracts.\n\nOther important features of myotonic dystrophy not mentioned in the options include cardiac conduction abnormalities, endocrine disturbances (such as insulin resistance and testicular atrophy), respiratory muscle weakness, excessive daytime sleepiness, and gastrointestinal symptoms. The condition exists in two main forms: myotonic dystrophy type 1 (DM1), caused by a CTG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the DMPK gene, and myotonic dystrophy type 2 (DM2), caused by a CCTG tetranucleotide repeat expansion in the CNBP gene."
