      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Topiramate",
      "explanation": "A rare but recognised adverse effect of lamotrigine therapy is Stevens-Johnson syndrome\n\nThis child has developed features clearly in keeping with the potential life-threatening skin reaction Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS). Although potential causes include viral infection and malignancies, drug reactions are by far the most common cause of SJS. Several medication can be responsible for SJS including two main anti-epileptics; phenytoin and\n\nlamotrigine\n\n. The other anti-epileptics options are not commonly known to be associated with the condition.\n\nLevetiracetam\n\n, commonly known by its brand name Keppra, is an anti-epileptic used in the management of partial-onset, myoclonic or tonic-clonic seizures. Compared to other anti-epileptics, the incidences of SJS with levetiracetam are extremely low with only sparse reporting of a potential association.\n\nSodium valproate\n\n, another common anti-epileptic is rarely associated with SJS and in most reported incidences it is found that other drugs along with valproate were administered.  Severe side effects associated with sodium valproate include hepatotoxicity, pancreatitis, and fetal abnormalities in pregnancy.\n\nTopiramate\n\nis a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor mainly used in the management of migraines, but it is also still used for both generalised and partial seizures. As a less commonly used anti-epileptic, topiramate is often used in complex seizures in children. It is also used in the management of overuse/rebound headaches secondary to chronic analgesia use. Topiramate is not associated with SJS, with side effects including impaired cognition, paraesthesia and rarely metabolic acidosis.\n\nValpromide\n\nis a derivative of valproic acid also used for the treatment of epilepsy and some affective disorders. It has a similar side effect profile as sodium valproate and again is not associated with SJS."
