      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Demyelination of the peripheral nervous system",
      "explanation": "Myasthenia gravis - antibodies against acetylcholine receptors\n\nThe history is suggestive of myasthenia gravis, which causes dysfunction at the neuromuscular junction due to the body forming antibodies against the acetylcholine receptor.\n\nNeurological symptoms associated with cerebral infarction would usually be unilateral, with an abrupt onset and non-fluctuating course.\n\nDemyelination of the central nervous system is the pathophysiology in multiple sclerosis (MS). This is an important differential to consider, but the history is more typical of myasthenia gravis. In particular, the typical eye symptom in MS is optic neuritis - painful loss of vision.\n\nDemyelination of the peripheral nervous system is the pathophysiology in Guillain-Barre syndrome. In this condition you would expect the weakness to be progressive and reflexes to be diminished.\n\nInclusion bodies in motor neurones causes amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, a type of motor neurone disease. In this condition you would expect the weakness to be progressive and associated with muscle stiffness/spasms. The extra-ocular muscles are usually spared until late in the disease, so the eye symptoms make this diagnosis less likely."
