      "id": 452,
      "question": "A 63-year-old female presents with a four-month history of progressive weakness in the lower limbs associated with numbness. She also complains of feeling tired and lightheaded lately. She has had recent investigation for this and showed macrocytic anaemia with vitamin B12 deficiency. She is currently awaiting to commence on B12 replacement. Otherwise, she is normally fit and well and is not on any regular medication.\n\nNeurological examination of the lower limb shows the following:\n\n\n\tLeft\tRight\nPower\t4/5\t4/5\nSensation to coarse touch, pain, temperature and pressure\tnormal\tnormal\nSensation to fine touch and vibration\treduced\treduced\nProprioception\treduced\treduced\nAnkle reflex\tabsent\tabsent\nBabinski response\tupgoing\tupgoing\n\nWhich of the following area of the spinal cord is most likely affected in this patient?",
      "options": [
        "Anterior and dorsal columns",
        "Anterior and lateral columns",
        "Lateral horn",
        "Ventral horn",
        "Dorsal and lateral columns"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Ventral horn",
      "explanation": "In subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord, the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts are affected\n\nThe correct answer is 'dorsal and lateral column'. This patient has reduced power in the lower limbs with reduced sensation to fine touch, loss of proprioception and vibratory sensation. These symptoms and signs are suggestive of subacute combined degeneration of the cord likely secondary to vitamin B12 deficiency. It most commonly affects the dorsal columns, and less commonly, the lateral columns. Involvement of the dorsal columns manifests clinically as issues with proprioception and vibration sense. Whilst the lateral columns primarily contain the corticospinal tracts which are the principal motor pathway.\n\nThe anterior column primarily contains the spinothalamic tracts which are responsible for pain, temperature, coarse touch and pressure sensations.\n\nThe lateral horns of the spinal cord contain the neuronal cell bodies of the sympathetic nervous system. Disruption of the lateral horns at the levels of T1-T2 can lead to the classic triad of partial ptosis, anhidrosis and miosis seen in Horner syndrome.\n\nThe ventral horns of the spinal cord contain motor neurones for skeletal muscles. Associated pathology includes amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease and progressive muscular atrophy."
