      "explanation": "For thrombectomy in acute ischaemic stroke, an extended target time of 6-24 hours may be considered if there is the potential to salvage brain tissue, as shown by imaging such as CT perfusion or diffusion-weighted MRI sequences showing limited infarct core volume\n\nThe answer is mechanical thrombectomy. This man is suffering from a partial anterior circulation stroke, and fits the criteria for mechanical thrombectomy. He scores 2 on the Rankin scale by being independent of his own affairs and scores 8 on the NIHSS (2 for 'some effort against gravity' in each right limb, 3 for facial palsy, 1 for mild dysarthria). He also has confirmed occlusion of the proximal anterior circulation and CT perfusion showing limited infarct core volume, which indicates potential to salvage brain tissue.\n\nGiving aspirin 300mg oral is appropriate as a haemorrhagic stroke has been excluded, although this should have been given immediately following the original CT Head, and it is not the definitive management when mechanical thrombectomy is indicated.\n\nNeurosurgical referral would be more appropriate for a haemorrhagic stroke.\n\nAlteplase can be used for intravenous thrombolysis but as this was a wake-up stroke, it cannot be guaranteed that onset of symptoms was in 4.5 hours and it is therefore contraindicated.\n\nWarfarin should be avoided in the acute phase following ischaemic stroke due to risk of haemorrhagic transformation. If this patient had atrial fibrillation, warfarin could be considered after the acute phase to prevent formation of further thromboses."
