      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Enlarged fourth ventricle",
      "explanation": "Urinary incontinence + gait abnormality + dementia = normal pressure hydrocephalus\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nPapilloedema\n\n. Normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) is a condition characterised by ventriculomegaly, usually with normal cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) pressures. The classic triad of symptoms includes dementia, urinary incontinence and gait abnormality. Papilloedema, which refers to swelling of the optic disc due to increased intracranial pressure, is not typically associated with NPH as the CSF pressure is usually normal.\n\nDementia\n\nis one of the key features of NPH. It may present as cognitive decline, memory impairment or changes in personality and behaviour. This happens due to the dilation of the ventricular system causing compression on adjacent brain structures.\n\nUrinary incontinence\n\nis also part of the classic triad of NPH symptoms. It often precedes other signs and can be mistaken for a symptom of ageing or a urological problem. The pathophysiology behind this symptom involves disruption of cortical control over bladder function due to ventricular enlargement.\n\nA\n\nGait abnormality\n\n, specifically a magnetic gait where feet appear stuck to the floor, is another characteristic feature seen in NPH patients. This symptom often presents before dementia and urinary incontinence and can lead to frequent falls.\n\nLastly, an\n\nEnlarged fourth ventricle\n\ncan indeed be seen on imaging studies such as MRI in patients with NPH; however, it's important to note that all cerebral ventricles tend to be enlarged symmetrically in this condition due to an imbalance between CSF production and absorption."
