      "correctText": "SOX18 gene",
      "explanation": "Ataxic telangiectasia is characterised by cerebellar ataxia and telangiectasia, onset is in childhood\n\nATM gene\n\nis correct. The patient's symptoms of ataxia and telangiectasia suggest he has Ataxia Telangiectasia, an autosomal recessive disorder caused by a defect in the ATM gene which encodes for DNA repair enzymes. This condition typically presents in early childhood with abnormal movements. Other features can include IgA deficiency resulting in recurrent chest infections and a 10% risk of developing malignancy, lymphoma or leukaemia, but also non-lymphoid tumours.\n\nACVRL1 gene\n\nis incorrect. Mutations of this gene are associated with hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia. The absence of bleeding and the presence of ataxia make this a less likely diagnosis. However, it is worth pointing out that cerebral atrioventricular malformations may rarely present with cerebellar dysfunction.\n\nFXN gene\n\nis incorrect. Mutations of this gene are associated with Friedreich ataxia. Although this condition can also present with ataxia, the presence of telangiectasia makes this a less likely diagnosis.\n\nSCA1-SCA3 gene\n\nis incorrect. Mutations of this gene are associated with spinocerebellar ataxia. Although this condition can also present with ataxia, the presence of telangiectasia makes this a less likely diagnosis.\n\nSOX18 gene\n\nis incorrect. Mutations of this gene are associated with hypotrichosis-lymphedema-telangiectasia syndrome. The absence of lymphedema and hypotrichosis makes this a less likely diagnosis."
