      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Treponema pallidum",
      "explanation": "Distal sensory loss, tingling + absent ankle jerks/extensor plantars + gait abnormalities/Romberg's positive → subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord\n\nThese examination findings consist of sensory findings and mixed upper and lower motor signs. These bilateral upper motor signs suggest myelopathy, with involvement of the lateral corticospinal tracts. The sensory signs would also be consistent with myelopathy, due to the involvement of the dorsal columns.\n\nCauses of both upper and lower motor neuron signs include:\n\nMotor neurone disease\nSubacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord\nSyphilitic tabes dorsalis\nFriedreich's ataxia\nDual pathology\nMyelopathy affecting the exiting roots\n\nNitrous oxide abuse can lead to subacute combined degeneration by inactivating vitamin B12. Therefore\n\nvitamin B12\n\nis most likely linked to this patient's presentation and so is the correct answer.\n\nTreponema pallidum\n\nis the organism which causes syphilis. Neurosyphilis can cause myelopathy by affecting the posterior columns of the spinal cord and the dorsal roots. Pain (typically lancinating in nature) is a common feature which is not mentioned in this case. Additionally, the interval between primary infection and neurosyphilis is around 20 years. Therefore, this is not likely the cause in this patient.\n\nAlcohol\n\ncould explain the distal sensory loss as it can cause neuropathy. However, alcohol is not associated with myelopathy, so this answer is incorrect.\n\nA syndrome similar to subacute combined degeneration of the cord can be seen in patients with acquired\n\ncopper\n\ndeficiency. However, there is no suggestion of this in the patient. It is associated with previous gastrointestinal surgery or excessive zinc ingestion.\n\nSerotonin\n\nrelated medication such as sertraline can lead to serotonin syndrome. Hyperreflexia is common. However, this patient also has absent reflexes. Also, there are several signs of serotonin syndrome that would be expected that are absent in this patient, including rigidity, agitation and tremor. Therefore, this answer is incorrect."
