      "correctText": "Nerve conduction studies",
      "explanation": "Multiple sclerosis diagnosis that requires demyelinating lesions that are separated in space and time\n\nMRI of the brain and spinal cord with contrast\n\nis correct because multiple sclerosis (MS) is diagnosed based on MRI findings showing demyelinating lesions separated in space and time, which is necessary to confirm the diagnosis. This patient’s history of relapsing neurological symptoms affecting different CNS regions (optic neuritis, sensory deficits, balance issues, and bladder dysfunction) strongly suggests MS, and MRI with contrast is the best test to demonstrate active and chronic demyelination. Contrast-enhancing lesions indicate recent inflammation, while T2 hyperintense lesions represent chronic damage, fulfilling the McDonald criteria for dissemination in time. Dissemination in space is confirmed if lesions appear in multiple characteristic locations, such as the periventricular, juxtacortical, infratentorial, and spinal cord regions. Given this patient’s optic neuritis, sensory deficits, and ataxic symptoms, an MRI is the most definitive test to confirm MS and rule out other causes of demyelination.\n\nCerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis for oligoclonal bands\n\nis incorrect because although oligoclonal bands in the CSF indicate chronic CNS inflammation, they do not provide evidence of dissemination in space and time, which is required for MS diagnosis. While CSF findings support MS, MRI remains the primary diagnostic tool, as it provides direct visualisation of demyelinating lesions. In this patient, CSF analysis may be useful if MRI findings are inconclusive, but it cannot replace MRI for definitive diagnosis.\n\nEvoked potentials testing\n\nis incorrect because while evoked potentials (e.g., visual evoked potentials for optic neuritis) can detect subclinical demyelination, they do not fulfill the diagnostic criteria for MS. This patient’s optic neuritis is already clinically apparent, making evoked potentials unnecessary. Furthermore, MS diagnosis requires radiological evidence of lesions, which evoked potentials cannot provide.\n\nNerve conduction studies\n\nare incorrect because they assess peripheral nerve function, while MS is a central nervous system demyelinating disorder. This patient’s symptoms, including optic neuritis, sensory loss, and ataxia, suggest CNS involvement rather than a peripheral nerve disorder, making nerve conduction studies unhelpful in this case.\n\nSerum anti-aquaporin-4 antibody testing\n\nis incorrect because these antibodies are specific for neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder (NMOSD), which primarily affects the optic nerves and spinal cord but follows a more severe and distinct course than MS. This patient’s relapsing-remitting pattern with multifocal CNS involvement strongly suggests MS rather than NMOSD. If NMOSD were suspected, MRI findings would typically show longitudinally extensive spinal cord lesions, which are not characteristic of MS."
