      "explanation": "There is a repeat expansion of CAG trinucleotide in Huntington's disease\n\nCAG repeat expansion on chromosome 4\n\nis the correct answer. Huntington's disease is caused by an autosomal dominant mutation involving a CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the huntingtin gene (HTT) located on chromosome 4. This leads to progressive degeneration of neurons, particularly in the striatum, resulting in characteristic motor, cognitive, and psychiatric features. The presence of anticipation—earlier onset in successive generations—is due to further CAG expansions during transmission.\n\nCGG repeat expansion on X chromosome\n\nrefers to fragile X syndrome, which presents with intellectual disability, macroorchidism, and facial dysmorphism rather than adult-onset movement disorder or psychiatric symptoms as seen here.\n\nCTG repeat expansion on chromosome 19\n\ncauses myotonic dystrophy type 1. This disorder presents with myotonia, muscle weakness, cataracts, cardiac conduction defects, and endocrinopathies—not chorea or prominent psychiatric features.\n\nGAA repeat expansion on chromosome 9\n\nresults in Friedreich’s ataxia, characterised by progressive ataxia, dysarthria, cardiomyopathy, and diabetes mellitus; it typically presents earlier in life with cerebellar rather than basal ganglia signs.\n\nDeletion of dystrophin gene on X chromosome\n\ncauses Duchenne muscular dystrophy (and Becker’s if partially functional), presenting with proximal muscle weakness in boys—distinct from the adult-onset movement disorder described here."
