      "explanation": "Lethargy is a very common early symptom of multiple sclerosis\n\nImportant risk factors for multiple sclerosis include female sex, ages between 20-40 and family history. Multiple sclerosis can present with monocular visual disturbances and peculiar sensory phenomena. Typically, the lesions associated with MS will be separated in anatomical location and time; giving a relapsing and remitting course of symptoms. However, lethargy, as well as other non-specific symptoms such as leg cramping, increased urinary frequency and bowel dysfunction, can commonly be seen in early presentations of multiple sclerosis.\n\nMyasthenia would be more commonly seen with fatiguability following exertion. Visual disturbances are more commonly binocular with other symptoms such as diplopia and ptosis.\n\nFibromyalgia may present with muscle aches and fatigue, however, the previous neurological disturbances would not be seen.\n\nWhilst the patient does have mild anaemia, the previous symptoms of visual disturbance would not be seen and are more suggestive of multiple sclerosis.\n\nGiant cell arteritis can present with muscle aches and fatigue. Visual loss can also be seen. Nevertheless, the presentation is typically in those over 50 years of age alongside other features in the history such as headaches, jaw claudication and scalp tenderness. Red desaturation is less commonly seen and is more in keeping with multiple sclerosis. Typically, visual loss giant cell arteritis does not recover if left untreated."
