      "explanation": "Motor neuron disease - treatment: NIV is better than riluzole\n\nNon-invasive ventilation (NIV) is the correct answer. The patient presents with progressive weakness of the limbs with mixed upper motor neuron and lower motor neuron signs with preserved sensation. The most likely diagnosis is motor neuron disease. The treatment with the greatest prognostic benefit in this condition is NIV.\n\nIntravenous immunoglobulin is incorrect. This treatment has an anti-inflammatory effect and can be used in conditions such as myasthenia gravis and Guillain-Barre syndrome. However, these conditions are characterized by a lower motor neuron or myopathic distribution of weakness. Hyperreflexia and hypertonia would not be typical.\n\nNatalizumab is incorrect. This is a monoclonal antibody directed against cell adhesion molecule α4-integrin. It is used in the treatment of multiple sclerosis (MS). If MS presents with weakness, it is characterized by an upper motor neuron distribution of weakness. Hypotonia and hyporeflexia would not be characteristic.\n\nPrednisolone is incorrect. This can be used in a wide variety of inflammatory conditions, some of which cause weakness. However, the distribution of examination findings, in this case, suggests a diagnosis of MND and prednisolone has no prognostic benefit in this condition.\n\nRiluzole is incorrect. This is a medication used in MND that works by blocking glutamatergic neurotransmission in the CNS. In MND, it is postulated that glutamate toxicity may accumulate to deleterious levels and contribute to motor neurone deterioration  It is associated with a prognostic benefit but is less effective than NIV."
