      "explanation": "Large artery acute ischaemic stroke? Consider mechanical clot retrieval\n\nMechanical thrombectomy\n\nis the correct choice. The clinical scenario describes a patient with an acute ischaemic stroke affecting a large artery, specifically the middle cerebral artery which is part of the anterior cerebral circulation. Mechanical thrombectomy, also known as mechanical clot retrieval, is indicated for occlusions in large arteries. The national institute for health and care excellence (NICE) recommends that mechanical thrombectomy be performed as soon as possible, ideally within 6 hours from symptom onset.\n\nAdmission to a rehabilitation programme\n\nis not the appropriate immediate step in acute stroke management. While rehabilitation is crucial in the post-acute phase of recovery, it does not represent an urgent intervention. As this patient falls within the therapeutic window for mechanical thrombectomy, this procedure should take priority over rehabilitation at this stage.\n\nAnticoagulation with rivaroxaban\n\nis inappropriate in this situation. The patient's ECG demonstrates normal sinus rhythm; anticoagulation is typically reserved for patients who present with atrial fibrillation on ECG. Moreover, anticoagulation initiation generally takes place no sooner than 14 days following an ischaemic stroke event.\n\nClopidogrel\n\nis not suitable as the next step in acute stroke management because it is primarily used for secondary prevention, not for acute treatment. According to the 2023 national clinical guidelines, clopidogrel is typically reserved for cases where aspirin is contraindicated and dual antiplatelet therapy is not recommended for major ischemic strokes due to the risk of hemorrhagic transformation. Since this patient has already received aspirin and is within the acute phase, mechanical thrombectomy is the appropriate intervention.\n\nIntravenous thrombolysis with streptokinase\n\nis not appropriate for this patient. Current guidelines recommend thrombolysis with agents like alteplase or tenecteplase rather than streptokinase. According to the national clinical guidelines for stroke issued in 2023, thrombolysis is indicated for acute ischaemic stroke within a 4.5 to 9-hour window from symptom onset, provided imaging shows salvageable brain tissue. Since streptokinase is not recommended for this purpose and the guidelines favour other thrombolytic agents, it is not suitable for managing this patient's acute ischaemic stroke."
