      "id": 498,
      "question": "A 62-year-old man with a history of hypertension and epilepsy is noted to have gingival hyperplasia on examination in the cardiology clinic. Which one of the following drugs is most likely to be responsible?",
      "options": [
        "Sodium valproate",
        "Lisinopril",
        "Atorvastatin",
        "Nifedipine",
        "Carbamazepine"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Lisinopril",
      "explanation": "Gingival hyperplasia: phenytoin, ciclosporin, calcium channel blockers and AML\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nNifedipine\n\n. Nifedipine is a calcium channel blocker used in the treatment of hypertension. One of its well-documented side effects is gingival hyperplasia, which occurs due to an overgrowth of the gum tissue. The exact mechanism of this side effect is not fully understood but it's suggested that nifedipine may alter collagen metabolism, thereby leading to an accumulation of extracellular matrix and resultant gingival overgrowth.\n\nSodium valproate\n\nis an antiepileptic drug used for the management of seizures. Its common side effects include gastrointestinal disturbances, tremor, weight gain and hair loss. Gingival hyperplasia is not typically associated with sodium valproate use.\n\nLisinopril\n\nis an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor used in the management of hypertension and heart failure. Common side effects are cough, dizziness, rash and renal impairment. It does not commonly cause gingival hyperplasia.\n\nAtorvastatin\n\nbelongs to the statin group of drugs and is primarily used for lowering cholesterol levels in patients at high risk of cardiovascular disease. While it can cause various side effects such as myalgia, hepatic impairment or even rhabdomyolysis in severe cases, gingival hyperplasia isn't a known complication associated with atorvastatin use.\n\nFinally,\n\nCarbamazepine\n\n, another antiepileptic drug like sodium valproate, has several potential adverse effects including dizziness, ataxia and blood dyscrasias but it doesn't typically result in gingival hyperplasia either."
