      "explanation": "Brown-Sequard syndrome is a result of lateral hemisection of the spinal cord\n\nThe patient has Brown-Sequard syndrome, which is a lateral hemisection of spinal cord. It causes same sided weakness and proprioception/vibration loss and loss of pain/temperature on the opposite side to the hemisection. This is because the pathway for pain/temperature sensation decussates at the level of the nerve root.\n\nDamage to the sciatic nerve would produce unilateral symptoms. Anterior hemisection is rare and unlikely given the mechanism of injury. It would also produce a different distribution of neurological symptoms. A left-sided hemisection would cause ipsilateral weakness. A posterior disk prolapse is also unlikely given the mechanism of trauma."
