      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Reduces episcleral venous pressure",
      "explanation": "Beta blockers such as timolol work in primary open-angle glaucoma by reducing aqueous production\n\nDecreases aqueous humour production\n\n: Timolol is a non-selective beta-blocker that acts by inhibiting beta-adrenergic receptors in the ciliary body, thereby reducing the production of aqueous humour. This leads to a reduction in intraocular pressure, which is key in managing primary open-angle glaucoma and preventing further optic nerve damage. This mechanism is well established and forms the basis for its use as an adjunctive treatment when first-line therapies are insufficient or not tolerated.\n\nIncreases uveoscleral outflow\n\n: This is the principal mechanism of prostaglandin analogues such as latanoprost, not beta-blockers like timolol. Prostaglandin analogues enhance alternative pathways for aqueous drainage but do not significantly affect its production.\n\nEnhances trabecular meshwork drainage\n\n: While improving trabecular meshwork function would lower intraocular pressure, this effect is more associated with interventions like laser trabeculoplasty or miotic agents (e.g., pilocarpine). Timolol does not act via this pathway.\n\nInduces miosis to improve outflow\n\n: Miotics such as pilocarpine work by causing pupillary constriction (miosis), which increases the angle between the iris and cornea to facilitate aqueous outflow through the trabecular meshwork. Timolol does not cause miosis nor act primarily on pupil size.\n\nReduces episcleral venous pressure\n\n: Lowering episcleral venous pressure could theoretically reduce intraocular pressure but is not a recognised action of any standard anti-glaucoma medication, including timolol. The mainstay remains modulation of aqueous dynamics rather than venous pressures."
