      "explanation": "Macular degeneration - smoking is risk factor\n\nIncreased oxidative stress\n\nis the correct answer. The progressive bilateral central visual distortion and history of dry age-related macular degeneration (AMD) point towards a progression of her disease. Smoking is one of the most important risk factors in the development of AMD, and is mediated via direct oxidation. Tobacco smoke introduces free radicals that directly damage retinal pigment epithelium (RPE), which accelerates drusen formation and RPE dysfunction. Oxidative stress is responsible for many health consequences associated with smoking, including cardiovascular disease, increased risk of neoplasm and smoking-related lung damage.\n\nImpaired retinal perfusion\n\nis incorrect. Although smoking can affect vascular health, impaired retinal perfusion is more typical of vascular occlusions or diabetic retinopathy. Perfusion is usually preserved in dry AMD until late stages. Additionally, the gradual bilateral central distortion points to degenerative rather than ischaemic pathology.\n\nIncreased lutein levels\n\nis incorrect. Lutein is a protective macular pigment with antioxidant properties. Therefore, increased lutein will be protective against pathologies such as macular degeneration. Smoking reduces, not increases, lutein levels.\n\nIncreased retinal vascular resistance\n\nis incorrect. This is more relevant to hypertensive or arteriolar retinal disease, and tends to result in asymmetrical, patchy changes to peripheral vision or retinal oedema. Therefore, this does not explain the slowly progressive central vision loss.\n\nInterference with macular photopigment regeneration\n\nis incorrect. This would be in keeping with vitamin A deficiency or inherited retinal dystrophies. Smoking is not a culprit in altering the visual cycle, but rather causes disruption of oxidative and inflammatory pathways."
