      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Epiretinal membrane formation",
      "explanation": "A decrease in night vision is a potential complication of panretinal photocoagulation\n\nThe patient’s fluctuating visual changes, following PRP, can indicate reduced night vision, a complication caused by\n\nreduced rod cell function\n\n. Rod cells, concentrated in the peripheral retina, are crucial for adapting to low-light conditions. PRP, designed to treat proliferative diabetic retinopathy by ablating hypoxic peripheral retinal tissue, inadvertently damages rod cells, impairing the retina's ability to adjust to varying light levels. His normal visual acuity during examination is likely due to the well-lit environment, which relies on cone cell function rather than rods. The peripheral vision changes further support PRP-induced damage.\n\nEpiretinal membranes\n\n(ERMs) typically cause central visual blurring or distortion, rather than fluctuating vision or reduced night vision. ERMs form at the macula, far from the peripheral retinal tissue targeted by PRP. This patient's fluctuating vision and normal visual acuity in a well-lit clinic argue against ERM-related findings, making it an unlikely diagnosis.\n\nFoveal ischaemia\n\nmanifests with central scotomas or blurred central vision and does not cause fluctuating vision or reduced night vision. PRP targets the peripheral retina and spares the macula, making foveal ischaemia improbable in this case. The patient’s stable visual acuity during examination further excludes this as a cause, as the fovea remains functional.\n\nNeovascularisation\n\ntypically presents with symptoms of recurrent proliferative diabetic retinopathy, such as vitreous haemorrhage or increased intraocular pressure. PRP aims to prevent this complication, and the patient’s examination findings of normal intraocular pressure and stable visual acuity suggest neovascularisation is unlikely to explain the symptoms.\n\nDiabetic macular oedema\n\nprimarily causes central vision distortion, not fluctuating vision or reduced night vision. The absence of central vision issues or macular involvement, in this case, makes macular oedema progression unlikely. Additionally, the patient’s normal visual acuity in the clinic supports the exclusion of this diagnosis, as the macula is not significantly affected."
