      "id": 65,
      "question": "Which one of the following is associated with the Holmes-Adie pupil?",
      "options": [
        "Decreased ankle reflexes",
        "Pupillary constriction",
        "Ptosis in 10-20% of cases",
        "An increased of developing multiple sclerosis",
        "Neurosyphilis"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Ptosis in 10-20% of cases",
      "explanation": "Holmes ADIe = DIlated pupil, females, absent leg reflexes\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nDecreased ankle reflexes\n\n. Holmes-Adie syndrome (also known as Adie's tonic pupil or Holmes-Adie pupil) is a neurological disorder characterised by a unilateral (occasionally bilateral) dilated pupil that reacts slowly to light but shows a more definite response to accommodation. This condition is typically associated with decreased or absent deep tendon reflexes, particularly the ankle reflexes. This association is known as Holmes-Adie syndrome and occurs due to damage to the ciliary ganglion and dorsal root ganglia. The pathophysiology involves denervation of the pupillary sphincter and subsequent aberrant reinnervation, leading to the characteristic tonic pupil. The decreased ankle reflexes occur in approximately 80-90% of patients with Holmes-Adie pupil and represent a key diagnostic feature of the syndrome.\n\nPupillary constriction\n\nis incorrect because Holmes-Adie pupil is characterised by pupillary dilation (mydriasis), not constriction. The affected pupil is typically larger than the unaffected pupil and demonstrates a delayed and tonic (slow) constriction to light. After prolonged illumination, the pupil may eventually constrict, but this response is significantly delayed compared to a normal pupil. The pupil also shows denervation hypersensitivity to dilute cholinergic agents like pilocarpine (0.1%), which can be used as a diagnostic test.\n\nPtosis in 10-20% of cases\n\nis incorrect because ptosis is not a typical feature of Holmes-Adie syndrome. Ptosis (drooping of the upper eyelid) is more commonly associated with other neurological conditions affecting the oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve III) or sympathetic innervation to the eye, such as Horner's syndrome. In Holmes-Adie syndrome, the pupillary abnormalities occur without ptosis because the levator palpebrae superioris muscle, which raises the eyelid, is not affected by the pathological process that damages the ciliary ganglion.\n\nAn increased risk of developing multiple sclerosis\n\nis incorrect because Holmes-Adie syndrome is not associated with an increased risk of developing multiple sclerosis (MS). Holmes-Adie syndrome is considered a benign condition that is often idiopathic or may follow a viral illness. It does not predispose individuals to demyelinating disorders like MS. The conditions are distinct entities with different pathophysiological mechanisms. While MS can cause pupillary abnormalities, these typically present as part of a constellation of neurological symptoms and signs, unlike the isolated findings in Holmes-Adie syndrome.\n\nNeurosyphilis\n\nis incorrect because Holmes-Adie syndrome is not associated with neurosyphilis. While neurosyphilis can cause pupillary abnormalities, most notably the Argyll Robertson pupil (which constricts with accommodation but not to light), this is distinct from the Holmes-Adie pupil. The Argyll Robertson pupil is typically bilateral, small, and irregular, whereas the Holmes-Adie pupil is often unilateral, dilated, and regular. Furthermore, neurosyphilis is caused by infection with the spirochete *Treponema pallidum*, whereas Holmes-Adie syndrome is thought to result from a viral or autoimmune process affecting the ciliary ganglion and dorsal root ganglia."
