      "explanation": "Scleritis is painful, episcleritis is not painful\n\nEpiscleritis\n\nis the correct answer as it typically presents with sectoral redness, mild discomfort, and no significant pain or visual disturbance. It is commonly associated with systemic inflammatory conditions such as rheumatoid arthritis. The absence of severe pain or photophobia helps distinguish it from more serious ocular involvement.\n\nScleritis\n\nusually causes severe, boring eye pain that may radiate to surrounding facial structures and can be associated with visual impairment. The scenario specifically notes only mild discomfort without significant pain, making scleritis less likely despite the background of rheumatoid arthritis.\n\nKeratoconjunctivitis sicca\n\n, also known as dry eye syndrome, is very common in patients with rheumatoid arthritis but usually presents with gritty sensation, dryness, and sometimes mild conjunctival injection rather than focal scleral redness or discomfort described here.\n\nAnterior uveitis\n\noften presents with photophobia, blurred vision, and a painful red eye; examination typically reveals ciliary injection rather than sectoral scleral erythema. The patient’s lack of photophobia and normal vision makes this unlikely.\n\nConjunctivitis\n\ngenerally causes diffuse conjunctival redness (not just sectoral), discharge (absent here), irritation or itchiness rather than focal scleral erythema and discomfort associated with episcleritis."
