      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Midazolam",
      "explanation": "Hyoscine hydrobromide or hyoscine butylbromide is generally used first-line to manage secretions in a palliative care setting\n\nHyoscine butylbromide\n\n(Buscopan) is an antimuscarinic normally used first-line for upper airway secretions in palliative care. It can be given orally or subcutaneously either as required or as a continuous infusion.\n\nGlycopyrronium bromide\n\nis an alternative antimuscarinic for excessive secretions in palliative care but is not normally used first-line. However, It is considered first-line for sialorrhoea (excessive drooling) in, for example, patients with neurodegenerative disorders.\n\nLevomepromazine\n\nis indicated for either nausea and vomiting or terminal agitation in palliative care. It is not indicated for upper airway secretions and there is no indication that this patient has either nausea or agitation.\n\nMetoclopramide\n\nis used for nausea and vomiting, bowel obstruction or hiccups in palliative care. It has no role in the management of secretions and there is no indication that this patient has nausea, bowel obstruction or hiccups.\n\nMidazolam\n\nis used as an anxiolytic or sedative for agitation or breathlessness in palliative care. It has no role in managing secretions and there is no indication in this scenario that the patient is agitated or breathless."
