      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Chlorhexidine mouthwash",
      "explanation": "Benzydamine hydrochloride mouthwash or spray may be useful in reducing the discomfort associated with a painful mouth that may occur at the end of life\n\nFrom the options above, the best answer is benzydamine mouthwash. This, or a spray formulation, may be useful in reducing the discomfort and pain in the mouth that can often occur towards the end of life. Benzydamine is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug which acts topically.\n\nChlorhexidine mouthwash may be useful to treat secondary infections or when pain is limiting other mouth care methods such as toothbrushing. It is not known to reduce mouth pain significantly.\n\nOral glycopyrronium is often used at the end of life to reduce excessive drooling and secretions. It does not play a role in the alleviation of oral pain.\n\nSoluble aspirin, used as a mouthwash, provides no topical relief from pain and so is not recommended. If benzydamine mouthwash is not sufficient, systemic analgesia may be needed.\n\nTransdermal hyoscine is another medication used to reduce oral secretions at the end of life; it does not alleviate oral pain."
