      "explanation": "Breakthrough dose = 1/6th of daily morphine dose\n\nPalliative care patients can require increasing doses of opioids for pain management as tolerance increase. In general, modified-release opioids should be used and the total required dose split into two separate doses over a 24-hour period. Additional breakthrough analgesia should also be provided throughout the day as required at a lower dose.\n\nThe patient's current total morphine requirement is 72mg (4x 18mg); the patient should therefore receive 36mg of modified-release morphine twice a day.\n\nBreakthrough does for increased pain is then set at one-sixth the total daily dose and should be given as required. The patient's current morphine breakthrough dose is 72mg divided by 6 = 12mg.\n\nMorphine modified-release should be suitable to cover the patients for a period of 12 hours and so a twice-daily dose should be used, not four times daily. These twice-daily doses can then be adjusted based on how much breakthrough morphine is required over a 24-hour period."
