      "explanation": "Metastatic bone pain may respond to analgesia, bisphosphonates or radiotherapy\n\nBisphosphonate are is generally used as a treatment when analgesic medications such as opioids are no longer effective in the treatment of bone cancer pain. It helps to cure the hypercalcaemic states in the body. Intravenous pamidronate and zoledronic acid have shown an analgesic effect in studies. Reduced bone pain is likely to be seen within 4-weeks of treatment.\n\nDexamethasone is a systemic corticosteroid indicated particularly if there is evidence to suggest metastatic spinal cord compression, which is unlikely based on the history provided.\n\nSurgery is very rarely considered an option to treat bone pain from metastatic lesions. This trend is less popular as various pharmacological drugs over the years have successfully achieved adequate pain control.\n\nLidocaine patch can be used as topical analgesics to help target localised back pain. In a situation such as this with diffuse metastatic back pain, this is unlikely to provide effective long-term relief.\n\nRadiotherapy can be delivered to the area of the bone affected by cancer. It can take up to 6-weeks before you feel the full effect of radiotherapy, meaning that bisphosphonates work quicker. Radiotherapy is a highly effective palliative treatment to control pain due to secondary bone disease from a wide range of cancers such as prostate, breast, and lung. Therefore this would not be suitable in the current situation given the acute back pain which the patient is experiencing."
