      "explanation": "Buprenorphine or fentanyl are the opioids of choice for pain relief in palliative care patients with severe renal impairment, as they are not renally excreted and therefore are less likely to cause toxicity than morphine\n\nParacetamol alone is clearly insufficient in the management of this patient's cancer pain. As such, a suitable opioid should be commenced. Ordinarily, morphine would be the first-line for palliative patients. In the context of moderate renal impairment, oxycodone is more appropriate. However, with regard to her end-stage renal failure, the only suitable option here is\n\nbuprenorphine\n\n. This is administered transdermally and, importantly, does not accumulate in renal impairment.\n\nCodeine\n\nis a weak opioid. Several of its active metabolites are renally excreted and so codeine should be avoided in patients with stage 4 or 5 chronic kidney disease.\n\nDiamorphine\n\nis a highly potent opioid sometimes used for severe pain. However, it is ultimately renally excreted and so should be avoided in stage 4 or 5 chronic kidney disease.\n\nDihydrocodeine\n\nis a weak opioid, similar to codeine. It is also renally excreted and should be avoided in patients with stage 4 or 5 chronic kidney disease.\n\nMorphine\n\nis generally a first-line agent for moderate to severe pain, particularly in a palliative context. However, it is renally excreted and so should not be used in patients with stage 4 or 5 chronic kidney disease."
