      "correctText": "Increase the morphine dose by 10-30%",
      "explanation": "In palliative patients increase morphine doses by 30-50% if pain not controlled\n\nThe 2012 NICE guidelines regarding the use of opioids for palliative care suggests if a patient has no other comorbidities, they should be initiated on 20-30mg of modified-release morphine per day, split between two doses with additional morphine doses of 5mg available as required. Ideally, if a palliative patient continues to have pain, their regular dose of morphine should be adjusted based on the amount of breakthrough morphine required, however, if this is not possible/breakthrough morphine has not been used then guidelines suggest the regular dose of morphine is\n\nincreased by 30-50%\n\n.\n\nChanging the form of opioid in palliative care patients is an option to consider, particularly if the patient is having specific side effects associated with morphine or they have other comorbidities altering the effect of morphine (i.e. oxycodone is preferred in renal patients). However, for this patient, it is not the form of opioid that is the issue it is the dose. As the dose of morphine for adequate pain relief is not yet established\n\nchange to a different form of opioid\n\nis not the best option. Instead, the dose of morphine should be increased, then changing to a different form of opioid can be considered at a later date if required, using a suitable, calculated equivalent dose.\n\nDividing the morphine into four doses a day\n\nis not a suitable solution as the total amount of morphine will remain under-dosed/too low. Modified-release morphine should be adequate to cover a palliative patient's pain throughout the day with two doses only. If the patient's pain is still not controlled then their dose should be increased.\n\nIncreasing the morphine dose by 10-30%\n\nwould be too small an increase for a palliative care patient. A small increase of this amount would result in a strong risk that the patient would remain in pain and therefore is not acceptable.\n\nIncreasing the morphine dose by 70-90%\n\nconversely would be too high an increase and would result in a risk of increased side effects including respiratory depression."
