      "explanation": "Severe depression can mimic dementia but gives a pattern of global memory loss rather than short-term memory loss - this is called pseudodementia\n\nPseudodementia due to depression\n\nis correct. The patient has a short history of memory problems with low mood, poor appetite, and sleep disturbance. Her tearfulness and concern about dementia show preserved insight, typical of pseudodementia. The MMSE score of 25/30 indicates mild impairment, and her emotional response supports depression. Cognitive symptoms related to depression can improve with treatment, though not always quickly or fully. Her global memory loss and vegetative symptoms align more with depression than dementia.\n\nAlzheimer’s disease\n\nis incorrect because it presents with gradual memory decline over months to years, not a sudden onset. Though many Alzheimer’s patients lack insight, some retain partial awareness early on. This patient’s preserved insight and emotional distress suggest depression. Alzheimer’s usually starts, specifically with short-term memory loss, unlike this patient’s global memory issues.\n\nFrontotemporal dementia\n\nis incorrect because it often affects younger adults, but can occur in older patients. It presents mainly with personality, behaviour, or language changes. This patient has no behavioural issues or language problems. Her preserved insight and emotional distress argue against frontotemporal dementia, where lack of awareness and behavioural changes predominate.\n\nLewy body dementia\n\nis incorrect because it typically shows fluctuating cognition, visual hallucinations, and Parkinsonism, absent here. The consistent low mood and cognitive complaints favour depression-related pseudodementia.\n\nNormal pressure hydrocephalus\n\nis incorrect because it presents with cognitive decline, gait disturbance, and urinary incontinence. This patient lacks gait or bladder symptoms. While cognitive decline in normal pressure hydrocephalus can mimic other dementias or depression, her rapid-onset mood and cognitive symptoms with preserved insight support pseudodementia."
