      "correctText": "Dissociative disorder",
      "explanation": "Unexplained symptoms\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nHypochondrial disorder\n\n. This condition, also known as Illness Anxiety Disorder in the DSM-5, is characterised by a preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious illness. Somatic symptoms are not present or, if they do occur, they are only mild in intensity. If a medical condition is present or there is high risk for developing a medical condition (e.g., strong family history of a disease), the preoccupation is clearly excessive or disproportionate. The patient has performed excessive health-related behaviours (e.g., repeatedly checking his body for signs of illness) or exhibits maladaptive avoidance (e.g., avoiding doctor appointments and hospitals). The illness preoccupation has been present for at least six months but the specific illness feared may change over that period of time.\n\nConversion disorder\n\n, also known as Functional Neurological Symptom Disorder, involves symptoms affecting voluntary motor or sensory function suggesting neurological conditions. However, these symptoms cannot be explained by any neurological or medical condition and are not the result of substance use or better explained by another mental disorder. In this case scenario, the patient does not display any such symptoms hence it's incorrect.\n\nMunchausen's syndrome\n\nnow referred to as Factitious Disorder Imposed on Self, involves individuals deliberately producing or feigning physical or psychological symptoms with no obvious gain other than assuming the sick role and receiving attention from healthcare professionals. These patients often have extensive knowledge about medical terminology and diseases which they use to deceive healthcare providers into unnecessary investigations and interventions. The given scenario does not provide information to suggest this diagnosis.\n\nA\n\nDissociative disorder\n\nrefers to conditions involving disruptions or breakdowns of memory, awareness, identity, perception etc., potentially leading to serious problems in an individual's everyday life. They are thought to primarily be caused by psychological trauma rather than organic pathology within the brain. The presented case does not exhibit such features.\n\nFinally,\n\nSomatisation disorder\n\nnow referred to as Somatic Symptom Disorder in DSM-5 includes one or more somatic symptoms that are distressing and/or result in significant disruption in daily life associated with excessive thoughts, feelings and behaviours related to these somatic symptoms. It differs from Hypochondriasis where the concern is more about having a disease rather than focusing on specific symptom(s). Thus this option too doesn't fit our case scenario."
