      "explanation": "Bulimia may result in erosion of teeth\n\nErosion of teeth\n\nis correct. This physical finding supports bulimia nervosa. Even when the patient does not report purging, enamel erosion, especially on the upper incisors, strongly suggests repeated exposure of teeth to gastric acid. It provides objective evidence supporting the diagnosis in the context of body image preoccupation, distress around eating, and subtle behavioural hints.\n\nAngular cheilitis\n\nis incorrect. This condition causes painful cracks at the corners of the mouth, usually due to fungal infection or nutritional deficiencies. It is neither common nor specific to bulimia nervosa and does not strongly support the diagnosis in a patient with a healthy BMI.\n\nDental caries\n\nis incorrect. Tooth decay can occur from acid exposure, but it is common in the general population and less specific than enamel erosion. The presence of caries alone does not strongly indicate bulimia nervosa.\n\nGingivitis\n\nis incorrect. Gingivitis is inflammation of the gums, usually caused by plaque accumulation or poor oral hygiene. It is not a distinguishing feature of bulimia nervosa and can be present in anyone with suboptimal oral care.\n\nGlossitis\n\nis incorrect. Inflammation of the tongue is usually related to deficiencies in iron, folate, or vitamin B12. It is more typical of anorexia nervosa or severe malnutrition and is not a specific finding for bulimia nervosa."
