      "explanation": "Gastrointestinal side-effects such as diarrhoea are seen in SSRI discontinuation syndrome\n\nThe correct answer is vomiting. Stopping a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) suddenly can lead to SSRI discontinuation syndrome. The most common symptom is gastrointestinal upset. Therefore, SSRIs should be gradually tapered instead.\n\nA blunted affect is unlikely. This is usually a side effect of taking antidepressants themselves rather than an effect of their cessation. The opposite may, in fact, occur upon stopping the medication - labile mood and swings.\n\nHypertension is unlikely - there have been a few isolated case reports of this with SSRI discontinuation, but gastrointestinal symptoms are far more likely.\n\nWeight gain, again, is unlikely - more commonly, weight loss is reported upon cessation.\n\nXanthopsia (yellow-tinted vision) is not known to occur with SSRIs. It may occur as a result of digoxin use."
