      "explanation": "When stopping a SSRI the dose should be gradually reduced over a 4 week period\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nWithdraw gradually over the next 4 weeks\n\n. This is because citalopram, like other selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs), can cause withdrawal symptoms if stopped abruptly. These symptoms include dizziness, sleep disturbances, anxiety and mood changes. The UK National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) guidelines recommend that antidepressants should be withdrawn over a period of at least four weeks to minimise the risk of withdrawal symptoms.\n\nCan be stopped immediately\n\nis not recommended as it increases the risk of withdrawal symptoms. This applies to most SSRIs including citalopram.\n\nWithdraw gradually over the next 3 days\n\nand\n\nwithdraw gradually over the next week\n\nare both too short periods for safe discontinuation. They would likely result in similar problems as immediate cessation due to inadequate time for physiological adjustment.\n\nWithdraw gradually over the next 2 weeks\n\n, although better than the previous options, is still less than the recommended minimum duration by NICE guidelines which suggests a gradual reduction over at least four weeks. Therefore, it may still lead to significant withdrawal symptoms in some patients."
