      "explanation": "Antidepressants should be continued for at least 6 months after remission of symptoms to decrease risk of relapse\n\nThe correct answer is that the fluoxetine\n\nshould be continued for at least another 6 months\n\n. According to NICE guidelines, after achieving remission from a depressive episode, antidepressant treatment should be continued for at least 6 months from the point of remission. This duration has been shown to significantly reduce the risk of relapse. The continuation phase of treatment is crucial even when patients feel well, as premature discontinuation is associated with a higher risk of recurrence.\n\nStopping straight away\n\nwould be inappropriate and potentially dangerous. Abrupt discontinuation of SSRIs like fluoxetine can lead to discontinuation symptoms and significantly increases the risk of relapse. Even when discontinuing after an appropriate treatment duration, gradual tapering is recommended.\n\nContinuing for\n\nanother 6 weeks\n\nwould be insufficient. This duration would not provide adequate protection against relapse, as the total treatment duration would only be about 3.5 months, which is too short according to evidence-based guidelines.\n\nContinuing for\n\nanother 3 months\n\nwould also be insufficient. While this would provide approximately 5 months of total treatment, it still falls short of the recommended minimum 6-month continuation phase after achieving remission.\n\nContinuing for\n\nanother 12 months\n\nwould be longer than necessary for a first episode of depression without complicating factors. While some patients with recurrent depression or other risk factors might benefit from longer treatment, 12 additional months would be excessive for a standard first episode of depression that has responded well to treatment."
