      "id": 46,
      "question": "A 32-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with severe back pain following a minor road traffic accident. He reports being unable to walk due to the pain, but examination reveals inconsistent findings. The patient mentions he is pursuing an insurance claim. During observation, he is seen walking normally when he thinks no one is watching. His medical records show multiple similar presentations after minor accidents.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
      "options": [
        "Conversion disorder",
        "Factitious disorder",
        "Illness anxiety disorder",
        "Malingering",
        "Somatisation disorder"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 3,
      "correctText": "Malingering",
      "explanation": "Lying or exaggerating for financial gain is malingering, for example someone who fakes whiplash after a road traffic accident for an insurance payment\n\nMalingering\n\nis the most likely diagnosis in this case. The patient is presenting with symptoms (severe back pain and inability to walk) that are inconsistent with the physical examination findings. The key feature suggesting malingering is that he was observed walking normally when he thought he wasn't being watched, indicating conscious symptom production. Additionally, the context is highly suggestive - he explicitly mentions pursuing an insurance claim, indicating a clear external incentive for his symptoms. His history of multiple similar presentations after minor accidents further supports this pattern of behaviour. Malingering is defined as the intentional production or exaggeration of physical or psychological symptoms motivated by external incentives such as financial compensation, avoiding work, obtaining medications, or evading legal responsibilities. Unlike other conditions where symptoms are not under conscious control, in malingering there is deliberate feigning of symptoms for personal gain.\n\nConversion disorder\n\n(functional neurological disorder) involves neurological symptoms such as weakness, paralysis, or sensory disturbances that cannot be explained by medical conditions. While this could potentially explain the patient's reported inability to walk, conversion disorder is not associated with conscious symptom production for external gain. In conversion disorder, patients genuinely experience their symptoms and do not typically show such clear evidence of deception as walking normally when unobserved. The symptoms arise from psychological factors but are not intentionally produced.\n\nFactitious disorder\n\n(Munchausen syndrome) involves the deliberate production or feigning of physical or psychological symptoms. While there is intentional symptom falsification as in malingering, the key difference is motivation. In factitious disorder, the motivation is to assume the sick role and receive attention and care from medical professionals, not external rewards like financial compensation. The absence of this psychological need for medical attention and the clear presence of external incentives (insurance claim) make factitious disorder less likely.\n\nIllness anxiety disorder\n\n(hypochondriasis) involves preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious illness despite minimal or absent symptoms. These patients genuinely believe they are ill and experience significant anxiety about their health. This patient's presentation does not suggest anxiety about illness but rather deliberate symptom exaggeration with clear external motivation, making illness anxiety disorder an unlikely diagnosis.\n\nSomatisation disorder\n\ninvolves multiple physical complaints across different organ systems over several years that cannot be fully explained by medical conditions. Patients with somatisation disorder genuinely experience their symptoms and are not consciously producing them for gain. The acute nature of this presentation following a specific event (accident) with clear external incentives makes somatisation disorder unlikely."
