      "correctIndex": 1,
      "correctText": "Hyperthyroidism",
      "explanation": "Hyperprolactinaemia is uncommon with certain atypical antipsychotics - consider other causes such as prolactinoma if prolactin raised\n\nThis patient has presented with symptoms of headache and fatigue, which are recognised as common side effects associated with her use of aripiprazole. Additionally, she reports irregular menstruation, potentially attributable to her significantly elevated prolactin level of 2234 ng/mL. Such an increase is atypically high for aripiprazole-induced hyperprolactinemia. Among atypical antipsychotics, aripiprazole is noted for its favourable side effect profile, particularly in terms of causing less hyperprolactinemia. In this context, it is prudent to first investigate alternative and more likely aetiologies, such as the presence of a\n\nmacroprolactinoma\n\n. The headaches reported by the patient could also be linked to an enlarging pituitary mass exerting pressure on the dura.\n\nAlthough hyperprolactinemia is a recognised\n\nadverse drug reaction\n\nto aripiprazole, it occurs infrequently when compared with other atypical antipsychotics such as olanzapine. The pronounced elevation in prolactin observed here suggests that other causes should be considered, including the possibility of a microprolactinoma.\n\nHypothyroidism\n\ncan manifest with fatigue and menstrual irregularities; nevertheless, her blood tests indicate an elevated TSH concurrent with normal T4 levels—findings indicative of subclinical hypothyroidism—which would not typically produce these symptoms.\n\nHyperthyroidism\n\nmay present with disruption to menstruation but given the patient's thyroid function tests suggest a subclinical hypothyroidism (elevated TSH with normal T4), we can discount this option.\n\nA\n\nnon-functioning pituitary adenoma\n\nmight induce a modest increase in prolactin due to compression of the pituitary stalk. However, an elevation to 2234 ng/mL as seen in this case would be unusually high. A non-functioning pituitary adenoma may also present with features such as headache and visual disturbances due to dural irritation and compression of the optic chiasm respectively. Compression of the normally functioning pituitary gland tissue by the tumour can lead to panhypopituitarism."
