      "explanation": "Duloxetine mechanism of action = serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor\n\nDuloxetine\n\nis the correct answer. Duloxetine is a serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor. Sertraline and citalopram are both selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs), and duloxetine is the only drug listed above that is not an SSRI. Duloxetine is licensed for depression and anxiety disorders, diabetic neuropathy and stress incontinence.\n\nEscitalopram\n\nis the incorrect answer as like sertraline and citalopram, it is an SSRI. Escitalopram is an active enantiomer of citalopram and is licensed for depression, anxiety disorders and obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD).\n\nFluoxetine\n\nis the incorrect answer as like sertraline and citalopram, it is an SSRI. Fluoxetine is licenced for treating depression, bulimia, obsessive-compulsive disorder, and vasomotor menopausal symptoms.\n\nFluvoxamine\n\nis the incorrect answer as like sertraline and citalopram, it is an SSRI. Fluvoxamine is licensed for depression and OCD but is rarely used in the UK.\n\nParoxetine\n\nis the incorrect answer as like sertraline and citalopram, it is an SSRI. Paroxetine is licensed for depression and anxiety disorders, OCD and vasomotor menopausal symptoms. However, it has a higher incidence of discontinuation symptoms than other SSRIs."
