      "id": 56,
      "question": "A 52-year-old woman has had 9 months of urinary incontinence, occurring only with laughing, sneezing, or exercise. She has had three previous vaginal deliveries. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. Urine dipstick and culture are negative. The patient has tried pelvic floor exercises with minimal improvement and now wishes to try medication.\n\nWhich option best describes the mechanism of action of the drug she is most likely to be offered?",
      "options": [
        "Activation of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors",
        "Activation of β3-adrenoceptors",
        "Inhibition of dopamine reuptake",
        "Inhibition of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors",
        "Inhibition of serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Inhibition of dopamine reuptake",
      "explanation": "Duloxetine mechanism of action = serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor\n\nInhibition of serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake\n\nis correct. The most likely diagnosis is stress urinary incontinence, characterised by urine leakage during increases in intra-abdominal pressure, such as laughing, sneezing, or exercise, in the absence of urgency or infection. Duloxetine, a serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor, increases synaptic levels in the central nervous system and at presynaptic neurones of the pudendal nerve. This enhances activation of the urethral sphincter, improving urethral closure during stress events. Given the patient’s persistent symptoms despite pelvic floor exercises, duloxetine is an appropriate pharmacological option.\n\nActivation of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors\n\nis incorrect because muscarinic agonists stimulate detrusor muscle contraction, which would worsen urinary leakage in stress urinary incontinence caused by urethral sphincter weakness. These agents, such as bethanechol, may sometimes be used in an underactive bladder or neurogenic bladder with retention, where bladder emptying is impaired. In this patient, with no voiding difficulty and stress-related leakage, muscarinic agonists would exacerbate symptoms and are therefore inappropriate.\n\nActivation of β3-adrenoceptors\n\nis incorrect because β3-adrenoceptor agonists, such as mirabegron, are used to treat urge urinary incontinence and overactive bladder. They relax the detrusor muscle during bladder filling, increasing bladder capacity. This patient does not report urgency or frequency, and her symptoms occur solely during exertion, which is consistent with stress incontinence. Therefore, β3-adrenoceptor activation would not target the underlying mechanism of her condition.\n\nInhibition of dopamine reuptake\n\nis incorrect because dopamine pathways do not play a primary role in urethral sphincter control or stress urinary incontinence. Dopamine reuptake inhibitors, such as bupropion, are used for depression and smoking cessation, but they have no known efficacy in the treatment of urinary incontinence. This pharmacological mechanism is unrelated to the pathophysiology of stress-related leakage.\n\nInhibition of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors\n\nis incorrect because antimuscarinic drugs, such as oxybutynin, are used for urge incontinence or overactive bladder, not stress urinary incontinence. These drugs block muscarinic receptors to suppress involuntary bladder contractions. Since this patient experiences leakage only during physical stress and without urgency, antimuscarinic therapy would not address her symptoms and is not an appropriate treatment option."
