      "id": 66,
      "question": "A 42-year-old man with mental health issues is reviewed. The patient has experienced symptoms in keeping with severe depression for several months and he reports little improvement with cognitive behaviour therapy.\n\nThere has been a significant deterioration in his mood and admits to a failed overdose a few weeks ago and he reports still having suicidal thoughts occasionally. Given his condition it is recommended he commence an anti-depressant but one with a lower risk of toxicity in overdose. He has no other medical conditions and is not on any medications.\n\nWhat medication should be avoided?",
      "options": [
        "Amoxapine",
        "Desipramine",
        "Dosulepin",
        "Lofepramine",
        "Nortriptyline"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 0,
      "correctText": "Amoxapine",
      "explanation": "Dosulepin - avoid as dangerous in overdose\n\nDosulepin\n\n, a tricyclic antidepressant, was previously prescribed for managing depression and anxiety. However, due to it posing a significant risk in overdose, it is rarely used and not recommended particularly as a first-line agent or in patients with a high risk of overdose. In excessive amounts, dosulepin can lead to life-threatening complications, including cardiac arrhythmias, seizures, and respiratory depression. Its toxicity arises from the blockade of various receptors, such as histaminic, cholinergic, and adrenergic receptors. The narrow therapeutic index increases the likelihood of severe adverse effects even with a slight overdose.\n\nAmoxapine\n\nis a tetracyclic antidepressant used to treat depressive disorders. It possesses a unique chemical structure, combining tricyclic and neuroleptic properties. One notable aspect of amoxapine is its relatively safer drug profile in overdose compared to some other tricyclic antidepressants. In instances of overdose, amoxapine tends to produce milder toxicity symptoms. This is attributed to its metabolite, amoxapine-2-OH, which acts as a serotonin reuptake inhibitor, potentially mitigating the severity of adverse effects associated with overdose.\n\nDesipramine\n\nis a tricyclic antidepressant primarily used to treat depressive disorders. Desipramine has a relatively safer drug profile in overdose compared to some other tricyclic antidepressants attributed to its selective inhibition of norepinephrine reuptake, which leads to a milder side effect profile and reduced toxicity. Desipramine's lower affinity for receptors associated with adverse effects, such as anticholinergic and cardiovascular effects, contributes to its decreased risk of severe complications in overdose situations.\n\nLofepramine\n\n, a tricyclic antidepressant, is known for its relatively safer drug profile in overdose compared to some other tricyclic antidepressants. Its active metabolite, desipramine, is produced in smaller amounts, leading to reduced toxicity. Lofepramine's selective inhibition of norepinephrine reuptake, coupled with a lower affinity for receptors associated with adverse effects, contributes to its diminished risk of severe complications in overdose.\n\nNortriptyline\n\n, a tricyclic antidepressant, has a relatively safer drug profile in overdose when compared to other tricyclic antidepressants, primarily attributed to its selective inhibition of norepinephrine reuptake, leading to a more favourable side effect profile and reduced toxicity. Nortriptyline's lower affinity for receptors associated with adverse effects, such as anticholinergic and cardiovascular effects, contributes to its diminished risk of severe complications in overdose."
