      "id": 90,
      "question": "A 35-year-old woman attends her GP for review. She says that she feels nothing is wrong but has attended at the insistence of her husband. When questioned further, she believes that a famous football player is in love with her.\n\nShe has no other delusions, nor hallucinations, nor any other obvious thought disorder. She has no insight into her belief about this football player.\n\nWhat is this condition known as?",
      "options": [
        "Capgras syndrome",
        "Charles Bonnet syndrome",
        "Cotard's syndrome",
        "De Clerambault's syndrome",
        "Othello syndrome"
      ],
      "correctIndex": 4,
      "correctText": "Othello syndrome",
      "explanation": "Erotomania (De Clerambault's syndrome) is the presence of a delusion that a famous is in love with them, with the absence of other psychotic symptoms\n\nThe correct answer is De Clerambault's syndrome or erotomania. This is listed in DSM-5 as a type of delusional disorder, whereby the patient has a fixed belief that another individual, usually someone famous or of high social standing, is infatuated with them. Onset is often sudden and may last for several months. The condition is relatively rare and the evidence base for treatment is limited.\n\nCapgras syndrome is a delusion where a patient believes that a friend, spouse or other close family member has been replaced by an identical impostor. It most commonly occurs in association with schizophrenia but has also been reported in patients with brain injury.\n\nCharles Bonnet syndrome, also known as visual release hallucinations, is a type of visual disturbance and complex visual hallucinations in patients with partial or severe blindness. Shapes and grid-like patterns are often reported. Patients have full insight into the nature of the hallucinations.\n\nCotard's syndrome is the persistent belief that the patient is dead, does not exist, is putrefying or has in some way lost their internal organs. It is again incredibly rare and so the evidence base is very limited.\n\nOthello syndrome, named after Shakespeare's Othello, is a delusional disorder where the patient is preoccupied with the thought that their spouse is being unfaithful - without any real proof."
