      "correctText": "Inhibition of serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake",
      "explanation": "Duloxetine mechanism of action = serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor\n\nThis patient is experiencing stress incontinence, characterised by episodes of uncontrollable voiding when laughing or coughing. Pelvic floor muscle training is typically tried first, and surgical procedures may be offered. However, if a patient declines these interventions, duloxetine may be offered. This\n\ninhibits serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake\n\n. It increases the synaptic concentration of serotonin and noradrenaline within the pudendal nerve, leading to increased stimulation of muscle tissue in the urethral sphincter.\n\nInhibition of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors\n\nis incorrect. An example is oxybutynin. This is instead used to manage urge incontinence, which would present with uncontrollable leakage of urine following a sudden unexpected urge, rather than following laughing or coughing.\n\nActivation of beta-3 receptors\n\nis incorrect - this refers to mirabegron. Like antimuscarinics, it is used for urge incontinence rather than stress incontinence.\n\nSelective inhibition of noradrenaline reuptake\n\nis incorrect. Examples of drugs using this mechanism include atomoxetine and reboxetine. These are not routinely used in the management of urinary incontinence and are primarily used as antidepressants.\n\nSelective inhibition of serotonin reuptake\n\nis incorrect. Examples of this class include fluoxetine and sertraline. These are not routinely used in the management of urinary incontinence."
