      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Binge eating disorder",
      "explanation": "Episodes of binge eating followed by intentional vomiting - bulimia nervosa\n\nBulimia nervosa\n\nis the correct answer. The combination of recurrent vomiting and secretive eating behaviour evidences this. Bulimia nervosa is characterised by recurrent episodes of binge eating followed by inappropriate compensatory behaviours, such as self-induced vomiting, to avoid weight gain. These episodes typically occur in secret due to feelings of shame or guilt.\n\nAnorexia nervosa\n\nis incorrect. This diagnosis involves significant food restriction and intense fear of gaining weight. Food packets would not be expected to be found, as there is often an associated fear of food and complete avoidance, rather than secretive behaviours.\n\nAvoidant-restrictive food intake disorder (ARFID)\n\nis incorrect. ARFID involves avoidance of food due to sensory characteristics, fear of choking, or a lack of interest in eating, without body image disturbance or fear of gaining weight. It does not involve binge eating or compensatory behaviours like vomiting. The girl in this scenario is consuming food in secret and vomiting, which are not features of ARFID. Therefore, this diagnosis does not fit the clinical picture.\n\nBinge eating disorder\n\nis incorrect. Recurrent episodes of binge eating characterise this diagnosis without compensatory behaviours such as vomiting, excessive exercise, or fasting. The presence of vomiting in this case excludes binge-eating disorder. Thus, the behaviours described are more consistent with bulimia nervosa.\n\nRumination disorder\n\nis incorrect. Rumination disorder involves the repeated regurgitation of food after eating, which may be re-chewed, re-swallowed, or spit out. It is typically observed in infants or individuals with developmental disorders, though it can rarely occur in adolescents. However, it is not associated with binge eating or hidden food, nor is it a voluntary behaviour aimed at weight control. The scenario points to deliberate vomiting after binge eating, which rules out rumination disorder."
