      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "Hyperkalaemia",
      "explanation": "Anorexia features\n\nParotidomegaly\n\nis correct. The patient presents with reduced oral intake and a very low body mass index along with lanugo hair. This suggests anorexia nervosa (AN) as the diagnosis. Parotidomegaly is a recognised feature of this condition.\n\nHigh follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)\n\nis incorrect. There is hypothalamic dysfunction in AN but this is characterized by hyposecretion of FSH rather than hypersecretion.\n\nHigh lutenising hormone (LH)\n\nis incorrect. Again, analogous to FSH, the LH level is low rather than high in AN.\n\nHyperkalaemia\n\nis incorrect. Most things are 'low' in AN and potassium follows this pattern. Hypokalaemia, rather than hyperkalemia, is a feature of AN.\n\nLow cortisol\n\nis incorrect. The cortisol level is high, rather than low, in AN. High cortisol is an adaptive mechanism to maintain euglycaemia in a state of low energy availability."
