      "explanation": "Olanzapine has a higher risk than other atypicals for dyslipidemia and obesity\n\nOlanzapine\n\nhas a higher risk than other atypical anti-psychotics for dyslipidaemia and is a first-line agent in the treatment of schizophrenia.\n\nAll atypical antipsychotics can increase the risk of dyslipidaemia and obesity, however, there are varying degrees of risk depending on the agent.\n\nAripiprazole\n\nis generally recognised as an atypical antipsychotic with a lower risk of causing dyslipidaemia.\n\nClozapine\n\n, one of the first atypical agents to be developed, does have a significant risk of dyslipidaemia. However, due to the significant risk of agranulocytosis, it is only used in patients resistant to other antipsychotic medication, not on the first presentation.\n\nQuetiapine\n\nhas a lower risk of dyslipidaemia when compared with clozapine and olanzapine, however is seen to have a higher risk than aripiprazole and risperidone.\n\nRisperidone\n\nis generally recognised as an atypical antipsychotic with a lower risk of causing dyslipidaemia."
