      "correctIndex": 2,
      "correctText": "D1",
      "explanation": "Haloperidol - dopamine D2 receptor antagonist, blocking dopaminergic transmission in the mesolimbic pathways\n\nHaloperidol is a first-generation, 'typical' antipsychotic commonly used for a number of purposes. Apart from its use as an antipsychotic in psychiatric patients, it is often used in the context of delirium as seen here. It also plays a role in the management of nausea, especially in the palliative setting. It binds to numerous receptors, with different affinities for each. Primarily, it antagonises the dopamine\n\nD 2 receptor\n\n.\n\nHaloperidol also antagonises the serotonin\n\n5HT 2\n\nreceptor. However, its effect on the D\n\n2\n\nreceptor is far more effective at any given concentration.\n\nThe drug is not known to significantly affect the serotonin\n\n5HT 3 receptor\n\n. Examples of antagonists of this receptor include the antiemetic ondansetron and newer-generation, 'atypical' antipsychotics such as quetiapine and olanzapine.\n\nThe drug also binds to the dopamine\n\nD 1 receptor\n\n. However, this effect is not as significant as the effect on the D\n\n2\n\nreceptor.\n\nHaloperidol has some effect on the histamine\n\nH 1 receptor\n\n. This effect, however, requires much higher concentrations of haloperidol."
