      "correctText": "Malingering",
      "explanation": "Functional neurological disorder (conversion disorder) - typically involves loss of motor or sensory function\n\nThe correct answer in this scenario is a\n\nConversion disorder\n\n. Conversion disorder, also known as functional neurological symptom disorder, is a psychiatric condition where psychological distress is expressed through physical symptoms. In this case, the patient's numb sensation below his knee and the sensory loss in a non-dermatomal distribution suggest that there may not be an identifiable organic cause for his symptoms. Instead, these symptoms could be manifestations of underlying psychological stress or anxiety related to his upcoming national athletics meeting.\n\nThe first incorrect option is\n\nHypochondrial disorder\n\n, now referred to as Illness Anxiety Disorder. This condition involves excessive worry about having a serious illness despite having no or only mild symptoms. The person may constantly seek medical reassurance and excessively research their perceived illness. In this case, the patient does not present with excessive worry about having an illness but rather reports an unusual sensation in his legs.\n\nSomatisation disorder\n\nis another incorrect option. This condition involves multiple unexplained physical symptoms across different body systems, causing significant distress and impairment in daily functioning. These symptoms cannot be explained by any medical condition or substance use. The patient in this scenario presents with isolated sensory loss below the knee, which does not fit the criteria for somatisation disorder.\n\nThe next incorrect option is\n\nMalingering\n\n. Malingering refers to intentionally feigning or exaggerating physical or psychological symptoms for external gain (e.g., avoiding work or obtaining financial compensation). While it might be possible that the athlete could have secondary motives for presenting with these symptoms, there is no indication in the given information that he has any external gain from doing so.\n\nFinally,\n\nMunchausen's syndrome\n\n, now known as Factitious Disorder Imposed on Self, is also incorrect. This psychiatric condition involves a person deliberately producing or falsifying signs and symptoms of an illness to assume the sick role and receive attention or sympathy. In this case, there is no indication that the patient is intentionally producing his symptoms for attention or sympathy."
