      "explanation": "Alcohol withdrawal\n\nThis patient is likely experiencing alcohol withdrawal. Initial symptoms, 6-12 hours after last consumption, include the features seen here - tremor, sweating, anxiety and tachycardia. Later features include seizures and delirium tremens. Patients presenting to hospital with features of alcohol withdrawal should be treated with long-acting benzodiazepines first-line, such as\n\nchlordiazepoxide\n\nor diazepam.\n\nLorazepam\n\nis a short-acting benzodiazepine and not as preferable for this patient. It may be more suitable for patients with a history of hepatic impairment.\n\nMethadone\n\nis an opioid agonist used in the management of withdrawal from opioid addiction. Although patients experiencing opioid addiction may present with similar symptoms, they typically experience nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea and rhinorrhoea, which are not seen here.\n\nNaloxone\n\nis used in the management of opioid overdose. Rather than being agitated as in this scenario, the patient would be drowsy, have pinpoint pupils and potentially respiratory depression.\n\nPhenytoin\n\nis an anticonvulsant. It is not typically recommended for alcohol withdrawal seizures as its effectiveness for these is questionable. It would not be appropriate here."
